window.INFO_TOPICS = [{"id":"13-overview","ch":"13","chTitle":"Fluid and electrolyte balance and imbalance","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">13 · Chapter 13</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Fluid &amp; electrolyte balance · perfusion</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Fluid and electrolyte balance and imbalance</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> Water is more than half of body weight, and it carries <b>electrolytes</b> — charged particles such as <b>sodium, potassium, calcium and magnesium</b> — whose levels must stay in narrow ranges for nerves, muscles and the heart to work. Three processes move water and particles (<b>filtration, diffusion, osmosis</b>), and three hormones steer the kidneys (<b>aldosterone, ADH, natriuretic peptides</b>), with the <b>renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system</b> guarding blood pressure. The exemplar is <b>dehydration</b>: the nurse finds it from <b>daily weight, orthostatic vital signs, pulse quality, urine and mental status</b>, replaces fluid by mouth or IV, and prevents falls. The rest of the chapter is <b>fluid overload</b> and each electrolyte too high or too low — and the <b>heart</b> is where most of them can kill.</p><div id=\"ch13-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Water is <b>55–60% of body weight</b> in younger adults (<b>50–55%</b> in older adults); <b>two-thirds</b> is inside cells (ICF, about 25 L) and <b>one-third</b> outside (ECF, about 15 L: plasma, interstitial \"third space\" and transcellular fluid). Osmolarity stays near <b>270–300 mOsm/L</b>; <b>sodium</b> holds water in the ECF, <b>potassium</b> sits inside cells, and hormones adjust what the kidneys keep.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Dehydration</b>: intake or retention falls short of need — vomiting, diarrhea, sweating, fever, bleeding, diuretics, NPO, poor thirst or mobility — so plasma volume falls (or fluid shifts out of the vessels). <b>Overload</b>: intake or retention exceeds need (heart or kidney failure, SIADH, too much IV fluid). <b>Electrolyte imbalances</b> come from losses, excess intake, kidney problems and shifts between compartments.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Dehydration: <b>weight loss, a fast weak thready pulse, low BP with orthostatic drops, flat neck veins</b>, fast breathing, poor turgor, dry sticky mouth, <b>confusion</b> (often the first sign in older adults), low-grade fever, <b>dark concentrated urine</b> under 500 mL/day. Overload: <b>weight gain, bounding pulse, distended neck veins, crackles, dependent edema</b>. Electrolytes: muscle weakness or twitching, paresthesias, GI changes and <b>dysrhythmias</b>.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">No single test proves dehydration: <b>hemoconcentration</b> — raised Hgb, Hct, osmolarity, BUN, glucose, protein and electrolytes (but not after hemorrhage) — plus urine specific gravity <b>above 1.030</b>; overload shows <b>hemodilution</b>. Electrolyte levels confirm each imbalance (<b>Na 136–145, K 3.5–5.0, Ca 9.0–10.5, Mg 1.3–2.1</b>), and the <b>ECG</b> shows potassium, calcium and magnesium effects.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Dehydration: <b>oral fluids</b> on a schedule (or oral rehydration solution), <b>IV crystalloids</b> when severe, drugs for the cause (antiemetics, antidiarrheals, antipyretics, antibiotics), <b>fall prevention</b>. Overload: <b>loop diuretics</b>, fluid and sodium limits, daily weights. Electrolytes: replace what is low (<b>IV potassium diluted and slow</b>, calcium, magnesium sulfate) and lower what is high (stop the source, diuretics, insulin with glucose, binders, saline).</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Dehydration → poor perfusion, <b>falls</b>, kidney injury, shock; overload → <b>pulmonary edema, heart failure</b>, and diluted sodium and potassium → <b>seizures, coma</b>. Potassium extremes → <b>heart block, ventricular fibrillation, asystole</b>; low potassium or very high magnesium → <b>respiratory muscle failure</b>; low calcium → <b>tetany, laryngospasm, seizures</b>.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">New <b>crackles, bounding pulse, distended neck veins or falling urine output</b> during IV therapy (pulmonary edema — call the provider); <b>potassium rising with a heart rate under 60 or peaked T waves</b> (call the Rapid Response Team); a potassium infusion <b>infiltrating</b> (stop and remove it); <b>weak breathing</b> with low potassium; <b>Trousseau or Chvostek</b> signs or stridor with low calcium; <b>absent reflexes or a slowing heart</b> on IV magnesium.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Fluid lost faster than it’s replaced<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>plasma volume ↓ → <b>BP ↓, pulse ↑</b>, pulses thready<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>RAAS and ADH hold sodium and water; <b>urine falls and darkens</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>blood <b>concentrates</b> (Hgb, Hct, BUN ↑)<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>brain perfusion ↓ → <b>dizziness, confusion, falls</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>untreated → <b>shock and kidney injury</b></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Dehydration vs fluid overload</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Dehydration</b>: weight <b>down</b>, pulse <b>fast and weak</b>, BP <b>low</b> (worse standing), neck veins <b>flat</b> lying down, urine <b>scant and dark</b>, labs <b>concentrated</b>. <b>Overload</b>: weight <b>up</b>, pulse <b>bounding</b>, BP <b>up</b>, neck veins <b>full even sitting</b>, <b>crackles</b>, <b>pitting edema</b>, labs <b>diluted</b>. In both, the <b>daily weight</b> is the most reliable measure: <b>1 L of fluid = 1 kg (2.2 lb)</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Actual vs relative imbalance</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Actual</b>: the body truly has too much or too little of the substance (diuretics wash potassium out; kidney failure keeps it in). <b>Relative</b>: the total is normal but it’s <b>diluted, concentrated or shifted</b> — water excess dilutes sodium; dehydration concentrates it; <b>insulin and alkalosis push potassium into cells</b>; acidosis and tissue damage pull it out.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Too low vs too high: excitability</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Sodium</b> and <b>potassium</b> low make excitable tissue <b>less</b> responsive (weakness, sluggish reflexes); potassium high makes it <b>more</b> irritable at first, then paralyzed. <b>Calcium and magnesium</b> work the opposite way: <b>low = overexcited</b> (tingling, twitching, Trousseau and Chvostek, tetany, seizures, hyperactive reflexes); <b>high = sluggish</b> (weakness, low reflexes, lethargy, constipation).</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the IV potassium rules exist</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Potassium sets the heart’s resting electrical charge, and its normal blood level is tiny (3.5–5.0 mEq/L), so a concentrated bolus can <b>stop the heart</b>. That is why it is <b>never given IV push, IM or subcutaneously</b>, is <b>diluted by the pharmacy</b> (The Joint Commission keeps concentrated vials off the units), runs at <b>5–10 mEq/hr</b> on a pump, and the IV site is checked <b>every hour</b> — it burns veins and kills tissue if it leaks.</div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Daily weight</b> (same scale, same time before breakfast, same clothes), <b>intake and output</b>, <b>orthostatic BP</b>, pulse rate and quality, neck veins, lung sounds, edema, skin turgor (over the sternum in older adults), mucous membranes, <b>mental status</b>, urine color and amount (and specific gravity), <b>electrolytes, BUN, Hgb and Hct</b>, the <b>ECG</b> for potassium, calcium and magnesium changes, muscle strength and deep tendon reflexes, bowel sounds, the IV site <b>hourly</b> with potassium, and <b>respiratory effort</b> when muscles weaken.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Signs of <b>pulmonary edema</b> during fluid replacement (crackles, dyspnea, bounding pulse, distended neck veins) — slow the fluid and call the provider; <b>potassium over 5.0 with bradycardia under 60 or peaked T waves</b> — Rapid Response Team, cardiac monitor; <b>potassium infusion infiltrating</b> — stop it, remove the catheter, notify, document and photograph; <b>shallow, weak breathing</b> with hypokalemia or hypermagnesemia; <b>positive Trousseau or Chvostek</b>, stridor or seizures with hypocalcemia; <b>confusion, seizures or coma</b> with sodium imbalance; <b>urine under 500 mL/day</b> in dehydration.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Filtration — pushed by pressure</b> — water moves through a membrane from <b>higher hydrostatic (water) pressure to lower</b>. Blood pressure is the push that moves fluid out of capillaries into tissues. When veins back up — as in <b>right-sided heart failure</b> — capillary pressure rises above tissue pressure and extra fluid filters out as <b>edema</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Diffusion — particles spread out</b> — dissolved particles move from <b>higher to lower concentration</b> until even; the steeper the difference, the faster. Cell membranes are choosy: <b>sodium pumps</b> keep sodium about <b>10 times higher outside</b> cells, and <b>glucose needs insulin</b> to open the way in (<b>facilitated diffusion</b> — still no extra energy).</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Osmosis — water follows particles</b> — when particles <b>can’t</b> cross a membrane, <b>water moves toward the more concentrated side</b> until the concentrations match. Normal osmolarity is <b>270–300 mOsm/L</b> (isotonic). <b>Hypertonic</b> fluid (over 300) pulls water <b>out of cells</b> into the vessels; <b>hypotonic</b> fluid (under 270) lets water move <b>into cells</b>, which swell. Thirst comes from <b>osmoreceptors</b> in the brain that shrink when the blood is too concentrated.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why sodium and potassium matter so much</b> — <b>Sodium</b> (136–145 mEq/L outside cells; about 14 inside) holds water in the ECF — \"where sodium goes, water follows.\" <b>Potassium</b> (3.5–5.0 outside; about <b>150 inside</b>) keeps the cell’s resting charge. Because blood potassium is so low, a fall of just <b>1 mEq/L (4 to 3) is a 25% change</b> — enough to disturb every nerve, muscle and heartbeat.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The body guards two volumes above all — the <b>blood (plasma)</b> for blood pressure and perfusion, and the <b>cells</b> for their work — and uses the <b>interstitial space as its buffer</b>, filling it or draining it to protect the other two. That is why edema can appear while the blood volume is low, and why the nurse judges hydration from <b>perfusion signs</b> (pulse, BP, urine, mental status) and <b>weight</b>, not from swelling alone.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","ch":"13","chTitle":"Fluid and electrolyte balance and imbalance","title":"Body water, compartments and IV solutions","kind":"topic","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Where the water is</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>How much</b> — water is <b>55–60%</b> of weight in younger adults and <b>50–55%</b> in older adults. <b>Women</b> have less than men of the same size (more fat, less muscle — muscle cells are mostly water, fat cells hold almost none), and an <b>obese</b> adult has less water than a lean one of the same weight.</div>\n<div><b>The compartments</b> — <b>intracellular fluid (ICF)</b> — inside the cells, about <b>two-thirds (25 L)</b>; <b>extracellular fluid (ECF)</b> — about <b>one-third (15 L)</b>: <b>plasma</b> (about 3.5–6 L), <b>interstitial fluid</b> between cells (about 10–12 L, the \"<b>third space</b>\"), and <b>transcellular</b> fluids (cerebrospinal, synovial, peritoneal, pleural).</div>\n<div><b>Solvent, solute, electrolytes</b> — water is the <b>solvent</b>; <b>solutes</b> are dissolved particles; solutes with a charge are <b>electrolytes (ions)</b> — <b>cations</b> positive (Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺), <b>anions</b> negative (Cl⁻, HCO₃⁻). Body fluids stay <b>electrically neutral</b>. Osmolarity is particles per liter, osmolality per kilogram — treated as the same in practice.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Because the ECF is only a third of body water and plasma a small part of that, <b>losing plasma volume quickly drops blood pressure</b> — the kidneys and hormones react within minutes, long before the cells run short.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">IV solutions by tonicity (Table 13.3)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Isotonic (270–300 mOsm/L)</b> — <b>0.9% saline (308)</b>, <b>lactated Ringer’s (273)</b>, <b>D5W (272)</b> and D5 in 0.225% saline (321) at the time they’re hung. Isotonic fluid <b>stays in the ECF</b> — it expands plasma and interstitial volume without shifting water into or out of cells.</div>\n<div><b>Hypotonic (under 270)</b> — <b>0.45% saline (154)</b>. Water moves <b>into the cells</b>, so cells and the interstitial space gain volume while the plasma loses some — useful for <b>cellular dehydration</b>, risky for anyone with <b>brain swelling or low BP</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Hypertonic (over 300)</b> — <b>3% or 5% saline</b>, <b>D10W (500)</b>, <b>D5 in 0.9% saline (560)</b>, <b>D5 in 0.45% saline (406)</b>, <b>D5 in lactated Ringer’s (525)</b>. Strongly hypertonic fluid <b>pulls water out of cells and tissues into the vessels</b> — plasma expands, cells shrink. <b>3% saline</b> is given in small volumes on a controller.</div>\n<div><b>The dextrose catch</b> — dextrose counts toward tonicity only <b>in the bag</b>: once infused, the glucose is <b>metabolized quickly</b>, and the fluid acts like whatever is left — <b>D5W behaves like free water (hypotonic)</b>, and <b>D5 in 0.45% saline</b> acts hypotonic once in the body.</div>\n<div><b>Crystalloids vs colloids</b> — <b>crystalloids</b> (saline, LR, dextrose solutions) are water with electrolytes or glucose that spread into <b>all</b> compartments — first choice for dehydration. <b>Colloids</b> (large molecules such as albumin) stay in the plasma and pull water in with less volume, but are a <b>second-line</b> option for hypovolemia.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Tonicity decides <b>which compartment the water ends up in</b>. That is the whole logic of choosing a fluid: isotonic to refill the vessels, hypotonic to rehydrate cells, hypertonic to pull water out of swollen cells or raise a dangerously low sodium.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Normal values and what moves them (Table 13.1)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Who is at risk</b> — healthy people get <b>mild</b> imbalances from changes in intake and output, easily corrected. <b>Severe</b> ones — real losses or retention — are life-threatening and can happen anywhere; the highest risk is in <b>older adults</b>, people with <b>chronic kidney or endocrine disease</b>, and anyone on <b>drugs that change fluid and electrolyte balance</b>. Every ill adult is at some risk.</div>\n<div><b>Sodium 136–145 mEq/L</b> — <b>high</b> with dehydration, kidney disease and too much cortisol; <b>low</b> with fluid overload, liver disease and adrenal insufficiency. Food: <b>processed and preserved</b> foods (smoked, pickled, snacks, many condiments) are highest; <b>fresh fish, poultry, vegetables and fruit</b> are lowest.</div>\n<div><b>Potassium 3.5–5.0 mEq/L</b> — <b>high</b> with dehydration, kidney disease, <b>acidosis</b>, adrenal insufficiency and <b>crush injuries</b>; <b>low</b> with fluid overload, <b>diuretics, alkalosis, insulin</b> and too much aldosterone. Highest in <b>meat, fish and many fruits and vegetables</b>; lowest in <b>eggs, bread and cereal grains</b>. About <b>80% leaves through the kidneys</b>, more under aldosterone.</div>\n<div><b>Calcium 9.0–10.5 mg/dL</b> — <b>high</b> with hyperparathyroidism and hyperthyroidism; <b>low</b> with low vitamin D, hypoparathyroidism, hypothyroidism, kidney disease and a high intake of <b>phosphorus-containing foods and drinks</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Chloride 98–106 mEq/L</b> — <b>high</b> with metabolic acidosis, respiratory alkalosis and too much cortisol; <b>low</b> with fluid overload, <b>heavy vomiting or diarrhea</b>, adrenal insufficiency and diuretics.</div>\n<div><b>Magnesium 1.3–2.1 mEq/L and osmolarity 270–300</b> — magnesium: <b>high</b> with kidney disease, hypothyroidism and adrenal insufficiency; <b>low</b> with malnutrition, <b>alcohol use</b> and ketoacidosis. Osmolarity: <b>high</b> with dehydration, hypernatremia and <b>hyperglycemia</b>; <b>low</b> with fluid overload, hyponatremia, low blood protein and malnutrition.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The same few causes keep appearing: <b>kidney disease</b> keeps electrolytes in, <b>diuretics and GI losses</b> take them out, <b>acid–base changes and insulin</b> move potassium between cells and blood, and <b>water gained or lost</b> dilutes or concentrates everything. Learning those four explains most of the table.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","ch":"13","chTitle":"Fluid and electrolyte balance and imbalance","title":"Hormones, RAAS and daily intake and output","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The three hormones</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Aldosterone</b> — made by the <b>adrenal cortex</b> when ECF <b>sodium is low</b> (and when RAAS calls for it). It makes the kidneys <b>keep sodium and water</b> — raising blood volume and osmolarity — and <b>excrete potassium</b>. Nickname: the <b>water-and-sodium-saving hormone</b>.</div>\n<div><b>ADH (vasopressin)</b> — released from the <b>posterior pituitary</b> when <b>osmoreceptors</b> in the hypothalamus sense <b>concentrated blood</b> (high sodium). It makes the kidney tubules let <b>water only</b> back into the blood, diluting it. Dilute blood (low sodium) switches ADH <b>off</b>, so more water leaves in the urine.</div>\n<div><b>Natriuretic peptides</b> — <b>ANP</b> from the atria and <b>BNP</b> from the ventricles, released when <b>high volume stretches the heart</b>. They do the <b>opposite of aldosterone</b>: the kidneys <b>lose sodium and water</b>, so blood volume and pressure fall.</div>\n<div><b>How sodium is kept in range</b> — <b>low sodium</b> → ADH and NPs are switched off and RAAS switches on → the kidneys <b>keep sodium and lose water</b>. <b>High sodium</b> → aldosterone is switched off, ADH and NPs on → the kidneys <b>lose sodium and keep water</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Each hormone answers one question: aldosterone — \"is there enough <b>sodium</b>?\"; ADH — \"is the blood too <b>concentrated</b>?\"; natriuretic peptides — \"is the heart <b>overstretched</b>?\" Reading an imbalance means asking which hormone is over- or under-working.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What starts it</b> — the kidneys sense that <b>perfusion is at risk</b>: <b>low blood pressure, low blood volume</b> (dehydration, hemorrhage), <b>low blood oxygen</b> or <b>low blood sodium</b>. Special kidney cells then release <b>renin</b>.</div>\n<div><b>The cascade</b> — renin activates <b>angiotensinogen</b> into <b>angiotensin I</b>; <b>angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE)</b> turns that into <b>angiotensin II</b>, the active form.</div>\n<div><b>What angiotensin II does</b> — <b>constricts arteries and veins</b> everywhere (raising BP without adding volume); <b>constricts the arterioles feeding the nephrons</b>, cutting filtration and <b>urine output</b> sharply; and makes the adrenals release <b>aldosterone</b>, so the kidneys keep <b>water and sodium</b>. Together: more volume, more pressure, better perfusion.</div>\n<div><b>The drugs that use it</b> — blood-pressure drugs work on this path: <b>diuretics</b> wash out sodium (and water); <b>ACE inhibitors</b> (the \"-prils\") make less angiotensin II; <b>ARBs</b> (the \"-sartans\") block its receptors. A <b>low-sodium diet</b> helps for the same reason — less sodium, less water held. (Chapter 30 covers the drugs.)</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Low blood volume can kill within minutes, so the body has <b>several overlapping defenses</b> that all push the same way: squeeze the vessels, stop making urine, and hold sodium and water. That is why a dehydrated patient’s <b>urine output falls before the BP does</b>.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Intake, output and aging</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Intake (Table 13.2)</b> — about <b>2300 mL a day</b> from drinks and food (solid foods are up to <b>85% water</b>). Thirst is triggered by <b>rising osmolarity or falling blood volume</b>. Measurable intake: oral and IV fluids, enemas and irrigations (amount instilled minus amount returned); not measurable: solid food and water made by metabolism. Measurable output: <b>urine, emesis, feces and drainage</b> from body cavities; not measurable: <b>sweat and water vapor from the lungs</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Output and the kidneys</b> — the <b>kidney</b> is the most important, adjustable route. <b>Obligatory urine output — 400 to 600 mL a day</b> — is the least that can clear the body’s wastes; below it, wastes, acid and electrolytes build up to lethal levels.</div>\n<div><b>Insensible loss</b> — water lost with no control — skin, lungs, stool, saliva, fistulas, drains, GI suction — about <b>500–1000 mL a day</b>, rising sharply with <b>fever, burns, trauma, thyroid crisis, mechanical ventilation, fast breathing and continuous GI suction</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults (age-related changes)</b> — skin loses elasticity, so <b>turgor becomes unreliable</b>; the kidneys filter less and <b>can’t concentrate urine</b>, losing water; less muscle means <b>less body water</b>; the <b>thirst reflex is weaker</b>; adrenal atrophy makes sodium and potassium control poor (<b>hyponatremia, hyperkalemia</b>). Always ask which <b>drugs</b> they take.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>An older adult starts with less water, feels less thirst and loses more through the kidneys — so the margin before dehydration is small, and the first sign is often <b>new confusion</b> rather than thirst.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","ch":"13","chTitle":"Fluid and electrolyte balance and imbalance","title":"Dehydration — the concept exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is and who gets it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Definition</b> — fluid intake or retention <b>below what the body needs</b>, so fluid volume — especially <b>plasma volume</b> — falls. It is a <b>condition, not a disease</b>. It may be a real loss of body water, or <b>vascular dehydration</b>: water shifts out of the plasma into the interstitial space while total body water is unchanged.</div>\n<div><b>Isotonic dehydration</b> — the <b>most common</b> kind: water and electrolytes are lost together, <b>only from the ECF</b> (plasma and interstitial); cells keep their volume. The result is <b>hypovolemia</b> and poorer perfusion; the body compensates by <b>constricting vessels</b> to hold the BP.</div>\n<div><b>Causes (Box 13.1)</b> — <b>hemorrhage, vomiting, diarrhea</b>, heavy salivation, <b>fistulas, ileostomy</b>, heavy sweating, <b>burns</b>, severe wounds, <b>long NPO</b>, <b>diuretics</b>, <b>GI suction</b>, hyperventilation, <b>fever</b>, arginine vasopressin deficiency or resistance (formerly diabetes insipidus), trouble swallowing, <b>impaired thirst</b>, unconsciousness, impaired movement.</div>\n<div><b>Higher risk</b> — <b>older adults</b> (less body water, weak thirst, trouble getting a drink, <b>diuretics, antihypertensives, laxatives</b>) — assess fluid status in every setting, especially long-term care; <b>women</b> (less body water than men).</div>\n<div><b>Prevention</b> — match intake to output; <b>drink more</b> with heavy or long exercise, in dry climates and at altitude; avoid <b>caffeine and alcohol</b> for rehydration — both increase fluid loss.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Dehydration isn’t its own disease — it is the body falling behind on fluid for some other reason. Treating it always has two parts: <b>refill the volume</b> and <b>fix why it was lost</b>.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — what and how much the patient drinks and eats (ice cream, gelatin and ices count as liquids), <b>exact intake and output</b>, <b>daily weights</b> (weigh, don’t ask), <b>drugs</b> — prescription and over-the-counter, especially <b>diuretics and laxatives</b> — kidney or endocrine disease, mental status, and whether <b>rings or shoes feel looser</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Weight is the best measure</b> — <b>1 L of water weighs 1 kg (2.2 lb)</b>, so a day-to-day weight change is the <b>best indicator</b> of fluid lost or gained: <b>1 lb ≈ 500 mL</b>. A loss of more than <b>half a pound a day</b> is fluid.</div>\n<div><b>Heart and vessels</b> — <b>heart rate rises</b>; peripheral pulses are <b>weak, thready and easily blocked</b>; <b>BP and pulse pressure fall</b> (systolic most); <b>orthostatic hypotension</b> — measure lying, then sitting, then standing — causes lightheadedness and <b>falls</b>. <b>Neck and hand veins are flat</b> even lying down or with the hands low (neck veins are normally full when lying flat).</div>\n<div><b>Lungs, skin, mouth</b> — breathing <b>speeds up</b> to keep oxygen delivery up. <b>Poor turgor</b> — pinched skin stays tented — and dry, scaly skin; in <b>older adults test turgor over the sternum</b> (hand skin tents from age alone). Mouth <b>dry, sticky, cracked</b>, tongue <b>deeply furrowed</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Brain and temperature</b> — <b>mental status changes</b> from poor brain perfusion — often the <b>first sign in older adults</b>. A <b>low-grade fever</b> is common, and fever itself worsens dehydration: each <b>1 °C above normal costs at least 500 mL</b> more fluid.</div>\n<div><b>Kidneys and urine</b> — urine <b>scant, dark amber, strong-smelling</b>, specific gravity <b>above 1.030</b>; output <b>under 500 mL a day</b> (without kidney disease) is a concern. Compare total output with total intake and the daily weight.</div>\n<div><b>Labs and imaging</b> — no single test: <b>hemoconcentration</b> — Hgb, Hct, osmolarity, glucose, protein, <b>BUN</b> and electrolytes all <b>rise</b> as water leaves (except after <b>hemorrhage</b>, which loses cells and plasma together). In intensive care, <b>ultrasound and echocardiography</b> help judge volume and fluid shifts.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every finding is the body protecting its blood pressure: a faster heart, tighter vessels and less urine. When those defenses are stretched, <b>standing up</b> is what tips the balance — which is why orthostatic vital signs and fall prevention sit at the center of care.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"13-rehydrating-safely","ch":"13","chTitle":"Fluid and electrolyte balance and imbalance","title":"Rehydrating safely","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Restoring fluid (Box 13.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Oral first</b> — <b>mild to moderate</b> dehydration in a patient who can swallow: <b>oral fluids</b> the patient likes, within any diet limits (sugar-free, low-sodium, <b>thickened</b>). Set the <b>24-hour amount with the team</b>, <b>divide it by shifts</b> and offer <b>small amounts hourly</b> — at least <b>every 2 hours around the clock</b> — for even spacing and less risk of overload. A <b>bedside water bottle with a long straw</b> helps patients who can’t lift a cup.</div>\n<div><b>Working with assistive personnel</b> — teach AP to offer <b>60–120 mL every hour</b> to patients who are dehydrated or at risk, to record the <b>exact amount</b>, and <b>never to withhold fluids to prevent incontinence</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Oral rehydration solutions</b> — glucose-and-electrolyte drinks that are <b>absorbed even during vomiting or diarrhea</b> — used at home, in long-term care, and when veins are poor.</div>\n<div><b>IV replacement</b> — for <b>severe</b> dehydration or when oral fluids aren’t tolerated. The amount is based on <b>weight loss and symptoms</b>; the <b>rate</b> on how dehydrated the patient is and on the <b>heart, lungs and kidneys</b>. <b>Crystalloids</b> first. The two most important things to watch: <b>urine output</b> and <b>pulse rate and quality</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Monitoring during replacement</b> — check the response at least <b>every 2 hours</b> — pulse quality and pulse pressure, urine output, and <b>weight every 8 hours</b> — and watch for <b>overload</b>: bounding pulse, trouble breathing, neck veins <b>distended while upright</b>, dependent edema. Check the IV site <b>every hour</b> for infiltration or phlebitis (swelling, pain, cordlike vein, slowing drip).</div>\n<div><b>Drugs for the cause</b> — <b>antiemetics</b> (vomiting), <b>antidiarrheals</b> (diarrhea), <b>antibiotics</b> (bacterial diarrhea), <b>antipyretics</b> (fever).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Replacing fluid too slowly leaves organs underperfused; too fast floods the lungs — especially with a weak heart or kidneys. The answer is a <b>planned, even schedule</b> and <b>reassessing every 2 hours</b>, not one big push.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Preventing injury and evaluating</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Falls</b> — dehydrated patients fall from <b>orthostatic hypotension, dysrhythmias, weakness and confusion</b>. Check vital signs (heart rate quality, BP), muscle strength, gait and alertness; teach them to <b>get up slowly</b> and <b>sit down at once if lightheaded</b>, and to <b>ask for help</b> walking; use fall precautions.</div>\n<div><b>Getting ready to walk</b> — before an older dehydrated patient walks to the bathroom, the priority check is <b>orthostatic BP</b> — it tells you whether standing will drop the pressure enough to cause a fall.</div>\n<div><b>Signs it is working</b> — drinks at least <b>1500 mL a day</b> (or <b>500 mL more than the urine output</b>); BP at or near the usual; <b>moist mucous membranes, normal turgor</b>; <b>neck veins full when lying flat</b>; steady urine output; asks for help walking and <b>doesn’t fall</b>; knows the signs of dehydration and starts fluids early; follows treatment for the conditions that cause it.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A normal neck-vein fill when lying flat is a quiet but reliable sign that the plasma volume is back: flat veins in the supine position meant the vessels were underfilled.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"13-fluid-overload","ch":"13","chTitle":"Fluid and electrolyte balance and imbalance","title":"Fluid overload","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What happens</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Definition and causes</b> — <b>overhydration</b>: intake or retention <b>exceeds</b> the body’s needs — usually <b>hypervolemia</b> (excess ECF). Causes (Box 13.1): <b>too much IV fluid</b>, late <b>kidney failure</b>, <b>heart failure</b>, long-term <b>corticosteroids</b>, <b>SIADH</b>, psychiatric polydipsia, water intoxication.</div>\n<div><b>How the body copes</b> — with mild or moderate overload the body <b>shifts fluid into the tissues (edema)</b>, turns <b>down ADH and aldosterone</b>, turns <b>up natriuretic peptides</b>, and <b>makes more urine</b>. When overload is severe — or the heart or kidneys are weak — it leads to <b>heart failure and pulmonary edema</b>, and diluted sodium and potassium cause <b>seizures, coma and death</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Key features (Box 13.3)</b> — heart: <b>fast, bounding pulse</b>, <b>high BP</b>, narrower pulse pressure, high central venous pressure, <b>distended neck and hand veins</b>, engorged varicose veins, <b>weight gain</b>. Lungs: fast shallow breathing, shortness of breath, <b>moist crackles</b>. Skin: <b>pitting edema</b> in dependent areas, pale and cool. Nerves and muscles: altered LOC, headache, visual changes, weakness, paresthesias. GI: more motility when acute, <b>enlarged liver</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Labs</b> — electrolytes are often <b>normal</b> in isotonic overload, but <b>Hgb, Hct and serum protein fall</b> from the extra water (<b>hemodilution</b>).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Overload shows up first where the pressure backs up: the <b>neck veins</b>, the <b>lungs</b> and the <b>dependent tissues</b>. Crackles mean fluid has crossed into the alveoli — the step before pulmonary edema.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Caring for it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Safety first: critical rescue</b> — assess at least <b>every 2 hours</b> for <b>pulmonary edema</b>, which can develop fast. <b>Bounding pulse, worsening neck-vein distention, crackles, spreading edema or falling urine output → notify the provider</b>. Older adults and anyone with heart, kidney, lung or liver problems are at greatest risk.</div>\n<div><b>Skin</b> — edematous skin breaks down easily: a <b>pressure-reducing overlay</b>, check the coccyx, elbows, hips and heels daily, and <b>reposition every 2 hours</b> (AP can do it).</div>\n<div><b>Drugs</b> — <b>loop (high-ceiling) diuretics</b> such as <b>furosemide</b> when the kidneys work. If too much sodium and electrolyte loss is a concern, or in <b>SIADH</b>, <b>conivaptan or tolvaptan</b> (they remove water, not sodium). Watch for <b>weight loss and more urine</b>, check <b>sodium and potassium</b> whenever drawn, and watch the <b>ECG</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diet</b> — chronic overload: <b>limit fluid and sodium</b>. Mild: \"<b>no added salt</b>\" at the table; more severe: a real sodium restriction — teach <b>label reading</b> and a <b>daily sodium record</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Weights and I&amp;O</b> — rapid <b>weight gain is the best sign</b> of hidden fluid retention: metabolism explains only about <b>½ lb (250 g) a day</b>; each pound beyond that is about <b>500 mL</b> retained. Weigh at the <b>same time (before breakfast), on the same scale, in the same clothes</b>; for bed scales, lift tubing off and keep the <b>same number of blankets and pillows</b>. Teach AP that measurements must be <b>exact, not estimated</b>, and to report the urine’s <b>color and character</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Going home</b> — teach a <b>daily weight log</b> (apps are fine) and to <b>call the provider for a gain of more than 3 lb (1.5 kg) in a week or more than 2 lb (1 kg) in 24 hours</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Fluid can accumulate for days before it shows as edema, but the scale sees it at once. That is why the discharge rule is about <b>pounds per day and per week</b>, not about swelling.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","ch":"13","chTitle":"Fluid and electrolyte balance and imbalance","title":"Sodium: hyponatremia and hypernatremia","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hyponatremia — sodium under 136 mEq/L</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Why it causes problems</b> — low sodium makes <b>depolarization slower</b>, so nerves and muscles are <b>less excitable</b>; and because the ECF is now less concentrated than the cells, <b>water moves into the cells</b> — they swell, work poorly and can burst. In the brain that swelling means <b>cerebral edema</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Causes (Box 13.4)</b> — actual loss: <b>vomiting, diarrhea</b>, heavy sweating, <b>diuretics</b> (a common cause, especially in older adults), large burns, <b>low aldosterone</b>, kidney disease, <b>hyperglycemia</b>. Dilution: <b>drinking too much water</b>, psychiatric polydipsia, kidney failure, <b>irrigation with hypotonic fluid</b>, <b>SIADH</b>, <b>heart failure</b>, <b>cirrhosis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Brain</b> — behavior changes from <b>cerebral edema and rising intracranial pressure</b>; <b>sudden or worsening confusion</b> is common in older adults; very low levels cause <b>seizures, coma and death</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Muscles and gut</b> — general <b>muscle weakness</b>, worse in the arms and legs, with <b>diminished deep tendon reflexes</b>. <b>Action alert</b>: when muscles are weak, <b>check breathing effectiveness at once</b> — the respiratory muscles may be failing too. The gut <b>speeds up</b>: nausea, diarrhea, cramping, <b>hyperactive bowel sounds</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Heart: depends on volume</b> — with <b>low volume</b>: fast, <b>weak, thready</b> pulse, low BP, <b>orthostatic</b> hypotension, low CVP. With <b>fluid overload</b>: <b>full, bounding</b> pulse, normal or high BP.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — find the <b>cause</b>. Cut back sodium-losing drugs (<b>diuretics</b>). With a <b>fluid deficit</b>: <b>IV saline</b>; for <b>severe</b> hyponatremia, <b>small volumes of 3% saline</b> on a <b>controller</b> with close monitoring. With <b>fluid excess</b>: drugs that <b>remove water, not sodium</b> (<b>conivaptan, tolvaptan</b>); for <b>SIADH</b>, <b>lithium or demeclocycline</b>. Mild: <b>more dietary sodium and less fluid</b>. Assess <b>hourly</b> for too much fluid, potassium or sodium change; the priorities are watching the response and preventing <b>hypernatremia and overload</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Sodium’s symptoms are mostly the <b>brain</b>: it is enclosed by the skull, so even small cell swelling raises pressure. That is why the correction must be <b>controlled</b> — a 3% infusion that runs too fast can swing the patient into new harm.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hypernatremia — sodium over 145 mEq/L</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Why it causes problems</b> — extra sodium outside the cells makes excitable tissue <b>irritable</b> — it overreacts — at first; and water leaves the cells to dilute the concentrated ECF, so cells <b>shrink and dehydrate</b>. Eventually the dried-out tissue <b>stops responding</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Causes (Box 13.5)</b> — actual excess: <b>hyperaldosteronism</b>, kidney failure, <b>corticosteroids</b>, <b>Cushing syndrome</b>, too much oral sodium or <b>sodium-containing IV fluid</b>. Relative (water loss): <b>dehydration</b>, high metabolic rate, <b>fever</b>, hyperventilation, infection, heavy sweating, <b>watery diarrhea</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Brain and muscles</b> — with normal or low volume: <b>short attention span, agitation, confusion</b>; with overload: <b>lethargy, stupor, coma</b>. Muscles <b>twitch</b> early, then grow <b>progressively weaker</b>, with reduced or absent reflexes on both sides — check hand grip, arm flexion and the knee and ankle reflexes.</div>\n<div><b>Heart</b> — contractility <b>falls</b> (high sodium slows calcium entry into heart cells). With <b>low volume</b>: fast pulse, weak thready pulses, <b>hypotension, orthostatic</b> drops, narrow pulse pressure. With <b>overload</b>: slow-to-normal <b>bounding</b> pulses, <b>neck veins full even upright</b>, raised BP (especially diastolic).</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — from <b>fluid loss</b>: <b>0.9% saline</b> or <b>D5 in 0.45% saline</b> (hypertonic in the bag, <b>hypotonic once infused</b> as the glucose is used). From <b>reduced kidney sodium excretion</b>: diuretics that remove sodium (<b>furosemide, bumetanide</b>). Assess <b>hourly</b> for losses of fluid, sodium or potassium. Diet: <b>enough water</b> (especially older adults); sodium limits with kidney problems — teach how to read sodium content in foods, drinks and drugs.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Hypernatremia is usually <b>too little water</b> rather than too much salt — which is why older adults with a weak thirst drive and fever or diarrhea are the classic patients, and why <b>water</b> is the treatment.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","ch":"13","chTitle":"Fluid and electrolyte balance and imbalance","title":"Potassium: hypokalemia and hyperkalemia","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hypokalemia — potassium under 3.5 mEq/L</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Why it matters</b> — most potassium is <b>inside cells</b>, so a small change outside them changes <b>every excitable cell</b>: low potassium makes nerves and muscles <b>less responsive</b>. A slow loss may cause no symptoms until severe; a <b>rapid fall is dramatic</b> — and it can kill through the <b>heart and the breathing muscles</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Causes (Box 13.6)</b> — actual loss: <b>diuretics</b> (loop and thiazide), <b>corticosteroids</b>, too much <b>aldosterone</b>, <b>Cushing syndrome</b>, <b>diarrhea, vomiting</b>, GI wound drainage, <b>prolonged NG suction</b>, heavy sweating, kidney disease. Relative (shift or dilution): <b>alkalosis</b>, <b>insulin</b> (it drives the sodium-potassium pump, pushing potassium into cells), TPN, water intoxication, potassium-poor IV fluids. <b>Low magnesium</b> usually comes with low potassium.</div>\n<div><b>Who to watch</b> — older adults (urine-concentrating ability falls, more diuretic use); anyone on <b>diuretics, corticosteroids, beta agonists or blockers</b>; a patient on an <b>IV insulin infusion</b> (for DKA, for example) — insulin moves potassium into cells fast. Ask about potassium supplements and potassium-rich foods.</div>\n<div><b>Breathing</b> — respiratory muscles weaken → <b>shallow</b> breathing. <b>Action alert</b>: assess respiratory status <b>at least every 2 hours</b> — respiratory insufficiency and dysrhythmias are the <b>main causes of death</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Muscles, nerves, gut</b> — <b>weakness</b> (may be too weak to stand), weak hand grasps, <b>hyporeflexia</b>, and with severe loss <b>flaccid paralysis</b>. Irritability and anxiety, then lethargy, <b>confusion and coma</b>. The gut <b>slows</b>: hypoactive bowel sounds, nausea, vomiting, <b>constipation</b>, distention, <b>paralytic ileus</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Heart and ECG</b> — pulse <b>weak and thready</b>, rate very slow to very fast, often <b>irregular</b>; <b>orthostatic hypotension</b>. ECG: <b>ST depression</b>, <b>flat or inverted T waves</b>, a <b>prominent U wave</b> (plus a slightly prolonged PR).</div>\n<div><b>Replacing potassium safely</b> — potassium chloride, gluconate or citrate; <b>IV for severe</b> loss. IV potassium is <b>high alert</b>: it is <b>diluted only by the pharmacy</b> (concentrated vials are kept off the units — a Joint Commission safety goal), given <b>slowly — 5 to 10 mEq/hr</b> on a pump, <b>never by IV push</b> (it can <b>stop the heart</b>) and <b>never IM or subcutaneously</b> (it kills tissue). Check the dilution twice before hanging it. Check the <b>IV site every hour</b> and ask about burning. <b>Infiltration</b>: stop the infusion, <b>remove the catheter</b>, notify the provider, and document with a description and <b>photograph</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Oral potassium and diet</b> — oral potassium <b>tastes bad</b> and causes <b>nausea</b> — give it <b>with or after a meal</b>, never on an empty stomach. Avoid potassium-wasting <b>loop and thiazide</b> diuretics; a <b>potassium-sparing</b> diuretic (<b>spironolactone</b>) can be used instead. Teach potassium-rich foods (<b>meat, fish, many fruits and vegetables</b> — bananas, citrus, raisins); food alone <b>prevents</b> further loss but <b>supplements</b> are needed to correct it.</div>\n<div><b>Safety</b> — weakness → <b>fall precautions</b>, a <b>gait belt</b>, call before getting up. For severe hypokalemia monitor breathing <b>hourly</b>: SpO₂, the strength of the <b>cough</b>, color, and ABGs for low oxygen or high CO₂.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Insulin is the hidden potassium drug: by driving the sodium-potassium pump it pulls potassium <b>into</b> cells within minutes. A patient on an insulin drip can drop from normal to dangerously low — which is why potassium is checked so often in DKA treatment.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hyperkalemia — potassium over 5.0 mEq/L</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Why it matters</b> — high potassium makes excitable tissue respond to <b>weaker stimuli</b>, and the <b>heart</b> is very sensitive: conduction is disrupted, leading to <b>heart block and ventricular fibrillation</b>. How <b>fast</b> it rises matters: a sudden rise causes severe problems at <b>6–7 mEq/L</b>; a slow one may not until <b>8 or more</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Causes (Box 13.7)</b> — rare with normal kidneys — most cases happen <b>during medical treatment</b>; the highest risk is in <b>older adults</b>, the <b>chronically ill or debilitated</b>, and anyone on a <b>potassium-sparing diuretic</b>. Ask about palpitations or skipped beats, twitching, leg weakness, tingling or numbness, and new diarrhea. Actual excess: potassium-rich foods or drugs, <b>salt substitutes</b>, potassium chloride, <b>rapid or bolus IV potassium</b>, <b>blood transfusions</b>, adrenal insufficiency, <b>kidney failure</b>, <b>potassium-sparing diuretics</b>, <b>ACE inhibitors</b> (and ARBs). Relative (shift out of cells): <b>tissue damage</b>, <b>acidosis</b>, high uric acid, <b>uncontrolled diabetes</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Heart (the main danger)</b> — <b>bradycardia, hypotension</b>, and ECG changes: <b>tall, peaked T waves</b>, <b>prolonged PR</b>, <b>flat or absent P waves</b>, <b>wide QRS</b>, ectopic beats — then <b>complete heart block, asystole or ventricular fibrillation</b>. Cardiac changes are the <b>most common cause of death</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Muscles, nerves, gut</b> — two phases: early <b>twitching</b> with tingling, burning, then <b>numbness</b> of the hands, feet and around the mouth; later <b>weakness</b> rising from the hands and feet to the arms and legs, then <b>flaccid paralysis</b> (the breathing muscles only at lethal levels). The gut <b>speeds up</b>: diarrhea, <b>hyperactive bowel sounds</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Labs</b> — potassium <b>over 5.0</b>. From dehydration, other electrolytes, Hct and Hgb are high too; from <b>kidney failure</b>, <b>creatinine and BUN rise</b>, <b>pH falls</b>, and Hct and Hgb are normal or low.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — the priorities: watch for <b>cardiac complications</b>, <b>prevent falls</b>, follow the response, teach. <b>Stop potassium-containing infusions but keep the IV open</b>; hold oral potassium; a <b>low-potassium diet</b>. <b>Patiromer</b> binds potassium in the gut. If the kidneys work, <b>potassium-wasting diuretics</b>; if not, dialysis. To move potassium <b>into cells</b> for a while: <b>IV insulin with glucose</b> — hypertonic, so through a central line or a large vein — watch for <b>hypoglycemia and hypokalemia</b>. <b>Continuous cardiac monitoring</b>, comparing with an ECG from when potassium was normal.</div>\n<div><b>Critical rescue</b> — in a patient with or at risk for hyperkalemia, a <b>heart rate under 60 or spiked T waves → call the Rapid Response Team</b>. In the ED with potassium <b>6.5</b>, the priority is a <b>continuous cardiac monitor</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Teaching</b> — avoid <b>high-potassium foods</b>, <b>read labels</b> on food and drugs, and <b>avoid salt substitutes</b> (they are potassium chloride).</div>\n<div><b>Beyond the book</b> — in a hyperkalemic emergency with ECG changes, many protocols give <b>IV calcium gluconate first</b> — it <b>protects the heart’s membranes</b> within minutes but <b>doesn’t lower potassium</b>; insulin with glucose, inhaled albuterol and binders such as <b>sodium zirconium cyclosilicate</b> or sodium polystyrene sulfonate then bring the level down. Your book lists patiromer, diuretics, insulin with glucose and dialysis.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Potassium’s danger depends on the <b>speed</b> of change because cells adapt: a kidney patient may tolerate 6 for weeks, while a rapid IV infusion can stop the heart at the same level. That is why <b>IV potassium is slow and diluted</b> and why the ECG — not the number alone — decides how urgent it is.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","ch":"13","chTitle":"Fluid and electrolyte balance and imbalance","title":"Calcium: hypocalcemia and hypercalcemia","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">How calcium is controlled</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Forms and jobs</b> — calcium is <b>bound</b> (mostly to <b>albumin</b>) or <b>ionized (free)</b> — the free form is the <b>active</b> one. It builds <b>bone</b>, activates enzymes, drives <b>skeletal and cardiac muscle contraction</b>, controls <b>nerve impulses</b> and helps <b>blood clot</b>. Most is stored in <b>bone</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Raising it: PTH and vitamin D</b> — <b>parathyroid hormone (PTH)</b> raises calcium by <b>releasing it from bone</b>, <b>activating vitamin D</b> (so the gut absorbs more), and making the kidneys <b>excrete less and reabsorb more</b>. Gut absorption needs <b>active vitamin D</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Lowering it: calcitonin</b> — when calcium is high, PTH falls and the thyroid’s <b>thyrocalcitonin (calcitonin)</b> rises: it <b>stops bone release</b>, blocks vitamin D–driven absorption and makes the kidneys <b>excrete calcium</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Calcium and excitability</b> — calcium <b>stabilizes</b> nerve and muscle membranes by <b>slowing sodium entry</b>. <b>Low</b> calcium lets sodium rush in — nerves fire <b>too easily and at the wrong times</b>; <b>high</b> calcium makes them need a <b>stronger stimulus</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Calcium works like a <b>brake on nerve firing</b> — which is why low calcium looks like tingling, cramps, twitching and tetany, and high calcium looks like weakness, lethargy and constipation.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hypocalcemia — calcium under 9.0 mg/dL</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Causes (Box 13.8)</b> — actual loss: <b>low calcium intake</b>, lactose intolerance, <b>malabsorption</b> (celiac disease, sprue, Crohn’s), <b>low vitamin D</b>, <b>end-stage kidney disease</b>, diarrhea, steatorrhea, GI wound drainage. Relative: high blood protein, <b>alkalosis</b>, <b>acute pancreatitis</b>, <b>high phosphorus</b>, immobility, and <b>removal or damage of the parathyroids</b> (thyroid or parathyroid surgery, neck irradiation).</div>\n<div><b>Acute vs chronic</b> — <b>acute</b> hypocalcemia brings <b>rapid, life-threatening</b> symptoms; <b>chronic</b> loss comes on slowly, and the nerve and muscle signs may be mild because the body has adjusted. Ask about <b>dairy intake and calcium supplements</b>, recent <b>orthopedic surgery or bone healing</b>, height loss and unexplained bone pain.</div>\n<div><b>Postmenopausal women</b> — lose calcium <b>chronically</b> — less weight-bearing activity and <b>falling estrogen</b> speed osteoporosis. Teach them to keep <b>walking and doing weight-bearing exercise</b> as health allows.</div>\n<div><b>Nerves and muscles</b> — first <b>tingling and numbness</b> in the hands, feet, lips, nose and ears; then <b>twitching, painful cramps and spasms</b>, often in the thigh, calf or foot at rest or during sleep; then <b>tetany</b>. Ask about recent thyroid surgery, neck irradiation or a neck injury.</div>\n<div><b>Trousseau and Chvostek</b> — <b>Trousseau sign</b>: inflate a BP cuff <b>above systolic for 1–4 minutes</b> — the hand and fingers spasm into <b>palmar flexion</b>. <b>Chvostek sign</b>: tap the face <b>just below and in front of the ear</b> — the mouth, nose and cheek on that side <b>twitch</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Heart, gut, bones</b> — heart rate slower or slightly faster, <b>weak thready</b> pulse; severe → <b>hypotension</b> and a <b>prolonged QT</b> (and ST) on the ECG. Gut <b>speeds up</b>: <b>hyperactive</b> bowel sounds, cramping, diarrhea. Chronic loss <b>thins bone</b> (osteoporosis): fragile bones, <b>fractures with little trauma</b>, loss of height and a forward-bent spine.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment and safety</b> — <b>calcium replacement</b> (oral and IV) and <b>vitamin D</b> to absorb it; drugs to calm nerve and muscle response if needed; a <b>calcium-rich diet</b>. <b>Reduce stimulation</b>: a quiet room, few visitors, adjusted lighting, a soft voice. <b>Prevent fractures</b>: move the patient with a <b>lift sheet</b> (never pull), and watch for bumps or dents over bones.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A blood pressure cuff above systolic makes the hand’s nerves <b>briefly short of oxygen</b>, which lowers their firing threshold further; with low calcium they fire on their own and the muscles spasm — that is the Trousseau sign.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hypercalcemia — calcium over 10.5 mg/dL</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Causes (Box 13.9)</b> — actual excess: too much <b>oral calcium or vitamin D</b>, chronic kidney disease, <b>thiazide diuretics</b>. Relative: <b>hyperparathyroidism</b>, <b>cancer</b>, hyperthyroidism, <b>immobility</b>, dehydration.</div>\n<div><b>Speed matters</b> — a <b>mild but rapid</b> rise often causes <b>worse</b> problems than a severe one that developed slowly.</div>\n<div><b>Heart (most serious)</b> — mild: heart rate and BP <b>rise</b>; severe or prolonged: conduction is <b>depressed</b> and the heart <b>slows</b>. ECG: a <b>shortened QT</b> (and T-wave changes). Watch for poor perfusion — cyanosis, pallor.</div>\n<div><b>Clots</b> — high calcium makes blood <b>clot more easily</b> where flow is slow — the lower legs, pelvis, and constricted or obstructed veins. Measure <b>calf circumference</b> and check the feet’s temperature, color and capillary refill.</div>\n<div><b>Muscles, brain, gut</b> — <b>severe muscle weakness</b>, <b>reduced reflexes</b>, <b>no paresthesias</b>; <b>confusion and lethargy</b>. The gut <b>slows</b>: <b>constipation</b>, anorexia, nausea, vomiting, distention, pain; hypoactive or absent bowel sounds — measure <b>abdominal girth</b> at the umbilicus.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — <b>stop the calcium</b>: calcium-containing IV fluids (<b>lactated Ringer’s</b>), calcium antacids, vitamin D. Give <b>IV 0.9% saline</b> (sodium makes the kidneys <b>excrete calcium</b>). Swap <b>thiazides for furosemide</b>. Drugs that keep calcium in bone: <b>phosphorus, calcitonin, bisphosphonates</b> (etidronate), and prostaglandin inhibitors (aspirin, NSAIDs). <b>Dialysis</b> if needed; <b>cardiac monitoring</b> (T waves, QT, rate and rhythm).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Thiazides and loop diuretics act on calcium in <b>opposite</b> ways: thiazides make the kidneys <b>keep</b> calcium, loop diuretics make them <b>lose</b> it — so a patient with high calcium is switched from one to the other.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes","ch":"13","chTitle":"Fluid and electrolyte balance and imbalance","title":"Magnesium: hypomagnesemia and hypermagnesemia","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hypomagnesemia — magnesium under 1.3 mEq/L</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What magnesium does</b> — stored mostly in <b>bone and cartilage</b>, and mostly <b>inside cells</b>: it is needed for muscle contraction, carbohydrate metabolism, energy stores, vitamin activation, clotting, cell growth and especially <b>heart muscle</b>. Its level is tied to <b>potassium and calcium</b> — when magnesium is low, they usually are too.</div>\n<div><b>Causes (Box 13.10)</b> — mainly <b>poor intake</b> and <b>loop or thiazide diuretics</b>; also malnutrition, starvation, diarrhea, steatorrhea, <b>acute pancreatitis</b>, celiac and Crohn’s disease, uncontrolled diabetes, <b>alcohol use disorder</b>, and drugs — <b>proton pump inhibitors</b>, diuretics, <b>aminoglycosides</b>, cisplatin, amphotericin B.</div>\n<div><b>Heart</b> — raises the risk of hypertension, atherosclerosis and a thick left ventricle, and causes <b>dysrhythmias</b> — premature beats, <b>atrial fibrillation, ventricular fibrillation, long QT</b> (with prolonged PR and QRS and ectopic beats). Low magnesium also means more heart damage after an MI.</div>\n<div><b>Nerves and muscles</b> — magnesium normally <b>damps</b> signals at synapses; without it, nerves fire easily: <b>hyperactive deep tendon reflexes</b>, numbness, tingling, painful contractions, <b>positive Chvostek and Trousseau</b> (low calcium often comes along), <b>tetany and seizures</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Gut</b> — <b>slows</b>: poor appetite, nausea, <b>constipation</b>, distention; severe → <b>paralytic ileus</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — stop drugs that waste magnesium (loop and osmotic diuretics, aminoglycosides, phosphorus-containing drugs). For <b>severe</b> loss, <b>IV magnesium sulfate</b> — check <b>deep tendon reflexes at least hourly</b> to see it working and to catch too much. Correct low calcium too.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Low magnesium makes low potassium <b>hard to correct</b>: magnesium is needed to keep potassium inside cells and to stop the kidneys losing it. When potassium keeps falling despite replacement, the magnesium level is the next thing to check.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hypermagnesemia — magnesium over 2.1 mEq/L</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Causes</b> — <b>rare</b> unless magnesium is being given or the kidneys fail: <b>magnesium-containing antacids and laxatives</b>, <b>IV magnesium replacement</b>, <b>kidney disease</b>. Symptoms usually appear only <b>above 4 mEq/L</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Heart</b> — <b>bradycardia</b>, vasodilation and <b>hypotension</b> (a low diastolic pressure); ECG: <b>prolonged PR, wide QRS, longer QT</b>. Severe cases risk <b>cardiac arrest</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Brain, muscles, breathing</b> — <b>drowsy, lethargic</b>, then <b>coma</b>; deep tendon reflexes <b>reduced or absent</b>; muscle contractions <b>weaken and finally stop</b>. It doesn’t affect the lungs directly, but <b>weak breathing muscles</b> cause respiratory failure and death.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — <b>stop all magnesium</b> (oral and IV); if the kidneys work, <b>magnesium-free IV fluids</b> and <b>loop diuretics (furosemide)</b>; for severe heart effects, <b>calcium</b> reverses them.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Deep tendon reflexes are the bedside <b>magnesium meter</b>: they fade before breathing and the heart are in danger. That is why reflexes are checked <b>every hour</b> during an IV magnesium infusion.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"13-adpie","ch":"13","chTitle":"Fluid and electrolyte balance and imbalance","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">For every fluid or electrolyte problem the nurse watches the same three things — <b>volume</b> (weight, pulse, BP, neck veins, lungs, urine), <b>excitable tissue</b> (mental status, muscles and reflexes, bowel sounds, the ECG) and <b>safety</b> (falls, breathing, the IV site) — then replaces what is short, removes what is extra, and fixes the cause.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div>intake (all liquids, including ice cream, gelatin and ices) and output; <b>daily weights</b> (weigh, don’t ask); every <b>drug</b> — diuretics, laxatives, corticosteroids, ACE inhibitors and ARBs, potassium supplements, <b>salt substitutes</b>, antacids; kidney, heart, endocrine and GI disease; recent surgery (thyroid, neck); diet (processed foods, potassium-rich foods, dairy); ring or shoe tightness; mental status.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical</div><b>pulse rate and quality</b>, <b>orthostatic BP</b>, pulse pressure, <b>neck and hand veins</b>, breathing rate and depth, <b>lung sounds</b>, dependent <b>edema</b>, skin turgor (sternum in older adults), mucous membranes, temperature, <b>level of consciousness</b>, muscle strength (<b>hand grip</b>), <b>deep tendon reflexes</b>, <b>Trousseau and Chvostek</b>, <b>bowel sounds</b>, urine color and amount, the <b>IV site</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Labs and ECG</div><b>Na 136–145, K 3.5–5.0, Ca 9.0–10.5, Cl 98–106, Mg 1.3–2.1 mEq/L (Ca mg/dL)</b>, <b>osmolarity 270–300</b>; Hgb and Hct (concentrated or diluted), BUN and creatinine, glucose, urine specific gravity; ABGs when breathing is weak; the <b>ECG</b> — <b>peaked T</b> (high K⁺), <b>U wave and ST depression</b> (low K⁺), <b>long QT</b> (low Ca²⁺, low Mg²⁺), <b>short QT</b> (high Ca²⁺).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The same few findings point to very different problems depending on the <b>volume picture</b>: confusion with a bounding pulse suggests diluted sodium, while confusion with a thready pulse and dry mouth suggests dehydration. Always read the electrolytes <b>together with the volume signs</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>dehydration: <b>fluid and electrolyte imbalance</b> from too much loss or too little intake, and <b>potential for injury</b> (falls) from poor perfusion. Overload: <b>excess fluid</b> with risk of <b>pulmonary edema</b> and skin breakdown. Electrolyte imbalances: risk of <b>dysrhythmias</b>, <b>respiratory muscle failure</b>, seizures and injury.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>fluid and electrolyte balance restored: intake at least <b>1500 mL a day</b> (or 500 mL more than urine output), weight stable, BP near the usual, moist mucous membranes, <b>electrolytes in range with a normal ECG</b>; <b>no falls</b> and no injury; no pulmonary edema; the patient knows the warning signs and the diet and drug changes that prevent a repeat.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Fluid</div>dehydration: <b>oral fluids on a schedule</b> (AP: <b>60–120 mL hourly</b>, never withheld for incontinence), ORS, IV crystalloids at a rate matched to the heart and kidneys, drugs for the cause, <b>reassess every 2 hours</b>. Overload: <b>furosemide</b> (or conivaptan/tolvaptan), <b>fluid and sodium limits</b>, <b>turn every 2 hours</b>, pressure overlay, <b>exact I&amp;O and daily weights</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Electrolytes</div><b>potassium</b>: oral with meals; IV <b>pharmacy-diluted, 5–10 mEq/hr, never push</b>, IV site hourly; for high K⁺ stop infusions, <b>cardiac monitor</b>, insulin with glucose, binders, diuretics or dialysis. <b>Sodium</b>: saline, <b>3% on a controller</b>, water-removing drugs, diet. <b>Calcium</b>: replace with vitamin D, quiet room, lift sheet — or stop calcium, give saline and furosemide. <b>Magnesium</b>: IV magnesium sulfate with <b>hourly reflexes</b> — or stop magnesium.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Safety and teaching</div><b>fall precautions</b> and a gait belt for weakness or orthostasis; breathing checks when muscles are weak; <b>reduce stimulation</b> in low calcium. Teach: drink more with heat, exercise and fever; avoid caffeine and alcohol for rehydration; <b>daily weights</b> at home (call for <b>&gt;2 lb a day or &gt;3 lb a week</b>); read <b>sodium and potassium on labels</b>; no salt substitutes with high potassium; work with the dietitian.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>weight back to baseline; <b>pulse full and steady</b>, BP at or near the usual with <b>no orthostatic drop</b>; <b>neck veins full when lying flat</b> (and flat when upright); clear lungs, no new edema; urine pale with steady output; moist mouth; <b>alert and oriented</b>; strength and reflexes normal; <b>electrolytes in range and the ECG back to baseline</b>; no falls; the patient can state the warning signs and when to call.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"14-overview","ch":"14","chTitle":"Acid–base balance and imbalance","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">14 · Chapter 14</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Acid–base balance · gas exchange</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Acid–base balance and imbalance</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> Metabolism makes acid all day long, yet arterial blood stays between <b>pH 7.35 and 7.45</b> — free <b>hydrogen ions (H⁺)</b> are the most tightly controlled substance in the body. Three defenses hold the line: <b>buffers</b> (seconds), the <b>lungs</b>, which breathe out <b>CO₂</b> (minutes, but limited), and the <b>kidneys</b>, which keep or dump <b>bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻)</b> and H⁺ (hours to days, but strongest). The exemplar is <b>acidosis</b> — <b>metabolic</b> (too much acid or too little bicarbonate: DKA, diarrhea, kidney failure, lactic acid) or <b>respiratory</b> (CO₂ trapped by weak or blocked breathing). Acidosis <b>depresses</b> nerves, muscles and the heart and pushes <b>potassium up</b>, so the nurse checks the <b>heart first</b>; <b>alkalosis</b> <b>overexcites</b> them through low calcium and low potassium. The <b>arterial blood gas</b> tells which problem it is.</p><div id=\"ch14-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Arterial blood is kept slightly alkaline at <b>pH 7.35–7.45</b> while metabolism keeps making acid — <b>CO₂</b> from carbohydrate, <b>sulfuric acid</b> from protein, <b>fatty acids and ketoacids</b> from fat, <b>lactic acid</b> when cells lack oxygen. The <b>bicarbonate buffer</b> holds <b>1 part carbonic acid to 20 parts bicarbonate</b>; the <b>lungs</b> set the CO₂ and the <b>kidneys</b> set the bicarbonate, so pH reflects <b>kidneys (HCO₃⁻) over lungs (CO₂)</b>.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Metabolic acidosis</b>: acid made faster than it’s cleared (<b>DKA, starvation, lactic acid</b> from hypoxia, seizures or heavy exercise, <b>alcohol, methanol or aspirin</b> poisoning), acid not excreted (<b>kidney failure</b>), or bicarbonate made too little (kidney, liver or pancreas failure, dehydration) or lost (<b>diarrhea</b>). <b>Respiratory acidosis</b> has one cause — <b>CO₂ retained</b> by depressed, weak, blocked or diseased breathing. <b>Alkalosis</b> comes from base gained or acid lost (antacids, vomiting, NG suction, diuretics) or <b>CO₂ blown off</b> by hyperventilation.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Acidosis <b>slows everything</b>: lethargy → confusion → coma, <b>weak muscles and low reflexes</b>, a fast heart early, then <b>bradycardia, heart block, tall T waves and wide QRS</b>, low BP and thready pulses. <b>Metabolic</b>: deep, fast <b>Kussmaul</b> breathing and <b>warm, flushed, dry</b> skin. <b>Respiratory</b>: shallow, ineffective breathing and <b>pale to cyanotic</b> skin. Alkalosis <b>excites</b>: anxiety, tingling, <b>Chvostek and Trousseau</b>, cramps, hyperreflexia, <b>tetany, seizures</b>, a fast heart — with weak muscles.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">The <b>arterial blood gas</b>: <b>pH</b> (&lt;7.35 acidosis, &gt;7.45 alkalosis), <b>PaCO₂ 35–45</b> (the lung value), <b>HCO₃⁻ 21–28</b> (the kidney value), <b>PaO₂ 80–100</b>. Metabolic acidosis: <b>HCO₃⁻ low</b> (15–20 or less), PaO₂ normal, PaCO₂ normal or low. Respiratory acidosis: <b>PaCO₂ over 50</b> (even over 100), <b>PaO₂ low</b>, HCO₃⁻ normal at first and <b>high when chronic</b>. <b>Potassium is often high</b> in acidosis and <b>low</b> in alkalosis.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Treat the cause</b> — acidosis and alkalosis are signs, not diseases. Metabolic acidosis: <b>fluids</b>, <b>insulin</b> for DKA, antidiarrheals, and <b>bicarbonate only when HCO₃⁻ is low and pH is under 7.2</b>. Respiratory acidosis: <b>bronchodilators, anti-inflammatories, mucolytics</b>, the <b>lowest oxygen</b> that prevents hypoxemia, positioning and breathing work, and <b>mechanical ventilation</b> if SpO₂ can’t stay at 90%. Alkalosis: stop the cause, replace fluid and electrolytes. <b>Fall precautions</b> for all.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A pH <b>below 6.9 or above 7.8</b> is usually fatal. Acidosis with hyperkalemia → <b>heart block and cardiac arrest</b>; CNS depression → <b>coma</b>; tiring respiratory muscles → <b>respiratory failure</b>; <b>combined metabolic and respiratory acidosis</b> is worse than either alone. Alkalosis → <b>tetany, seizures</b>, dysrhythmias (and <b>digoxin toxicity</b> with low potassium) and <b>weak breathing</b>.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">In a patient with or at risk for acidosis: <b>slowing heart rate, tall T waves, a widening QRS</b> or <b>falling grip strength</b> (rising potassium) — assess pulse and BP and report <b>immediately</b>; a <b>rising PaCO₂ with falling PaO₂</b>, <b>SpO₂ that can’t stay at 90%</b>, tiring breathing muscles or a falling level of consciousness — airway first, then call for help with ventilation. In alkalosis: <b>tetany, seizures</b> or weakening breathing.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Breathing can’t clear CO₂ (opioids, COPD, rib fractures)<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>CO₂ ↑ → carbonic acid ↑ → <b>H⁺ ↑, pH ↓</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>H⁺ moves into cells and <b>K⁺ moves out</b> → hyperkalemia<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>nerves and muscles <b>depressed</b>: lethargy, weakness, ↓ reflexes<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>heart slows: <b>tall T, wide QRS, heart block</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>untreated → <b>respiratory failure and cardiac arrest</b></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Metabolic vs respiratory acidosis</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Metabolic</b>: the problem is <b>bicarbonate or metabolic acid</b> — HCO₃⁻ <b>low</b>, PaO₂ <b>normal</b>, PaCO₂ normal or <b>low</b> (the lungs compensating); <b>Kussmaul</b> breathing; <b>warm, flushed, dry</b> skin; DKA, diarrhea, kidney failure, lactic acid. <b>Respiratory</b>: the problem is <b>trapped CO₂</b> — PaCO₂ <b>high</b>, PaO₂ <b>low</b>, HCO₃⁻ normal (acute) or <b>high</b> (chronic, kidneys compensating); <b>shallow, ineffective</b> breathing; <b>pale to cyanotic</b> skin; opioids, COPD, airway blockage, chest injury. The symptoms overlap; <b>the gases decide</b> — and the treatments differ.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Acidosis vs alkalosis: depressed vs excited</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Acidosis</b> makes excitable tissue <b>less</b> responsive — lethargy to coma, low reflexes, <b>flaccid</b> weakness, slowing heart — mostly through <b>high potassium</b>. <b>Alkalosis</b> makes it <b>overrespond</b> — anxiety, tingling around the mouth and toes, <b>Chvostek and Trousseau</b>, hyperreflexia, cramps, <b>tetany, seizures</b>, fast heart — through <b>low calcium and low potassium</b>. Both can leave the muscles <b>weak</b>: acidosis from too much potassium, alkalosis from too little.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Uncompensated, partly or fully compensated</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Uncompensated</b>: pH abnormal and only the <b>cause</b> value is off. <b>Partly compensated</b>: pH still abnormal, but the <b>other</b> value has started moving. <b>Fully compensated</b>: pH back in range (usually on the side of the original problem) with <b>both</b> values abnormal. In compensation <b>PaCO₂ and HCO₃⁻ move in the same direction</b>; when they move in <b>opposite</b> directions and the pH is far off, two problems are adding together (<b>combined acidosis</b>, such as DKA plus COPD). The lungs compensate within <b>minutes</b>; the kidneys need <b>24–48 hours</b> or longer.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why acidosis raises potassium</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">To buffer extra acid, <b>H⁺ moves into cells</b>; to keep the charges even, <b>K⁺ moves out</b> into the blood — so acidosis brings <b>hyperkalemia</b>, the reason acidosis can <b>stop the heart</b> and the reason the nurse <b>monitors potassium</b> in every patient with acidosis. <b>Alkalosis</b> does the reverse: H⁺ leaves the cells, K⁺ moves in → <b>hypokalemia</b> (and higher <b>digoxin toxicity</b> risk). In <b>chronic</b> respiratory acidosis the compensating kidneys can leave potassium normal or low.</div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>ABGs</b> (pH, PaCO₂, HCO₃⁻, PaO₂) to judge each treatment, <b>serum potassium</b> in any patient with acidosis (calcium and potassium in alkalosis), the <b>cardiac monitor</b> (rate, T waves, QRS, PR), <b>pulse and BP</b>, breathing <b>rate, depth and ease</b> — can the patient <b>finish a sentence</b> — <b>SpO₂</b>, breath sounds, retractions and <b>accessory (neck) muscle use</b>, an audible wheeze or grunt, nail beds and mouth for <b>cyanosis</b> (a late sign), <b>mental status</b> against baseline at least <b>every 2 hours</b>, <b>hand-grip and leg strength</b>, deep tendon reflexes, <b>Chvostek and Trousseau</b> in alkalosis, skin color and warmth, intake, output and skin turgor; breathing status <b>hourly</b> in respiratory acidosis.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Slowing heart rate, tall T waves, a widening QRS or heart block</b> in acidosis — report at once (cardiac arrest from hyperkalemia); <b>grip or leg strength falling</b> in acidosis — check <b>pulse and BP</b> first, then report; <b>airway</b> of anyone with <b>acute respiratory acidosis</b>; <b>PaCO₂ rising and PaO₂ falling</b>, <b>SpO₂ that can’t stay at 90%</b>, breathing muscles tiring or the patient growing harder to rouse — call for help (ventilation support); <b>pH under 7.2</b>; in alkalosis — <b>tetany, seizures</b> or <b>weakening breathing</b>, and signs of <b>digoxin toxicity</b> with low potassium.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>pH is a hydrogen-ion scale run backward</b> — pH measures the <b>free hydrogen ions</b> in a fluid, on a logarithmic scale that runs <b>opposite</b> to their amount: the <b>lower the pH, the more H⁺</b> (more acid). The scale runs from 1 (most acid) to 14 (most alkaline), with 7 neutral, and <b>one pH unit is a tenfold change</b> — so a drop from 7.4 to 7.3 is a large rise in acid. Normal arterial blood is <b>7.35–7.45</b>; below <b>6.9</b> or above <b>7.8</b> is usually fatal. When the balance is off, <b>hormones, enzymes and many drugs</b> work poorly, <b>fluid and electrolyte</b> balance shifts, and the <b>heart, nerves, muscles and gut</b> become under- or overactive.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Acids, bases and buffers</b> — <b>Acids</b> release H⁺ (a strong acid such as hydrochloric acid lets go of all of it; a weak acid only some); <b>bases</b> bind H⁺ — the main one is <b>bicarbonate</b>. <b>Buffers</b> work either way, like <b>sponges</b>: soaking up H⁺ when there’s too much and releasing it when there’s too little. <b>Bicarbonate</b> buffers the ECF; <b>phosphate</b> works inside cells; <b>proteins</b> (albumin, globulins and <b>hemoglobin</b> in red cells) buffer too. Health keeps <b>1 carbonic acid to 20 bicarbonate</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>CO₂ is acid you can breathe out</b> — with the enzyme <b>carbonic anhydrase</b>, <b>CO₂ + water ⇌ carbonic acid ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻</b> — so <b>more CO₂ means more H⁺</b> (pH falls) and less CO₂ means less (pH rises). <b>Chemoreceptors in the brain</b> sense the CO₂ and change the <b>rate and depth</b> of breathing within <b>minutes</b>: faster and deeper to blow off acid, slower and shallower to keep it. Room air holds almost no CO₂, so it keeps diffusing out even when breathing is somewhat weak.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The kidneys: slow but strongest</b> — the kidneys <b>reabsorb or make bicarbonate</b> when acid is high (and let it go in the urine when acid is low), and <b>excrete H⁺</b> by binding it to <b>phosphate</b> (forming an acid in the urine) and to <b>ammonia</b> (forming <b>ammonium</b>). They need <b>24–48 hours</b> to respond fully but are the <b>most powerful</b> and longest-lasting defense. Bicarbonate is made in the <b>kidneys, pancreas and red cells</b> — and <b>no disease makes too much of it</b>, which is why acidosis is far more common than alkalosis.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The three defenses act in order of speed: <b>buffers</b> in seconds, the <b>lungs</b> in minutes (sensitive but easily overwhelmed), the <b>kidneys</b> over hours to days (slow but powerful). Each organ can cover for the other: when <b>metabolism</b> is the problem, the <b>lungs</b> compensate fast; when the <b>lungs</b> are the problem, the <b>kidneys</b> compensate slowly — which is why chronic lung disease shows a <b>high bicarbonate</b>.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","ch":"14","chTitle":"Acid–base balance and imbalance","title":"Reading an arterial blood gas","kind":"topic","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What each value measures (Table 14.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>pH 7.35–7.45</b> — the <b>overall acid level</b> of arterial blood. <b>Below 7.35 = acidosis</b>; <b>above 7.45 = alkalosis</b>. It says how sick the balance is, <b>not why</b> — the other values name the cause.</div>\n<div><b>PaCO₂ 35–45 mm Hg — the lungs</b> — the carbon dioxide in arterial blood, set by <b>breathing</b>. CO₂ acts as an <b>acid</b>: <b>high</b> (over 45) pushes toward <b>acidosis</b> (hypoventilation, lung disease); <b>low</b> (under 35) toward <b>alkalosis</b> (hyperventilation).</div>\n<div><b>HCO₃⁻ 21–28 mEq/L — the kidneys</b> — bicarbonate, set by the <b>kidneys</b> (and metabolism). It is a <b>base</b>: <b>low</b> (under 21) means <b>metabolic acidosis</b>; <b>high</b> (over 28) means <b>metabolic alkalosis</b> — or the kidneys compensating for a long-standing lung problem.</div>\n<div><b>PaO₂ 80–100 mm Hg and lactate</b> — <b>PaO₂</b> shows <b>oxygenation</b>, not acid–base: it is <b>normal in metabolic acidosis</b> and <b>low in respiratory acidosis</b>, and usually falls <b>before</b> the PaCO₂ rises because CO₂ crosses the alveoli more easily than oxygen. PaO₂ also falls with respiratory depression, high altitude, carbon monoxide poisoning and low cardiac output, and rises with oxygen therapy. <b>Lactate</b> (arterial <b>3–7 mg/dL</b>, 0.3–0.8 mmol/L; venous 5–20 mg/dL, 0.6–2.2 mmol/L) climbs when cells work <b>without oxygen</b> — hypoxia, sepsis, shock — and with exercise, an insulin infusion, alcohol use, pregnancy and dehydration; it falls with fluid overload.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Think of pH as the <b>result</b>, PaCO₂ as the <b>lungs’ vote</b> and HCO₃⁻ as the <b>kidneys’ vote</b>. Whichever vote agrees with the pH is the <b>cause</b>; the other one, if it has moved the opposite way from normal, is <b>compensating</b>.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Interpret in order</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>1 · Look at the pH</b> — acidosis (<b>&lt;7.35</b>), alkalosis (<b>&gt;7.45</b>) or in range. If it’s in range but both other values are abnormal, note which side of <b>7.40</b> it leans toward — that side is usually the original problem.</div>\n<div><b>2 · Look at the PaCO₂</b> — is it moving <b>opposite</b> to the pH (pH down, CO₂ up — or pH up, CO₂ down)? Then the problem is <b>respiratory</b>. A common memory aid: <b>ROME — Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal</b>.</div>\n<div><b>3 · Look at the HCO₃⁻</b> — is it moving the <b>same way</b> as the pH (both down, or both up)? Then the problem is <b>metabolic</b>.</div>\n<div><b>4 · Check for compensation</b> — if the <b>other</b> value has moved away from normal in the direction that pulls the pH back, the body is <b>compensating</b> — <b>partly</b> if the pH is still out of range, <b>fully</b> if it’s back in range. If the two values pull the <b>same</b> way on the pH (CO₂ up <b>and</b> HCO₃⁻ down), it’s a <b>combined</b> problem.</div>\n<div><b>5 · Look at the oxygen and the patient</b> — <b>PaO₂</b> and SpO₂ for oxygenation; then match the numbers to the <b>person</b> — breathing pattern, mental status, heart rhythm, potassium and the likely cause.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Working in a fixed order stops the most common mistake — seeing one abnormal number and naming the problem before checking whether that number is the <b>cause</b> or the body’s <b>compensation</b>.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Practice gases</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>pH 7.30 · PaCO₂ 58 · HCO₃⁻ 25</b> — acid pH; CO₂ high (opposite) → <b>respiratory acidosis</b>; HCO₃⁻ still normal → <b>uncompensated</b> (acute) — for example, slowed breathing after opioids.</div>\n<div><b>pH 7.36 · PaCO₂ 62 · HCO₃⁻ 34</b> — pH in range but on the <b>acid</b> side; CO₂ high → respiratory cause; HCO₃⁻ high → the kidneys have caught up → <b>fully compensated respiratory acidosis</b> — typical of long-standing <b>COPD</b>.</div>\n<div><b>pH 7.28 · PaCO₂ 28 · HCO₃⁻ 13</b> — acid pH; HCO₃⁻ low (same way) → <b>metabolic acidosis</b>; CO₂ low as the lungs blow off acid, but pH still low → <b>partly compensated</b> — for example, <b>DKA</b> with Kussmaul breathing.</div>\n<div><b>pH 7.50 · PaCO₂ 29 · HCO₃⁻ 23</b> — alkaline pH; CO₂ low (opposite) → <b>respiratory alkalosis</b>; HCO₃⁻ normal → <b>uncompensated</b> — for example, <b>hyperventilating</b> from panic or pain.</div>\n<div><b>pH 7.52 · PaCO₂ 44 · HCO₃⁻ 35</b> — alkaline pH; HCO₃⁻ high (same way) → <b>metabolic alkalosis</b>; CO₂ normal → <b>uncompensated</b> — for example, prolonged <b>vomiting or NG suction</b>.</div>\n<div><b>pH 7.10 · PaCO₂ 60 · HCO₃⁻ 15</b> — very acid pH; CO₂ <b>high</b> and HCO₃⁻ <b>low</b> — both pulling toward acid → <b>combined respiratory and metabolic acidosis</b>, more dangerous than either alone (for example, <b>DKA in a patient with COPD</b>).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every gas answers the same three questions — <b>which way</b> is the pH, <b>who</b> caused it (lungs or metabolism), and <b>is the other organ helping</b>? Practicing on worked examples makes the pattern automatic.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"14-buffers-lungs-kidneys-and-compensation","ch":"14","chTitle":"Acid–base balance and imbalance","title":"Buffers, lungs, kidneys and compensation","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Three lines of defense (Table 14.1)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>1 · Buffers — seconds</b> — <b>bicarbonate and phosphate</b> (chemical) and <b>albumin, globulins and hemoglobin</b> (protein) are always present and react <b>at once</b>, binding or releasing H⁺. They handle the <b>small swings</b> of everyday metabolism but are quickly used up by a big acid load.</div>\n<div><b>2 · Lungs — minutes</b> — more H⁺ or CO₂ makes the brain <b>speed up and deepen breathing</b> (hyperventilation) to blow CO₂ off; less makes it <b>slow and shallow</b> (hypoventilation) to keep CO₂. They respond within <b>seconds to minutes</b> but are <b>limited</b> and easily overwhelmed.</div>\n<div><b>3 · Kidneys — hours to days</b> — to lower acid, the kidneys <b>keep and make bicarbonate</b> and <b>excrete H⁺</b> (as phosphate acid and ammonium); to raise it, they let bicarbonate go and keep H⁺. They are the <b>strongest</b> regulator and last longest, but take <b>24–48 hours</b> to respond fully.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Speed and strength trade off: the fastest defense is the weakest and the strongest is the slowest. That is why an <b>acute</b> lung problem shows an uncompensated gas, while a <b>chronic</b> one shows the kidneys’ work as a high bicarbonate.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Compensation</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Lungs cover for metabolic problems</b> — when metabolic acid builds up (lactic acid after hard running, ketoacids in DKA), breathing becomes <b>faster and deeper</b> to blow off CO₂ and raise the pH — the deep, rapid <b>Kussmaul</b> pattern is this compensation.</div>\n<div><b>Kidneys cover for lung problems</b> — in <b>COPD</b> the lungs keep retaining CO₂, so the kidneys <b>excrete more H⁺ and keep more bicarbonate</b>, holding the pH in or near range. The gas then shows a <b>high PaCO₂ with a high HCO₃⁻</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Full, partial — or not at all</b> — <b>fully compensated</b>: the pH is back in range though the other values stay abnormal; <b>partly compensated</b>: the problem is too big and the pH is still off, but compensation keeps it from getting worse. Compensation <b>never overshoots</b> — the pH doesn’t swing past normal to the other side.</div>\n<div><b>Combined (mixed) acidosis</b> — metabolic and respiratory acidosis can happen <b>together</b> — for example, <b>DKA in a patient with COPD</b>: neither organ can cover for the other, and the acidosis is <b>worse than either alone</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Compensation buys time but doesn’t fix anything — the <b>cause</b> still has to be treated. A compensated gas in a chronically ill patient is that person’s <b>baseline</b>, which is why trends and the admission gas matter more than one result.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","ch":"14","chTitle":"Acid–base balance and imbalance","title":"Acidosis — the concept exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Metabolic acidosis — four ways in (Table 14.3)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Too much acid made</b> — <b>fat broken down too fast</b> (<b>diabetic ketoacidosis</b>, starvation); <b>hypermetabolism</b> — heavy exercise, <b>seizures</b>, fever; <b>lactic acid</b> when cells lack oxygen (hypoxia, ischemia, shock, sepsis); <b>acids swallowed</b> — ethanol or methanol intoxication, <b>aspirin (salicylate)</b> overdose.</div>\n<div><b>Too little acid excreted</b> — <b>kidney failure</b> — acid is made at the normal rate but the kidneys can’t get rid of it.</div>\n<div><b>Too little bicarbonate made</b> — bicarbonate comes from the <b>kidneys and pancreas</b>, so <b>kidney failure, pancreatitis, liver failure</b> — and <b>dehydration</b> — leave too little base to balance normal acid (a <b>base deficit</b>).</div>\n<div><b>Too much bicarbonate lost</b> — <b>diarrhea</b> carries bicarbonate out of the gut — the classic base-loss acidosis.</div>\n<div><b>Actual vs relative</b> — <b>actual</b> acidosis: there is truly <b>more acid</b> (made or kept). <b>Relative</b> acidosis: the acid is normal but there is <b>too little base</b>, so the balance tips toward acid.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The four routes come down to two sides of one scale — <b>acid in versus acid out</b>, and <b>base made versus base lost</b> — so for any patient the nurse asks: is something making acid, keeping acid, or using up or losing bicarbonate?</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Respiratory acidosis — one cause: trapped CO₂</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Depressed breathing</b> — the brainstem’s drive to breathe is blunted — <b>anesthetics, opioids</b> and other sedating drugs, electrolyte imbalances, <b>head injury or raised intracranial pressure</b>.</div>\n<div><b>The chest can’t expand</b> — <b>respiratory muscle weakness</b>, rib or spine injury and chest deformities inside; <b>casts, tight chest scars or obesity</b> from outside.</div>\n<div><b>Air can’t move or gas can’t cross</b> — <b>airway obstruction</b>; or a thick <b>alveolar-capillary barrier</b> — pneumonia, tuberculosis, <b>emphysema</b>, ARDS, chest trauma, <b>pulmonary embolism</b>, pulmonary edema, near-drowning.</div>\n<div><b>A ventilator set too low</b> — if the <b>tidal volume or rate</b> is set too low, the patient can’t clear CO₂.</div>\n<div><b>Who is at risk</b> — anyone with a problem that <b>impairs breathing</b> (the highest risk for acute acidosis), and <b>older adults</b> with heart, kidney or lung disease — often taking <b>diuretics or aspirin</b>. <b>COPD</b> is the most common chronic cause.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every respiratory cause ends in the same place: <b>CO₂ in faster than it goes out</b>. So the fix is never a drug that changes pH — it is <b>better ventilation</b>: open the airway, reverse the sedation, help the chest move, treat the lung.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"14-recognizing-acidosis","ch":"14","chTitle":"Acid–base balance and imbalance","title":"Recognizing acidosis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Body systems (Box 14.1)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Heart — assess first</b> — early: <b>faster rate and cardiac output</b>. Worse acidosis (and the <b>hyperkalemia</b> that comes with it): <b>bradycardia → heart block</b>, <b>tall T waves</b>, <b>wide QRS</b>, <b>long PR</b>, low BP from vasodilation, <b>thready</b> pulses that are hard to find. Assess the heart <b>first</b> — cardiac arrest is the danger.</div>\n<div><b>Brain</b> — <b>depressed</b>: lethargy → confusion (especially in older adults) → stupor → coma. <b>Cognitive change may be the first sign</b>: check orientation, ask the patient to <b>count backward by threes</b>, and ask the family whether the behavior is usual.</div>\n<div><b>Muscles</b> — <b>low reflexes</b>, <b>bilateral weakness</b> that can progress to <b>flaccid paralysis</b> (from hyperkalemia). Test the arms with a <b>hand squeeze</b> and the legs by having the patient <b>push both feet against a board</b> against resistance.</div>\n<div><b>Breathing and skin</b> — <b>metabolic</b>: deep, rapid, involuntary <b>Kussmaul</b> breathing (compensation) and <b>warm, flushed, dry</b> skin from vasodilation. <b>Respiratory</b>: breathing is the problem — usually <b>shallow and rapid</b> and ineffective, worse with weak muscles — and the skin is <b>pale to cyanotic</b>. Check SpO₂.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Acidosis looks similar whatever its cause because <b>excess H⁺ disturbs potassium</b>, and potassium sets how every excitable cell fires. Breathing and skin are the clues to the type: the patient who <b>breathes hard and looks flushed</b> is compensating for a metabolic problem; the one who <b>can’t breathe well and looks dusky</b> has the respiratory kind.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">History and the older adult</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Ask about</b> — breathing problems, <b>kidney failure, diabetes, diarrhea, pancreatitis, fever</b>; every drug including over-the-counter and herbal (<b>diuretics, aspirin, antacids</b>); what the patient <b>drank in the last 24 hours</b> and any change in urine; headache, drowsiness, short attention span, poor appetite, nausea, <b>weakness and fatigue</b> over the past day. If the patient is confused, ask the family.</div>\n<div><b>Assessment points for older adults</b> — compare <b>mental status with baseline</b>; check breathing <b>rate and depth</b> and whether the patient can <b>finish a sentence</b> without stopping for breath; look at <b>nail beds and mucous membranes</b>; check turgor over the <b>sternum and forehead</b>; measure the <b>pulse rate and quality</b>; keep watching symptoms and labs during correction.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Older adults have less reserve in the <b>lungs and kidneys</b> — the two organs that compensate — and more of the diseases and drugs that cause imbalance, so a small trigger tips them over sooner and the first clue is often a <b>change in thinking</b>.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The gases and potassium (Boxes 14.2 and 14.3)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Metabolic acidosis</b> — <b>pH under 7.35</b>; <b>HCO₃⁻ low</b> (15–20 or less) — lost or not made (in DKA it is used up buffering ketoacids); <b>PaO₂ normal</b> (oxygen intake isn’t impaired); <b>PaCO₂ normal or low</b> (the lungs compensating). A low pH with a <b>normal PaO₂ and normal or low PaCO₂</b> is the classic metabolic picture.</div>\n<div><b>Respiratory acidosis</b> — <b>pH under 7.35</b>; <b>PaCO₂ over 50</b> (can pass 100); <b>PaO₂ low</b> (under 90) — the hallmark is a <b>falling PaO₂ with a rising PaCO₂</b>, with the oxygen usually dropping first; <b>HCO₃⁻ normal</b> when it’s acute, <b>high</b> once the kidneys have compensated for 24 hours or more.</div>\n<div><b>Potassium</b> — often <b>high</b> in acidosis — H⁺ enters cells and K⁺ leaves to balance the charge. <b>High in acute respiratory acidosis</b>; <b>normal or low</b> in chronic respiratory acidosis once the kidneys compensate. Monitor potassium in <b>every</b> patient with acidosis.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The pH confirms acidosis but <b>can’t tell the type</b>, and metabolic and respiratory acidosis feel alike to the patient — yet they need <b>opposite kinds of help</b> (fluids, insulin or bicarbonate versus ventilation). The ABG and electrolytes make that call.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"14-caring-for-acidosis","ch":"14","chTitle":"Acid–base balance and imbalance","title":"Caring for acidosis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Metabolic acidosis (Table 14.4)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Treat the cause</b> — <b>hydration</b>, plus the fix for the cause: <b>insulin</b> for DKA (stops ketone production), <b>rehydration and antidiarrheals</b> for prolonged diarrhea.</div>\n<div><b>Bicarbonate — only sometimes</b> — IV sodium bicarbonate is given when the <b>HCO₃⁻ is low and the pH is under 7.2</b>, when the kidneys <b>can’t compensate</b>, or for <b>rhabdomyolysis</b> that doesn’t respond to IV fluids.</div>\n<div><b>Monitor continuously</b> — the <b>heart</b> (rate, rhythm, T waves, QRS) and <b>skeletal muscles</b> (grip, leg strength, reflexes) are the most sensitive to acidosis; follow <b>ABGs and potassium</b> as treatment works.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Giving bicarbonate treats the number, not the problem — and in DKA, <b>insulin and fluids</b> clear the ketoacids themselves. Bicarbonate is kept for <b>severe</b> acidosis (pH under 7.2) or when the body can’t make its own.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Respiratory acidosis — improving gas exchange</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Priority problem</b> — <b>reduced gas exchange</b> from the underlying lung disease. The goal is <b>fewer signs of acidosis</b> — and, in a chronic disease such as COPD, the best balance this patient can reach.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs and oxygen</b> — drugs aim at <b>ventilation, not pH</b>: <b>bronchodilators, anti-inflammatories and mucolytics</b>. Give <b>oxygen</b> at the <b>lowest flow that prevents hypoxemia</b>, watching the saturation, to avoid oxygen-induced harm. Add <b>pulmonary hygiene</b>: positioning and breathing techniques.</div>\n<div><b>Ventilation support</b> — <b>mechanical ventilation</b> when the patient <b>can’t keep SpO₂ at 90%</b> or the breathing muscles are <b>tiring</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Hourly breathing checks</b> — breath sounds and how easily air moves; <b>retractions</b>; use of <b>accessory muscles</b> (especially the neck’s sternocleidomastoids); a <b>grunt or wheeze heard without a stethoscope</b>; <b>cyanosis</b> of nail beds and mouth — a <b>late</b> sign. Keep the <b>airway</b> clear.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Respiratory acidosis is fixed by moving air, so the nurse’s most powerful tool is <b>frequent reassessment</b>: work of breathing changes before the gas does, and catching tiring muscles early can prevent intubation in a crisis.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Safety and evaluation</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Prevent falls</b> — hypotension, poor perfusion and slowed thinking put patients with acidosis — and any acid–base problem — at <b>risk for falls</b>: fall precautions, call light in reach, help getting up.</div>\n<div><b>Neuro checks</b> — check neurologic status at least <b>every 2 hours</b> while an acid–base problem is being treated.</div>\n<div><b>Signs it is working</b> — adequate gas exchange; <b>pH above 7.2 and moving toward 7.35</b>; <b>PaO₂ above 90</b> or at least <b>10 mm Hg higher</b> than on admission; <b>PaCO₂ below 45</b> or at least <b>15 mm Hg lower</b> than on admission. Some patients with chronic disease won’t return fully to normal.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>For a patient with chronic lung disease, “normal” may never come back — so success is measured against <b>their own admission values</b> and their breathing comfort, not the textbook range.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"14-alkalosis","ch":"14","chTitle":"Acid–base balance and imbalance","title":"Alkalosis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Causes (Table 14.5)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Metabolic — base gained</b> — <b>bicarbonate antacids</b> taken in excess; <b>IV sodium bicarbonate</b>; <b>massive blood transfusion</b> (the citrate preservative becomes bicarbonate); <b>total parenteral nutrition</b>. Gas: <b>pH and HCO₃⁻ both high</b>, oxygen and CO₂ normal.</div>\n<div><b>Metabolic — acid lost</b> — <b>prolonged vomiting</b> and <b>NG suction</b> (stomach acid removed); <b>excess cortisol or aldosterone</b>; <b>loop and thiazide diuretics</b> at higher doses.</div>\n<div><b>Respiratory — CO₂ blown off</b> — <b>hyperventilation</b> from <b>anxiety, fear</b> or pain; a <b>ventilator</b> set too fast or deep; direct stimulation of the breathing center by <b>fever, brain lesions or salicylates</b>; <b>high altitude</b>; the early stage of acute lung problems. Gas: <b>pH high with PaCO₂ low</b>, oxygen and bicarbonate usually normal.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Alkalosis is less common than acidosis because no disease makes too much bicarbonate — it usually comes from <b>something the patient takes or loses</b>, which means the nurse can often <b>find and stop the cause</b> (an NG tube, an antacid habit, a diuretic, panic breathing).</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What the nurse finds (Box 14.4)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Overexcited nerves</b> — from the <b>low calcium</b> that comes with alkalosis: dizziness, <b>anxiety, irritability, agitation</b>, confusion, <b>numbness and tingling around the mouth and in the toes</b>, positive <b>Chvostek and Trousseau</b> signs, and <b>seizures</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Muscles: twitchy but weak</b> — <b>hyperactive reflexes</b>, cramps, twitching, “charley horses” and <b>tetany</b> — painful, sustained contractions that mean the condition is <b>worsening fast</b>. At the same time <b>low potassium</b> makes the muscles <b>weak</b>: grip fades, the patient may not be able to stand or walk, and <b>breathing effort weakens</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Heart and breathing</b> — <b>faster heart rate</b>, thready pulse, normal or <b>low BP</b> (severe if volume is also low); low potassium raises the risk of <b>digoxin toxicity</b>. Breathing is <b>fast</b> in respiratory alkalosis (usually the cause — each breath is near normal size, but the air moved per minute rises) and <b>weak</b> in metabolic alkalosis.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Alkalosis pulls <b>calcium onto proteins</b> (less free calcium) and <b>potassium into cells</b>, so it looks like <b>hypocalcemia plus hypokalemia</b> at once: nerves fire too easily while muscles lose their strength.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Care (Table 14.4)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Stop the cause</b> — work with the provider to <b>modify or stop</b> what may be causing it — prolonged <b>gastric suction</b>, excess IV solutions, drugs that make the kidneys lose H⁺. <b>Antiemetics</b> for vomiting; for diuretic-caused alkalosis, <b>replace fluid and electrolytes</b> and adjust or stop the diuretic.</div>\n<div><b>Replace and monitor</b> — prevent further loss of <b>hydrogen, potassium, calcium and chloride</b>; give fluids and electrolyte replacement as prescribed; check <b>electrolytes daily</b> until near normal; watch the ECG and the breathing muscles.</div>\n<div><b>Prevent falls</b> — <b>hypotension and muscle weakness</b> make falls the nursing priority during correction — especially in older adults.</div>\n<div><b>Respiratory alkalosis from anxiety</b> — stay with the patient, give <b>calm reassurance</b>, and coach <b>slow breathing</b>; oxygen as needed; watch for the <b>bicarbonate dropping</b> as the kidneys compensate. Your book lists <b>breathing into a paper bag</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Beyond the book</b> — many current references <b>discourage paper-bag rebreathing</b>: it can drop the oxygen level, and fast breathing may be the body’s response to <b>hypoxia</b> — a <b>pulmonary embolism</b>, asthma attack or heart attack — rather than anxiety. Check <b>SpO₂</b> and rule out those causes before calling it panic.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The patient with alkalosis is in danger from <b>injury</b> (falls, tetany, seizures) and <b>electrolyte loss</b> more than from the pH itself, so care centers on <b>stopping the loss</b> and <b>keeping the patient safe</b> while the kidneys and replacements restore balance.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"14-adpie","ch":"14","chTitle":"Acid–base balance and imbalance","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">For any acid–base problem the nurse asks <b>which way</b> (pH), <b>which organ</b> (PaCO₂ = lungs, HCO₃⁻ = metabolism and kidneys) and <b>what it is doing to the patient</b> — the <b>heart</b> and potassium in acidosis, the <b>nerves</b>, calcium and potassium in alkalosis, the <b>breathing</b> in both — then treats the <b>cause</b> and prevents <b>falls</b>.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div>lung disease (<b>COPD</b>), <b>kidney failure, diabetes</b>, diarrhea, vomiting or NG suction, pancreatitis, fever, seizures; recent <b>opioids or sedation</b>; every drug — <b>diuretics, aspirin, antacids</b>, bicarbonate, digoxin; fluids in the last 24 hours and urine changes; drowsiness, headache, weakness, fatigue, tingling or cramps; ask the family if the patient is confused.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical</div><b>heart first</b>: rate, rhythm, <b>T waves and QRS</b> on the monitor, BP, pulse quality; breathing <b>rate, depth, pattern (Kussmaul?)</b> and effort, <b>finishing a sentence</b>, accessory muscles, breath sounds, <b>SpO₂</b>; mental status against <b>baseline</b> (count back by threes); <b>hand grip and leg push</b>, reflexes; <b>Chvostek and Trousseau</b>; skin color and warmth; turgor.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Labs</div><b>ABG</b>: pH 7.35–7.45, <b>PaCO₂ 35–45</b>, <b>HCO₃⁻ 21–28</b>, <b>PaO₂ 80–100</b>; arterial <b>lactate</b> 3–7 mg/dL; <b>potassium</b> (high in acidosis, low in alkalosis), <b>calcium</b>, chloride, glucose and ketones (DKA), BUN and creatinine; the <b>ECG</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The same complaint — weakness, confusion — can come from acidosis or alkalosis, from potassium too high or too low. Only the <b>gas plus the electrolytes plus the bedside picture</b> together point to the right treatment.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>respiratory acidosis: <b>reduced gas exchange</b> from the underlying lung disease. Acidosis: risk for <b>dysrhythmias and cardiac arrest</b> from hyperkalemia, and <b>falls</b> from low BP, poor perfusion and slowed thinking. Alkalosis: risk for <b>injury</b> — falls from hypotension and weakness, tetany, seizures — and <b>weak breathing</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>fewer signs of the imbalance; <b>pH above 7.2 and moving toward normal</b> (or back to the patient’s baseline); PaO₂ and PaCO₂ improving from admission; <b>potassium and calcium in range</b> with a stable ECG; adequate gas exchange; <b>no falls or injury</b>; the patient takes drugs as prescribed and knows the warning signs.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Acidosis</div><b>metabolic</b>: fluids, <b>insulin</b> for DKA, antidiarrheals, <b>bicarbonate if pH under 7.2</b> with low HCO₃⁻; continuous <b>heart and muscle</b> monitoring. <b>Respiratory</b>: keep the <b>airway</b> open; <b>bronchodilators, anti-inflammatories, mucolytics</b>; <b>lowest oxygen</b> that prevents hypoxemia; positioning and breathing techniques; <b>ventilator</b> if SpO₂ can’t stay at 90% or muscles tire; breathing checks <b>hourly</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Alkalosis</div><b>stop the cause</b> (NG suction, excess IV fluids, diuretics, antacids); <b>fluids and electrolytes</b> (potassium, calcium, chloride) as prescribed; <b>antiemetics</b>; electrolytes <b>daily</b>; for anxious hyperventilation, <b>calm coaching and slow breathing</b> after checking oxygenation.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Safety and teaching</div><b>fall precautions</b> for every acid–base problem; <b>neuro checks at least every 2 hours</b>; trend <b>ABGs and potassium</b>. Teach: take <b>diuretics, blood-pressure and heart drugs</b> exactly as prescribed; don’t overuse <b>antacids or baking soda</b>; manage diabetes and lung disease; report <b>vomiting or diarrhea that won’t stop</b>, new weakness, confusion or trouble breathing.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>breathing easier with adequate gas exchange; <b>pH above 7.2 and heading toward 7.35–7.45</b> (or the patient’s baseline); <b>PaO₂ above 90</b> or 10 mm Hg better than on admission; <b>PaCO₂ under 45</b> or 15 mm Hg better; <b>potassium and calcium in range</b>, heart rhythm at baseline; alert and oriented; strength and reflexes back to normal; no tetany, seizures or falls.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"15-overview","ch":"15","chTitle":"Infusion therapy","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">15 · Chapter 15</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Fluid &amp; electrolyte balance · tissue integrity</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Infusion therapy</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> <b>Infusion therapy</b> delivers fluids and drugs through the skin into a vein (or bone, artery, abdomen or spine) — the <b>most common invasive treatment in hospitals</b>, given in every setting. The nurse’s job runs in a chain: <b>check that the prescription is complete</b>, <b>match the fluid to the vein</b> (its tonicity, <b>pH</b> and <b>osmolarity</b> decide whether a peripheral vein can take it or it needs the fast flow of a <b>central</b> vein), <b>choose the device</b> for how long therapy will last (short peripheral catheter, midline, PICC, central catheter, tunneled catheter or implanted port), <b>place it with asepsis</b>, and <b>keep it safe</b> — site checks, dressings, flushing, “scrub the hub.” The dangers are <b>local</b> (infiltration, extravasation, phlebitis, thrombosis, infection) and <b>systemic</b> (<b>catheter-related bloodstream infection</b>, circulatory overload, speed shock, embolism). The RN is accountable for all of it, including what is delegated.</p><div id=\"ch15-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Why infuse</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Infusions <b>keep or restore fluid balance</b>, <b>correct electrolytes and acid–base</b>, deliver <b>drugs</b> fast, and <b>replace blood and blood products</b> — in hospitals, homes, clinics and long-term care. IV drugs act at once, so mistakes act at once too (<b>adverse drug events</b>). The <b>RN is accountable</b> for all infusion care and anything delegated; specialized <b>infusion teams</b> lower complications.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · Match the fluid to the vein</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Tonicity sets where the water goes (<b>isotonic 270–300 mOsm/L</b> stays in the ECF — overload risk in older adults; <b>hypertonic</b> pulls water out of cells; <b>hypotonic</b> moves it in). Most solutions have a <b>pH of 3.5–6.2</b>. A fluid with <b>pH under 5 or over 9</b> or <b>osmolarity over 600 mOsm/L</b> damages small veins and belongs in a <b>central</b> vein, where about <b>2 L/min</b> of blood dilutes it (<b>TPN, over 1400 mOsm/L, is never peripheral</b>). <b>Irritants</b> (amiodarone, vancomycin, ciprofloxacin) inflame veins; <b>vesicants</b> (dopamine, many chemotherapy drugs) destroy tissue if they leak.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · Choose the device</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Short peripheral catheters</b> (¾–1¼ inch, 26 to 14 gauge) for most therapy, removed when clinically indicated; <b>midlines</b> (3–8 inches, tip no farther than the axillary vein) for <b>6–14 days</b> of peripheral-compatible therapy; <b>PICCs</b> (tip at the <b>cavoatrial junction</b>) for <b>more than 14 days</b> or any infusate; <b>nontunneled central catheters</b> for short-term critical care; <b>tunneled catheters</b> for weeks to months; <b>implanted ports</b> for <b>more than a year</b>; <b>hemodialysis catheters</b> only for dialysis.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · Place it safely</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Peripheral: <b>upper arm or forearm, nondominant, distal first</b>; never the <b>wrist, joints of flexion</b>, a hard vein, or an arm with a <b>mastectomy, lymph node dissection, fistula or paralysis</b>; <b>HANDS</b> — hygiene, antisepsis (chlorhexidine–alcohol, 30 seconds, let it dry), no touching, documentation, <b>scrub the hub</b>; <b>two attempts per clinician</b>, then escalate; stop at once for tingling or “pins and needles” (nerve). Central lines: the <b>CRBSI prevention bundle</b> — checklist, time-out, full barrier precautions, <b>chlorhexidine</b>, best site (avoid the femoral vein), <b>daily review of need</b>.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · Keep it safe</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Check the system <b>from the insertion site up the tubing to the bag</b> — at least <b>every 4 hours</b> with a continuous infusion (every 1–2 hours if critically ill or confused, more often with vesicants). Keep dressings <b>clean, dry and intact</b>; <b>no BP cuff or blood draw</b> on the catheter arm; <b>Luer-Lok</b> connections, never taped; <b>scrub the hub</b> before every access; <b>flush with saline</b> before and after drugs (a <b>10-mL syringe</b> for central lines; <b>stop at any resistance</b>); change tubing on schedule; use <b>smart pumps</b> but still watch the patient.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · What goes wrong</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Local: <b>infiltration</b>, <b>extravasation</b> (a vesicant — blisters, sloughing, necrosis), <b>phlebitis</b>, <b>thrombosis</b>, <b>hematoma</b>, <b>site infection</b>, <b>venous spasm</b>, <b>nerve injury</b>. Systemic: <b>circulatory overload</b>, <b>speed shock</b>, <b>catheter embolism</b>. Central lines during dwell: <b>migration, dislodgment, rupture, lumen occlusion</b> and <b>catheter-related bloodstream infection (CRBSI)</b> — preventable, and potentially fatal.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Fever and chills</b> in a patient with a central line (CRBSI); <b>shortness of breath, cough, crackles, engorged neck veins, rising BP</b> during an infusion (overload — slow it, sit the patient up, notify); <b>dizziness, chest tightness, flushing and an irregular pulse</b> after a fast drug (speed shock — stop it); a catheter <b>shorter on removal</b> than at insertion (embolism — tourniquet high on the limb, notify at once); a <b>vesicant leaking</b>; an <b>arterial line disconnected</b>; <b>neurologic change</b> or a <b>temperature over 101 °F</b> with an epidural or intrathecal catheter.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">A break in asepsis or a hub that wasn’t scrubbed<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>organisms enter the lumen or track along the catheter<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>they grow on the catheter surface<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>they seed the bloodstream (<b>CRBSI</b>)<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span><b>fever, chills, malaise</b> → tachycardia, hypotension, low urine<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>untreated → <b>sepsis and death</b></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Infiltration vs extravasation</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Both are fluid leaking from the vein into the tissue, with the same early signs — <b>swelling, coolness, skin tightness, burning or discomfort, a slowing rate</b>. <b>Infiltration</b> is a <b>nonvesicant</b>: stop, <b>remove the catheter</b>, elevate, apply warm or cool compresses per policy, restart in the other arm. <b>Extravasation</b> is a <b>vesicant</b> and destroys tissue (<b>blisters, sloughing, necrosis</b> — sometimes days later): <b>stop and disconnect</b>, <b>aspirate</b> the drug, <b>leave the catheter in</b> if an antidote will be given, cold compresses (<b>except</b> vinca alkaloids and epipodophyllotoxins), <b>photograph</b> and assess the whole limb.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Phlebitis vs thrombosis vs site infection</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Phlebitis</b>: an inflamed vein — <b>pain, redness, warmth, a hard cord</b> (mechanical, chemical or bacterial) → remove a peripheral catheter, warm compresses. <b>Thrombosis</b>: a clot — <b>swollen limb, slowed infusion</b>, tenderness (with a central line, <b>engorged veins</b> on that side of the chest and arm) → stop, remove a peripheral catheter, <b>cold</b> compresses, elevate, notify for a central line. <b>Site infection</b>: <b>red, swollen, warm, purulent</b> drainage at the site without bloodstream infection → clean, remove peripheral lines with sterile technique, culture the tip if asked.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Peripheral vs central lines</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Peripheral</b> (short catheters, midlines): small veins with slow flow — infusates at <b>pH 5–9</b> and <b>under about 500–600 mOsm/L</b>, <b>no vesicants</b> in a midline, <b>no TPN</b>; easy to place, short dwell. <b>Central</b> (PICCs, central catheters, tunneled catheters, ports): the tip sits at the <b>cavoatrial junction</b>, where blood flow (~2 L/min, against ~200 mL/min in the axillary vein) dilutes anything — <b>any pH or osmolarity, vesicants, TPN</b> — but the tip must be <b>confirmed before use</b>, <b>CRBSI and air embolism</b> are the big risks, and flushing needs a <b>10-mL syringe</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why syringe size matters</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Pressure is force spread over the plunger’s area: a <b>small syringe</b> concentrates the same push into <b>more pressure</b>, which can <b>rupture a central catheter</b> — so central lines are flushed with <b>10-mL syringes</b>, and you <b>stop at any resistance</b>. Pulling works the opposite way: a small syringe creates <b>less suction</b>, so it is the right choice for <b>drawing blood</b> from a small catheter that collapses under a vacuum tube.</div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">The <b>insertion site</b> (redness, swelling, hardness, drainage, skin under the dressing for adhesive injury), the <b>whole limb and upper chest</b> with a midline or PICC, the <b>tunnel</b> of a tunneled catheter, the <b>port pocket</b>; the dressing (<b>clean, dry, adherent</b>); every connection (Luer-Lok, not taped); the <b>rate and volume</b> infused and the <b>label</b> on every bag; <b>external catheter length</b> at each dressing change; <b>baseline arm circumference</b> before a PICC; <b>intake, output and overload signs</b> (older adults especially); <b>temperature</b> and signs of infection; distal <b>pulse, warmth, color and sensation</b> with arterial or intraosseous lines; <b>alertness, breathing and itching</b> with intraspinal infusions.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Fever, chills or malaise</b> with a central line (CRBSI: change the whole system, cultures, antibiotics as prescribed); <b>dyspnea, cough, crackles, engorged neck veins, edema or rising BP</b> during an infusion (overload: slow the rate, upright, oxygen, notify); <b>dizziness, chest tightness, flushed face, irregular pulse</b> after a rapid drug (speed shock: stop it, run isotonic fluid to keep the vein open, notify); a catheter that is <b>shorter than at insertion</b> or broken (embolism: tourniquet high on the limb, notify <b>emergently</b>); a <b>vesicant</b> leaking; <b>resistance</b> when flushing (stop); an <b>arterial line disconnected or bleeding</b>; a <b>cool, mottled or swollen limb</b> with an intraosseous needle (compartment syndrome); <b>headache, stiff neck, temperature over 101 °F</b> or any <b>neurologic change</b> with an epidural or intrathecal catheter.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Flow dilutes — why the vein matters</b> — the <b>superior vena cava</b> carries about <b>2 L of blood a minute</b>; the axillary vein about <b>200 mL</b>. A harsh infusate (extreme <b>pH</b> or high <b>osmolarity</b>) dripping into a small, slow vein stays concentrated against its lining — the <b>endothelium</b> is injured, platelets gather, and <b>phlebitis and thrombosis</b> follow. At the cavoatrial junction the same drug is diluted almost at once.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>How catheters become infected</b> — organisms reach the blood two ways: <b>along the outside</b> of the catheter from the skin (the warm, moist skin of the <b>neck and chest</b>, and the <b>groin</b>, carries far more than the cool, dry upper arm — one reason PICCs infect less often), and <b>through the inside</b> when hubs are handled (<b>scrub the hub</b>). A <b>tunnel</b> and a <b>cuff</b> that tissue grows into block the outside route; every extra day of dwell adds risk.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Pressure, syringes and rupture</b> — pressure equals force divided by area. The <b>narrower the syringe barrel</b>, the <b>higher the pressure</b> a thumb generates — enough to <b>burst</b> a central catheter or push a clot loose against a partial blockage. Gravity and pumps stay within a catheter’s limits; hand flushing does not, which is why <b>10-mL syringes</b> and <b>stopping at resistance</b> are the rules.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Air embolism on removal</b> — when a central catheter comes out, the <b>tract</b> from skin to vein can draw air in whenever pressure in the chest falls below air pressure (as it does with every breath in). Lying <b>flat or in Trendelenburg</b>, <b>holding the breath or bearing down (Valsalva)</b>, keeping the line <b>clamped</b> and covering the site with an <b>occlusive dressing for 24 hours</b> keep chest pressure up and the tract sealed.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Almost every infusion rule comes from three facts: <b>small veins tolerate little</b> (match the infusate to the vein), <b>anything inside the body is a doorway for microbes</b> (asepsis, scrub the hub, remove lines early), and <b>the system is under pressure</b> (Luer-Lok, right syringe size, clamps, careful removal).</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"15-iv-fluids-blood-and-the-prescription","ch":"15","chTitle":"Infusion therapy","title":"IV fluids, blood and the prescription","kind":"topic","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What is infused (Table 15.1)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Tonicity</b> — <b>isotonic</b> (270–300 mOsm/L — <b>0.9% saline 308</b>, <b>D5W 272</b>, <b>lactated Ringer’s 273</b>) stays in the ECF, so older adults on it risk <b>fluid overload</b>; <b>hypertonic</b> (<b>D5 in 0.45% saline 406</b>, <b>D10W 500</b>; parenteral nutrition) pulls water out of cells; <b>hypotonic</b> (<b>0.45% saline</b>) moves water into them. Hypertonic and hypotonic fluids raise the risk of <b>phlebitis and infiltration</b>.</div>\n<div><b>pH and osmolarity limits</b> — most IV solutions have a <b>pH of 3.5 to 6.2</b>. Anything with a <b>pH under 5.0 or over 9.0</b>, or an <b>osmolarity over 600 mOsm/L</b>, is best given <b>centrally</b>. <b>TPN</b> runs <b>over 1400 mOsm/L</b> and is <b>never</b> given into a peripheral vein — it damages blood cells and the vein lining.</div>\n<div><b>Irritants and vesicants (Drug Alert)</b> — <b>irritants</b> with a low pH — <b>amiodarone, vancomycin, ciprofloxacin</b> — cause <b>phlebitis</b> over a long peripheral course. <b>Vesicants</b> — vasoconstrictors such as <b>dopamine</b> and many <b>chemotherapy</b> drugs — cause <b>extravasation</b> injury: blistering, sloughing, necrosis. Check each drug’s pH and osmolarity <b>before</b> choosing the access. Early infiltration signs (<b>swelling, coolness, tingling, redness</b>) → stop the drug and call the infusion team, or remove the catheter and notify the provider.</div>\n<div><b>Blood and blood products</b> — <b>packed red cells</b> (most of the plasma removed), <b>platelets, fresh frozen plasma, albumin</b> and clotting factors — each with its own compatibility and infusion rules. National Patient Safety Goal: <b>two patient identifiers</b> and <b>two qualified professionals</b> before any blood product; bar coding helps. The <b>ISBT 128</b> label shows the <b>facility, donor lot number, product code, and ABO and Rh type</b>, in bar code and readable text.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A drug that is safe by mouth can be dangerous in a vein: by IV it reaches the heart in seconds and touches the vein lining at full strength. That is why IV administration needs its own checks — <b>dilution, rate, pH and osmolarity, compatibility and the right vein</b> — never assume the IV route works like the others.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The prescription and drug safety</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>A complete fluid order</b> — the <b>type of fluid</b>, the <b>rate</b> in mL/hr (or the total volume over a set number of hours — <b>125 mL/hr</b> or <b>1000 mL over 8 hr</b>), and any <b>additives</b> (electrolytes, vitamins). “<b>D5W to keep the vein open</b>” names no rate — it is <b>incomplete</b>; clarify before giving it.</div>\n<div><b>A complete drug order</b> — drug name (preferably <b>generic</b>), <b>dose and route</b>, <b>frequency</b>, <b>time(s)</b>, <b>how long</b> (doses or days) and, in some agencies, the <b>purpose</b>. Some infusions (pain drugs) are ordered in <b>mg/hr</b>; the pharmacist may set the diluent.</div>\n<div><b>Action Alert</b> — make sure the IV prescription is <b>right for this patient</b> and <b>complete</b>, and ask the provider about anything unclear <b>before</b> giving it.</div>\n<div><b>Medication safety (NPSG)</b> — label drugs properly, take <b>special care with anticoagulants</b>, and reconcile medications. Know the <b>high-alert</b> and <b>look-alike, sound-alike</b> drugs, limit available concentrations, use <b>single-dose containers</b> (flushes too), and pair <b>smart pumps</b> with computerized order entry and <b>bar-code administration</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Most infusion errors start before the needle — an order missing a rate, a look-alike drug, a pump programmed wrong. Checking the order and using layered technology catch those errors when they are still harmless.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"15-peripheral-catheters-short-catheters-and","ch":"15","chTitle":"Infusion therapy","title":"Peripheral catheters: short catheters and midlines","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Short peripheral catheters</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What they are</b> — a plastic cannula over a sharp <b>stylet</b>; the stylet punctures the vein, the cannula is advanced, the stylet is withdrawn into a <b>safety mechanism</b> (required by OSHA — the hollow, blood-filled stylet is a high needlestick risk). Lengths <b>¾ to 1¼ inch</b>, gauges <b>26 (smallest) to 14 (largest)</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Choosing the gauge (Table 15.2)</b> — the <b>smallest gauge</b> that delivers the therapy. <b>24–26 g</b>: small veins, slow viscous fluids, blood takes longer. <b>22 g</b>: most therapies, <b>blood without damage</b>. <b>20 g</b>: all therapies (anesthesia’s minimum for surgery). <b>18 g</b>: preferred for surgery. <b>14–16 g</b>: <b>trauma and rapid resuscitation</b>. A thin-walled 24 g flows about like a standard 22 g; <b>larger catheters cause more phlebitis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Choosing the site (Box 15.1)</b> — <b>upper extremity</b>, never the <b>wrist</b>; the <b>nondominant</b> arm; start <b>distal</b> and move <b>proximal</b>; avoid <b>joints of flexion</b>, <b>hard or cordlike</b> veins, and skin with cellulitis or old complications; <b>never</b> an arm with a <b>mastectomy, lymph node dissection, fistula or graft, or paralysis</b> (a provider’s order is needed). Avoid <b>leg veins</b> in adults (<b>DVT, infiltration</b>) and <b>hand veins</b> in older adults and active outpatients. Use the <b>external jugular</b> only in emergencies.</div>\n<div><b>Two attempts, then escalate</b> — limit <b>unsuccessful attempts to two per clinician</b>, then call a more skilled clinician or the IV team or consider another route — repeated sticks hurt and raise infection risk. <b>Ultrasound</b> reaches deeper veins (trained users only; arteries and nerves lie nearby, and deep infiltration is spotted late); <b>vein illuminators</b> see only about 10 mm deep.</div>\n<div><b>Nerve injury (Critical Rescue)</b> — avoid the <b>palm side of the wrist</b> (the <b>median nerve</b>) and the <b>cephalic vein near the wrist</b>. <b>Tingling, “pins and needles” or numbness</b> during the stick → <b>stop at once, remove the catheter</b>, choose a new site.</div>\n<div><b>Dwell, checks and locks</b> — there is <b>no set dwell time</b> — remove or move it when <b>clinically indicated</b> (phlebitis, infection, malfunction); routine replacement every 72–96 hours prevents no more phlebitis or infection (Cochrane review). Check the site at least <b>every 4 hours</b> with a continuous infusion, <b>every 1–2 hours</b> if critically ill or cognitively impaired, <b>more often</b> with vesicants, otherwise <b>once a shift</b>. A <b>saline lock</b> keeps access for IV push and intermittent drugs (flush before and after); don’t use it for routine blood samples (<b>hemolysis</b>). <b>Winged needles</b> infiltrate easily — for single doses and blood draws.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The smallest catheter in the biggest suitable vein leaves room for blood to flow around it — that flow dilutes the infusate and keeps the lining healthy. A big catheter in a small vein blocks the flow, rubs the wall and invites <b>phlebitis and clots</b>.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">HANDS and midline catheters</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>HANDS (Box 15.2)</b> — <b>H</b>ygiene — wash hands, gloves, clean the skin (soap and water if soiled). <b>A</b>ntisepsis — <b>chlorhexidine 2% with 70% alcohol</b> (or 70% alcohol or povidone-iodine), back and forth for <b>30 seconds</b>, <b>let it dry</b>. <b>N</b>o touching the prepped site. <b>D</b>ocumentation — site, dressing, tubing, <b>dates</b>. <b>S</b>crub the hub — alcohol, <b>at least 15 seconds</b>, every access.</div>\n<div><b>Midline catheters</b> — <b>3–8 inches</b>, inserted in an upper-arm vein (the <b>basilic</b> is preferred — larger and straighter; the median antecubital without ultrasound), with the tip <b>no farther than the axillary vein</b>. Used for <b>6 to 14 days</b> of hydration or drugs, or when veins and skin make short catheters hard to keep. <b>Sterile technique</b> for insertion and dressings; extra training to insert.</div>\n<div><b>What a midline can’t take</b> — it is still a <b>peripheral</b> line: infusates at <b>pH 5–9</b> and <b>under 600 mOsm/L</b>; <b>no vesicants</b> (a leak there is found late), <b>no parenteral nutrition</b> of any strength; <b>no routine blood draws</b>; not in a limb with a <b>mastectomy with lymphedema, paralysis, or a dialysis graft or fistula</b>; don’t run <b>incompatible drugs</b> through two lumens at once (axillary flow is too slow to keep them apart).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A midline lives in the arm’s peripheral veins even though it is long, so it follows peripheral rules. Treating it like a central line — running TPN or a vesicant through it — is one of the classic infusion errors.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"15-central-lines-piccs-central-catheters-tu","ch":"15","chTitle":"Infusion therapy","title":"Central lines: PICCs, central catheters, tunneled catheters and ports","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">PICCs and nontunneled central catheters</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Where the tip belongs</b> — every central device ends in the <b>superior vena cava</b> at its junction with the right atrium (the <b>cavoatrial junction</b>), where blood flow is about <b>2 L/min</b>. The tip is confirmed by <b>chest x-ray</b>, fluoroscopy or <b>ECG/magnet tip-location</b> systems <b>before any infusion</b>. Only catheters labeled for <b>power injection</b> can take CT contrast — others can <b>rupture</b>.</div>\n<div><b>PICCs</b> — inserted through an arm vein — the <b>basilic</b> preferred (cephalic if needed; not the brachial veins, which lie by the artery) — <b>18–29 inches</b> long; best placed <b>early</b>, before the arm’s veins are damaged. A tip that stops short (<b>midclavicular</b>) causes far more <b>thrombosis</b>. PICCs take <b>any infusate</b> — antibiotics for <b>more than 14 days</b>, chemotherapy, <b>PN</b>, vasopressors; “power PICCs” take contrast at <b>5 mL/sec, 300 psi</b>. Infection is <b>less common</b> than with neck or chest lines, and there is <b>no pneumothorax risk</b>.</div>\n<div><b>PICC care</b> — main complications: <b>phlebitis, thrombophlebitis, DVT, CRBSI</b> (use the smallest size to cut upper-arm DVT). Measure the <b>upper-arm circumference</b> before insertion as a baseline. Blood draws need a lumen of <b>4 Fr or larger</b>; blood transfusion usually needs a <b>pump</b> (cold, thick red cells must finish within <b>4 hours</b>). Patients do usual ADLs but <b>avoid heavy lifting</b>; PICCs may not suit people who use their <b>arms or crutches</b> to move. Flush per the manufacturer: <b>10 mL saline</b> before and after drugs, <b>20 mL after blood draws</b>, always a <b>10-mL syringe</b>; saline vs heparin lock follows policy.</div>\n<div><b>Nontunneled central catheters</b> — <b>7–10 inches</b>, one to <b>five lumens</b>, placed through the <b>subclavian or internal jugular</b> vein (the <b>femoral</b> vein only when unavoidable — highest infection rate, removed as soon as possible). Used for <b>emergencies, trauma, critical care and surgery</b>, short term. Insertion needs <b>Trendelenburg</b> with a towel between the shoulder blades — hard for people with <b>breathing problems, spinal curvature or raised ICP</b>; neck and chest skin carries <b>more organisms</b>, so infections are more common; a <b>tracheostomy</b> nearby adds contamination risk.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A PICC brings central-line power through an arm vein, trading the chest risks (pneumothorax, dirtier skin) for arm risks (DVT, phlebitis, limited lifting). Choosing among central devices is a choice about <b>how long</b> therapy lasts and <b>which risks this patient</b> can best tolerate.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Long-term devices and the prevention bundle</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Tunneled catheters</b> — part of the catheter runs under the skin in a <b>tunnel</b>, so the skin exit is away from the vein entry; a rough <b>cuff</b> in the tunnel (often antibiotic-treated) lets tissue grow in — a <b>barrier to microbes</b> and an anchor. Placed and removed <b>surgically or in radiology</b> (Broviac, Hickman, Leonard). For <b>weeks to months</b> when a PICC won’t do — a <b>paraplegic</b> person needing 6–8 weeks of antibiotics, or someone who can’t face port needlesticks.</div>\n<div><b>Implanted ports</b> — a <b>port body</b> with a self-sealing <b>septum</b> over a reservoir, in a pocket under the skin, attached to a central catheter — <b>nothing outside</b>, so the <b>least effect on body image</b>; chosen for therapy lasting <b>more than a year</b> (often <b>chemotherapy</b>). Access only with a <b>noncoring (Huber) needle</b>, by trained staff, with a <b>mask and aseptic technique</b>; a <b>chest</b> port takes about <b>2000</b> punctures, an arm port about <b>750</b>. <b>Power ports</b> (some have <b>three bumps and a triangle</b>) take contrast with a <b>power-rated</b> noncoring needle; patients carry a <b>wallet card</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Check blood return first (Drug Alert)</b> — <b>before any drug</b> through a port, aspirate for a <b>blood return</b> (the color and consistency of whole blood). <b>No blood return → hold the drug</b> until patency and needle placement are confirmed — a <b>fibrin sheath</b> at the tip can send a vesicant <b>backward into the tissue</b>. Watch the needle’s <b>rebound</b> when you pull it from the dense septum (needlestick risk).</div>\n<div><b>Hemodialysis catheters</b> — very large lumens for dialysis or pheresis; <b>heparin</b> dwells inside to keep them open and must be <b>aspirated before use</b>. Not for other fluids or drugs <b>except in an emergency</b>; maintained by <b>dialysis nurses</b>.</div>\n<div><b>CRBSI prevention bundle (Box 15.3)</b> — a <b>checklist</b> with authority to <b>stop</b> anyone who breaks technique; thorough <b>hand hygiene</b>; a <b>time-out</b>; <b>maximal barrier precautions</b> (patient draped head to toe; inserter in <b>sterile gloves, gown and mask</b>; everyone in the room masked); <b>minimal traffic</b> and a line cart; <b>chlorhexidine</b> skin prep; the <b>best site</b> (arm or subclavian first, jugular next, <b>femoral last</b>); meticulous <b>post-insertion care</b> and hub scrubbing; and <b>reviewing every day whether the line is still needed</b> — remove it as soon as it isn’t.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every day a central line stays in, the chance of a bloodstream infection rises — so the single most powerful bundle item is the simplest: <b>ask every day whether the line is still needed</b>.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"15-infusion-systems-containers-tubing-conne","ch":"15","chTitle":"Infusion therapy","title":"Infusion systems: containers, tubing, connectors and pumps","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Containers, tubing and add-ons</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Containers</b> — <b>glass</b> is inert and easy to read but heavy and needs a <b>vented</b> set; <b>plastic</b> bags collapse as they empty (no vent) and are light and shatterproof. Some plastics leach the plasticizer <b>DEHP</b> (a concern for repeated exposure and male reproductive development), so many hospitals use <b>DEHP-free</b> bags — which are stiffer and can <b>burst in pneumatic tubes</b>. <b>Insulin, nitroglycerin, lorazepam</b> and <b>lipids</b> may bind to or interact with PVC. <b>Action Alert</b>: check every container for <b>cracks or pinholes</b> and every fluid for <b>cloudiness, particles or odd color</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Administration sets and change times</b> — a <b>primary</b> set runs the main fluid; a short <b>secondary (piggyback)</b> set joins it at a Y-site for intermittent drugs. <b>Primary and secondary continuous sets</b>: up to <b>7 days</b> (may be changed every <b>96 hours</b>). <b>Intermittent sets</b> (both ends handled each dose): <b>every 24 hours</b>. <b>Parenteral nutrition</b>: every <b>24 hours</b>; <b>lipids alone</b>: every <b>12 hours</b>; <b>blood tubing</b>: within <b>4 hours</b>. Sets are sterile only inside and under their caps — never on a sterile field.</div>\n<div><b>Connections</b> — use <b>Luer-Lok</b> (threaded) connections everywhere — <b>critical on central lines</b> to prevent <b>air embolism</b>; <b>tape is never</b> an adequate connection. After any disconnection, retrace the line from the patient to the bag before reconnecting — tubing has been connected to the <b>wrong port</b> (even a tracheostomy) with fatal results.</div>\n<div><b>Filters</b> — remove <b>particles, microbes and air</b>; place them <b>close to the catheter hub</b>. <b>1.2-micron</b> filters for lipid-containing PN; <b>0.2-micron</b> filters remove particles and bacteria. A red cell (~5 microns) is about the largest thing that passes the <b>lung capillaries</b>, so particles can lodge there (and in spleen, kidney and liver) and add to <b>phlebitis</b>. <b>Blood filters</b> (170–260 microns; microaggregate 20–80; <b>leukocyte-removal</b> to prevent febrile reactions) are different and not interchangeable.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>An infusion is a closed path from the bag to the bloodstream: every container, junction and set change is a point where <b>air, particles or microbes</b> can enter. The rules — inspect, lock, filter, change on schedule — each close one of those doors.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Needleless connectors and pumps</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Needleless connectors</b> — required since the <b>Needlestick Safety and Prevention Act</b> (staff help choose the devices; employers keep a <b>sharps injury log</b>). Their crevices trap blood and bacteria: <b>scrub the hub</b> vigorously with alcohol or chlorhexidine–alcohol for <b>5–15 seconds</b> (Box 15.2 says at least 15 — scrubbing for 15 meets both), twisting “like juicing an orange,” before every access (or use <b>disinfecting caps</b> per the maker). Change connectors <b>with the primary set</b>, at least <b>weekly</b> for intermittent use, if <b>blood remains</b> after sampling, or if they <b>leak</b>. Flushing technique depends on the valve type (positive, negative or neutral displacement).</div>\n<div><b>Electronic pumps</b> — pumps measure volume by a syringe mechanism, <b>peristaltic</b> action or microchambers, with <b>dedicated cassettes</b>, alarms (air in line, <b>occlusion</b>, infusion complete, low battery) and <b>free-flow protection</b> when the cassette is removed. <b>Syringe pumps</b> give small volumes (antibiotics, <b>PCA</b>; good for <b>fluid-restricted</b> patients); <b>ambulatory pumps</b> (under 6 lb) let home patients get PN, pain drugs and other infusions on the move. A pump <b>never replaces watching the site and rate</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Smart pumps</b> — pumps with <b>drug libraries</b> and dose limits for <b>high-alert drugs</b> — programming errors are among the most common drug errors. Wireless updates, <b>dose-guard</b> alerts and records of near misses add safety, but more steps and <b>more alarms</b>: keep your <b>manual calculation</b> skills, <b>validate every alarm</b>, and guard against <b>alarm fatigue</b> (a National Patient Safety Goal).</div>\n<div><b>Mechanical infusers</b> — <b>elastomeric balloons</b>, spring-coiled syringes and roller devices (such as <b>ON-Q</b>) push a preset rate without power — used for antibiotics at home and <b>pain catheters</b> after surgery; they hold only about <b>50–100 mL</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Technology moves the errors rather than ending them: a smart pump stops a 10-fold overdose but can’t see a swollen arm, a wrong patient or a line connected to the wrong port. The nurse remains the final safety check.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","ch":"15","chTitle":"Infusion therapy","title":"Daily care: assessing, dressing, flushing and removing","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Teaching, assessing and dressing</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Teaching before insertion</b> — the <b>type of catheter</b>, <b>hand hygiene</b> and aseptic care, the <b>therapy</b>, <b>alternatives</b>, <b>activity limits</b>, and the <b>signs to report</b> — in writing for long-term devices, with pictures for low literacy and an <b>interpreter</b> as needed. Preventing <b>CRBSI</b> is a National Patient Safety Goal.</div>\n<div><b>A systematic check</b> — know the <b>catheter type, length, insertion site and tip location</b>. Start at the <b>insertion site</b> (redness, swelling, hardness, drainage; skin under the dressing for <b>adhesive injury</b>), check the <b>whole limb and upper chest</b> for midlines and PICCs, the <b>tunnel</b> for tunneled lines and the <b>pocket</b> for ports; then follow the <b>tubing</b> (secure, untaped connections), the <b>rate</b> (pump or drops; volume infused is right) and the <b>bag label</b> (right patient, right fluid).</div>\n<div><b>Action Alerts at the bedside</b> — tell AP: <b>no blood pressure cuff</b> on an arm with any catheter (pressure causes <b>leaking and infiltration</b>, or irritates the vein of a midline or PICC). Draw blood from the <b>opposite arm</b> — never <b>above</b> an infusing peripheral catheter (diluted results) or near a midline or PICC (catheter damage). Cover the IV arm with plastic for <b>bathing</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Securing and dressings</b> — use <b>securement devices</b> (they cut dislodgment, phlebitis and infiltration) or clean, kit <b>tape</b> (never tape from rolls carried between rooms or stuck to bedrails or uniforms). <b>Short peripheral</b>: the transparent dressing isn’t changed routinely — only if <b>loose, damp or soiled</b>. <b>Central lines and midlines</b>: <b>gauze</b> every <b>48 hours</b>, <b>transparent</b> every <b>7 days</b>; the first PICC or midline dressing within <b>24 hours</b> (bleeding). Remove dressings <b>sideways or toward the insertion site</b> — never pulling away from it (dislodgment); note the <b>external catheter length</b> each time and report a change.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Most catheter problems announce themselves at the site long before they become emergencies — a little swelling, a damp dressing, a few centimeters more catheter outside. Regular, systematic checks catch them while they are still small.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Flushing, sampling and removing</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Flushing (Critical Rescue)</b> — flushing keeps lumens <b>open</b> and keeps <b>incompatible drugs apart</b>. Saline is standard (heparin only if the device calls for it). Flush with <b>slow, gentle pressure</b> and <b>aspirate for a brisk blood return</b>; <b>any resistance → stop</b> — forcing it can <b>rupture the catheter</b> or push a <b>clot</b> into the circulation. Volumes: about <b>3 mL</b> for a short peripheral catheter, <b>5–10 mL</b> of preservative-free saline for others; <b>bacteriostatic</b> saline no more than <b>30 mL a day</b>. Use <b>prefilled single-use syringes</b>, flush <b>right after</b> each use, and use a <b>push-pause</b> technique for central lines.</div>\n<div><b>Blood from central lines</b> — not routinely from short peripheral catheters. From a central line or midline only when the <b>benefit outweighs the risk</b> (no veins, frequent draws, needle fear) — every hub entry risks <b>CRBSI</b>. Consider what has been infused (<b>heparin</b> skews clotting tests, IV electrolytes skew electrolytes), no <b>blood cultures within an hour</b> after an antibiotic infusion, scrub the hub, use vacuum tubes or <b>small syringes</b> for small catheters, then flush <b>10–20 mL</b> and clear the cap of blood.</div>\n<div><b>Removing peripheral lines and PICCs</b> — short catheter: lift the dressing sideways, withdraw slowly, <b>pressure with dry gauze</b> until bleeding stops, check that the <b>tip is intact</b>, document. Midlines and PICCs come out <b>slowly, in short segments</b> — <b>never force</b> against resistance (<b>venospasm</b>; a broken catheter can <b>embolize</b>). If it sticks: distraction, deep breathing, re-dress, <b>apply heat</b>, <b>warm drinks</b>, wait; drugs or imaging if hours pass.</div>\n<div><b>Removing central lines</b> — position <b>flat or in Trendelenburg</b>, keep it <b>clamped</b>, have the patient <b>hold the breath or bear down (Valsalva)</b> — on a ventilator, remove during an <b>inhalation breath</b> — then gauze until hemostasis and an <b>occlusive dressing for at least 24 hours</b> (prevents <b>air embolism</b>). <b>Measure the catheter</b>: if it is shorter than at insertion, notify the provider <b>immediately</b> — an emergency. Tunneled catheters and ports are removed by providers.</div>\n<div><b>Documentation</b> — date and time; <b>who</b> inserted it; the <b>vein</b>; the <b>device, gauge and length</b>; the <b>number and sites of attempts</b>; the patient’s response; the <b>dressing</b> and <b>securement</b>; barrier precautions; <b>teaching</b> — then ongoing assessments and any complications.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Removal and flushing are where catheters break and air or clots get in — the moments when gentle technique matters most. <b>Stopping at resistance</b> and <b>checking the catheter after removal</b> turn a potential embolism into a non-event.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","ch":"15","chTitle":"Infusion therapy","title":"Complications of IV therapy","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Local complications (Table 15.3)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Infiltration</b> — a <b>nonvesicant</b> leaking into the tissue — catheter through the vein wall, leaky capillaries, backflow at the puncture, a <b>dislodged port needle</b>. <b>Slowing rate, growing edema, tight cool skin, burning or tenderness, leaking at the site</b>, no blood return. <b>Stop and remove</b> the peripheral catheter, sterile dressing if weeping, <b>elevate</b>, warm or cool compresses per policy, restart in the <b>opposite arm</b>. Prevent: small catheter, <b>avoid flexion</b> (armboard if needed), no restraints over the site, frequent checks.</div>\n<div><b>Extravasation</b> — a <b>vesicant</b> leaking — peripheral or central. Same early signs, then <b>blistering and sloughing</b> that may appear days later. <b>Stop and disconnect</b>, <b>aspirate</b> what you can, <b>leave the catheter</b> in if an antidote will be given, <b>cold</b> compresses for all drugs <b>except vinca alkaloids and epipodophyllotoxins</b>, <b>photograph</b>, assess above and below the site, written instructions; surgery may follow. Prevention is key: the <b>right device and site</b> for any vesicant.</div>\n<div><b>Phlebitis</b> — <b>inflammation of the vein</b> — mechanical (insertion, size, poor securement), <b>chemical</b> (extreme pH or osmolarity) or <b>bacterial</b> (broken asepsis, long dwell); can appear <b>48–96 hours after removal</b>. <b>Pain, redness, a hard cordlike vein</b>. Remove a peripheral catheter at the <b>first sign</b>, <b>warm</b> compresses, document on a <b>phlebitis scale</b>, restart in the other arm. In the <b>first week after a PICC</b>, it may be treated in place: continuous heat, rest and elevation (better in 24 hours, gone in 72). Prevent: small gauge, avoid flexion, peripheral infusates at <b>pH 5–9</b> and <b>under 500 mOsm/L</b> (elsewhere your book says 600; under 500 meets both), secure the catheter, asepsis.</div>\n<div><b>Thrombosis and thrombophlebitis</b> — a <b>clot</b> from damaged lining — traumatic or repeated sticks, an oversized catheter, hypercoagulability, stasis. <b>Slowed infusion, swollen tender limb</b>; with a central line, <b>engorged veins</b> of the chest and arm on that side. <b>Stop and remove</b> a peripheral line, <b>cold</b> compresses, elevate; for central lines notify for imaging (low-dose thrombolytics). Thrombophlebitis (clot plus inflammation): <b>cold first, then warm</b>. Prevent: <b>two attempts at most</b>, the smallest catheter in the largest vein, good securement.</div>\n<div><b>Bleeding, infection, spasm and nerves</b> — <b>Hematoma/ecchymosis</b> (fragile veins, coagulopathy, steroids): <b>light</b> pressure on removal, direct pressure for a hematoma, elevate, <b>ice for 24 hours, then warmth</b>. <b>Site infection</b> (red, swollen, warm, pus): clean with alcohol, remove peripheral lines with sterile technique, culture the tip if asked. <b>Venous spasm</b> (cramping above the site, slowing rate): <b>don’t remove</b> — slow the rate, <b>warm</b> compress; room-temperature fluids. <b>Nerve injury</b> (pain, tingling, numbness): <b>stop the insertion</b>; remove if it persists.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Heat and cold are chosen by goal: <b>cold</b> narrows vessels — it slows a clot’s spread, limits bleeding and keeps most vesicants in place; <b>warmth</b> opens vessels — it eases a spasm, soothes phlebitis and spreads the few vesicants (vinca alkaloids) that do more harm when concentrated.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Systemic and central-line complications (Tables 15.4 and 15.5)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Circulatory overload</b> — fluid faster than the body can handle: <b>shortness of breath, cough</b>, high BP, <b>puffy eyes</b>, dependent edema, <b>engorged neck veins</b>, moist breath sounds, falling SaO₂. <b>Slow the IV and notify</b>, sit the patient <b>upright</b>, monitor vital signs, oxygen and <b>diuretics</b> as prescribed. Prevent with careful <b>intake and output</b> and early reporting.</div>\n<div><b>Speed shock</b> — a drug pushed too fast reaches <b>toxic levels</b>: <b>lightheadedness, chest tightness, flushed face, irregular pulse</b> → loss of consciousness, shock, <b>cardiac arrest</b>. <b>Stop the drug</b>, hang <b>isotonic fluid to keep the vein open</b>, monitor and notify. Prevent: know and <b>follow each drug’s rate</b>; use <b>rate-controlling devices</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Catheter embolism</b> — a piece of catheter breaks off and travels — possibly <b>cardiopulmonary arrest</b>. <b>Notify emergently</b>, remove the catheter, <b>tourniquet high on the limb</b>, inspect how much is missing, x-ray, surgery. Prevent: <b>never reinsert the needle</b> into an over-the-needle catheter, never pull a catheter back through a needle, <b>no scissors</b> near the line.</div>\n<div><b>Migration and dislodgment</b> — <b>migration</b>: the tip moves to another vein with <b>no change in external length</b> (coughing, vomiting, heavy lifting, heart failure) — a <b>gurgling or running-water sound</b> in the ear (jugular), <b>back pain between the shoulder blades</b> (azygos), neurologic problems. <b>Dislodgment</b>: the <b>external length changes</b>. For both: <b>stop infusions, flush, notify</b>, x-ray; <b>never push a catheter back in</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Rupture and occlusion</b> — <b>rupture</b>: forceful flushing, <b>scissors</b>, or <b>pinch-off</b> of a subclavian line between clavicle and first rib → leaking, pain or swelling during infusion, no blood return; repair kit or removal. <b>Occlusion</b>: a <b>sudden</b> block suggests a <b>drug precipitate</b> (calcium, diazepam, phenytoin; acid or bicarbonate to clear it by pH), a <b>gradual</b> one a <b>clot</b> (<b>alteplase</b>); prevent by <b>flushing between and after</b> every drug and after blood draws.</div>\n<div><b>Catheter-related bloodstream infection</b> — from poor asepsis, contaminated <b>hubs</b>, long dwell, poor hand hygiene: early <b>fever, chills, headache, malaise</b>; later <b>tachycardia, hypotension, low urine output</b>. <b>Change the whole infusion system</b>, notify, <b>cultures</b>, antibiotics as prescribed; send the <b>infusate</b> or <b>catheter tip</b> to the lab if suspected. Prevent with <b>sterile technique and the CRBSI bundle</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The external length of a central catheter is a simple ruler for its hidden tip: if more catheter shows outside, the tip has moved inside — and a drug meant for the superior vena cava may now be going somewhere it can do harm.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","ch":"15","chTitle":"Infusion therapy","title":"IV therapy in older adults","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Skin, veins and insertion</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Fragile skin</b> — thinner skin with less fat and fewer nerve endings — older adults may <b>not feel a traumatic stick</b> and tolerate probing, which raises <b>infiltration and extravasation</b> risk; tape and dressings can <b>tear</b> the skin. <b>Chlorhexidine</b> is preferred (alcohol dries protective skin lipids; povidone-iodine needs <b>2 minutes</b> of contact and an iodine allergy check); let antiseptics <b>dry</b>; use gentle friction.</div>\n<div><b>Hair and adhesives</b> — <b>never shave</b> (microabrasions invite infection) — <b>clip</b> if needed. Use a <b>skin protectant</b> under dressings and securement devices, and adhesive remover or alcohol to take them off.</div>\n<div><b>Choosing the vein</b> — avoid the fragile, tortuous <b>veins on the back of the hand</b> — start higher on the arm. Veins <b>roll</b> and take longer to fill: use a <b>flat tourniquet</b> over a washcloth or the gown (not too long — <b>hematoma</b>), or a <b>BP cuff</b> just below diastolic; light tapping, fist pumping, <b>hanging the arm down</b>, or <b>warm compresses for 10–20 minutes</b>. Insert at a <b>low angle (10–15 degrees)</b> with good skin traction, and release the tourniquet as soon as the catheter enters.</div>\n<div><b>Device choice and confusion</b> — with poor turgor or few, fragile, hard veins, a <b>midline or PICC</b> may be best. Central catheters are harder: insertion needs <b>Trendelenburg</b> and good hydration, which <b>COPD or kyphosis</b> may not allow. For a <b>confused</b> patient, keep equipment out of reach and use a <b>site protector</b> or netting — but the site must stay <b>visible</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Aging changes both sides of the needle: the skin is easier to tear and slower to warn of pain, and the veins are tougher, rolling and slow to fill. Gentle technique and higher, larger veins compensate for both.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Heart, kidneys and vein preservation</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Accurate volumes</b> — older hearts and kidneys swing easily into <b>overload (heart failure)</b> or <b>dehydration</b> — check that the prescription fits the patient, use <b>electronic pumps</b> for accuracy, and watch for overload signs.</div>\n<div><b>Fluid restriction and sodium</b> — dilute drugs in the <b>smallest volume</b> (ask the pharmacist) and give them by <b>syringe pump or IV push</b> to leave room for drinking. Routine <b>saline</b> diluent adds <b>sodium</b> — a concern with hypertension or heart disease.</div>\n<div><b>Saving veins</b> — many chronically ill patients are <b>vein-depleted</b>. <b>Subcutaneous</b> and <b>intraosseous</b> routes work in emergencies and can give <b>isotonic, nonirritant</b> fluids routinely to people whose veins need preserving.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Because older adults have little margin, the infusion has to be precise — the right volume at the right rate through the gentlest device — and the veins they have left are worth protecting for future care.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"15-other-routes-subcutaneous-intraosseous-a","ch":"15","chTitle":"Infusion therapy","title":"Other routes: subcutaneous, intraosseous, arterial, peritoneal and spinal","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Subcutaneous and intraosseous</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Subcutaneous infusion</b> — for <b>pain drugs, insulin</b> and palliative care when oral drugs fail, IM is too painful, or veins are unavailable. <b>Hypodermoclysis</b> is a <b>slow infusion of isotonic fluid</b> into the fat for steady rehydration — <b>not for emergencies</b> or large volumes. Sites with intact skin and more fat: the <b>front and sides of the thighs and hips, the upper abdomen, under the clavicle</b> — <b>not the arms</b> (and under the clavicle or abdomen if the patient walks). <b>Hyaluronidase</b> may be added to each liter to speed absorption.</div>\n<div><b>Running a subcutaneous line</b> — a <b>25–27-gauge</b> winged needle, small catheter or subcutaneous set; chlorhexidine–alcohol prep; <b>pinch about 2 inches</b> and insert; transparent dressing. Check every <b>4 hours</b> in hospital, <b>twice daily</b> at home; <b>redness, warmth, leaking, bruising, swelling or pain</b> → remove it. Rotate the site <b>at least weekly</b>. Pooling or an uneven rate → restart elsewhere (a pump may help).</div>\n<div><b>Intraosseous access</b> — into the vascular <b>red marrow</b> — absorption like IV — for <b>cardiac arrest, trauma, burns, DKA</b> and other crises when veins can’t be reached, including before hospital. <b>15–16-gauge</b> IO needles (often a <b>powered driver</b>); sites: <b>proximal tibia (most common)</b>, distal femur, medial malleolus, proximal humerus, iliac crest. <b>Short-term only — no longer than 24 hours</b> while IV access is sought. The only <b>absolute contraindication</b> is a <b>fracture of that bone</b> (also severe osteoporosis, osteogenesis imperfecta, skin infection). Pain is usually 2–3/10; <b>lidocaine 1%</b> (preservative- and epinephrine-free) eases insertion and the first infusion.</div>\n<div><b>IO complications</b> — <b>infiltration</b> from a misplaced needle (fluid under the skin at the site or on the far side of the limb) — the <b>most common</b>; <b>obstruction</b> with marrow if flushing is delayed; <b>osteomyelitis</b> (asepsis, remove early); rarely <b>compartment syndrome</b> — pressure in a closed space cuts perfusion: watch for <b>coolness, swelling, mottling, discoloration</b> and report at once (it can end in amputation).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Bone marrow is a noncollapsible vascular sponge — it stays open even in shock, when veins flatten. That is why an IO line can deliver resuscitation fluids and drugs as fast as an IV when no vein can be found.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Arterial, intraperitoneal and intraspinal</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Arterial lines</b> — catheters in the <b>radial, brachial or femoral</b> artery for <b>blood sampling and continuous pressure monitoring</b> (a pressurized flush bag and a <b>transducer leveled at the atrium</b>); rarely, <b>arterial chemotherapy</b> into a tumor’s own artery (hepatic, carotid, pelvic). <b>Critical Rescue</b>: <b>Luer-Lok every connection</b> — a disconnection can cause <b>life-threatening hemorrhage</b>; pumps must overcome arterial pressure; check the limb’s <b>warmth, sensation, capillary refill and pulse</b>. Carotid lines: <b>neuro checks</b>; femoral lines: <b>DVT prevention</b>. Bleeding or occlusion in an artery is <b>more serious</b> than in a vein.</div>\n<div><b>Intraperitoneal therapy</b> — <b>chemotherapy</b> into the abdomen for ovarian and GI cancers that have spread to the peritoneum, through a port or external catheter with many side holes. Three phases: <b>instill, dwell (1–4 hours), drain</b>. <b>Semi-Fowler’s</b>; <b>nausea, vomiting</b> or <b>breathing trouble</b> from pressure → slow the rate, antiemetics; <b>severe pain</b> may mean <b>migration</b> (x-ray). Turn <b>side to side</b> to spread the fluid. Watch for <b>exit-site infection</b> and <b>peritonitis</b> (fever, rigid abdomen, rebound tenderness, ileus) — prevented by strict asepsis.</div>\n<div><b>Epidural and intrathecal</b> — <b>epidural</b> drugs sit outside the dura and diffuse in (some absorbed systemically); <b>intrathecal</b> drugs go into the <b>CSF</b> in the subarachnoid space, so doses are <b>much smaller</b> — always know <b>which space</b> a catheter is in. Used for <b>postoperative and chronic pain</b>, CNS cancer chemotherapy and <b>spasticity</b>. Temporary epidural catheters are taped up the back to the shoulder; long-term ones are <b>tunneled</b> or attached to <b>ports or implanted pumps</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Intraspinal safety</b> — an <b>in-line filter</b> on every intraspinal infusion; <b>preservative-free</b> drugs only (alcohol, phenols and sulfites are toxic to the nervous system); <b>no alcohol</b> at the site — it can track along the catheter and damage nerves — so <b>povidone-iodine</b> is used. Check <b>alertness, breathing and itching</b>. Watch for <b>infection/meningitis</b> (headache, <b>stiff neck</b>, temperature over <b>101 °F</b>), <b>bleeding</b> — an <b>epidural hematoma</b> (mind anticoagulant timing) — <b>CSF leak</b>, occlusion and migration. <b>Report any neurologic change at once.</b></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Each special route trades one risk for another: arteries carry high pressure (bleeding), the peritoneum is a large closed space (pressure, peritonitis), and the spinal spaces sit next to the cord (infection and bleeding reach the nervous system). The nursing checks follow each route’s particular danger.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"15-adpie","ch":"15","chTitle":"Infusion therapy","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">For any infusion the nurse checks the <b>order</b> (complete and right for this patient), the <b>match</b> (fluid to vein, device to duration), the <b>access</b> (site, dressing, connections, patency, external length), the <b>patient</b> (fluid balance, infection, comfort) — and acts fast on the complications that kill: <b>CRBSI, overload, speed shock, embolism</b>.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Before</div>the prescription (<b>fluid, rate, additives</b>; drug order complete), the infusate’s <b>pH, osmolarity and vesicant potential</b>, the expected <b>duration</b>, <b>allergies</b> (antiseptics, anesthetics), the arms (<b>mastectomy, fistula, paralysis</b>, skin), age and <b>cardiac and kidney</b> status, cognition, and the patient’s preference.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">During</div>the <b>site</b> (redness, swelling, coolness, hardness, drainage, pain), the <b>whole limb/chest</b> for midlines and PICCs, the <b>dressing</b>, <b>connections</b>, <b>rate and volume</b>, <b>labels</b>, <b>blood return</b> before port use, <b>external catheter length</b>, <b>intake and output</b> and overload signs, <b>temperature</b>, and — for special routes — distal perfusion, neurologic status and breathing.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Assessment runs in a fixed path — the patient, the site, the tubing, the pump, the bag — so nothing is skipped when a shift is busy.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>risk for <b>infection</b> (CRBSI, site infection); risk for <b>injury</b> — infiltration and extravasation, phlebitis, thrombosis, embolism, speed shock; risk for <b>fluid imbalance</b> (overload in older adults); risk for impaired <b>tissue integrity</b> (skin tears, vesicant injury).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>the right fluid and drugs arrive at the <b>prescribed rate</b> through a <b>patent, secure</b> device; <b>no infection</b>; complications caught early or <b>prevented</b>; fluid balance maintained; skin intact; the patient knows the device, the limits and the <b>signs to report</b>; lines are <b>removed as soon as they are no longer needed</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Placing</div>check the order; choose the <b>device for the duration</b> and the <b>site</b> by Box 15.1; <b>HANDS</b>; <b>two attempts</b> then escalate; stop for nerve symptoms; for central lines, the <b>CRBSI bundle</b> and <b>tip confirmation before use</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Maintaining</div>site checks (<b>q4h</b> continuous, more often when needed), <b>scrub the hub</b>, <b>Luer-Lok</b>, no tape on connections, saline <b>flushes</b> (10-mL syringe centrally; <b>stop at resistance</b>), dressing and tubing changes on schedule, <b>smart pumps</b> with your own checks, no BP or blood draws on the catheter arm, <b>daily “still needed?”</b></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Responding and teaching</div>act on complications by the tables — <b>stop and remove</b> for infiltration and phlebitis; <b>stop, aspirate, antidote</b> for extravasation; <b>slow, upright, notify</b> for overload; <b>stop</b> for speed shock; <b>tourniquet and notify</b> for embolism; <b>change the system and culture</b> for CRBSI. Teach the <b>device, hand hygiene, activity limits</b> and <b>signs to report</b>; document insertion and every assessment.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>the infusion runs <b>on time</b> at the right rate; the site is <b>free of redness, swelling and pain</b>; the dressing is <b>clean, dry and intact</b>; <b>blood return</b> and easy flushing; external length <b>unchanged</b>; <b>afebrile</b>, no signs of CRBSI; <b>balanced intake and output</b>, no overload; skin intact; the patient can <b>state the signs to report</b>; the device is <b>removed when therapy ends</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"16-overview","ch":"16","chTitle":"Inflammation and immunity","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">16 · Chapter 16</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Immunity · inflammation · infection</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Inflammation and immunity</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> The body defends itself in two layers. <b>Innate immunity</b> — skin, mucous membranes, the normal microbiome, <b>inflammation</b>, complement and natural killer cells — acts <b>at once and the same way every time</b>. <b>Adaptive immunity</b> learns: <b>B cells</b> make <b>antibodies</b> (antibody-mediated immunity) and <b>T cells</b> organize, regulate and kill (cell-mediated immunity), leaving <b>memory</b> so a second exposure is handled fast. All of it depends on telling <b>self from non-self</b> (each person’s <b>HLA</b> “product code”). Immunity can be <b>active or passive</b>, <b>natural or artificial</b> (vaccines, immune globulin). The nurse reads the <b>WBC differential</b> (neutrophils, a <b>left shift</b>, eosinophils), knows the <b>five cardinal signs</b> of inflammation — and remembers that <b>older adults</b> may show <b>no fever and no rise in WBCs</b> even with serious infection.</p><div id=\"ch16-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Two-part defense</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Innate immunity</b> is present from birth: <b>skin, cornea and mucous membranes</b>, the <b>microbiome</b> (normal flora that crowd out pathogens while they stay where they belong), <b>inflammation</b>, <b>complement</b> and <b>natural killer cells</b>. <b>Adaptive immunity</b> develops after exposure: <b>antibody-mediated (AMI)</b> through B cells and <b>cell-mediated (CMI)</b> through T cells. Full protection — being <b>immunocompetent</b> — needs inflammation, AMI and CMI working together.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · Self vs non-self</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Every person’s cells carry <b>human leukocyte antigens (HLAs)</b> — a genetic “<b>product code</b>” matched only by an identical twin. Immune cells compare codes at every encounter: a match is <b>self</b> and is left alone (<b>self-tolerance</b>, kept by <b>regulatory T cells</b>); a mismatch — microbes, infected or cancerous cells, another person’s tissue — is <b>non-self</b> and is neutralized, destroyed or eliminated. <b>Toll-like receptors</b> help spot microbes and stressed self cells.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · Inflammation (innate)</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Any injury or invasion triggers the <b>same three stages</b>: (I) <b>vascular</b> — histamine, serotonin and kinins dilate arterioles and leak plasma (<b>redness, warmth, swelling, pain</b>; usually 24–72 hours); (II) <b>cellular exudate</b> — a surge of <b>neutrophils</b>, pus, and the <b>arachidonic acid–COX</b> cascade that NSAIDs block; (III) <b>repair</b> — or <b>scar</b>, which never works like the original tissue. Five cardinal signs: <b>warmth, redness, swelling, pain, lost function</b>. Inflammation can occur <b>without infection</b>.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · Antibodies (AMI)</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A <b>B cell</b> sensitized to one antigen (with help from macrophages and <b>helper T cells</b>) becomes <b>plasma cells</b> — up to <b>300 antibodies a second</b> — and <b>memory cells</b>. <b>IgM</b> comes first, <b>IgG</b> dominates on re-exposure, <b>IgA</b> guards mucous membranes, <b>IgE</b> drives allergy and fights parasites, <b>IgD</b> is a B-cell receptor. Antibodies <b>clump, burst, coat, precipitate or neutralize</b> antigens and switch on <b>complement</b>.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · T cells and NK cells (CMI)</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Helper (CD4+) T cells</b> coordinate everything through <b>cytokines</b>; <b>regulatory T cells</b> keep immunity from attacking self; <b>cytotoxic T cells</b> deliver a lethal hit to <b>virus-infected cells, grafts and transplants</b>; <b>natural killer cells</b> destroy <b>cancer cells</b> and infected cells without prior sensitization. CMI guards against <b>cancer</b> and <b>intracellular infections</b> and causes <b>transplant rejection</b>.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · When defenses weaken</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Immunity is strongest in the <b>20s and 30s</b> and declines with age: fewer working neutrophils, <b>no leukocytosis and often no fever</b> in infection, slower antibody responses (<b>vaccines and boosters</b> matter more), weaker T-cell function (<b>false-negative TB skin tests</b>), more <b>autoantibodies</b>. Disease, injury, drugs and treatments can also suppress immunity; excess or misdirected immunity causes allergy and autoimmune disease (Chapter 17).</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · What to report</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A <b>left shift</b> (many <b>bands</b>) — the marrow can’t keep up, <b>sepsis risk</b>; a <b>low neutrophil count (ANC)</b>; <b>pus</b>, spreading redness or new pain in a wound; in an <b>older adult</b>, <b>confusion, a fall or a vague decline</b> even without fever or a raised WBC — subtle infection can become <b>sepsis</b> quickly.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Bacteria enter through a wound<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>tissue macrophages and mast cells release <b>histamine, kinins, cytokines</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>arterioles dilate, capillaries leak → <b>red, warm, swollen, painful</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>cytokines call <b>neutrophils</b> from the marrow → <b>pus</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>if infection outlasts supply → <b>bands</b> released (<b>left shift</b>)<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>untreated → <b>sepsis</b>; healed → repair or <b>scar</b></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Inflammation vs infection</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Inflammation</b> is the body’s nonspecific tissue response to <b>any</b> injury or invasion — a sprain, a burn, an MI, an allergy — and it is <b>not always caused by infection</b>. <b>Infection</b> is invasion by <b>pathogens that multiply</b> and cause illness; it usually <b>triggers</b> inflammation (otitis media, appendicitis, hepatitis). Redness and swelling alone don’t prove infection; <b>pus, fever, a rising WBC with a left shift</b>, or a positive culture point to it.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Innate vs adaptive immunity</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Innate</b>: present at birth, <b>nonspecific</b>, <b>immediate</b>, the same every time, no memory, can’t be transferred (barriers, inflammation, complement, NK cells). <b>Adaptive</b>: <b>learned</b>, <b>specific</b> to one antigen, slower the first time but <b>faster and stronger on re-exposure</b> (memory), can be partly transferred as antibodies (AMI by <b>B cells</b>, CMI by <b>T cells</b>).</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Active vs passive immunity</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Active</b>: your own immune system <b>makes</b> the antibodies — after the illness (<b>natural</b>; the strongest and longest) or after a <b>vaccine</b> (<b>artificial</b>; lasts years with <b>boosters</b>). Takes days to weeks to develop; leaves <b>memory</b>. <b>Passive</b>: <b>ready-made antibodies</b> from someone else — across the <b>placenta</b> or in <b>breast milk</b> (natural), or by <b>injection</b> of immune globulin or <b>convalescent plasma</b> (artificial). Works <b>immediately</b> but is <b>short-lived</b> (the foreign antibodies are cleared) and leaves <b>no memory</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">IgM vs IgG</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>IgM</b> is the <b>first</b> antibody a newly sensitized B cell makes — a five-unit molecule with <b>10 binding sites</b>, good at clumping, so it signals a <b>new or recent</b> infection. <b>IgG</b> is made in large amounts on <b>re-exposure</b> — the <b>most abundant</b> antibody and the basis of <b>long-term protection</b> (and the one that crosses the placenta). A positive IgM means “<b>now</b>”; IgG alone means “<b>before</b>.”</div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">The <b>WBC count and differential</b> — total <b>5000–10,000/mm³</b>, <b>segmented neutrophils 55–70%</b>, <b>bands about 5%</b> (more = <b>left shift</b>), lymphocytes 20–40%, monocytes 2–8%, <b>eosinophils 1–4%</b> (high with <b>allergy, asthma, parasites</b>), basophils 0.5–1% — and the <b>absolute neutrophil count</b>; the <b>five cardinal signs</b> at any wound or site; <b>temperature</b> (absent fever in older adults doesn’t rule out infection); <b>pus</b> and wound changes (photograph, document); <b>mental status and function</b> in older adults; <b>vaccination history</b> and boosters (influenza, pneumococcal, shingles, tetanus and pertussis).</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A <b>left shift</b> or falling <b>ANC</b>; <b>pus</b> or spreading redness at a wound (take the <b>temperature</b>, document and photograph, notify the provider); <b>new confusion, falls or decline</b> in an older adult even with a normal temperature and WBC; signs of <b>sepsis</b> (fever or low temperature, tachycardia, hypotension, low urine); a deep, dirty wound in someone <b>never vaccinated against tetanus</b> (needs antibodies now and the vaccine series).</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why inflammation looks the way it does</b> — <b>histamine</b> dilates arterioles and narrows small veins, so blood <b>pools</b> in the capillaries (<b>redness, warmth</b>); <b>kinins</b> (especially bradykinin) open gaps in capillary walls so <b>plasma leaks</b> out (<b>swelling</b> — a protective cushion — and <b>pain</b> from pressure and kinins); cytokines such as <b>IL-1 and TNF</b> reset the brain’s thermostat (<b>fever</b>). Swelling and pain make the part harder to use (<b>lost function</b>), which rests it.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The neutrophil supply line</b> — it takes about <b>12–14 days</b> to grow a neutrophil, yet each lives only <b>12–18 hours</b> and can phagocytize <b>once</b> — so a healthy marrow releases <b>over 100 billion a day</b>, and colony-stimulating factors (<b>G-CSF, GM-CSF</b>) can cut maturation to hours in an emergency. When infection outruns supply, the marrow releases <b>immature bands</b> that can’t phagocytize: a <b>left shift</b> means the defense is losing ground.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why the second exposure is different</b> — the first time, a few B cells must be found, sensitized and multiplied — days of illness while <b>IgM</b> slowly rises. They leave <b>memory cells</b>; the next time, memory cells divide at once and flood the blood with <b>IgG</b> (the <b>anamnestic response</b>), so the invader is often cleared before illness starts. <b>Vaccines</b> create this memory without the disease; <b>boosters</b> top it up as it fades.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why tissue type matters</b> — <b>HLAs</b> are inherited from both parents, so only an <b>identical twin</b> matches exactly. Another person’s cells read as <b>non-self</b>: cytotoxic T cells and NK cells attack a <b>transplant</b> as they would a virus-infected cell. That is why donors are <b>HLA-matched</b> as closely as possible and recipients need <b>immunosuppression</b> — which, in turn, weakens their defense against infection.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Immunity is a balance between <b>attack and restraint</b>: too little and infections and cancers win; too much or misdirected and the body attacks harmless things (allergy) or itself (autoimmunity). Regulatory T cells, self-tolerance and cytokines keep the balance — and many drugs work by pushing it one way or the other.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","ch":"16","chTitle":"Inflammation and immunity","title":"How immunity is gained: innate and adaptive, active and passive","kind":"topic","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Two systems working together</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Innate (general) immunity</b> — present at birth, <b>nonspecific</b>, <b>immediate</b>, short-term, with <b>no memory</b> and can’t be passed to another person: <b>skin, cornea, mucous membranes</b> of the airways, gut and genitourinary tract; antimicrobial chemicals on the skin; <b>inflammation</b>; <b>complement</b>; <b>natural killer cells</b>. It keeps most organisms out and attacks those that get in.</div>\n<div><b>The microbiome</b> — the <b>normal flora</b> in the mouth, gut, nose and sinuses, vagina and on the skin — harmless while they stay in their own places — <b>support immunity</b> and cellular regulation by crowding out pathogens.</div>\n<div><b>Adaptive (acquired) immunity</b> — learned after exposure and <b>specific</b> to one antigen: <b>antibody-mediated (humoral) immunity</b> by <b>B cells</b>, and <b>cell-mediated immunity</b> by <b>T cells</b> (and NK cells). It is slower the first time but leaves <b>memory</b> for a faster, stronger response later — and it needs innate immunity to get started.</div>\n<div><b>Immunocompetence and infection</b> — <b>immunocompetent</b> means all parts work and protection is maximal. <b>Infection</b> is invasion by pathogens that <b>multiply</b> and cause disease. Immunity can be weakened <b>temporarily or permanently</b> by disease, injury and treatments — or act <b>too strongly or at the wrong time</b> (hypersensitivity and autoimmunity, Chapter 17).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Innate immunity buys time; adaptive immunity wins the war and remembers it. Most invasions never cause illness because the first line holds — and when it doesn’t, the second line is already being trained.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Four ways to be immune (Table 16.1)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Natural active</b> — catching the disease (for example <b>varicella</b>) and recovering — the body makes its own antibodies and memory. The <b>most effective and longest-lasting</b> kind.</div>\n<div><b>Artificial active (vaccine-induced)</b> — a <b>killed or weakened (attenuated)</b> organism or its parts given by vaccine (tetanus, diphtheria, polio, varicella) — the body makes its own antibodies without the disease. Lasts years but needs <b>boosters</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Natural passive</b> — antibodies from the mother cross the <b>placenta</b> to the fetus or reach the infant in <b>colostrum and breast milk</b> — immediate but temporary.</div>\n<div><b>Artificial passive</b> — <b>injected antibodies</b> made by another person or animal — <b>immune globulin</b> after exposure to <b>rabies, tetanus, snake venom</b> or other serious diseases, or <b>convalescent plasma</b> from people who recovered from COVID-19. Works <b>at once</b>, but the foreign antibodies are <b>cleared quickly</b> and <b>no memory</b> forms.</div>\n<div><b>Putting them together: a tetanus-prone wound</b> — a deep, dirty puncture wound in someone <b>never vaccinated</b> against tetanus: first an injection of <b>antitetanus antibodies</b> (passive — protection <b>now</b>), then a few weeks later the <b>tetanus toxoid vaccine</b> to start the person’s <b>own</b> antibodies (active — protection <b>later</b>).</div>\n<div><b>Convalescent plasma</b> — plasma rich in <b>anti–COVID-19 antibodies</b> can lessen the severity and length of infection; it helps most for people with <b>severe</b> COVID-19 or at <b>high risk</b> of a severe course, ideally within <b>72 hours</b> of symptom onset.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Active immunity takes time but lasts because the body keeps the recipe (memory cells); passive immunity is instant but borrowed — the body sees the donated antibodies as foreign and clears them. That is why passive protection is used to <b>bridge</b> a dangerous gap until active immunity can develop.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","ch":"16","chTitle":"Inflammation and immunity","title":"Self vs non-self, immune cells and the WBC count","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing self</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>HLAs — the “product code”</b> — <b>human leukocyte antigens</b> (also called histocompatibility or class I antigens) sit on almost all body cells and define a person’s <b>tissue type</b>. They are inherited from both parents and are <b>identical only in an identical twin</b>. To another person’s immune system they are <b>antigens</b> — proteins that trigger an immune response.</div>\n<div><b>Self-tolerance</b> — immune cells compare HLAs at every encounter: a perfect match is <b>self</b> and left alone; anything else — <b>infected cells, cancer cells, other people’s cells, invaders</b> — is <b>non-self</b> and is neutralized, destroyed or eliminated. <b>Regulatory T cells</b> maintain this tolerance; when it fails, the body attacks itself.</div>\n<div><b>Toll-like receptors</b> — receptors on immune cells (shared with animals, insects and plants) that recognize the surfaces of <b>microorganisms</b> — and <b>injured or stressed self cells</b> — so the right defense is triggered.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Without self-tolerance the immune system’s weapons would be turned on the body itself; without non-self recognition it couldn’t find invaders. Both depend on reading the same surface code.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Where immune cells come from (Table 16.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Stem cells and growth factors</b> — the <b>bone marrow</b> makes <b>pluripotent stem cells</b> that can become any blood or immune cell; <b>growth factors</b> steer them (for example <b>erythropoietin</b> → red cells). Some mature in the marrow; others mature elsewhere (T cells in the <b>thymus</b>).</div>\n<div><b>Granulocytes and agranulocytes</b> — <b>granulocytes</b> have large cytoplasmic granules: <b>neutrophils, basophils, eosinophils</b> (and tissue <b>mast cells</b>). <b>Agranulocytes</b> — <b>monocytes/macrophages and lymphocytes</b> — have only fine granules.</div>\n<div><b>Who does what</b> — inflammation: <b>neutrophils</b> (nonspecific phagocytosis), <b>macrophages</b> (phagocytosis, antigen presentation, help for AMI and CMI), <b>monocytes</b> (become macrophages), <b>eosinophils</b> (allergy and parasites), <b>basophils</b> (histamine, kinins, heparin). AMI: <b>B cells</b>, <b>plasma cells</b> (secrete antibody), <b>memory cells</b>. CMI: <b>helper T</b>, <b>cytotoxic T</b>, <b>regulatory T</b>, <b>natural killer</b> cells.</div>\n<div><b>What leukocytes do</b> — recognize <b>self vs non-self</b>; <b>destroy</b> invaders, debris and abnormal self cells; <b>make antibodies</b>; activate <b>complement</b>; maintain <b>self-tolerance</b>; and make <b>cytokines</b> that boost production and activity of other white cells.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Each white cell type is specialized, so the <b>differential</b> — which types are high or low — tells far more than the total count: bacteria raise neutrophils, allergies and parasites raise eosinophils, and a shortage of mature neutrophils means vulnerability.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The WBC count and differential (Table 16.3)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Normal values</b> — total WBC <b>5000–10,000/mm³</b>; <b>segmented neutrophils 55–70%</b> (2500–7000); <b>bands about 5%</b> (250–500); <b>lymphocytes 20–40%</b> (1000–4000); <b>monocytes 2–8%</b> (100–800); <b>eosinophils 1–4%</b> (50–400); <b>basophils 0.5–1%</b> (25–100).</div>\n<div><b>Absolute neutrophil count</b> — only <b>mature</b> neutrophils can phagocytize, so the <b>number of mature circulating neutrophils (ANC)</b> measures resistance to infection: <b>the higher, the better protected; the lower, the more susceptible</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Left shift (bandemia)</b> — in severe or prolonged infection (sepsis) the marrow can’t keep pace and releases <b>immature bands</b> — the differential “shifts left” toward young cells on the maturation diagram. Bands <b>can’t phagocytize</b>, so a left shift signals a <b>struggling defense</b> and <b>sepsis risk</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Eosinophils up</b> — <b>eosinophilia</b> with <b>allergic</b> reactions (allergic rhinitis, asthma, atopic dermatitis, chronic hives), <b>parasites</b>, and <b>transplant rejection</b>; very high counts can cause or worsen allergic responses. With hay fever, expect <b>eosinophils</b> — not bands or basophils — to rise.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A normal total WBC can hide a problem in its parts — a high eosinophil count in an allergic patient, or a normal count with poor neutrophil function in an older adult. Read each line of the differential against its own range.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the","ch":"16","chTitle":"Inflammation and immunity","title":"Inflammation: cells, phagocytosis and the three stages","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The cells of inflammation</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Neutrophils</b> — <b>55–70%</b> of white cells; mature in the marrow in <b>12–14 days</b>, live <b>12–18 hours</b>, so <b>over 100 billion</b> are released daily. Mature ones are <b>segs</b> (PMNs, “polys”); immature ones are <b>bands</b> (stabs). Each can phagocytize only <b>once</b> — the army is huge but short-lived.</div>\n<div><b>Macrophages</b> — start as <b>monocytes</b> in the blood and mature in tissues (many in the <b>liver, spleen, gut</b>), some fixed, some mobile. They <b>phagocytize repeatedly</b>, live long, help <b>repair</b>, <b>present antigens</b> to T cells and secrete <b>cytokines</b> — linking inflammation to AMI and CMI.</div>\n<div><b>Basophils and mast cells</b> — basophils (~<b>1%</b>) and tissue <b>mast cells</b> carry <b>IgE</b> on their surface; when an allergen binds, they release <b>vasoactive amines</b>: <b>histamine</b> (dilates arterioles, narrows small veins — blood pools), <b>kinins</b> (leaky capillaries — “vascular leak”), <b>heparin</b> (slows clotting), serotonin and leukotrienes. They drive both general and <b>allergic</b> inflammation.</div>\n<div><b>Eosinophils</b> — <b>1–4%</b>; most active against <b>parasitic larvae</b>; prolong later inflammation; rise in <b>allergy</b> and can trigger <b>asthma</b> attacks in allergic asthma.</div>\n<div><b>Complement</b> — <b>20 inactive plasma proteins</b> that, once triggered (by IgG or IgM), activate one another in a cascade, stick to an antigen and form a <b>membrane attack complex</b> — making phagocytosis and destruction far more efficient.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Inflammation’s cells split the work: <b>mast cells and basophils</b> open the vessels, <b>neutrophils</b> arrive in numbers to kill, and <b>macrophages</b> clean up and call in the specific defenses. The visible signs are the vessels opening; the invisible part is the cells moving in.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Phagocytosis and the three stages</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Seven steps of phagocytosis</b> — (1) <b>exposure/invasion</b>; (2) <b>attraction</b> by chemotaxins from damaged tissue; (3) <b>adherence</b>, helped by <b>opsonins</b> (antibodies, complement, dead neutrophils) that coat the target; (4) <b>recognition</b> as non-self; (5) <b>ingestion</b> (engulfing); (6) <b>phagosome formation</b> — granules release enzymes; (7) <b>degradation</b> — the target is digested.</div>\n<div><b>Stage I — vascular</b> — injured tissue, leukocytes and mast cells release <b>histamine, serotonin and kinins</b> (especially bradykinin): <b>arterioles dilate</b>, small veins narrow → <b>redness, warmth</b> (more nutrients delivered); <b>capillaries leak</b> → <b>swelling and pain</b> (the fluid cushions the area). Lasts <b>24–72 hours</b>. Resident <b>macrophages</b> act first and secrete <b>colony-stimulating factor</b>, cutting neutrophil production from <b>14 days to hours</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Stage II — cellular exudate</b> — <b>neutrophilia</b> within about <b>12 hours</b>; <b>pus</b> (dead WBCs, dead tissue, fluid) forms. The <b>arachidonic acid cascade</b> begins: <b>cyclooxygenase (COX)</b> turns AA into mediators (prostaglandins, leukotrienes, histamine, serotonin, kinins) that sustain inflammation — the step <b>anti-inflammatory drugs</b> block. If infection lasts more than a few days, <b>immature neutrophils</b> appear and <b>sepsis risk</b> rises.</div>\n<div><b>Stage III — repair</b> — begins at the moment of injury: healthy cells divide to <b>replace</b> tissue; where cells can’t divide, <b>new vessels and scar</b> form. <b>Scar never works like the original</b> — after an MI, scar replacing 20% of the left ventricle cuts its pumping by <b>at least 20%</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Five cardinal signs</b> — <b>warmth, redness, swelling, pain and decreased function</b> — the same whatever the cause (a scald and an ear infection look alike at the tissue level); their degree depends on the <b>intensity, severity and duration</b> of the trigger, from a splinter to a <b>systemic</b> response after a large burn.</div>\n<div><b>Inflammation without infection</b> — joint <b>sprains, MI, blisters</b> (injury) and <b>allergic rhinitis, contact dermatitis</b> (noninfectious invasion) inflame without germs; <b>otitis media, appendicitis, viral hepatitis</b> are infections that inflame. <b>Inflammation alone gives no lasting immunity</b> — lymphocytes do that.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Because inflammation runs the same program for every trigger, its signs tell you <b>that</b> something is wrong and <b>where</b>, not <b>what</b>. The cause — germ, injury or allergen — comes from the history, cultures and the differential.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"16-antibody-mediated-immunity","ch":"16","chTitle":"Inflammation and immunity","title":"Antibody-mediated immunity","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">From B cell to antibody</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Where B cells mature</b> — B cells start as stem cells in the <b>bone marrow</b> (primary lymphoid tissue) and mature in <b>secondary lymphoid tissues</b> — the <b>spleen</b>, parts of the <b>lymph nodes</b>, the <b>tonsils</b> and the <b>intestinal mucosa</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Exposure and recognition</b> — antigens must arrive in <b>large enough numbers</b> to slip past or overwhelm innate defenses. <b>Macrophages</b> attach to the (opsonized) antigen and <b>present</b> it to <b>helper T cells</b>; together they process it to expose its recognition sites, and the helper T cell brings it to an unsensitized B cell.</div>\n<div><b>Sensitization</b> — the B cell recognizes the antigen as non-self and becomes <b>sensitized</b> — to that <b>one</b> antigen, for life, as are all its descendants. It divides into <b>plasma cells</b> (start making antibody <b>at once</b> — up to <b>300 molecules a second</b>) and <b>memory cells</b> (wait for the next exposure).</div>\n<div><b>Antibodies travel</b> — antibodies leave the cell and circulate in the blood and body fluids (“humors” — hence <b>humoral</b> immunity). Because they are free, they can be <b>transferred</b> to another person for <b>immediate, short-term</b> protection.</div>\n<div><b>Memory (sustained immunity)</b> — on re-exposure, <b>memory cells</b> divide quickly into plasma cells that pour out the specific antibody — the <b>anamnestic response</b> — so the person usually <b>doesn’t get sick again</b>. Without it, every exposure would mean a new illness.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Antibody immunity is a cooperative effort: the macrophage finds and shows the antigen, the helper T cell organizes, and the B cell makes the weapon. That is why losing <b>helper T cells</b> (as in HIV) cripples antibody responses too.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What antibodies do and the five classes (Table 16.4)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Antibody shape</b> — a <b>Y-shaped</b> immunoglobulin: the two <b>arm tips (Fab)</b> grip the specific antigen (two antigens, or two spots on one); the <b>stem (Fc)</b> sticks to receptors on white cells, arming them with antibody.</div>\n<div><b>Five actions</b> — <b>agglutination</b> — clumping antigens into immune complexes that phagocytes find easily; <b>lysis</b> — holes in the invader’s membrane (usually with complement); <b>complement fixation</b> (IgG, IgM start the cascade); <b>precipitation</b> — complexes so large they drop out of suspension for removal; <b>inactivation (neutralization)</b> — covering the antigen’s active site to make it harmless.</div>\n<div><b>IgM and IgG</b> — <b>IgM</b> — the <b>first</b> antibody after a new sensitization; a five-unit group with <b>10 binding sites</b>, efficient at clumping and complement; keeps a first illness to days. <b>IgG</b> — made in <b>large amounts on re-exposure</b>, the <b>most abundant</b> antibody, the source of <b>long-term protection</b>; activates complement and boosts phagocytes.</div>\n<div><b>IgA, IgE, IgD</b> — <b>IgA</b> — the <b>secretory</b> antibody of mucous membranes and the gut, guarding the <b>respiratory, GI and genitourinary</b> tracts. <b>IgE</b> — drives <b>immediate hypersensitivity (allergy)</b> and fights <b>parasites (helminths)</b>. <b>IgD</b> — a <b>B-cell antigen receptor</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Immunoglobulins = gamma globulins</b> — all antibodies are <b>immunoglobulins</b> — globular proteins of immunity — also called <b>gamma globulins</b> because they separate in the gamma fraction of plasma proteins.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Antibodies rarely kill on their own — they <b>mark and gather</b> antigens so complement and phagocytes can finish the job. That makes AMI and inflammation partners: each works far better with the other.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","ch":"16","chTitle":"Inflammation and immunity","title":"Cell-mediated immunity and cytokines","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">T cells and NK cells</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Where and how</b> — T-cell stem cells mature in the <b>thymus</b> and parts of the <b>lymph nodes</b>. T cells form <b>clones</b>, each with receptors for <b>one</b> environmental antigen — millions of clones recognize millions of antigens. Subsets are named by surface <b>markers</b> (CD proteins).</div>\n<div><b>Helper T cells (CD4+)</b> — the <b>organizers</b>: when they recognize non-self they secrete <b>cytokines</b> that boost marrow production and the activity of every other part of immunity — inflammation, AMI and CMI.</div>\n<div><b>Regulatory T cells (Tregs)</b> — begin as CD4 cells, carry <b>CD25</b> receptors, and are activated by <b>IL-2</b> and the factor <b>FoxP3</b>; they secrete <b>IL-10</b> to <b>restrain</b> immune cells. They prevent overreactions and antibodies against <b>self</b> — when they fail, inflammation runs unchecked and <b>autoimmune disease</b> develops.</div>\n<div><b>Cytotoxic T cells</b> — recognize <b>self cells carrying foreign antigens</b> — especially cells infected by <b>viruses or protozoa</b> — bind to them, punch a hole and deliver a <b>lethal hit</b> of enzymes. They also attack <b>grafts and transplanted organs</b>. They protect healthy self cells by secreting <b>CTLA-4</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Natural killer cells (CD16+)</b> — kill some non-self cells <b>without prior sensitization</b> — “seek and destroy” — most effectively <b>cancer cells</b> and <b>virus-infected cells</b>; they also attack grafts.</div>\n<div><b>What CMI protects against</b> — CMI best recognizes <b>cancer cells</b> and cells infected by organisms that live <b>inside</b> host cells (especially <b>viruses</b>); it helps prevent cancer and <b>metastasis</b> after carcinogen exposure, and it regulates AMI and inflammation through cytokines.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Antibodies can’t reach a virus hiding inside a cell — only a T cell or NK cell that kills the whole infected cell can. That is why people with weak cell-mediated immunity struggle most with <b>viruses, fungi, intracellular bacteria and cancers</b>.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Cytokines (Table 16.5)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What they are</b> — small <b>hormone-like proteins</b> — <b>monokines</b> (from macrophages, neutrophils, eosinophils, monocytes) and <b>lymphokines</b> (from T cells) — that act on <b>responder cells</b> carrying the matching <b>receptor</b>, nearby or far away. Groups: <b>interleukins</b> (the largest), <b>interferons, colony-stimulating factors, tumor necrosis factors, transforming growth factors</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Proinflammatory</b> — <b>IL-1</b>: <b>fever</b>, inflammation and clotting. <b>IL-2</b>: growth and activation of <b>all T and B cells</b>; boosts NK cells against cancer. <b>IL-5</b>: from helper T cells; increases <b>eosinophils</b>. <b>IL-6</b>: the liver makes fibrinogen and other acute-phase proteins (your book says “protein C”; IL-6 is best known for raising <b>C-reactive protein</b>); more stem cells and B cells. <b>IL-17</b>: more proinflammatory cytokines (G-CSF, GM-CSF), neutrophil-rich inflammation. <b>IL-23</b>: expands <b>Th17</b> cells that direct gut bacterial killing.</div>\n<div><b>Inhibitory</b> — <b>IL-10</b>: from macrophages and Tregs; <b>suppresses</b> proinflammatory cytokines and maintains <b>self-tolerance</b> (the chapter text also lists IL-10 among the proinflammatory cytokines; its table and the Treg section describe it as inhibitory). <b>Tumor necrosis factor</b>: fever, the main cytokine of <b>rheumatoid arthritis</b> damage and of the <b>septic</b> response, graft rejection, cell death, delayed hypersensitivity (your book lists TNF here although it acts largely to inflame).</div>\n<div><b>Growth factors</b> — <b>G-CSF</b> — more and more mature <b>neutrophils</b>; <b>GM-CSF</b> — growth of myeloid stem cells; <b>erythropoietin</b> — <b>red cells</b>; <b>thrombopoietin</b> — <b>platelets</b>. Several are given as drugs (for example to restore neutrophils after chemotherapy).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Cytokines are the immune system’s messages — and messages can be blocked or sent as drugs. That is how <b>TNF blockers</b> calm rheumatoid arthritis and IBD, and how <b>colony-stimulating factors</b> rebuild the marrow after chemotherapy.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"16-immunity-in-older-adults","ch":"16","chTitle":"Inflammation and immunity","title":"Immunity in older adults","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What changes and what to do</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Peak and decline</b> — immunity works best in the <b>20s and 30s</b> and declines slowly; <b>nutrition, environment, drugs and disease</b> also shape it. Older adults lose function in both <b>innate</b> and <b>adaptive</b> immunity — more <b>infections</b> and more <b>autoimmune</b> disease.</div>\n<div><b>Inflammation changes</b> — <b>neutrophil activity</b> falls even when counts are normal; <b>no leukocytosis</b> during acute infection; often <b>no fever</b>; fewer <b>toll-like receptors</b>; the <b>microbiome</b> shifts toward more pathogenic organisms. Minor infections can be <b>missed until the person is septic</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Antibody changes</b> — fewer B cells and slower maturation → poorer response to <b>new</b> antigens: give <b>influenza, pneumococcal and shingles</b> vaccines. Antibody levels from old exposures <b>fade faster</b> and memory cells respond <b>slowly</b>: give <b>boosters</b>, especially <b>tetanus and pertussis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Cell-mediated changes</b> — <b>weaker T-cell function</b> (your book’s table also says circulating T cells decrease, while its text says numbers stay the same) → <b>false-negative TB skin tests</b> and more <b>bacterial and fungal infections</b> of the skin, mucous membranes, lungs and urinary tract. Loss of self-recognition raises <b>autoantibodies</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>An older adult’s first sign of infection is often not fever but a <b>change in thinking, walking or eating</b> — because the inflammation that produces classic signs is muted. The nurse has to look for <b>function</b>, not just temperature.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"16-adpie","ch":"16","chTitle":"Inflammation and immunity","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">For immunity the nurse asks: <b>are the defenses intact</b> (history, drugs, age, vaccines), <b>is there inflammation or infection</b> (cardinal signs, temperature, the differential and ANC, pus), and <b>who won’t show it</b> (older adults, immunosuppressed) — then prevents infection, catches it early and keeps immunity current.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div>recurrent or recent <b>infections</b>, <b>vaccination</b> history and boosters, chronic disease, <b>drugs that suppress immunity</b> (steroids, chemotherapy, transplant drugs), nutrition, age, <b>allergies</b>, recent wounds or exposures (e.g., influenza in a dorm).</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical and labs</div>the <b>five cardinal signs</b> at any site, <b>wound drainage (pus)</b>, temperature (may be absent), vital signs, <b>mental status and function</b> in older adults; the <b>WBC count with differential</b>, <b>ANC</b>, <b>bands (left shift)</b>, <b>eosinophils</b>; cultures as ordered.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Because inflammation can be muted, the lab differential and the patient’s <b>change from baseline</b> are often more telling than a thermometer.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div><b>risk for infection</b> from reduced immunity (aging, drugs, disease, low ANC); <b>potential for sepsis</b> when infection outruns the defenses (left shift); inflammation causing <b>pain and lost function</b>; incomplete <b>immunization</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>no new infection — or infection <b>found early</b>; WBC and differential returning to normal ranges; wounds healing without pus; <b>vaccines and boosters up to date</b>; the patient knows the signs to report and how to protect against infection.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Prevent</div><b>hand hygiene</b>, aseptic technique, intact skin and mucous membranes, good nutrition; <b>vaccinate</b> (influenza, pneumococcal, shingles for older adults; tetanus and pertussis <b>boosters</b>); after a tetanus-prone wound in an unvaccinated person, <b>antitoxin now and the vaccine series</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Detect and respond</div>check a draining wound: <b>temperature</b>, <b>photograph and document</b>, notify the provider (cultures, antibiotics as prescribed) — don’t seal it under an occlusive dressing or swab it with harsh antiseptics; report a <b>left shift, low ANC</b> or signs of <b>sepsis</b>; in older adults, treat <b>new confusion or decline</b> as possible infection.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Teach and handle drugs safely</div>teach that <b>vaccines need boosters</b>, how to recognize infection, and that <b>inflammation isn’t always infection</b>; follow special <b>handling precautions</b> for immunosuppressant drugs.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>temperature and vital signs back to baseline; inflammation <b>subsiding</b> (less redness, swelling, pain; function returning); <b>WBC and differential normalizing</b> (fewer bands, ANC recovering); wound clean and healing; vaccinations <b>current</b>; the patient states the signs of infection to report.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"17-overview","ch":"17","chTitle":"Allergy and immunity conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">17 · Chapter 17</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Immunity · inflammation · gas exchange · infection</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Allergy and immunity conditions</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> When immunity misfires, it goes wrong in one of three directions. <b>Too much</b>: a <b>hypersensitivity</b> (allergy). There are four types, from the <b>IgE-driven type I</b>, which strikes within minutes (hives, <b>angioedema</b>, <b>anaphylaxis</b>), to the <b>T-cell–driven type IV</b>, which appears after 12–72 hours (contact dermatitis). <b>Misdirected</b>: <b>autoimmunity</b>, where the body attacks itself. The exemplar is <b>systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)</b>, whose immune complexes damage skin, joints, <b>kidneys</b> and <b>blood vessels</b>. <b>Transplant rejection</b> belongs here too: the body attacks a graft it needs. <b>Too little</b>: <b>HIV</b>, a retrovirus that destroys <b>CD4+ helper T cells</b> until <b>opportunistic infections</b> take hold (stage III, AIDS). The nurse rescues the <b>airway</b> with <b>epinephrine first</b>, protects the organs in lupus, prevents HIV transmission and infection, and keeps transplant drugs and <b>Lyme</b> prevention on track.</p><div id=\"ch17-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Four kinds of overreaction</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A <b>hypersensitivity</b> is an overactive immune response to an <b>allergen</b>, usually after an earlier exposure. There are four types. <b>Type I</b> (<b>IgE</b> on mast cells, <b>within an hour</b>): hives, wheeze, angioedema, anaphylaxis. <b>Type II</b> (<b>IgG</b> destroys tagged self cells, minutes to hours): a <b>wrong-blood transfusion</b>. <b>Type III</b> (<b>immune complexes</b> lodge in small vessels, 4–10 days): <b>lupus, rheumatoid arthritis</b>. <b>Type IV</b> (<b>sensitized T cells</b>, <b>12–72 hours</b>): <b>contact dermatitis</b>. Corticosteroids help type IV; antihistamines don’t.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · Airway emergencies</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Angioedema</b> is a deep swelling of the lips, tongue, face and larynx, most often from <b>ACE inhibitors</b> (bradykinin builds up). It can start even years into therapy. <b>Anaphylaxis</b> is systemic: the vessels dilate and the bronchioles constrict within <b>seconds to minutes</b>. <b>Delay in giving epinephrine</b> is the main cause of death. Call the Rapid Response Team, stop any suspect infusion and give <b>IM epinephrine</b> (repeat every <b>5–15 minutes</b>). Lay the patient flat with the <b>legs raised</b>, give <b>nonrebreather oxygen at 90–100%</b> and a <b>rapid normal saline</b> infusion. Antihistamines and steroids are add-ons.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · Attacking self (lupus)</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">In <b>autoimmunity</b>, self-tolerance fails. It affects <b>three times as many women</b> as men, and genes plus a trigger set it off. In <b>SLE</b>, autoantibodies against nuclear proteins form <b>type III complexes</b> throughout the body. They cause a <b>butterfly rash</b>, <b>photosensitivity</b> and <b>arthritis without deformity</b>. They damage the glomeruli (<b>lupus nephritis</b>, foamy urine) and vessel walls (<b>vasculitis</b>), and they inflame the heart (<b>pericarditis</b>). The disease <b>flares and remits</b>, and <b>UV light</b> worsens every part of it. Kidney and heart disease are the top causes of death.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · HIV: from infection to AIDS</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>HIV</b> docks on <b>CD4+ T cells</b>. Its <b>reverse transcriptase</b> writes the virus’s RNA into the cell’s DNA, and the cell becomes a virus factory. <b>Stage I</b> (acute) is a flulike illness within about <b>4 weeks</b>, with a very high viral load. <b>Stage II</b> (chronic) is controlled by treatment. <b>Stage III (AIDS)</b> means a <b>CD4+ count &lt; 200 cells/mm³</b> or an <b>AIDS-defining</b> opportunistic infection or cancer. Examples are <b>PCP, TB, MAC, Candida, toxoplasmosis</b> and <b>Kaposi sarcoma</b>. Once given, the stage III diagnosis is <b>permanent</b>.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · HIV: prevent, test, treat</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Prevent</b>: <b>safer sex</b> (a new condom for every act), <b>syringe services</b>, <b>PrEP</b> before exposure, and <b>PEP</b> within <b>72 hours</b> of an exposure, taken for <b>28 days</b>. <b>TasP</b> keeps an infected partner’s viral load undetectable. <b>Test</b>: there is a <b>3-week window</b>, and the <b>rapid test</b> is recommended. Track the <b>CD4+ count</b> (normal <b>600–1500</b>) and the <b>viral load</b>. <b>Treat</b>: <b>combination antiretroviral therapy (cART)</b> controls the virus but doesn’t kill it. <b>Missed doses breed permanent resistance.</b> <b>IRIS</b> can flare 1–12 weeks after starting; cART continues.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · Rejection and Lyme</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Transplant rejection</b>: <b>hyperacute</b> rejection (minutes; preformed antibodies) can’t be stopped, and the organ is removed. <b>Acute</b> rejection (1 week–3 months) is common and treatable. <b>Chronic</b> rejection (months–years) scars the graft. Recipients take <b>lifelong immunosuppressants</b>. Teach infection risk and <b>no grapefruit</b> with cyclosporine and sirolimus, and <b>flulike symptoms → emergency</b>. <b>Lyme disease</b> comes from a <b>deer tick</b> bite. Stage 1 brings a <b>bull’s-eye rash</b> and is treated with doxycycline. Later stages affect the joints, heart and brain. Prevent it with <b>DEET</b>, tucked-in clothing and tick checks.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">After an allergen: a <b>lump in the throat</b>, <b>hoarseness, stridor</b>, <b>inability to swallow</b>, wheeze or <b>hypotension</b> → <b>IM epinephrine now</b>. In HIV, report <b>at once</b> any change in <b>level of consciousness, pupils or limb strength</b>, a seizure, or signs of <b>TB</b> (start <b>Airborne Precautions</b>). After a transplant, <b>fever &gt; 100°F (37.8°C)</b> or <b>flulike symptoms</b> need emergency care. So do a <b>dry cough</b> in a lung recipient and <b>falling urine</b> in a kidney recipient.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">HIV docks on <b>CD4+ T cells</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span><b>reverse transcriptase</b> writes its genes into the cell’s DNA<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>the cell makes <b>new virus</b> instead of directing immunity<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>CD4+ count <b>falls</b>, viral load <b>rises</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>below <b>200 cells/mm³</b>, normally harmless organisms <b>overgrow</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span><b>opportunistic infections</b> and cancers → <b>stage III (AIDS)</b></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Angioedema vs anaphylaxis</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Angioedema</b> is a <b>deep, local</b> swelling of the lips, tongue, face or larynx. It often comes from <b>ACE inhibitors</b> through <b>bradykinin</b> and can begin <b>days to years</b> into therapy; the danger is the <b>airway</b>. <b>Anaphylaxis</b> is <b>systemic</b> and comes <b>seconds to minutes</b> after an allergen: all vessels dilate (<b>hypotension, shock</b>) and the bronchioles constrict (<b>wheeze</b>). In both, secure the airway first; anaphylaxis also needs <b>IM epinephrine at once</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Type I vs type IV</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Type I</b> runs through <b>IgE</b> on mast cells and starts <b>within an hour</b>: hives, wheeze, angioedema, anaphylaxis. <b>Antihistamines and epinephrine</b> work. <b>Type IV</b> runs through <b>sensitized T cells</b> and appears <b>12–72 hours</b> later at the <b>contact site</b>: red, firm, swollen skin (poison ivy, metals). <b>Corticosteroids</b> help, <b>antihistamines don’t</b>, and it fades over 5–7 days.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">HIV vs AIDS (stage III)</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>HIV infection</b> means the virus is present, at any stage: stage I (acute, flulike, very contagious) or stage II (chronic, controlled with cART). <b>Stage III (AIDS)</b> is diagnosed only with a <b>CD4+ count &lt; 200 cells/mm³</b> or an <b>AIDS-defining</b> infection or cancer, and the label is <b>permanent</b>. Everyone with AIDS has HIV, but most treated people with HIV never reach stage III.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">PrEP vs PEP</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>PrEP</b> is taken by an HIV-<b>negative</b> person at ongoing risk, <b>before</b> exposure. It gives about <b>99%</b> protection from sexual transmission, with HIV tests every <b>3 months</b> and condoms. <b>PEP</b> is an <b>emergency</b> course after <b>one</b> possible exposure: start <b>within 72 hours</b> (ideally within 2), take it for <b>28 days</b>, and test before and after. Neither is for people who already have HIV; for them, treatment itself prevents spread (<b>TasP</b>).</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Hyperacute vs acute vs chronic rejection</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Hyperacute</b> rejection comes within <b>minutes to hours</b>: <b>preexisting antibodies</b> clot the graft. It <b>can’t be stopped</b>, the organ is removed, and it is now rare. <b>Acute</b> rejection comes at <b>1 week to 3 months</b> (or later), through antibodies and T cells; it is <b>common and treatable</b> with more immunosuppression. <b>Chronic</b> rejection develops over <b>months to years</b>: scarring and narrowed vessels slowly destroy function.</div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">In <b>allergic reactions</b>, watch the airway, <b>stridor</b>, breath sounds, <b>SpO₂</b>, BP (systolic <b>&lt; 90</b> or a <b>30%</b> drop) and pulse, plus swelling of the lips and tongue. In <b>lupus</b>, check the skin (<b>butterfly rash</b>, discoid lesions, mouth ulcers), joints, temperature and fatigue. Check the <b>urine</b> (foam, blood), <b>creatinine</b>, urine protein and BP, plus heart and lung signs and the <b>CBC</b>. Patients on hydroxychloroquine need <b>eye exams every 6 months</b>; patients on immunosuppressants need watching for signs of <b>infection</b>. In <b>HIV</b>, follow the <b>CD4+ count</b> (normal <b>600–1500</b>; <b>&lt; 200</b> = high risk of opportunistic infection), the <b>viral load</b>, the CBC and <b>ANC</b>, and the temperature. Inspect the mouth every shift, the lungs every 8 hours, and the skin, including the <b>perirectal</b> area. Watch weight (a loss of <b>≥ 10%</b> = wasting), <b>neuro status</b>, adherence and drug toxicity. In <b>transplant</b> recipients, watch for organ-specific rejection signs (<b>Table 17.5</b>), weight, urine output, glucose, BP, kidney and liver function, lipids and infection.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">After an allergen, call the <b>Rapid Response Team</b> and give <b>IM epinephrine</b> now for a <b>lump in the throat</b>, <b>hoarseness, stridor</b>, <b>inability to swallow</b>, wheeze or <b>hypotension</b>. Act at once on lip or tongue swelling in anyone taking an <b>ACE inhibitor</b>. In <b>HIV</b>, act on any change in <b>level of consciousness, pupils or limb strength</b>, a <b>seizure</b>, signs of <b>TB</b> (start <b>Airborne Precautions</b>) or fever with a <b>CD4+ count &lt; 200</b>. Report fever, rash or aches in the <b>first 6 weeks of abacavir</b>, and rash, jaundice or abdominal pain on <b>maraviroc</b>. In <b>lupus</b>, act on <b>blurred vision</b> with hydroxychloroquine, a patient <b>unable to take</b> prescribed corticosteroids, fever <b>&gt; 100°F (37.8°C)</b> on immunosuppressants, foamy urine or chest pain. After a <b>transplant</b>, <b>flulike symptoms</b>, fever <b>&gt; 100°F (37.8°C)</b>, falling urine output, rapid weight gain or a <b>dry cough</b> in a lung recipient need <b>emergency</b> evaluation.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why epinephrine comes first</b> — anaphylaxis runs on <b>many mediators</b>: histamine, leukotrienes, bradykinin and prostaglandins. Blocking one of them with an <b>antihistamine</b> can’t stop it. <b>Epinephrine</b> reverses the <b>effects</b> instead. It <b>constricts</b> the dilated vessels (BP rises), <b>strengthens</b> the heartbeat and <b>dilates</b> the bronchioles, all within minutes. Steroids act slowly; they serve to prevent a <b>late</b> recurrence.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why ACE inhibitors cause swelling</b> — <b>angiotensin-converting enzyme</b> also <b>breaks down bradykinin</b>, a strong vasodilator. Block the enzyme and bradykinin slowly <b>builds up</b> in the tissues. So angioedema can appear <b>long after</b> the first dose, and drugs aimed at histamine may not work well. The drugs made for <b>hereditary angioedema</b> (icatibant, ecallantide) act on the bradykinin pathway.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why lupus targets kidneys and vessels</b> — lupus autoantibodies bind nuclear antigens and form <b>immune complexes</b>. These circulate and <b>lodge where blood is filtered or flows through small vessels</b>: the <b>glomeruli</b>, skin, joints and vessel walls. There they activate <b>complement</b> and inflammation. In the kidneys this is <b>glomerulonephritis</b>, and protein leaks out (<b>foamy urine</b>); in vessel walls it is <b>vasculitis</b>, which cuts perfusion to the organs.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why the CD4+ count predicts infections</b> — <b>CD4+ helper T cells</b> direct the B cells, cytotoxic T cells and macrophages (Chapter 16). HIV turns them into virus factories, and they die or stop working, so <b>every</b> immune response weakens. Below about <b>200 cells/mm³</b>, organisms the body normally controls overgrow and cause <b>opportunistic infections</b>: <b>Candida, Pneumocystis, Toxoplasma</b> and <b>mycobacteria</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The disorders in this chapter are immunity that goes <b>too far</b> (allergy), turns <b>inward</b> (lupus, rejection) or is knocked <b>out</b> (HIV). The direction of the error tells you the danger (airway, organs or infection) and the treatment.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","ch":"17","chTitle":"Allergy and immunity conditions","title":"Hypersensitivity: the four types","kind":"topic","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Allergens and the immediate reaction</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Allergy and allergens</b> — a <b>hypersensitivity</b> (allergy) is an <b>overactive</b> immune response, with excess inflammation, to an <b>allergen</b>. An allergen is an antigen that troubles only <b>susceptible</b> people, usually after an <b>earlier exposure</b>. Allergens can be <b>inhaled</b> (pollen, mold spores, dander, dust, grass, ragweed), <b>ingested</b> (foods, additives, drugs), <b>injected</b> (venom, drugs, contrast dye) or <b>touched</b> (latex, pollen, foods, proteins).</div>\n<div><b>The tendency is inherited, not the allergy</b> — the <b>predisposition</b> to allergy runs in families, but <b>specific</b> allergies are not inherited. A parent allergic to penicillin may have a child who is allergic to peanuts instead.</div>\n<div><b>How type I works</b> — the <b>first</b> exposure makes allergen-specific <b>IgE</b>, which coats <b>mast cells and basophils</b>; the person is now <b>sensitized</b>. On <b>re-exposure</b>, the allergen links two IgE molecules and the cell <b>degranulates</b>. In the <b>primary phase</b>, <b>histamine</b> and other vasoactive amines cause warmth, swelling, itching, redness and mucus. This lasts about <b>10 minutes</b>, or longer if the allergen stays. In the <b>secondary phase</b>, white cells arrive, and <b>leukotrienes, bradykinin and prostaglandins</b> widen and prolong the reaction.</div>\n<div><b>Local or systemic</b> — the mechanism is the same whether the reaction stays <b>local</b> (runny nose, sneezing, itchy watery red eyes, hives) or becomes <b>anaphylaxis</b>. In anaphylaxis, all the vessels dilate, cardiac output falls and the bronchioles constrict. It is a <b>medical emergency</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A type I reaction needs a <b>previous</b> exposure, because the first meeting only sets the trap: IgE armed on mast cells. So a reaction is still possible to a drug the patient took before without trouble, and every allergy history matters.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The four types (Table 17.1)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Type I — immediate (IgE)</b> — IgE on mast cells meets the antigen and releases mediators, mainly <b>histamine</b>. Onset is <b>within 1 hour</b>. Signs include <b>urticaria</b> (hives), itchy watery eyes, a runny nose, <b>bronchospasm</b>, allergic asthma, <b>angioedema</b> and <b>anaphylaxis</b>. Common triggers are <b>bee venom, drugs, iodine, latex, peanuts, pollen</b> and <b>shellfish</b>; hay fever is type I too. Nursing: <b>rescue at once</b> if anaphylaxis develops.</div>\n<div><b>Type II — cytotoxic (IgG)</b> — <b>IgG</b> autoantibodies bind a self cell that carries a foreign protein, and the cell is destroyed. Onset is <b>minutes to hours</b>. Examples: a <b>hemolytic transfusion reaction</b> (the wrong blood type), <b>drug-induced hemolytic anemia</b>, <b>Goodpasture syndrome</b>, <b>immune thrombocytopenic purpura</b> and <b>hyperacute graft rejection</b>. Linked drugs include penicillin, thiazides, cephalosporins and methyldopa. Nursing: <b>stop the drug or blood at once</b> and give prescribed drugs. Expect possible <b>plasmapheresis</b>, and watch for <b>hemolytic crisis and kidney failure</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Type III — immune complex</b> — excess antigen forms <b>antigen–antibody complexes</b>. They <b>lodge in small vessels</b> of the <b>kidneys, skin and joints</b>, activate complement and cause inflammation. Onset is <b>4–10 days</b>. Examples: <b>rheumatoid arthritis</b>, <b>SLE</b> and <b>serum sickness</b>; most autoimmune diseases are type III. Care is <b>individualized</b> to the disease.</div>\n<div><b>Type IV — delayed (T cells)</b> — sensitized <b>T cells</b> release lymphokines and call in <b>macrophages</b>. Onset is <b>12–72 hours</b>. The site of exposure shows <b>edema, induration (hardening), ischemia and tissue damage</b>, which clear over <b>5–7 days</b>. Examples: <b>contact dermatitis</b> (poison ivy, jewelry metals), local reactions to <b>insect stings</b>, and <b>sarcoidosis</b>. <b>Corticosteroids</b> ease it. <b>Antihistamines don’t</b>, because histamine isn’t the driver, and <b>desensitization doesn’t</b>, because IgE isn’t involved.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Read the type from <b>timing and target</b>. A reaction within the hour is IgE (type I). Destroyed blood cells mean type II. Trouble in the kidneys and joints days later is type III. A firm red patch a day or two after contact is type IV, so a rash <b>48 hours</b> after a hike in the woods is <b>type IV</b>.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Allergy testing</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Skin tests</b> — these are done in the <b>outpatient</b> setting under <b>close supervision</b>, because a test can trigger a reaction. In a <b>skin prick (scratch)</b> test, a tiny amount of allergen is pricked into the skin; it is used for <b>environmental and food</b> allergies. In an <b>intradermal</b> test, a small amount is injected just under the surface; it is used for <b>insect venom and penicillin</b>. <b>Patch tests</b> check contact allergens.</div>\n<div><b>Blood tests</b> — these can help <b>confirm</b> a specific allergy, but they are <b>less specific</b> than skin tests and can give <b>false positives</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Testing deliberately exposes the person to an allergen, so it is done where a reaction can be treated at once.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"17-angioedema","ch":"17","chTitle":"Allergy and immunity conditions","title":"Angioedema","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is and who is at risk</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Deep swelling</b> — <b>angioedema</b> is a severe <b>type I</b> reaction in the blood vessels and <b>all layers</b> of the skin, mucous membranes and the tissue beneath them. Unlike the surface reaction of hay fever, it is a <b>deep</b> swelling, driven especially by <b>bradykinin</b>. It is most common in the <b>lips, face, tongue, larynx and neck</b>. It can also affect the limbs, the genitals or the <b>intestine</b>, where it causes severe cramping pain, nausea and vomiting that are easily mistaken for another acute abdomen.</div>\n<div><b>ACE inhibitors (and NSAIDs)</b> — any drug can cause it, but <b>ACE inhibitors</b> are the most common cause, along with <b>NSAIDs, including aspirin</b>. ACE normally <b>breaks down bradykinin</b>. Blocking the enzyme lets bradykinin <b>build up over time</b>, so the reaction can appear <b>late</b>. The risk is highest in the <b>first 24 hours</b> after the first dose, yet it can start <b>years</b> into therapy. Patients may not link it to a drug they have taken for a long time.</div>\n<div><b>Health equity</b> — <b>Black patients</b> have a <b>three to five times higher</b> rate of ACE-inhibitor angioedema (possibly from a gene variant), and <b>Black women</b> more than Black men. Watch closely after the <b>first dose</b>, and teach the patient to <b>call 911</b> at the first sign.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The cause is <b>bradykinin</b>, not histamine. So ACE-inhibitor angioedema can come on slowly and resist the usual allergy drugs, and the patient may never suspect a blood pressure pill taken for years.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognize and act</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Airway first</b> — time matters. Even <b>lip swelling</b> or a <b>slight itch at the back of the throat</b> can progress quickly to a blocked airway. Ask what drugs the patient takes, especially <b>for blood pressure</b>, then <b>put off the rest of the history</b> until treatment is under way. Later, ask about past episodes (how long they lasted and what helped) and other allergies.</div>\n<div><b>What you see</b> — a <b>deep, firm swelling</b> of the face, lips, tongue and neck. The patient has <b>trouble speaking or drinking</b> (stiff lips), the nose may be swollen, and the face can be so distorted that family may not recognize the person. <b>Inability to swallow</b>, the feeling of a <b>lump in the throat</b>, or <b>stridor</b> means the <b>larynx</b> is involved: act now. The diagnosis is made from the signs; <b>don’t lose time on lab tests</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — severe angioedema is an <b>acute emergency</b>: secure the airway and stop the reaction. Give <b>oxygen by nasal cannula</b> and the usual drugs, <b>epinephrine and corticosteroids</b>. <b>Stridor or inability to swallow</b> means <b>intubation</b>. If intubation isn’t possible, an <b>emergency tracheostomy below the swelling</b> is needed. If standard drugs fail, the hereditary-angioedema drugs may be used: <b>C1 esterase inhibitor</b> and <b>lanadelumab</b> prevent attacks, and <b>ecallantide</b> or <b>icatibant</b> treat an acute attack.</div>\n<div><b>Don’t stop too soon</b> — the drug that caused the swelling often outlasts the drugs that treat it. So treatment may continue for <b>hours</b> after the swelling resolves; then keep the <b>IV access</b> and monitor for <b>2 to 6 hours</b>. If the swelling doesn’t come back, the patient usually goes home. Teach the <b>cause</b>, how to <b>avoid it</b>, and to seek <b>emergency care</b> at the first sign.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Swelling spreads inward from the lips to the larynx, and once the larynx closes the airway is lost. So the nurse acts on early signs and watches for a <b>rebound</b> after treatment wears off.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"17-anaphylaxis","ch":"17","chTitle":"Allergy and immunity conditions","title":"Anaphylaxis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Prevention and the autoinjector (Boxes 17.1 and 17.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — the most <b>life-threatening</b> type I reaction. Within <b>seconds to minutes</b> of exposure, all the vessels <b>dilate</b>, cardiac output <b>falls</b> and the bronchioles <b>constrict</b>. The main factor in deaths is <b>delay in giving epinephrine</b>. In the hospital, the usual causes are <b>drugs and contrast dye</b>; in the community, <b>foods and insect stings</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Prevention</b> — teach the patient to <b>avoid known allergens</b>, wear a <b>medical alert bracelet</b> and tell every health care worker about the allergy. Some patients carry an <b>emergency kit</b> (epinephrine, <b>H1 and H2 antihistamines, albuterol</b>). Many carry a spring-loaded <b>epinephrine autoinjector</b>, and they should carry <b>two</b>, in case a second dose is needed. An <b>IM</b> injection appears to work better than a subcutaneous one.</div>\n<div><b>Caring for the autoinjector (Box 17.1)</b> — practice with the <b>trainer</b> device. Keep the device <b>with you always</b>, and tell family where it is. Protect it from <b>light and temperature extremes</b>. Carry it in its case with the <b>safety release on</b> until use. Replace it if it has <b>expired</b> (or nearly has), if the drug is <b>discolored</b>, or if the safety release is <b>loose</b>. Use it at <b>any</b> symptom, <b>before calling 911</b>, then call 911 and go to the hospital. It is better to use it when it wasn’t needed than to need it and not use it.</div>\n<div><b>Using it (Box 17.2)</b> — remove the safety release and inject into the <b>middle of the outer thigh</b> at <b>90 degrees</b>. Injecting <b>through clothing</b> is fine; avoid seams and pockets. Listen for the <b>click</b> and <b>hold for 3 seconds</b>, then remove the device and <b>massage the site for 10 seconds</b>. Get <b>medical care right away, even if you feel better</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Beyond the book</b> — in 2024 the FDA approved an <b>epinephrine nasal spray</b> for people who can’t or won’t use a needle. Your book teaches the <b>autoinjector</b>, so answer exam questions with the injector.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Epinephrine works only if it is <b>at hand and used early</b>. So teaching covers carrying two, checking them, and using one at the first sign instead of waiting to be sure.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing anaphylaxis (Box 17.3)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Flag the allergy (Action Alert)</b> — list the patient’s <b>specific allergens prominently</b> in the record. <b>Ask before giving any drug</b>, document the allergen <b>and the reaction</b>, and tell the team. Know the <b>cross-reactions</b>: a patient allergic to penicillin is likely to react to <b>cephalosporins</b>. If a known allergen must be given, have <b>IV access</b> and <b>intubation and tracheostomy equipment at the bedside</b>, and expect <b>premedication</b> with diphenhydramine or a corticosteroid.</div>\n<div><b>The two criteria</b> — anaphylaxis is <b>highly likely</b> when either criterion is met. (1) <b>Sudden skin or mucosal signs</b> (hives, itching, flushing, swollen lips, tongue or uvula) <b>plus</b> at least one of: <b>breathing compromise</b>, <b>low BP or end-organ signs</b> (fainting, incontinence), or <b>severe GI symptoms</b>. (2) After a <b>known or likely allergen</b>, sudden <b>hypotension, bronchospasm or laryngeal involvement</b> (stridor, voice change, painful swallowing), <b>even without skin signs</b>. Hypotension means a systolic BP <b>&lt; 90 mm Hg</b> or a drop of <b>more than 30%</b> from baseline.</div>\n<div><b>Early and late signs</b> — early signs may be subtle: <b>sudden abdominal cramping and diarrhea</b>, or a sense of <b>apprehension or impending doom</b>. <b>Generalized itching and hives</b>, angioedema and flushing follow quickly. Then come congestion, a runny nose, <b>dyspnea</b>, and <b>wheezing, crackles or reduced breath sounds</b>. As it worsens: a “<b>lump in the throat</b>,” <b>hoarseness, stridor</b>, less chest movement, <b>hypotension</b> and a <b>rapid, weak, irregular pulse</b>. Respiratory failure can follow.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Skin signs are common but <b>not required</b>. After a known allergen, low blood pressure or a tightening airway is enough on its own to call it anaphylaxis and give epinephrine.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Emergency care (Box 17.4)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Call and assess</b> — <b>notify the Rapid Response Team</b>. Assess the <b>airway, breathing and oxygen saturation</b> at once. If an <b>IV drug or solution</b> may be the cause, <b>stop it immediately</b>. Have <b>intubation and tracheotomy</b> equipment ready.</div>\n<div><b>Epinephrine IM first</b> — <b>intramuscular epinephrine</b> is the <b>first-line</b> drug. It constricts the vessels, strengthens the heartbeat and opens the bronchioles, and there is <b>no absolute contraindication</b> to it during anaphylaxis. Repeat it <b>every 5–15 minutes</b> as needed. If <b>three IM doses</b> don’t work, <b>IV epinephrine</b> may be infused.</div>\n<div><b>Position, oxygen, fluids</b> — after the epinephrine, lay the patient <b>flat with the legs raised</b>, if tolerated. Give <b>high-flow oxygen by nonrebreather mask at 90–100%</b>, without waiting for a blood gas. Treat hypotension with a <b>rapid normal saline</b> infusion. If an IV drug was the cause, <b>change the tubing and flush the catheter</b> before the saline; if there is no IV, <b>start one</b>. Once the BP allows, raise the head of the bed to <b>45 degrees</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Add-on drugs</b> — give <b>albuterol</b> for bronchospasm, and <b>H1</b> (cetirizine, diphenhydramine) and <b>H2</b> (famotidine) antihistamines for itching and hives. <b>Glucocorticoids</b> (methylprednisolone) are <b>not effective immediately</b>; lower oral doses continue afterward to prevent a <b>late recurrence</b>. Give <b>vasopressors</b> and <b>glucagon</b> as ordered. <b>Stay with the patient</b> and reassure them.</div>\n<div><b>Monitoring</b> — use pulse oximetry, and draw ABGs as ordered. <b>Suction</b> secretions. Keep checking the respiratory rate and depth and the <b>breath sounds</b> (wheeze, crackles, <b>stridor</b>), every body system, and the effect of each drug. Severe anaphylaxis needs the <b>ICU</b>. The patient goes home only when breathing and circulation are <b>back to normal</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Antihistamines block only histamine, but anaphylaxis also runs on leukotrienes, bradykinin and prostaglandins. <b>Epinephrine</b> reverses the <b>effects</b> in the vessels, heart and airways at once. That is why it comes first and the other drugs are add-ons.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","ch":"17","chTitle":"Allergy and immunity conditions","title":"Autoimmunity and lupus (SLE): recognizing it","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Autoimmunity (Box 17.5)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What goes wrong</b> — the immune system loses some of its <b>self-tolerance</b>, and its antibodies or lymphocytes attack <b>healthy self tissue</b>. Autoimmune disorders are <b>common, chronic, progressive and self-perpetuating</b>. For now they are <b>incurable</b>, though remissions can occur. Nearly <b>4%</b> of people worldwide have one.</div>\n<div><b>Who and why</b> — they affect <b>three times as many women</b> as men; hormonal changes in puberty and pregnancy are suspected. There is a strong <b>genetic</b> link: some families have several affected members, and certain <b>HLA tissue types</b> (DR2, DR3 and others) raise the risk. The disease is then <b>triggered</b> by infection, injury, drugs, hormones or something in the environment. Ask about a <b>family history</b> of autoimmune disease.</div>\n<div><b>One target or many</b> — some disorders are <b>selective</b> and attack one tissue. In <b>myasthenia gravis</b>, antibodies block the acetylcholine receptors, so nerve impulses don’t reach the muscles. Others are <b>widespread</b>, because they target a component found in many organs: <b>SLE</b> and <b>rheumatoid arthritis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Common examples (Box 17.5)</b> — ankylosing spondylitis; autoimmune hemolytic anemia; autoimmune thrombocytopenic purpura; <b>celiac disease</b>; <b>Crohn’s disease</b> and <b>ulcerative colitis</b>; dermatomyositis; eczema; Goodpasture syndrome; <b>Graves disease</b>; <b>Guillain-Barré syndrome</b>; <b>Hashimoto thyroiditis</b>; <b>multiple sclerosis</b>; <b>myasthenia gravis</b>; pernicious anemia; <b>psoriasis</b> and psoriatic arthritis; <b>rheumatoid arthritis</b>; scleroderma; Sjögren syndrome; <b>SLE</b>; <b>type 1 diabetes</b>; and vasculitis.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Autoimmunity is a <b>gene–environment</b> story. The inherited tissue type loads the gun, and a trigger (an infection, a drug, hormones, sunlight) pulls the trigger. That is why the history asks about relatives and about what happened just before the symptoms began.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What lupus does</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Immune complexes everywhere</b> — in <b>SLE</b>, autoantibodies target proteins in <b>cell nuclei</b>, especially in connective tissue. The complexes they form (<b>type III</b>) deposit in many tissues, cause <b>systemic inflammation</b> and draw in more immune cells. <b>Blood vessels</b> are a major target: the resulting <b>vasculitis</b> cuts blood flow to the organs. The most common causes of death are <b>chronic kidney disease</b> and <b>cardiovascular</b> damage. <b>Arthritis and joint pain</b> occur in virtually everyone with SLE.</div>\n<div><b>Flares and remission</b> — symptoms may be constant, low (<b>remission</b>) or suddenly worse (a <b>flare</b>, or exacerbation). <b>Disease activity</b> means how much disease is present at a given time. <b>Most organ damage happens during high activity</b>, so <b>flares speed up the damage</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Triggers and who gets it</b> — genetic, immune, hormonal and environmental factors combine. In a susceptible person, the triggers include <b>certain viral infections</b>, <b>drugs</b>, <b>estrogen</b> (contraceptives, postmenopausal hormones) and especially <b>UV light and sun</b>. About <b>1.5 million</b> people in the US have SLE. It mainly affects <b>women of childbearing age</b>, and <b>women of color</b> are affected out of proportion. The onset is <b>slow and vague</b>: on average, about <b>54 months</b> pass between the first symptoms and the diagnosis.</div>\n<div><b>Skin-only lupus</b> — three less common forms affect only the skin: <b>acute</b>, <b>subacute</b> and <b>chronic cutaneous</b> lupus. The chronic form often has a <b>discoid</b> (disk-shaped) rash.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The complexes travel in the blood, so lupus can show up <b>anywhere</b>. The kidneys filter the blood through tiny capillaries, so they catch the complexes and become one of the deadliest targets.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Assessing lupus (Box 17.6)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — ask about changes over the <b>past 5 years</b> and about <b>pain without injury</b>, constant or on and off. Ask about <b>relatives with SLE or another autoimmune disease</b>, any infection or exposure before a new symptom, and <b>unexplained fevers</b> that come and go with fatigue when no one else at home is ill. <b>Drug-induced lupus</b> can follow long-term <b>hydralazine, isoniazid or procainamide</b>, so ask about drugs taken in the year before the symptoms. Report a woman with <b>chronic fatigue, fevers of unknown origin and persistent joint and muscle pain</b> so she can be evaluated for SLE.</div>\n<div><b>Skin</b> — skin changes are often the <b>first visible sign</b>. A dry, scaly, raised <b>malar (“butterfly”) rash</b> across the cheeks and nose often appears after <b>sun exposure</b>. It doesn’t scar at first, and it worsens in flares. <b>Coin-shaped discoid lesions</b> on the face, scalp and sun-exposed areas <b>do scar</b>. Look also for <b>photosensitivity</b>, darkening of the face, <b>alopecia</b> and <b>ulcers in the mouth and throat</b>. Document skin changes and check them <b>daily</b> during care.</div>\n<div><b>Fever, fatigue, joints and muscles</b> — chronic inflammation causes an <b>intermittent low-grade fever</b> and <b>chronic fatigue</b>; stress, depression, poor sleep from pain, and infections add to the fatigue. <b>Arthritis and joint pain</b> are usually <b>bilateral</b>, in the feet, ankles, knees, wrists and fingers. The joints swell painfully, but there is <b>no erosion or deformity</b> (unlike RA). There may also be <b>myositis</b> (muscle inflammation) and fibromyalgia-like <b>tender points</b>. <b>Osteonecrosis</b> can follow <b>5 or more years</b> of corticosteroids.</div>\n<div><b>Kidneys and heart</b> — in <b>lupus nephritis</b>, the complexes damage the glomeruli and <b>protein</b> leaks into the urine. Look for <b>cloudy, bloody or foamy urine</b>; nephritis can progress to <b>chronic kidney disease</b>. In the <b>heart</b>, complexes in any layer cause <b>pericarditis</b> and <b>thickened valves</b>. Check the BP, heart rate and pulse quality, <b>neck vein distention, leg edema</b>, capillary refill and <b>SpO₂</b>. Other features include <b>Raynaud phenomenon</b>, vasculitis, esophagitis, peripheral neuropathy, <b>retinal</b> vessel disease, <b>anemia, leukopenia</b>, osteoporosis and frequent infections.</div>\n<div><b>Psychosocial</b> — a chronic disease that can be <b>disfiguring</b>, with pain and fatigue, can lead to withdrawal, <b>anxiety and depression</b>. Listen actively and plan care <b>with</b> the patient. Correct the myth that lupus is <b>fatal</b>: with monitoring, drugs and sun protection, <b>most people have a normal life span</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Lupus has no single sign. The diagnosis comes from <b>clusters</b>: rash plus joint pain plus fever plus protein in the urine. The nurse’s history and the skin, joint and urine checks supply those pieces.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Lupus tests (Table 17.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>No single test</b> — SLE is diagnosed from <b>several tests together</b> with the physical findings. The <b>ANA</b> (antinuclear antibody) is <b>positive</b> when autoantibodies are present. Normal is <b>negative at 1:40</b>, though older adults may be normal at lower dilutions. The ANA is <b>never used alone</b> to diagnose SLE.</div>\n<div><b>Blood counts</b> — look for <b>low RBCs</b> (anemia); a low MCV and MCHC with a high RDW suggests <b>iron deficiency</b>. Look for <b>low WBCs</b> (<b>leukopenia</b>). A <b>low platelet count</b> suggests <b>immune thrombocytopenia</b>; your book’s table says “increased,” which is a slip. High <b>monocytes</b> point to chronic inflammation, and high <b>eosinophils</b> can occur in autoimmune disorders. Normal values: WBC <b>5000–10,000/mm³</b>, platelets <b>150,000–400,000/mm³</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Kidneys</b> — a rising <b>serum creatinine</b> means <b>kidney damage</b>. Normal in younger adults is about <b>0.5–1.0 mg/dL</b> for women and <b>0.6–1.2 mg/dL</b> for men. On urinalysis, normal is <b>RBCs ≤ 2</b> and a <b>urine protein-to-creatinine ratio &lt; 0.15</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Other tests</b> — other blood tests include the <b>ESR and CRP</b> (inflammation), <b>complement C3, C4 and CH50</b>, <b>antiphospholipid antibodies</b> and <b>anti–double-stranded DNA</b>. Imaging follows the symptoms. Chest pain gets an <b>ECG</b>, and pleuritic chest pain or dyspnea gets a <b>PE workup</b>. Kidney ultrasound rules out obstruction, and joint ultrasound looks for synovitis. Abdominal pain or suspected lung disease gets a <b>CT</b>, and focal neuro deficits or cognitive change get an <b>MRI</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A positive ANA shows only that autoantibodies exist; many conditions, and some healthy older adults, have them. What makes it lupus is the <b>pattern</b>: the autoantibody plus damage in the typical places (blood counts, kidneys, skin, joints).</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","ch":"17","chTitle":"Allergy and immunity conditions","title":"Caring for lupus: pain, fatigue, drugs and organs","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Pain and fatigue (Box 17.7)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — for SLE there are three: (1) <b>persistent pain</b> from chronic inflammation; (2) <b>fatigue</b> from chronic inflammation; and (3) the <b>potential for organ failure</b> from inflammation and vasculitis.</div>\n<div><b>Pain</b> — the drugs that control the disease come first. For daily joint and muscle pain, use <b>acetaminophen</b> (it dulls pain but <b>doesn’t reduce inflammation</b>) and <b>NSAIDs</b> (patients may need to try several). In flares, <b>corticosteroids</b> are raised and then <b>tapered exactly</b> back to the usual dose; stopping suddenly risks <b>adrenal crisis</b>. Also: <b>rest</b>, <b>avoid stress</b>, do <b>low-impact activity</b>, <b>protect against sun</b>, and use <b>moist heat</b> (warm baths, showers, spa tubs). Some states allow <b>medical cannabis</b> for SLE.</div>\n<div><b>Fatigue</b> — health comes <b>first</b>. Have the patient sort activities into <b>must do</b>, <b>someone else can do</b> and <b>not needed</b>. Suggest sharing chores, <b>grocery delivery</b>, and a talk with the supervisor at work. <b>Box 17.7</b>: do the most important things first, <b>plan rest</b> through the day, shop online or at one store, make meals ahead, say “<b>no</b>,” and accept help. Keep good <b>sleep</b> and <b>diet</b>. Plan <b>vaccines</b> with the provider; most are fine, and <b>live vaccines</b> are the exceptions. <b>Stop smoking.</b> Low-impact and strength exercise (walking, stretching) builds endurance, even split into <b>two 15–30-minute sessions</b>, with <b>rest afterward</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Pain and fatigue come from the same inflammation that damages the organs, so controlling disease activity treats all three. Pacing, rest and sun avoidance keep flares from restarting the cycle.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Drugs that protect the organs</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Corticosteroids</b> — <b>prednisone</b> is a cornerstone of treatment: a general anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressant that affects every cell. Early side effects: <b>acne, sodium and fluid retention, hypertension</b>, anxiety, poor sleep and emotional changes. Long-term side effects: <b>weight gain</b>, a <b>moon face and buffalo hump</b>, <b>GI ulcers and bleeding</b>, <b>fragile skin that bruises</b>, muscle loss, thin scalp hair, more facial and body hair, <b>infections</b> and stretch marks. <b>Never stop it abruptly</b>, because <b>adrenal crisis</b> can follow. If the patient <b>can’t keep the pill down</b>, they must call the rheumatology provider <b>at once</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Antimalarials</b> — <b>hydroxychloroquine</b> (and chloroquine) often brings <b>remission</b>. It modulates immunity, has <b>anticlotting</b> effects (useful with vasculitis) and lowers the skin’s UV absorption, so there are fewer lesions. The major risk is <b>retinal toxicity</b>, which can cause an <b>irreversible loss of central vision</b>. Teach the patient to have an <b>eye exam with visual fields before starting and every 6 months</b>, and to report <b>blurred vision</b>.</div>\n<div><b>NSAIDs and aspirin</b> — <b>ibuprofen, naproxen, indomethacin, nabumetone</b> and <b>celecoxib</b> treat pain and inflammation. Teach to <b>take them with food</b> and to expect <b>kidney function</b> labs. <b>Low-dose aspirin</b> may be used to <b>prevent clots</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Immunosuppressives</b> — <b>cyclophosphamide, azathioprine, mycophenolate mofetil, voclosporin</b> and <b>methotrexate</b> keep the body from attacking itself. Teach the <b>higher infection risk</b>: avoid sick people, wash hands often, and report a fever <b>over 100°F (37.8°C)</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Monoclonal antibodies</b> — two are approved for SLE. <b>Belimumab</b> lowers autoantibody levels and is given <b>IV or subcutaneously</b>. <b>Anifrolumab</b> damps the overactive <b>interferon</b> signals and is given IV. <b>Infusion reactions, including severe hypersensitivity</b>, can occur. So a professional gives them where <b>anaphylaxis</b> can be managed, with monitoring <b>during the infusion and for up to 2 hours after</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every SLE drug trades <b>less inflammation for some cost</b>: steroids for many side effects, hydroxychloroquine for the retina, immunosuppressants and biologics for infection. Teaching targets each drug’s own danger.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Sun, lifestyle and home</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Avoid UV (Action Alert)</b> — <b>UV light worsens every aspect</b> of SLE, not just the skin. Teach the patient to avoid long exposure to <b>sunlight</b> and to some kinds of <b>fluorescent</b> light. Outdoors, wear <b>long sleeves and a wide-brimmed hat</b> and use <b>SPF 30 or higher</b>. Go out in the <b>early morning or later evening</b>, and stay in the <b>shade</b> or use an umbrella.</div>\n<div><b>Bones and organs</b> — women with organ damage from SLE often have <b>musculoskeletal</b> damage too, so encourage <b>bone monitoring</b>. <b>Kidney and heart</b> disease are the top causes of death, so urge <b>regular provider visits</b> to catch changes early. No <b>smoking</b> or nicotine in any form; identify and avoid <b>stressors</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Home and self-management</b> — most care happens <b>at home and in clinics</b>. During flares, <b>one-floor living</b> may help for a while. Advanced disease may need 24-hour care or long-term care. Patients who are educated and involved do better. They should know their <b>drugs and dose timing, the adverse effects, the signs of infection and when to get help</b>. Resources include the <b>Lupus Foundation of America</b> and <b>Lupus Canada</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — the patient <b>follows the long-term plan</b>, has <b>pain at a level they accept</b>, keeps <b>energy for valued activities</b>, and has <b>longer remissions</b> with low disease activity.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Sunlight doesn’t just inflame the skin; it can set off a flare throughout the body. Sun protection is a <b>disease-control</b> measure, not a cosmetic one.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","ch":"17","chTitle":"Allergy and immunity conditions","title":"HIV: the virus, the stages and how it spreads","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Three stages and the AIDS-defining conditions (Box 17.8)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Stage I — acute infection</b> — within about <b>4 weeks</b> of infection, most adults have a <b>flulike illness</b>: <b>fever, sore throat, rash, night sweats, chills, headache</b> and <b>muscle aches</b>. It passes, so HIV is easily missed, but the virus keeps attacking the immune system. Viral levels in the blood and genital fluids are <b>very high</b>, so <b>sexual transmission is likely</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Stage II — chronic infection</b> — most people are diagnosed and started on drugs in this stage, after <b>seroconversion</b> (antibodies appear). With treatment it is a <b>chronic disease</b>. Viral reproduction is suppressed and immunity holds; some people keep the virus <b>undetectable</b> for decades. The <b>viral load (HIV RNA)</b> test is the best guide here to prognosis, progression and the response to treatment.</div>\n<div><b>Stage III — AIDS</b> — the drugs <b>suppress</b> the virus but don’t <b>kill</b> it, so some people progress anyway; untreated people progress sooner. CD4+ cells fall and work poorly, causing <b>leukopenia</b> and <b>lymphocytopenia</b>, <b>antibodies that don’t work</b> and abnormal macrophages. Stage III requires HIV plus either a <b>CD4+ count &lt; 200 cells/mm³</b> or an <b>AIDS-defining opportunistic infection</b>. Once it is diagnosed, it <b>stays stage III</b>, even if the count recovers.</div>\n<div><b>Opportunistic infections (Box 17.8)</b> — these come from organisms of the normal microbiome that healthy immunity keeps in check. When immunity fails, they <b>overgrow</b>; <i>Candida albicans</i> in the mouth and vagina is an example. The AIDS-defining conditions include <b>candidiasis</b>, <b>cryptococcosis</b>, <b>cytomegalovirus</b>, <b><i>Pneumocystis jirovecii</i> pneumonia (PCP)</b>, <b><i>Mycobacterium avium</i> complex</b>, <b>toxoplasmosis</b>, <b>tuberculosis</b> and chronic <b>cryptosporidiosis</b>. They also include three cancers: <b>Kaposi sarcoma</b>, certain <b>non-Hodgkin lymphomas</b> and <b>invasive cervical cancer</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults</b> — HIV can be caught at <b>any age</b>, so ask older adults about their <b>sexual and drug-use</b> history. Lower immunity with age makes infection <b>more likely</b> after an exposure.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>HIV and AIDS are not the same. Everyone with stage III has HIV, but most people with HIV, especially if treated, <b>never reach stage III</b>. The dividing line is the <b>CD4+ count</b> and whether an <b>opportunistic infection</b> has occurred.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">How HIV takes over a cell</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>A retrovirus</b> — HIV carries its genes as <b>RNA</b> and uses its enzyme <b>reverse transcriptase</b> to make DNA that it can <b>insert into the host cell’s DNA</b>. It finds <b>CD4+ T cells</b> by means of “docking” proteins and takes control of them. They stop working as immune cells and become <b>virus factories</b>; new particles bud off and infect more cells.</div>\n<div><b>The seven-step life cycle</b> — (1) <b>binding</b> to the CD4 receptors; (2) <b>fusion</b> of the virus envelope with the cell membrane; (3) <b>reverse transcription</b> of the viral RNA into DNA; (4) <b>integration</b> into the cell’s DNA by the enzyme <b>integrase</b>; (5) <b>replication</b> of long chains of HIV proteins; (6) <b>assembly</b> of immature, noninfectious virus at the cell surface; (7) <b>budding</b>, when the enzyme <b>protease</b> cuts the chains to make <b>mature</b>, infectious virus. <b>Each drug class blocks one step</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Types and genetics</b> — <b>HIV-1</b> is the more common subtype worldwide and the more <b>virulent</b>. <b>HIV-2</b> is found mainly in <b>West Africa</b>. Susceptibility is partly <b>inherited</b>. A few <b>long-term nonprogressors</b> stay well, with normal CD4 counts, <b>without treatment</b>, but they can still <b>pass on</b> the virus while it is detectable.</div>\n<div><b>How common</b> — about <b>38.4 million</b> people worldwide (2021) and <b>1.2 million</b> in the US (2022) live with HIV, and <b>13%</b> of the US group don’t know it. It affects some groups out of proportion: <b>Black/African American</b> and <b>Hispanic/Latinx</b> people, people of multiple races, and <b>gay, bisexual and other men who have sex with men</b>. New infections continue among <b>transgender</b> adults.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>HIV attacks the <b>helper T cells that organize all immunity</b> (Chapter 16). As they fall, antibody responses, inflammation and cell-mediated defense all weaken together, and organisms that were harmless become deadly.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">How HIV spreads</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Fluids and routes</b> — HIV is present in <b>all body fluids</b> of an infected person. <b>Blood, semen, vaginal secretions and breast milk</b> carry the <b>highest</b> amounts. It spreads <b>sexually</b> (mucous membranes exposed to semen or vaginal fluid), <b>parenterally</b> (<b>shared needles</b>, contaminated blood) and <b>perinatally</b> (across the placenta, at birth, or by <b>breastfeeding</b>).</div>\n<div><b>What doesn’t spread it</b> — <b>casual contact</b> at home, school or work doesn’t spread HIV, and neither does <b>sharing utensils, towels, linens or toilets</b> or being bitten by <b>mosquitoes and other insects</b>. A <b>splash</b> of body fluid on <b>intact skin</b> is low risk, but the person should still see a provider to decide whether treatment is needed.</div>\n<div><b>Sexual risk</b> — only <b>abstinence</b> or <b>mutual monogamy with an uninfected partner</b> protects completely. The risk depends on the sex act, the partner’s <b>viral load</b>, and contact with mucous membranes or broken skin. <b>Receptive anal intercourse</b> carries the <b>highest</b> risk, because the rectal lining tears easily. <b>Women</b> are infected more easily than men, because the vagina has a larger mucosal surface. Anal sex used as contraception still spreads HIV and other STIs.</div>\n<div><b>Blood and donation</b> — all donated blood in North America is <b>screened</b>. The transfusion risk is about <b>1 in 1.5–2 million units</b>: tiny, but real, because infected blood can test negative during the <b>window period</b> before antibodies appear. Anyone who has ever tested <b>HIV-positive</b> can’t donate. Your book adds a <b>3-month</b> deferral for injecting anything not prescribed, sex between men, exchanging sex for payment, or sex with someone who did any of these. <i>Beyond the book:</i> since 2023, US blood centers ask <b>everyone the same risk questions</b> instead of having a rule for men who have sex with men.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The virus needs a <b>way into the blood or a mucous membrane</b>, and enough virus to get there. That is why fluids with high viral levels and acts that break the mucosa carry the risk, while a kiss on the cheek or a shared bathroom does not.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","ch":"17","chTitle":"Allergy and immunity conditions","title":"Preventing HIV: safer sex, syringes, PrEP, PEP and TasP","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Safer sex and safer injection (Box 17.9)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Teach everyone</b> — anyone can be infected, whatever their age, gender, ethnicity or orientation, so give <b>every</b> adult the teaching. <b>Safer sex</b>: use a <b>new latex or polyurethane condom for every act</b>, and change it between types of intercourse. Add a <b>water-based lubricant</b> to the condom to reduce friction. Use a <b>latex barrier (dental dam)</b> for oral–genital or oral–anal contact, and <b>latex gloves</b> for finger or hand contact with the vagina or rectum, changed between sites. Never share needles or syringes.</div>\n<div><b>Syringe services programs</b> — <b>SSPs</b>, often run through pharmacies, give <b>sterile needles and supplies free, without a prescription</b>. They cut <b>HIV and hepatitis C</b> by about <b>50%</b>. They also teach about <b>PrEP</b>, how to recognize an <b>overdose</b> and how to get <b>naloxone</b>. An SSP is the <b>first choice</b>, ahead of cleaning syringes.</div>\n<div><b>Cleaning a syringe (Box 17.9)</b> — when an SSP isn’t available: wash your hands and set out <b>three containers</b>, filled with <b>water, bleach and water</b> (fresh water for the last one). Use each container in turn: <b>fill</b> the syringe, <b>tap or shake it for 30 full seconds</b>, then <b>empty it into a sink</b>. Water, then bleach, then fresh water: <b>nine steps, in order</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Prevention means blocking the routes: barriers for sex, sterile equipment for injection. Teaching works best when it is practical, free of judgment and offered to everyone, not just to people who “look” at risk.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">PrEP, PEP and TasP</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>PrEP — before exposure</b> — in <b>preexposure prophylaxis</b>, an <b>HIV-negative</b> person at risk takes antiretroviral drugs <b>before</b> sex or injection. Taken as prescribed, it cuts the risk from sex by about <b>99%</b>. The drugs are <b>cabotegravir</b> (oral or <b>injection</b>), <b>tenofovir disoproxil fumarate/emtricitabine</b> (for everyone) and <b>tenofovir alafenamide/emtricitabine</b> (for sexually active men and transgender women). Both tenofovir combinations carry a <b>black box warning</b>: they are not for people with <b>hepatitis B</b>, because stopping them can cause a <b>severe hepatitis B flare</b>. <i>Beyond the book:</i> in 2025 the FDA approved <b>lenacapavir</b> as a twice-yearly PrEP injection.</div>\n<div><b>PrEP teaching (Drug Alert)</b> — PrEP and TasP <b>don’t replace safer sex</b>. If someone on PrEP becomes infected, <b>drug resistance</b> is likely. So teach <b>HIV testing every 3 months</b>, along with condoms and the other safer-sex practices.</div>\n<div><b>PEP — after exposure</b> — <b>postexposure prophylaxis</b> is for <b>emergencies only</b>, not frequent use. <b>nPEP</b> (nonoccupational PEP) follows unprotected sex, mucous-membrane contact with someone’s fluids, a shared needle, or a <b>sexual assault</b>; after an assault, also test for other STIs and discuss <b>emergency contraception</b>. <b>Occupational PEP</b> follows a needlestick: <b>wash the area well</b>, then <b>contact employee health immediately</b>. Start PEP <b>within 72 hours</b>, the sooner the better (even within <b>2 hours</b>), and take it for <b>28 days</b>. Test the HIV status <b>before and after</b>. Typical drugs: <b>tenofovir disoproxil fumarate + emtricitabine, plus raltegravir or dolutegravir</b>.</div>\n<div><b>TasP — treatment as prevention</b> — a person <b>with HIV</b> takes their medication to keep an <b>undetectable viral load</b>, which <b>greatly reduces</b> the risk of passing the virus on through sex. It <b>doesn’t replace</b> safer sex. <i>Beyond the book:</i> the CDC now says that people who <b>keep</b> an undetectable viral load have <b>effectively no risk</b> of passing HIV through sex (“<b>U = U</b>,” undetectable equals untransmittable). On the exam, use your book’s wording.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>All three use the same drugs at different moments. <b>PrEP</b> keeps the virus from taking hold in an uninfected person. <b>PEP</b> stops it in the first hours after an exposure. <b>TasP</b> keeps an infected person’s virus too low to pass on.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Protecting patients and health care workers</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Standard Precautions</b> — the <b>best protection</b> for health care workers is <b>Standard Precautions for every patient</b>, which The Joint Commission requires. The risk from a splash or needlestick is low, but precautions are <b>always</b> used. <b>Sharps with built-in injury protection</b> have cut needlesticks. Most needlesticks that still happen come from workers <b>not using the safety feature</b>, especially during <b>disposal</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Protecting patients (Action Alert)</b> — health care workers <b>wear gloves</b> for contact with patients’ <b>mucous membranes or broken skin</b>. A worker with HIV who has <b>weeping dermatitis or open sores</b> must <b>wear gloves or not give direct care</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Precautions are universal because HIV status is often <b>unknown</b>: 13% of people with HIV don’t know they have it.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","ch":"17","chTitle":"Allergy and immunity conditions","title":"HIV: history and testing","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">History and assessment (Box 17.10)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Who and how often</b> — the CDC recommends that <b>everyone aged 13–64</b> be tested for HIV <b>at least once</b>, and more often with higher risk. People in stage II or III are monitored every <b>2–6 months</b> for drug effects and progression.</div>\n<div><b>History</b> — take it in a <b>private</b> place. Ask about any current illness or change in health. For a new patient who is HIV-positive, ask <b>when the diagnosis was made</b> and for a timeline of infections, treatments and problems. Ask everyone about <b>risk factors</b>: unprotected sex, <b>needle sharing</b>, a past <b>transfusion</b>, and <b>clotting factors</b> for hemophilia. Ask about <b>STIs, TB and hepatitis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Sexual history</b> — take it <b>after rapport</b> is built. Explain that it is for a <b>personal plan of care, not judgment</b>, and promise <b>confidentiality</b>. The limits are disclosed <b>abuse</b>, an intent to <b>harm others</b>, or <b>suicidal or homicidal</b> intent. <b>Don’t assume</b> the patient’s orientation, behaviors, number of partners or gender identity, and use <b>gender-neutral</b> words (“partners”). Cover the <b>five Ps</b>: <b>Partners</b>, <b>Practices</b>, <b>Protection</b> from STIs, <b>Past history</b> of STIs, and <b>Prevention of pregnancy</b>. Box 17.10 lists the questions: partners in the past 6 months, kinds of sexual contact in the past 12 months, condom use, PrEP, HPV and hepatitis vaccines, past STIs and HIV tests, and pregnancy plans.</div>\n<div><b>Physical and psychosocial</b> — in HIV-negative patients, look for <b>flulike</b> signs (swollen lymph nodes, sore throat, fever). In stages I and II, there may be <b>no symptoms or only minor ones</b>; check for drug side effects. <b>Always assess for opportunistic infections</b>, even if the patient feels well, and report clusters of stage III findings (<b>Table 17.3</b>). Assess neurologic and mental status for subtle change. Screen for <b>depression, anxiety and suicidal ideation</b>. Confirm that the <b>health care proxy</b> and <b>advance directives</b> are in the record.</div>\n<div><b>Critical rescue</b> — report <b>immediately</b> any change in <b>level of consciousness, vital signs, pupil size or reactivity, or limb strength</b>. A stage III patient with <b>TB symptoms</b> needs <b>Airborne plus Standard Precautions</b> and a <b>rapid molecular assay</b> to confirm or rule out TB.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A sexual history helps only if it is honest, and patients are honest when they feel <b>safe and unjudged</b>. Neutral language and an explained purpose are clinical tools, not just courtesy.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Testing for HIV</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The window period</b> — after an exposure there is a <b>3-week seroconversion window</b>, when a test can be <b>falsely negative</b>. Anyone exposed within the <b>past month</b> whose result is negative should be <b>retested</b> after the window.</div>\n<div><b>Screening tests</b> — one option is a <b>chemiluminescent immunoassay</b> followed by a <b>confirmatory</b> test, such as the <b>Western blot</b>. The <b>recommended</b> option is a <b>point-of-service rapid antibody test</b>, with results in <b>under an hour</b>. It is useful in emergencies, and the person doesn’t have to come back for the result. The <b>antigen/antibody (Ag/Ab) combination</b> test detects infection <b>5–7 days earlier</b> than antibody-only tests. A positive result is followed by a test to tell <b>HIV-1 from HIV-2</b> and, if needed, a <b>nucleic acid test (NAT)</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Other ways to test</b> — <b>home kits</b> use a fingerstick sample mailed to a lab, with results in about a week and counseling by phone. Screen patients carefully, since a positive result by phone is distressing, and have <b>support people</b> present. The <b>oral mucosal transudate</b> (gum swab) and <b>urine</b> tests can be used when a blood draw is difficult or distressing.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Antibodies take time to appear, so a negative test right after an exposure proves little. Tests that also detect the <b>virus’s own antigen</b> close the gap by days.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Monitoring the immune system and the virus</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Lymphocytes</b> — the WBC count is normally <b>5000–10,000/mm³</b>, with about <b>20–40% lymphocytes</b> (1000–4000/mm³). Stage III often brings <b>leukopenia</b> (WBC <b>&lt; 4000</b>) and <b>lymphopenia</b> (<b>&lt; 1500</b> lymphocytes/mm³).</div>\n<div><b>CD4+ count and ratio</b> — the normal CD4+ count is <b>600–1500 cells/mm³</b>; your book also says a healthy adult usually has at least <b>800–1000</b>. Below <b>200/mm³</b>, the risk of <b>opportunistic infection is high</b>. CD8+ cells stay normal, so the <b>CD4:CD8 ratio</b>, normally <b>greater than 1</b>, <b>falls</b>. A low count and a low ratio go with <b>more symptoms</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Viral load</b> — the <b>HIV RNA</b> test counts the copies of virus in each mL of blood. It is the guide to <b>prognosis, progression, treatment effect</b> and the need for prophylaxis. An uninfected person’s result reads “<b>undetected</b>.” The higher the load (it can exceed <b>30,000 copies/mL</b>), the faster the disease progresses. Do two <b>baseline</b> tests <b>2–4 weeks apart</b>, then test every <b>3–4 months</b>, and every <b>4–6 weeks</b> after therapy starts or changes. “<b>Undetectable</b>” means <b>below the test’s limit</b>, not free of virus, so safer sex continues.</div>\n<div><b>Other tests and genetics</b> — other tests include chemistries, urinalysis, a CBC, a <b>toxoplasmosis titer</b>, liver tests, <b>STI and hepatitis</b> tests, lipids, <b>TB testing</b> (QuantiFERON or PPD), <b>cervical and anal Pap tests</b>, and tests for drug toxicity. <b>Resistance testing</b> (genotype, tropism) guides the drug choice. The <b>HLA-B∗5701</b> test must be done <b>before abacavir</b> is ever prescribed, because of the risk of a fatal hypersensitivity reaction. The <b>Trofile assay</b> must show <b>CCR5 receptors</b> before <b>maraviroc</b> is started, or the drug won’t work.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The CD4+ count shows <b>how much immunity is left</b>. The viral load shows <b>how active the virus is</b> and whether the drugs are working. Together they set the stage and steer treatment.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","ch":"17","chTitle":"Allergy and immunity conditions","title":"HIV drugs (cART), resistance and IRIS","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">How cART works (Table 17.4)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Principles</b> — antiretroviral drugs <b>control the virus’s replication; they don’t kill it</b>. Drugs from <b>different classes</b> are combined (<b>combination antiretroviral therapy, cART</b>) to lower the viral load, raise the CD4+ count and slow progression. Therapy is <b>lifelong and costly</b>; refer to <b>social work or case management</b> for help paying.</div>\n<div><b>NRTIs</b> — <b>nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors</b> block reverse transcriptase. All of them can cause <b>lactic acidosis and a fatty liver</b>, so monitor lactate and liver tests. <b>Abacavir</b>: report <b>fever, rash, muscle or joint aches, or GI symptoms</b> at once; they can mean a <b>hypersensitivity reaction</b>, possibly fatal, in the first <b>6 weeks</b>. <b>Emtricitabine</b>: rare darkening of the <b>palms and soles</b>. <b>Lamivudine</b>: in someone who also has <b>hepatitis B</b>, stopping it can cause a severe hepatitis flare, so watch liver tests for months. <b>Tenofovir</b>: <b>kidney</b> damage and <b>bone loss</b>. <b>Zidovudine</b>: <b>anemia, neutropenia, marrow suppression</b>.</div>\n<div><b>NNRTIs</b> — <b>non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors</b> bind and change the enzyme; they commonly cause <b>anemia</b>. <b>Efavirenz</b> is <b>teratogenic</b>: teach an oral contraceptive plus condoms. With <b>rilpivirine</b>, it can also harm the <b>liver</b> and cause <b>CNS changes</b>. <b>Nevirapine</b>: severe <b>liver toxicity</b>, highest in the first <b>12 weeks</b>. <b>Etravirine</b>: its levels are lowered by <b>St. John’s wort</b>, anticonvulsants, dexamethasone and rifampin. <b>Rilpivirine</b>: depression, insomnia and headache. <b>Doravirine</b>: diarrhea, fatigue, nausea and headache.</div>\n<div><b>Protease inhibitors</b> — <b>atazanavir</b> and <b>darunavir</b> block protease. Monitor <b>glucose and lipids</b>: they can cause high glucose, new or worse <b>diabetes</b> (even <b>DKA</b>) and high cholesterol and triglycerides. Teach that <b>body fat may shift</b> to the abdomen, breasts and between the shoulders. They can slow conduction in the heart (a <b>long PR interval</b>, bundle branch block). <b>Atazanavir</b> can cause <b>jaundice</b>, from a rise in unconjugated bilirubin. <b>Darunavir contains sulfa</b>, so don’t give it to anyone with a <b>sulfa allergy</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Entry, integrase and boosters</b> — <b>ibalizumab</b> is a monoclonal antibody that blocks the CD4 receptors; watch during and after the <b>infusion</b>. <b>Enfuvirtide</b> (a fusion inhibitor) is given <b>subcutaneously twice a day</b>: teach to <b>rotate sites</b> and watch for infection. <b>Fostemsavir</b> (an attachment inhibitor): report fever, rash, aches or GI symptoms in the first 6 weeks. <b>Maraviroc</b> (a CCR5 blocker) needs a <b>Trofile</b> test first; <b>rash, jaundice, vomiting or abdominal pain</b> call for <b>emergency care</b>. The <b>integrase inhibitors</b> are <b>dolutegravir</b> (raises <b>liver enzymes and glucose</b>) and <b>raltegravir</b> (raises <b>amylase and lipase</b>). The <b>boosters</b> (pharmacokinetic enhancers) are <b>cobicistat</b> (check with the provider before taking <b>antacids</b> or ulcer drugs, because the timing matters) and <b>ritonavir</b> (<b>peripheral neuropathy</b>). Many of these drugs come only as <b>combination pills</b>; check a drug reference before giving one.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Each class stops HIV at a <b>different step</b> of its life cycle. Combining classes makes it far harder for the virus to get through, and far harder for a resistant mutant to survive.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Adherence and resistance</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Take it exactly as prescribed (Drug Alert)</b> — HIV <b>mutates</b>. When doses are missed, drug levels fall too low to stop replication, and <b>resistant virus</b> emerges. Teach patients to <b>avoid missing or delaying doses</b>. When a dose is missed, they <b>resume the regimen as soon as they discover it</b> and carry on as usual, <b>with no doubled doses</b>. Today’s drugs have <b>longer half-lives</b>, so a few missed doses are more forgiving, but adherence still matters most.</div>\n<div><b>Resistance is permanent</b> — once resistant virus exists, it is <b>stored in the body indefinitely</b>. The drugs it resists <b>won’t work again</b>, even years later. A person with resistant virus who isn’t taking cART consistently can <b>pass on the resistant strain</b> through sex or shared needles. <b>Resistance testing</b> guides a new regimen. <b>Lenacapavir</b> (approved in 2022) treats heavily treated patients whose virus resists many drugs.</div>\n<div><b>Complementary therapies</b> — many people with HIV use them, and some <b>change cART levels</b> (St. John’s wort with etravirine, for example). Ask about <b>vitamins, herbs and supplements</b>, and check for interactions with the <b>pharmacist</b>. Yoga or aromatherapy may bring comfort.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Resistance is why adherence is taught so firmly. Each stretch of low drug levels gives the virus a chance to mutate, and a mutation that survives the drug never goes away.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What happens</b> — after cART starts, the <b>CD4+ count rises</b>. The recovering T cells suddenly <b>recognize infections that were already present</b> but hidden by poor immunity, and they <b>overreact</b>. The result is <b>fever, night sweats, fatigue</b>, and <b>new or worsening</b> disease, usually <b>1–12 weeks</b> after cART begins.</div>\n<div><b>Common culprits and care</b> — the usual organisms are <b>TB, cryptococcosis, MAC, PCP, CMV, hepatitis</b> and <b>herpes simplex</b>. IRIS looks like drug side effects and other illnesses, so it can be <b>missed</b>, which raises the risk of death. Treatment is <b>short-term corticosteroids</b> for the inflammation plus treatment for the specific infection (for example, <b>anti-TB drugs</b>). <b>cART almost always continues</b>, because IRIS usually <b>resolves on its own</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>IRIS is a sign the treatment is <b>working</b>: immunity is coming back. So the answer is to manage the inflammation, not to stop the drugs.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","ch":"17","chTitle":"Allergy and immunity conditions","title":"Stage III (AIDS): infections, complications and care","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Preventing infection (Boxes 17.11 and 17.12)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — there are two: (1) the <b>potential to transmit</b> infection, from lack of teaching or risky behavior (all stages); and (2) the <b>potential for infection and life-threatening complications</b> from reduced immunity (stages II and III). The goals are <b>no transmission</b>, and freedom from infection for as long as possible, with a quick recovery if one occurs.</div>\n<div><b>Catch it early</b> — early recognition and treatment are the <b>most important</b> nursing actions, because they lower the risk of <b>sepsis</b>. Patients with <b>leukopenia may not show the usual signs</b>, so <b>act even on a low-grade fever</b>. In the hospital: a <b>daily CBC with differential and ANC</b>; <b>vital signs every 4 hours</b>, with the temperature; <b>inspect the mouth every shift</b>; check the <b>lungs every 8 hours</b> (crackles, wheezes, reduced sounds); check the urine for odor and cloudiness and ask about urgency or burning; and inspect <b>all the skin</b>, especially the <b>perirectal</b> area.</div>\n<div><b>At home (Box 17.11)</b> — teach <b>hand washing</b> with antimicrobial soap (before eating or drinking, and after touching pets, shaking hands, coming home and using the toilet). Avoid <b>crowds</b>, and wear an <b>N95 or KN95</b> mask among many people. <b>No gardening or houseplants.</b> Avoid travel to areas with poor sanitation, and take the <b>temperature daily</b>. Report a <b>temperature &gt; 100°F (37.8°C)</b>, a persistent cough, <b>pus or foul drainage</b>, boils or abscesses, <b>mouth ulcers</b>, and cloudy or burning urine. Don’t share razors or toothbrushes. <b>Bathe daily</b>, brush and floss for <b>2 minutes</b>, replace the toothbrush every <b>3 months</b>, and see the dentist every <b>6 months</b>. <b>No undercooked meat, fish or eggs.</b> <b>Don’t change litter boxes</b> (gloves if unavoidable), keep <b>no reptiles or turtles</b>, and don’t feed pets raw meat.</div>\n<div><b>In the hospital (Box 17.12)</b> — use a <b>private room</b> when possible, <b>limit the staff</b> who enter, perform <b>hand hygiene</b> on entering and leaving, allow <b>only healthy adult visitors</b>, and have the room cleaned <b>daily</b>. Keep <b>dedicated equipment</b> (BP cuff, stethoscope, thermometer) and a <b>dedicated box of gloves</b> in the room; take no supplies from common areas, and use individually wrapped supplies and single-use utensils. Inspect the <b>mouth, skin and mucous membranes (including the anal area)</b> every shift and the <b>IV sites every 4 hours</b>. Change <b>gauze dressings daily</b>, <b>culture</b> suspicious areas, encourage <b>coughing and deep breathing</b>, and use <b>strict asepsis</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>With few working white cells, the body can’t produce the heat, redness and pus that normally announce an infection. So the nurse looks harder, with scheduled checks of the mouth, lungs, skin and urine, and treats small clues as big ones.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Breathing, nutrition, diarrhea and skin</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Gas exchange</b> — in stage III, <b>trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole</b> is often used to <b>prevent PCP</b>, and it also treats it. Monitor <b>kidney function and electrolytes (potassium)</b>. Assess the respiratory rate, rhythm and depth, breath sounds and vital signs at least <b>every 8 hours</b>, and <b>report cyanosis</b>. Raise the <b>head of the bed</b>. Give <b>oxygen</b>, bronchodilators and, for moderate to severe PCP, <b>glucocorticoids</b>, then <b>reassess</b> soon after. <b>Pace activities</b> to save energy; when fatigue is extreme, postpone nonessential activity, teaching and visitors.</div>\n<div><b>Pain</b> — in stage II, <b>peripheral neuropathy</b> from the drugs or the disease. In stage III, <b>enlarged organs</b> stretching the abdomen or pressing on nerves, and <b>tumors</b> invading bone. Some states allow <b>medical cannabis</b> for stage III.</div>\n<div><b>Nutrition</b> — anorexia, nausea, painful swallowing and poor absorption can lead to <b>AIDS (HIV-associated) wasting syndrome</b>: an unplanned loss of <b>10% or more</b> of body weight and lean muscle. Find the cause; the provider may change a drug that is to blame. Treat <b>thrush and Candida esophagitis</b> with <b>systemic fluconazole</b> and brushing with a <b>soft toothbrush</b> several times a day (brush dentures and soak them in <b>chlorhexidine</b>). The dietitian plans <b>high-protein, high-calorie</b> meals, with <b>soft, cool, easy-to-swallow</b> foods (pudding, yogurt) for mouth and throat pain. Avoid raw or rare meat, fish and poultry, wash produce, and refrigerate food. <b>Somatropin</b> (growth hormone) may be used for wasting in a patient on ART with no active cancer. It is a daily <b>subcutaneous injection at bedtime</b>, with sites rotated. Report <b>thirst, frequent urination, headache, vision change</b> or <b>abdominal pain</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diarrhea and skin</b> — persistent <b>diarrhea</b> comes from infections or drugs. Give antidiarrheals and do <b>perineal care</b> (keep the area clean and dry; involve the wound care specialist). Report even <b>a small pimple</b> there, and expect IV fluids and electrolytes for dehydration. <b>Kaposi sarcoma</b> appears as <b>pink, red, purple or brown</b> raised lesions, sometimes with a <b>yellow halo</b>, on the skin, mucous membranes or organs; a stool test for blood and a chest x-ray check for internal spread. It often <b>shrinks with ART</b>; local chemotherapy, radiation or <b>alitretinoin</b> may also be used. Keep open, <b>weeping lesions clean and dressed</b>; long sleeves or hats can help with appearance. For <b>herpes simplex and zoster</b>, keep the area clean and dry, give analgesics for zoster, and give <b>acyclovir, valacyclovir or famciclovir</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>In stage III, ordinary problems such as a sore mouth, diarrhea or a small skin break quickly become infection, malnutrition or sepsis. Care is about keeping the <b>body’s barriers and fuel</b> intact.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Conditions found in stage III (Table 17.3)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Lungs</b> — <b>PCP</b>: an ongoing <b>dry cough</b>, dyspnea, <b>rapid breathing</b> and a persistent <b>low-grade fever</b> → oxygen and antimicrobials. <b>TB</b>: cough, chest pain, <b>fever, night sweats</b> and <b>weight loss</b> → a <b>rapid molecular assay</b> (not a sputum smear) to detect it, <b>Airborne Precautions</b> and anti-TB drugs. <b>MAC</b>: a chronic cough, <b>coughing up blood</b>, and a flulike illness → antimicrobials; the fever may take <b>2–4 weeks</b> to fall. <b>COPD</b>: stop smoking; home oxygen.</div>\n<div><b>Brain and nerves</b> — <b>cryptococcosis</b> (a meningitis): <b>headache, stiff neck, mental status change</b> → safety precautions and pain relief. <b>Toxoplasmosis encephalitis</b> (from <b>cat feces</b> or <b>undercooked meat</b>): mental change, headache, fever, trouble with speech or gait, and <b>seizures</b> → <b>report any new deficit or seizure at once</b>. <b>HIV-associated neurocognitive disorders</b> range from mild impairment to <b>HIV-associated dementia</b> → cognitive training, exercise, home safety and help with ADLs. <b>Increased intracranial pressure</b> (a falling level of consciousness, <b>unequal pupils</b>) → <b>notify the provider immediately</b>. <b>Neuropathy</b> and changes in gait or vision → safety measures.</div>\n<div><b>Mouth, gut and hormones</b> — <b>Candida</b> in the mouth, throat or vagina causes <b>painful swallowing</b> → soft foods, <b>watch for aspiration</b>, antifungals. <b>Cryptosporidiosis</b> and <b>Salmonella</b> cause diarrhea and electrolyte loss → fluids, electrolytes, skin care, antibiotics. <b>Wasting</b> with fever and <b>≥ 10% weight loss</b> → high-protein supplements. <b>Adrenal insufficiency</b> causes fatigue and weight loss → teach energy conservation. There can also be low testosterone or menstrual changes, and <b>lipodystrophy</b> (fat redistribution, less common with newer drugs; tesamorelin may help).</div>\n<div><b>Heart, kidneys, skin and cancers</b> — <b>coronary disease</b> and <b>dysrhythmias</b>, with high lipids and glucose → monitor and report. <b>HIV-associated nephropathy</b> causes a fast decline in kidney function and <b>proteinuria</b> → monitor the <b>GFR</b> and report any drop. <b>Kaposi sarcoma</b> → track the number, size and location of the lesions. Generalized <b>rashes</b> → reposition at least every 2 hours. <b>HIV-related lymphomas</b> are often <b>advanced</b> when found. <b>HPV</b> causes anal and cervical dysplasia → encourage <b>cervical screening</b> and the <b>HPV vaccine</b> (ideally before age 26, and up to 47 on the provider’s advice). For <b>zoster</b>, encourage patients without HIV symptoms to ask the provider about the <b>Shingrix</b> vaccine.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Table 17.3 is organized by body system, but the theme doesn’t change. An infection or cancer that a healthy immune system would contain becomes <b>visible in the organ it attacks</b>: the lungs, brain, gut or skin. Assess every system every time.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Home care and evaluation (Box 17.13)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Planning for home</b> — HIV is managed mostly <b>at home</b>; hospital stays come with severe infections. Before discharge, assess self-care ability, the caregivers, and plans to stay in touch with providers. With the case manager, arrange <b>respite or home care</b> and help with <b>housing, finances, insurance, legal matters and spiritual needs</b>; refer to <b>hospice</b> at the end of life. Teach the patient to use <b>electronic personal health records</b> or apps for medications, appointments and symptoms. Veterans can register for the VA’s <b>My HealtheVet</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Infection control at home (Box 17.13)</b> — follow <b>Standard Precautions</b> and good hand washing. Use <b>new gloves</b> for cleaning, laundry and waste, and never share razors or toothbrushes. Wash dishes in <b>hot, soapy water</b> or a dishwasher. For a disinfectant, mix <b>¼ cup of bleach in 1 gallon of water</b>. Wipe up body fluids with gloves on, then disinfect; clean the toilet often and bedpans after each use. <b>Laundry</b>: a normal wash kills the virus (bleach is optional). Handle soiled items with gloves, flush any solid waste, bag the items until washed, and <b>soak stains in cold water</b>. Flush liquid and solid waste, and <b>bag and seal</b> items soiled with blood or body fluids. Put <b>sharps</b> in a puncture-proof container with a tight cap, such as a laundry detergent bottle. At <b>75% full</b>, tape it shut, write “<b>DO NOT RECYCLE</b>” on it, and put it in the regular trash.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — the patient takes the drugs <b>as prescribed</b>, practices <b>safer sex</b>, and keeps a <b>high quality of life</b> (in their own view) by <b>not spreading HIV</b>, <b>avoiding acute infections</b> and <b>avoiding opportunistic infections</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Home infection control protects <b>both ways</b>: caregivers from the patient’s body fluids, and the patient from the household’s germs.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"17-lyme-disease","ch":"17","chTitle":"Allergy and immunity conditions","title":"Lyme disease","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Stages, treatment and prevention (Box 17.14)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Cause</b> — the spirochete <i>Borrelia burgdorferi</i>, spread by the bite of an infected <b>deer tick</b> (black-legged tick, <i>Ixodes</i>). It is the <b>most common vector-borne disease</b> in North America and is <b>reportable</b>; most US cases occur in the <b>Northeast, upper Midwest and Northwest</b>. It isn’t a true autoimmune disease, because the antibodies target the <b>organism</b>, not self tissue. But the ongoing attack causes <b>chronic inflammation</b> that adds to the damage.</div>\n<div><b>Stage 1 — early localized</b> — a <b>bull’s-eye rash (erythema migrans)</b> and a <b>low-grade fever</b>, sometimes with muscle aches and headache. Your book lists <b>doxycycline, amoxicillin or cefuroxime</b> after a bite, and <b>azithromycin or clarithromycin</b> with a penicillin allergy. With early treatment, most people recover within about <b>20 days</b>. <i>Beyond the book:</i> current guidelines give a <b>single dose of doxycycline</b> within 72 hours of removing a high-risk tick to prevent infection, and longer courses to <b>treat</b> the rash stage.</div>\n<div><b>Stages 2 and 3</b> — if it goes untreated, or treatment fails, <b>early disseminated</b> disease can follow <b>3–12 weeks</b> later: flulike symptoms, <b>knee, ankle and wrist pain</b>, <b>dysrhythmias</b>, palpitations, dyspnea and encephalopathy. <b>Late</b> disease, <b>months to years</b> later, brings <b>knee arthritis</b>, radicular (nerve-root) pain, dysrhythmias or <b>transient heart block</b>, and <b>cognitive deficits</b>. Treatment depends on age and progression; antibiotics are common, and patients with <b>heart involvement</b> are often admitted.</div>\n<div><b>Prevention and tick removal (Box 17.14)</b> — avoid <b>wooded and brushy</b> areas, especially in spring and summer, and walk in the <b>center of trails</b>. Put <b>DEET</b> on skin and clothes, and wear <b>light-colored</b> clothing. Wear <b>long sleeves and long pants</b>, with the shirt tucked into the pants and the pants into the socks or boots, plus closed shoes and a hat. <b>Bathe</b> after being outdoors, and check for ticks (about pinhead size), especially on the <b>arms, legs and scalp</b>; check pets too. Remove a tick with <b>tweezers, pulling straight up with steady pressure</b>. Dispose of it in <b>alcohol, a sealed bag, tape or the toilet</b>. <b>Don’t use nail polish, petroleum jelly or heat</b> to make it let go. Clean the bite with alcohol or soap and water. Report a <b>rash or fever</b> within several weeks of a bite.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Lyme disease is easiest to cure in stage 1, when the rash is the clue. Teaching people to check for ticks and to report a rash or flulike illness after a bite catches it before it reaches the joints, heart and brain.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"17-transplant-rejection-and-anti-rejection","ch":"17","chTitle":"Allergy and immunity conditions","title":"Transplant rejection and anti-rejection drugs","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Why grafts are rejected (Table 17.5)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Non-self tissue</b> — unless the donor is an <b>identical sibling</b>, the donor organ’s <b>HLAs</b> won’t match perfectly, so the recipient’s immune system reads it as <b>non-self</b>. <b>NK cells and cytotoxic T cells</b> lead the attack, and <b>cell-mediated immunity</b> contributes most. Every recipient takes <b>lifelong combination immunosuppression</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Hyperacute</b> — within <b>minutes to hours</b>. The recipient already has <b>antibodies</b> to the donor’s antigens, including blood-group antigens. Complexes in the graft’s vessels activate <b>complement</b>, and <b>clots</b> form throughout the organ, causing <b>ischemic necrosis</b>. It <b>can’t be stopped</b>, so the organ is <b>removed</b>. It is now <b>extremely rare</b> thanks to careful HLA matching (it used to occur mostly with kidneys).</div>\n<div><b>Acute</b> — at <b>1 week to 3 months</b> after the transplant, or even years later; <b>most recipients have at least one episode</b>. Antibodies inflame and destroy the graft’s vessels (<b>vasculitis</b>, causing necrosis rather than blockage), and <b>cytotoxic T cells and NK cells</b> enter the graft and destroy its cells. After a kidney transplant, look for tenderness over the graft, <b>proteinuria &gt; 1 g/day</b> or a <b>new rise in creatinine</b>; a <b>biopsy</b> confirms it. An episode <b>doesn’t mean the organ is lost</b>, because prompt drug treatment can save it.</div>\n<div><b>Chronic</b> — over <b>months to years</b>, chronic inflammation and <b>scarring</b> set in. The smooth muscle of the arteries overgrows and <b>blocks</b> them, the tissue becomes <b>fibrotic</b>, and function falls with the scarring. In the lungs, the <b>small airways thicken</b>; in the liver, the <b>bile ducts</b> are destroyed. In the heart, <b>cardiac allograft vasculopathy</b> is one of the top three causes of death after the first year.</div>\n<div><b>Signs of rejection (Table 17.5)</b> — <b>fever &gt; 100°F (37.8°C)</b> and chills (after a kidney, liver, lung or pancreas transplant). <b>Heart</b>: ankle edema, hypotension, low energy, <b>a fast heart rate or arrhythmias</b>, less tolerance for exercise, shortness of breath, and <b>weight gain of at least 2.2 lb (1 kg) a day</b>. <b>Kidney</b>: <b>less or no urine</b>, swelling of the hands, legs or eyelids, <b>tenderness over the graft</b>, and <b>weight gain of 2–4 lb (1–2 kg) in 24 hours</b>. <b>Liver</b>: abdominal tenderness. <b>Lung</b>: a <b>dry cough</b>, low energy, <b>falling home spirometry</b>, chest pain, a poor appetite or nausea, and dyspnea. <b>Pancreas</b>: <b>rising glucose</b> and tenderness over the graft. Teach that <b>flulike symptoms</b> after a transplant need <b>emergency</b> evaluation.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The signs of rejection are the signs of a <b>failing organ</b> plus <b>inflammation</b> (fever, tenderness). Anti-rejection drugs blunt inflammation, so even mild flulike symptoms deserve an urgent look.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Anti-rejection drugs (Table 17.6)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Calcineurin inhibitors</b> — <b>cyclosporine</b>: about <b>50%</b> of patients develop <b>hypertension</b>. It can damage the liver and kidneys, so expect periodic labs. <b>Avoid grapefruit juice</b>, which can raise the drug level by <b>50–200%</b>. <b>Tacrolimus</b>: <b>gum overgrowth</b> and <b>nerve toxicity</b> (headaches, <b>tremors</b>, insomnia). It can damage the kidneys; expect periodic labs.</div>\n<div><b>Steroids and cytotoxic drugs</b> — <b>prednisone</b>: thin skin and <b>osteoporosis</b>, so teach <b>fall and fracture prevention</b>. <b>Azathioprine</b>: <b>neutropenia, thrombocytopenia</b>, and (rarely) marrow suppression and <b>cancer</b>; nausea, vomiting and <b>pancreatitis</b> are common. <b>Cyclophosphamide</b>: take it <b>with food</b>; <b>hair loss</b> can occur; stay <b>hydrated</b> and report urinary changes, because it can cause <b>hemorrhagic cystitis</b>. <b>Leflunomide</b>: <b>teratogenic</b>, so contraception is needed, and it can stay teratogenic for <b>up to 2 years</b> after stopping. <b>Mycophenolate</b>: diarrhea and vomiting; <b>avoid magnesium or aluminum antacids and cholestyramine</b>, which reduce its absorption.</div>\n<div><b>mTOR inhibitors and antibodies</b> — <b>everolimus</b>: edema, constipation, hypertension, nausea, anemia, UTIs and <b>high lipids</b>; <b>avoid grapefruit</b> and <b>large high-fat meals</b>. <b>Sirolimus</b>: <b>cholesterol and triglycerides</b> rise, and high-fat food raises its absorption by <b>35%</b>. Watch the <b>kidneys closely when it is given with cyclosporine</b>. <b>No live vaccines</b>, and no grapefruit. <b>Basiliximab</b> (a monoclonal antibody): <b>anaphylaxis</b> is rare; monitor after the dose, with the <b>crash cart ready</b>. <b>Antithymocyte globulin</b> (horse or rabbit): ask about <b>premedication</b> (a steroid, an antihistamine, an antipyretic). Infuse it at <b>room temperature over at least 4–6 hours</b> the first time. <b>Slow it for fever or chills</b>, and <b>stop it</b> if a reaction starts; keep the crash cart ready. <b>No live vaccines.</b></div>\n<div><b>Teaching and safety</b> — all of these drugs <b>suppress immunity</b>, so teach the <b>risk of infection</b>. People who may become <b>pregnant</b> should talk with the provider <b>before</b> a pregnancy, because some of the drugs are unsafe. Follow <b>hazardous-drug handling</b> (NIOSH) for cyclosporine, tacrolimus, everolimus, sirolimus, azathioprine, cyclophosphamide and mycophenolate. Some patients stop the drugs, by choice or because of the cost. That doesn’t usually cause immediate death, but the <b>pain, fever, weakness and delirium</b> of acute rejection follow, so discuss <b>palliative care</b> beforehand. The <b>transplant team</b> shares the follow-up: physicians, coordinators, nurses, social workers and case managers.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Anti-rejection drugs walk a line: enough suppression to protect the graft, but not so much that infection or toxicity wins. <b>Grapefruit</b>, missed doses and drug interactions can tip the balance either way.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"17-adpie","ch":"17","chTitle":"Allergy and immunity conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">For allergy and immunity, the nurse asks three questions. <b>Is the airway safe?</b> (allergen exposure, swelling, stridor, hypotension.) <b>Is the body attacking itself?</b> (lupus flares, organ damage, transplant rejection.) <b>Is immunity failing?</b> (HIV stage, CD4+ count, opportunistic infections.) Then the nurse rescues, protects the organs, prevents infection and transmission, and teaches.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div>ask about <b>allergies and the reaction</b> each one caused, and about drugs, especially <b>ACE inhibitors</b>, NSAIDs, hydralazine, isoniazid and procainamide. Ask about a family history of <b>autoimmune</b> disease. For lupus, ask about symptoms over <b>5 years</b>, <b>sun</b> exposure, fevers and fatigue. For HIV, ask about <b>risk factors</b>, take a respectful <b>sexual history</b> (the 5 Ps), and ask when the diagnosis was made, about past infections, and about <b>adherence</b>. For transplant recipients, ask about the drug schedule and <b>grapefruit</b>. Ask about tick exposure.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical and labs</div>check the airway, <b>breath sounds and stridor</b>, SpO₂, BP and the skin (<b>hives, angioedema</b>, butterfly rash, Kaposi lesions), plus the joints, <b>urine</b> (foam, blood) and weight. In HIV, check the mouth, lungs and perirectal skin, and the neuro status (level of consciousness, pupils). Look for organ-specific signs of rejection. Labs: the <b>CBC with differential</b>, ANA, <b>creatinine</b>, urine protein, the <b>CD4+ count</b>, <b>viral load</b> and ANC, and drug levels with kidney and liver function.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The same few findings answer the three questions: airway and BP for a reaction, urine and creatinine for lupus, the CD4+ count and new infections for HIV.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>airway obstruction and shock (<b>anaphylaxis, angioedema</b>). In SLE: <b>pain</b>, <b>fatigue</b> and the potential for <b>organ failure</b>. In HIV: the <b>potential to transmit</b> the virus, the <b>potential for infection and life-threatening complications</b>, poor nutrition (wasting) and skin breakdown. In transplant recipients: <b>rejection</b> and <b>infection</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>the airway and BP are maintained. In SLE, pain is at a level the patient accepts, energy lasts for valued activities, and <b>remissions are longer</b>. In HIV, there is <b>no transmission</b>, infections are prevented or treated quickly, weight is stable and drugs are taken as prescribed. In transplant recipients, the graft keeps working without infection.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Rescue</div>for <b>anaphylaxis</b>: call the Rapid Response Team, stop any suspect infusion and give <b>IM epinephrine</b>, repeated every 5–15 minutes. Lay the patient flat with the legs raised, give nonrebreather oxygen at 90–100% and a rapid <b>normal saline</b> infusion, then albuterol, antihistamines and steroids. For <b>angioedema</b>: oxygen, epinephrine and steroids; <b>intubate</b> for stridor or inability to swallow; watch for 2–6 hours afterward.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Protect and treat</div>in <b>lupus</b>: steroids (taper them; never stop abruptly), <b>hydroxychloroquine with eye exams</b>, immunosuppressants with <b>infection teaching</b>, <b>sun protection</b> and pacing. In <b>HIV</b>: <b>cART taken as prescribed</b>, prophylaxis (TMP-SMX), the precautions in Boxes 17.11 and 17.12, nutrition (soft, cool, high-protein foods), and skin and perineal care. In <b>transplant</b> recipients: lifelong drugs, no grapefruit, and report flulike symptoms.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Teach and prevent</div>teach autoinjector care and use (<b>carry two</b>, inject into the outer thigh, then call 911) and an allergy bracelet. Teach <b>safer sex</b>, syringe services, <b>PrEP, PEP and TasP</b>, and testing. Teach tick prevention and removal. Use <b>Standard Precautions</b> for every patient.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>after a reaction, breathing and BP are normal, and there is a plan to avoid the allergen. In lupus, pain is acceptable, energy is kept, remissions are longer and eye exams are done. In HIV, the viral load is falling or undetectable, the CD4+ count is rising, and there are no new infections, no transmission and a stable weight. The graft keeps working. The patient explains the drug and safety plan.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"22-overview","ch":"22","chTitle":"Assessment of the respiratory system","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">22 · Chapter 22</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Gas exchange · perfusion</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Assessment of the respiratory system</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> This chapter is the toolkit every later respiratory chapter uses. You are answering one question in four ways — inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation — and the question is whether air is reaching alveoli that can actually exchange it. Learn what each finding <i>means</i> mechanically and you will never memorize a list of adventitious sounds again.</p><div id=\"ch22-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;font-size:11.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Air moves in (ventilation), crosses a one-cell membrane (diffusion), and binds hemoglobin; CO₂ leaves the same way.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Any step can fail: blocked airway, stiff or flooded alveoli, weak muscles, or depressed drive.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Tachypnea, accessory muscles, tripoding, restlessness, adventitious sounds, falling SpO₂.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">SpO₂ (oxygenation), ETCO₂ and ABG PaCO₂ (ventilation), CXR, PFTs, bronchoscopy, thoracentesis.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Oxygen, upright position, airway clearance — and fixing whichever step failed.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Fatigue → hypoventilation → rising CO₂ → acidosis → respiratory failure.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Silent chest, stridor, new confusion, SpO₂ &lt;90%, RR falling with drowsiness, tracheal deviation.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Opioid or sedative<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>respiratory center depressed<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>slow, shallow breathing<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>CO₂ rises while SpO₂ looks fine on oxygen<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>monitor RR, sedation and ETCO₂, not SpO₂ alone</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Fluid in alveoli<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>fine crackles that do not clear with cough<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>longer diffusion path<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>SpO₂ falls<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>oxygen plus treating the fluid cause</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Rhonchi vs fine crackles</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Rhonchi = secretions in big airways, often clear with cough. Fine crackles = alveoli popping open against fluid, do not clear.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Dead space vs shunt</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Dead space (PE) = air without blood. Shunt (pneumonia) = blood without air — responds poorly to oxygen alone.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Amiodarone/bleomycin → lung injury; ACE inhibitors → bradykinin → dry cough; nonselective beta blockers → beta2 blocked → bronchospasm. <a href=\"Exam 2 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Connections to other chapters</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Every later chapter uses this toolkit: ch 24 obstruction, ch 25 consolidation, ch 29 pulmonary edema. Anemia (ch 34) lowers oxygen content even with normal SpO₂.</div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Stable SpO₂ at the client’s baseline, clear speech, unchanged sounds.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">New confusion, stridor, silent chest, sudden dyspnea after a procedure, SpO₂ &lt;90% not responding.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the anatomy and physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Upper airway</b> — nose and sinuses (warm, humidify, filter), pharynx, larynx with the epiglottis and vocal cords. This is where obstruction is mechanical and fast, and where the <span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>the flap that closes the airway when you swallow</em><b>epi-</b> upon · <b>glottis</b> the opening of the larynx</i><u>epi</u><s>·</s><u>glottis</u></span> protects the lungs from everything you swallow.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Lower airway</b> — trachea, right and left mainstem bronchi (the <b>right is more vertical</b>, which is why aspiration and a dislodged tube go right), bronchioles with smooth muscle that constricts in asthma, and alveoli lined with <span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>the fatty film that keeps alveoli from collapsing</em><b>surf</b>ace-<b>act</b>ive <b>agent</b></i><u>surfactant</u></span>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Alveolar-capillary membrane</b> — one cell thick on each side. <span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>gases moving from where there is more to where there is less</em><b>dia-</b> apart · <b>-fus-</b> to pour</i><u>Diffusion</u></span> happens down a pressure gradient, so anything that thickens the membrane (fluid, fibrosis, pus) or removes surface area (collapse, resection, emphysema) reduces it.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Ventilation versus perfusion (V/Q)</b> — air without blood is <b>dead space</b> (pulmonary embolism); blood without air is a <b>shunt</b> (pneumonia, <span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>alveoli that never fully inflate</em><b>atel-</b> incomplete · <b>-ectasis</b> expansion</i><u>atel</u><s>·</s><u>ectasis</u></span>, pulmonary edema). Both cause hypoxemia; only one improves much with oxygen alone.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Control of breathing</b> — central chemoreceptors respond to <b>CO₂ and pH</b> (the normal drive); peripheral chemoreceptors respond to low O₂. This is the physiology behind the caution about high-flow oxygen in chronic CO₂ retainers.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Aging changes</b> — alveoli lose elasticity, the chest wall stiffens, respiratory muscle strength falls, cough and cilia weaken, and PaO₂ falls with age. Same pneumonia, fewer symptoms, less reserve.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every respiratory finding you will be asked about is one of four mechanical problems: the airway is narrowed, the alveoli are collapsed, the membrane is thickened, or the blood is not arriving. Wheeze = narrowed. Crackles = fluid or reopening alveoli. Diminished = air not getting there. Absent with tracheal deviation = a space filled by air or blood pushing the lung away. Naming the mechanism gives you both the priority and the intervention.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","ch":"22","chTitle":"Assessment of the respiratory system","title":"Inhalation injury and smoking cessation","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Inhalation injury and smoking cessation</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The single biggest modifiable factor</b> — exposure to inhalation irritants, above all cigarette smoke, is the most common cause of chronic respiratory problems and physical limitation. The damaging compounds are <b>tar, nicotine, and carbon monoxide</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Quantify it</b> — <b>pack-years = packs per day × years smoked</b>. Ask separately about <b>secondhand</b> exposure (same environment) and <b>thirdhand</b> (smoke clinging to hair, clothing, and surfaces).</div>\n<div><b>Catch the social smoker</b> — ask \"do you ever smoke in social situations?\" They do not identify as smokers, yet intermittent smoking still damages and still leads to addiction.</div>\n<div><b>Gauge dependence</b> — do you smoke as soon as you wake? wake at night to smoke? find it hard not to smoke where it is prohibited? smoke when ill? More yes answers, greater dependence.</div>\n<div><b>The 5 As</b> — <b>Ask</b> about use, <b>Advise</b> to quit, <b>Assess</b> willingness, <b>Assist</b> with the attempt, <b>Arrange</b> follow-up. It is a brief intervention, under three minutes, and it is a nursing responsibility at every encounter.</div>\n<div><b>Nicotine replacement</b> — patches, gum, and lozenges over the counter; nasal spray and inhaler by prescription. <b>Never smoke while using NRT</b> — the combined nicotine load raises the risk of stroke and heart attack.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs</b> — <b>bupropion</b> reduces cravings, withdrawal, and associated depression but carries a <b>black box warning for suicidal thoughts and behavior</b>: teach the patient and family to report any change in thinking or behavior immediately. <b>Varenicline</b> blocks nicotine receptors so smoking is less rewarding.</div>\n<div><b>Vaping and hookah</b> — hookah exposure is <b>as great as or greater than</b> cigarettes despite the widespread belief it is safer. <b>EVALI</b> (e-cigarette or vaping product use–associated lung injury) has caused serious injury in people vaping under two years, with higher risk from THC-containing products.</div>\n<div><b>Occupational and environmental screening — I PREPARE</b> — <b>I</b>nvestigate exposures, <b>P</b>resent work, <b>R</b>esidence, <b>E</b>nvironment, <b>P</b>ast work, <b>A</b>ctivities and hobbies, <b>R</b>esources and referrals, <b>E</b>ducate. Higher-risk trades include coal miners, stone masons, welders, woodworkers, potters, bakers, printers, and grain and foundry workers.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Exposure history is a <i>physical</i> assessment finding, not social small talk — it changes the differential before you touch the patient. And the reason the 5 As are compressed into three minutes is that willingness to quit is not a precondition for the conversation: advice given briefly and repeatedly at every contact is what moves patients between stages, which is why \"the patient isn't interested\" is not a reason to skip it.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"22-reading-the-numbers-oximetry-capnography","ch":"22","chTitle":"Assessment of the respiratory system","title":"Reading the numbers — oximetry, capnography, PFTs","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Reading the numbers — oximetry, capnography, PFTs</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Oximetry thresholds</b> — normal 95–100%. <b>Below 90%</b> in an adult without chronic lung disease demands immediate assessment and intervention; <b>below 85%</b> tissues struggle to oxygenate; <b>below 75%</b> is critical and life-threatening. In chronic hypercarbia, above 90% may be the accepted target.</div>\n<div><b>When the reading lies</b> — poor perfusion, hypothermia, motion, dark nail polish, and carbon monoxide poisoning (falsely normal). With impaired peripheral perfusion the <b>forehead</b> is the most accurate site.</div>\n<div><b>Health equity</b> — oximetry is less accurate in patients with darker skin and hypoxia is missed more often, so judge the <b>trend</b> alongside ABGs and clinical signs rather than one absolute number.</div>\n<div><b>Capnometry versus capnography</b> — both measure exhaled CO₂; capnometry gives a color or number, capnography adds the waveform. <b>Normal end-tidal CO₂ is 35–45 mm Hg.</b></div>\n<div><b>Why ETCO₂ warns earlier</b> — carbon dioxide leaves the body more readily than oxygen enters it, so ETCO₂ shifts <i>before</i> hypoxia is detectable. Raised by hypoventilation, partial obstruction, rebreathing, fever, acidosis; lowered by pulmonary embolism, apnea, total obstruction, tube malposition, and cardiac arrest. During a code it also reports whether CPR is effective and whether circulation has returned.</div>\n<div><b>PFTs — what and why</b> — most often ordered to find the cause of dyspnea, and with exercise they separate lung causes from cardiac ones and from muscle weakness. Results are compared with expected values for age, sex, race, height, weight, and smoking status.</div>\n<div><b>PFT preparation</b> — no smoking for <b>6–8 hours</b>; bronchodilators may be withheld 4–6 hours depending on the question being asked. The test is effort-dependent, so coaching quality <i>is</i> data quality.</div>\n<div><b>The values</b> — <b>FVC</b>, all air forcibly exhaled after a maximal breath in. <b>FEV1</b>, the volume in the first second. The <b>FEV1/FVC ratio</b> is the hallmark of obstruction — reduced in obstructive disease, normal or high in restrictive. RV, FRC, and TLC rise with air trapping in emphysema and fall in restrictive disease. <b>DLCO</b> measures the alveolocapillary surface area and drops in emphysema, fibrosis, and pulmonary hypertension.</div>\n<div><b>After the test</b> — assess for bronchospasm or increased dyspnea from the repeated maneuvers and document any drugs given during testing.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Saturation and ventilation are different questions, and the pulse oximeter only answers the first. A patient on high-flow oxygen can hold a perfect SpO₂ while their CO₂ climbs and they slide into narcosis — which is exactly the gap capnography fills, and exactly why an opioid-sedated patient is monitored for <i>respiratory rate and CO₂</i>, not saturation alone.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","ch":"22","chTitle":"Assessment of the respiratory system","title":"Oxygen as a drug — devices, doses, hazards","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--cal-amber);border-top:5px solid var(--cal-amber);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--cal-amber)\">Oxygen as a drug — devices, doses, hazards</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The principle</b> — oxygen is prescribed with a dose, has therapeutic and toxic effects, and requires assessment of response. The goal is the <b>lowest FiO₂ that achieves an acceptable blood oxygen level</b>. Room air is 21%; most hypoxic patients need 2–4 L/min by cannula or up to 40% by Venturi mask. Fever, sepsis, and anemia raise the requirement.</div>\n<div><b>Low-flow versus high-flow</b> — low-flow systems do not supply the whole inspired volume, so room air dilutes the oxygen and the delivered concentration varies with the patient's rate and pattern. High-flow systems supply the total inspired volume and deliver a precise concentration.</div>\n<div><b>Nasal cannula</b> — 1–6 L/min, roughly 24% at 1 L rising about 4% per liter to 44%. <b>Not run above 6 L/min</b>: anatomic dead space is already full, so exchange does not improve and the mucosa dries and cracks.</div>\n<div><b>Simple mask</b> — 40–60% at 5–8 L/min, with a <b>minimum of 5 L/min to flush exhaled CO₂</b> out of the mask. <b>Partial rebreather</b> — 60–75% at 6–11 L/min; the reservoir bag must stay slightly inflated at end-inspiration and must not kink.</div>\n<div><b>Nonrebreather</b> — the highest low-flow concentration, 80–95%+ at 10–15 L/min. <b>Check at least hourly that the valve and flaps move freely</b>; a stuck valve drops the FiO₂ sharply and can suffocate the patient.</div>\n<div><b>Venturi mask</b> — the most accurate concentration short of intubation, 24–50%. <b>Never cover or obstruct the entrainment orifice.</b> <b>High-flow nasal cannula</b> — precise FiO₂ at 30–60 L/min, heated and humidified, better tolerated, and the patient can still talk and clear secretions. <b>Face tent</b> for facial trauma or burns; <b>aerosol mask</b> when high humidity is needed.</div>\n<div><b>NPPV</b> — positive pressure without intubation for dyspnea, hypercarbia, COPD exacerbation, pulmonary edema, and acute asthma. <b>CPAP</b> holds one pressure through the whole cycle and reopens collapsed alveoli; <b>BiPAP</b> delivers a higher inspiratory and lower expiratory pressure, improving tidal volume.</div>\n<div><b>Combustion</b> — oxygen does not burn or explode but it <b>enhances combustion</b>: no open flame or smoking, signage posted, grounded three-prong equipment, no frayed cords, and no high-alcohol or oil-based solutions in the room.</div>\n<div><b>Oxygen toxicity</b> — determined by concentration, duration, and existing lung disease; above <b>50% for more than 24–48 hours</b> may injure the lung. Early signs: dyspnea, nonproductive cough, substernal chest pain, GI upset, crackles. Notify when the <b>PaO₂ rises above 90 mm Hg</b> so the dose can come down — prevention is the only management.</div>\n<div><b>Absorptive atelectasis</b> — nitrogen (79% of room air) normally splints alveoli open. High oxygen washes the nitrogen out, oxygen diffuses into the blood, and the alveolus collapses. Watch for new crackles and reduced breath sounds; assess <b>every 1–2 hours</b> when high-level therapy begins.</div>\n<div><b>Drying and infection</b> — humidify above <b>4 L/min</b> and confirm the oxygen actually bubbles through the water. Drain condensation by disconnecting the tubing; <b>never drain it back into the humidifier</b>. Check ears, neck, and face every 4–8 hours, mouth care every 8 hours, and lubricate nostrils and lips with a <b>nonpetroleum</b> product.</div>\n<div><b>Home oxygen</b> — Medicare criteria are a <b>PaO₂ below 55 mm Hg or SpO₂ below 88%</b> on room air at rest. Sources: compressed gas in a green tank (check the gauge, secure the tank in a stand), liquid oxygen (lasts longer, costs more), and a concentrator that extracts nitrogen from room air — which <b>needs power</b>, so teach a backup plan for outages.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every hazard on this list comes from the same fact: oxygen is a treatment with a dose-dependent toxicity, so \"more is safer\" is wrong in three separate ways. Too much for too long inflames the lung, washes out the nitrogen that holds alveoli open, and in the chronic retainer blunts the very stimulus to breathe. That is why the order names a dose and why titrating <i>down</i> when the PaO₂ climbs is a nursing action rather than an afterthought.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"22-endoscopy-thoracentesis-and-biopsy-the-n","ch":"22","chTitle":"Assessment of the respiratory system","title":"Endoscopy, thoracentesis, and biopsy — the nursing care","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Endoscopy, thoracentesis, and biopsy — the nursing care</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The three endoscopic studies</b> — <b>bronchoscopy</b> views the airways to the secondary bronchi and takes samples; <b>laryngoscopy</b> assesses vocal cords, removes foreign bodies, and biopsies; <b>mediastinoscopy</b> enters through the chest wall above the sternum to examine between the lungs.</div>\n<div><b>Before</b> — consent, <b>two patient identifiers</b> per National Patient Safety Goals, NPO 4–8 hours, and often CBC, platelets, prothrombin time, electrolytes, and a chest x-ray.</div>\n<div><b>After bronchoscopy</b> — <b>NPO until the gag reflex returns</b>; vital signs, breath sounds, and oxygen saturation <b>every 15 minutes for 2 hours</b>. Blood-streaked sputum is expected; frank bleeding, stridor, or new dyspnea is reported.</div>\n<div><b>Thoracentesis positioning</b> — sitting upright leaning over a bedside table, arms supported; the patient is told to <b>hold still and not cough or breathe deeply</b> during needle insertion. The nurse steadies the patient and the table and watches for shock, pain, nausea, pallor or ash-gray skin, diaphoresis, cyanosis, tachypnea, and dyspnea.</div>\n<div><b>Thoracentesis limits and after care</b> — generally <b>no more than about 1000 mL at once</b> to avoid re-expansion pulmonary edema; chest x-ray afterward; document the patient's response, the volume and character of the fluid, specimens sent, the puncture site, and respiratory assessment before, during, and after.</div>\n<div><b>Teach the pneumothorax signs for the next 24 hours</b> — pain on the affected side worst at the end of inhalation and exhalation, rapid heart rate, rapid shallow breathing, air hunger, a prominent side that does not move with breathing, the trachea slanting toward the unaffected side, a new nagging cough, and cyanosis: go to the emergency department immediately.</div>\n<div><b>Lung biopsy</b> — transbronchial or needle aspiration during bronchoscopy, percutaneous transthoracic aspiration, or an open biopsy by thoracotomy with a chest tube. A <b>chest x-ray or CT always follows</b> to rule out pneumothorax. Vital signs and breath sounds at least every 4 hours for 24 hours; <b>report reduced or absent breath sounds immediately</b>, and watch for hemoptysis.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Two nursing points cover every procedure in this list, which is why they are worth holding as a pair rather than as seven separate protocols: <b>protect the airway until the gag reflex returns</b>, and <b>watch the pleural space for a pneumothorax</b>. Anything entering the chest can let air in where it does not belong, and anything numbing the throat can let food in where it does not belong.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"22-breath-sounds-sound-to-mechanism","ch":"22","chTitle":"Assessment of the respiratory system","title":"Breath sounds — sound to mechanism","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Breath sounds — sound to mechanism</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Fine crackles</b> — alveoli popping open against fluid or collapse. Late inspiratory, at the bases. Heart failure, pneumonia, atelectasis, fibrosis. Do not clear with cough.</div>\n<div><b>Coarse crackles</b> — air bubbling through secretions in larger airways. Wetter, louder, may partially clear.</div>\n<div><b>Wheeze</b> — air forced through narrowed airways. Expiratory in asthma and COPD; a <b>silent chest</b> in a known wheezer is worse, not better, because no air is moving at all.</div>\n<div><b>Rhonchi</b> — low-pitched snoring from secretions in the large airways; often clears with an effective cough.</div>\n<div><b>Stridor</b> — high-pitched crowing on inspiration from upper airway narrowing. <b>Emergency.</b> Airway, call for help, prepare for intubation.</div>\n<div><b>Pleural friction rub</b> — creaking leather, inspiration and expiration, painful, unchanged by coughing. Inflamed pleura.</div>\n<div><b>Diminished or absent</b> — air is not reaching that area: effusion, pneumothorax, consolidation, or a very poor effort.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Sounds are made by air meeting resistance, so the sound names the obstacle: fluid pops, narrowing whistles, secretions rattle, inflamed surfaces grate, and nothing at all means nothing is moving. The exam version of this is always \"which finding requires immediate action\" — and the answer is the one that means the airway itself is closing: stridor, or a wheeze that went silent.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","ch":"22","chTitle":"Assessment of the respiratory system","title":"Diagnostic tests, and your job around each","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Diagnostic tests, and your job around each</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Pulse oximetry</b> — noninvasive saturation. Unreliable with poor perfusion, hypothermia, motion, dark nail polish, and falsely <i>normal</i> in carbon monoxide poisoning.</div>\n<div><b>ABG</b> — the truth about ventilation and acid-base. Apply pressure 5 minutes after an arterial draw (20 minutes on anticoagulants), place the sample on ice, and note the oxygen the patient was on.</div>\n<div><b>Chest x-ray</b> — structure, infiltrates, effusion, pneumothorax, tube and line placement.</div>\n<div><b>Pulmonary function tests</b> — volumes and flow rates. No bronchodilator or smoking for 4–6 hours before; the test is effort-dependent, so coaching matters.</div>\n<div><b><span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>a scope passed into the bronchi to look and sample</em><b>bronch/o-</b> airway · <b>-scopy</b> to look</i><u>Broncho</u><s>·</s><u>scopy</u></span></b> — consent, NPO 4–8 hours before, sedation. <b>After: NPO until the gag reflex returns</b>, watch for bleeding, and expect blood-streaked sputum; report frank bleeding, stridor, or dyspnea.</div>\n<div><b><span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>a needle draining fluid from the pleural space</em><b>thorac/o-</b> chest · <b>-centesis</b> puncture to withdraw</i><u>Thoraco</u><s>·</s><u>centesis</u></span></b> — consent; position sitting up leaning over a table; <b>hold still, do not cough</b> during insertion. After: chest x-ray, watch for pneumothorax (sudden dyspnea, tracheal deviation, absent sounds), and limit removal to about 1000 mL at a time.</div>\n<div><b>Sputum culture</b> — early morning, deep cough not saliva, rinse the mouth with water first, and collect <b>before</b> antibiotics.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every airway procedure shares two nursing points: the gag reflex and the pleural space. Sedation and topical anesthesia remove the reflex that keeps food out of the lung, so NPO until it returns is not a formality. And any needle or scope near the pleura can let air in, which is why a new sudden dyspnea after a thoracentesis is a pneumothorax until proven otherwise.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"22-questions-to-ask-this-patient","ch":"22","chTitle":"Assessment of the respiratory system","title":"Questions to ask this patient","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Questions to ask this patient</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\">\n<div>\"How far can you walk before you have to stop — and has that changed?\"</div>\n<div>\"How many pillows do you sleep on? Do you wake up short of breath?\"</div>\n<div>\"Tell me about the cough. Is anything coming up? What color, how much?\"</div>\n<div>\"Did you ever smoke? How many packs a day, and for how many years?\" — then compute pack-years.</div>\n<div>\"What do you do, or did you do, for work? Any dust, chemicals, asbestos, or fumes?\"</div>\n<div>\"Which inhalers do you have at home? Show me how you use them.\"</div>\n<div>\"What makes it worse — cold air, activity, lying down, mornings?\"</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Dyspnea has no lab value — the only measure is what the patient can no longer do, which is why the useful question is about blocks and stairs rather than \"are you short of breath.\" \"Show me how you use it\" is also the whole inhaler item: most patients who are \"adherent\" are using the device wrong, and demonstration is the only assessment that detects it.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"22-adpie","ch":"22","chTitle":"Assessment of the respiratory system","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">Respiratory assessment always answers the same question in the same order: is the airway open, is air moving, and is it reaching alveoli that can exchange it. The process below applies to any patient with a breathing problem, whatever the diagnosis turns out to be.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div>Smoking in <b>pack-years</b>, occupational and environmental exposure using <b>I PREPARE</b>, allergies, childhood and family lung disease, travel and geographic history, immunizations, and current drugs — including the ones that damage lungs (<b>bleomycin, amiodarone</b>) and the ones that cause cough (<b>ACE inhibitors, ARBs</b>). Then the symptom story: cough and its timing, sputum volume in teaspoons or cups and its color, hemoptysis, chest pain, and dyspnea graded by <b>what the patient can no longer do</b> — blocks walked, stairs climbed, pillows needed.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0;opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Inspect</div>Position and work of breathing first — <b>tripod posture, pursed lips, accessory muscle use, retractions, seesaw motion</b> beat the pulse oximeter as a first assessment. Then rate, depth, and rhythm; <b>barrel chest</b> (AP:lateral approaching 1:1); <b>clubbing</b> (nail angle 160° → 180°); cyanosis at <b>lips, oral mucosa, and conjunctivae</b>; and long-term signs — weight loss, thin limbs, hypertrophied neck and chest muscles.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0;opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Palpate and percuss</div><b>Fremitus</b> (increased over consolidation, decreased over fluid or air), <b>crepitus</b>, <b>tracheal position</b> — pushed away by tension pneumothorax or effusion, pulled toward atelectasis or resection — chest expansion symmetry, and percussion notes: resonant, <b>hyperresonant</b> (trapped air), <b>dull</b> (fluid or solid), flat, tympanic.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0;opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Auscultate and measure</div><b>Apex to base, side to side, comparing left with right</b>, mouth open, stethoscope on skin. Name each adventitious sound by its mechanism. Then <b>SpO₂ read as a trend</b>, ETCO₂ where available, ABGs by <b>ROME</b>, and the results of imaging, PFTs, and any procedure already done.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The order is not arbitrary — it is triage. Observation identifies the patient in trouble before any equipment does, which matters because the two findings that demand immediate action, <b>stridor and a wheeze gone silent</b>, are both heard rather than measured, and a pulse oximeter can read normal while an airway is closing.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\">\n<div><b>The pattern to match:</b> work of breathing + adventitious sounds + saturation + the mechanism they point to (narrowed airway, collapsed alveoli, thickened membrane, or blood not arriving).</div>\n<div><b>1. Decreased gas exchange</b> related to ventilation/perfusion imbalance or alveolar-capillary membrane changes — evidenced by hypoxemia, abnormal ABGs, and confusion or restlessness.</div>\n<div><b>2. Ineffective airway clearance</b> related to thick secretions, weak cough, or bronchospasm — evidenced by rhonchi, coarse crackles, and ineffective cough effort.</div>\n<div><b>3. Ineffective breathing pattern</b> related to increased work of breathing — evidenced by tachypnea, accessory muscle use, and retractions.</div>\n<div><b>4. Activity intolerance</b> related to reduced oxygen delivery.</div>\n<div><b>5. Anxiety</b> related to dyspnea and the sensation of suffocation.</div>\n<div><b>6. Need for health teaching</b> related to smoking cessation, inhaler technique, oxygen safety, and procedure aftercare.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Airway clearance outranks gas exchange whenever secretions or obstruction are the cause, because clearing the airway <i>is</i> the gas-exchange intervention. And <b>restlessness is ranked as hypoxia, not anxiety</b> — sedating the restless respiratory patient treats your discomfort rather than theirs.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\">\n<div>Airway remains patent; no stridor, and no silent chest in a patient who was wheezing.</div>\n<div>Saturation at or above the ordered target — commonly 95%, or <b>88–92% in chronic carbon dioxide retention</b> — on the lowest effective FiO₂.</div>\n<div>ABGs move toward normal ranges; ETCO₂ stays 35–45 mm Hg where monitored.</div>\n<div>Secretions cleared with an effective cough; adventitious sounds diminish and breath sounds are audible in all fields.</div>\n<div>Completes ADLs with rest periods and no dyspnea at rest.</div>\n<div>Patient states inhaler technique, oxygen safety rules, and the symptoms that mean returning for care — and after any airway procedure, states the 24-hour pneumothorax warning signs.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The oxygen outcome is written as <b>the lowest effective FiO₂</b>, not the highest saturation, because oxygen has a dose-dependent toxicity. Writing “SpO₂ 100%” as a goal invites absorptive atelectasis, lung injury, and in the retainer a blunted drive to breathe.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Independent</div>Position upright or high-Fowler to maximize chest expansion. <b>Coughing and deep breathing with an incentive spirometer</b> on a schedule; reposition regularly. Hydration to thin secretions. Pace activity and cluster care. Humidify oxygen <b>above 4 L/min</b>, drain condensation away from the humidifier, and protect skin at the ears, neck, and nose. Post oxygen signage and remove fire hazards. Teach cessation with the <b>5 As</b> at every encounter. Stay with the patient in acute distress.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Dependent</div>Oxygen titrated to the ordered target by the correct device — and <b>check the nonrebreather valve and flaps hourly</b>, never occlude the Venturi entrainment port. Bronchodilators, steroids, mucolytics, antibiotics as ordered. ABGs, cultures, imaging, PFTs. NPPV (<b>CPAP</b> to reopen alveoli, <b>BiPAP</b> to improve tidal volume) and preparation for intubation when indicated.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Procedure care</div><b>NPO until the gag reflex returns</b> after any throat-anesthetized procedure. After bronchoscopy, vitals, breath sounds, and saturation <b>every 15 minutes for 2 hours</b>. Position and coach for thoracentesis, then chest x-ray and pneumothorax surveillance. After lung biopsy, assessment <b>at least every 4 hours for 24 hours</b> — report reduced or absent breath sounds immediately.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Collaborative</div>Respiratory therapy for devices and technique; pulmonary rehabilitation; dietitian for the weight loss of chronic lung disease; smoking cessation and home oxygen referrals, including a <b>power-outage backup plan</b> for a concentrator.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Most of the independent column is mechanical rather than pharmacologic — position, humidity, hydration, spirometry — because most of what goes wrong in this chapter is mechanical: alveoli collapse, secretions dry into plugs, and the chest cannot expand. Those are nursing problems with nursing solutions.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\">\n<div>The cues that named the problem are the cues that measure the fix: work of breathing settles, accessory muscle use and retractions resolve, adventitious sounds diminish, saturation holds at target on <i>less</i> oxygen, and ABGs normalize. The patient completes a sentence without stopping for breath and walks further before stopping. Teach-back confirms inhaler technique, oxygen safety, and when to return.</div>\n<div><b>Unmet looks like:</b> stridor, a wheeze that has gone silent, a rising ETCO₂ with a normal saturation, new tracheal deviation with absent breath sounds, or a patient becoming restless and then quiet. Each escalates rather than being re-planned.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The most dangerous evaluation error here is reading <i>less noise</i> as improvement. A wheezing chest that falls silent and a restless patient who becomes calm are both usually deterioration — the first because no air is moving, the second because hypoxia has progressed to obtundation.</div>\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"23-overview","ch":"23","chTitle":"Noninfectious upper respiratory conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">23 · Chapter 23</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Gas exchange · tissue integrity</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Noninfectious upper respiratory conditions</h2>\n</div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:var(--space-2);align-items:center;border:1px dashed var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3) var(--space-4);border-radius:var(--radius-md)\"><span class=\"tag tag-neutral\">Not on the study guide</span><span style=\"font-size:14px;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">No questions listed for this chapter — lowest priority.</span></div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> Everything in this chapter happens above the trachea, which means every problem is a <i>plumbing</i> problem with a very short fuse: the upper airway is the only part of the respiratory system where swelling, blood, or a foreign body can close the whole thing in minutes. The nursing question is never \"how bad is this\" but \"is the airway open, and how fast is that changing.\"</p><div id=\"ch23-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;font-size:11.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">The upper airway is one lumen with no backup; it warms, filters and humidifies air.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Obstruction (edema, collapse, bleeding, tumor, trauma) narrows that single tube.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Stridor, drooling, snoring/apnea, hoarseness, epistaxis, clear nasal drainage after trauma.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Observation of airway sounds, sleep study (OSA), glucose/halo test (CSF), imaging for fractures.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">CPAP splints collapse; pressure and leaning forward stop anterior bleeding; tracheostomy bypasses obstruction.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Complete obstruction, aspiration, hypoxic arrest.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Stridor, inability to swallow secretions, dislodged fresh tracheostomy, flap color change, packing in the throat.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Post-op neck swelling<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>lumen narrows<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>airflow falls with the square of the radius<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>stridor and drooling<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>stay, call for help, prepare emergency airway</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Tracheostomy vs total laryngectomy</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Trach: upper airway still connected. Laryngectomy: permanent stoma, no mouth-to-lung path — oxygen goes to the stoma.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Oxymetazoline → alpha vasoconstriction → shrinks mucosa and slows bleeding; prolonged use → vessels rebound → worse congestion. Opioids → less drive + more airway collapse in OSA. <a href=\"Exam 2 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Connections to other chapters</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Ch 22 stridor recognition; ch 30 anticoagulants and ch 34 low platelets worsen epistaxis.</div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Controlled bleeding, CPAP adherence, stable trach with spare tube at bedside.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Stridor, drooling, trach dislodged, pale/cool or dusky flap, CSF leak.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the anatomy</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The nose</b> is vascular on purpose — it warms and humidifies air — and Kiesselbach plexus in the anterior septum is why most nosebleeds are anterior and reachable. Posterior bleeds come from larger vessels, bleed into the throat, and are far more dangerous.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The pharynx</b> collapses during sleep when muscle tone falls. Anything that narrows it — obesity, large tonsils, a small jaw, alcohol, sedatives — turns that into <span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>repeated pauses in breathing during sleep</em><b>a-</b> without · <b>-pnea</b> breathing</i><u>a</u><s>·</s><u>pnea</u></span>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The larynx</b> holds the vocal cords and the epiglottis. Remove it and the patient breathes through a permanent neck stoma with no connection between nose and lungs: no humidification, no filtering, no smell, and no voice through the mouth.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Trauma and swelling</b> anywhere in this space reduces the lumen by the square of the radius — halving the diameter quarters the airflow. That is why upper airway problems deteriorate so suddenly rather than gradually.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Upper airway = one lumen, no redundancy. The lower airway has millions of parallel paths, so losing some is survivable; the upper airway has one, so losing it is immediate. Every priority answer in this chapter follows from that single fact.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","ch":"23","chTitle":"Noninfectious upper respiratory conditions","title":"Obstructive sleep apnea — the full picture","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Obstructive sleep apnea — the full picture</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The mechanism</b> — during sleep the head and neck muscles relax and the tongue and soft palate displace backward. Neural control of chest movement stays intact, so the effort continues against a closed airway. Apnea raises CO₂ and drops pH, the chemoreceptors fire, the patient partially wakes and corrects the obstruction — <b>sometimes as often as every 5 minutes</b>, which prevents the deep sleep needed for restoration.</div>\n<div><b>Definitions</b> — OSA is partial or complete airway collapse during sleep; <b>OSAS</b> is symptomatic OSA, with apneic episodes of <b>10 seconds or longer</b> and intermittent hypoxia.</div>\n<div><b>Underdiagnosis</b> — an estimated <b>90% of adults with OSA are undiagnosed</b>, partly because heavy snoring is dismissed as normal. Risk rises with age, male sex, obesity, and enlarged tonsils, uvula, or adenoids, and increases sharply in both sexes after 60.</div>\n<div><b>Health equity</b> — Black adults, especially Black men, have a higher incidence and more severe disease. Three drivers: reduced access and screening, sleepiness attributed to other conditions, and mistrust or misunderstanding of the risk. Sleep disorders are underdiagnosed in specific populations, so screen deliberately rather than waiting for a complaint.</div>\n<div><b>What to ask</b> — daytime sleepiness or falling asleep reading, at a computer, or driving; being woken by their own snoring; frequency of nightmares; whether a family member has <b>witnessed the breathing pattern</b> — breaths spacing out, a pause, then gasping and snorting with partial waking. Ask about over-the-counter sleep aids and evening alcohol, both of which worsen it.</div>\n<div><b>GERD connection</b> — many patients develop nighttime reflux from the strong abdominal and chest effort during apneas. Ask about waking with heartburn, stomach contents in the mouth, or a burning choking cough.</div>\n<div><b>What to examine</b> — height and weight, the jaw, external neck, and chin (OSA goes with a <b>retracted lower jaw, smaller chin, shorter neck</b>), and the oral cavity for pharynx shape, uvula size, tongue thickness, and enlarged tonsils or soft palate. Check blood pressure and rhythm: <b>hypertension that does not respond as expected to therapy</b> is a classic clue.</div>\n<div><b>Psychosocial</b> — irritability, depression, loss of interest in social activity, poor recall and concentration. Long-term untreated OSA raises the risk of mild cognitive impairment and dementia.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnosis</b> — a screening questionnaire first (<b>STOP-Bang</b>, Epworth Sleepiness Scale, Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index, Multiple Sleep Latency Test), then possibly a home study, then the definitive <b>polysomnography</b>: an overnight laboratory study recording ECG, EEG, oximetry, eye movement (EOM), and muscle activity (EMG) with direct observation of sleep depth, respiratory effort, saturation, CO₂, and movement.</div>\n<div><b>Nonsurgical management</b> — weight loss and position change may correct mild disease. Oral appliances (<b>maxillomandibular advancement</b>) hold the jaw forward, useful for a retracted jaw but bulky and a risk to oral mucosal integrity. <b>CPAP is the mainstay</b>: one continuous pressure through inhalation and exhalation via mask or nasal pillows, requiring a proper tight seal. <b>Drug therapy has not been successful</b> — sedatives worsen OSA and stimulants do not treat the cause.</div>\n<div><b>CPAP adherence and care</b> — modern machines are quiet and humidified; Medicare covers much of the cost for older adults who use it at least <b>6 hours daily</b>. Clean the mask and tubing daily, use distilled water, never share equipment — with humidification, <b>fungal infection</b> is possible.</div>\n<div><b>Surgical options</b> — reserved for anatomic causes and severe resistant disease. An <b>implanted hypoglossal nerve stimulator</b> (electrode in the neck, generator in the chest) contracts the airway muscles without waking the patient. Tonsillectomy, adenoidectomy, uvulectomy, or septal repair address specific anatomy. <b>UPPP</b> remodels the posterior oropharynx; older versions removed extensive tissue and risked permanent taste and voice changes, while <b>modUPPP</b> repositions and reinforces support structures instead, with 2+ days in hospital and 3–6 weeks of recovery. Tracheostomy is a last resort.</div>\n<div><b>After surgery</b> — assess <b>every 2 hours for the first 24 hours</b> for airway narrowing: rising respiratory effort, stridor, drooling or inability to swallow secretions, a smaller oropharynx, falling saturation, or rising ETCO₂ — and call the Rapid Response Team. Give analgesia <b>on a schedule, not PRN</b>, because the oropharynx is richly innervated; avoid aspirin and NSAIDs for the first 24 hours. Darker blood mixed with secretions is normal; bright red or increasing bleeding is reported. Watch for <b>excessive swallowing or belching</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Opioids are the recurring danger in this population, and the mechanism explains why: OSA exists because muscle tone fails to hold the airway open during sleep, and opioids and sedatives remove exactly that tone. So the drug given for postoperative pain reproduces the disease — which is why these patients are monitored continuously, dosed cautiously, and why sedatives are never the answer to their insomnia.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","ch":"23","chTitle":"Noninfectious upper respiratory conditions","title":"Obstruction, epistaxis, and trauma","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--cal-red);border-top:5px solid var(--cal-red);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--cal-red-2)\">Obstruction, epistaxis, and trauma</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Causes of upper airway obstruction</b> — tongue edema (surgery, trauma, angioedema), tongue occlusion (lost gag reflex, lost tone, unconsciousness), laryngeal edema (smoke or toxin inhalation, inflammation, anaphylaxis), peritonsillar or pharyngeal abscess, head and neck cancer, <b>thick secretions</b>, stroke and cerebral edema, facial or laryngeal trauma or burns, and foreign-body aspiration.</div>\n<div><b>The preventable one</b> — thickly crusted oral and nasopharyngeal secretions can completely block the airway and cause death. It comes from poor oral hygiene, and it is <b>eliminated by nursing care</b>. Highest risk: altered mental status, dehydration, inability to communicate, ineffective cough, aspiration risk. Assess oral care needs daily and make sure assistive personnel understand why the technique matters.</div>\n<div><b>Recognizing it</b> — partial obstruction gives vague general signs: diaphoresis, tachycardia, anxiety, raised blood pressure. Then hypoxia and hypercarbia, <b>restlessness</b>, increasing anxiety, sternal retractions, \"seesawing\" chest motion, abdominal movement, and a <b>feeling of impending doom</b>. Monitor with oximetry and ETCO₂ and watch continuously for stridor, cyanosis, and falling consciousness.</div>\n<div><b>Acting on it</b> — extend the head and neck slightly and insert a nasal or oral airway if the tongue or secretions are the cause; suction. For a foreign body, <b>abdominal thrusts in the conscious patient</b>. If the patient is unconscious, <b>start CPR chest compressions instead of thrusts</b>, even when a foreign body is suspected. Beyond that: laryngoscopy, endotracheal intubation (placed <i>before</i> swelling makes it impossible), <b>cricothyroidotomy</b> through the cricothyroid membrane, or tracheotomy — which takes 5–10 minutes electively but can establish an airway in under 2 minutes as an emergency.</div>\n<div><b>Epistaxis — why it bleeds</b> — the nose is dense with capillaries. Causes include trauma, hypertension, blood dyscrasias such as leukemia, inflammation, tumor, low humidity, nose blowing and picking, chronic cocaine use, and NG suctioning. <b>Older adults tend to bleed posteriorly.</b></div>\n<div><b>Emergency care</b> — Standard Precautions; sit the patient <b>upright and leaning forward</b> to keep blood out of the larynx; reassure and keep them quiet, since anxiety raises blood pressure; apply <b>direct lateral pressure for 15–20 minutes</b> with ice or cool compresses; pack both nares loosely if needed; and <b>no nose blowing for 24 hours</b> after bleeding stops, so clots are not dislodged.</div>\n<div><b>When it does not stop</b> — cautery with silver nitrate or electrocautery and anterior packing. <b>Posterior bleeding</b> is more severe and harder to control, with greater risk of airway compromise: posterior packing, epistaxis catheters, or gel tampons, usually removed after 1–3 days. Observe for respiratory distress, assess patients on opioids <b>at least hourly for gag and cough reflexes</b>, and use oximetry. Refractory bleeding may need vessel ligation or <b>embolization</b> — whose complications include facial pain, skin or mucosal necrosis, facial nerve paralysis, and blindness.</div>\n<div><b>Nasal fracture</b> — document deviation, a malaligned bridge, changed nasal breathing, <b>crepitus</b>, bruising, and pain. <b>Closed reduction within the first 24 hours</b>, because after that edema and scar formation make it harder and reduction may be delayed for days.</div>\n<div><b>The CSF test</b> — clear drainage may be cerebrospinal fluid from a skull fracture. CSF <b>contains glucose</b> and tests positive on a dipstick, and dried on filter paper it leaves a yellow <b>halo ring</b> at the edge. This is rare and indicates serious injury.</div>\n<div><b>Rhinoplasty and nasoseptoplasty</b> — the patient returns with packing in both nostrils and cannot breathe through the nose, plus a \"mustache\" drip pad and often a splint. <b>Assess how often the patient swallows</b> — repeated swallowing signals posterior bleeding; examine the throat with a penlight. Semi-Fowler position, move slowly, cool compresses, at least 2500 mL/day once the gag reflex returns. Avoid forceful coughing, straining, sniffing upward, nose blowing, and sneezing with the mouth closed; avoid aspirin and NSAIDs. Final cosmetic result takes <b>6–12 months</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Facial trauma and the Le Fort classification</b> — mandibular fractures are most common. <b>Le Fort I</b> is a nasoethmoid complex fracture; <b>Le Fort II</b> adds the maxilla; <b>Le Fort III</b> combines both plus an orbital-zygoma fracture — craniofacial disjunction, where the midface has no connection to the skull. The rich facial blood supply means extensive bleeding and bruising.</div>\n<div><b>Assessment priorities in facial trauma</b> — <b>airway first</b>: stridor, dyspnea, anxiety, restlessness, hypoxia, cyanosis, falling consciousness. Then bleeding and fractures, soft tissue edema, facial asymmetry, CSF leak from ears or nose, <b>vision and eye movement</b> (orbital and maxillary fractures entrap nerves and muscles), and bruising behind the ears — the <b>battle sign</b>, associated with skull fracture and brain trauma. Facial trauma co-occurs with spinal trauma, so cervical spine imaging is obtained.</div>\n<div><b>Fixed occlusion and IMF</b> — the jaws are wired closed for <b>6–10 weeks</b>. Teach oral care with an electronic irrigating device and a dental liquid diet. <b>Always keep wire cutters on hand</b>: if the patient vomits they cannot open the jaws, so the wires are cut to prevent aspiration — then return to the surgeon for rewiring immediately.</div>\n<div><b>Laryngeal trauma</b> — from a crush or direct blow, fracture, or prolonged intubation. Difficulty breathing, <b>aphonia</b> (inability to produce sound), hoarseness, subcutaneous emphysema, and hemoptysis. Assess gas exchange and vital signs <b>every 15–30 minutes</b>; if signs of respiratory difficulty appear, stay with the patient and have the team prepare for emergency intubation or tracheotomy.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Two of these rules invert what intuition suggests, which is precisely why they are tested. Abdominal thrusts stop the moment the patient becomes unconscious — <b>you switch to CPR compressions even though you know an object is there</b> — and a nosebleed patient leans forward rather than back, because tipping the head back hides the blood loss and sends it toward the airway. In both cases the instinctive action protects the appearance of the problem and endangers the patient.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","ch":"23","chTitle":"Noninfectious upper respiratory conditions","title":"Head and neck cancer — treatment and surgical care","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--cal-amber);border-top:5px solid var(--cal-amber);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--cal-amber)\">Head and neck cancer — treatment and surgical care</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The disease</b> — about 4% of US cancers, usually <b>squamous cell carcinoma</b>, slow-growing and curable when found early. Chronic irritation thickens the mucosa, genes controlling growth are damaged, and lesions appear first as white patches (<b>leukoplakia</b>) or red velvety patches (<b>erythroplakia</b>). Spread is first to local nodes, muscle, and bone, later to lung and liver.</div>\n<div><b>Risk factors</b> — <b>tobacco and alcohol are the two major ones</b>; also voice abuse, chronic laryngitis, chemical and dust exposure, poor oral hygiene, long-term GERD, and <b>oral HPV infection</b>. More common in men and over age 50. Calculate pack-years and quantify alcohol in drinks per day and years.</div>\n<div><b>Warning signs to teach</b> — pain; a lump in the mouth, throat, or neck; difficulty swallowing; color change in the mouth or tongue to red, white, gray, dark brown, or black; an oral sore that does not heal in <b>2 weeks</b>; unexplained oral bleeding; numbness of mouth, lips, or face; a change in denture fit; burning with citrus juice or hot liquids; <b>persistent one-sided ear pain</b>; hoarseness or voice change; recurrent sore throat; shortness of breath; anorexia and weight loss.</div>\n<div><b>Nose and sinus cancer</b> — rare, linked to chronic exposure to wood, textile, leather, and flour dusts, nickel and chromium, mustard gas, and radium, with smoking multiplying the risk. Onset is slow and the symptoms mimic sinusitis: persistent obstruction, drainage, bloody discharge, and <b>pain that persists after sinusitis treatment</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Radiation</b> — cure rate of at least 40% for small localized tumors. Expect hoarseness, sore throat, dysphagia, skin reaction, impaired taste, and dry mouth for weeks. Teach the patient to protect irradiated skin from sun, heat, cold, and shaving, wear soft cotton, and wash gently with mild soap. For <b>xerostomia</b>: sip cool fluids all day, sugar-free candy or gum, moisturizing sprays, humidification — and a dental consult, because dry mouth plus radiation sharply raises caries risk.</div>\n<div><b>Chemotherapy and targeted therapy</b> — usually cisplatin (or another platinum drug) with 5-FU, often given concurrently with radiation (chemoradiation). <b>Cetuximab</b> targets EGFR but also blocks it in normal tissue, so severe skin reactions are common and distressing. Nivolumab and pembrolizumab are used for some HPV-associated cancers.</div>\n<div><b>Surgery and voice</b> — from cord stripping and transoral cordectomy (normal or hoarse voice, high cure rate) through laryngofissure and supraglottic or hemilaryngectomy (hoarse), to <b>total laryngectomy</b> — larynx, hyoid, strap muscles, and one or two tracheal rings removed, leaving <b>no natural voice</b> and a permanent stoma. A <b>neck dissection</b> removes lymph nodes, the sternocleidomastoid, the jugular vein, the 11th cranial nerve, and soft tissue; <b>shoulder drop is expected</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Before surgery</b> — teach airway self-management, suctioning, pain control, the ICU environment, nutrition support and feeding tubes, and discharge plans. With the speech-language pathologist, <b>establish and practice the communication method before surgery</b>, while the patient can still speak.</div>\n<div><b>After surgery</b> — the operation often lasts 8+ hours and the patient recovers in ICU. Vital signs and hemorrhage checks <b>hourly for the first 24 hours</b>. A <b>laryngectomy tube</b> (shorter, wider than a tracheostomy tube) prevents shrinkage of the skin-tracheal border. Clean the suture line with sterile saline every <b>1–2 hours for the first days</b>, then every 4 hours; healthy stoma mucosa is bright pink and shiny, like oral mucosa.</div>\n<div><b>Flaps and wound breakdown</b> — evaluate grafts and flaps <b>hourly for the first 72 hours</b>: capillary refill, color, drainage, and Doppler signal. Position so the flaps are not dependent. Wound breakdown is common, especially after preoperative radiation, and an extensive wound can leave the <b>carotid artery exposed</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Carotid artery rupture</b> — assess hourly for a leak. If a leak is suspected, <b>call the Rapid Response Team and do not touch the area</b> — pressure could cause immediate rupture. If it ruptures, apply constant direct manual pressure, secure the airway, <b>do not leave the patient</b>, and transport immediately to surgery. The risk of stroke and death is high.</div>\n<div><b>The team</b> — respiratory therapist for ventilation and tracheostomy teaching; <b>dietitian</b>, because patients lose an average of at least 20 lb after surgery and many are already depleted; <b>speech-language pathologist</b> for communication and ongoing swallow assessment.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The instruction not to touch a suspected carotid leak runs against every reflex a nurse has, and it is the right one: a leaking vessel under a breaking-down wound is held together by very little, and palpation is enough to convert a leak into an exsanguinating rupture. Notice also that the communication plan is built <i>before</i> the operation — once the larynx is gone, the patient cannot tell you they are in distress, so the writing board is airway safety equipment, not a courtesy.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea","ch":"23","chTitle":"Noninfectious upper respiratory conditions","title":"Obstructive sleep apnea","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Obstructive sleep apnea</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — repeated apneic periods of <b>10 seconds or longer</b> during sleep, from upper airway collapse. Each episode drops oxygen and jolts the sympathetic system awake.</div>\n<div><b>Risk factors</b> — obesity and a large neck circumference, large tonsils or uvula, a small or receding jaw, nasal obstruction, smoking, alcohol and sedatives at bedtime, male sex, and increasing age.</div>\n<div><b>Cues</b> — loud snoring with witnessed pauses, gasping arousals, morning headache, dry mouth, unrefreshing sleep, daytime sleepiness, irritability and poor concentration, and over time hypertension, atrial fibrillation, and right-sided heart failure.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnosis</b> — <span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>an overnight sleep study</em><b>poly-</b> many · <b>somn/o-</b> sleep · <b>-graphy</b> recording</i><u>poly</u><s>·</s><u>somno</u><s>·</s><u>graphy</u></span>, which counts apneic and hypopneic episodes per hour (the AHI).</div>\n<div><b>Management</b> — weight loss, side-lying position, no alcohol or sedatives before bed, treat nasal obstruction; <b>CPAP</b> is the mainstay (a splint of positive pressure holding the airway open); oral appliances; surgery such as uvulopalatopharyngoplasty for selected patients.</div>\n<div><b>Nursing priority</b> — CPAP adherence is the whole battle: assess mask fit, skin breakdown, dry nose, claustrophobia, and bring the patient's own machine to the hospital. Be extremely cautious with opioids and sedatives, which worsen the collapse.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>OSA is a cardiovascular disease wearing a sleep costume: every apnea causes hypoxemia and a sympathetic surge, and repeated hundreds of times a night that becomes hypertension, dysrhythmias, and eventually right heart failure. It is also the reason a postoperative OSA patient is a monitoring priority — the drugs that relieve pain remove exactly the muscle tone that was holding the airway open.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"23-epistaxis-trauma-and-obstruction","ch":"23","chTitle":"Noninfectious upper respiratory conditions","title":"Epistaxis, trauma, and obstruction","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Epistaxis, trauma, and obstruction</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b><span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>nosebleed</em><b>epi-</b> upon · <b>-staxis</b> dripping</i><u>Epi</u><s>·</s><u>staxis</u></span> first aid:</b> sit upright and <b>lean forward</b>, pinch the soft part of the nose for <b>10–15 minutes</b> continuously, apply ice to the nose and face, and keep the patient calm. Leaning back sends blood down the throat and hides how much is being lost.</div>\n<div><b>If it will not stop</b> — nasal packing or balloon, cautery, and sometimes arterial ligation. With posterior packing, monitor closely: airway obstruction, hypoxia, infection, and pain are all real risks, and humidification plus oral care keep the patient tolerable.</div>\n<div><b>Teach after a bleed</b> — no nose blowing, no straining or heavy lifting, no aspirin or NSAIDs, humidify the air, and use saline spray or water-soluble lubricant.</div>\n<div><b>Facial and nasal trauma</b> — the priority is always <b>airway first</b>, then bleeding, then the fracture. Watch for clear drainage that tests positive for glucose or forms a halo ring on gauze: that is cerebrospinal fluid and means a skull base fracture — do not pack, do not suction the nose, notify immediately.</div>\n<div><b><span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>surgical reshaping of the nose</em><b>rhin/o-</b> nose · <b>-plasty</b> to reshape</i><u>Rhino</u><s>·</s><u>plasty</u></span> after care</b> — head elevated, ice, expect edema and bruising, and watch for <b>frequent swallowing</b>, which is the sign of bleeding running down the back of the throat.</div>\n<div><b>Upper airway obstruction</b> — causes include the tongue in an unresponsive patient, aspiration of food or object, edema (anaphylaxis, burns, infection), tumor, and trauma. Cues: stridor, choking, gasping, wheezing, restlessness, cyanosis. <b>Actions:</b> call for help, position and open the airway, abdominal thrusts for a foreign body in a responsive adult, suction, prepare for emergency intubation or cricothyroidotomy.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The recurring test point is \"frequent swallowing.\" After any procedure in the nose or throat, blood does not appear on a dressing — it runs backward and the patient swallows it, so repeated swallowing is the earliest sign of hemorrhage. Lean-forward positioning exists for the same reason: it keeps blood where you can see and measure it instead of in the stomach, where it causes vomiting and aspiration.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-and-the-laryngectom","ch":"23","chTitle":"Noninfectious upper respiratory conditions","title":"Head and neck cancer, and the laryngectomy patient","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Head and neck cancer, and the laryngectomy patient</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Risk factors</b> — tobacco in any form and alcohol, especially together; HPV infection; sun exposure for lip cancer; occupational exposures; poor oral hygiene and chronic irritation.</div>\n<div><b>Warning signs to teach</b> — hoarseness lasting more than 2 weeks, a lump in the neck or mouth, a sore that does not heal, difficulty or painful swallowing, unexplained ear pain, persistent unilateral nasal obstruction or bleeding.</div>\n<div><b>Airway is the postoperative priority</b> — airway and bleeding first, then pain, nutrition, and communication. Keep the head of the bed elevated, suction available, a spare tracheostomy tube and obturator at the bedside, and never remove a fresh airway.</div>\n<div><b>Communication plan</b> — establish it <i>before</i> surgery: writing board, picture board, tablet, and an agreed signal for distress. After healing, options include esophageal speech, an electrolarynx, and a tracheoesophageal puncture prosthesis.</div>\n<div><b>Nutrition</b> — swallowing evaluation before oral intake, upright positioning, thickened liquids or tube feeding as indicated, and monitoring weight and albumin.</div>\n<div><b>Stoma care teaching</b> — cover the stoma with a loose cloth or shield, humidify at home, no swimming and shower with care, protect from aerosols and dust, and wear medical alert identification stating \"neck breather\" — <b>mouth-to-stoma</b> rescue breathing is required if they ever arrest.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A total laryngectomy permanently separates the airway from the mouth and nose, and almost every teaching point falls out of that one anatomical change: no humidification (so humidify), no filtering (so cover), no nose-holding for swimming (so never swim), and no air through the mouth (so rescue breathing goes to the stoma). Teach the anatomy once and the safety list is self-evident.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"23-adpie","ch":"23","chTitle":"Noninfectious upper respiratory conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">The upper airway is one lumen with no backup, so every assessment in this chapter runs airway-first and every plan is built around the fact that swelling, blood, or an object can close it in minutes.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Airway, always first</div>Voice quality and ability to speak; <b>stridor, drooling, or inability to swallow secretions</b>; rising respiratory effort; retractions and seesaw motion; <b>restlessness and a feeling of impending doom</b>; saturation and ETCO₂. In partial obstruction the early signs are vague — <b>diaphoresis, tachycardia, anxiety, rising blood pressure</b> — so treat the whole cluster as airway until proven otherwise.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Sleep and breathing history</div>Snoring with <b>witnessed pauses and gasping arousals</b>, daytime sleepiness, morning headache, dry mouth, unrefreshing sleep, irritability and poor concentration. Screen with <b>STOP-Bang</b> or the Epworth scale. Examine the <b>jaw, chin, neck, uvula, tonsils, and tongue</b>, and note hypertension that does not respond as expected to therapy.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Bleeding and trauma</div>Site and estimated volume of epistaxis and whether it is anterior or posterior; <b>frequent swallowing</b> as the earliest sign of posterior or postoperative bleeding. In facial trauma: deviation, crepitus, asymmetry, <b>vision and eye movement</b>, <b>battle sign</b>, and clear drainage that is <b>glucose-positive or leaves a halo ring</b>. Assume cervical spine involvement.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Cancer and stoma</div>Warning signs lasting beyond <b>2 weeks</b> — hoarseness, a neck or oral lump, a nonhealing sore, dysphagia, one-sided ear pain. Quantify tobacco in pack-years and alcohol in drinks. For a stoma or fresh flap: <b>mucosa color, secretion character and thickness, flap capillary refill and Doppler signal</b>, and the suture line.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Restlessness and anxiety are listed as airway findings rather than emotional ones on purpose. In a narrowing upper airway they are the earliest reliable cue, and the intuitive response — reassurance, or worse, sedation — removes the muscle tone and the report that were keeping the patient alive.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\">\n<div><b>1. Potential for airway obstruction</b> related to edema, secretions, bleeding, a foreign body, or loss of tone — evidenced by stridor, retractions, restlessness, or falling saturation. <b>This outranks everything in this chapter, every time.</b></div>\n<div><b>2. Potential for hemorrhage</b> related to a vascular surgical field or nasal vessels — evidenced by frequent swallowing, bright red bleeding, or a falling pressure with rising pulse.</div>\n<div><b>3. Decreased gas exchange</b> related to apneic episodes or obstruction.</div>\n<div><b>4. Impaired verbal communication</b> related to laryngectomy, tracheostomy, or wired jaws — a <b>safety</b> problem, not a comfort one.</div>\n<div><b>5. Inadequate nutrition</b> related to dysphagia, oral surgery, or fixed occlusion.</div>\n<div><b>6. Disturbed body image and grieving</b> related to permanent change in voice, breathing, smell, and appearance.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Communication ranks above nutrition and body image because a patient who cannot speak cannot report the airway problem that is first on the list. The writing board is airway equipment. Body image is a genuine priority in this chapter and still never outranks a threatened airway.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\">\n<div>Airway remains patent; no stridor, no unrelieved obstruction, and no episode requiring emergency intubation.</div>\n<div>For OSA: <b>CPAP used nightly including naps</b>, with an adherent mask fit and intact skin; postoperative patients monitored continuously while receiving opioids or sedatives.</div>\n<div>Bleeding controlled; no unrecognized posterior bleed — the patient and staff both watching for <b>frequent swallowing</b>.</div>\n<div>A communication method is established <b>before</b> surgery and demonstrated back, with a distress signal agreed and the call light always within reach.</div>\n<div>Swallowing evaluated before oral intake; weight and nutritional markers stable.</div>\n<div>Before discharge the patient states stoma or wound care, humidification needs, the no-blowing and no-straining rules, and which findings require immediate return.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The communication outcome carries a deadline — <b>before surgery</b> — because afterward the patient cannot participate in building the plan. Teaching a writing board to someone who has already lost their voice is remediation, not preparation.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Independent</div>Keep suction, a <b>spare tube of the same size and one smaller, an obturator, and a manual resuscitation bag</b> at the bedside of any new airway. <b>Never remove a fresh tracheostomy tube.</b> Meticulous oral care — crusted secretions are a preventable cause of fatal obstruction. Continuous humidification for a stoma. Suction sterilely, <b>preoxygenate, 10–15 seconds, suction on withdrawal only</b>. Upright positioning; ice and pressure for epistaxis with the patient <b>leaning forward</b>. Keep <b>wire cutters at the bedside</b> for fixed occlusion.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Dependent</div>Oxygen and humidification; scheduled (not PRN) analgesia for the richly innervated oropharynx, <b>avoiding aspirin and NSAIDs in the first 24 hours</b>; antiemetics; packing, cautery, or embolization for refractory bleeding; enteral feeding or thickened liquids after a swallow study; antibiotics and wound care.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Emergency actions</div>Obstruction: call for help, open and position the airway, insert an oral or nasal airway, suction, <b>abdominal thrusts in the responsive adult — but chest compressions once unresponsive, even with a known foreign body</b>. Prepare for intubation before swelling closes the option, or cricothyroidotomy. Suspected carotid leak after neck surgery: <b>call the Rapid Response Team and do not touch the area</b>; if it ruptures, constant direct pressure, secure the airway, do not leave the patient.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Collaborative</div>Speech-language pathology for the communication plan and swallow assessments; respiratory therapy; <b>dietitian</b> — head and neck surgery patients lose an average of at least 20 lb; sleep medicine and CPAP supply; smoking and alcohol cessation; social work and support groups.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Two instructions here override reflex. You stop abdominal thrusts the moment the patient goes unconscious and start compressions instead, and you do <i>not</i> palpate a suspected carotid leak. Both feel wrong and both are right: the first because compressions generate better airway pressure and circulate blood, the second because pressure on a breaking-down vessel converts a leak into an exsanguination.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\">\n<div>Airway patency maintained throughout, with no emergency airway event. Breathing effort, saturation, and ETCO₂ stable. Bleeding stopped and no recurrence with the teaching in place. The patient communicates needs reliably by the agreed method and uses the call light. Swallowing is safe, intake adequate, weight stable. Stoma mucosa stays <b>bright pink and shiny</b>, secretions stay thin, and the patient or family demonstrates care, suctioning, and humidification.</div>\n<div><b>Unmet looks like:</b> new or worsening stridor, drooling, rising respiratory effort, falling saturation or rising ETCO₂, a shrinking oropharynx after surgery, frequent swallowing or bright red bleeding, a dusky or poorly perfused flap, or any suspicion of a carotid leak.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Evaluation in this chapter is mostly done at the bedside in the first 24 hours, on a clock: hourly bleeding checks, <b>every 2 hours</b> for airway narrowing after airway surgery, <b>hourly for 72 hours</b> on flaps. The interval is the evaluation — problems here declare themselves in minutes, not shifts.</div>\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"24-overview","ch":"24","chTitle":"Noninfectious lower respiratory conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">24 · Chapter 24</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Gas exchange · perfusion · inflammation · cellular regulation</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Noninfectious lower respiratory conditions</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> Below the larynx the airway stops being one lumen and becomes a tree — two mainstem bronchi, five secondary bronchi, thousands of bronchioles, then alveolar ducts and alveoli. Nothing here closes in minutes, so the chapter is not about speed; it is about <i>chronic, progressive loss</i>. Every disease in it narrows the tube (asthma, chronic bronchitis), destroys the elastic sac (emphysema), thickens the membrane (fibrosis), plugs it with thick mucus (cystic fibrosis), squeezes the vessel (pulmonary arterial hypertension), or fills the space with tumor (lung cancer). Name which of those six is happening and the assessment findings, the drug class, and the nursing priority all follow.</p><div id=\"ch24-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;font-size:11.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Millions of small airways and alveoli exchange gas across a thin, elastic membrane.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Airways narrow (asthma, COPD), alveoli are destroyed (emphysema), secretions thicken (CF), vessels constrict (PAH), or the membrane scars (IPF).</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Wheeze, prolonged exhalation, barrel chest, pursed-lip breathing, clubbing, JVD in right heart strain.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">PFTs (FEV₁/FVC; ≥12% bronchodilator response = asthma), ABGs, sweat chloride, right heart catheterization, HRCT.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">SABA opens airways now; ICS calms inflammation over time; oxygen to target; airway clearance; PAH vasodilators.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Air trapping → fatigue → CO₂ retention; chronic hypoxia → cor pulmonale.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Silent chest, drowsiness with rising PaCO₂, chest tube drainage &gt;100 mL/hr, tracheal deviation, interrupted epoprostenol.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Airway narrowing<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>wheeze and prolonged exhalation<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>air trapping<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>rising work of breathing<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>fatigue and rising CO₂<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>silent chest is an emergency</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Beta2 agonist<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>bronchial smooth muscle relaxes<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>airways widen<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>wheeze improves<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>tremor and tachycardia because beta receptors outside the lung are stimulated too</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Asthma vs COPD</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Asthma is reversible and episodic; COPD is progressive and poorly reversible.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">SABA vs ICS</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">SABA = rescue in minutes. ICS = controller over weeks; rinse mouth to prevent thrush.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">ICS → local immune suppression → oral thrush. LABA alone in asthma → symptoms masked while inflammation worsens → must pair with ICS. Epoprostenol half-life minutes → interruption = rebound PAH. <a href=\"Exam 2 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Connections to other chapters</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Ch 22 PFTs and ABGs; ch 29 right-sided failure (cor pulmonale); ch 34 polycythemia from chronic hypoxia.</div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">SpO₂ at ordered target (often 88–92% in CO₂ retainers), stable tidaling in the water seal.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Silent chest, PaCO₂ rising with confusion, continuous bubbling with new distress, facial swelling (SVC syndrome).</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the anatomy and physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The tree</b> — trachea → two mainstem bronchi → five secondary bronchi → thousands of bronchi and bronchioles → alveolar ducts → alveoli, where gas exchange happens. Air must flow through the <i>entire</i> tubular system for oxygen to reach the exchanging surface, so a problem anywhere along it impairs both <b>gas exchange and systemic perfusion</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Elastic recoil</b> is what empties the lung. Exhalation is passive: stretched elastin pulls the alveoli back down. Destroy elastin and the lung inflates but will not deflate — air is trapped, the alveoli become large flabby <span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>enlarged air-filled spaces where alveoli have merged</em><b>bulla</b> a bubble · plural <b>bullae</b></i><u>bullae</u></span>, and surface area for exchange falls.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Proteases versus elastin</b> — proteases normally destroy inhaled particulates. Cigarette smoke drives protease synthesis far above normal, and the excess breaks down elastin itself. <b>Alpha₁-antitrypsin</b> is the brake on proteases; inherited AAT deficiency removes the brake, which is why emphysema in a non-smoker or before age 40 points to genetics.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The flattened diaphragm</b> — a hyperinflated lung pushes the diaphragm down and flat, and a flat muscle cannot generate the same pull. The patient recruits neck, chest wall, and abdominal accessory muscles, which burn more oxygen for less air. Inhalation starts before exhalation finishes, producing the uncoordinated pattern and the sensation of <b>air hunger</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The pressure chain to the heart</b> — trapped air, collapsed airways, and stiff alveolar walls raise lung tissue pressure and narrow the pulmonary vessels. The right ventricle, built thin for a low-pressure circuit, must generate high pressures. It hypertrophies and then fails: <span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>right-sided heart failure caused by lung disease</em><b>cor</b> heart · <b>pulmonale</b> of the lung</i><u>cor pulmonale</u></span>. Normal pulmonary pressure is 15–18 mm Hg; hypertension begins above 25.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why CO₂ retention is the late sign</b> — CO₂ diffuses far more easily than oxygen, so hypoxemia comes first and the patient compensates by raising the rate. ABGs can look near-normal until disease is advanced. Then CO₂ is produced faster than it can be blown off: chronic <span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>high blood carbon dioxide</em><b>hyper-</b> above · <b>capnia</b> smoke, vapour — carbon dioxide</i><u>hyper</u><s>·</s><u>capnia</u></span> with respiratory acidosis, and the kidney retains bicarbonate as compensation.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Lower airway = millions of parallel paths, so losing some is survivable and deterioration is measured in months and years rather than minutes. That single structural fact inverts the nursing emphasis from chapter 23: here the <b>trend</b> matters more than any single finding, the patient's own baseline is the reference value, and the work of breathing — not the saturation number — is what tells you whether the disease is winning.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"24-asthma-the-full-picture","ch":"24","chTitle":"Noninfectious lower respiratory conditions","title":"Asthma — the full picture","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Asthma — the full picture</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Definition</b> — a chronic disease in which <b>reversible</b> acute airway obstruction occurs intermittently, reducing airflow. Two mechanisms run together: <b>inflammation</b> narrows the lumen from the inside, and <b>airway hyperresponsiveness</b> constricts bronchial smooth muscle from the outside. Most adults have both — which is why two drug classes are needed. More than <b>3500 deaths a year</b> in the United States; over 20 million adults affected, and about half also had it as children.</div>\n<div><b>Two inflammatory types</b> — <b>eosinophilic asthma</b> (about 50% of cases) is driven by <b>IL-5</b>; <b>neutrophilic asthma</b> is driven by <b>IL-17</b>. Symptoms are identical; the long-term effect on the epithelium differs, and some drugs are specific to one type — which is why the monoclonals are targeted by mediator.</div>\n<div><b>Triggers and history</b> — intermittent dyspnea, chest tightness, cough, wheeze, and increased mucus. Ask whether symptoms are continuous, seasonal, activity- or work-related, or <b>worse at night</b>; some patients have symptoms for <b>4–8 weeks after a cold</b>. Atopic patients usually have other allergies. <b>Wheezing in a non-smoker</b> is important diagnostically.</div>\n<div><b>Control level (GINA)</b> — four questions: daytime symptoms more than twice weekly, night waking with symptoms, reliever needed more than twice weekly, activity limited by symptoms. <b>None = controlled · 1–2 = partly controlled · 3–4 = uncontrolled.</b></div>\n<div><b>During an attack</b> — audible wheeze, <b>louder on exhalation at first</b>, prolonged exhalation, accessory muscle use with retractions at the sternum, suprasternal notch and between the ribs, inability to speak more than a few words between breaths. Long-standing severe disease gives a <b>barrel chest</b>: the normal AP:lateral ratio of 1:1.5 becomes 1:1 from chronic air trapping, with a flattened diaphragm and widened rib spaces.</div>\n<div><b>Labs</b> — PaO₂ falls; <b>PaCO₂ falls early</b> (blowing it off), then <b>rises late</b> with end-tidal CO₂ as retention begins. Allergic asthma shows raised serum <b>eosinophils and IgE</b>; sputum may contain eosinophils and <b>Curschmann spirals</b> (mucous plugs with shed epithelial cells).</div>\n<div><b>PFTs are the diagnosis</b> — FVC, <b>FEV₁</b> and <b>peak expiratory flow</b>. Asthma is diagnosed when values <b>increase by 12% or more after a bronchodilator</b> — reversibility is the whole point. Responsiveness can be provoked with inhaled <b>methacholine</b>, which induces bronchospasm in susceptible adults.</div>\n<div><b>Reliever versus control</b> — <b>SABA</b> (albuterol, levalbuterol, terbutaline) stops an attack and premedicates before trigger activity; always carry it and check it still holds doses. <b>LABA</b> (salmeterol, formoterol) prevents but <b>cannot stop</b> an attack, needs days to build, and carries an FDA <b>black box warning</b>: never as sole therapy. <b>Cholinergic antagonists / LAMAs</b> (ipratropium short-acting, tiotropium once daily) bronchodilate and dry secretions. <b>Methylxanthines</b> (theophylline) are a last resort — therapeutic dose sits close to the toxic one, so blood levels are monitored.</div>\n<div><b>Antiinflammatories are control drugs only</b> — inhaled corticosteroids are the most effective (fluticasone, budesonide, mometasone); systemic steroids are short-term for moderate disease and daily only in severe. <b>Cromolyn</b> blocks mast cell membranes; <b>nedocromil</b> inhibits mediator release. <b>Leukotriene modifiers</b>: montelukast and zafirlukast block the receptor, zileuton blocks synthesis. <b>Monoclonals</b> — omalizumab (anti-IgE), dupilumab (IL-4 receptor), benralizumab/mepolizumab/reslizumab (IL-5) — and <b>none</b> are for an acute attack.</div>\n<div><b>Inhaler technique is examinable</b> — <b>MDI</b>: shake 5 seconds, tongue under the mouthpiece, <b>inhale slowly and deeply</b>, hold 5–10 seconds; spacer if timing is hard. <b>DPI</b>: exhale away from the device, <b>inhale quickly and deeply over 2–3 seconds</b>, hold 4–10 seconds, and <b>never wash it with water</b>. <b>SMI</b>: prime three times before first use and again after 3 days unused, don't cover the side vents, hold 10 seconds.</div>\n<div><b>Action plan and self-management</b> — the plan carries the daily controller schedule, reliever directions, personal control questions, how to step the controller up, when to call, and emergency actions. Add a <b>symptom diary</b> and peak-flow self-monitoring during exacerbations, after a treatment change, and whenever symptoms worsen. Exercise is recommended; adjust the routine or premedicate with a SABA rather than stopping.</div>\n<div><b>Status asthmaticus</b> — a severe life-threatening episode that <b>intensifies once it begins</b> and does not respond to usual therapy: extreme labored breathing, wheezing, accessory muscle use, distended neck veins. IV fluids, systemic bronchodilators, steroids, epinephrine and oxygen immediately; prepare for <b>emergency intubation</b>. <b>Sudden absence of wheezing with a low saturation means complete obstruction</b> and calls for a tracheotomy. Untreated it ends in pneumothorax and cardiac or respiratory arrest.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every asthma drug error on an exam comes from one confusion: <b>bronchodilators open the tube, antiinflammatories calm the lining, and neither does the other's job</b>. That is why a patient with both bronchospasm and inflammation needs two classes, why a steroid inhaler is useless in an attack, and why a LABA alone kills — it masks worsening inflammation while doing nothing about it. And notice the silent chest: a wheeze requires moving air, so its disappearance is deterioration, not improvement.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","ch":"24","chTitle":"Noninfectious lower respiratory conditions","title":"COPD — the gas exchange concept exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--cal-amber);border-top:5px solid var(--cal-amber);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--cal-amber)\">COPD — the gas exchange concept exemplar</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — a collection of lower airway disorders that interfere with airflow and gas exchange, comprising <b>emphysema</b> and <b>chronic bronchitis</b>. They are separate pathologies and most patients have both. Almost <b>15.7 million</b> Americans diagnosed; roughly 3 million deaths a year worldwide.</div>\n<div><b>Emphysema — the sac</b> — destruction of elastic tissue reduces recoil, so the lung hyperinflates. Alveoli are destroyed or become large and flabby; air is trapped; the diaphragm flattens; accessory muscles take over. Primary emphysema is <b>AAT deficiency</b> (suspect it in non-smokers or onset before 40); secondary emphysema is mainly <b>cigarette smoke</b>, plus pollution, occupational exposure, and childhood respiratory infection. Late disease: <b>CO₂ retention with chronic respiratory acidosis</b> and a low PaO₂, and a <b>decreased diffusion capacity</b> because the surface area is gone.</div>\n<div><b>Chronic bronchitis — the tube</b> — inflammation of bronchi and bronchioles with increased mucus and a <b>chronic productive cough at least 3 months a year for 2 consecutive years</b>. Irritants trigger inflammation, vasodilation, mucosal edema, congestion and bronchospasm; mucus glands grow in number and size; walls thicken. Mucus is a breeding ground, so chronic infection follows. Diffusion capacity in bronchitis alone is usually <b>normal</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Risk</b> — smoking is the greatest risk factor, and a <b>20 pack-year history or longer</b> usually already shows PFT changes. Ask about e-cigarettes and vaping too. Asthma is itself a risk factor: COPD incidence is <b>12 times greater</b> in adults with asthma, and the overlap is named <b>asthma-COPD overlap syndrome</b>. Seen more often in women and in adults 65 and over.</div>\n<div><b>Complications</b> — hypoxemia and acidosis, <b>respiratory infection</b>, cardiac failure (especially <b>cor pulmonale</b>), <b>dysrhythmias</b> (from hypoxemia, drugs, acidosis), and respiratory failure. <b>Cor pulmonale key features</b>: hypoxia and hypoxemia, increasing dyspnea, fatigue, an <b>enlarged tender liver</b>, <b>warm cyanotic hands and feet with bounding pulses</b>, cyanotic lips, distended neck veins, right ventricular hypertrophy, visible pulsations below the sternum, nausea or anorexia, dependent edema, metabolic and respiratory acidosis, pulmonary hypertension.</div>\n<div><b>What the patient looks like</b> — thin, muscle mass lost in the extremities but <b>neck muscles enlarged</b>, slow-moving, slightly stooped, sitting forward with arms braced in the <b>orthopneic (tripod) position</b>. Rapid shallow breathing, jerky uncoordinated respiratory movement, abdominal wall sucked in on inspiration; <b>40–50 breaths/min in an exacerbation demands immediate attention</b>. Limited diaphragmatic excursion, <b>decreased fremitus</b>, <b>hyperresonance</b> on percussion, reduced breath sounds, wheezes on inspiration and expiration but <b>crackles usually absent</b>. <b>A silent chest means serious obstruction or pneumothorax.</b> Chronic bronchitis adds a dusky cyanotic appearance, copious sputum, delayed capillary refill and <b>finger clubbing</b>.</div>\n<div><b>GOLD classification — by FEV₁ % predicted</b> · <b>1 Mild</b> ≥80% · <b>2 Moderate</b> ≤50 to &lt;80% · <b>3 Severe</b> ≤30 to &lt;50% · <b>4 Very severe</b> &lt;30%. PFTs are run before and after a bronchodilator; <b>residual volume is the most affected</b> volume, and the <b>FEV₁/FVC ratio falls</b> as disease progresses. The <b>CAT</b> (eight items, 0–5 each, score 0–40) adds an <b>A–D</b> symptom designation, because a GOLD 4 patient can be an A (low exacerbation risk) and a GOLD 1 patient a D (high risk).</div>\n<div><b>Other labs</b> — ABGs then oximetry for trend; <b>polycythemia</b> (raised Hgb/Hct) from chronic hypoxia; electrolytes, because <b>low phosphate, potassium, calcium and magnesium reduce muscle strength</b>; WBC and sputum culture with infection; serum AAT if there is a family history. Saturation often runs well below 90%, and patients usually know their own usual number — <b>a change from their baseline is what requires attention</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — 1. decreased gas exchange; 2. weight loss from dyspnea-limited intake; 3. fatigue from an oxygen supply-demand imbalance; 4. potential for pneumonia and other respiratory infections.</div>\n<div><b>Interventions — airway maintenance first</b> — assess rate, rhythm, depth and accessory muscle use <b>before</b> any intervention, and look for a contributing infection. Same drug classes as asthma, weighted to <b>long-acting</b> agents and combinations (fluticasone/vilanterol; fluticasone/umeclidinium/vilanterol), plus <b>mucolytics</b> (nebulised saline or acetylcysteine, oral guaifenesin). <b>Stepped therapy</b>: up with exacerbations, down when stable. Have the patient demonstrate inhaler technique at <b>every</b> visit. Assess the hospitalised COPD patient <b>at least every 2 hours</b> even when admitted for something else. A <b>sudden sharp CO₂ rise or severe PaO₂ fall</b> means escalate — NIV is preferred over intubation in stable very severe COPD with daytime hypercapnia.</div>\n<div><b>The techniques</b> — <b>diaphragmatic breathing</b> (supine, knees bent, hands or a book on the abdomen for resistance, chest still) and <b>pursed-lip breathing</b> (in through the nose, out slowly through pursed lips, <b>exhalation at least twice as long as inhalation</b>, never holding the breath; inhale before an activity and exhale during it). Teach both <b>when the patient is not dyspneic</b>. Upright with the head elevated and arms supported; out of bed to a chair for <b>1-hour periods two to three times a day</b>. <b>Controlled coughing</b>: feet flat, shoulders inward, head slightly down, pillow hugged; after three to five breaths in through the nose and out through pursed lips, take a deeper breath and cough <b>two or three mini-coughs on one breath</b>, then repeat the whole sequence at least twice. Cough <b>on arising, before meals and before bed</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Oxygen, exercise, hydration, nutrition</b> — <b>2–4 L/min by cannula or up to 40% by Venturi mask</b>, titrated slowly to an <b>SpO₂ of 88–92%</b> with frequent ABGs, knowing oxygen can worsen CO₂ retention — and treating hypoxemia still comes first. No open flames. Pulmonary rehabilitation: walk at a self-paced rate to symptom limit, rest, continue to <b>20 minutes of actual walking</b>, at least <b>two or three times weekly</b>. Fluids <b>at least 2 L/day</b> unless contraindicated. Nutrition: body weight <b>within 10% of ideal</b>, four to six small meals, biggest meal when most rested, bronchodilator <b>30 minutes before eating</b>, high-calorie high-protein food that is easy to chew, avoiding gas-forming and dry foods and caffeine, no fluids before or during meals if satiety is early; monitor weight and <b>prealbumin</b> with a dietitian.</div>\n<div><b>Energy conservation</b> — never rush morning activities; divide each task, pace with rest periods, keep items within reach, sit at a table leaning on the elbows, <b>avoid working with the arms raised</b> (the accessory muscles are busy stabilising the shoulders instead of breathing), don't talk while walking, and never hold the breath during effort.</div>\n<div><b>Surgery</b> — <b>lung volume reduction surgery</b> removes hyperinflated tissue so the remaining lung works better: candidates are under 75, smoke-free for at least 6 months, able to follow an exercise plan, with end-stage emphysema, minimal bronchitis and stable cardiac function; usually bilateral by <b>VATS</b>. After surgery, <b>incentive spirometry 10 times per hour while awake</b>, chest physiotherapy from day one, hourly pulmonary assessment. Transplantation is the treatment of choice when nothing else remains.</div>\n<div><b>Transition to home</b> — an individualised <b>COPD action plan</b> in green / yellow / red zones, given <i>before</i> discharge with follow-up appointments booked, because readmission is common and non-adherence is usually about cost, misunderstanding and transport rather than willingness. The red zone — severe dyspnea at rest, no activity possible, fever or shaking chills, confusion or drowsiness, chest pain, coughing up blood — means call 911, and the plan tells paramedics the patient is a <b>CO₂ retainer</b> and to keep SpO₂ at 88–92%.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The 88–92% target looks like undertreatment and is not. In a chronic retainer the drive to breathe has shifted to hypoxemia, so flooding the patient with oxygen removes the stimulus and lets CO₂ climb — but withholding oxygen from a hypoxic patient kills faster than hypercapnia does. So the rule is <b>give oxygen, titrate it slowly, and watch the CO₂</b>, never \"give less because of the COPD.\" Pursed-lip breathing works on the same physiology in reverse: the mild resistance raises airway pressure and holds the collapsing bronchioles open long enough to empty the trapped air.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","ch":"24","chTitle":"Noninfectious lower respiratory conditions","title":"Lung cancer and the chest tube","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--cal-red);border-top:5px solid var(--cal-red);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--cal-red-2)\">Lung cancer and the chest tube</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The disease</b> — second most common cancer in both men and women, and the prognosis for advanced disease stays poor: 5-year survival about <b>26% for NSCLC and 7% for SCLC</b>. Lung cancers are bronchogenic carcinomas, divided into <b>small cell (SCLC)</b> and <b>non-small cell (NSCLC)</b>. Spread is by direct extension, blood, and lymph — to lung and distant nodes, <b>bone, liver, brain and adrenal glands</b> — and staging uses <b>TNM</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Risk</b> — cigarette smoking causes about <b>81% of lung cancer deaths</b>; second- and thirdhand smoke raise risk too; vaping risk is unknown. Occupational and environmental: asbestos, beryllium, chromium, coal distillates, cobalt, iron oxide, mustard gas, petroleum distillates, radiation, tar, nickel, uranium, and air pollution with benzopyrenes and hydrocarbons. Non-smoking-related cases are rising. Screening is an <b>annual CT for high-risk adults</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Warning signals to teach</b> — hoarseness; a change in respiratory pattern; a persistent or changed cough; blood-streaked, rust-coloured or purulent sputum; frank hemoptysis; chest pain or tightness; shoulder, arm or chest wall pain; <b>recurring pleural effusion, pneumonia or bronchitis</b>; dyspnea; fever with one or two other signs; wheezing; weight loss; <b>finger clubbing</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Assessment</b> — symptoms are nonspecific and <b>late</b>. Pain may be localised, one-sided, pleuritic on inspiration, or radiating to the arm from nerve plexus invasion; fullness or tightness suggests obstruction. <b>Increased fremitus</b> where air space is replaced by tumor or fluid, <b>decreased or absent fremitus</b> when the bronchus is obstructed; the trachea may be <b>displaced from midline</b>. Wheeze = partial obstruction, <b>absent breath sounds = complete obstruction</b>, friction rub = inflammation. Non-pulmonary: muffled heart sounds (tamponade), dysrhythmias, cyanosis, clubbing, and late fatigue, weight loss, anorexia, dysphagia, nausea. <b>Superior vena cava syndrome is an emergency.</b> Confusion or personality change suggests brain metastasis.</div>\n<div><b>Paraneoplastic syndromes</b> — hormones secreted by the tumor itself, most often in SCLC: <b>ACTH</b> → Cushing syndrome; <b>ADH</b> → SIADH with weight gain, general edema and diluted electrolytes; <b>FSH</b> → gynecomastia; <b>parathyroid hormone</b> → hypercalcemia; <b>ectopic insulin</b> → hypoglycemia.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnosis and treatment</b> — chest x-ray finds the lesion, CT defines it and guides biopsy, and <b>definitive diagnosis is cells</b> from biopsy or pleural fluid; thoracoscopy, MRI, radionuclide scans and PET stage it. Chemotherapy is first choice especially in SCLC; <b>targeted therapy</b> requires the target — <b>EGFR</b> (afatinib, erlotinib) or <b>ALK</b> (alectinib, crizotinib) — so cells are tested first; <b>immunotherapy</b> (atezolizumab, durvalumab, nivolumab, pembrolizumab) is not curative and not first-line alone. Radiation for locally confined disease, with skin irritation, fatigue, nausea, taste changes and esophagitis — soft, bland, high-calorie food, and <b>no sun exposure to the field during treatment and for at least a year after</b>. <b>Photodynamic therapy</b> injects a photosensitiser that concentrates in cancer cells over <b>48–72 hours</b>, then laser light kills them. Surgery is the main treatment for <b>stage I and II NSCLC</b>: wedge or segmental resection, <b>lobectomy</b>, sleeve resection, or <b>pneumonectomy</b>, open or by VATS.</div>\n<div><b>Chest tube — what the three chambers do</b> — the <b>air</b> tube sits near the front apex, the <b>fluid</b> tube low on the side at the base; wounds get airtight dressings, and about <b>6 feet of tubing</b> reaches a collection device kept <b>below chest level</b> so gravity drains and the patient can still move. <b>Chamber 1</b> collects drainage — measured hourly for the first 24 hours, and the fluid must <b>never reach the tubes</b> or drainage stops and tension pneumothorax follows. <b>Chamber 2</b> is the water seal, a one-way valve needing <b>at least 2 cm of water</b>; it <b>bubbles</b> as trapped air leaves and stops when the lung is re-expanded. <b>Chamber 3</b> is suction control, wet (gentle bubbling to the prescribed level) or dry (dialled in).</div>\n<div><b>Reading the system</b> — <b>tidaling</b>, the water rising 2–4 inches on inhalation and falling on exhalation, is <b>normal</b>; its absence means the lung has fully re-expanded <i>or</i> the tube is obstructed. Bubbling during forceful expiration or coughing is expected; <b>continuous bubbling means an air leak</b> — check connections and notify the surgeon. Keep the tubing straight without kinks or dependent loops, coil extra tubing loosely on the bed, tape the connections, and <b>clamp only briefly</b> to change the system or hunt a leak. Keep <b>sterile gauze and padded clamps at the bedside</b>. Do not strip the tube; milk it hand-over-hand only if prescribed. Assess hourly for the first hours after <b>removal</b>, because respiratory distress may mean the lung has collapsed again.</div>\n<div><b>Notify the surgeon or Rapid Response Team immediately for</b> — tracheal deviation from midline; sudden or increasing dyspnea; <b>saturation below 90%</b>; <b>drainage greater than 70 mL/hr</b>; visible eyelets on the tube; the tube <b>falling out</b> (first cover the site with dry sterile gauze); the tube <b>disconnecting</b> from the system (first put the end in sterile water below chest level); drainage stopping in the first 24 hours.</div>\n<div><b>After thoracic surgery</b> — airway, ventilation and complication prevention, assessed <b>at least every 2 hours</b>: check <b>tracheal alignment</b>, saturation, rate and depth, and listen to the <b>non-operative side</b> for crackles. Oxygen by mask or cannula for the first 2 days, semi-Fowler or up in a chair early, <b>incentive spirometry every hour while awake</b>, splint the incision to cough, and make sure the chest tube does not pull. PCA taught before pain escalates; vital signs before and after opioids. <b>Pneumonectomy</b> has no chest tube, and its complications are <b>empyema</b> and a <b>bronchopleural fistula</b>; positioning is by surgeon preference — non-operative side to protect the bronchial stump, or operative side to let fluid fill the space.</div>\n<div><b>Palliation</b> — oxygen for hypoxemia and for the sensation of dyspnea; radiation in higher doses over shorter periods for hemoptysis, bronchial or vena cava obstruction, esophageal compression and bone pain; <b>thoracentesis</b> for pleural effusion, knowing fluid re-forms; continuous <b>morphine infusion</b> and positioning for dyspnea, often most comfortable sleeping upright in a recliner; around-the-clock analgesia with PRN doses for breakthrough; <b>hospice</b> in the terminal phase.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The chest tube is a physics problem, not a memory list. The pleural space is normally at negative pressure; open it and air rushes in and the lung collapses. Everything the nurse does protects a one-way exit: water seal above 2 cm, collection below the chest, no kinks, no prolonged clamping. That is also why the two emergencies have opposite instincts — a tube <b>out of the patient</b> gets an occlusive gauze over the hole, while a tube <b>disconnected from the system</b> goes into sterile water rather than being reconnected in haste, because in each case you are re-establishing the valve, not the tubing.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"24-cystic-fibrosis-in-the-adult","ch":"24","chTitle":"Noninfectious lower respiratory conditions","title":"Cystic fibrosis in the adult","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Cystic fibrosis in the adult</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>One defect, every organ</b> — an <b>autosomal recessive</b> disease of the <b>CFTR</b> gene: blocked chloride transport across cell membranes produces mucus with little water, so it is thick and sticky. It plugs airways in the lung and glandular tissue in the pancreas, liver, salivary glands and testes. Over <b>2000 mutations</b> are known, about 25 account for most patients, and the mutation explains why severity differs so much. Nearly <b>half of US patients with CF are adults</b>; average life expectancy is about <b>46 years</b> and <b>respiratory failure is the main cause of death</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnosis</b> — <b>sweat chloride 60 mmol/L or more is positive</b>; 30–59 means possible, needing more testing. Genetic testing names the mutation. Usually diagnosed by age 2, but some adults are diagnosed late.</div>\n<div><b>Pulmonary picture</b> — chest congestion, limited exercise tolerance, cough, sputum, accessory muscle use, falling <b>FVC and FEV₁</b>, chest x-ray infiltrate with increased AP diameter. Constant mucus means chronic infection, chronic bronchitis and <b>bronchiectasis</b>, with lung abscesses common; complications are pneumothorax, arterial erosion with hemorrhage, and respiratory failure.</div>\n<div><b>Non-pulmonary</b> — abdominal distention, GERD, rectal prolapse, foul-smelling stools and <b>steatorrhea</b>; malnutrition with <b>fat-soluble vitamin (A, D, E, K) deficiency</b>; pancreatic insufficiency, male sterility, cirrhosis, osteoporosis, and <b>diabetes in more than 50% of adults with CF</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Exacerbation is defined</b> — increased congestion, reduced activity tolerance, new or increased crackles, and a <b>10% decrease in FEV₁</b>, with more sputum (bloody or purulent), more cough, appetite and weight loss, fatigue, falling SpO₂ and retractions. Treat with intensified airway clearance <b>four times a day</b>, bronchodilators and mucolytics, steroids, oxygen by SpO₂, and a <b>14- to 21-day antibiotic course</b> guided by sputum — IV if oral fails or the exacerbation is severe. <b>Avoid mechanical ventilation wherever possible</b>; BiPAP may be daily therapy in advanced disease.</div>\n<div><b>Airway clearance</b> — positive expiratory pressure, active cycle of breathing, individualised exercise, and daily chest physiotherapy with postural drainage. The <b>HFCWO vest</b> compresses and releases the chest wall up to <b>25 times per second</b>, creating mini-coughs that dislodge and thin mucus.</div>\n<div><b>The two organisms</b> — <b>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</b> is the most common. <b>Burkholderia cepacia</b> is the dangerous one: often antibiotic-resistant and <b>spread by casual contact between people with CF</b>, so infected and non-infected patients are separated on units and seen on different clinic days, equipment and rooms get CF-approved cleaning, and patients are taught <b>not to shake hands routinely</b> and to wash hands meticulously, since surfaces and tissues also carry it. Treatment adds co-trimoxazole.</div>\n<div><b>Modulators</b> — <b>ivacaftor</b> (a potentiator) holds the chloride channel open longer for responsive mutations; <b>ivacaftor/lumacaftor</b> is a corrector for the <b>F508del</b> mutation, the most common one; the FDA-approved <b>triple combination ivacaftor + tezacaftor + elexacaftor</b> now helps up to 90% of patients. Recurrent hemoptysis may need <b>arterial embolisation</b>, sometimes repeatedly. Transplantation relieves lung disease only — the pancreatic and other organ problems continue, and lifelong antirejection therapy raises infection risk.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every feature of CF is downstream of water. Chloride cannot move, so water does not follow it into the secretions, so every secretion in the body is too thick to flow: mucus plugs the bronchiole, enzymes never leave the pancreas, bile thickens in the liver. That is why the therapy is mechanical (vest, drainage, mucolytics) rather than merely pharmacologic, and why the newest drugs are aimed at the channel itself instead of the symptoms.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"24-pulmonary-arterial-hypertension-and-idio","ch":"24","chTitle":"Noninfectious lower respiratory conditions","title":"Pulmonary arterial hypertension and idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Pulmonary arterial hypertension and idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>PAH — the numbers</b> — pulmonary hypertension is a chronic pulmonary vascular pressure <b>above 25 mm Hg</b> when normal is <b>15–18</b>. The thin-walled right ventricle must overcome it, and over time that is <b>cor pulmonale</b>, which can be fatal. Secondary pulmonary hypertension follows COPD or fibrosis — controlling the cause delays cor pulmonale. <b>Primary (idiopathic) PAH</b> is vascular and lung tissue growth change for unknown reasons, diagnosed only in the absence of other lung disease.</div>\n<div><b>Who and how fast</b> — mostly <b>women aged 30–60</b>, clustering in families; the familial form is <b>autosomal dominant</b>, and <b>15–20% carry a BMPR2 mutation</b> whose over-activation thickens pulmonary arterial smooth muscle. Appetite-suppressant exposure (fenfluramine/phentermine, dexfenfluramine) raises risk. <b>Without treatment death usually occurs within 2 years</b>, most often from heart failure — and it is often diagnosed late, after the heart and lungs are already damaged. Teach adults, especially women, with a first-degree relative to have regular checks.</div>\n<div><b>Presentation and diagnosis</b> — the earliest symptoms are <b>dyspnea and fatigue in an otherwise healthy adult</b>, sometimes angina-like chest pain. Diagnosis is <b>right-sided heart catheterisation</b> showing elevated pulmonary pressures; V/Q scanning, PFTs with reduced volumes and diffusion, and CT support it. <b>Class I</b> no activity limitation → <b>II</b> no symptoms at rest, symptoms with mild-moderate activity → <b>III</b> symptoms with less than ordinary activity → <b>IV</b> dyspnea and fatigue <b>at rest</b> with signs of right heart failure.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs and the teaching that goes with them</b> — <b>endothelin receptor antagonists</b> (bosentan, ambrisentan, macitentan): take with a full glass of water, do not break, chew or crush; all cause some hypotension, carry <b>liver toxicity</b> (teach jaundice, nausea, dark urine, right upper quadrant pain, and no alcohol), and are <b>contraindicated in pregnancy</b> — <b>two reliable methods of contraception and monthly pregnancy testing</b>. <b>Prostacyclin agonists</b>: IV <b>epoprostenol</b> by continuous central infusion <b>must never be stopped abruptly</b> (rebound pulmonary hypertension); treprostinil can be subcutaneous, IV, inhaled or oral. <b>Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors</b> (sildenafil, tadalafil) improve exercise capacity and cause general and postural hypotension. Oxygen to keep saturation <b>above 90%</b>, diuretics with <b>daily weights</b>. Refer to a specialised centre — PAH-specific therapy can harm the wrong patient. Surgery is lung transplantation, or <b>heart-lung transplantation once cor pulmonale is present</b>.</div>\n<div><b>IPF — excessive healing</b> — a <b>restrictive</b> disease of unknown cause, most often <b>men aged 50–70</b>, with smoking and inhalation irritant exposure as risks. Inflammation that should stop continues past normal healing, and fibrosis and scarring thicken alveolar tissue until gas exchange fails. <b>Death usually occurs within 3–5 years</b> of diagnosis.</div>\n<div><b>IPF findings and care</b> — slow onset with mild exertional dyspnea, decreased <b>FVC</b>, and a <b>\"honeycomb\" pattern on high-resolution CT</b>; later, rapid shallow breathing and hypoxemia severe enough to resist high-flow oxygen. <b>Nintedanib</b> (tyrosine kinase inhibitor) and <b>pirfenidone</b> (antifibrotic) slow progression and are started <b>early</b>; corticosteroids, N-acetylcysteine and immunosuppressants are also used. Transplantation is the only cure and rarely available. Nursing focus: prevent respiratory infection, teach continuous home oxygen, energy conservation and pacing for profound fatigue, and in late disease <b>oral, parenteral or nebulised morphine for the sensation of dyspnea</b>, with hospice when survival is expected to be under 6 months.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>These two diseases are the chapter's reminder that \"lung problem\" does not mean \"airway problem.\" PAH is a <b>vessel</b> disease, so the treatment is vasodilation and the organ that fails is the right heart; IPF is a <b>membrane</b> disease, so no bronchodilator helps and oxygen eventually stops working because the distance from alveolus to capillary — not the width of the tube — is the problem. Matching the failing structure to the drug class is the whole skill.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"24-adpie","ch":"24","chTitle":"Noninfectious lower respiratory conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">These diseases are chronic and progressive, so the nurse's reference value is <b>this patient's own baseline</b>, the assessment is the work of breathing rather than a single number, and the plan is mostly teaching the patient to co-manage a disease that will outlast the admission.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0;opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Work of breathing</div>Rate, rhythm, depth and <b>accessory muscle use before any intervention</b>; <b>how many words between breaths</b>; retractions, jerky uncoordinated movement, abdominal wall sucked in on inspiration; the <b>tripod position</b>; <b>40–50 breaths/min in an exacerbation needs immediate attention</b>. Percussion hyperresonance, decreased fremitus and excursion, reduced breath sounds, wheeze — and a <b>silent chest as obstruction or pneumothorax</b>, not improvement. Grade dyspnea on the <b>Visual Analog Dyspnea Scale</b> and reuse it to judge therapy.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History that changes the plan</div><b>Pack-years</b>, vaping, occupational and environmental exposure, family history (AAT deficiency, CF, PAH). Asthma pattern — continuous, seasonal, activity, work, <b>night waking</b>, 4–8 weeks after a cold — and the four <b>GINA control questions</b>. Compare activity and dyspnea with a month and a year ago; ask about <b>orthopnea</b>, ADLs, sexual activity, sleep, and eating. <b>Weigh and compare with previous weights.</b></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Numbers and trends</div>ABGs for gas exchange and acid-base, then oximetry for trend against the patient's <b>usual</b> saturation; <b>PaCO₂ falls early and rises late</b>. PFTs: <b>12% or more improvement after a bronchodilator diagnoses asthma</b>; <b>GOLD stages by FEV₁ % predicted</b>; RV most affected; falling FEV₁/FVC. Polycythemia, electrolytes (<b>low phosphate, potassium, calcium, magnesium weaken muscle</b>), WBC and sputum culture, serum AAT, <b>sweat chloride ≥60 mmol/L</b>, right heart catheterisation for PAH, <b>honeycombing on HRCT</b> for IPF.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Right heart, nutrition, psychosocial</div><b>Cor pulmonale</b>: distended neck veins, dependent edema, enlarged tender liver, warm cyanotic extremities with bounding pulses, pulsations below the sternum. Clubbing and delayed capillary refill for chronic hypoxia. Weight, intake, early satiety, meal-related dyspnea, prealbumin. Isolation from dyspnea or embarrassment about coughing and sputum; <b>cost of inhalers</b> — many patients use them only during exacerbations; and the equity gap in COPD prevalence and access.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Asking how many words the patient can say between breaths is a better measurement than a saturation reading, and it costs nothing. Saturation is a late, blunt signal in chronic lung disease — it can sit at the patient's usual 89% while the work of breathing has doubled. Effort fails before oxygenation does, so effort is what you watch.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\">\n<div><b>1. Decreased gas exchange</b> due to physiologic changes in the respiratory system — evidenced by dyspnea, accessory muscle use, a saturation below the patient's baseline, or a rising PaCO₂.</div>\n<div><b>2. Weight loss</b> due to decreased intake associated with dyspnea — the work of breathing raises calorie and protein needs while dyspnea, nausea and early satiety cut intake.</div>\n<div><b>3. Fatigue</b> due to an imbalance between oxygen supply and demand.</div>\n<div><b>4. Potential for pneumonia or other respiratory infection</b> — excess mucus plus poor clearance is a breeding ground, and infection is what precipitates most exacerbations.</div>\n<div><b>Above all of them, when present:</b> acute airway obstruction — status asthmaticus, a silent chest, a sudden CO₂ rise, or a chest tube emergency. Chronic priorities yield to an airway that is closing now.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Weight loss ranks second, above fatigue, because malnutrition here is not cosmetic: the patient loses muscle mass and strength, lung elasticity and alveolar-capillary surface area — so poor nutrition <i>directly</i> reduces gas exchange and makes problem 1 worse. Feeding this patient is a respiratory intervention.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\">\n<div>Gas exchange is <b>attained and maintained at the patient's usual baseline</b> — not at a textbook value.</div>\n<div>The patient coughs and clears secretions effectively, with a respiratory rate and rhythm appropriate to the activity, and a breathing pattern that <b>decreases the work of breathing</b>.</div>\n<div>Body weight is achieved and maintained <b>within 10% of ideal</b>.</div>\n<div>The patient reports increased endurance and decreased fatigue, and activity rises on a basis of <b>balanced oxygen supply and demand</b>.</div>\n<div>Serious respiratory infection is avoided, with crowd avoidance and <b>pneumonia and yearly influenza vaccination</b>.</div>\n<div>Before discharge the patient holds a personal <b>action plan</b> — asthma or COPD, green/yellow/red — demonstrates inhaler technique, states when to call and when to call 911, and has follow-up appointments already booked.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>\"At the patient's usual baseline\" is the most important six words in this chapter's outcomes. A patient whose chronic saturation is 89% and whose PaCO₂ chronically sits at 55 is <i>at goal</i> there; driving those numbers to normal is not a success but an intervention error. The plan is written against the patient's own physiology.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Independent</div><b>Airway maintenance is the most important focus.</b> Upright positioning with arms supported; out of bed to a chair <b>1 hour, two to three times a day</b>; <b>pursed-lip and diaphragmatic breathing</b> taught while the patient is not dyspneic; <b>controlled coughing</b> on arising, before meals and before bed; fluids <b>at least 2 L/day</b>; small frequent high-calorie high-protein meals with the bronchodilator <b>30 minutes before</b>; energy conservation and pacing, arms down, no talking while walking; <b>assess the hospitalised COPD patient at least every 2 hours</b>; <b>suction only when needed, never routinely</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0;opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Dependent</div>Oxygen <b>2–4 L/min by cannula or up to 40% by Venturi mask, titrated to SpO₂ 88–92%</b> with ABG monitoring; SABA for relief and inhaled corticosteroid plus LABA or LAMA for control, stepped up and down; mucolytics and nebulised saline or acetylcysteine; systemic steroids and antibiotics in exacerbation; <b>PAH-specific therapy through a specialist centre</b>; nintedanib or pirfenidone early in IPF; CFTR modulators; chemotherapy, targeted therapy by tested mutation, immunotherapy, radiation, or morphine for palliation.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0;opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Emergency actions</div><b>Status asthmaticus</b>: IV fluids, systemic bronchodilators, steroids, epinephrine, oxygen — and prepare for intubation; <b>sudden loss of wheezing with a low saturation is complete obstruction and needs a tracheotomy</b>. A sudden sharp CO₂ rise or severe PaO₂ fall means escalate to NIV or ventilation. <b>Chest tube out</b> — occlusive dry sterile gauze over the site; <b>tube disconnected</b> — end into sterile water below chest level; then call. Notify immediately for tracheal deviation, new or worse dyspnea, <b>SpO₂ &lt;90%</b>, <b>drainage &gt;70 mL/hr</b>, visible eyelets, or drainage stopping in the first 24 hours.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0;opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Collaborative</div><b>Dietitian</b> for the weight and prealbumin problem; respiratory therapy for inhaler technique, airway clearance and BiPAP; <b>pulmonary rehabilitation</b>, walking to 20 minutes at least two or three times weekly; occupational therapy for adaptive tools; case manager for home oxygen, nebuliser, hospital bed, shower chair and home visits; <b>smoking cessation using I-PREPARE</b>; social work for drug cost and transport; <b>hospice</b> in end-stage IPF and lung cancer; CF and transplant teams, with <b>infection separation for Burkholderia cepacia</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Almost every independent action in this list is a <b>mechanical</b> answer to a mechanical problem: pursed lips add the resistance the destroyed elastin no longer provides, the tripod position lends the arms to the accessory muscles, controlled coughing moves mucus with the airways splinted open instead of collapsing them, and hydration thins what the cough has to move. None of it needs an order, and all of it is what the patient will still be doing at home.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\">\n<div>Gas exchange is back at the patient's chronic baseline; secretions are cleared effectively; rate and rhythm suit the activity; the breathing pattern costs less effort. Weight is within 10% of ideal and prealbumin is stable. Endurance is up and fatigue down. No serious respiratory infection, with vaccinations current. Inhaler technique is <b>demonstrated, not described</b>, peak flows are self-monitored, and the action plan is in the patient's hands.</div>\n<div><b>Unmet looks like:</b> rising respiratory effort or rate, a saturation falling below the patient's usual value, <b>a sudden sharp PaCO₂ rise</b>, severe dyspnea with respiratory muscle fatigue and unresponsive hypoxemia — the signs of <b>impending respiratory failure</b>; new crackles or a silent chest; a <b>10% fall in FEV₁</b> in cystic fibrosis; weight still falling; dependent edema or neck vein distention appearing; or a readmission with an exacerbation, which usually means the action plan, the appointment, or the drug cost failed rather than the patient.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Evaluation in chapter 23 was measured in minutes on a clock; here it is measured in <b>trends and demonstrations</b>. The two findings that still demand a clock are the ones that mean the chronic disease has become acute: a sharp rise in CO₂, and respiratory muscle fatigue with hypoxemia that will not lift. Both say the patient can no longer do the work of breathing — and that is respiratory failure, whatever the chart says the baseline is.</div>\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"25-overview","ch":"25","chTitle":"Infectious respiratory conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">25 · Chapter 25</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Gas exchange · infection · immunity · inflammation</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Infectious respiratory conditions</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> Chapter 24 was structure failing slowly; this chapter is an <i>organism</i> filling the space where gas should be. Two concepts run in parallel the whole way through — <b>gas exchange</b> (the alveolus is full of exudate, so oxygen cannot cross) and <b>infection</b> (the organism is transmissible, so the patient is also a source). Every condition here is therefore two questions at once: how badly is diffusion impaired, and who else is at risk. The four that matter most are <b>influenza, pneumonia, COVID-19 and tuberculosis</b>, and the nursing work divides cleanly into <b>prevent transmission, restore gas exchange, and finish the drug course</b>.</p><div id=\"ch25-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;font-size:11.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Alveoli stay dry and clean because of cough, cilia and immune cells.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Pathogens multiply; exudate, WBCs and edema fill alveoli and thicken the membrane.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Fever, productive cough, crackles/bronchial sounds, dyspnea, pleuritic pain; in older adults, confusion.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">CXR infiltrate, sputum culture, blood cultures, CBC with left shift, AFB smear/culture for TB.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Antibiotics or antivirals targeted to the organism, oxygen, fluids, mobility, isolation.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Sepsis, respiratory failure, empyema, spread to others.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">New confusion, RR ≥30, SBP &lt;90, SpO₂ falling despite oxygen, muffled voice with drooling (abscess).</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Organism in alveoli<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>exudate fills air spaces<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>diffusion falls<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>SpO₂ drops and crackles appear<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>oxygen, cultures, then antibiotics without delay</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Droplet vs airborne</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Influenza: surgical mask. TB: negative-pressure room and N95.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Influenza vs inhalation anthrax</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Both start flu-like; anthrax adds mediastinal widening and sudden collapse.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Isoniazid → B6 depletion → peripheral neuropathy → give pyridoxine. Rifampin → pigmented metabolites → orange fluids; enzyme inducer → lowers warfarin and contraceptive effect. <a href=\"Exam 2 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Connections to other chapters</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Ch 22 assessment and shunt; ch 34 neutropenia hides fever.</div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Fever trend down, SpO₂ improving, sputum clearing.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Sepsis signs, confusion, rising RR, airway swelling.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>How infection destroys diffusion</b> — pathogens penetrate the airway mucosa and multiply in the alveolar spaces. White cells move in, capillaries leak, edema and <b>exudate</b> form in and around the alveoli, and the alveolar walls thicken. Red cells and fibrin follow. Gas now has to cross a thick, wet wall instead of a thin dry one: <b>hypoxemia</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Then the lung stiffens</b> — fibrin and edema reduce compliance and vital capacity, and <span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>collapse of alveoli</em><b>atel-</b> incomplete · <b>ectasis</b> expansion</i><u>atel</u><s>·</s><u>ectasis</u></span> takes more units out of service. Capillary leak spreads the infection laterally; blood flowing past unventilated alveoli is <b>shunted</b> past the exchange surface unoxygenated.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Two spread patterns</b> — <b>lobar</b> pneumonia produces <span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>lung tissue solidified by exudate, with no air spaces</em><b>con-</b> together · <b>solidus</b> solid</i><u>consolidation</u></span> in a segment or a whole lobe; <b>bronchopneumonia</b> scatters patches around the bronchi. How far it goes depends on host defences — bacteria multiply quickly where immunity is compromised. An abscess that perforates the bronchial wall causes tissue necrosis.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Where it goes next</b> — into the bloodstream is <b>septicemia and sepsis</b>; into the pleural cavity is <span class=\"wp\" tabindex=\"0\"><i><em>pus in the pleural cavity</em><b>em-</b> in · <b>pyo-</b> pus</i><u>em</u><s>·</s><u>pyema</u></span>. Pneumonia is a frequent cause of sepsis, which is why a sepsis screening tool and a saturation with every set of vital signs are standing orders in this chapter.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why the findings are the opposite of COPD's</b> — fluid conducts sound better than air. So pneumonia gives <b>increased fremitus</b>, <b>dull percussion</b>, <b>bronchial breath sounds over consolidated lung</b> and crackles, while emphysema gives decreased fremitus and hyperresonance. Same stethoscope, opposite readings, because one lung is too full and the other too empty.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why immunity is the whole prevention story</b> — viral respiratory strains change slightly every year, so last year's antibodies are only partly effective: that is why adults get several colds a year and why influenza vaccination is annual. A <b>pandemic</b> organism is one humans have <i>no</i> ancestral immunity to, usually because it previously infected only birds or other animals and then mutated — 1918 influenza, 2009 H1N1, COVID-19.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The single most tested idea in this chapter is that <b>the older adult does not present typically</b>. Fever and cough may be absent and the WBC count may stay normal until infection is severe, but hypoxemia is usually present — so the most common symptom of pneumonia in an older adult is <b>a change in cognition</b>, acute confusion from hypoxia. Waiting for the textbook picture greatly increases the risk of sepsis and death, which is why new confusion in an older adult is a respiratory assessment, not a neurological one.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","ch":"25","chTitle":"Infectious respiratory conditions","title":"Pneumonia — the gas exchange concept exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--cal-amber);border-top:5px solid var(--cal-amber);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--cal-amber)\">Pneumonia — the gas exchange concept exemplar</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — excess fluid in the lungs from an inflammatory process; infectious pneumonia adds a <b>thick exudate of proteins and other particles</b> that seriously reduces gas exchange. Inflammation sits in the interstitial spaces, the alveoli and often the bronchioles. Any organism can cause it — bacteria, viruses, mycoplasmas, fungi, rickettsiae, protozoa, helminths — and non-infectious causes are inhaled toxic gases, chemical fumes and smoke, or aspiration of water, food, fluid including <b>saliva</b>, and vomitus.</div>\n<div><b>The organisms to name</b> — most common bacterial cause <b>Streptococcus pneumoniae</b>; most common viral causes <b>influenza A, influenza B and COVID-19</b>. Drug-resistant S. pneumoniae (DRSP) is increasingly common, so local resistance patterns drive the antibiotic choice.</div>\n<div><b>Three categories</b> — <b>community-acquired (CAP)</b>: acquired in the community, most common, peaks in late fall and winter, often as a complication of influenza; antibiotics are usually empirical, minimum <b>5 days</b>, and in the emergency department the <b>first dose should be given within 4 hours</b>. <b>Hospital-acquired (HAP)</b>: onset <b>≥48 hours after admission</b>. <b>Ventilator-associated (VAP)</b>: onset <b>≥48 hours after intubation</b>, because the tube bypasses protective airway mechanisms and lets oropharyngeal and gastric secretions be aspirated — dental plaque raises the risk too. The old term HCAP is no longer used.</div>\n<div><b>Preventing HAP and VAP</b> — pulmonary hygiene and progressive ambulation, adequate hydration, <b>aspiration risk screening with an evidence-based tool</b>, monitoring for early sepsis, <b>vigorous oral care</b>, and hand hygiene, which is critical. The VAP bundle: <b>head of bed at least 30 degrees</b>, daily weaning assessment, DVT prophylaxis, an oral care regimen, stress ulcer prophylaxis, and suctioning as needed or continuous subglottal suction.</div>\n<div><b>Risk factors — CAP</b>: older adult; never had the pneumococcal vaccine or had it more than <b>5 years</b> ago; no influenza vaccine last year; COVID-19 series and boosters not current; a chronic condition that reduces immunity; recent exposure to a respiratory viral or influenza infection; tobacco or alcohol use or heavy secondhand smoke. <b>Nosocomial</b>: older adult, chronic lung disease, <b>gram-negative colonisation of mouth, throat and stomach</b>, altered consciousness, a recent aspiration event, an endotracheal or nasogastric tube or tracheostomy, poor nutrition, reduced immunity, <b>drugs that raise gastric pH</b> (H₂ blockers, antacids) or alkaline tube feedings, and mechanical ventilation.</div>\n<div><b>Prevention teaching</b> — annual influenza vaccine, pneumococcal vaccine as recommended (<b>PPSV23 and PCV15</b>, or <b>PCV20</b> alone for convenience; recommended over 65 and for adults 19–64 with risk factors), COVID-19 primary series and boosters; avoid crowds in flu season; cough, turn, move and deep breathe if mobility is limited; clean home respiratory equipment as instructed; avoid dust, secondhand smoke and aerosols; do not smoke or vape; rest, eat well and drink <b>at least 3 L of non-alcoholic fluid a day</b> unless restricted. Teach a patient with a cold or flu to be seen if <b>fever lasts more than 24 hours</b>, the illness lasts more than a week, or symptoms worsen.</div>\n<div><b>Assessment</b> — flushed cheeks and an anxious expression; chest pain or discomfort, myalgia, headache, chills, fever, cough, tachycardia, dyspnea, tachypnea, hemoptysis, sputum; severe chest muscle weakness from sustained coughing. The patient sits upright balancing on the hands — the <b>tripod position</b>. <b>Crackles</b> where fluid is in interstitial and alveolar areas, diminished breath sounds, wheeze if exudate narrows airways, <b>bronchial breath sounds over consolidation</b>, <b>increased fremitus</b>, <b>dull percussion</b>, and diminished or unequal chest expansion. Compare vital signs with baseline: hypotension with orthostatic change from vasodilation and dehydration, and a <b>rapid weak pulse</b> that may mean hypoxemia, dehydration, or impending sepsis and shock; dysrhythmias from cardiac tissue hypoxia.</div>\n<div><b>Why each sign happens</b> — raised rate and dyspnea from chemoreceptor and J-receptor stimulation, reduced compliance, anxiety and pain; hypoxemia from alveolar consolidation and pulmonary capillary shunting; cough from fluid in tracheal, bronchial and bronchiolar receptors; <b>purulent, blood-tinged or rust-coloured sputum</b> from capillary fluid and red cells moving into the alveoli; fever because phagocytes release pyrogens that reset the hypothalamus; <b>pleuritic pain</b> from inflammation of the parietal pleura hurting on inspiration.</div>\n<div><b>Labs and imaging</b> — sputum for Gram stain, culture and sensitivity (often the organism is never identified; a <b>sputum trap</b> during suctioning for the very ill); CBC for a raised WBC — <b>often normal in older adults</b>; blood cultures for bloodstream invasion; ABGs in the severely ill; electrolytes, BUN and creatinine (a <b>high BUN and hypernatremia signal dehydration</b>); a <b>lactate</b> for sepsis; influenza testing alongside CAP. <b>Chest x-ray is the most common test</b> but may not change for <b>2 or more days</b> after symptoms — and in the older adult it is essential precisely because the symptoms are vague. Pulse oximetry for hypoxemia; thoracentesis if there is a pleural effusion.</div>\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — 1. decreased gas exchange due to decreased diffusion at the alveolar-capillary membrane; 2. potential for airway obstruction due to inflammation with excessive secretions, fatigue and muscle weakness; 3. <b>potential for sepsis</b> due to organisms in a very vascular area plus reduced immunity.</div>\n<div><b>Interventions</b> — oxygen by cannula or mask (a confused patient may not tolerate a mask; check skin under the device and the elastic band, especially at the ears); <b>incentive spirometry</b> — sit up, exhale fully, seal the mouthpiece, long slow deep breath raising the piston as high as possible, <b>hold 2–4 seconds</b>, exhale slowly, <b>5–10 breaths per session every hour while awake</b>, recording the volume; cough and deep breathe <b>at least every 2 hours</b>; <b>at least 2 L of fluid daily</b> to thin secretions, with intake and output, mucous membranes and skin turgor watched; bronchodilators for bronchospasm, inhaled or IV steroids for airway swelling, expectorants such as guaifenesin. <b>Antiinfectives for every pneumonia except viral</b>: about <b>5–7 days for uncomplicated CAP</b>, up to <b>21 days</b> for severely impaired immunity or HAP. Aspiration of acidic stomach contents can cause widespread inflammation leading to <b>ARDS and permanent lung damage</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Transition and evaluation</b> — finish the whole antibiotic course; expect <b>fatigue, weakness and a residual cough for weeks</b>, and reassure that complete recovery will occur; stay on one floor if stairs are tiring, consider a bedside commode; report returning or persistent chills, fever, cough, dyspnea, wheezing, hemoptysis, increased sputum, chest discomfort or increasing fatigue; avoid crowds, ill people and irritants; vaccinate after recovery. Outcomes: <b>SaO₂ at least 95% or the patient's normal level</b>, clear breath sounds, no fever with a normal WBC, and return to the pre-pneumonia health status. <b>Readmission within 30 days is high, especially in older adults.</b></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Notice what the pneumonia intervention list actually is: oxygen, spirometry, coughing, fluid, and finishing the antibiotic. Three of those five are things the <b>patient</b> does — which is why teaching technique and completing the course is not discharge paperwork but the treatment itself. And aspiration is described as often <b>silent</b>: no cough, no gag, no complaint. That is why screening for aspiration risk is a routine assessment rather than a response to an event.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","ch":"25","chTitle":"Infectious respiratory conditions","title":"COVID-19 — the infection concept exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">COVID-19 — the infection concept exemplar</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The virus and the receptor</b> — COVID-19 is caused by <b>SARS-CoV-2</b>, a coronavirus. It enters host cells by binding <b>ACE2 receptors</b>, which sit in vascular endothelium, lungs, heart, brain, kidneys, intestine, liver and pharynx — so this is a disease that can injure <i>any</i> organ. Its effects are inflammation, vasoconstriction, <b>hypercoagulability</b>, endothelial dysfunction and edema. Severe illness is attributed to a <b>cytokine storm</b> triggered when the virus infects type 2 alveolar epithelial cells. Earlier coronavirus jumps produced SARS (2002–2003) and MERS (2012); variants — omicron, alpha, beta, gamma, delta — evade immunity and some transmit more easily.</div>\n<div><b>Vaccination</b> — recommended from <b>6 months</b> of age. Pfizer-BioNTech and Moderna are <b>mRNA</b> vaccines whose material instructs cells to make a protein that generates lymphocytes ready to fight the virus; Novavax is a <b>protein subunit</b> vaccine; the Janssen single-dose <b>vector</b> vaccine is used less because of thrombosis with thrombocytopenia syndrome. Primary series: same brand, Pfizer <b>3–8 weeks</b> apart, Moderna <b>4–8 weeks</b> apart; fully vaccinated <b>2 weeks after the final primary dose</b>; boosters for most at <b>5 months</b>, a second booster for adults 50+ at least <b>4 months</b> after the first. Expected side effects — injection site pain and swelling, headache, muscle pain, chills, fever, fatigue, nausea — are signs of immunity building; <b>rare: anaphylaxis and myocarditis</b>, so observe <b>at least 15 minutes</b> after each dose. Do not mix products in the primary series; wait until isolation is complete to vaccinate; after infection, vaccination is recommended <b>3 months</b> after recovery.</div>\n<div><b>Equity</b> — Black/African American, Hispanic/Latino, American Indian and Alaska Native adults have had substantially higher incidence and death rates, and these disparities persist after adjusting for other demographic and socioeconomic factors. Mitigation strategies themselves — physical distancing among them — had unequal economic, social and secondary health consequences. The key nursing strategy is <b>addressing misinformation and rebuilding trust</b>, not repeating the recommendation louder.</div>\n<div><b>History</b> — upper respiratory symptoms, cough, fever, chills, GI upset, and <b>new loss of taste (ageusia) or smell (anosmia)</b>. Ask about travel, whether household members are sick, whether the patient or anyone in the household has been in a hospital or long-term care facility in the last <b>30 days</b>, access to and results of home testing, and vaccination status — remembering COVID-19 occurs in vaccinated people too. Symptoms appear <b>2–14 days</b> after exposure.</div>\n<div><b>The five dyspnea questions</b> — are you breathing harder or faster than usual while sitting still? Does it feel difficult to catch your breath? When you walk, do you feel dizzy? What brings the shortness of breath on? Has it got worse over the last one or two days? Assessment may be by telehealth or webcam, because home management is preferred wherever possible.</div>\n<div><b>Key features</b> — fever or chills, cough, shortness of breath, fatigue, muscle aches, headache, ageusia or anosmia, sore throat, nausea or vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain. <b>Unique features in some patients</b>: conjunctivitis, a <b>prothrombotic state</b> with venous and arterial thromboembolism, neurologic findings including encephalopathy with agitated delirium, and reddish nodules on distal digits in young adults. <b>Emergency indications</b>: trouble breathing, persistent chest pain or pressure, <b>new confusion</b>, inability to wake or stay awake, bluish lips or face.</div>\n<div><b>Testing</b> — the preferred confirmation is a <b>NAAT, usually RT-PCR</b>, collected by someone other than the patient, with results in days; rapid point-of-care antigen tests and some NAATs give minutes; home antigen testing is widely available. Both NAAT and antigen are <b>viral</b> tests — they look for current infection. <b>Antibody (serology) tests indicate past infection and do not diagnose current infection.</b> Samples are usually nasal swabs, sometimes saliva. Chest imaging for infiltrates when there is dyspnea or progression risk. <b>A saturation of 94% or less on room air requires in-person evaluation</b> — and a normal saturation does not dismiss worsening dyspnea.</div>\n<div><b>Risk factors for severe disease</b> — age 65+, cancer, cerebrovascular disease, chronic kidney, lung or liver disease, cystic fibrosis, diabetes type 1 and 2, heart conditions, disabilities, HIV, dementia, mental health disorders, obesity and overweight, current or former smoking, tuberculosis, and immunosuppressive drugs.</div>\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — 1. potential to transmit infection; 2. potential for reduced oxygenation; 3. potential for progression to severe or critical disease.</div>\n<div><b>Isolation and quarantine</b> — <b>exposure</b> means less than 6 feet from a suspected or confirmed case for a cumulative <b>15 minutes or more in 24 hours</b>. Not up to date on vaccination: quarantine <b>5 days</b>, mask, no travel, test at day 5 even without symptoms. Up to date: <b>no quarantine</b>, still test at day 5. <b>Isolation</b> if symptomatic or positive regardless of vaccination: stay home and isolate within the home <b>5 days</b>, mask, no travel — ending after <b>5 full days if fever-free for 24 hours without antipyretics</b>, or 5 days after a positive test if never symptomatic, or <b>at least 10 days</b> if very sick or immunocompromised.</div>\n<div><b>Protecting everyone else</b> — a non-intubated inpatient wears a mask, is in a <b>single room with a private bathroom and the door closed</b>, and moves to an <b>airborne infection isolation room</b> for aerosol-generating procedures. Staff wear an <b>N95 or higher respirator, eye protection (goggles or face shield — eyeglasses are insufficient), gown and non-sterile gloves</b>. Give inhaled drugs by <b>MDI with a spacer rather than nebuliser</b> to avoid aerosolisation. <b>Donning</b>: hand hygiene, gown, respirator snug below the chin, face shield, gloves over the gown cuff. <b>Doffing</b>: gloves, then gown (inside only, rolled away from the body), exit the room, then goggles or shield by the headband, then the respirator — <b>only outside the room</b> — then wash hands immediately. At home: separate room and bathroom, 6 feet apart if that is impossible, well-fitting masks on patient and household, limited and preferably vaccinated caregivers, no shared dishes or bedding, and disinfection of frequently touched surfaces.</div>\n<div><b>Care</b> — acetaminophen preferred for fever, myalgia and headache, though NSAIDs may be used. Encourage rest but also mobility: reposition frequently, walk as tolerated, and <b>self-prone</b> (lying on the stomach), which improves respiratory symptoms. Cough and deep breathe at least every 2 hours; dextromethorphan or prescription benzonatate for a sleep-disrupting cough; fluids to prevent dehydration and thin secretions. A patient needing oxygen is usually hospitalised.</div>\n<div><b>Progression and specific therapy</b> — mild dyspnea is common, but <b>dyspnea at rest or increasing dyspnea signals progression</b>, and worsening can reach ARDS <b>within 2 days</b>. No COVID-specific therapy for asymptomatic patients without risk factors; for symptomatic or at-risk patients the preferred drug is oral <b>nirmatrelvir-ritonavir</b>, with <b>remdesivir</b> (3 days of IV infusion) and the monoclonal <b>bebtelovimab</b> as alternatives, and high-titer convalescent plasma if those are unavailable. Teach patients with asthma or COPD <b>not</b> to attribute a breathing change to their underlying disease, and to report chest pain, edema, hemoptysis or orthopnea — possible pulmonary embolism, heart failure or pericarditis.</div>\n<div><b>Duration and after</b> — acute illness ranges up to <b>4 weeks</b>; <b>post-COVID condition</b> is symptoms during or after acute illness lasting <b>2 months or more</b> — fatigue, dyspnea, cough, altered taste and smell, \"brain fog\" with attention and memory problems, anxiety, depression and some functional disability, sometimes for over a year, and more common after hospitalisation or severe illness. Outcomes: no transmission to others, <b>oxygen saturation 95% or greater</b>, no progression to severe disease or post-COVID condition, and return to pre-COVID health status.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The doffing order is not arbitrary and it is exactly what gets tested: you remove the <b>most contaminated items first</b> (gloves, then gown) while still in the room, and the <b>respirator last and outside</b> the room, because it is the one thing protecting your airway while the air is still contaminated. Same logic explains the MDI-with-spacer rule — a nebuliser aerosolises the patient's virus into the room you are standing in.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"25-tuberculosis","ch":"25","chTitle":"Infectious respiratory conditions","title":"Tuberculosis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--cal-red);border-top:5px solid var(--cal-red);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--cal-red-2)\">Tuberculosis</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The organism and the route</b> — a highly communicable disease caused by <b>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</b>, transmitted by <b>aerosolisation — the airborne route</b>. Coughing, laughing, sneezing, whistling or singing puts infected droplets into the air to be inhaled by others. Before COVID-19 it was the leading cause of death from a single infectious agent; about <b>95% of cases occur in resource-limited countries</b>, highest in Africa, India and southeast Asia.</div>\n<div><b>What happens in the lung</b> — the bacillus multiplies freely on reaching bronchi or alveoli, producing inflammation and an exudative response — <b>pneumonitis</b>. Acquired immunity then controls further growth in most people, and lesions resolve leaving little or no residual bacilli. <b>Cell-mediated immunity develops 2–10 weeks after infection</b> and shows up as a positive tuberculin test. The primary infection is often too small to appear on x-ray, and initial infection is seen more often in the <b>upper lobes</b>, with local nodes infected and enlarged.</div>\n<div><b>Latent, active, secondary</b> — an asymptomatic period follows primary infection and can last <b>years or decades</b>: this is <b>latent TB</b>, and <b>the person is not contagious until symptoms of disease occur</b>. Only a small percentage of infected adults ever develop active TB. <b>Secondary TB</b> is reactivation in a previously infected person, more likely when immunity falls — older adults, chronic disease, HIV.</div>\n<div><b>Presentation</b> — early detection depends on <b>what the patient reports</b>, because onset is slow and patients are often unaware until the disease is advanced: progressive fatigue, lethargy, nausea, anorexia, <b>weight loss</b>, low-grade fever, <b>night sweats</b>, and a cough with mucopurulent sputum often streaked with blood, plus chest tightness and dull aching chest pain. Symptoms may have been present for weeks or months. On examination: dullness to percussion over involved fields, bronchial breath sounds, crackles, increased transmission of spoken or whispered sounds, and localised wheezing where a bronchus is partly obstructed or compressed by nodes.</div>\n<div><b>Skin testing</b> — the <b>Mantoux test (TST)</b>: purified protein derivative placed <b>intradermally</b> in the forearm, read at <b>48–72 hours</b>. Measure <b>induration — hardness — not redness</b>: <b>10 mm or more</b> indicates exposure and possible infection, and <b>5 mm</b> is positive in adults with reduced immunity. A positive test means exposure or dormant disease, <b>not active disease</b>. Ask about <b>BCG vaccination</b>, which produces a positive skin test for up to 10 years. A reduced or negative reaction does not rule out TB in the very old or severely immunocompromised — failure to react despite infection is <b>anergy</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Blood, sputum, x-ray</b> — <b>IGRAs</b> (QuantiFERON-TB Gold Plus, T-SPOT) show how the immune system responds to the bacterium; a positive result means infection but <b>does not distinguish latent from active</b>. <b>Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra</b> detects drug-resistant strains. <b>Sputum culture confirms the diagnosis</b> and measures treatment effect — enhanced cultures take up to <b>4 weeks</b>, samples are repeated about every 4 weeks on therapy, and cultures are usually negative after <b>3 months</b> of effective treatment. Chest x-ray is routine but <b>cannot distinguish active from inactive disease</b> on its own. Screen annually in homeless shelters and correctional facilities, and regularly in HIV, communal living and active illicit drug use.</div>\n<div><b>Drug therapy</b> — <b>combination therapy</b> is the most effective way to treat active TB and prevent transmission, killing organisms fast and limiting resistance. Traditional regimens run <b>6–9 months</b>: <b>isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol</b>. A shortened <b>4-month</b> regimen uses isoniazid, rifapentine, moxifloxacin and pyrazinamide. Nausea is common — take once-daily drugs at night, use antiemetics, and take pills with a small simple-carbohydrate snack if food does not interfere with absorption.</div>\n<div><b>The four drugs, one teaching point each</b> — <b>isoniazid</b>: take on an <b>empty stomach</b>, 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals, and take a daily <b>B-complex</b> multivitamin because the drug depletes it. <b>Rifampin</b>: expect <b>reddish-orange staining</b> of skin, urine and all secretions, with permanent staining of soft contact lenses, and it <b>reduces the effectiveness of oral contraceptives</b> — use an additional method during therapy and for 1 month after; it interacts with many drugs. <b>Pyrazinamide</b>: raises <b>uric acid</b> so it worsens gout — at least 8 ounces of water with the dose and increased fluids — and causes <b>photosensitivity</b>, so protective clothing, a hat and sunscreen. <b>Ethambutol</b>: report <b>any vision change</b> — reduced colour vision, blurring, reduced fields — immediately, because it causes <b>optic neuritis</b> and can lead to blindness; it also raises uric acid, and alcohol with it induces severe nausea and vomiting. <b>All four can damage the liver: no alcohol for the entire duration of therapy</b>, and report dark urine, yellowing of skin or sclera, or easy bruising and bleeding.</div>\n<div><b>Resistance</b> — <b>MDR TB</b> resists isoniazid and rifampin; <b>XDR TB</b> resists the first-line drugs plus second-line antibiotics including the fluoroquinolones and at least one aminoglycoside. The most common cause is <b>misuse or mismanagement of drug therapy</b>, and a patient infected by someone with a resistant strain has a resistant strain from the start; patients with AIDS are more likely to develop MDR TB. Treatment needs higher doses for longer. <b>Bedaquiline</b> targets MDR TB but can <b>prolong the QT interval, cause ventricular dysrhythmias and lead to sudden death</b> — regular ECGs and electrolytes, and it is not used when other drugs will work. Teach patients with XDR TB that <b>absolute adherence is critical for survival</b>, and they should receive <b>directly observed therapy</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Infection control</b> — the hospitalised patient with active TB is on <b>Airborne Precautions</b> in a well-ventilated room with <b>at least six air exchanges per minute</b>, and every health care worker uses a <b>personal respirator</b>; precautions stop when the patient is no longer contagious. <b>At home, Airborne Precautions are unnecessary because the family has already been exposed</b> — but every household member must be tested. Teach covering the mouth and nose with a tissue, bagging used tissues, masking in crowds, and distancing until the drugs suppress the infection. <b>Three consecutive negative sputum cultures means no longer infectious</b> and former activities can resume. <b>Directly observed therapy</b> — in person or by video (VDOT) — produces more treatment successes, fewer relapses and less resistance.</div>\n<div><b>The person</b> — TB is a frightening diagnosis, and patients may feel isolated and shunned. Explain the disease, the hygiene, and why close contacts should be tested; explain DOT as adherence support rather than surveillance. Involve the <b>dietitian, pharmacist, social worker, patient navigator and community health worker</b>, and delay discharge if the living situation is high risk or non-adherence is likely. Offer a positive outlook to the adherent patient, while being clear that not taking the drugs as prescribed can produce a drug-resistant infection. Screening participation rises when programmes are <b>culturally sensitive and non-threatening</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>TB is the chapter's clearest demonstration that <b>adherence is a clinical intervention, not a personality trait</b>. Four drugs for six to nine months, with nausea, orange secretions, sun sensitivity and vision checks, in a patient who feels better long before the course ends — and the consequence of stopping early is not relapse alone but a <b>resistant organism</b> that then spreads as a resistant organism. That is why directly observed therapy exists, why the dietitian and social worker are on the team, and why a nurse's teaching time here is measured against a public health outcome.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"25-seasonal-influenza-and-pandemic-infectio","ch":"25","chTitle":"Infectious respiratory conditions","title":"Seasonal influenza and pandemic infection","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Seasonal influenza and pandemic infection</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Seasonal influenza</b> — a highly contagious acute viral respiratory infection at any age, from strains of virus families <b>A, B and C</b>. Epidemics cause pneumonia and death especially in older adults, those with heart failure or chronic lung disease, and the immunocompromised. Most patients are treated at home.</div>\n<div><b>The picture and the window</b> — rapid onset of severe headache, muscle aches, fever, chills, fatigue and weakness, with sore throat, cough and watery nasal discharge; <b>strain B can add nausea, vomiting and diarrhea</b>. Adults are <b>contagious 24 hours before symptoms and up to 5 days after they begin</b>, and many feel fatigued for <b>1–2 weeks</b> after the acute episode resolves.</div>\n<div><b>Vaccination</b> — annual, because the vaccine is rebuilt each year for the strains expected that season (trivalent or quadrivalent), usually IM with an intranasal alternative and a <b>higher-dose quadrivalent for adults over 65</b>. The CDC recommends no one product over another — the point is that everyone 6 months and older is vaccinated yearly. Nurses are patients' most important information source on vaccination, and being vaccinated protects patients from the nurse as well as the nurse from infection.</div>\n<div><b>Containment teaching</b> — thorough handwashing especially after nose blowing, sneezing, coughing, rubbing the eyes or touching the face; stay home from work, school and crowded places; cover mouth and nose with a tissue and dispose of it immediately; distance from others. Because most people cannot wash immediately, the CDC technique is to <b>sneeze or cough into the upper sleeve rather than the hand</b> — droplets on hands contaminate surfaces.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnosis and drugs</b> — in a community with known cases, diagnosis is usually clinical. The <b>rapid influenza diagnostic test has a high false-negative rate — treat if influenza is suspected even when it is negative</b>; the preferred test is <b>RT-PCR</b> (1–8 hours), which distinguishes A from B. Viral infection <b>does not respond to antibiotics</b>. Neuraminidase inhibitors — <b>oseltamivir, zanamivir, peramivir, baloxavir</b> — block the enzyme the virus uses to penetrate respiratory cells and may shorten illness, but must be started <b>within 24–48 hours of symptom onset</b>; they reduce hospitalisation in patients at increased risk. Otherwise: rest several days, increase fluids, saline gargles for throat pain, antihistamines for rhinorrhea.</div>\n<div><b>Pandemic respiratory infection</b> — an infection with an organism to which most humans have <b>no immunity</b> and which can spread globally, usually a bird or animal virus that mutated into human transmissibility. Prevention is everyone's responsibility: global, national and state surveillance, <b>early case recognition</b>, community and personal quarantine, social distancing, stockpiled vaccine released to the affected area, stopping non-essential public activity — gatherings, schools, services, shopping, much employment — halting travel, and households living on <b>at least 2 weeks of stockpiled food, water and medication per person</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Care priorities</b> — supportive measures plus preventing spread. Ask <b>every</b> patient about travel in the last <b>14 days</b> to an outbreak area; if yes, mask the patient and isolate until assessment is complete. Until the organism and its transmission routes are known, use <b>Airborne, Droplet and Contact Precautions together</b>. Stable patients recover at home with rest, fluids and acetaminophen for fever or myalgia; an antiviral such as oseltamivir may be prescribed within 48 hours. Oxygen when hypoxia, breathlessness or a <b>change in cognition</b> occurs; bronchodilation and secretion clearance; intubation and mechanical ventilation if hypoxemia does not respond; antibiotics only for a <b>bacterial pneumonia complicating</b> the influenza.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The 24-to-48-hour antiviral window is the reason a \"flu\" phone call is a triage decision rather than reassurance. By day 5 of symptoms, oseltamivir has little left to offer and the honest answer is that the window has closed — which is also the strongest argument the nurse has for next year's vaccine. And the contagious period starting <b>a day before symptoms</b> is why containment teaching cannot wait for a diagnosis.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"25-rhinosinusitis-peritonsillar-abscess-ant","ch":"25","chTitle":"Infectious respiratory conditions","title":"Rhinosinusitis, peritonsillar abscess, anthrax and endemic infection","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Rhinosinusitis, peritonsillar abscess, anthrax and endemic infection</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Rhinosinusitis</b> — inflammation of the mucous membranes of one or more sinuses, usually with rhinitis and most often the common cold. Anything that blocks sinus drainage can cause it: deviated septum, polyps or tumors, inhaled pollutants or <b>cocaine</b>, allergies, facial trauma, dental infection. Even a non-infectious start — seasonal allergy — blocks secretion flow, and the trapped secretions then become infected. Most episodes are <b>viral</b> and develop in the <b>maxillary and frontal</b> sinuses. Complications: <b>cellulitis, abscess and meningitis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Recognising and treating it</b> — pain over the cheek radiating to the teeth, tenderness to percussion over the sinuses, referred pain to the temple or back of the head, and facial pain <b>worse on bending forward</b>. Bacterial infection adds purulent drainage with postnasal drip, sore throat, fever, swelling, fatigue, dental pain and ear pressure. Manage as an outpatient: decongestants, antihistamines and intranasal steroid spray, antipyretics and analgesics; broad-spectrum antibiotics for bacterial infection, sometimes nasal or systemic steroids. Supportive care — humidification, nasal irrigation, moist heat over the sinuses, rest, more fluid, sleeping with the head elevated and avoiding cigarette smoke. <b>Complete the whole antibiotic course even when symptoms subside</b>, to eradicate the organism and prevent resistance.</div>\n<div><b>First-generation antihistamines in older adults</b> — often inappropriate: reduced drug clearance, higher risk of confusion, and anticholinergic effects such as dry mouth and constipation. Avoid <b>chlorpheniramine, diphenhydramine and hydroxyzine</b>, and teach older adults not to self-medicate with them.</div>\n<div><b>Peritonsillar abscess</b> — a rare complication of acute bacterial tonsillitis: infection spreads from the tonsil into surrounding tissue and the abscess can grow <b>large enough to obstruct the airway</b>. Pus behind the tonsil swells one side of the throat and <b>pushes the uvula toward the unaffected side</b>; severe throat pain radiates to the ear or teeth, with a <b>muffled voice</b>, fever, difficulty swallowing, <b>trismus</b> (spasm and pain of the chewing muscles), difficulty breathing, bad breath and swollen nodes on the affected side. Most are treated as outpatients with antibiotics, sometimes steroids for swelling, and drainage if needed — and the teaching is to finish the antibiotics and <b>come to the emergency department immediately if drooling or stridor appear</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Inhalation anthrax</b> — a bacterial infection with the gram-positive <b>Bacillus anthracis</b>, which lives as a spore in soil where grazing animals live. Most natural cases are cutaneous; inhaled, the disease is <b>nearly 100% fatal without treatment</b>. It is an occupational hazard for veterinarians, farmers and others handling animal wool, hides, bone meal and skin — <b>so a case in someone without occupational risk may be bioterrorism and is reported to hospital authorities immediately</b>. Inhaled spores enter white cells, leave their capsules, replicate and release toxins; massive edema, hemorrhage and lung cell destruction follow, and infected white cells spread the organism to nodes and blood, producing bacteremia, sepsis and meningitis. <b>The lethal toxins are the most common cause of death.</b></div>\n<div><b>Anthrax's two stages</b> — symptoms may take up to <b>8 weeks</b> to appear. <b>Prodromal</b>: low-grade fever, fatigue, mild chest pain, a dry harsh cough, and — distinctively — <b>no upper respiratory symptoms</b>, no sore throat or rhinitis; mediastinal widening may show on x-ray. The patient usually <b>starts to feel better in 2–4 days</b>, and antibiotics started here mean a high likelihood of survival. <b>Fulminant</b>: a sudden severe onset after that apparent improvement — respiratory distress, hematemesis, dyspnea, diaphoresis, stridor, chest pain, cyanosis, high fever, hemorrhagic mediastinitis, pleural effusion, hypotension and septic shock, with hemorrhagic meningitis as it spreads. <b>Death often occurs within 24–36 hours even with antibiotics started at this stage.</b> Treatment combines <b>ciprofloxacin, doxycycline, levofloxacin, moxifloxacin and clindamycin</b>, with the antitoxins <b>obiltoxaximab and raxibacumab</b> added once symptoms and diagnosis are established.</div>\n<div><b>Endemic and geographic infection</b> — organisms much more common in a particular geographic area, though the actual incidence stays low because residents develop some immunity; most are <b>spore-forming fungi</b>. <b>Hantavirus pulmonary syndrome</b> from rodent urine, droppings and saliva in the southwest US, Mexico and Central America — supportive care and oxygen, ventilation in severe cases, <b>38% fatal</b>. <b>Aspergillosis</b> from indoor and outdoor mold in damp areas. <b>Blastomycosis</b> from moist soil and decomposing wood and leaves around the Great Lakes and the Ohio and Mississippi River valleys. <b>Coccidioidomycosis</b> from soil in the southwest and far western US, Mexico, Central and South America. <b>Cryptococcosis</b> from trees and the soil beneath them on the US Pacific coast and southern British Columbia. <b>Histoplasmosis</b> from soil with large amounts of bird and bat droppings in the central and eastern US.</div>\n<div><b>Why they are missed, and how they are treated</b> — all of them <b>resemble influenza or pneumonia</b> — fever, cough, headache, muscle aches, chest pain, night sweats — and are often misdiagnosed; none are contagious person to person. Heavy exposure comes from digging, farming, construction that disturbs soil, dust storms, tornadoes and flooding, and demolition or reconstruction that releases spores trapped in walls. <b>Always ask a patient with respiratory infection symptoms whether they have visited an endemic region.</b> Older adults, pregnant patients and the immunocompromised get more severe disease, and chronic fungal infection is possible. Moderate disease takes <b>oral antifungals for weeks to months</b>; severe disease starts with <b>IV antifungals including amphotericin</b> and then long-term oral therapy, with influenza- and pneumonia-style supportive care throughout.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Two lessons hide in this card. First, the <b>anthrax teaching point generalises</b>: teach any patient with a lower respiratory infection to watch for a setback <i>after</i> they think they are getting well, and to seek care immediately if breathlessness starts — improvement followed by collapse is the pattern that kills. Second, geography is diagnostic data. The same fever and cough means something different in the Ohio River valley, the Arizona desert and a building under demolition, so the travel and exposure question is not small talk.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"25-adpie","ch":"25","chTitle":"Infectious respiratory conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\">Every plan here carries two aims that never separate: <b>restore gas exchange in this patient</b>, and <b>stop the organism reaching anyone else</b> — including you, the family, and the next patient down the hall.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Exposure history — ask first</div><b>Travel in the last 14 days</b> to an outbreak area; travel to or from countries with high TB incidence and country of origin; previous TB test results and <b>BCG</b> vaccination; household illness and any hospital or long-term care contact in <b>30 days</b>; visits to <b>endemic regions</b>; occupational exposure to animal wool, hides, bone meal or skin; vaccination status for influenza, pneumococcus and COVID-19. In pneumonia also document age, living, work and school environment, diet, exercise and sleep, <b>swallowing problems</b>, a nasogastric tube, tobacco and alcohol, illicit drug use, and home respiratory equipment with its cleaning routine.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0;opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Gas exchange and effort</div>General appearance — flushed cheeks, anxious expression, the <b>tripod position</b>, broken sentences from muscle fatigue and obstruction. Rate, pattern and accessory muscle use; cough with the <b>amount, colour, consistency and odour</b> of sputum; <b>crackles, diminished sounds, wheeze, bronchial sounds over consolidation, increased fremitus, dull percussion</b>, unequal expansion. Saturation <b>with every set of vital signs</b> — and in COVID-19, <b>94% or less on room air means in-person evaluation</b>, while a normal number never dismisses worsening dyspnea.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">The atypical presentations</div><b>Older adult pneumonia</b>: weakness, fatigue and falls, lethargy, <b>acute confusion</b> as the most common symptom, poor appetite — often with no fever, no cough and a normal WBC, but with hypoxemia. <b>Sepsis</b>: a rapid weak pulse, hypotension with orthostatic change, dysrhythmias, and a screening tool used deliberately. <b>TB</b>: weeks to months of fatigue, anorexia, weight loss, low-grade fever, night sweats and blood-streaked sputum. <b>Anthrax</b>: no upper respiratory symptoms, and improvement for 2–4 days before collapse. Aspiration is often <b>silent</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0;opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Diagnostics and psychosocial</div>Sputum Gram stain, culture and sensitivity (trap during suctioning if too ill); CBC, blood cultures, ABGs, electrolytes, <b>BUN and sodium for dehydration</b>, <b>lactate for sepsis</b>; chest x-ray, <b>knowing it may lag symptoms by 2 or more days</b>; <b>RT-PCR</b> for influenza and COVID-19, antigen tests, <b>serology only for past infection</b>; <b>TST induration at 48–72 hours</b>, IGRA, sputum culture. Psychosocially: pain, fatigue and dyspnea drive anxiety — read facial and shoulder tension, keep the interview short in severe dyspnea, and remember that <b>PPE makes you hard to hear</b>, deepening isolation and fear. TB carries stigma and fear of being shunned.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The exposure history is placed first on purpose: it is the only assessment that protects other people. A travel question answered at the door changes where the patient sits, what mask they wear, and which precautions the unit uses — and none of that can be retrofitted after the patient has been through a waiting room.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\">\n<div><b>Pneumonia.</b> 1. <b>Decreased gas exchange</b> due to decreased diffusion at the alveolar-capillary membrane. 2. <b>Potential for airway obstruction</b> due to inflammation with excessive secretions, fatigue and muscle weakness. 3. <b>Potential for sepsis</b> due to organisms in a very vascular area and reduced immunity.</div>\n<div><b>COVID-19.</b> 1. <b>Potential to transmit infection</b>, because the disease is highly communicable. 2. <b>Potential for reduced oxygenation</b>. 3. <b>Potential for progression</b> to severe or critical infection.</div>\n<div><b>Tuberculosis.</b> Potential to transmit infection by the airborne route; decreased gas exchange; inadequate nutrition and weight loss; and <b>potential for non-adherence</b> to a six-to-nine-month regimen — which is also a potential for drug-resistant disease in the patient and the community.</div>\n<div><b>Notice the ordering difference.</b> In pneumonia the patient's diffusion comes first; in COVID-19 and TB <b>transmission comes first</b>, because the organism's reach is the larger harm and the window to contain it is now.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>\"Potential for sepsis\" sits third in pneumonia but it is the problem that kills: when sepsis occurs with pneumonia the risk of death is high. It ranks below the airway because gas exchange and secretions are the problems you act on <i>continuously</i>, while sepsis is the one you <b>screen for</b> relentlessly — a sepsis tool plus a saturation with every set of vital signs. Ranking it third does not mean watching it less.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\">\n<div>Gas exchange and oxygenation are adequate — <b>SaO₂ at least 95% or the patient's normal level</b> in pneumonia; <b>95% or greater</b> in COVID-19.</div>\n<div>The airway stays patent, evidenced by the <b>absence of crackles and wheezes</b> on auscultation.</div>\n<div>The patient is free of the invading organism — no fever, a normal WBC — and <b>returns to the pre-illness health status</b>.</div>\n<div>The patient <b>does not transmit the infection to others</b>: isolation or precautions followed, household members tested in TB, contacts encouraged to test.</div>\n<div>The patient does <b>not progress</b> — no severe or critical COVID-19, no post-COVID condition, no sepsis, no empyema, no ARDS.</div>\n<div>Before discharge the patient states the <b>whole drug course</b> and why it is finished even when feeling well, the warning signs that mean return, and the vaccinations due once recovered.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The outcome \"does not transmit\" is the one that makes this chapter different from every other respiratory chapter. It is measured in other people's charts, not this one — contacts tested, household screened, staff uninfected — and it is achieved almost entirely by nursing actions: precautions, technique, teaching and timing.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0;opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Independent</div><b>Cough and deep breathe at least every 2 hours</b>; <b>incentive spirometry 5–10 breaths hourly while awake</b>, holding 2–4 seconds, recording volume; upright positioning; <b>at least 2 L of fluid daily</b> (3 L in prevention teaching) with intake, output, mucous membranes and turgor monitored; <b>vigorous oral care</b> and progressive ambulation to prevent HAP; <b>head of bed at least 30 degrees</b> and aspiration screening; check skin under oxygen devices and elastic bands; self-proning and repositioning in COVID-19; sepsis screening tool plus <b>saturation with every set of vital signs</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Dependent</div>Oxygen by cannula or mask; <b>antiinfectives for every pneumonia except viral</b> — 5–7 days uncomplicated CAP, up to 21 days for HAP or reduced immunity, <b>first ED dose within 4 hours</b>; bronchodilators for bronchospasm, inhaled or IV steroids for swelling, guaifenesin; <b>neuraminidase inhibitors within 24–48 hours</b> of influenza onset; <b>nirmatrelvir-ritonavir</b>, remdesivir or bebtelovimab for at-risk COVID-19; <b>four-drug TB therapy for 6–9 months</b> or the 4-month regimen, bedaquiline with ECG monitoring for MDR TB; antifungals, oral for weeks to months or IV amphotericin in severe endemic disease; antitoxin plus combination antibiotics in anthrax.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0;opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Infection control</div><b>TB</b>: Airborne Precautions, a well-ventilated room with <b>at least six air exchanges per minute</b>, a personal respirator for every worker; at home, precautions are unnecessary but <b>the whole household is tested</b>; three consecutive negative cultures ends infectiousness. <b>COVID-19</b>: single room with private bathroom, door closed, mask on a non-intubated patient, <b>AIIR for aerosol-generating procedures</b>, <b>N95 or higher plus eye protection, gown and gloves</b>, MDI with spacer instead of nebuliser, and the <b>doffing order — gloves, gown, exit, eye protection, respirator outside the room, then hand hygiene</b>. <b>Unknown pandemic organism</b>: Airborne, Droplet <i>and</i> Contact Precautions until transmission is known. Cough into the upper sleeve, bag used tissues, mask in crowds.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0;opacity:1;filter:none;transition:opacity .2s\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Teaching and collaborative</div>Finish the <b>entire</b> antibiotic or antituberculosis course; drug-specific teaching — isoniazid on an empty stomach with B-complex, rifampin's orange secretions and contraceptive failure, pyrazinamide's uric acid and photosensitivity, ethambutol's vision changes, <b>no alcohol with any of them</b>; <b>directly observed therapy or VDOT</b>; vaccination after recovery; isolation and quarantine timing; the setback-after-improvement warning. Team: <b>dietitian, pharmacist, social worker, patient navigator, community health worker</b>, respiratory therapy, case management for a high-risk living situation, and public health for contact testing.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Almost every prevention action in this chapter is unglamorous and nurse-owned: oral care, ambulation, head of bed at 30 degrees, hand hygiene, aspiration screening, spirometry coaching. VAP and HAP are not treated so much as <b>prevented by the bundle</b>, and each element is something a nurse either does or does not do that shift. That is why \"hand hygiene is critical\" appears against every single type of pneumonia in the table.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\">\n<div>Gas exchange is adequate with <b>SaO₂ at least 95% or the patient's usual level</b>; breath sounds clear of crackles and wheezes; the patient is free of infection with no fever and a normal WBC; no one else has been infected; and the patient returns to the pre-illness health status. In TB, <b>three consecutive negative sputum cultures</b> — cultures are usually negative after 3 months of effective therapy — and every household contact tested.</div>\n<div><b>Unmet looks like:</b> new or increasing confusion in an older adult; a rapid weak pulse, hypotension or a rising lactate — sepsis; <b>dyspnea at rest or worsening dyspnea in COVID-19</b>, which can reach ARDS within 2 days; chest pain, edema, hemoptysis or orthopnea suggesting embolism, heart failure or pericarditis; a <b>setback after apparent improvement</b>; persistent fever, cough, sputum or fatigue that does not resolve; <b>drooling or stridor</b> in peritonsillar abscess; vision or liver warning signs on TB therapy; and readmission within 30 days, which is common after pneumonia in older adults.</div>\n<div><b>Set expectations so recovery is not read as failure:</b> fatigue, weakness and a residual cough last <b>weeks</b> after pneumonia, influenza fatigue lasts <b>1–2 weeks</b>, acute COVID-19 can run <b>up to 4 weeks</b>, and post-COVID condition can last <b>2 months or more</b>. Tell the patient the course, and reassure that complete recovery does occur.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The recovery timeline belongs in evaluation because patients judge themselves against it. A patient three weeks past pneumonia who still cannot climb the stairs concludes something has gone wrong, stops the follow-up, and either despairs or presents to the emergency department. Telling them in advance that weeks of fatigue are <b>expected</b> — while naming exactly which findings are not — is what makes the difference between a worried call and a missed relapse.</div>\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"45-overview","ch":"45","chTitle":"Assessment of the GI system","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">45 · Chapter 45</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Nutrition · elimination · pain</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Assessment of the GI system</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> The GI tract is one <b>25-foot tube</b> from mouth to anus, with the salivary glands, liver, gallbladder and pancreas emptying into it. Its jobs are <b>secretion, digestion, absorption, motility and elimination</b> — so every GI disease shows up as a problem with <b>nutrition, elimination or pain</b>. This chapter is the toolkit for chapters 46–49: what each part does, how to examine the abdomen in the <b>right order</b> (and when <b>not</b> to touch it), which labs point to which organ, and how to get a patient safely <b>through endoscopy and bowel prep</b>.</p><div id=\"ch45-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Food is chewed, swallowed and churned into <b>chyme</b>; acid, bile and enzymes break it down; the small intestine <b>absorbs</b>; the large intestine absorbs water and forms stool.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Structural or mechanical problems, impaired motility, infection, inflammation or autoimmune disease, and cancer anywhere along the tube or its accessory organs.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Appetite and weight change, dysphagia, heartburn, nausea and vomiting, pain (by <b>PQRST</b>), change in bowel pattern, blood or tarry stool, distention, jaundice, itching, bruising.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Inspection, auscultation, light palpation; CBC, liver enzymes, amylase and lipase, bilirubin; stool tests (FIT, gFOBT, stool DNA); x-ray, CT, MRI, ultrasound; EGD, ERCP, capsule, colonoscopy.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Treatment of the cause found; safe preparation and recovery for each test; screening from <b>age 45</b>.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Bleeding, perforation, aspiration after sedation, pancreatitis after ERCP, dehydration and electrolyte loss from bowel prep.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A <b>pulsating abdominal mass</b> (possible aortic aneurysm — don't touch); <b>rigidity</b> (peritoneal inflammation); visible peristalsis (obstruction); severe pain, fever or a falling BP after endoscopy.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Endoscopy under moderate sedation<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>throat numbed — gag reflex gone<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>patient can't protect the airway<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>nothing by mouth until the gag reflex returns<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>then fluids, then home with a driver</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Nurse vs provider exam</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">The nurse <b>inspects, auscultates and palpates lightly</b>; the provider adds <b>deep palpation and percussion</b>. Auscultate <b>before</b> palpating so touching doesn't change bowel sounds.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">FIT vs gFOBT vs stool DNA</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>FIT yearly</b> — no diet or drug limits. <b>gFOBT yearly</b> — no NSAIDs for 7 days, no red meat or vitamin C &gt; 250 mg for 3 days. <b>Stool DNA (Cologuard) every 3 years.</b> Any positive → <b>colonoscopy</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">EGD vs colonoscopy recovery</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">EGD: <b>NPO until the gag reflex returns</b>. Colonoscopy: fluids <b>after flatus is passed</b>; lie on the <b>left side</b>; cramping and fullness for hours are expected.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Connections to other chapters</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Ch 46–47 use EGD; ch 48–49 use colonoscopy and stool tests; ch 56 diabetes patients need instructions while NPO for prep.</div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Vital signs every 15–30 minutes after sedation, respiratory rate and SpO₂, gag reflex, pain, bleeding, passing flatus, intake and output during bowel prep.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Pulsating mass or aortic bruit → don't palpate, notify now. After endoscopy: severe pain, fever, bleeding or BP drop (perforation, hemorrhage). Respiratory rate &lt; 10 under sedation → stimulate (sternal rub).</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The wall</b> — four layers from the inside out: <b>mucosa</b> (thin smooth muscle and gland cells), <b>submucosa</b> (connective tissue), <b>muscularis</b> (circular and longitudinal smooth muscle that keeps contents moving), and <b>serosa</b> (connective tissue outside). The structure is the same the whole length of the tube.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Digestion and absorption</b> — food must become a liquid, <b>chyme</b>, before it can be absorbed. <b>Digestion</b> is mechanical and chemical breakdown: the stomach adds <b>hydrochloric acid</b>, the liver <b>bile</b>, the accessory organs <b>enzymes</b>. <b>Absorption</b> moves the products into the circulation.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Mouth and esophagus</b> — teeth cut, tear, crush and grind (adults have <b>32</b>); <b>saliva</b> softens food and starts digestion with <b>salivary amylase</b> (carbohydrates) and <b>lipase</b> (fats). The esophagus carries food through the diaphragm to the stomach; the <b>upper sphincter</b> stays closed to keep air out, the <b>lower esophageal sphincter (LES)</b> stays closed to keep stomach contents from refluxing — when it fails, <b>GERD</b> develops.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Stomach</b> — midline and <b>left upper quadrant</b>: cardia, fundus, body and <b>antrum (pylorus)</b>, guarded by the LES above and the <b>pyloric sphincter</b> below. <b>Parietal cells</b> make <b>hydrochloric acid</b> and <b>intrinsic factor</b> (needed to absorb <b>vitamin B₁₂</b> — without it, <b>pernicious anemia</b>); <b>chief cells</b> make <b>pepsinogen</b>. It stores, churns and mixes food into chyme. When chyme reaches the duodenum, <b>secretin and cholecystokinin</b> slow acid production and stomach motility.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Pancreas</b> — behind the stomach from the duodenal C-loop to the spleen (head, body, tail). <b>Exocrine</b>: enzymes for carbohydrate, fat and protein — <b>amylase, lipase, proteases</b>. <b>Endocrine</b>: islets — <b>alpha (glucagon), beta (insulin)</b>, delta and PP cells.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Liver and gallbladder</b> — the liver (mostly <b>RUQ</b>) gets about <b>1200 mL of blood a minute</b> from the hepatic artery and portal vein and does 400+ jobs: <b>makes bile</b>; <b>stores</b> iron, copper, vitamins A, D, E and B₁₂; <b>protects</b> (Kupffer cells engulf bacteria and old RBCs; <b>detoxifies drugs and alcohol</b> — so drug toxicity risk rises with aging); <b>metabolizes</b> — turns <b>ammonia into urea</b>, makes <b>albumin, prothrombin and fibrinogen</b>, stores and releases glycogen, handles fats. Bile flows through the common bile duct to the duodenum at the <b>sphincter of Oddi</b>; when that is closed it goes to the <b>gallbladder</b>, which <b>concentrates and stores</b> it and releases it when <b>fat</b> is eaten.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Small intestine</b> — <b>9–16 feet</b>: <b>duodenum</b> (first 8–10 inches; the bile and pancreatic ducts join at the <b>ampulla of Vater</b>), <b>jejunum</b> (~8 ft), <b>ileum</b> (~10 ft), ending at the <b>ileocecal valve</b>. <b>Villi</b> and circular folds make a huge surface — it is the <b>main organ of absorption</b>. Contents take <b>3–6 hours</b> to pass.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Large intestine</b> — <b>5–6 feet</b>: cecum (with the <b>appendix</b>, likely part of gut immunity), ascending, transverse, descending and sigmoid colon, rectum, and the last 1–1½ inches, the <b>anal canal</b> with internal and external sphincters. Slow segmental contractions allow <b>water and electrolyte absorption</b>, turning chyme into <b>feces</b>; distention triggers peristalsis toward the rectum.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Knowing which organ sits where turns vague abdominal pain into a short list. <b>RUQ</b>: liver, gallbladder, duodenum, head of pancreas. <b>LUQ</b>: stomach, spleen, body and tail of pancreas. <b>RLQ</b>: cecum and <b>appendix</b>. <b>LLQ</b>: sigmoid colon. <b>Midline</b>: the <b>aorta</b> — the one structure you must never press if it pulsates.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"45-gi-changes-with-aging","ch":"45","chTitle":"Assessment of the GI system","title":"GI changes with aging","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">GI changes with aging</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Stomach</b> — <b>gastric mucosa atrophies</b> → less hydrochloric acid (<b>hypochlorhydria</b>) → <b>less iron and vitamin B₁₂ absorbed</b> and <b>bacterial overgrowth</b>, which leads to <b>atrophic gastritis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Bowel</b> — <b>peristalsis slows</b> and nerve signals dull → less urge to defecate → postponed bowel movements → <b>constipation and impaction</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Pancreas</b> — ducts dilate and vessels calcify; <b>less lipase</b> → poor fat digestion and absorption → <b>steatorrhea</b> (fatty stools).</div>\n<div><b>Liver</b> — fewer and smaller liver cells, more fibrous tissue → less protein synthesis, lower enzyme activity and cholesterol synthesis → <b>drugs metabolized slowly and can build up to toxic levels</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Gut microbiota</b> — abnormal microbial activity with age contributes to <b>anorexia and undernutrition</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Lab values in older adults (Table 45.2)</b> — <b>ALT, AST, alkaline phosphatase and amylase</b> may run <b>slightly higher</b> than the adult range, and total <b>calcium slightly lower</b>. (ALT also runs slightly higher in men and African American people; AST slightly lower in females.)</div>\n<div><b>Who gets what</b> — older adults have more <b>stomach cancer</b>; younger adults more <b>inflammatory bowel disease</b>; <b>colon cancer is rising in adults under 50</b>. A recent trip abroad or <b>camping near lakes and streams</b> can explain diarrhea.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Aging moves the GI tract in one direction — <b>less</b>: less acid, less motility, less lipase, less liver enzyme. So the expected findings are slower drug clearance, constipation, fatty stools and poor B₁₂ and iron absorption — never more lipase, more frequent stools or better fat absorption. Lab ranges shift a little with age too, so a value just outside the adult range can be an older adult's normal — but a <b>critical</b> value never is.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","ch":"45","chTitle":"Assessment of the GI system","title":"GI history and the abdominal exam","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">History</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Questions to ask (Box 45.1)</b> — daily food; drugs with dose and frequency; <b>vitamins, minerals, herbs</b>; appetite and <b>weight change (intended or not)</b>; special diet; <b>trouble chewing or swallowing</b>, denture fit; heartburn — how often, what triggers and relieves it; past GI disorders and surgery; <b>family history</b>; smoking or vaping, chewing tobacco; <b>alcohol</b>; pain, diarrhea, constipation or gas and the foods involved; <b>travel</b>; usual bowel pattern; <b>laxative use</b>; pain or <b>bleeding with bowel movements</b>; change in stool; past endoscopy or colonoscopy.</div>\n<div><b>Nutrition history</b> — many conditions start with intake or absorption. Ask about allergies, usual foods and meal times, <b>lactose intolerance</b> (about <b>50% of US adults</b> acquire it; more common in African American, Asian, Hispanic, Native American and Jewish people), <b>anorexia</b>, taste change (<b>COVID-19</b> can remove taste), <b>dysphagia</b>, pain with eating, nausea, vomiting, <b>dyspepsia</b> (heartburn), <b>unintentional weight loss</b> (some GI cancers), <b>alcohol and caffeine</b> (gastritis, ulcers).</div>\n<div><b>Money, culture and genes</b> — limited budgets and <b>food deserts</b> limit healthy food; people may substitute cheaper <b>OTC drugs or herbs</b> and delay care until disease is advanced. Ask about <b>spices and hot peppers</b> (indigestion) and <b>fasting</b> practices. Family history matters: <b>familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)</b> is autosomal dominant and predisposes to colon cancer.</div>\n<div><b>The current problem</b> — GI symptoms are vague, so build a <b>timeline</b> (a symptom diary helps). Bowel changes: pattern, <b>color and consistency</b>, diarrhea or constipation and what was tried, <b>frank blood or tarry stools</b>, distention, gas, unintended weight change. Weight: usual, change and when, appetite. Skin: rashes, <b>itching, jaundice, bruising or bleeding</b> (liver and biliary disease).</div>\n<div><b>Pain — PQRST</b> — <b>P</b>recipitating and palliative factors (what started it, what helps or worsens it); <b>Q</b>uality (sharp, dull, burning, crushing, twisting…); <b>R</b>egion and radiation (where, does it spread or move); <b>S</b>everity 0–10 and its effect on activity; <b>T</b>iming — onset, duration, frequency, sudden or gradual, night waking, relation to <b>meals</b>, seasons.</div>\n<div><b>Psychosocial</b> — elimination problems are <b>personal and embarrassing</b> — ask privately and matter-of-factly about how daily life is affected. <b>Stress</b> can trigger or worsen <b>IBS</b> and other GI disorders. A cancer diagnosis brings <b>grief</b> (denial, anger, depression) — refer for support when needed.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Examining the abdomen</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Set-up and order</b> — have the patient <b>empty the bladder</b>, lie <b>supine with knees bent</b> and arms at the sides (relaxes the abdominal muscles). Order: <b>inspect → auscultate → palpate</b> (and percuss) — so touching doesn't change bowel sounds. The generalist nurse does <b>inspection, auscultation and light palpation</b>; the provider does <b>deep palpation and percussion</b>. <b>No palpation if appendicitis or an aortic aneurysm is suspected.</b></div>\n<div><b>Inspect</b> — start at the <b>RUQ, then LUQ, LLQ, RLQ</b>; examine <b>painful areas last</b>. Note asymmetry, discoloration, scars, <b>distention</b>, bulging flanks, <b>taut glistening skin</b>, skin folds, fat, pressure injuries; contour (flat, rounded, concave, distended — look across at eye level); the umbilicus; movement with breathing. A pulsation over the aorta is occasionally visible; a <b>bulging</b> pulsating <b>mass</b> is the alarm (below).</div>\n<div><b>Organs by quadrant (Table 45.1)</b> — <b>RUQ</b>: most of the <b>liver</b>, <b>gallbladder</b>, <b>duodenum</b>, <b>head of the pancreas</b>, hepatic flexure, part of the ascending and transverse colon. <b>LUQ</b>: left lobe of the liver, <b>stomach</b>, <b>spleen</b>, <b>body and tail of the pancreas</b>, splenic flexure, part of the transverse and descending colon. <b>RLQ</b>: <b>cecum</b>, <b>appendix</b>, right ureter, right ovary and fallopian tube, right spermatic cord. <b>LLQ</b>: part of the descending colon, <b>sigmoid colon</b>, left ureter, left ovary and fallopian tube, left spermatic cord. <b>Midline</b>: <b>abdominal aorta</b>, <b>uterus</b> (if enlarged), <b>bladder</b> (if distended). Asymmetry or a finding in one area points to the structures beneath it.</div>\n<div><b>Two action alerts</b> — <b>visible peristaltic waves</b> are rare — note where they start and which way they go and <b>report them</b> (possible <b>intestinal obstruction</b>). A <b>bulging, pulsating mass</b>: <b>don't touch it</b> with hands or stethoscope — possible <b>abdominal aortic aneurysm</b> — notify the provider <b>immediately</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Auscultate</b> — with the <b>diaphragm</b> (bowel sounds are high pitched), lightly, <b>starting in the RLQ</b> (ileocecal valve), then the other quadrants. Normal: high-pitched irregular gurgles <b>5–30 per minute</b>. <b>Hypoactive or absent</b> after abdominal surgery, with <b>peritonitis</b> or <b>paralytic ileus</b>. <b>Hyperactive, loud gurgling (borborygmus)</b> with diarrhea, gastroenteritis, or <b>above a complete obstruction</b>. Listen for <b>bruits</b> over the aorta, renal and iliac arteries — an <b>aortic bruit suggests an aneurysm: don't percuss or palpate</b>, notify immediately.</div>\n<div><b>Is peristalsis back after surgery?</b> — the most reliable sign is not bowel sounds but asking: <b>flatus within the past 8 hours</b> or <b>a stool within 12–24 hours</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Light palpation</b> — four fingers together, pressing <b>½–1 inch (1.25–2.5 cm)</b> with a rotating motion, quadrant by quadrant, to find large masses and tenderness. Note <b>guarding</b> (voluntary) and <b>rigidity</b> — rigidity is involuntary and a sign of <b>peritoneal inflammation</b>. Tender areas are examined last and cautiously.</div>\n<div><b>Percussion (provider)</b> — <b>tympany</b> — high, loud, musical — over air-filled bowel; <b>dullness</b> — softer, thud-like — over solid organs such as the liver. Used to size the liver (<b>hepatomegaly</b>) and spleen (<b>splenomegaly</b>: dullness in the left anterior axillary line, before it can be felt) and to detect masses, fluid and air.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The exam order protects the findings and the patient. Listening first means the bowel sounds you hear are the patient's, not the ones your hands stirred up; examining painful areas last keeps the abdomen relaxed long enough to feel anything; and refusing to press a pulsating mass or an inflamed appendix avoids rupturing what you were trying to assess.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","ch":"45","chTitle":"Assessment of the GI system","title":"GI labs and stool tests","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Blood and urine (Table 45.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>CBC and clotting</b> — a <b>CBC</b> finds anemia and infection — <b>GI bleeding is the most frequent cause of anemia in adults</b> (cancer, peptic ulcer, diverticulitis, IBD). <b>PT</b> depends on the <b>vitamin K–dependent clotting factors</b> the liver makes, so <b>liver disease prolongs PT</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Liver enzymes</b> — <b>ALT 4–36 units/L</b> and <b>AST 0–35 units/L</b> rise in most liver disorders, <b>highest with necrosis</b> such as <b>hepatitis and cirrhosis</b>. ALT: <b>significant</b> rise in hepatitis, <b>moderate</b> in cirrhosis, <b>mild</b> in pancreatitis; runs slightly <b>higher in older adults, men and African American people</b>. AST: also up with liver metastasis and <b>drug-induced liver injury</b>, down with acute kidney disease and chronic dialysis; slightly higher in older adults, lower in females. <b>Alkaline phosphatase 30–120 units/L</b> (slightly higher in older adults) rises with cirrhosis, <b>biliary obstruction</b> (inside or outside the liver) and liver tumor; it is low with hypophosphatemia and undernutrition. <b>Albumin 3.5–5.0 g/dL</b>: low in <b>liver disease and undernutrition</b>, high in dehydration.</div>\n<div><b>Pancreas</b> — <b>amylase</b> (60–120 Somogyi units/dL; 30–220 units/L) and <b>lipase (0–160 units/L)</b> rise in <b>acute pancreatitis</b> (and in chronic pancreatitis) — amylase within <b>24 hours</b>, staying up about <b>5 days</b>. They may be <b>normal when extensive pancreatic necrosis</b> leaves few cells to make enzymes. Amylase also rises with cholecystitis and perforated ulcer or perforated or necrotic bowel, and runs slightly higher in older adults; lipase rises with biliary disease, bowel obstruction and peptic ulcer. <b>Urine amylase</b> stays high <b>5–7 days</b> — useful when symptoms have lasted days.</div>\n<div><b>Bilirubin and ammonia</b> — <b>total bilirubin 0.3–1.0 mg/dL</b> (critical &gt; 12): <b>direct (conjugated) 0.1–0.3</b> rises with <b>biliary obstruction</b>, gallstones, liver metastasis; <b>indirect (unconjugated) 0.2–0.8</b> with <b>hemolysis</b>, hepatitis, cirrhosis. <b>GGT</b> also signals impaired excretion. <b>Urine urobilinogen</b> can show biliary or liver problems <b>before jaundice</b>. <b>Ammonia 10–80 mcg/dL</b> (6–47 μmol/L; the book's table prints mg/dL, a misprint) rises when the liver can't convert it to urea — <b>cirrhosis, hepatitis, hepatic encephalopathy and coma</b>, <b>portal hypertension</b>, and GI bleeding or obstruction with mild liver disease.</div>\n<div><b>Electrolytes and others</b> — <b>potassium 3.5–5.0 mEq/L</b> (critical <b>&lt; 3 or &gt; 6.1</b>) falls with <b>diarrhea, vomiting and gastric suction</b> and with ascites; sodium is lost with vomiting and diarrhea too, so both need replacing. Potassium rises with hemolysis and kidney failure. <b>Calcium 9.0–10.5 mg/dL</b> (slightly lower in older adults; critical <b>&lt; 6 or &gt; 13 mg/dL</b>) is absorbed in the gut, so it is measured to detect <b>malabsorption</b>; it falls with malabsorption, low albumin, hypoparathyroidism and pancreatitis, and rises with hyperparathyroidism and bone metastasis. Cholesterol &lt; 200 mg/dL (up with biliary obstruction and pancreatitis, down with liver damage). <b>D-xylose absorption</b> (plasma <b>20–57 mg/dL at 60 minutes</b>, <b>30–58 mg/dL at 120 minutes</b>) is low in malabsorption and short-bowel syndrome.</div>\n<div><b>Tumor markers</b> — <b>CEA (&lt; 5 ng/mL)</b> and <b>CA 19-9 (0–37 units/mL)</b> are used to <b>monitor treatment and detect recurrence</b> of GI cancers — <b>not to diagnose</b>, because benign conditions (inflammation, cirrhosis, Crohn's, pancreatitis, peptic ulcer, gallstones) also raise them. CA 19-9 rises with cancer of the pancreas, stomach, colon and gallbladder; CEA with GI, pancreatic, hepatobiliary, breast and lung cancer.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Stool tests</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Colorectal screening at home</b> — options (American Cancer Society): <b>high-sensitivity FIT every year</b>, <b>guaiac-based FOBT every year</b>, or <b>multitargeted stool DNA (mt-sDNA, e.g., Cologuard) every 3 years</b> — take-home kits, not a sample from a rectal exam. The DNA kit is <b>prescription only</b>: it is mailed to the patient after the provider orders it and mailed back in a prepaid container. It is <b>less specific than a colonoscopy</b> but appeals to people who <b>fear colonoscopy or worry about cost</b>. Covered by Medicare (no out-of-pocket cost when guidelines are met and the provider orders it) and most insurers; no bowel prep. Cost-effective, but <b>false negatives and false positives</b> occur. <b>Any positive result must be followed by a colonoscopy</b> — teach the patient to follow up promptly on results.</div>\n<div><b>gFOBT preparation</b> — guaiac reacts with more than blood, so it gives more <b>false positives</b> than FIT. <b>No NSAIDs for 7 days</b> before and during collection; <b>no red meat and no vitamin C over 250 mg/day for 3 days</b> before and during. <b>FIT has no food or drug limits</b>, so adherence is better.</div>\n<div><b>Who should get a colonoscopy instead</b> — a <b>personal or family history of colon cancer</b>, <b>inflammatory bowel disease (Crohn's or ulcerative colitis)</b>, or a <b>previous positive</b> screening test. <b>Black, American Indian and Alaska Native</b> people have higher colorectal cancer incidence — advocate screening for everyone.</div>\n<div><b>Other stool tests</b> — <b>ova and parasites</b> for parasitic infection; <b>fecal fat</b> when <b>steatorrhea</b> or malabsorption is suspected (fat should be absorbed with bile and pancreatic enzymes); infectious agents such as <b><i>Clostridium difficile</b></i> (now named <i>Clostridioides</i>) — a common cause of diarrhea in <b>older adults and after prolonged antibiotics</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A guaiac test detects the <b>peroxidase activity</b> of hemoglobin, but red meat contains animal hemoglobin and some foods and drugs interfere — so the diet and drug rules exist to stop the test from reacting to dinner instead of to a tumor. FIT uses antibodies to <b>human</b> hemoglobin, which is why it needs no preparation.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","ch":"45","chTitle":"Assessment of the GI system","title":"Imaging and endoscopy — EGD, ERCP, capsule","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Imaging</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Plain film and acute abdomen series</b> — a <b>plain abdominal film</b> shows masses, strictures and <b>bowel gas patterns (obstruction)</b> — no prep except a gown and no metal. For severe pain or suspected <b>perforation</b>: an <b>acute abdomen series</b> — chest x-ray (may show a <b>hiatal hernia</b>), supine and <b>upright</b> abdominal films (<b>free air</b> under the diaphragm = perforation). Ultrasound and CT are used more often now.</div>\n<div><b>CT</b> — cross-sectional view of liver, pancreas, spleen, biliary tract. With <b>contrast</b>: ask about <b>past contrast reactions and severe drug allergies</b>, <b>NPO at least 4 hours</b>, IV access; warn of a <b>warm, flushed feeling or metallic taste</b>; a mild sedative for claustrophobia; lie still and hold breath on cue. About <b>10 minutes without contrast, 30 with</b>.</div>\n<div><b>MRI, ultrasound and others</b> — <b>MRI</b> (including <b>MRCP</b> of the bile and pancreatic ducts): screen for <b>implants</b> (magnets); longer than CT but <b>no radiation</b>. <b>Ultrasound</b>: painless, noninvasive, no radiation, little or no prep (may need fasting, depending on the organ), lie still, gel on the skin, 15–30 minutes, no aftercare. <b>Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS)</b>: through a scope — tumors of the wall, nodes, pancreas, stomach, rectum; <b>prep and aftercare as for endoscopy</b> (and ultrasound). <b>Liver-spleen scan</b>: IV radioactive tracer taken up by the liver and spleen (tumors, abscesses, size, blood flow); reassure that the dose is small and not dangerous; ask about <b>pregnancy and breastfeeding</b> (the tracer enters breast milk); about 15 minutes for uptake, then scans in several positions — lie still; eliminated in urine within <b>24 hours</b> — <b>wash hands well after toileting</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Endoscopy — general rules</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What and why</b> — a flexible fiberoptic scope to see <b>bleeding, ulcers, inflammation, tumors</b>; biopsies and samples (e.g., for <b><i>Helicobacter pylori</b></i>) can be taken. Every endoscopic procedure needs <b>signed informed consent</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">EGD (esophagogastroduodenoscopy)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it can do</b> — views the <b>esophagus, stomach and duodenum</b> (called esophagoscopy or gastroscopy when it focuses on one organ — Fig. 45.5); can <b>stop bleeding</b> (clips, heat, injection, topical agents), <b>dilate a stricture</b>, view gastric lesions and support a <b>celiac</b> diagnosis.</div>\n<div><b>Before</b> — avoid <b>anticoagulants, aspirin and NSAIDs</b> for several days to a week (unless essential); usual morning meds unless told otherwise; <b>diabetes → get instructions</b> (NPO); <b>NPO 6–8 hours</b> (NPO means no food, no fluids — not even water — and <b>no smoking</b>); <b>remove dentures</b>.</div>\n<div><b>During</b> — <b>moderate sedation</b> (<b>midazolam, fentanyl or propofol</b>) — these <b>depress breathing</b>; <b>atropine</b> may dry secretions; a <b>local anesthetic spray numbs the gag reflex</b> (swallowing will be hard). Head of bed up, <b>bite block</b> in, 15–30 minutes. The nurse watches <b>respiratory rate and depth, SpO₂ and capnography</b>: <b>RR below 10 or exhaled CO₂ below 20% → stimulate with a sternal rub</b>.</div>\n<div><b>After — prevent aspiration</b> — vital signs <b>every 15–30 minutes</b> until sedation wears off; <b>side rails up</b>; <b>nothing by mouth until the gag reflex returns</b> (usually <b>30–60 minutes</b>) — the <b>priority is preventing aspiration</b>. Stop IV fluids once oral fluids stay down. Watch for <b>perforation: pain, bleeding, fever</b>. Needs <b>a driver</b>; <b>no driving for 12–18 hours</b>; a hoarse voice or sore throat can last days — <b>throat lozenges</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">ERCP (endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it does</b> — views the liver, gallbladder, <b>bile ducts and pancreatic duct</b> to find the cause of <b>obstruction</b> — now mostly <b>therapeutic</b>: a cannula in the common bile duct, <b>contrast dye</b> and x-rays; a <b>papillotomy</b> (cut in the sphincter at the ampulla of Vater) to remove <b>gallstones</b>; <b>stents</b> for strictures; biopsies.</div>\n<div><b>Before and during</b> — prepare as for EGD: <b>NPO 6–8 hours</b>, dentures out, IV for sedation; <b>medication reconciliation</b> (anticoagulants, NSAIDs, antiplatelets, antihyperglycemics — the provider decides what stops); ask about <b>contrast reactions and allergies</b> and <b>implanted devices</b> (an <b>ICD may need to be deactivated</b> for electrocautery). <b>Left lateral</b>, then <b>prone</b>; a tilt table spreads the contrast; <b>30 minutes to 2 hours</b>.</div>\n<div><b>After</b> — vital signs <b>every 15 minutes</b> until stable; <b>check the gag reflex before food or fluids</b>; stop IV fluids once oral fluids stay down without nausea or vomiting. Teach the complications that can appear <b>hours to 2 days later</b>: <b>pancreatitis, cholecystitis, infection, bleeding, perforation, biloma</b> — all bring <b>severe pain</b>; <b>fever means sepsis</b>. Colicky pain and flatulence from instilled air are expected. Report pain, fever, nausea or vomiting that doesn't resolve. Driver; no driving 12–18 hours.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Video capsule endoscopy (small bowel)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What and how</b> — a swallowed camera capsule photographs the <b>whole small bowel</b> to find <b>bleeding</b> (battery ~10 hours, so not for the colon). <b>No food or drink, including water, for 12 hours before</b> and <b>for the first 2 hours</b> of the test; <b>clear liquids after 2 hours</b>, a <b>light lunch after 4 hours</b>. Sensors are placed on the abdomen and a <b>belt with a data recorder</b> is worn; swallow the capsule with water; normal activity but <b>no vigorous activity</b>; about <b>8 hours</b>, then return the equipment.</div>\n<div><b>Afterward</b> — the capsule is <b>single use</b> — it passes in the stool and is <b>discarded</b>. Report signs of <b>obstruction</b>, fever, chest pain or trouble breathing, or a capsule <b>not passed within 2 weeks</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The same three drugs used for sedation in every scope — midazolam, fentanyl, propofol — all <b>depress breathing</b>, and the throat spray removes the <b>gag reflex</b>. Put those together and the two dangers are hypoventilation during the procedure and aspiration after it, which is why the nurse watches the respiratory rate and capnography during, and withholds every sip until the gag reflex is back.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"45-colonoscopy-sigmoidoscopy-and-colorectal","ch":"45","chTitle":"Assessment of the GI system","title":"Colonoscopy, sigmoidoscopy and colorectal screening","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Screening schedule (American Cancer Society)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>From age 45</b> — healthy adults choose one: <b>colonoscopy every 10 years</b>, <b>flexible sigmoidoscopy every 5 years</b>, <b>CT colonography (virtual colonoscopy) every 5 years</b>, or a stool test (FIT or gFOBT yearly, stool DNA every 3 years). <b>High risk</b> (family history, past polyps) → more often. <b>Young adults with obesity</b> are developing colon cancer (possibly from chronic low-grade inflammation).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Colonoscopy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it does</b> — views the <b>entire large bowel</b>; can take <b>biopsies and remove polyps</b>, find the cause of chronic diarrhea or <b>bleeding</b>, and treat bleeding.</div>\n<div><b>Preparation</b> — first-timers are often anxious — explain sedation and that pain will be controlled. <b>Stop aspirin, anticoagulants and antiplatelets</b> several days before; <b>diabetes → ask about drugs</b> for the NPO day. Bowel prep starts the <b>night before</b> (solutions taste better <b>chilled</b>); <b>clear liquids the day before</b>; <b>electrolyte or sports drinks</b> replace losses; <b>no red, orange or purple (grape) drinks or gelatin</b>; NPO for some hours before. Watery diarrhea starts about <b>an hour</b> after the prep; bisacodyl, suppositories or a small enema may be added. <b>Poor prep hides adenomas</b> and can make the test fail.</div>\n<div><b>During</b> — IV <b>moderate sedation</b> (midazolam, fentanyl, propofol); music may help but doesn't replace sedation. <b>Left side, knees drawn up</b>; air or <b>carbon dioxide</b> (less pain and distention) is instilled; <b>30–60 minutes</b>. <b>Atropine</b> is kept ready for <b>vasovagal bradycardia</b>. Watch RR, depth and SpO₂ — <b>RR &lt; 10 or exhaled CO₂ &lt; 20% → sternal rub</b>.</div>\n<div><b>After (Box 45.2)</b> — nothing by mouth until sedation wears off — <b>fluids once flatus is passed</b>; VS <b>every 15–30 minutes</b> until alert; <b>left lateral</b> position to pass gas; <b>side rails up</b>. Expected: <b>fullness, mild cramping and flatus</b> for hours; a <b>small amount of blood</b> in the first stool after biopsy or polypectomy. Watch for <b>perforation</b> (<b>severe abdominal pain and guarding</b>; fever may come later) and <b>hemorrhage or hypovolemic shock</b> (dizziness, light-headedness, <b>falling BP, tachycardia</b>, pallor or ash-gray skin, <b>altered mental status — may be the first sign in older adults</b>). Complications are uncommon, but <b>report excessive bleeding or severe pain</b> immediately. <b>Driver home</b>; <b>no driving, legal decisions or other work that needs focus</b> the rest of the day.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Virtual colonoscopy and sigmoidoscopy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Virtual colonoscopy (CT colonography)</b> — noninvasive 3-D images of the whole colon with the <b>same bowel prep and diet</b>. But a <b>polyp or bleeding found means a real colonoscopy</b> to treat it — the advantage of traditional colonoscopy is <b>diagnosis and treatment in one procedure</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Flexible sigmoidoscopy</b> — views the <b>rectum and sigmoid colon</b>: cancer screening (from 45, <b>every 5 years</b>), bleeding, inflammatory bowel disease. Prep: stop drugs the provider names (NSAIDs, anticoagulants); <b>clear liquids</b>; a laxative the night before; a <b>cleansing or Fleet enema the morning of</b>. <b>Left side, knee-chest</b>, <b>no sedation</b>, <b>5–15 minutes</b>; biopsies can't be felt. Afterward mild gas pain and flatulence; a little blood after biopsy; report heavy bleeding.</div>\n<div><b>Health literacy</b> — people vary widely in finding, understanding and using health information — and missed prep means missed screening. Have each patient <b>explain the test, prep and follow-up in their own words</b> (<b>teach-back</b>) and clarify gently until it's right.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Bowel prep works by flooding the colon with fluid the gut can't absorb, so the patient loses water and electrolytes along with stool — which is why the prep comes with electrolyte drinks, why red and purple liquids are banned (they look like blood on camera), and why diabetes medicines need a plan when the patient can't eat.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"45-adpie","ch":"45","chTitle":"Assessment of the GI system","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">For an assessment chapter the nursing process has two jobs: <b>collect a history and exam that point to the right organ</b>, and <b>get the patient safely through the test</b> that confirms it — the sedation, the bowel prep, the dye, and the hours afterward when complications show up.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div>Box 45.1: diet and supplements, appetite and <b>weight change</b>, swallowing and heartburn, <b>bowel pattern and stool color</b>, <b>blood or tarry stools</b>, laxatives, <b>alcohol and tobacco</b>, travel and camping, family history (FAP, colon cancer), <b>lactose intolerance</b>, food access and culture, past scopes; pain by <b>PQRST</b>; jaundice, itching, bruising.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Exam</div><b>empty bladder, supine, knees bent</b>; <b>inspect → auscultate → light palpation</b>; RUQ → LUQ → LLQ → RLQ with painful areas last; bowel sounds starting <b>RLQ</b> (5–30/min); <b>bruits</b>; guarding vs <b>rigidity</b>; <b>never touch a pulsating mass</b>; skip palpation if appendicitis or aneurysm is suspected.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Before a test</div><b>allergies to contrast</b> and drugs; <b>anticoagulants, aspirin, NSAIDs, antiplatelets</b>; <b>diabetes drugs</b>; implanted devices (ICD, pacemaker, metal for MRI); <b>pregnancy or breastfeeding</b> (scans, contrast); dentures; NPO status; someone to <b>drive</b> them home; health literacy.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>potential for <b>aspiration</b> after sedation and a numbed throat; potential for <b>ineffective breathing</b> from sedation; potential for <b>bleeding or perforation</b> after biopsy, polypectomy, papillotomy or dilation; potential for <b>pancreatitis or sepsis</b> after ERCP; <b>fluid and electrolyte loss</b> from bowel prep; <b>anxiety</b> and embarrassment about elimination and procedures; <b>need for teaching</b> about prep and screening.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>the patient completes the prep correctly (clear returns), stays <b>free of aspiration</b>, keeps <b>RR ≥ 10 and SpO₂</b> in range during sedation, has <b>no perforation or hemorrhage</b>, goes home with a <b>driver</b>, can <b>teach back</b> the warning signs, and is screened on schedule from <b>age 45</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Before</div>informed consent; NPO as ordered (<b>6–8 h</b> EGD/ERCP, <b>4 h</b> for contrast CT, <b>12 h</b> capsule); stop anticoagulants, aspirin and NSAIDs as directed; diabetes instructions; dentures out; IV access; teach bowel prep (<b>clear liquids, no red/orange/purple</b>, electrolyte drinks, chilled solution, diarrhea starts in about an hour); explain sedation and expected sensations; ease anxiety.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">During</div>monitor <b>respiratory rate and depth, SpO₂ and capnography</b> — <b>RR &lt; 10 or exhaled CO₂ &lt; 20% → sternal rub</b>; <b>atropine</b> available for vasovagal bradycardia; bite block for EGD; left lateral positioning for colonoscopy.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">After</div>VS <b>every 15–30 minutes</b> until awake; <b>side rails up</b>; <b>EGD/ERCP: nothing by mouth until the gag reflex returns</b>; colonoscopy: <b>left side, fluids after flatus</b>; watch for <b>severe pain, guarding, fever, bleeding, falling BP and confusion</b>; stop IV fluids once oral fluids stay down; <b>driver</b>, no driving <b>12–18 hours</b>, no legal decisions that day; lozenges for sore throat; teach ERCP complications that can appear <b>up to 2 days</b> later.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>the gag reflex returned <b>before</b> any oral intake; vital signs returned to baseline; no signs of perforation, bleeding, pancreatitis or sepsis; fluids tolerated; the patient states the warning signs to report (<b>severe or worsening abdominal pain, fever, vomiting, black or bloody stools, trouble swallowing or chest pain</b>), who is driving, and when the next screening is due.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-overview","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">46 · Chapter 46</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Tissue integrity · nutrition · gas exchange · pain</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Oral cavity and esophageal conditions</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> The mouth and esophagus start digestion and move food to the stomach, so their diseases threaten three things at once: <b>tissue integrity</b> (ulcers, lesions, burns), <b>nutrition</b> (it hurts or is hard to swallow) and the <b>airway</b> (swelling, secretions, aspiration). The exemplars are <b>stomatitis</b> for tissue integrity and <b>GERD</b> for nutrition. Around them sit oral cancer, salivary gland problems, hiatal hernia, esophageal cancer and esophageal trauma — and in every one of them the first question is <b>can this patient breathe and swallow safely?</b></p><div id=\"ch46-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Teeth and saliva begin digestion; the <b>lower esophageal sphincter (LES)</b> stays closed so stomach contents don't reflux; intact mucosa protects against infection.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Inflamed or ulcerated mucosa (stomatitis), precancerous or cancerous lesions, a weak or relaxed LES (GERD), the stomach slipping through the diaphragm (hiatal hernia), tumors, chemical burns or tears.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Mouth ulcers and white plaques; red or white patches that don't heal; heartburn and regurgitation after meals or lying down; dysphagia and odynophagia; night cough, hoarseness; weight loss.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Inspection and biopsy; EGD; <b>ambulatory pH monitoring</b> (most accurate for GERD); manometry; barium swallow (most specific for hiatal hernia); EUS, CT, MRI, PET for cancer staging.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Oral care and topical drugs; reflux diet and habits (no peppermint, chocolate, fatty food, caffeine or carbonated drinks; small meals, none within 3 hours of bed; head of bed raised); antacids, H₂ blockers, PPIs; Stretta, Nissen fundoplication, LINX; surgery, chemo and radiation, dilation and stents for cancer.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Airway obstruction and aspiration pneumonia; malnutrition; Barrett's esophagus and stricture; esophageal cancer; anastomotic leak, mediastinitis and sepsis after surgery.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Stridor or choking; coughing or choking with swallowing; fever, fluid collection, tachycardia or tachypnea after esophageal surgery (leak); chest pain that may be cardiac.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">LES relaxes (fatty meal, caffeine, smoking, lying down)<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>acid refluxes into the esophagus<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>inflammation → reflux esophagitis<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>healing with Barrett's epithelium or scar<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>stricture, dysphagia, cancer risk</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Leukoplakia vs erythroplakia</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Leukoplakia</b>: thick <b>white</b> patches that <b>can't be scraped off</b>, mostly benign, linked to tobacco and chronic irritation. <b>Erythroplakia</b>: <b>red, velvety</b>, <b>precancerous</b>. Candida plaques, by contrast, <b>wipe off</b> and leave a red, sore base.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Sliding vs paraesophageal hiatal hernia</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Sliding (type I, most common)</b>: GE junction slides up — main problem is <b>reflux</b>, treated like GERD. <b>Paraesophageal (II–IV)</b>: stomach rolls up beside the esophagus — fullness, breathlessness after meals, <b>volvulus, obstruction, strangulation</b> risk → <b>surgery</b> when complications threaten (medical care is favored when possible).</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">GERD chest pain vs cardiac</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">GERD pain can radiate to the neck, jaw or back and mimic angina; it worsens bending or lying down and is relieved by upright posture, fluids or antacids. <b>Rule out the heart first</b> — stay with the frightened patient.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>PPIs</b> shut off the acid pump, so tissue heals fast — but acid helps absorb calcium, so long-term use raises <b>hip fracture</b> risk. <b>Viscous lidocaine</b> numbs the mouth — and the gag reflex — so watch for <b>burns and aspiration</b>. <a href=\"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Airway and breath sounds, swallowing (cough or choking with intake), oral mucosa, pain, weight and intake, NG tube drainage and placement after surgery, temperature and heart rate after esophageal surgery.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Stridor or airway swelling; choking; after esophagectomy: fever, fluid accumulation, tachycardia, tachypnea or confusion (anastomotic leak) → surgeon and Rapid Response Team.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The LES and the pressure gradient</b> — the <b>lower esophageal sphincter</b> keeps a higher pressure than the stomach so contents can't move up. Reflux happens when the LES <b>relaxes</b> (foods, drugs, smoking) or when <b>pressure in the abdomen or stomach rises</b> above it (obesity, pregnancy, tight belts, bending, large meals, ascites). An <b>NG tube holds the sphincter open</b>. Lying flat at night removes <b>gravity</b>, so acid sits on the esophagus longer.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>What lowers LES pressure (Box 46.5)</b> — <b>caffeine</b> (coffee, tea, cola), <b>chocolate</b>, <b>nitrates</b>, <b>citrus</b>, <b>tomatoes</b>, <b>alcohol</b>, <b>peppermint and spearmint</b>, <b>smoking</b>, <b>calcium channel blockers</b>, <b>anticholinergics</b>, high <b>estrogen and progesterone</b>, an <b>NG tube</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>How reflux injures</b> — acid and duodenal contents inflame the squamous lining (<b>reflux esophagitis</b>). Healing may replace it with <b>Barrett's epithelium</b> — columnar cells that resist acid but are <b>premalignant</b> — or with fibrous scar that narrows the lumen (<b>stricture</b>) → progressive dysphagia. Reflux reaching the throat and lungs causes <b>asthma, laryngitis, hoarseness, night cough, dental decay</b>, aspiration pneumonia; bleeding can occur.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why esophageal cancer spreads early</b> — the esophagus has <b>no serosa</b> to contain a tumor, and its mucosa is rich in <b>lymphatics</b> — so tumors grow quickly and reach nodes early; most are large at diagnosis and more than half metastasize. <b>Squamous cell</b> cancers arise in the upper two-thirds; <b>adenocarcinoma</b> — now the most common — at the distal end and upper stomach (where Barrett's develops).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Swallowing is a protected airway maneuver: the tongue, palate, epiglottis and a working gag reflex keep food out of the trachea. Anything in this chapter that hurts, numbs, swells or narrows the mouth and esophagus — ulcers, viscous lidocaine, a tumor, a stricture, a fresh fundoplication — weakens that protection, which is why <b>aspiration precautions</b> appear in almost every care plan here.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Stomatitis — the tissue integrity exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Definition</b> — inflammation in the mouth, commonly painful <b>aphthous ulcers (\"canker sores\")</b> that break the mucosa → <b>pain, bleeding and infection</b> risk. <b>Recurrent aphthous stomatitis</b> affects about <b>20%</b> of people, more often women.</div>\n<div><b>Primary vs secondary</b> — <b>Primary</b> (most common): aphthous (noninfectious), <b>herpes simplex</b>, traumatic ulcers. <b>Secondary</b>: opportunistic viruses, fungi or bacteria in people who are <b>immunocompromised</b> or on <b>chemotherapy, radiation or steroids</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Candidiasis</b> — <b><i>Candida albicans</b></i> overgrows when <b>long-term antibiotics</b> destroy normal flora: <b>white plaques</b> on the tongue, palate, pharynx and buccal mucosa that <b>wipe off to a red, sore base</b>. <b>Older adults</b> are at high risk (aging immunity, diabetes, malnutrition, stress, drugs that dry the mouth) — teach them <b>mouth care</b>, because preventing candidiasis is far easier than treating it.</div>\n<div><b>Other causes</b> — allergy; <b>deficiencies of B vitamins, folate, zinc or iron</b>; systemic disease; irritants such as <b>tobacco and alcohol</b>; foods that can trigger aphthous ulcers (coffee, potatoes, cheese, nuts, <b>citrus</b>, gluten); possibly a genetic tendency.</div>\n<div><b>Who is at risk for mouth disease</b> — tobacco or alcohol use, <b>high-sugar diets</b>, too little <b>fluoride</b>, existing oral disease, <b>poor access to dental care</b>, and systemic disease (atherosclerosis, <b>diabetes</b>, Alzheimer's).</div>\n<div><b>Prevention (Box 46.1)</b> — balanced diet and <b>water</b>; manage stress; <b>weekly self-exam</b> of the mouth; report pain, swelling or bite changes; <b>dentures that fit</b>; <b>brush and floss twice daily</b>; <b>no alcohol-containing mouthwash</b>; avoid drugs that inflame the mouth or reduce saliva; <b>regular dental visits</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Care</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Assess</b> — recent infections, nutrition, <b>oral hygiene</b>, trauma, stress; drugs, OTC and herbal products; past episodes; whether lesions affect <b>swallowing, eating or speaking</b>. Document each lesion's location, size, shape, odor, color and drainage. Lesions in the pharynx with <b>dysphagia or throat pain</b> may extend down the esophagus (swallowing studies). <b>Severe stomatitis and edema can obstruct the airway.</b> Assess coping with pain and body image. Watch <b>hydration</b>: fluid loss (for example, persistent vomiting with a climbing sodium and BUN) dries the mucosa — restore fluids (IV if needed) along with mouth care.</div>\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — 1. <b>Impaired tissue integrity</b> from oral or esophageal lesions. 2. <b>Pain</b> from the lesions.</div>\n<div><b>Dysphagia action alert (PASS)</b> — dysphagia risks <b>airway obstruction, aspiration pneumonia and malnutrition</b>. Watch for coughing or choking when swallowing and food \"sticking\". <b>PASS</b>: is swallowing trouble <b>P</b>robable? <b>A</b>ccount for past problems; <b>S</b>creen for signs; get a <b>S</b>peech-language pathologist referral. Report and start <b>aspiration prevention</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Oral care (Box 46.2)</b> — remove dentures if the mouth is severely sore; oral hygiene <b>twice daily, after meals and as needed</b> (give the care if the patient can't) — <b>every 2 hours or more</b> if uncontrolled; <b>soft toothbrush or gauze</b>, toothpaste <b>without sodium lauryl sulfate</b>; <b>no commercial mouthwash or lemon-glycerin swabs</b>; rinse often with <b>warm saline and/or baking soda (sodium bicarbonate)</b> solution; <b>soft, bland, nonacidic</b> foods; topical analgesics or anesthetics as prescribed — <b>document their effect</b>. Oral care can be <b>delegated to AP</b> — then <b>inspect the mouth yourself</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs</b> — for infection: <b>clotrimazole troches</b>, <b>nystatin suspension (swish and spit)</b>, <b>chlorhexidine (swish and spit)</b>. For pain (\"swish and spit\"): <b>2% viscous lidocaine</b>, diphenhydramine liquid, dyclonine lozenges, aluminum-magnesium hydroxide with simethicone, attapulgite. Teach which are <b>swish and swallow, rinse only, or taken by mouth</b>; <b>finish antibiotics</b> even after feeling better. Severe pain may need <b>opioid analgesics</b> — for any prescribed pain drug, teach its side effects and <b>no driving or tasks that need concentration</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Viscous lidocaine alert</b> — it numbs the mouth and throat — the patient <b>may not feel burns from hot drinks</b>, and as sensation falls <b>aspiration risk rises</b>. Use with extreme caution.</div>\n<div><b>Comfort and recovery</b> — <b>cool or cold liquids</b> soothe; avoid hard, spicy, salty and acidic foods; <b>high-protein</b> foods for healing; vitamin C supplements (citrus may hurt). Most stomatitis is self-limiting; ongoing pain from chemo or radiation may need a support group. <b>Evaluate</b>: healthy mucosa without inflammation or infection; minimal or no pain.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Commercial mouthwash and lemon-glycerin swabs feel cleansing but work against healing: alcohol and acid <b>dry and sting</b> the mucosa, and dryness lets organisms thrive. Saline and baking-soda rinses clean without chemical irritation and help neutralize acid — which is why the evidence-based mouth-care box replaces the familiar products.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Oral tumors, oral cancer and salivary glands","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Premalignant lesions</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Erythroplakia</b> — <b>red, velvety</b> patches on the floor of the mouth, tongue, palate and mandibular mucosa — <b>precancerous</b>, and hard to tell from inflammation.</div>\n<div><b>Leukoplakia</b> — thick <b>white</b> patches firmly attached — <b>can't be scraped off</b>; slightly raised, sharply rounded. Mostly benign, but lesions on the <b>lips or tongue can become cancer</b>. Linked to <b>tobacco</b> and long-term irritation (<b>poorly fitting dentures, cheek chewing, broken teeth</b>). <b>Oral hairy leukoplakia</b> appears with immune compromise — an early sign of <b>HIV</b>, or with Epstein-Barr virus. Ask every patient about tobacco and offer counseling and treatment.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Oral cancer</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Prevention and risk</b> — <b>dentist at least twice a year</b> with an oral cancer screening (inspecting and palpating the mouth); <b>stop tobacco and alcohol</b>; protect lips from the <b>sun</b>; avoid <b>HPV</b> (sexually transmitted). <b>White men</b> have much higher HPV-related oropharyngeal cancer than other men — advocate <b>HPV vaccination and regular oral cancer screening for men</b>. Long industrial exposure to <b>polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAH)</b> — riskier still with HPV — and periodontal disease with jaw bone loss raise risk. <b>Genetics</b>: ask at-risk patients about <b>any history of cancer</b>; oral cancers often carry a <b>TP53</b> mutation (the gene for p53, a protein that regulates cell division and prevents tumors).</div>\n<div><b>Types</b> — most are <b>squamous cell carcinomas</b> of the lips, tongue, buccal mucosa and oropharynx after <b>40</b>: a <b>red, raised, eroded</b> lesion, or any lesion <b>not healed in 2 weeks</b>, or a lump in the cheek → assess further. <b>Basal cell</b> cancer on the <b>lips</b> from <b>sun</b>: a raised scab that becomes an ulcer with a <b>pearly border</b>; rarely metastasizes. <b>Kaposi sarcoma</b>: painless purple, red or brown nodule (palate, gums, tongue) — associated with <b>AIDS</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Key features (Box 46.3)</b> — mouth <b>bleeding</b>, poor appetite, <b>difficulty chewing or swallowing</b>, <b>unplanned weight loss</b>, <b>thick or absent saliva</b>, a <b>painless red, raised or eroded lesion</b>, a lump or thickening in the cheek.</div>\n<div><b>Speech, eating and self-image</b> — oral tumors and their treatment can impair <b>chewing, swallowing and speaking</b>, and pain limits daily activities and self-care. Removing the <b>tongue or part of the jaw</b>, or a <b>tracheostomy</b>, also changes appearance and speech → assess <b>self-image</b>, the <b>support system</b>, and educational, cultural and spiritual needs. The team includes a <b>speech therapist</b>, registered dietitian nutritionist, social worker and a spiritual leader if the patient wishes.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnose</b> — inspect every area with gloves, tongue blade and penlight; the provider palpates <b>cervical nodes</b>. <b>Needle or incisional biopsy</b> (excisional removes a small lesion completely); <b>CT or MRI</b> for spread and staging (MRI shows nerve involvement and tongue tumor thickness).</div>\n<div><b>Airway first</b> — <b>airway maintenance is the priority</b>. Assess dyspnea, rate and depth, <b>wheezes</b> (aspiration), <b>stridor</b> (partial obstruction). Promote <b>deep breathing</b> for an effective cough. <b>Semi- or high-Fowler</b>; fluids to thin secretions if the gag reflex is intact; chest physiotherapy; <b>oral suction with a Yankauer (tonsil tip)</b>, teaching family to suction. <b>Steroids</b> for edema, antibiotics for infection, <b>cool mist face tent</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Aspiration precautions</b> — check <b>LOC, gag reflex (before any fluids) and swallowing</b>; upright; suction at the bedside; AP feed <b>small amounts</b>; family and visitors <b>ask the nurse before giving food or drink</b>; <b>thickened liquids</b> and a swallow study with the <b>SLP</b> as needed.</div>\n<div><b>Oral care</b> — every <b>2 hours</b>; <b>soft-bristle or ultrasoft \"chemobrush\"</b> (especially with low platelets); <b>no foam swabs</b> (don't remove plaque, dry the mouth); <b>water-based lubricant</b> for lips and mucosa; <b>no alcohol mouthwash or lemon-glycerin</b>; rinse with <b>baking soda or warm saline</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — excision, radiation, cryosurgery, chemotherapy and targeted therapy, often combined (multimodal); <b>Mohs surgery</b> for basal cell cancer of the lip. Surgeries include dental extraction before radiation, <b>glossectomy</b> (tongue), <b>gastrostomy tube</b>, <b>laryngectomy</b>, mandible or maxilla resection, microsurgical or flap reconstruction, <b>neck dissection</b>, <b>tracheostomy</b>, tumor resection with margins. Good <b>oral care before</b> surgery lowers complications. After a small excision: <b>liquids for a day</b>, then advance; keep follow-up.</div>\n<div><b>At home (Box 46.4)</b> — <b>inspect the mouth daily</b>; meticulous oral hygiene with an <b>ultrasoft brush</b> cleaned after each use; follow-up visits; <b>thickened liquids</b> for dysphagia; soft foods for stomatitis; <b>saliva substitute</b> as prescribed.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Sialadenitis and xerostomia</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Sialadenitis</b> — inflammation of a <b>salivary gland</b> — usually the <b>parotid or submandibular</b> — from bacteria or viruses (often CMV), <b>immune compromise</b> (HIV, Sjögren syndrome), <b>less saliva</b> (radiation for head and neck or thyroid cancer), or drugs (<b>phenothiazines, tetracyclines</b>). Can become chronic; an untreated abscess can rupture into the <b>neck and mediastinum</b>. Best prevention: <b>routine oral hygiene</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Assess and treat</b> — dryness, pain, <b>facial swelling</b> over the gland, pus that can be massaged out; check <b>facial nerve (CN VII)</b> function; fever and malaise. Treat the cause and <b>increase saliva flow</b>: <b>hydration</b>, <b>moist heat</b>, <b>massage along the gland</b>, NSAIDs, antibiotics if infected, <b>sialagogues</b> (saliva stimulants).</div>\n<div><b>Xerostomia</b> — radiation (including <b>radioactive iodine</b>) dries the glands within <b>24 hours</b> → <b>severe dry mouth</b>, temporary or permanent depending on dose. Best help: <b>frequent sips of water</b> and <b>frequent mouth care, especially before meals</b>; <b>saliva substitutes</b> after radiation ends.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Saliva is not just moisture: it washes the teeth, buffers acid, carries antibacterial proteins and lets food form a bolus that can be swallowed. Lose it to radiation or drugs and the mouth becomes a place where bacteria climb into the salivary ducts, teeth decay, and food sticks — so the treatments all aim to <b>bring saliva back</b> or replace it.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"GERD — the nutrition exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Definition</b> — <b>gastroesophageal reflux (GER)</b> is backward flow of stomach contents (<b>regurgitation</b>); <b>GERD</b> is the chronic, more serious disease from <b>persistent</b> reflux. The <b>most common upper GI disorder</b> in the US, affecting <b>18–28%</b> of North American adults — most often middle-aged and older adults, but increasingly <b>younger</b> adults, so don't rule it out by age. It may cause <b>no symptoms at first</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Risk factors</b> — <b>overweight and obesity</b> (raised abdominal pressure) are the biggest; <b><i>H. pylori</b></i> (gastritis → slow emptying); <b>NG tubes</b>; pregnancy, tight belts or binders, bending over, ascites; <b>obstructive sleep apnea</b> (night reflux); strong genetic link plus lifestyle.</div>\n<div><b>Complications</b> — <b>reflux esophagitis</b>; <b>Barrett's esophagus</b> (premalignant); <b>stricture</b> with progressive dysphagia; <b>asthma, laryngitis, dental decay, cardiac disease</b>; <b>hemorrhage</b>; <b>aspiration pneumonia</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Health equity</b> — recognition and treatment lag for <b>Hispanic and Latino Americans</b>, especially when they aren't proficient in English and providers don't speak Spanish — advocate for <b>professional medical translators (interpreters)</b> for every English language learner.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Ask</b> — heartburn or <b>atypical chest pain</b>; new <b>asthma</b>, <b>morning hoarseness</b>, <b>coughing or wheezing at night</b> (reflux reaching the pharynx or lungs); <b>dysphagia</b> and <b>odynophagia</b> (painful swallowing).</div>\n<div><b>Key features (Box 46.6)</b> — the main symptoms are <b>dyspepsia</b> (indigestion) and <b>regurgitation</b> (can cause aspiration or bronchitis). Also <b>water brash</b> (sudden salivation — fluid in the throat <b>without</b> a sour taste), <b>pyrosis</b> (heartburn), epigastric or general abdominal pain, chest pain, <b>globus</b> (something in the throat), pharyngitis, <b>dental caries</b>, belching, flatulence, nausea, bloating. Severe GERD: discomfort after <b>every meal for 20 minutes to 2 hours</b>, <b>worse lying down, bending or straining</b>, <b>relieved by fluids, antacids or sitting up</b>. Severe pain may radiate to the <b>neck, jaw or back like cardiac pain</b>. Listen for <b>crackles</b> (aspiration).</div>\n<div><b>The frightened patient</b> — many come to the ED <b>fearing a heart attack</b> — stay with them until the diagnosis is clear and support coping.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults</b> — often <b>atypical</b>: <b>dysphagia, vomiting, anorexia, anemia, cough, respiratory problems</b> instead of heartburn; more complications (comorbidity, polypharmacy) → report so a GERD workup can be considered.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnosis</b> — usually from <b>symptoms and history</b>. <b>EGD</b> (with biopsy, moderate sedation, needs a driver) for atypical symptoms. <b>Ambulatory esophageal pH monitoring is the most accurate</b>: a nasal catheter or wireless capsule on the distal esophagus records pH for <b>24–48 hours</b> while the patient keeps a <b>diary</b> of activities and symptoms. <b>Manometry</b> (pressure of the LES and peristalsis) can't diagnose GERD alone — it checks function <b>before surgery</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — 1. <b>Potential for compromised nutrition</b> from food choices. 2. <b>Acute pain</b> from reflux.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every GERD symptom follows gravity and pressure. Bending, straining, a big meal and lying flat all push stomach contents up against a weak sphincter; sitting up, a small meal and an antacid all reverse it. That is why the most effective treatments are not drugs at all but <b>positions, portions and timing</b>.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Treating GERD — diet, lifestyle, drugs, procedures","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Nutrition and lifestyle</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Food choices</b> — GERD is <b>chronic</b> — teaching is the nurse's most important role (treat it more aggressively in older adults). Work with the <b>RDN</b>. <b>Avoid foods that lower LES pressure or irritate</b>: <b>peppermint, chocolate, fatty (especially fried) foods, caffeine, carbonated drinks</b>; limit <b>spicy and acidic</b> foods (orange juice, tomatoes) until healed. Diet apps can help.</div>\n<div><b>Meals and sleep</b> — <b>4–6 small meals</b> instead of 3 large ones (large meals raise stomach pressure and slow emptying); <b>eat slowly and chew well</b> (less belching); <b>nothing within 3 hours of bedtime</b> (night reflux does the most damage, and regurgitation lying down risks <b>aspiration</b>); <b>sleep propped up</b> — <b>blocks under the head of the bed or a wedge pillow</b>, not just extra pillows.</div>\n<div><b>Habits</b> — <b>no alcohol or tobacco</b> (refer to cessation programs); <b>weight loss</b> if needed; <b>upright at least 1 hour after eating</b>; no <b>heavy lifting, straining or working bent over</b>; <b>loose, nonrestrictive clothing</b>. People with obesity often have sleep apnea too — <b>CPAP</b> reduces night reflux. These habits work and can bring prompt relief in uncomplicated cases. Teach the warning signs of complications such as <b>stricture</b> (worsening dysphagia) and <b>Barrett's esophagus</b>; an RDN or support group can help with meal planning.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Drug therapy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Drugs that cause reflux</b> — review with the provider: <b>oral contraceptives, anticholinergics, sedatives, NSAIDs, nitrates, calcium channel blockers</b> all lower LES pressure.</div>\n<div><b>The three classes</b> — <b>antacids</b>, <b>histamine (H₂) blockers</b> and <b>proton pump inhibitors (PPIs)</b> — the same drugs used for peptic ulcer disease (Table 47.1). They <b>inhibit acid secretion, speed stomach emptying, or protect the mucosa</b>. IV <b>esomeprazole or pantoprazole</b> prevent <b>stress ulcers</b> after surgery. PPIs heal tissue fast but symptoms <b>recur</b> when stopped; long-term use can <b>mask</b> reflux symptoms, and stopping the drug shows whether reflux has resolved.</div>\n<div><b>Long-term PPI cautions</b> — linked to kidney, liver and heart disease, dementia, GI tumors, <b>poor nutrient absorption</b>, and more respiratory and GI infections — monitor long-term users. In <b>older adults</b>, PPIs reduce <b>calcium absorption</b> (and protein digestion) → brittle bones → <b>hip fracture</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Procedures and surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Stretta procedure</b> — nonsurgical: <b>radiofrequency energy</b> through an endoscope near the GE junction <b>reduces vagus nerve activity</b>. Patients with obesity or severe symptoms may not be candidates; it can replace surgery when other measures fail. Afterward (Box 46.7): <b>clear liquids for 24 hours</b>, then <b>soft diet</b>; <b>no NSAIDs or aspirin for 10 days</b>; keep taking PPIs; <b>liquid medicines</b> when possible; <b>no NG tube for at least 1 month</b> (perforation); report abdominal or <b>chest pain, bleeding, dysphagia, nausea or vomiting, shortness of breath</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication (LNF)</b> — the minimally invasive <b>standard surgery</b> for severe GERD — only a small share of patients need it: reflux that fails medical treatment, high-volume reflux, drug intolerance or nonadherence, a <b>large hiatal hernia</b>, sometimes reflux-related respiratory symptoms. The <b>fundus is wrapped around the lower esophagus</b>. Keep following the antireflux diet and antacids — <b>recurrence is common</b>. (Care is in the hiatal hernia topic.)</div>\n<div><b>LINX</b> — a ring of <b>magnets</b> around the LES raises closing pressure but lets food through — for typical GERD with an abnormal pH study and partial PPI response, without hiatal hernia or severe esophagitis. <b>Action alert</b>: tell <b>every provider</b>; <b>older LINX devices must never go in an MRI</b>; newer \"MR Conditional\" ones only under set conditions.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>PPIs block the final step of acid making — the proton pump in every parietal cell — which is why they heal esophagitis faster than any other drug. But stomach acid also frees calcium, iron and B₁₂ from food and kills swallowed microbes; switch it off for years and the bill comes due as fractures, deficiencies and infections.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Hiatal hernia and fundoplication care","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hiatal (diaphragmatic) hernia</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Types</b> — the stomach pushes through the <b>esophageal hiatus</b> of the diaphragm into the chest. <b>Type I — sliding</b> (most common): the GE junction and part of the fundus slide up and down with position and pressure; main concern is <b>reflux</b> (volvulus and obstruction are rare). <b>Types II–IV — paraesophageal (rolling)</b>: II, the fundus rolls up <b>beside</b> the esophagus while the GE junction stays put; III, both herniate; IV, colon, spleen, pancreas or small bowel in the sac. High risk of <b>volvulus, obstruction and strangulation</b>; slow venous oozing causes <b>iron deficiency anemia</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Key features (Box 46.8)</b> — <b>sliding</b>: heartburn, regurgitation, chest pain, dysphagia, belching. <b>Paraesophageal</b>: <b>fullness, breathlessness or a feeling of suffocation after eating</b>, chest pain that <b>mimics angina</b>, worse <b>lying down</b>. <b>Most</b> are asymptomatic; when symptoms occur they usually worsen <b>after meals or lying flat</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnose</b> — <b>barium swallow with fluoroscopy is the most specific</b> (sliding hernias show with position changes); <b>EGD</b> to view the esophagus and gastric lining; high-resolution manometry for larger sliding hernias.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — <b>sliding</b>: like GERD — <b>antacids or PPIs</b>, diet and lifestyle (teaching is the key nursing action). <b>Paraesophageal</b>: depends on type, symptoms and risk — medical management is favored when possible; <b>surgery</b> for volvulus, bleeding, obstruction, strangulation, perforation or respiratory compromise. Before elective surgery: <b>lose weight</b> if overweight, <b>quit smoking</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">After fundoplication</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The operation</b> — the fundus is <b>wrapped around the distal esophagus</b> and sutured to anchor it <b>below the diaphragm</b> — open (abdominal or thoracic) or <b>laparoscopic (LNF, fewer complications)</b>. A <b>thoracic approach</b> brings a <b>chest tube and NG tube</b> for several days.</div>\n<div><b>After LNF (Box 46.9)</b> — bleeding and infection are possible but uncommon — watch for them, and teach: <b>soft diet about a week</b> (no carbonated drinks, tough foods, raw vegetables); <b>antireflux drugs at least a month</b>; <b>no driving for a week</b> or on opioids; <b>walk daily, no heavy lifting</b>; remove small dressings and <b>shower at 2 days</b>, leave closure strips until <b>day 10</b>; wash incisions with soap and water, report redness or drainage; report <b>fever &gt; 101°F (38.3°C)</b> — <b>&gt; 100°F (37.8°C) if older than 65</b> — nausea, vomiting, uncontrollable bloating or pain; follow-up in <b>3–4 weeks</b>.</div>\n<div><b>After open repair — lungs first</b> — the priority is <b>preventing respiratory complications</b> (large hernias are highest risk): <b>HOB at least 30°</b>, early <b>ambulation</b>, <b>splint the incision</b> to cough, incentive spirometry and deep breathing, VTE prevention.</div>\n<div><b>The NG tube</b> — a <b>large-bore NG tube</b> keeps the wrap from becoming too tight. Drainage is <b>dark brown (old blood) at first</b>, then <b>yellowish green within 8 hours</b>. Check placement every <b>4–8 hours</b> and <b>anchor it well</b> — <b>reinserting it could perforate the fundoplication</b>. Keep it <b>patent</b> so the stomach stays decompressed (vomiting or retching can rupture the sutures). Frequent <b>oral care</b>; accurate I&amp;O.</div>\n<div><b>Complications (Box 46.10)</b> — <b>temporary dysphagia</b> when eating starts (<b>persistent</b> dysphagia = wrap too tight → may need dilation); <b>gas bloat syndrome</b> (can't belch to relieve distention); <b>atelectasis or pneumonia</b> (dyspnea, chest pain, fever); <b>obstructed NG tube</b> (nausea, vomiting, distention, no drainage).</div>\n<div><b>Eating again and gas</b> — clear fluids, then a near-normal diet over <b>4–6 weeks</b>; <b>smaller, more frequent meals</b> (smaller stomach reservoir); limit <b>caffeine, carbonated drinks, alcohol</b>; <b>supervise the first oral feedings</b>. For <b>aerophagia</b> and <b>gas bloat</b>: relax before meals, eat and drink slowly, chew well; <b>no carbonated drinks, gas-producing or high-fat foods, gum or straws</b>; move and walk often; <b>simethicone</b> if needed.</div>\n<div><b>Going home</b> — activity limits <b>3–6 weeks</b> (open) or about <b>1 week</b> (laparoscopic); keep eating <b>smaller, more frequent meals</b>, advancing to a near-normal diet over <b>4–6 weeks</b>, with <b>no carbonated drinks, gas-producing or high-fat foods, gum or straws</b>; <b>stool softeners or bulk laxatives</b> (no straining); <b>inspect the incision daily</b>; report swelling, redness, tenderness, discharge or fever; <b>avoid people with respiratory infections</b> — coughing can make the incision or wrap <b>dehisce</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A fundoplication is a sutured cuff around the esophagus, and everything after surgery protects it: the large NG tube sizes the wrap and keeps the stomach empty so retching can't tear it, the tube is never blindly reinserted because the tip could pierce it, and coughing, straining and vomiting are prevented because each is a sudden pressure spike against fresh sutures.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Esophageal cancer and esophagectomy","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The disease</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Risk factors</b> — <b>alcohol</b>, <b>smoking</b>, diets low in <b>fresh fruits and vegetables</b>, foods high in <b>nitrates and nitrosamines</b> (pickled, fermented), <b>malnutrition</b>, <b>obesity</b>, <b>untreated GERD</b>. <b>Men</b> have higher incidence and mortality regardless of race.</div>\n<div><b>Key features (Box 46.11)</b> — silent early. The most common feature is <b>persistent, progressive dysphagia</b> — often not until the lumen narrows — with <b>weight loss, often more than 20 lb</b> over months. Also food <b>sticking</b>, <b>odynophagia</b>, <b>halitosis</b>, chronic <b>hiccups</b>, chronic <b>cough</b> with more secretions, <b>hoarseness</b>, severe persistent chest or abdominal pain, anorexia, regurgitation, nausea and vomiting, bowel changes.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnose and support</b> — <b>EGD with biopsy</b>; staging, often by <b>endoscopic ultrasound</b>; <b>PET</b> finds metastases more accurately than CT and tracks chemotherapy response. The diagnosis brings <b>anxiety</b>, fear of <b>choking at meals</b>, lost pleasure in eating — assess coping and support; refer to counseling, pastoral care, social work; plan end-of-life care when needed.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Without surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Nutrition and swallowing</b> — RDN sets calorie needs; <b>daily weight before breakfast, same scale</b>; <b>upright for several hours after meals, never flat</b>; <b>HOB at least 30°</b>; <b>semisoft foods and thickened liquids</b>; record intake; liquid supplements between meals; <b>enteral feeding</b> if dysphagia is severe; <b>gastrostomy or jejunostomy</b> for complete obstruction or fistula. <b>SLP</b> swallowing therapy; <b>OT</b> feeding techniques. <b>Critical rescue</b>: watch every meal for <b>aspiration</b> (airway obstruction, pneumonia) and teach caregivers to feed safely and respond to choking.</div>\n<div><b>Cancer treatments</b> — chemotherapy (often before surgery), radiation, or <b>chemoradiation</b>; <b>photodynamic therapy</b> with <b>porfimer sodium</b> (light-sensitive drug) as palliation; <b>esophageal dilation</b> for quick but temporary relief (<b>perforation</b> risk); <b>stents</b> (metal, plastic or biodegradable) to keep it open longer — palliation for patients who can't have surgery; a covered stent can seal a perforation (bacteremia → <b>antibiotics</b> to prevent sepsis and endocarditis; pain, <b>stent migration</b>, recurrent dysphagia); <b>trastuzumab</b> (HER2 target) IV every 3 weeks.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Esophagectomy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Before</b> — removal of all or part of the esophagus — <b>minimally invasive (MIE)</b> for early cancer, but most need open surgery. <b>Stop smoking 2–4 weeks before</b>; <b>respiratory rehab</b> and <b>nutrition support</b> (oral, tube or parenteral) reduce lung complications — teach the patient and caregiver to track <b>weight and I&amp;O</b>; treat dental disease; <b>oral care 4 times a day</b>; teaching and emotional support; <b>prophylactic antibiotics</b> and oxygen on the day.</div>\n<div><b>Lungs first</b> — <b>respiratory care is the highest priority</b>. After open surgery: <b>mechanical ventilation for at least 16–24 hours</b>. Then <b>turn, cough and deep breathe every 1–2 hours</b>, check breath sounds and dyspnea every 1–2 hours, <b>splint the incision</b>, give adequate analgesia. Pre-op radiation raises the risk of <b>atelectasis and pneumonia</b>. Keep the patient <b>semi- or high-Fowler</b>; keep the <b>chest tube</b> patent and watch its drainage.</div>\n<div><b>Heart and fluids</b> — <b>hypotension</b> from pressure on the back of the heart; <b>fluid overload</b> (older adults, node dissection) — edema, <b>crackles</b>, raised JVP; ICU hemodynamic monitoring every 2 hours; <b>atrial fibrillation</b> from vagus nerve irritation.</div>\n<div><b>Anastomotic leak</b> — wound infection can appear at <b>4–5 days</b>; a <b>leak at the anastomosis</b> — the dreaded complication — at <b>2–10 days</b> → <b>stop all oral intake</b> until healed; <b>mediastinitis</b> from a leak can cause <b>fatal sepsis</b>. <b>Critical rescue</b>: <b>fever, fluid accumulation, signs of inflammation, early shock (tachycardia, tachypnea, altered mental status)</b> → report <b>immediately to the surgeon and Rapid Response Team</b>.</div>\n<div><b>NG tube after esophageal surgery (Box 46.12)</b> — decompresses the stomach so the suture line isn't strained. Check placement <b>every 4–8 hours</b>; drainage <b>bloody → yellowish green by the end of day 1</b>; <b>secure it well</b>; <b>never irrigate or reposition without an order</b>; <b>oral and nasal care every 2–4 hours</b>; <b>HOB at least 30°</b>; first sips of water <b>upright</b> — watch for dysphagia; watch for leak signs.</div>\n<div><b>Feeding after surgery</b> — <b>jejunostomy (J-tube) feedings start on day 2</b> — <b>don't aspirate for residual</b> (tears the mucosa) — increased slowly through day 5. <b>Barium swallow on day 7</b>: no leak → NG tube out. <b>Minimal liquid diet for 2 weeks</b>, then as the surgeon orders.</div>\n<div><b>At home</b> — keep up <b>walking and the incentive spirometer</b>; avoid bed rest. <b>Small, frequent, high-calorie, high-protein, soft</b> meals and <b>supplement shakes</b>; <b>sit upright to eat and stay upright</b> after. Patients can lose up to <b>10% of body weight</b> — <b>report a loss of 5 lb or more in a month</b>; home <b>tube feeding or parenteral nutrition</b> if oral intake falls short. <b>Handwashing</b>, avoid sick people and crowds, report respiratory infection. Report returning <b>dysphagia or odynophagia</b> (stricture, reflux, recurrence). Focus on <b>quality of life</b>; plan hospice when needed. A rehab program of <b>brisk walking and diet education</b> improved reflux, sleep, mood and quality of life.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>After an esophagectomy the stomach is pulled up into the chest and joined to what remains of the esophagus, so the suture line sits next to the lungs, heart and mediastinum. A leak spills swallowed saliva and gastric juice into the chest — that is why nothing goes by mouth until a barium swallow proves the join is sealed, and why fever with tachycardia after day 2 is treated as a leak until proven otherwise.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-esophageal-trauma","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Esophageal trauma","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Esophageal trauma</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Causes</b> — blunt injury, <b>chemical burns</b>, surgery or endoscopy (rare), or <b>continuous severe vomiting</b>. Perforation: <b>straining, seizures, trauma, foreign objects, instruments or tubes</b>, complications of esophageal surgery, ulcers, chemicals. A tear lets <b>caustic acid into the mediastinum</b> → high mortality from <b>shock, respiratory failure or sepsis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Acid vs alkali</b> — caustic ingestion (accidental or intentional) damages the mouth and esophagus fast. <b>Acids burn the superficial lining</b>; <b>alkalis penetrate deeper</b> — strong alkalis can <b>perforate the esophagus within 1 minute</b>. Later: aspiration pneumonia, hemorrhage, and <b>strictures</b> as scar forms.</div>\n<div><b>Assess</b> — in the ED: <b>airway, breathing</b>, chest pain, <b>dysphagia</b>, vomiting, bleeding; the circumstances. Endoscopy if it won't extend the damage; <b>chest CT for mediastinal air</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Treat</b> — <b>NPO</b> to stop further leakage; esophageal and gastric <b>suction</b>; <b>esophageal rest for more than a week</b>; <b>TPN</b> for calories and protein; <b>broad-spectrum antibiotics</b> (sepsis); <b>high-dose corticosteroids</b> (inflammation, prevent <b>strictures</b>); opioid and nonopioid analgesics; <b>viscous lidocaine</b> for mouth burns. Surgery if healing fails — resection with <b>gastric pull-through</b> or a bowel segment; a <b>G-tube</b> for nutrition while healing.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Acids cause coagulation of the surface proteins, forming a crust that limits how deep they go; alkalis dissolve proteins and fats (liquefaction) and keep burning downward — the same chemistry that makes drain cleaner work. That is why a swallowed alkali is the more dangerous injury, perforating in minutes.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"46-adpie","ch":"46","chTitle":"Oral cavity and esophageal conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Every patient in this chapter is assessed and protected in the same order: <b>airway and swallowing first</b>, then <b>tissue integrity and pain</b>, then <b>nutrition</b> — because a sore mouth, a numbed throat, a narrowed esophagus or a fresh suture line can all turn a meal into an aspiration.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Mouth</div>oral hygiene habits, dentures and fit, tobacco and alcohol, recent infections, nutrition, stress, drugs (antibiotics, chemo, steroids, drying drugs); inspect with gloves, tongue blade and penlight: <b>ulcers, white plaques (wipe off?)</b>, red or white patches, <b>lesions not healed in 2 weeks</b>, bleeding, dryness, swelling; effect on <b>eating, swallowing and speaking</b>; body image.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Swallowing and airway</div><b>coughing or choking</b> with swallowing, food sticking, <b>dysphagia and odynophagia</b>, <b>stridor</b>, wheezes or <b>crackles</b> (aspiration), secretions, gag reflex and LOC; use <b>PASS</b> and involve the <b>SLP</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Esophagus</div><b>heartburn and regurgitation</b> — timing with meals, lying down and bending; <b>atypical chest pain</b>, night cough, hoarseness, new asthma; foods, drugs and habits that lower LES pressure; weight and BMI; <b>weight loss</b> and progressive dysphagia (cancer); older adults' atypical signs (anemia, anorexia, vomiting, cough).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>stomatitis: <b>impaired tissue integrity</b> and <b>pain</b>. GERD: <b>potential for compromised nutrition</b> and <b>acute pain</b>. Oral and esophageal cancer and surgery: <b>airway obstruction and aspiration</b>, <b>malnutrition</b>, anxiety and grief. After esophagectomy or fundoplication: <b>respiratory complications</b>, <b>anastomotic leak</b>, hypotension, fluid overload and dysrhythmia.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>healthy oral mucosa without inflammation or infection and with minimal pain; <b>no aspiration</b>; the patient avoids reflux foods (peppermint, chocolate, fatty food, caffeine, carbonated drinks), eats small meals and nothing within 3 hours of bed, sleeps with the head of the bed raised, avoids alcohol and tobacco, and takes the antireflux drugs; weight stable or rising; after surgery, <b>clear lungs</b>, no leak, and a diet progressing as ordered.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Oral care and comfort</div>oral care twice daily and after meals (<b>every 2 hours</b> if uncontrolled) with a <b>soft or ultrasoft brush</b>, <b>no alcohol mouthwash, lemon-glycerin or foam swabs</b>; <b>saline or baking soda rinses</b>; soft, bland, cool foods; <b>nystatin, clotrimazole, chlorhexidine</b> as ordered; viscous lidocaine with <b>burn and aspiration</b> precautions; delegate oral care to AP and <b>inspect afterward</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Airway and swallowing</div><b>semi- or high-Fowler</b>; <b>suction (Yankauer)</b> at the bedside; check <b>gag reflex before fluids</b>; small bites, <b>thickened liquids</b>; visitors ask before feeding; steroids and cool mist for edema; <b>SLP</b> swallow evaluation.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">GERD teaching</div><b>4–6 small meals</b>; avoid <b>peppermint, chocolate, fatty food, caffeine, carbonated drinks, alcohol and tobacco</b>; <b>no eating 3 hours before bed</b>; <b>HOB on blocks or a wedge</b>; <b>upright an hour after meals</b>; no straining, lifting or bending; loose clothes; weight loss; antacids, <b>H₂ blockers or PPIs</b> as ordered (PPI fracture risk in older adults); LINX → <b>MRI alert</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">After surgery</div>fundoplication: <b>HOB ≥ 30°</b>, early walking, splint and cough; <b>anchor the NG tube</b> (never reinsert blindly), keep it patent; supervise first feedings; gas-bloat teaching. Esophagectomy: ventilator then <b>TCDB every 1–2 hours</b>; watch BP, fluid overload and <b>A-fib</b>; <b>don't irrigate or move the NG tube</b>; J-tube feeds without residual checks; <b>fever, fluid collection, tachycardia or tachypnea → surgeon and Rapid Response</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>mouth healing and pain controlled; swallowing safe with no coughing or choking and clear lung sounds; reflux symptoms reduced with the reflux diet (no peppermint, chocolate, fatty food, caffeine or carbonated drinks), small meals with nothing in the 3 hours before bed, the head of the bed raised, and drugs; weight stable; after surgery, lungs clear, NG drainage as expected, no signs of leak; the patient can state which drugs to swish and spit, the GERD rules, and the symptoms to report (dysphagia, chest pain, fever, weight loss).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"47-overview","ch":"47","chTitle":"Stomach conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">47 · Chapter 47</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Infection · inflammation · nutrition · pain</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Stomach conditions</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> The stomach survives its own acid only because of a <b>protective mucosal barrier</b> kept up by <b>prostaglandins</b>. Break the barrier — with <b>NSAIDs</b>, alcohol, smoking or <b><i>Helicobacter pylori</b></i> — and acid and pepsin injure the lining: <b>gastritis</b> if it is inflamed, <b>peptic ulcer disease (PUD)</b> — the infection exemplar — if it is eroded through. The emergencies are the same in both: <b>upper GI bleeding</b>, <b>perforation</b> with peritonitis, and <b>gastric outlet obstruction</b>. The chapter ends with <b>gastric cancer</b> and the life after a gastrectomy, including <b>dumping syndrome</b>.</p><div id=\"ch47-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Parietal cells make acid and intrinsic factor; prostaglandins keep the mucus-bicarbonate barrier and blood flow that stop the stomach digesting itself.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>NSAIDs</b> block prostaglandins; <b><i>H. pylori</b></i> burrows under the mucus and neutralizes acid around itself while inflammation damages the lining; acid back-diffuses → edema, bleeding, erosion, ulcer.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Epigastric burning or gnawing pain (dyspepsia), nausea, early fullness, anorexia; <b>hematemesis</b> (red or coffee-ground) and <b>melena</b>; dizziness on standing; a rigid board-like abdomen if perforated.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>EGD with biopsy</b> (gold standard; rapid urease test); <i>H. pylori</i> serology, <b>urea breath test</b>, <b>stool antigen</b>; Hgb/Hct; stool occult blood; nuclear bleeding scan.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Remove the cause; <b>PPI-based triple or quadruple therapy</b> for <i>H. pylori</i>; PPIs, H₂ blockers, antacids, sucralfate; endoscopic hemostasis; surgery for perforation or bleeding that won't stop.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Hemorrhage (most serious), perforation with peritonitis and sepsis, pyloric obstruction with metabolic alkalosis, pernicious anemia, gastric cancer.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Hematemesis or melena with <b>falling BP, rising pulse</b>, confusion; <b>sudden severe epigastric pain spreading over the abdomen</b> with a rigid abdomen; persistent vomiting.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">NSAID or <i>H. pylori</i><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>prostaglandin barrier fails<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>acid and pepsin reach the mucosa<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>ulcer erodes into a vessel or through the wall<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>bleeding or perforation</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Gastric vs duodenal ulcer (Table 47.2)</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Gastric</b>: age 50–70, normal or low acid, pain <b>30–60 minutes after a meal</b>, <b>worsened by food</b>, rarely at night, more <b>hematemesis</b>, slightly higher cancer risk. <b>Duodenal</b>: age 20–50, more men, type O, <b>acid hypersecretion</b>, pain <b>1½–3 hours after meals</b> and <b>1–2 a.m.</b>, <b>relieved by food</b>, more <b>melena</b>, recurs often.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Early vs late dumping</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Early</b> (within ~20 min): fluid rushes into the jejunum → <b>vertigo, tachycardia, sweating, pallor, palpitations, syncope, wanting to lie down</b>. <b>Late</b> (1–3 h): sugar spike → <b>too much insulin</b> → <b>hypoglycemia</b> symptoms (dizziness, sweating, confusion).</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Aluminum vs magnesium antacids</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Aluminum → <b>constipation</b>, safe in kidney disease (binds phosphate). Magnesium → <b>diarrhea</b>, <b>avoid in kidney disease and heart failure</b> (hypermagnesemia). Combined, they cancel each other out.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">PPIs need food to switch on the pump they block → <b>30 minutes before the main meal</b>. Antacids bind other drugs → <b>space them 1–2 hours apart</b>. Bismuth is a salicylate → <b>no aspirin</b>, and it <b>blackens stool and tongue</b> harmlessly. <a href=\"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Vital signs and orthostatics, hemoglobin and hematocrit, stool and vomitus for blood, pain character, abdomen for rigidity, NG drainage, intake and output, electrolytes (K⁺, Na⁺) with vomiting or suction.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Upper GI bleeding → <b>ABCs, oxygen, two large-bore IVs</b>, isotonic fluids, labs, type and crossmatch. Sudden severe pain with a board-like abdomen → perforation (surgical emergency). After gastric surgery: a blocked NG tube with pain, fullness, hiccups, tachycardia and hypotension (acute gastric dilation).</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The mucosal barrier</b> — <b>prostaglandins</b> maintain the protective barrier (mucus, bicarbonate, mucosal blood flow) that keeps the stomach from digesting itself. When the barrier is disrupted, <b>histamine release</b> and <b>vagal stimulation</b> worsen the injury, and <b>hydrochloric acid diffuses back</b> into the mucosa and injures small vessels → <b>edema, bleeding and erosion</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why NSAIDs injure</b> — NSAIDs <b>inhibit prostaglandin production</b> (COX inhibition) — everywhere, not just at the site of pain — so the barrier thins wherever acid touches it. Steroids, aldosterone antagonists and SSRIs also contribute to ulceration.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>How <i>H. pylori</i> survives acid</b> — spread <b>oral-to-oral or fecal-to-oral</b>. Its <b>urease</b> turns urea into <b>ammonia</b>, which <b>neutralizes</b> the acid around it; its <b>helical shape</b> lets it <b>burrow into the mucus layer</b>, hidden from immune cells. The body's response — neutrophils, lymphocytes, cytokines — damages the lining. Most carriers never get sick, but it is a <b>major risk for ulcers and gastric cancer</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Chronic gastritis and B₁₂</b> — patchy chronic inflammation slowly <b>thins and atrophies</b> the stomach wall. <b>Parietal cells</b> fail, acid falls, and <b>intrinsic factor</b> is lost — without it <b>vitamin B₁₂</b> can't be absorbed and, once stores run out, <b>pernicious anemia</b> follows. <i>H. pylori</i> is the most common cause; alcohol, radiation, smoking, bile reflux after pyloric surgery, Crohn's and uremia also contribute.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>This is why the ulcer drugs come in three kinds: <b>lower the acid</b> (PPIs, H₂ blockers, antacids), <b>rebuild the barrier</b> (sucralfate, misoprostol, bismuth), and <b>remove the cause</b> (antibiotics for <i>H. pylori</i>, stopping NSAIDs). Healing an ulcer without killing <i>H. pylori</i> is why so many ulcers used to come back.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"47-gastritis","ch":"47","chTitle":"Stomach conditions","title":"Gastritis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Gastritis</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Acute gastritis</b> — inflammation of the mucosa or submucosa from local irritants: a thick, red mucosa with prominent folds and some necrosis. If the muscle layer isn't involved it <b>heals in a few days</b>; if it is, <b>bleeding or hemorrhage</b> can occur. Causes: <b>long-term NSAIDs</b> (the big one), <b>alcohol, coffee and caffeine, stress, smoking</b>, radiation, swallowed <b>corrosives</b> (acids, alkalis like lye and drain cleaner), steroids, aldosterone antagonists, SSRIs. Food poisoning from <b>staphylococcal endotoxin</b> starts abruptly with severe vomiting within hours.</div>\n<div><b>Acute signs</b> — <b>rapid onset of epigastric pain and dyspepsia</b>; bleeding shows as <b>hematemesis</b> (bright red or coffee-ground) or <b>melena</b> (black, tarry). Gastric <b>hemorrhage</b> can be the first sign — a life-threatening emergency.</div>\n<div><b>Chronic gastritis</b> — few symptoms unless it ulcerates: nausea, vomiting, upper abdominal discomfort, epigastric pain <b>after meals</b>, anorexia. The most common cause is <b><i>H. pylori</b></i> (the more organisms, the more damage). Chronic <i>H. pylori</i> and <b>autoimmune gastritis</b> raise the risk of <b>gastric cancer</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnosis</b> — <b>EGD with biopsy is the gold standard</b> — symptoms alone can't make the diagnosis, and the biopsy names the type (classed by cause, cell changes or lesion pattern, and as <b>erosive</b> — ulcerating — or <b>nonerosive</b>); several biopsies if lesions are patchy; cytology rules out cancer; tissue tested for <i>H. pylori</i> (<b>rapid urease test</b>, culture, PCR). Results are more reliable if <b>antacids and PPIs are stopped for at least a week</b> before.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — usually managed in the community; <b>admitted</b> only for a flare with <b>fluid and electrolyte imbalance, bleeding or worsening pain</b>. <b>Remove the cause</b> — the main treatment for acute gastritis; supportive care; it usually heals within days. Bleeding → transfusion and fluids; major bleeding or ulceration may need partial gastrectomy, pyloroplasty or vagotomy. Diet: avoid what causes distress — <b>caffeine, acidic foods (tomato, citrus), bell peppers and onions</b>; <b>bland, nonspicy, smaller and more frequent meals</b>; no alcohol or tobacco. Drugs: <b>famotidine</b> (H₂ blocker), <b>sucralfate</b>, <b>omeprazole or pantoprazole</b>, aluminum-magnesium antacids. <b>Calcium carbonate</b> is a potent antacid but triggers <b>gastrin</b> → <b>rebound acid</b>. Chronic gastritis may need <b>vitamin B₁₂ injections</b>; treat <i>H. pylori</i> if present.</div>\n<div><b>Prevention (Box 47.1)</b> — balanced diet and exercise; <b>limit alcohol</b>; <b>avoid long-term aspirin, NSAIDs and corticosteroids</b>; avoid excess coffee (<b>even decaf</b>); safe food and water; <b>manage stress</b> (relaxation, meditation, yoga); <b>stop smoking</b>; protect against workplace toxins (lead, nickel); get GERD treated. Respect patients who can't easily do these (money, time, family duties).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Even decaffeinated coffee is on the avoid list because coffee itself — not just its caffeine — stimulates gastric acid secretion. The rule is about the stomach's acid output, not the stimulant.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"47-ulcer-and-gastritis-drugs-table-47-1","ch":"47","chTitle":"Stomach conditions","title":"Ulcer and gastritis drugs (Table 47.1)","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Ulcer and gastritis drugs (Table 47.1)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Antacids</b> — raise gastric pH and deactivate pepsin. <b>Liquid suspensions work better than chewable tablets.</b> <b>No other drugs within 1–2 hours</b> (they block absorption). <b>Magnesium hydroxide with aluminum hydroxide</b>: give <b>2 hours after meals and at bedtime</b>; watch for <b>diarrhea</b>; check for <b>chronic kidney disease or heart failure</b> — poor kidney excretion causes <b>hypermagnesemia</b>. <b>Aluminum hydroxide</b>: give <b>1 hour after meals and at bedtime</b>; causes <b>constipation</b> (alternate with a magnesium antacid); <b>safe in CKD</b> — it binds phosphate and has no magnesium.</div>\n<div><b>H₂ blockers</b> — <b>famotidine, nizatidine</b> block <b>histamine receptors on parietal cells</b> → less acid. A <b>single dose at bedtime</b> suppresses <b>night-time acid</b> for heartburn and PUD. <b>IV famotidine</b> prevents surgical <b>stress ulcers</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Mucosal protectors</b> — <b>sucralfate</b> coats the ulcer: give <b>1 hour before and 2 hours after meals and at bedtime</b> (food interferes), and <b>not within 30 minutes of antacids</b> or other drugs. <b>Bismuth subsalicylate</b> stops <i>H. pylori</i> from binding to the lining and boosts mucosal protection and prostaglandin production. It is a salicylate — <b>no aspirin</b> with it (salicylate overdose); it <b>turns stool and tongue black</b> — temporary and harmless.</div>\n<div><b>PPIs — the drug of choice</b> — <b>omeprazole, lansoprazole, dexlansoprazole, esomeprazole, rabeprazole, pantoprazole</b> suppress the <b>H⁺/K⁺-ATPase (proton pump)</b>. Give <b>30 minutes before the main meal</b> (food activates the pump the drug blocks) — rabeprazole <b>after the morning meal</b>. <b>Don't crush</b> delayed-release capsules or enteric-coated tablets (rabeprazole, pantoprazole). <b>IV pantoprazole</b>: on a pump <b>with a filter, in a separate line</b>, with <b>no other IV drugs</b> (precipitates). Esomeprazole: low dose with severe liver disease. Watch <b>drug interactions</b> (they change how other drugs are metabolized). <b>Don't stop abruptly — step down over several days</b> (rebound); reassess long-term use (<b>osteoporotic fractures</b>).</div>\n<div><b>PPIs through a feeding tube</b> — omeprazole, lansoprazole and dexlansoprazole can be <b>dissolved in sodium bicarbonate</b> (protects the granules from acid) for any feeding tube; capsules can be opened into <b>apple or orange juice</b> for a large-bore tube; esomeprazole, omeprazole and pantoprazole come as <b>oral suspensions</b>; esomeprazole and pantoprazole come <b>IV</b> for NPO patients.</div>\n<div><b>Misoprostol</b> — a <b>prostaglandin analog</b>: protects the mucosa and lowers acid — mainly for patients who must take <b>NSAIDs</b>. Avoid <b>magnesium antacids</b> (diarrhea). <b>Never in pregnancy</b> — it can cause <b>abortion, premature birth or birth defects</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Antibiotics for <i>H. pylori</i></b> — <b>clarithromycin</b>: caution in kidney impairment (watch BUN). <b>Amoxicillin</b>: <b>with food</b> (GI upset). <b>Tetracycline</b>: <b>1 hour before or 2 hours after meals</b> (dairy blocks absorption); <b>sunscreen</b> (photosensitivity). <b>Metronidazole</b>: <b>with food</b>; <b>no alcohol during and for at least 3 days after</b> — severe nausea, vomiting and headache (drug-alcohol reaction).</div>\n<div><b>Regimens</b> — <b>PPI-based triple therapy</b>: a PPI (e.g., lansoprazole) <b>plus two antibiotics</b> — metronidazole + tetracycline, or clarithromycin + amoxicillin — for <b>10–14 days</b>. <b>Quadruple therapy</b> adds <b>bismuth</b> to a PPI and two antibiotics — often for <b>penicillin allergy</b>. No single drug eradicates <i>H. pylori</i>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A proton pump inhibitor only blocks pumps that are <b>switched on</b>, and food is the switch. Given 30 minutes before the main meal, the drug is in the blood just as the pumps activate — taken after the meal, many pumps have already done their work. That timing rule is the most commonly missed PPI teaching point.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"47-peptic-ulcer-disease-the-infection-exemp","ch":"47","chTitle":"Stomach conditions","title":"Peptic ulcer disease — the infection exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Definition and cause</b> — mucosal defenses fail and <b>acid and pepsin</b> erode the epithelium. Most ulcers come from <b><i>H. pylori</b></i> or <b>long-term NSAIDs</b>; other risks are the same as gastritis (alcohol, smoking, stress, caffeine). Duodenal ulcers often run in <b>families</b>. About <b>4.5 million</b> Americans a year; visits, admissions and deaths have fallen with <b>PPIs</b> and early <i>H. pylori</i> treatment.</div>\n<div><b>Duodenal ulcers (most common)</b> — deep, sharply edged ulcers in the <b>upper duodenum</b>, through mucosa and submucosa into the muscle. Main feature: <b>high acid secretion</b> — protein-rich meals, calcium and vagal stimulation drive acid, and fast stomach emptying dumps a large acid load into the duodenum before food can buffer it.</div>\n<div><b>Gastric ulcers</b> — usually in the <b>antrum</b> near acid-secreting mucosa: a break in the barrier (<i>H. pylori</i>) lets acid in; <b>back-diffusion</b> of acid, a weak <b>pyloric sphincter</b> that lets <b>bile reflux</b>, <b>delayed emptying</b> and reduced mucosal blood flow add injury.</div>\n<div><b>Stress ulcers</b> — acute mucosal lesions after a <b>crisis</b>: sepsis, head injury, surgery (NPO patients), <b>burns (Curling ulcer)</b>, sepsis (ischemic ulcer), <b>raised intracranial pressure (Cushing ulcer)</b>. They start as <b>ischemic</b> areas, erode and can <b>hemorrhage</b> massively — the main sign is <b>bleeding</b>. They lengthen stays and raise mortality.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Complications</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Hemorrhage — the most serious</b> — more often with <b>gastric and stress ulcers</b> and in <b>older adults</b>; recurs if <i>H. pylori</i> stays untreated or therapy lacks an H₂ blocker or PPI. Massive bleeding → <b>hematemesis</b> (bright red, or <b>coffee-ground</b> when acid has digested the blood) — bleeding at or above the duodenojejunal junction. Slow bleeding → <b>melena</b> (more common with duodenal ulcers). Box 47.2: hematemesis, melena, <b>falling Hgb/Hct and BP, rising heart rate</b>, weak pulses, <b>acute confusion in older adults</b>, vertigo, dizziness, syncope. Early detection decides the outcome: <b>monitor vital signs often</b> and report changes at once. Common sources of upper GI bleeding (Fig. 47.2): <b>esophageal varices</b>, esophageal cancer, <b>gastritis</b>, <b>gastric ulcer</b>, gastric cancer, <b>duodenal ulcer</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Perforation</b> — the ulcer erodes through the <b>full thickness</b> and contents spill into the peritoneum: <b>sudden, sharp mid-epigastric pain spreading over the whole abdomen</b>; <b>tender, rigid, board-like abdomen</b> (<b>peritonitis</b>), rebound tenderness; the patient lies in a <b>fetal position</b>; bowel sounds hyperactive then diminishing → <b>paralytic ileus</b>; <b>septicemia and hypovolemic shock</b> within hours. <b>A surgical emergency.</b></div>\n<div><b>Pyloric (gastric outlet) obstruction</b> — scarring, edema or inflammation block the pylorus: <b>bloating, nausea, vomiting</b> of retained food. Persistent vomiting loses acid (H⁺ and Cl⁻) → <b>metabolic alkalosis</b> and <b>hypokalemia</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Intractable disease</b> — recurrent pain despite treatment — from complications, life stressors or trouble with long-term therapy → refer to gastroenterology.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Assessment and diagnosis</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — past <i>H. pylori</i>; all drugs — <b>NSAIDs, corticosteroids, chemotherapy</b>; radiation; GI surgery (partial gastrectomy, <b>bariatric</b>); alcohol, tobacco, foods that worsen symptoms; stressors; pain pattern with eating and sleep and what relieves it. <b>A change in the pain</b> — intermittent and relieved by food or antacids becoming <b>constant and radiating to the back</b> — suggests <b>perforation</b>. Many people with active ulcers have <b>no symptoms</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Physical</b> — <b>epigastric tenderness</b> at the midline between umbilicus and xiphoid; <b>dyspepsia</b> — sharp, burning or gnawing, or pressure, fullness or hunger. Perforation: <b>rigid board-like abdomen, rebound tenderness, intense pain</b>. <b>Orthostatic BP</b> and dehydration signs for blood loss — <b>dizziness on standing</b>; older adults risk <b>falls</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Gastric vs duodenal (Table 47.2)</b> — <b>gastric</b>: age <b>50–70</b>; men and women equally; no blood-group link; <b>normal or low acid</b>; pain <b>30–60 minutes after a meal</b>, rarely at night, <b>worsened by eating</b>; bleeds more as <b>hematemesis</b> than melena; cancer risk raised but under 5%; heals with treatment but tends to <b>recur in the same spot</b>; the surrounding mucosa shows <b>atrophic gastritis</b>. <b>Duodenal</b>: age <b>20–50</b>; more often <b>men</b>; most often <b>blood type O</b>; <b>acid hypersecretion</b>; <b>family history</b>; pain <b>1½–3 hours after a meal</b> and often <b>wakes the patient at 1–2 a.m.</b>; <b>relieved by eating</b>; bleeds more as <b>melena</b>; no added cancer risk; remissions and flare-ups — <b>60% recur within 1 year, 90% within 2</b>; no surrounding gastritis.</div>\n<div><b>Psychosocial</b> — how the ulcer affects <b>lifestyle, work, family, social and leisure life</b>, and how lifestyle changes will affect the patient and family. This shows whether the patient can <b>adhere to treatment</b> and has the <b>social support</b> to change habits — unstable housing, no money for food or drugs, or depression are <b>barriers</b> to follow up.</div>\n<div><b>Tests for <i>H. pylori</i></b> — <b>serology</b> (antibodies) is the most common; the <b>urea breath test</b> (swallow ¹³C urea, exhale — labeled carbon means urease is present) and <b>stool antigen</b> are <b>more accurate</b>. <b>EGD is the most accurate test for PUD</b>: it shows the crater and takes biopsies (rapid urease test, cancer cytology); may be repeated every <b>4–6 weeks</b> to check healing. <b>Nuclear scan</b> (technetium) finds bleeding — no prep; a repeat scan <b>1–2 days after treatment</b> shows whether it worked. Bleeding → low <b>Hgb/Hct</b>, stool <b>occult blood</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults</b> — many have <b>undiagnosed <i>H. pylori</b></i> because symptoms are vague; the average age of gastric cancer diagnosis is <b>68</b> — teach PUD symptoms and consider <b>screening</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Treating the ulcer</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Priority problems and goals</b> — <b>acute or persistent pain</b> and <b>potential for upper GI bleeding</b>. Pain goal: <b>3 or less on a 0–10 scale</b>. Drug goals: <b>relieve pain, eradicate <i>H. pylori</i>, heal the ulcer, prevent recurrence</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diet</b> — no diet has been shown to lower acid or heal ulcers; a <b>bland</b> diet may ease symptoms during the acute phase. Food buffers acid for <b>30–60 minutes</b>, then acid <b>rebounds</b>. Avoid foods that cause discomfort — commonly the same ones as in gastritis: <b>caffeine, acidic foods (tomato, citrus), bell peppers, onions and spicy food</b>; <b>avoid bedtime snacks</b> (stimulate night acid); six small meals are <b>no longer routine</b> (may stimulate acid); <b>no alcohol or tobacco</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Stress</b> — emotional stress worsens GI disorders through the <b>gut-brain axis</b>; teach relaxation, <b>imagery, hypnosis, yoga and meditation</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The pain clock separates the two ulcers. A duodenal ulcer hurts when the duodenum is bathed in acid and nothing buffers it — <b>hours after a meal and in the early morning</b> — and food brings relief. A gastric ulcer sits where the food goes, so eating triggers the pain <b>soon after the meal</b> and food makes it worse.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"47-upper-gi-bleeding-and-ulcer-emergencies","ch":"47","chTitle":"Stomach conditions","title":"Upper GI bleeding and ulcer emergencies","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Upper GI bleeding and ulcer emergencies</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Critical rescue</b> — active upper GI bleeding is <b>life-threatening</b>: priority is <b>airway, breathing and circulation</b> — <b>oxygen</b> and ventilatory support as needed, <b>two large-bore IV lines</b> for fluids and blood, and monitoring of <b>vital signs, hematocrit and SpO₂</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Fluids and blood</b> — start <b>isotonic</b> fluid at once (<b>0.9% normal saline or lactated Ringer's</b>); <b>packed RBCs</b> to restore volume and Hgb/Hct; <b>fresh frozen plasma</b> if the PT is more than <b>1.5 times</b> the control. Watch I&amp;O and, in <b>older adults, fluid overload</b>; check electrolytes and <b>replace</b> what vomiting and NG suction remove (e.g., <b>K⁺</b>); trend <b>Hgb, Hct and coagulation studies</b> against baseline. Under <b>500 mL</b> loss: mild weakness and sweating. <b>Over 1 L in 24 hours</b>: <b>hypovolemic shock</b> — hypotension, chills, palpitations, diaphoresis, weak thready pulse.</div>\n<div><b>NG tube</b> — a <b>large-bore NG tube</b> to: <b>find blood</b> in the stomach, <b>assess the rate</b> of bleeding, <b>prevent gastric dilation</b>, and <b>decompress</b> to rest the GI tract.</div>\n<div><b>Pyloric obstruction</b> — decompress with <b>NG suction</b>, then correct <b>metabolic alkalosis and dehydration</b>. Edema and spasm usually respond to medical treatment. The NG tube is typically <b>clamped after about 72 hours</b> and residual checked: <b>50 mL or less in 30 minutes</b> (or the ordered limit) → oral fluids may start. Some need surgery.</div>\n<div><b>Endoscopic hemostasis</b> — EGD stops bleeding by <b>injecting chemicals</b>, applying <b>heat, electric current or laser</b>, or <b>banding or clipping</b> the vessel. Before: one or two <b>large-bore IVs</b> (moderate sedation, e.g., <b>midazolam</b> plus an opioid; transfusion), <b>NPO 4–6 hours</b>, signed <b>consent</b>. <b>After: VS, heart rhythm and SpO₂ until baseline; check swallowing — no food or fluid until the gag reflex returns</b> (the throat was numbed).</div>\n<div><b>If bleeding continues</b> — <b>catheter-directed embolization</b> by interventional radiology (usually femoral access; an arteriogram finds the bleeder and material is injected to stop it) — care like any percutaneous vascular procedure. Then <b>aggressive acid suppression with PPIs</b> to <b>stabilize the clot</b> by raising gastric pH and prevent rebleeding; once bleeding stops, PPIs are also the main drug for <b>healing the ulcer</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Surgery</b> — now less common thanks to <i>H. pylori</i> treatment and endoscopy: for ulcers that <b>don't respond</b> or <b>emergencies like perforation</b>. <b>Minimally invasive</b> (laparoscopic) when possible — less pain, shorter stay, fewer complications. Procedures: <b>subtotal gastrectomy</b>, <b>pyloroplasty</b> (opens the pylorus), <b>vagotomy</b> (cuts the vagus to reduce acid).</div>\n<div><b>Going home</b> — discharge when there is <b>no ongoing bleeding, no orthostatic changes and no cardiopulmonary distress</b>. After major surgery or hemorrhage, one or two <b>home care visits</b>, especially for <b>older adults</b>. Home care (Box 47.3): VS with orthostatics, skin color, pain, <b>stool color and consistency</b>, bowel pattern, <b>Hgb/Hct</b>, bowel sounds; diet and <b>caffeine and alcohol</b>; understanding of drugs (<b>avoid NSAIDs</b>), smoking cessation. <b>Action alert — seek care at once</b> for <b>sudden, sharp, severe epigastric or abdominal pain</b>, <b>bloody or black stools</b>, or <b>bloody or coffee-ground vomit</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — no active PUD or <i>H. pylori</i>; pain relieved; the drugs are taken as prescribed (including the full <i>H. pylori</i> course) and the client avoids NSAIDs, alcohol, tobacco, bedtime snacks and the foods that cause pain; no upper GI bleeding — or bleeding controlled promptly.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/><b>Coffee-ground</b> vomitus means blood has sat in the stomach long enough for acid to turn hemoglobin brown — bleeding that is slower or older. <b>Bright red</b> means fast, fresh bleeding. <b>Melena</b> is blood digested on its way through the small intestine. Reading the color tells you how fast and how far up the bleeding is.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"47-gastric-cancer-and-life-after-gastrectom","ch":"47","chTitle":"Stomach conditions","title":"Gastric cancer and life after gastrectomy","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Gastric cancer</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The disease</b> — mostly <b>adenocarcinoma</b> of the mucosal glands; more common in <b>men</b>, rising sharply <b>after 65</b>. Precancerous: <b>atrophic gastritis</b> and <b>intestinal metaplasia</b> — low acid lets bacteria (<i>H. pylori</i>) multiply; it can also cause <b>MALT lymphoma</b>. Spreads through the wall to <b>lymph nodes</b>, directly into the liver, pancreas and colon, through the <b>portal vein to the liver</b> and blood to <b>lungs and bones</b> (blood-borne spread is the <b>most common</b> route of metastasis), and by <b>peritoneal seeding</b>. <b>5-year survival ~30%</b> (late diagnosis) but <b>~70%</b> if found early.</div>\n<div><b>Risk factors</b> — <b><i>H. pylori</b></i> is the <b>largest</b> (CagA gene). <b>Pernicious anemia, gastric polyps, chronic atrophic gastritis and achlorhydria</b> raise risk 2–3 times. Diet: <b>pickled foods, nitrates in processed food, added salt</b>, <b>few fruits and vegetables</b> (screen for <b>food insecurity</b> and link the patient to community food resources). Past <b>gastric surgery</b>; <b>Barrett's esophagus</b> (cancer at the cardia). Higher incidence and mortality in <b>non-White groups</b>, especially Black and Hispanic people, and highest in <b>Korean and Japanese Americans</b> — smoking, untreated <i>H. pylori</i>, poor access to care and to pre-surgery chemotherapy.</div>\n<div><b>Signs (Box 47.4)</b> — <b>early</b>: <b>chronic dyspepsia</b> and abdominal discomfort <b>relieved by antacids at first</b> (often ignored), fullness, epigastric, back or retrosternal pain. <b>Advanced</b>: <b>nausea and vomiting</b>, <b>iron deficiency anemia</b>, a <b>palpable epigastric mass</b>, <b>enlarged lymph nodes</b>, weakness, fatigue, <b>progressive weight loss</b>. Labs: low Hgb/Hct (micro- or macrocytic), <b>occult blood</b>, low albumin, abnormal liver tests with metastasis, <b>elevated CEA</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnose and treat</b> — <b>EGD with biopsy</b> (definitive) plus <b>endoscopic ultrasound</b> for depth and nodes; CT, PET and MRI for extent. Treatment depends on the <b>stage</b>: <b>chemotherapy</b> before and after surgery (oxaliplatin, fluorouracil, cisplatin, epirubicin — marrow suppression, nausea, vomiting); chemo and radiation <b>prolong survival</b> in advanced disease, but <b>radiation</b> is limited (spread, organ tolerance). <b>Surgery is preferred</b>: <b>subtotal gastrectomy</b> for mid or distal tumors, <b>total gastrectomy</b> (esophagus joined to the jejunum or duodenum; Fig. 47.3) for proximal tumors, with omentum and nodes; in early stages <b>laparoscopic resection plus chemo or radiation may cure</b>, and <b>laparoscopic + ERAS</b> recovers faster. Spread to supraclavicular or inguinal nodes, liver, umbilicus or perirectal wall means <b>cure is no longer possible</b> — <b>palliative</b> surgery (e.g., <b>gastroenterostomy</b> for outlet obstruction) for obstruction, bleeding or pain.</div>\n<div><b>Before surgery</b> — open surgery: an <b>NG tube to suction</b> empties the stomach so secretions don't contaminate the peritoneum, and stays a few days after (prevents vomiting and distention). Laparoscopic surgery doesn't need one. Correct <b>malnutrition</b> first — enteral supplements or <b>TPN</b>, vitamins, minerals, <b>iron and protein</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">After gastric surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Routine care</b> — prevent <b>atelectasis, paralytic ileus, wound infection and peritonitis</b>: lung sounds, <b>return of bowel sounds</b>, vital signs for infection or bleeding, aggressive pulmonary exercises and <b>early walking</b> (lungs, DVT), incision checks <b>every 8–12 hours</b>, <b>head of bed up</b> (reflux and aspiration).</div>\n<div><b>Acute gastric dilation</b> — a <b>clogged NG tube</b> lets the stomach distend: <b>epigastric pain, fullness, hiccups, tachycardia, hypotension</b> → notify the surgeon for an order to <b>irrigate or replace</b> the tube.</div>\n<div><b>Early dumping syndrome</b> — vasomotor symptoms <b>within about 20 minutes</b> of eating as food rushes into the small intestine and <b>pulls fluid into the gut</b> → distention and a drop in circulating volume: <b>vertigo, tachycardia, syncope, sweating, pallor, palpitations, the urge to lie down</b>. Report and <b>have the patient lie down</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Late dumping syndrome</b> — <b>1–3 hours</b> after eating: carbohydrate enters the jejunum fast → glucose spikes → <b>too much insulin</b> → <b>hypoglycemia</b>: <b>dizziness, light-headedness, palpitations, diaphoresis, confusion</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Managing dumping</b> — <b>small amounts at a time</b>; <b>no liquids with meals</b>; <b>high-protein, high-fat, low- to moderate-carbohydrate</b> diet (with the dietitian); <b>avoid concentrated sweets</b>; <b>lie down flat for a short time after eating</b> (slows emptying). Drugs: <b>acarbose</b> (slows carbohydrate absorption); in severe cases <b>octreotide</b> (somatostatin analog) 2–4 times a day <b>30 minutes before meals</b> to slow transit and reduce gut hormones.</div>\n<div><b>Other problems</b> — <b>delayed gastric emptying</b> for about a week (edema at the anastomosis, adhesions, or low K⁺, protein or Na⁺) — resolves with NG suction, fluids, electrolytes and nutrition. <b>Vitamin B₁₂, folic acid and iron deficiency</b>, poor <b>calcium and vitamin D</b> absorption — less <b>intrinsic factor</b> and fast transit. Watch for <b>pernicious anemia</b>, <b>atrophic glossitis</b> (shiny, smooth, <b>\"beefy\" tongue</b>), <b>megaloblastic anemia and leukopenia</b> on the CBC → <b>vitamin B₁₂</b> (plus folic acid or iron).</div>\n<div><b>Going home</b> — total gastrectomy or advanced disease → maximal help at home, transitional care or a skilled facility; subtotal gastrectomy without debility → home with partial help with ADLs; <b>recurrence is common</b> — regular follow-up and imaging, coordinated with the case manager. Teach dressing changes and <b>infection signs</b>; chemo and radiation side effects and <b>antiemetics</b>. Diet: <b>small, frequent meals</b>, <b>no liquids with meals</b>, <b>no concentrated sweets</b> (dumping), avoid foods that cause discomfort, <b>no caffeine or alcohol</b>, stop smoking, <b>B₁₂ injections as prescribed</b>, <b>lie flat briefly after eating</b>. Support coping; <b>hospice</b> and end-of-life care when needed; home care, dietitian, counselor or spiritual referral; cancer support groups.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Without a stomach to hold food and release it slowly, a meal hits the small intestine all at once. The concentrated contents <b>draw water into the bowel</b> (early dumping looks like a sudden drop in blood volume), and the sugar is absorbed in a rush that provokes an <b>insulin overshoot</b> an hour or two later (late dumping looks like hypoglycemia). Every dumping rule — small meals, no fluids with meals, little sugar, more protein and fat, lying down — <b>slows the delivery</b>.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"47-adpie","ch":"47","chTitle":"Stomach conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Stomach care is about <b>acid, blood and the barrier</b>: find what broke the barrier (NSAIDs, <i>H. pylori</i>, alcohol, smoking), protect against the bleeding and perforation it can cause, heal it with the right drug at the right time, and — after a gastrectomy — rebuild eating habits around a stomach that can no longer hold a meal.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div><b>NSAIDs, aspirin, steroids</b>, SSRIs, chemotherapy; <i>H. pylori</i> history; <b>alcohol, tobacco, caffeine</b>; stress; diet (pickled, salted, processed foods; fruit and vegetable intake; <b>food insecurity</b>); GI and <b>bariatric</b> surgery; family history; <b>pain pattern</b> with meals and night waking and what relieves it; <b>any change in pain</b> (constant, radiating to the back). <b>Psychosocial</b>: effect on work and family, support, and <b>barriers to adherence (housing, money, mental health)</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical and labs</div>epigastric tenderness; abdomen for <b>rigidity and rebound</b>; bowel sounds; <b>vital signs and orthostatics</b>; skin color; <b>vomitus and stool</b> for red, coffee-ground or black blood; <b>Hgb/Hct</b>, BUN, Na⁺, K⁺, WBC; <i>H. pylori</i> tests; signs of <b>B₁₂ deficiency</b> (beefy tongue).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>BUN rising with a normal creatinine in a vomiting or bleeding patient is a clue in itself: digested blood is a protein load the liver turns into urea, and dehydration concentrates it — so a high BUN supports an upper GI bleed.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>PUD: <b>acute or persistent pain</b> and <b>potential for upper GI bleeding</b> (and perforation, obstruction). Active bleeding: <b>hypovolemia</b> — the priority over pain and vomiting. Gastritis: pain and nutrition. After gastrectomy: <b>dumping</b>, <b>nutrition deficits</b> (B₁₂, iron, folate, calcium), respiratory and wound complications. Gastric cancer: grief, coping, nutrition.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>pain <b>≤ 3/10</b>; <i>H. pylori</i> eradicated and the ulcer healed; <b>no bleeding</b> — or bleeding controlled with vital signs back to normal; stable weight; after gastrectomy, <b>no dumping</b> symptoms with the meal plan and normal B₁₂ and CBC.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Drugs, timed right</div><b>triple or quadruple therapy</b> for 10–14 days — stress <b>finishing it</b>; <b>PPI 30 minutes before the main meal</b>, don't crush, taper rather than stop; <b>antacids 1–2 hours from other drugs</b>; <b>sucralfate 1 hour before meals, 30 minutes from antacids</b>; <b>no aspirin with bismuth</b> (black stool is harmless); <b>metronidazole — no alcohol</b>; <b>misoprostol never in pregnancy</b>; <b>avoid NSAIDs</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Bleeding</div><b>ABCs</b>, <b>oxygen</b>, <b>two large-bore IVs</b>, <b>NS or LR</b>, PRBCs (FFP if PT &gt; 1.5×), <b>large-bore NG</b>, VS, <b>Hct and SpO₂</b>, I&amp;O, electrolytes (<b>replace K⁺</b> lost to vomiting and suction); prepare for <b>EGD</b> (NPO 4–6 h, consent) and afterward <b>nothing by mouth until the gag reflex returns</b>; <b>PPIs</b> to stabilize the clot; watch for <b>perforation</b> (sudden pain, rigid abdomen).</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Diet and lifestyle</div>bland during flares, avoid irritating foods (<b>caffeine, tomato, citrus, bell peppers, onions, spicy food</b>), <b>no bedtime snacks</b>, <b>no alcohol or tobacco</b>, stress reduction; after gastrectomy — <b>small meals, no fluids with meals, high protein and fat, low sugar, lie down after eating</b>, <b>B₁₂ injections</b>, acarbose or octreotide if ordered.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>pain controlled; stools and vomitus free of blood; <b>Hgb/Hct, BP and pulse stable</b> with no orthostatic drop; <i>H. pylori</i> test negative after treatment; after gastrectomy, eating small meals <b>without dumping</b>, weight stable, B₁₂ given; the patient states when to <b>seek care at once</b> (sudden severe pain, black or bloody stool, coffee-ground vomit).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-overview","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">48 · Chapter 48</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Elimination · fluid &amp; electrolyte balance · pain</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Noninflammatory intestinal conditions</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> This chapter is about the bowel when something <b>blocks, grows in, injures or pushes through</b> it — without primary inflammation. The exemplar is <b>intestinal obstruction</b>, where elimination stops and fluid shifts out of the circulation; next to it sit <b>colorectal cancer</b> (and the <b>colostomy</b> that may follow), <b>irritable bowel syndrome</b>, <b>abdominal trauma</b>, <b>hernias</b> and <b>hemorrhoids</b>. The common thread for the nurse: watch <b>fluid and electrolytes</b>, watch the <b>character of the pain</b> (a change means a complication), and teach the habits — fiber, fluids, no straining — that prevent half of these problems.</p><div id=\"ch48-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Peristalsis moves contents along; the small bowel absorbs nutrients and much of the fluid; the colon absorbs water and electrolytes and stores stool; the abdominal wall holds the contents in.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A physical block (adhesions, tumor, hernia, impaction, volvulus, intussusception) or lost peristalsis (paralytic ileus); cancer from adenomatous polyps; disordered motility (IBS); injury; a weak wall (hernia); swollen veins (hemorrhoids).</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Distention, cramping or constant pain, vomiting (profuse and early when the block is high), obstipation; blood in or on the stool, change in stool shape; a bulge that changes with straining; bruising patterns after trauma.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">CT or MRI, ultrasound, endoscopy; Hgb/Hct, BUN and electrolytes; FIT/FOBT and <b>colonoscopy</b> (definitive for CRC); CEA to monitor; FAST scan in trauma; hydrogen breath test in IBS.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">NPO, <b>NG decompression</b>, IV fluids with potassium, alvimopan for post-op ileus; surgery to relieve obstruction or remove cancer (resection, colostomy, AP resection); chemo; fiber, drugs and stress care for IBS; hernia repair; hemorrhoid care.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Hypovolemic shock, acute kidney injury, <b>strangulation</b> with ischemia, perforation, peritonitis and sepsis; metastasis to the liver; abdominal compartment syndrome; stoma necrosis.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Pain that changes from <b>colicky to constant</b> or stops and becomes tenderness; <b>temperature above 100°F</b> with rising pulse; a hernia that won't reduce; a dark or black stoma; Cullen, Turner or Kehr sign after trauma.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Bowel blocked<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>contents and gas pile up above it<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>peristalsis and secretions increase → more distention<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>bowel wall swells and leaks plasma<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>hypovolemia and electrolyte loss<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>strangulation → ischemia → sepsis</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Small- vs large-bowel obstruction (Box 48.1)</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Small</b>: upper/epigastric distention, visible peristaltic waves in the upper and middle abdomen, <b>early profuse vomiting</b> (may be fecal), obstipation, <b>severe fluid and electrolyte loss</b>, possible <b>metabolic alkalosis</b>. <b>Large</b>: <b>lower distention</b>, intermittent lower cramping, <b>little or no vomiting</b>, <b>ribbon-like stools</b> or obstipation, <b>no major fluid and electrolyte imbalance</b>, possible <b>metabolic acidosis</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Mechanical vs paralytic ileus</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Mechanical</b>: <b>colicky, cramping</b> pain, <b>high-pitched bowel sounds (borborygmi) above the block</b> early, then silence. <b>Paralytic (nonmechanical)</b>: <b>constant, diffuse</b> discomfort, bowel sounds <b>decreased then absent</b>, vomitus rarely foul or profuse.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Right- vs left-sided colon cancer</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Right</b>: liquid stool, so tumors grow large without changing bowel habits — <b>intermittent dark (mahogany) blood</b>, <b>anemia</b>, a mass in the right lower quadrant. <b>Left / rectosigmoid</b>: <b>obstruction</b>, \"gas pains\", <b>narrow stools</b>, <b>bright red blood</b>, straining.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Alvimopan</b> blocks opioid receptors <b>only in the gut</b>, so it restores motility after surgery without taking away pain relief. <b>Alosetron</b> (a 5-HT₃ blocker) slows the gut — and can cause severe constipation or <b>ischemic colitis</b> — so report constipation or bloody diarrhea at once. <a href=\"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">VS and urine output, daily weight (best fluid measure), electrolytes (K⁺, Na⁺, Cl⁻), bowel sounds and flatus, distention, NG output and placement, pain character, stoma color, perineal wound drainage.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Pain that becomes constant or localized, abdominal rigidity, fever &gt; 100°F with tachycardia, hypotension (strangulation, perforation, peritonitis); a <b>purple, black or dry stoma</b>; an irreducible hernia; signs of hemorrhage after trauma.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why obstruction causes hypovolemia</b> — contents (fluid, food, saliva, gastric, pancreatic and biliary secretions, swallowed air) <b>pile up at and above the block</b>. Peristalsis speeds up to push them on, which <b>stimulates more secretions</b> and more distention. The swollen bowel wall becomes <b>leaky</b>: plasma escapes into the peritoneum and fluid is trapped in the lumen, so less returns to the circulation → <b>hypovolemia</b> (mild to shock) and electrolyte loss. Severe hypovolemia can cause <b>acute kidney injury</b> or death.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Acid-base by location</b> — <b>High small-bowel</b> block → loss of gastric <b>hydrochloric acid</b> → <b>metabolic alkalosis</b>. Below the duodenum but above the colon → <b>acids and bases</b> both lost. At the end of the small bowel and below → loss of <b>alkaline</b> fluid → <b>metabolic acidosis</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Strangulation and sepsis</b> — stagnant bacteria in a blocked loop are harmless <b>until blood flow fails</b>: in a <b>closed-loop</b> (blocked at two points) <b>or strangulated</b> obstruction, ischemic bowel releases <b>endotoxins</b> into the peritoneum and blood → <b>peritonitis and septic shock</b>; blood is lost into the bowel and peritoneum; rising pressure can cause <b>abdominal compartment syndrome</b>. Mesenteric arterial occlusion causes the same gangrene and sepsis.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>From polyp to cancer</b> — most colorectal cancers are <b>adenocarcinomas</b> from glandular epithelium. Loss of tumor-suppressor genes and activation of oncogenes speed cell division in the mucosa, forming <b>adenomatous polyps</b> that can turn malignant. <b>Polyps</b> are small mucosa-covered growths on the bowel surface — <b>most are benign</b>, but some can become cancer, so they are removed when seen at sigmoidoscopy or colonoscopy. More than half of cancers arise in the <b>rectosigmoid</b> colon (Fig. 48.2). Spread is by direct extension through the bowel wall, by <b>lymph</b>, by blood — <b>the liver is the most common metastatic site</b> (then lungs, brain, bones, adrenals) — and by <b>peritoneal seeding</b> during surgery.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Pain is the best real-time monitor in this chapter. Colicky, coming-and-going pain is the bowel <b>fighting</b> a block; when it turns <b>constant and localized</b> — or suddenly stops and becomes tenderness — the bowel has stopped fighting because it is <b>losing its blood supply or has perforated</b>. That is why analgesics are sometimes held during the workup: the pain pattern is the diagnosis.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Intestinal obstruction — the elimination exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Causes and who gets it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Mechanical obstruction</b> — the bowel is <b>physically blocked</b> — from outside (<b>adhesions</b>, the most common cause after surgery), in the wall (Crohn's strictures, radiation), or in the lumen (<b>tumors</b>). Causes: adhesions, benign or malignant <b>tumors</b>, appendicitis complications, <b>hernias</b>, <b>fecal impaction</b> (especially older adults), strictures, <b>intussusception</b> (bowel telescoping into itself), <b>volvulus</b> (twisting; both in Fig. 48.1), fibrosis (endometriosis). The <b>ileum</b>, the narrowest part, is the most common site.</div>\n<div><b>Nonmechanical (paralytic ileus)</b> — no physical block — <b>peristalsis decreases or stops</b> from a neuromuscular disturbance. <b>Postoperative ileus</b> from handling the bowel in abdominal surgery lasts hours to days; <b>hypokalemia</b> predisposes to it. Also <b>peritonitis</b>, and <b>intestinal ischemia</b> from mesenteric thrombosis or embolus (→ infarction, gangrene, sepsis).</div>\n<div><b>Who and prevention</b> — most common after <b>bowel surgery</b>, with <b>intestinal tumors</b>, and in <b>older adults</b> (fecal impaction, cancer). Prevent impaction: <b>fluids, exercise, high fiber, stool softener</b> if needed; get <b>colon cancer screening</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — GI disorders and surgeries; <b>nausea and vomiting and its color</b> (green-yellow, bilious, bloody); a full pain assessment — <b>severe pain that stops and becomes tenderness may mean perforation</b> (report promptly); <b>flatus and the last bowel movement</b>; <b>hiccups (singultus)</b> are common. <b>Suspect obstruction → keep NPO and call the provider.</b> Ask about family history of colorectal cancer and blood or change in the stool.</div>\n<div><b>Temperature and vital signs</b> — uncomplicated obstruction rarely raises temperature above <b>100°F (37.8°C)</b>. A higher temperature, with or without guarding and tenderness, and a <b>sustained rise in pulse</b> suggest <b>strangulation, peritonitis or ischemia</b>. Report at once: <b>fever, tachycardia, hypotension, increasing pain, rigidity, or a color change of the abdominal skin</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Small-bowel obstruction</b> — <b>mid-abdominal cramping</b> that comes and goes (comfortable between); <b>localized, persistent</b> pain if strangulated; <b>vomiting</b> — more profuse the higher the block; bile and mucus, or <b>orange-brown and foul (feculent)</b> with a low ileal block. <b>Obstipation</b> and no flatus with complete obstruction; <b>diarrhea</b> may occur with partial obstruction. Prolonged vomiting upsets fluid and electrolyte balance — Box 48.1 lists <b>severe fluid and electrolyte imbalance</b> and possible <b>metabolic alkalosis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Large-bowel obstruction</b> — <b>mild, intermittent, colicky</b> lower abdominal pain; <b>lower distention</b>; <b>minimal or no vomiting</b>; obstipation or <b>ribbon-like stools</b> if partial; bowel changes and <b>blood</b> if the cause is diverticulitis or cancer. Unlike a small-bowel block there is <b>no major fluid and electrolyte imbalance</b>; <b>metabolic acidosis</b> is possible (Box 48.1).</div>\n<div><b>The abdomen</b> — <b>distention</b> in all types; maybe <b>visible peristaltic waves</b>. Mechanical: <b>high-pitched bowel sounds (borborygmi) above the block</b> early as the bowel tries to push through, then <b>absent</b> — especially below it; tenderness and rigidity usually minimal. A tense, fluid-filled loop felt like a mass suggests a <b>closed-loop strangulating</b> obstruction. Nonmechanical: <b>constant diffuse</b> discomfort (severe and constant with vascular causes), bowel sounds <b>decreased then absent</b>, frequent but rarely foul vomiting.</div>\n<div><b>Labs and imaging</b> — no single lab confirms it. <b>WBC normal</b> unless strangulation, infarction or gangrene. <b>Hgb, Hct and BUN rise</b> (dehydration); <b>Na⁺, Cl⁻ and K⁺ fall</b>; <b>amylase</b> may rise with strangulation. <b>CT or MRI</b> as soon as obstruction is suspected (fluid and gas in the small bowel with <b>no gas in the colon</b> = small-bowel obstruction); ultrasound; sigmoidoscopy or colonoscopy — <b>not if perforation or complete obstruction is suspected</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Priority problem</b> — <b>potential for life-threatening complications</b> from reduced or blocked flow of intestinal contents.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Caring for obstruction — the NG tube, fluids and surgery","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Nonsurgical care (Box 48.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>When</b> — partial obstruction without strangulation or ischemia, <b>paralytic ileus</b> (responds very well), and patients with terminal disease. <b>Fecal impaction</b> resolves with <b>disimpaction and enemas</b>; <b>intussusception</b> may reduce with a <b>barium enema</b>.</div>\n<div><b>The NG tube</b> — <b>NPO</b> plus a <b>Salem sump</b> NG tube to <b>low continuous suction</b> to drain fluid and air; its <b>vent (\"pigtail\")</b> keeps the stomach lining from being pulled into the tube. <b>At least every 4 hours</b>: check <b>placement, patency and output</b> (amount and character); check the <b>nasal skin</b>; secure with an approved device. <b>Listen for bowel sounds with the suction turned off</b> (suction noise masks them); assess the abdomen for <b>bowel sounds, distention and flatus at least twice a day</b> (Box 48.2). Less output or stasis, nausea, vomiting or more distention → the tube may be <b>out of position or plugged</b>; a repositioned or new tube may need <b>x-ray confirmation</b>; then aspirate and <b>irrigate with 30 mL normal saline every 4 hours</b> or as ordered. <b>Frequent mouth and nares care</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Fluids and electrolytes</b> — vomiting, NPO and NG suction drain <b>potassium and sodium</b>: <b>IV isotonic fluid (NS or LR), often 2–4 L, with potassium</b>, guided by electrolytes and BUN — careful in older adults with heart or kidney disease (<b>overload</b>). <b>Daily weight is the most reliable fluid measure</b>; also lung sounds and I&amp;O. VS, urine output, turgor and mucous membranes <b>every 2–4 hours</b>. Blood for strangulation; <b>parenteral nutrition</b> if long NPO or malnourished.</div>\n<div><b>Comfort</b> — thirst → <b>frequent mouth care</b>, delegated to assistive personnel (some older adults don't feel thirst); <b>a few ice chips</b> only if no surgery is planned and per protocol. <b>Report pain that increases or changes from colicky to constant</b> (perforation, peritonitis). Analgesics may be <b>held during the workup</b> so perforation signs aren't masked — explain why; <b>morphine slows motility and can cause vomiting</b> (also a sign of a blocked tube or worsening obstruction). Use non-drug comfort, frequent position changes (promote peristalsis) and <b>semi-Fowler</b> (less pressure on the chest, easier breathing). Food and fluids worsen pain; pain is usually <b>milder with ileus</b> than with mechanical obstruction.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs</b> — <b>alvimopan</b> — an oral, <b>peripherally acting mu-opioid antagonist</b> — for short-term use in <b>postoperative ileus</b> (raises GI motility). <b>IV broad-spectrum antibiotics</b> if strangulation is suspected. <b>Metoclopramide</b> (prokinetic) for partial obstruction or ileus.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Surgery and going home</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>When and what</b> — <b>complete</b> mechanical obstruction (and some incomplete) needs surgery; <b>strangulation always does</b>. <b>Exploratory laparotomy</b> finds the cause: <b>adhesions are lysed</b>; tumor or diverticulitis → <b>colon resection</b> with anastomosis or a <b>colostomy</b>; infarction → <b>embolectomy, thrombectomy or resection</b> of gangrenous bowel; severe cases → <b>colectomy</b>. Most now have <b>laparoscopic surgery</b> (longer to do, less blood loss, faster healing, often <b>no NG tube</b>, home in 1–2 days). If the patient is too ill to absorb teaching, reinforce it with the family.</div>\n<div><b>After open surgery</b> — NG tube until <b>peristalsis returns</b>; clear liquids are tried with the tube <b>clamped for 1–2 hours</b> — vomiting means suction resumes; when peristalsis is back the tube is clamped on schedule and removed if there is no nausea.</div>\n<div><b>Prevent fecal impaction (older adults)</b> — <b>high-fiber foods</b> — raw fruit, vegetables, whole grains (mind the cost); <b>plenty of fluids, especially water</b>; <b>avoid routine laxatives</b> (overuse weakens abdominal tone → <b>atonic colon</b>); <b>daily walks</b>; <b>warm drinks and prune juice</b>; <b>bulk-forming products</b> and <b>stool softeners</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Teaching</b> — report <b>abdominal pain or distention, nausea or vomiting</b>, with or without constipation (recurrence) — though recurrent ileus is uncommon. Incision care, activity limits; with opioid analgesics add a <b>stool softener plus laxative</b> (e.g., <b>docusate with senna</b>). Home health for incision or colostomy care if needed. <b>Evaluate</b>: obstruction relieved, no life-threatening complications, usual bowel elimination back.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Listening for bowel sounds with the suction running is a classic error: the suction itself gurgles, so the nurse \"hears\" peristalsis that isn't there. The same logic explains the clamp trial — the only reliable proof that the bowel is working again is that it can <b>move contents forward on its own</b> without the tube draining them.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Colorectal cancer — risk, screening and diagnosis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Risk</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>How common</b> — the <b>4th most common cancer</b> and <b>2nd most common cause of cancer death</b> in US adults — yet <b>highly curable when found early</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Risk factors</b> — <b>age over 50</b>, genetic predisposition, personal or <b>family history</b> (a first-degree relative → <b>3–4 times</b> the risk); a rising number of adults <b>under 50</b> without clear cause; <b>FAP, Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis</b>; possibly <i>H. pylori</i>, <i>Streptococcus bovis</i>, <b>HPV</b>; <b>long-term smoking, obesity, inactivity, heavy alcohol</b>; a <b>high-fat diet</b> (red-meat animal fat → more bile acids and anaerobic bacteria, thought carcinogenic); <b>refined carbohydrates low in fiber</b> (alter transit, favor polyps).</div>\n<div><b>Inherited syndromes</b> — <b>familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)</b>: autosomal dominant <b>APC</b> gene mutation, ~1% of CRC — <b>thousands of polyps</b> over 10–15 years with <b>nearly 100%</b> chance of cancer → most need <b>colectomy (with ileostomy or ileoanal pull-through) by age 20</b>. <b>Lynch syndrome (HNPCC)</b>: autosomal dominant mismatch-repair mutations (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS1, PMS2), 3–5% of CRC — <b>80% lifetime risk</b> at an average age of <b>45</b>, plus endometrial, ovarian, stomach, small-bowel, brain and ureteral cancers. <b>Genetic testing</b> is available — teach families of hereditary CRC to be tested.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Prevention and screening</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Healthy People 2030 (Box 48.3)</b> — the national colorectal cancer objectives: <b>reduce the CRC death rate</b> and <b>increase the proportion of adults screened</b> for CRC.</div>\n<div><b>Screening schedule</b> — discuss at <b>40</b>; average-risk adults from <b>45</b>: <b>FOBT/FIT yearly</b>, <b>stool DNA every 3 years</b>, <b>colonoscopy every 10 years</b>, or <b>flexible sigmoidoscopy or CT colonography every 5 years</b>. Personal or family history → <b>earlier and more often</b>. <b>75–85</b>: optional by life expectancy; <b>over 85</b>: not recommended (treatment risks), and surgery is withheld when CRC is diagnosed after 85 — an ethical question as CRC rises after 80. When everyone has access to needed screening and treatment, the ethical principle of <b>justice</b> is upheld. Screening rates are lowest among the <b>uninsured</b> and those with <b>less than a high school education</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Health equity</b> — <b>5-year survival is lowest for Black people</b>, especially under 50; mortality is <b>30–40% higher in poor regions</b> (Appalachia, parts of the South and Midwest) — lack of screening, insurance, education and modern cancer care. Teach early screening and point to resources.</div>\n<div><b>Lifestyle</b> — less <b>fat, refined carbohydrate and low-fiber food</b>; <b>baked or broiled</b>, high-fiber, low animal fat; more <b>broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, sprouts</b>; healthy weight (obesity is a major cancer risk); <b>stop smoking, limit alcohol, exercise</b>; check <b>food security</b> and access to online cessation programs.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Signs and diagnosis</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Signs</b> — ask about <b>changes in bowel habits or stool shape</b>, constipation, vomiting, <b>blood</b>; fatigue (anemia), fullness, vague pain and <b>weight loss</b> suggest <b>advanced</b> disease. The most common signs: <b>rectal bleeding, anemia, and a change in stool consistency or shape</b> — occult, <b>mahogany (dark)</b> or bright red blood. Ascending colon: gross blood uncommon; descending colon and rectum: common but not massive. Other common sources of lower GI bleeding: <b>ulcerative colitis, polyps, diverticulosis or diverticulitis, hemorrhoids</b> (Fig. 48.3).</div>\n<div><b>By location</b> — <b>transverse and descending</b>: <b>obstruction</b> symptoms — \"gas pains\", cramping, incomplete evacuation. <b>Rectosigmoid</b>: <b>hematochezia</b> (red blood per rectum), straining, <b>narrow stools</b>, dull pain. <b>Right side (ascending)</b>: stool is liquid, so tumors grow <b>large without changing bowel habits</b>; they ulcerate and bleed intermittently (<b>mahogany</b> stool); <b>RLQ mass</b>; <b>anemia</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Stool blood tests</b> — a positive <b>FOBT or FIT</b> means GI bleeding. Some vitamins and drugs cause <b>false positives</b>, so the <b>preparation depends on the type of test</b>: some call for <b>no aspirin, vitamin C, iron or red meat for the 48 hours before</b> and stop antiinflammatory drugs (ibuprofen, corticosteroids, salicylates) for a set period first <i>(ch 45 gives the guaiac test's own schedule — no NSAIDs for 7 days, no red meat or vitamin C over 250 mg for 3 days — and FIT needs no preparation)</i>. For the FOBT, test <b>2–3 samples on 3 consecutive days</b>. A <b>negative result doesn't rule out</b> cancer.</div>\n<div><b>Other tests</b> — <b>CEA</b> (normal <b>&lt; 5 ng/mL</b>) is high in many with CRC but also in other diseases and <b>smokers</b> — used to <b>monitor treatment and detect recurrence</b>. <b>CT colonography (virtual)</b> — no biopsy or treatment possible. <b>Sigmoidoscopy</b> — lower colon, polyps removed. <b>Colonoscopy</b> — rectum to ileocecal valve, polyps removed and biopsied — <b>the definitive test</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Where the tumor sits decides how it shows itself. Stool in the right colon is still <b>liquid</b>, so it flows around a growing mass — the first clue is often <b>anemia</b> from slow bleeding. By the left colon and rectum, stool is <b>formed</b>, so the same mass narrows it, blocks it and streaks it with <b>bright red</b> blood that hasn't had time to darken.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Colorectal cancer — treatment, surgery and colostomy care","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Treatment</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The plan</b> — goal: <b>remove all or as much of the tumor as possible</b>; staging uses <b>TNM</b>. <b>Surgery</b> is primary; <b>adjuvant</b> therapies before or after aim to cure and prevent recurrence.</div>\n<div><b>Radiation</b> — pre-op radiation doesn't improve survival in <b>colon</b> cancer but helps local control; post-op radiation shows no consistent benefit. <b>Rectal</b> cancer: radiation is <b>often part of the plan</b>. <b>Palliative</b> radiation for pain, hemorrhage, obstruction or lung metastases. Side effects: <b>diarrhea, fatigue</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Chemotherapy and targeted drugs</b> — adjuvant chemo for <b>stage II or III</b>: <b>IV 5-fluorouracil with leucovorin (5-FU/LV)</b>, <b>capecitabine</b>, <b>irinotecan</b>, <b>oxaliplatin</b>, usually combined. They don't spare healthy cells: <b>diarrhea, mucositis, leukopenia, mouth ulcers, peripheral neuropathy</b> (some use cannabis for side effects). <b>Bevacizumab</b> (<b>VEGF</b> inhibitor) <b>cuts the tumor's blood supply</b> in metastatic CRC; <b>cetuximab, panitumumab</b> (<b>EGFR</b> inhibitors) block growth signals. <b>Intrahepatic arterial</b> 5-FU for liver metastases; opioids and antiemetics for symptoms.</div>\n<div><b>Surgery</b> — removal with <b>cancer-free margins</b> plus <b>lymph nodes</b> (the number with cancer predicts prognosis). Procedures: <b>colon resection</b> with reanastomosis; partial colectomy with a temporary or permanent <b>colostomy</b>; total colectomy with <b>ileostomy or ileoanal pull-through</b>; <b>abdominoperineal (AP) resection</b> for <b>rectal</b> tumors — removes the <b>sigmoid colon, rectum and anus</b> through abdominal and perineal incisions → <b>permanent colostomy</b>. <b>Laparoscopic</b> resection is common: shorter stay, less pain, faster recovery.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Before surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Preparing the patient</b> — explain incisions and drains; the surgeon may not know until surgery whether a <b>colostomy</b> is needed — the patient is told it is <b>possible</b>. A <b>certified wound, ostomy and continence nurse (CWOCN)</b> teaches ostomy care and <b>marks the stoma site</b> (Box 48.4): knowledge, education, <b>sensory limits</b>, support, work, hobbies, <b>money for supplies</b>; abdominal contour <b>lying, sitting and standing</b>, <b>skinfolds, creases, bony prominences, scars</b>, the <b>belt line</b>, a site the patient can <b>see</b>, ideally in the <b>rectus muscle</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Low rectal surgery</b> — <b>AP resection</b> risks <b>sexual dysfunction and urinary incontinence</b> from nerve damage — the surgeon discusses it before consent; let the patient voice concerns. Teach turning, deep breathing, and the pain plan (<b>PCA</b>, epidural).</div>\n<div><b>Bowel prep and antibiotics</b> — for elective surgery (no obstruction or perforation) a <b>bowel prep</b> (laxatives, enemas or whole-gut lavage) may be ordered — <b>controversial</b>; <b>older adults can become dehydrated</b>. One dose of <b>oral or IV antibiotic</b> before the incision. An NG tube after open surgery (not after laparoscopic); IV or central line for fluids while NPO. After <b>open</b> resection expect the NG tube plus <b>IV PCA for the first 24–36 hours</b>, then a slow diet advance from liquids to solids once the tube is out; after <b>laparoscopic</b> resection, <b>solid food very soon</b>, earlier walking and home in <b>1–2 days</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The colostomy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Types</b> — <b>loop</b> stoma — a loop of (usually <b>transverse</b>) colon brought out and opened, usually <b>temporary</b>, often supported by an <b>external rod</b> (don't dislodge it during pouch changes). <b>End</b> stoma — usually <b>descending or sigmoid</b>, often <b>permanent</b>. <b>Double-barrel</b> (least common) — two stomas: the <b>proximal</b> one (closer to the head) <b>passes stool</b>, the <b>distal</b> one is a <b>mucous fistula</b>. The site follows the tumor (Fig. 48.4): <b>ascending</b> for right-sided tumors, <b>descending</b> for left-sided tumors, <b>sigmoid</b> for rectal tumors; a <b>transverse (double-barrel)</b> colostomy is often made in <b>emergencies</b> such as obstruction or perforation because it is <b>quick to create</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Stoma assessment</b> — after surgery: a <b>clear pouch</b> to see the stoma (or <b>petrolatum gauze</b> to keep it moist, under a dry sterile dressing) — then a pouch system as soon as possible. A healthy stoma is <b>reddish pink to dark red-pink, moist</b>, protruding <b>1–3 cm (usually ~2 cm)</b>; slight <b>edema</b> and a little <b>bleeding</b> are common early and settle in <b>6–8 weeks</b>. <b>Report at once</b>: <b>ischemia or necrosis</b> (<b>dark red, purple or black, dry</b>), <b>continuous heavy bleeding</b>, <b>mucocutaneous separation</b> (the suture line between stoma and skin breaking down).</div>\n<div><b>Output</b> — the colostomy usually starts working <b>2–3 days</b> after surgery, with lots of gas at first. <b>Empty the pouch at one-third to one-half full</b>. Stool consistency follows location: <b>ascending → liquid</b>, <b>transverse → pasty</b>, <b>descending → formed</b>. An <b>ascending</b> colostomy keeps draining <b>liquid</b> stool, so its pouch is <b>worn at all times</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Peristomal skin and fit</b> — skin should be <b>intact, smooth, not red</b>. Watch for <b>irritant dermatitis</b> (stool), <b>skin stripping</b> (adhesive), <b>candidiasis</b> (under the wafer). Choose the pouch for the abdomen: flat firm → flexible or nonflexible; creases → <b>flexible</b>; deep creases, soft abdomen, <b>retracted, flush or concave stoma</b> → <b>convex appliance with a belt</b> (pushes the stoma out, prevents leaks). The stoma <b>shrinks over 6–8 weeks</b> → <b>measure weekly</b> (and with weight change); cut the opening <b>⅛–1/16 inch larger</b> than the stoma.</div>\n<div><b>Changing the pouch</b> — clip or shave hair <b>away from the stoma</b>; clean with <b>mild soap and water</b> — <b>not moisturizing soap</b> (blocks adhesion); apply <b>skin sealant (preferably alcohol-free) and let it dry</b>; <b>stoma powder or paste</b> for raw skin, paste to fill creases; <b>antifungal cream or powder</b> for candidiasis.</div>\n<div><b>Gas and odor</b> — odor usually means a <b>leaking or poorly closed pouch</b>; <b>no foods are strictly forbidden</b>, but some (vegetables) add gas, or odor when the pouch is open. <b>Charcoal filters, pouch deodorizers, a breath mint</b> in the pouch, vented filters. <b>Never put aspirin in the pouch</b> — it can <b>ulcerate the stoma</b>. Avoid <b>gas-producing foods and carbonated drinks</b>; it can take <b>4–6 weeks</b> to learn how foods affect output. A <b>sigmoid</b> colostomy may be regulated by <b>irrigation</b> (like an enema through the stoma) or by diet.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">AP resection wound and recovery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Perineal wound</b> — usually closed with <b>two Jackson-Pratt bulb drains</b>. <b>Serosanguineous drainage for 1–2 months</b>; complete healing <b>6–8 months</b>; it can hurt <b>more than the abdominal incision</b>. <b>Phantom rectal sensations</b>, later pain and itching (benzocaine, warm compresses). Watch for <b>infection and abscess</b>. Box 48.5: an <b>absorbent pad</b> (a feminine napkin works) and <b>jockey-type briefs</b>; <b>sitz baths 10–20 minutes, 3–4 times a day</b> or warm compresses; analgesics; <b>side-lying</b> in bed; <b>avoid long sitting</b>; sit on <b>foam pads or a soft pillow — never an air ring or rubber donut</b>; <b>I&amp;O including wound drainage</b>; check for redness, swelling, bleeding, odor, constant pain.</div>\n<div><b>Going home</b> — no <b>heavy lifting or straining</b> (protects the anastomosis); open surgery → <b>no driving or vigorous activity for 4–6 weeks</b>; laparoscopic → usual activity in <b>1–2 weeks</b>. <b>Stool softeners</b>; note stool frequency and character; <b>report cramping, abdominal pain, nausea or vomiting</b> (obstruction, perforation). Store ostomy supplies where it is <b>neither hot nor cold</b> (barriers stiffen or melt).</div>\n<div><b>Teaching the ostomy</b> — practice time to <b>handle, assemble and apply</b> equipment; the <b>normal stoma</b> (red-pink, moist), the complications to report (<b>dark red, purple or black stoma; heavy bleeding; separation from the skin; retraction, prolapse or narrowing; red, broken or itchy skin under the wafer</b>), <b>measuring</b>, choosing and applying the appliance, <b>skin protection</b>, diet for gas and odor (<b>limit gas-producing foods and carbonated drinks</b>; no food is forbidden; it takes <b>4–6 weeks</b> to learn how foods affect output), expected stool, and <b>resuming work, travel and sex</b>. Home care (Box 48.6) checks intake, weight, bowel pattern, <b>stoma (size, color, retraction, prolapse, stenosis, hernia, ischemia)</b>, skin, appliance fit and coping.</div>\n<div><b>Feelings and resources</b> — fear of <b>rejection</b>, <b>grief over body image</b>, concerns about <b>sexuality</b> (all patients in one study reported sexual problems 6 months after rectal surgery — prepare them before surgery). Invite feelings, involve family, teach about <b>leaks, odor control and intimacy</b>. Refer to case managers and social workers; the <b>United Ostomy Associations of America (UOAA)</b> and its <b>ostomate visitor program</b> (with consent); the <b>American Cancer Society</b>; home health; <b>hospice</b> for advanced disease.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A stoma has <b>no nerve endings</b> for pain, so the patient can't feel it being cut by a too-small wafer opening or rubbed by a belt — and its color is the only window into its blood supply. That is why the opening is measured and cut slightly larger every week while it shrinks, and why a stoma that turns dark or dry is reported at once: it is the bowel itself, dying.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-irritable-bowel-syndrome","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Irritable bowel syndrome","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Irritable bowel syndrome</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — a <b>functional</b> GI disorder (no structural damage) with chronic or recurrent <b>diarrhea, constipation, or both</b>, plus <b>abdominal pain and bloating</b> (also called spastic, mucous or nervous colon). The <b>most common digestive disorder</b> in practice (<b>10–15%</b> worldwide); begins in <b>young adulthood</b>, lifelong. Types: <b>IBS-D</b> (diarrhea), <b>IBS-C</b> (constipation), <b>IBS-M</b> (mixed). In the US <b>women are affected twice as often</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Causes</b> — unclear — environmental (<b>caffeinated or carbonated drinks, dairy</b>), <b>small-bowel bacterial overgrowth</b> (bloating), immune and genetic factors (cytokines such as IL-6, IL-1β, TNF-α), hormones, and <b>stress</b>. Strong links to <b>anxiety and depression</b> — primary, or secondary to chronic pain and fear of having no bathroom nearby (staying home, avoiding travel).</div>\n<div><b>Assess</b> — fatigue, malaise, pain, <b>bowel pattern</b> and stool consistency, <b>mucus</b>; usually <b>no weight loss</b>; past GI infections; drugs that mimic IBS; <b>caffeine, sorbitol or fructose</b> drinks; triggers (diet, stress, anxiety); <b>dairy, raw fruit, grains</b> → bloating and gas; a <b>food diary</b>. Pain is often in the <b>left lower quadrant</b> (visceral hypersensitivity); nausea with meals or defecation; <b>small hard stools followed by several softer ones</b>, or soft watery stools with <b>mucus</b>; belching, gas, anorexia. The patient <b>looks well</b>; weight and fluids normal; bowel sounds normal (hypo with constipation, hyper with diarrhea).</div>\n<div><b>Tests</b> — routine labs are <b>normal</b> (CBC, albumin, ESR, occult blood). <b>Hydrogen breath test</b>: bacterial overgrowth or malabsorption makes <b>extra hydrogen</b>, which is exhaled. <b>NPO (water allowed) for at least 12 hours</b>; blow a baseline, drink <b>lactulose</b>, then breath samples <b>every 20 minutes for 1–2 hours</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Self-management</b> — outpatient: teaching, drugs, <b>stress reduction</b>, holistic care. <b>30–40 g of fiber a day</b>, <b>regular meals</b>, <b>8–10 glasses of water</b>, <b>chew slowly</b>; <b>regular exercise</b>. Stress: relaxation, meditation, yoga, counseling, problem solving. <b>Probiotics</b> for bacterial overgrowth; <b>peppermint oil capsules</b> may help.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs for IBS-C</b> — <b>psyllium</b> (bulk former) at meals with a glass of water; <b>lubiprostone</b> — for <b>women</b> with IBS-C, raises intestinal fluid — <b>with food and water</b>; <b>linaclotide</b> — stimulates guanylate cyclase receptors (more fluid, faster transit, less pain and cramping) — <b>once a day, about 30 minutes before breakfast</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs for IBS-D and pain</b> — <b>loperamide</b> and <b>psyllium</b>. <b>Alosetron</b> (5-HT₃ antagonist) — <b>women only, last resort</b> — can cause <b>ischemic colitis</b>: take a full drug history (many interactions), <b>no psychoactive drugs or antihistamines</b>, and <b>stop and report</b> severe constipation, fever, worsening pain or fatigue, dark urine, <b>bloody diarrhea or rectal bleeding</b>. <b>Rifaximin</b> (gut-acting antibiotic, barely absorbed) for bloating and IBS-D. <b>Tricyclic antidepressants</b> (amitriptyline) for pain — <b>30–45 minutes before meals</b> if pain follows eating.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Fiber helps <b>both</b> halves of IBS, which seems contradictory until you see what it does: soluble fiber like psyllium <b>holds water</b> — it softens hard stool and firms loose stool toward the same bulky, soft middle. That is why the same advice (fiber plus plenty of water) is given whether the patient's problem is constipation or diarrhea.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-abdominal-trauma","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Abdominal trauma","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Abdominal trauma</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Mechanisms</b> — the <b>3rd leading cause of death</b> from injury. <b>Blunt</b>: direct impact (punch, kick) or <b>sudden deceleration</b> (car crash, fall). <b>Penetrating</b>: <b>stab and gunshot wounds</b>. Injuries: hematomas, lacerations, ruptures of the stomach, <b>liver, spleen</b>, bladder and bowel.</div>\n<div><b>Complications</b> — two most common killers: <b>hemorrhage → hypovolemic shock</b> (liver or spleen; graded I–V) and <b>hollow-viscus perforation → peritonitis → septic shock</b>. Also abscess, paralytic ileus, bowel obstruction, and <b>abdominal compartment syndrome</b> — leaky capillaries → tissue edema → <b>intraabdominal hypertension</b> → pain, organ compression, ischemia, organ failure; often needs <b>surgical decompression</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Assess</b> — after the <b>primary survey</b> and resuscitation, learn the <b>mechanism of injury</b>. Inspect lying flat for abrasions, discoloration, distention; <b>significant palpation and percussion by the trained provider</b>; repeat often. <b>Box 48.7</b>: <b>Cullen sign</b> (purple around the <b>umbilicus</b> — blood in the abdominal wall); <b>Turner sign</b> (bruising of the <b>flank</b> — retroperitoneal bleeding or pancreatic injury); <b>flank hematoma</b> (kidney); <b>Kehr sign</b> (pain referred to the <b>left shoulder</b> — ruptured <b>spleen</b> or diaphragm irritation); <b>distention</b> (bleeding, ascites, leaking contents). Bowel sounds ↓ or absent; nausea, vomiting (± blood); frequent VS; <b>worsening pain</b> = bleeding, perforation or compartment syndrome.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnose and treat</b> — <b>FAST scan</b> at the bedside finds <b>free fluid</b> (blood, GI contents) fast; <b>CT</b> after resuscitation. Unstable or actively bleeding → straight to <b>surgery (exploratory laparotomy)</b>. Stable → <b>ICU</b> monitoring: focused assessments, pain, VS, <b>Hgb/Hct</b> for bleeding; <b>transfusion</b>; wound care for penetrating injury; oxygen or ventilation; <b>antibiotics</b> for hollow-viscus injury; laparotomy if they deteriorate; transfer out after <b>24–48 stable hours</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Kehr sign is referred pain: blood under the diaphragm irritates the <b>phrenic nerve</b>, which enters the spinal cord at the same level (C3–C5) as nerves from the shoulder — so the brain reports diaphragm irritation as <b>left shoulder pain</b>. A trauma patient with left shoulder pain and no shoulder injury may be bleeding from the spleen.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Hernias and hemorrhoids","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hernias</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What and why</b> — bowel or another structure <b>protrudes through a weak spot</b> in the abdominal wall (or diaphragm). Two ingredients: <b>muscle weakness</b> (congenital or acquired) and <b>raised intraabdominal pressure</b> — <b>obesity, pregnancy, heavy lifting</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Types</b> — <b>indirect inguinal</b> (most common; mostly <b>men</b> — follows the tract of the descended testes, may reach the <b>scrotum</b>); <b>direct inguinal</b> (through a weak point; <b>older adults</b>); <b>femoral</b> (through the femoral ring; pregnant or obese women); <b>umbilical</b> (congenital, or acquired with <b>obesity</b>); <b>incisional (ventral)</b> at a past incision (infection, poor nutrition, obesity).</div>\n<div><b>Reducible, incarcerated, strangulated</b> — <b>reducible</b>: contents go back with gentle pressure. <b>Irreducible (incarcerated)</b>: can't be pushed back → <b>immediate surgical evaluation</b>. <b>Strangulated</b>: the hernial ring cuts off the <b>blood supply</b> → ischemia, obstruction, <b>necrosis, sepsis, perforation</b> — signs: <b>distention, nausea, vomiting, pain, fever, tachycardia</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Assess</b> — a <b>lump</b> felt, often after straining or lifting. Inspect <b>lying and standing</b> (a reducible hernia may vanish lying flat); the provider has the patient <b>strain or cough</b> and feels the ring (men standing, finger up the spermatic cord). <b>Absent bowel sounds</b> → obstruction or strangulation = emergency. <b>Never force a hernia back</b> — strangulated bowel could rupture.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — non-surgical candidates: a <b>truss</b> (firm pad on a belt) applied <b>after the hernia is reduced</b>, put on <b>on waking</b>; check and lightly powder the skin daily. Surgery: <b>herniorrhaphy</b>, preferably <b>minimally invasive inguinal hernia repair (MIIHR)</b>; <b>hernioplasty</b> reinforces the wall with <b>mesh</b>; strangulation may need <b>bowel resection or colostomy</b>. The <b>most important</b> pre-op teaching is to stay <b>NPO</b> for the hours the surgeon specifies; for same-day surgery arrange <b>a ride and an adult at home</b> for the rest of the day.</div>\n<div><b>After repair (Box 48.8)</b> — home in <b>3–5 hours</b> after MIIHR. VS for <b>internal bleeding</b>; analgesics (report severe uncontrolled pain); <b>deep breathing and incentive spirometry but avoid coughing</b>; walk within hours; <b>ice packs</b>; indirect inguinal repair → <b>scrotal support, ice and elevation</b> on a pillow. <b>Help men stand to void</b>; report <b>urine output &lt; 30 mL/hr</b>; running water; <b>1500–2500 mL fluid a day</b>; straight catheter if unable to void. Teach: rest several days and avoid strenuous activity; <b>soreness is expected, severe pain is not</b>; a <b>stool softener</b> may be needed; <b>no driving or operating machinery while taking opioids</b>; report <b>fever, chills, redness, swelling, heat, drainage, separation or increasing pain</b> at the incisions; keep them clean and dry; <b>shower 24–36 hours after removing the bandage</b>, leave closure strips (fall off in ~1 week); <b>lift no more than 10 lb</b> until cleared, and avoid straining and lifting for several weeks; fiber and fluids (no constipation); work in <b>1–2 weeks</b>; follow-up call the next day.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hemorrhoids</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What and why</b> — swollen anorectal veins (normally part of continence). Raised <b>intraabdominal pressure</b> → raised venous pressure, plus arterioles shunting blood in → veins distend and <b>separate from their supporting muscle → prolapse</b>. <b>Internal</b> (above the sphincter, not visible) or <b>external</b> (below, visible); prolapsed ones can <b>thrombose, inflame or bleed</b>. Worse with <b>pregnancy, constipation and straining, obesity, heart failure, prolonged sitting or standing</b>, strenuous exercise and weightlifting, <b>low fluid intake</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Prevention and signs</b> — prevent <b>constipation</b>: whole grains, raw fruits and vegetables, <b>water</b> (unless heart or kidney disease limits fluids), <b>no straining</b>, gradual exercise, healthy weight. Signs: <b>bleeding</b> — <b>bright red on the toilet tissue or streaked on stool</b> — swelling, <b>prolapse</b>, <b>pain</b> (especially sudden <b>thrombosis</b>), itching, mucous discharge; diagnosed by inspection and digital exam.</div>\n<div><b>Conservative care</b> — <b>cold packs</b> a few minutes at a time from the onset of pain and <b>tepid sitz baths 3–4 times a day</b>; <b>topical lidocaine</b> for severe pain; OTC <b>dibucaine</b> only <b>short term</b> (can mask a worse disorder); <b>hydrocortisone</b> for itching and inflammation; <b>moist cleansing tissues</b> and <b>dab, don't wipe</b>; <b>high fiber and fluids</b>; <b>docusate</b> short term; <b>no irritating laxatives</b>; <b>avoid long sitting</b>; mild oral analgesics. Relief expected in <b>3–5 days</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Hemorrhoidectomy</b> — outpatient removal (ultrasound, laser, or a <b>circular stapler</b> for prolapse). Complications: pain, thrombosis, infection, <b>bleeding</b>, abscess. <b>Moist heat</b> (sitz baths, warm compresses) 3–4 times a day; <b>stool softeners</b> before and after; analgesics and anti-inflammatories; a <b>mild laxative if no BM by day 3</b>; avoid <b>habit-forming stimulant laxatives</b>. <b>Action alert: the first bowel movement can be very painful</b> — make sure someone is <b>with or near</b> the patient: <b>vasovagal</b> light-headedness, sweating and <b>syncope</b> can occur.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Hernias and hemorrhoids share a cause — <b>pressure from inside the abdomen</b> — which is why the same teaching prevents and protects both: avoid constipation and straining, lift with care, keep a healthy weight, and after surgery avoid the coughing, lifting and straining that would push on a fresh repair.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"48-adpie","ch":"48","chTitle":"Noninflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Every patient in this chapter is watched for the same turning points — <b>fluid loss</b>, <b>pain that changes character</b>, and <b>signs that blood supply to the bowel is failing</b> — while being taught the elimination habits (fiber, fluids, no straining, timely screening) that prevent obstruction, hemorrhoids, hernias and late-found cancer.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div>GI disease and <b>past surgery</b> (adhesions); vomiting and its color; <b>last bowel movement and flatus</b>; stool shape, blood and color; weight loss and fatigue; <b>family history</b> of CRC, FAP or Lynch; screening history; diet, fiber, fluids, laxative use; mechanism of any injury; triggers and stress in IBS; lifting or straining with a lump.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical</div>vital signs and <b>temperature</b> (&gt; 100°F with tachycardia = strangulation?); distention, visible peristalsis; bowel sounds (<b>borborygmi</b> above a block, then silence; listen with suction off); pain character; rigidity and guarding; <b>Cullen, Turner and Kehr</b> signs; a hernia lying and standing; <b>stoma color, size and skin</b>; perineal wound; hemorrhoids; hydration and daily weight.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Labs and tests</div><b>Hgb, Hct, BUN</b> (dehydration, bleeding), <b>Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻</b>, WBC (strangulation), amylase; FIT/FOBT; <b>CEA</b>; CT/MRI, ultrasound, <b>FAST</b>; colonoscopy; hydrogen breath test.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>obstruction: <b>potential for life-threatening complications</b> (hypovolemia, electrolyte imbalance, strangulation, perforation, sepsis). Colorectal surgery: <b>infection</b> (AP resection perineal wound), stoma complications, body image, sexuality and grief. Trauma: <b>hemorrhage and shock</b>. Hernia: <b>strangulation</b>; urinary retention after repair. IBS: pain, altered elimination, anxiety.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>the obstruction resolves with normal elimination and <b>no life-threatening complications</b>; fluid and electrolytes stay balanced (<b>stable weight</b>, urine output ≥ 30 mL/hr); the stoma stays <b>pink and moist</b> with intact skin and the patient manages the pouch; the perineal wound heals without infection; IBS symptoms are controlled; screening happens on schedule.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Obstruction</div><b>NPO</b>; <b>NG to low continuous suction</b> (placement, patency, output <b>every 4 hours</b>; irrigate 30 mL NS as ordered; <b>bowel sounds with suction off</b>); <b>IV isotonic fluid with K⁺</b>; <b>daily weight</b>, I&amp;O, VS every 2–4 hours; mouth and nares care; <b>semi-Fowler</b>; analgesia as allowed; <b>alvimopan</b> for post-op ileus; report <b>pain that becomes constant</b>, fever &gt; 100°F, tachycardia or rigidity; prepare for surgery if complete or strangulated.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Colorectal surgery and ostomy</div>CWOCN site marking; discuss colostomy possibility and sexual and urinary risks; antibiotic before incision; post-op <b>stoma checks</b> (report purple, black, dry, heavy bleeding, separation); empty at <b>⅓–½ full</b>; <b>measure weekly</b> and cut <b>⅛–1/16 inch larger</b>; <b>alcohol-free sealant</b>; <b>no aspirin in the pouch</b>; perineal care — <b>sitz baths, side-lying, foam cushion not a donut</b>, JP drains, I&amp;O; no lifting or straining; <b>UOAA visitor</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">IBS, trauma, hernia, hemorrhoids</div>IBS: <b>fiber 30–40 g, 8–10 glasses of water</b>, exercise, stress care, drugs by type (<b>alosetron warnings</b>). Trauma: <b>primary survey</b>, FAST, VS and Hgb/Hct, transfusion, antibiotics, prepare for laparotomy. Hernia: <b>never force reduction</b>; after repair <b>deep breathe, don't cough</b>, ice, <b>scrotal support</b>, <b>stand to void</b>, report UO &lt; 30 mL/hr, ≤ 10 lb lifting. Hemorrhoids: <b>cold packs, sitz baths</b>, fiber and fluids, softeners; <b>stay near for the first post-op BM</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>bowel sounds and flatus return and the patient tolerates diet; weight, electrolytes and urine output are stable; no fever, rigidity or new constant pain; the stoma stays healthy and the patient can <b>demonstrate pouch care</b> and state what to report (a <b>dark or purple stoma, heavy bleeding, separation from the skin, retraction or prolapse, skin breakdown</b>, no output with cramping); the perineal wound drains less over weeks without infection; IBS flares are fewer; the patient knows their <b>screening schedule</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"49-overview","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">49 · Chapter 49</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Infection · inflammation · nutrition, elimination &amp; pain</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Inflammatory intestinal conditions</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> This chapter is about the bowel when it is <b>inflamed or infected</b>. The acute problems — <b>peritonitis</b> (the infection exemplar), <b>appendicitis</b> and <b>gastroenteritis</b> — can become life-threatening within hours. The chronic ones — <b>ulcerative colitis</b> (the inflammation exemplar), <b>Crohn's disease</b> and <b>diverticular disease</b> — come and go for years, along with <b>celiac disease</b> and <b>parasitic infections</b>. The common thread for the nurse: inflammation pulls <b>fluid</b> out of the circulation, chronic diarrhea and malabsorption drain <b>nutrition and electrolytes</b>, digestive enzymes injure <b>skin</b>, and the <b>pain</b> pattern tells you when a local problem has become peritonitis.</p><div id=\"ch49-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">The small intestine digests food and absorbs nutrients; the large intestine reabsorbs water to keep fluid balance; the peritoneal cavity holds about 50 mL of sterile fluid so organs glide during peristalsis; inflammation normally walls off contamination.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Bacteria or chemicals (bile, pancreatic enzymes, gastric acid) reach the peritoneum, usually by perforation; a fecalith blocks the appendix; viruses or bacteria inflame the mucosa; immune-driven chronic inflammation (UC continuous in the lining from the rectum, Crohn's patchy through all layers); food or bacteria trapped in diverticula; gluten triggers an autoimmune attack; parasites invade.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Rigid, boardlike abdomen, rebound tenderness, fever, tachycardia; periumbilical pain that moves to the McBurney point; vomiting then diarrhea and dehydration; 10–20 bloody stools with tenesmus (UC); 5–6 loose nonbloody stools, steatorrhea, weight loss, fistulas (Crohn's); left lower quadrant pain with fever (diverticulitis).</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">WBC elevation (about 20,000 in peritonitis; 10,000–18,000 with a shift to the left in appendicitis); blood cultures; x-ray free air; ultrasound and CT; CRP and ESR; Hgb/Hct, albumin, electrolytes; MR enterography; colonoscopy; stool analysis for parasites; celiac blood test with endoscopy.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Broad-spectrum antibiotics, IV fluids, NG decompression and surgery (laparotomy or laparoscopy, appendectomy, colon resection); oral rehydration; 5-ASA drugs, steroids, immunomodulators and biologics; bowel rest and TPN; RPC-IPAA or ileostomy; fiber timing in diverticular disease; gluten-free diet; metronidazole for parasites.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Third spacing → hypovolemic shock → acute kidney injury; ileus; bacteremia → septic shock; breathing compromise; perforation; toxic megacolon; GI hemorrhage; fistulas, abscesses and malnutrition; colorectal cancer after 10 years of UC.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A boardlike abdomen, or pain that suddenly eases then spreads (perforation); fever with tachycardia and falling blood pressure or urine output (sepsis); bright red or tarry stools with falling Hgb; a pale, gray, bluish or dark stoma; an ileostomy that stops draining; new confusion, weakness or numbness on natalizumab (PML).</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Bacteria or chemicals reach the peritoneum<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>inflammation tries to wall it off<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>walling off fails → diffuse infection<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>leaky vessels shift fluid into the abdomen (third spacing)<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>hypovolemia → kidney injury<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>bacteria enter the blood → septic shock</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Ulcerative colitis vs Crohn's disease (Table 49.2)</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>UC</b>: starts in the <b>rectum</b> and spreads <b>continuously</b> toward the cecum, in the <b>lining</b>; <b>10–20 liquid, bloody stools</b> a day; <b>hemorrhage</b> is the classic complication; peaks at 15–35 and 55–70; <b>25–35%</b> need surgery (and it works well). <b>Crohn's</b>: most often the <b>terminal ileum</b>, <b>patchy (skip lesions) through all layers</b>; <b>5–6 soft, loose, nonbloody</b> stools; <b>fistulas</b> (common) and <b>malabsorption</b>; peaks at 15–40; <b>65–75%</b> need surgery (less successful). <b>Both</b> list <b>nutritional deficiencies</b> as a complication.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Appendicitis vs gastroenteritis — which came first?</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Pain first, then nausea and vomiting</b> → think <b>appendicitis</b>. <b>Nausea and vomiting first, then cramping and diarrhea</b> → think <b>gastroenteritis</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Diverticulosis vs diverticulitis</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Diverticulosis</b>: pouches, usually <b>no symptoms</b> → prevent with a <b>high-fiber</b> diet. <b>Diverticulitis</b>: an <b>inflamed or infected</b> pouch → LLQ pain, fever, ↑ WBC → <b>low fiber, clear liquids or NPO</b> while active, antibiotics, rest, <b>no laxatives or enemas</b>; fiber returns <b>gradually</b> once it heals.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Sulfasalazine</b> is split by gut bacteria into <b>5-ASA</b> (calms the lining) and <b>sulfapyridine</b> (most side effects, and the <b>sulfa</b>) → check sulfa allergy, give <b>folic acid</b>. <b>Mesalamine</b> is the 5-ASA alone → better tolerated. <b>Infliximab and adalimumab</b> block <b>TNF</b> → infection risk → <b>avoid crowds</b>. <b>Natalizumab</b> stops white cells from entering tissue → risk of <b>PML</b> in the brain. <a href=\"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">VS (fever, tachycardia, hypotension, narrowing pulse pressure = sepsis), pain and the abdomen (rigidity, rebound, distention, bowel sounds), <b>I&amp;O and daily weight</b> (hourly I&amp;O right after abdominal surgery), mental status (the first sign of dehydration in older adults), stools (number, blood, mucus, fat), Hgb/Hct, WBC, CRP/ESR, K⁺ and Mg²⁺, albumin, stoma color and output, perianal and peristomal skin, fistula output.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A <b>rigid, boardlike abdomen</b>; pain that eases then becomes diffuse (perforation); fever &gt; 101°F with tachycardia; hypotension or falling urine output (septic or hypovolemic shock); <b>frank or tarry blood</b> with falling Hgb — call the <b>Rapid Response Team</b>; a <b>pale, gray, bluish or dark stoma</b>; an ileostomy that stops draining with cramps; new cognitive, motor or sensory changes on natalizumab.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why peritonitis empties the circulation</b> — inflammation <b>dilates vessels and makes them leaky</b> so white cells can reach the bacteria. If walling off fails, blood is shunted to the area (<b>hyperemia</b>) and fluid shifts from the extracellular space into the peritoneal cavity, connective tissue and GI tract (<b>third spacing</b>) → <b>hypovolemic shock</b> → poor kidney perfusion → <b>acute kidney injury</b>. <b>Peristalsis stops</b>, and the <b>7–8 L</b> of fluid that normally flows on for reabsorption collects in the bowel. Toxins or bacteria entering the blood cause <b>septicemia → septic shock</b>. Distention pushes on the <b>diaphragm</b> and pain limits breathing just when oxygen demand is high.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why the appendix perforates</b> — the lumen is <b>blocked, usually by a fecalith</b>; the lining keeps secreting, <b>pressure rises</b> and <b>blood flow is cut off</b> → pain and <b>bacteria invade the wall</b>. Slowly → an <b>abscess</b>; quickly → <b>peritonitis</b>. <b>Gangrene and sepsis within 24–36 hours</b>; <b>perforation within 24 hours</b>, with risk rising rapidly <b>after 48 hours</b> (temperature &gt; 101°F and a rising pulse).</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Lining vs all layers</b> — <b>UC</b> inflames the <b>mucosa</b>: it becomes <b>hyperemic, edematous and red</b>, then <b>bleeds</b> and forms <b>erosions, ulcers and abscesses</b> — hence <b>bloody stools with mucus</b> and tenesmus; thickening can narrow the colon. <b>Crohn's</b> goes <b>through all layers (transmural)</b>: the wall <b>thickens</b>, <b>strictures and deep \"cobblestone\" ulcers</b> form, and the inflammation <b>tunnels into neighbors as fistulas</b> — with <b>malabsorption</b>, steatorrhea and anemia. In both, <b>TNF-alpha and interleukins</b> drive the damage — which is why drugs that block TNF work.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why diverticula form and get infected</b> — a <b>low-fiber</b> diet makes small, hard stools; the colon muscle <b>thickens and stiffens</b>, and <b>high pressure inside</b> pushes the lining out through <b>weak points where blood vessels cross the muscle</b> — usually the <b>sigmoid</b>. <b>Undigested food or bacteria trapped</b> in a pouch reduce its blood supply, bacteria invade → <b>diverticulitis</b> → <b>perforation, abscess, peritonitis</b> or bleeding.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>In this chapter the body's defense is also the danger. Inflammation makes vessels <b>leaky</b> on purpose, so white cells can reach and wall off bacteria. When walling off fails, the same leak pours fluid into the abdomen — which is how a patient with peritonitis can go into <b>hypovolemic shock without losing a drop you can see</b>. So peritonitis is treated as a <b>fluid problem</b> as much as an infection: hourly I&amp;O after surgery, daily weights and IV fluids go alongside the antibiotics.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Peritonitis — the infection exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is and what causes it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The peritoneum</b> — the peritoneal cavity normally holds about <b>50 mL of sterile fluid</b> (transudate) that prevents friction during peristalsis. <b>Peritonitis</b> is a life-threatening, acute <b>inflammation and infection</b> of the visceral and parietal peritoneum and the lining of the abdominal cavity. As a <b>post-op complication</b> its mortality is <b>up to 50%</b>.</div>\n<div><b>How contamination gets in</b> — most often <b>bacteria or chemicals</b>. Bacteria enter by <b>perforation</b> (appendicitis, diverticulitis, <b>peptic ulcer</b>), a <b>penetrating wound</b>, a <b>gangrenous gallbladder or bowel segment</b>, <b>bowel obstruction</b>, or infection ascending through the <b>genital tract</b>; less often invasive tumors, leakage or contamination <b>during surgery</b>, or skin organisms in <b>continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis (CAPD)</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Organisms and chemicals</b> — common bacteria: <b><i>Escherichia coli</b></i>, <b><i>Streptococcus</b></i>, <b><i>Staphylococcus</b></i>, <b>pneumococci</b>, <b>gonococci</b>. <b>Chemical peritonitis</b> comes from leaking <b>bile, pancreatic enzymes or gastric acid</b>. There is <b>no genetic risk</b>. Most common in <b>young adults with appendicitis</b> and in <b>older adults</b>, whose immunity may be lower.</div>\n<div><b>Walled off or spread</b> — the body first <b>walls off</b> a localized area: vessels dilate and capillaries leak so <b>leukocytes</b> arrive and <b>phagocytose</b> the organisms. If walling off fails, contamination becomes massive → <b>diffuse (generalized) peritonitis</b>, third spacing, ileus and possible <b>sepsis</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing it (Box 49.1)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — ask about <b>abdominal pain</b> — its <b>character</b> (cramping, sharp, aching), <b>location</b>, and whether it is <b>localized or generalized</b> — and a history of <b>low-grade fever</b> or recent <b>temperature spikes</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Key features</b> — <b>rigid, boardlike abdomen</b> (classic); abdominal pain — localized, poorly localized, or <b>referred to the shoulder or chest</b>; <b>distention</b>; nausea, anorexia, vomiting; <b>diminishing bowel sounds</b>; <b>no flatus or feces</b>; <b>rebound tenderness</b>; <b>high fever</b>; <b>tachycardia</b>; <b>dehydration</b> from fever (poor skin turgor); <b>decreased urine output</b>; <b>hiccups</b>; possible <b>respiratory compromise</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Cardinal signs</b> — <b>abdominal pain, tenderness and distention</b>. <b>Localized</b> peritonitis → a tender abdomen with rebound tenderness in <b>one well-defined area</b>; <b>generalized</b> peritonitis → tenderness is <b>widespread</b>.</div>\n<div><b>How the patient looks</b> — acutely ill, <b>lying still, possibly with the knees flexed</b>; movement is <b>guarded</b>; grimacing with <b>coughing or any movement</b>. Watch for <b>progressive distention</b> as motility falls; bowel sounds usually <b>disappear</b> as it progresses.</div>\n<div><b>Feelings</b> — fear and anxiety about the diagnosis and distress from the pain → provide a <b>calm presence</b>, reassure them you will be there, let them <b>express fear</b>, and listen without judgment.</div>\n<div><b>Labs</b> — <b>WBC often about 20,000/mm³</b> with many neutrophils (normal <b>5000–10,000</b>). <b>Blood cultures</b> — has septicemia occurred, and which antibiotic? <b>BUN, creatinine, Hgb and Hct</b> for fluid, electrolyte and kidney status; <b>SpO₂ and ABGs</b> for breathing and acid-base balance.</div>\n<div><b>Imaging</b> — <b>abdominal x-ray</b>: <b>free air or fluid = perforation</b>; also dilation, edema and inflammation of the bowel. <b>Ultrasound or CT</b> may follow.</div>\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — 1. <b>Acute pain</b> from abdominal inflammation and infection. 2. <b>Potential for fluid volume shift</b> — fluid moving into the interstitial or peritoneal space.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Treating it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Watch for septic shock</b> — patients are <b>hospitalized</b> — in <b>critical care</b> if complications are extensive. Treatment targets the <b>cause</b> and the <b>infection</b>, usually nonsurgically at first. Check <b>vital signs often</b> for septic shock: <b>unresolved or progressive hypotension</b>, <b>decreased pulse pressure</b>, <b>tachycardia</b>, fever, skin changes, <b>tachypnea</b>; and <b>mental status</b> — confusion or a lower level of consciousness. Expected outcome: pain <b>2–3 on a 0–10 scale</b> (or acceptable) as the infection resolves.</div>\n<div><b>Infection control</b> — <b>handwashing</b> and <b>strict asepsis</b> with wounds, drains and dressings; <b>sterile technique</b> and catheter care for a urinary catheter; <b>document wound drainage and report changes immediately</b>; give <b>broad-spectrum antibiotics</b> as prescribed; <b>oxygen</b> by SpO₂ and respiratory status.</div>\n<div><b>Fluid balance</b> — the provider orders <b>hypertonic IV fluids and broad-spectrum antibiotics immediately</b> after diagnosis <i>(the book's wording here; lost volume is usually replaced with isotonic fluids such as normal saline or lactated Ringer's, as in chapter 47)</i>. <b>Daily weight on the same scale each morning</b> (remind assistive personnel) and careful <b>I&amp;O</b>; <b>NG tube</b> to decompress the stomach and intestine if an <b>open laparotomy</b> is expected. Fluid moves to the peritoneal cavity, and <b>NG suction and NPO</b> add losses → IV replacement, with rates changed by labs and assessment. <b>Compare irrigation fluid returned with fluid instilled</b> — retained fluid causes distention or pain. The day after a rupture or abdominal surgery, <b>fluid and electrolyte status is the priority assessment</b> (NCLEX Challenge 49.1).</div>\n<div><b>Surgery</b> — needed to find and repair the cause, remove abscesses or foreign material, and <b>drain fluid</b> — <b>delayed</b> if the patient is too critically ill. <b>Exploratory laparotomy</b> (large incision) or <b>laparoscopy</b>: e.g., <b>appendectomy</b>, or <b>colon resection with or without colostomy</b> for a perforated diverticulum. The surgeon <b>irrigates the peritoneum with antibiotic solution</b> and may leave <b>drains</b> for irrigation after surgery. Infected open wounds may heal by <b>second or third intention</b>, needing irrigation or packing; use <b>sterile technique</b> for peritoneal irrigation.</div>\n<div><b>Right after surgery (Action Alert)</b> — monitor <b>level of consciousness, vital signs, respiratory status (rate, effort, breath sounds) and I&amp;O at least hourly</b>. Keep the patient in <b>semi-Fowler's position</b> — it lets peritoneal contents <b>drain into the lower abdomen</b> and helps the <b>lungs expand</b>. Routine post-op care (Chapter 9) applies too — <b>oxygen until fully awake</b>, <b>incentive spirometry</b>, <b>sequential compression devices</b> — and with NPO status and NG suction the patient keeps <b>IV fluids and IV antibiotics</b> (NGN Challenge 49.1).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>After a ruptured abdominal abscess or peritonitis surgery, the wound is not the first thing that kills — the <b>fluid shift</b> is. Fluid is leaving the circulation into the abdomen while NG suction and NPO add losses, so the textbook's <b>priority assessment is fluid and electrolyte status</b>. Semi-Fowler's position is the companion move: gravity keeps infected fluid low in the pelvis instead of under the diaphragm, where it would also crowd the lungs.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Going home</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>How long</b> — depends on how far the infection spread: a <b>drained localized abscess</b> that responds to antibiotics and IV fluids → home in <b>several days</b>; others need <b>mechanical ventilation or hemodialysis</b>, or a <b>transitional care unit</b> to finish antibiotics. Recovery takes longer than after other surgeries (multisystem involvement).</div>\n<div><b>Report right away</b> — <b>unusual or foul-smelling drainage</b>; <b>swelling, redness or hyperpigmentation, warmth or bleeding</b> at the incision; <b>temperature above 101°F (38.3°C)</b>; <b>abdominal pain</b>; signs of <b>wound dehiscence or ileus</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Wound and help at home</b> — large incisions left open heal by <b>secondary or tertiary intention</b> and need dressings, solution and <b>catheter-tipped syringes</b> to irrigate; a <b>home care nurse</b> can assess, irrigate or pack until the family is comfortable; a home care aide or a <b>skilled care facility</b> if ADLs need help.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs and activity</b> — review antibiotics and analgesics. With short-term opioids such as <b>oxycodone with acetaminophen</b>, a <b>stool softener (docusate)</b> may be given with a <b>laxative</b>; <b>older adults</b> constipate easily on <b>codeine-based</b> drugs; <b>no extra acetaminophen</b> (liver toxicity). <b>No lifting over 5–10 lb (2.3–4.5 kg) for at least 6 weeks</b> after open surgery; after <b>laparoscopy</b>, usual activity in <b>1–2 weeks</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — the patient <b>verbalizes relief or control of pain</b> as the infection resolves and has <b>fluid and electrolyte balance</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"49-appendicitis","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Appendicitis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What happens and how it shows</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — acute inflammation of the <b>vermiform appendix</b>, which hangs off the proximal <b>cecum</b> just below the <b>ileocecal valve</b>. It peaks at <b>ages 15–19</b> but can occur at any age.</div>\n<div><b>Why it happens</b> — the lumen is <b>obstructed</b>, usually by a <b>fecalith</b> (very hard piece of feces); the lining secretes fluid, <b>pressure rises</b> and <b>blood flow falls</b> → pain and bacterial invasion of the wall. A slow process → <b>abscess</b>; a fast one → <b>peritonitis</b>. <b>Gangrene and sepsis can occur within 24–36 hours</b>; <b>perforation within 24 hours</b>, with the risk rising rapidly <b>after 48 hours</b>. Perforation → peritonitis with <b>temperature &gt; 101°F (38.3°C)</b> and a <b>rising pulse</b>.</div>\n<div><b>The order of symptoms</b> — track the sequence: <b>abdominal pain followed by nausea and vomiting</b> suggests <b>appendicitis</b>; <b>nausea or vomiting before pain</b> suggests <b>gastroenteritis</b>. Classically, <b>cramping pain in the epigastric or periumbilical area</b>; <b>anorexia</b> is frequent.</div>\n<div><b>Where the pain moves</b> — pain can start anywhere in the abdomen or flank; as it progresses it becomes <b>more severe and shifts to the RLQ</b>, at the <b>McBurney point</b> (Fig. 49.1) — <b>midway between the anterior iliac crest and the umbilicus</b>. Pain that <b>gets worse with coughing or moving</b> and <b>eases when the right hip or the knees are flexed</b> points to <b>perforation and peritonitis</b>. So does RLQ pain that <b>suddenly eases for a while, then returns spread across the abdomen</b>, with fever, a rising pulse and falling blood pressure — the picture in <b>NGN Challenge 49.1</b> (perforated appendix → peritonitis). The provider checks for <b>rigidity and guarding</b>; pain after pressure is released is <b>rebound tenderness</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Labs and imaging</b> — labs <b>don't establish</b> the diagnosis, but the <b>WBC is often 10,000–18,000/mm³</b> with a <b>\"shift to the left\"</b> (more immature WBCs); a <b>WBC &gt; 20,000</b> may mean a <b>perforated</b> appendix. <b>Ultrasound</b> may show an <b>enlarged appendix</b>; <b>CT</b> if symptoms are recurrent or prolonged (may show a <b>fecaloma</b>).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Care (Action Alert)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Before surgery</b> — all suspected or confirmed cases are <b>hospitalized</b>. Keep the patient <b>NPO</b> — surgery is likely and food would worsen the inflammation. Give <b>IV fluids</b> for fluid and electrolyte balance; <b>semi-Fowler's</b> as tolerated so drainage stays in the <b>lower abdomen</b>. <b>Manage pain adequately before surgery</b>; once the diagnosis is confirmed and surgery scheduled, give <b>opioid analgesics and antibiotics</b> as prescribed.</div>\n<div><b>Never</b> — <b>no laxatives or enemas</b> — they can <b>perforate</b> the appendix. <b>No heat</b> to the abdomen — it increases circulation to the appendix → <b>more inflammation and perforation</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Surgery</b> — <b>appendectomy as soon as possible</b>. Uncomplicated cases: <b>laparoscopy</b> (small incisions near the umbilicus) — few complications, usually <b>home the same day</b> with mild to moderate pain, back to usual activity in <b>1–2 weeks</b>. Unclear diagnosis but high risk → <b>exploratory laparotomy</b> (large incision). Pre-op teaching is limited by pain and urgency; post-op care is that of any general anesthesia.</div>\n<div><b>If peritonitis or abscess is found</b> — open surgery with <b>wound drains</b> and possibly an <b>NG tube</b> (decompress, prevent distention); <b>IV antibiotics and opioids</b>; get the patient <b>out of bed the evening of surgery</b> to prevent <b>atelectasis</b>; hospital stay <b>3–5 days</b>, usual activity in <b>4–6 weeks</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every \"never\" in appendicitis comes from the same <b>pressure problem</b>. The blocked appendix is a closed tube whose wall is already starved of blood by rising pressure. A <b>laxative or enema</b> drives more contents and motility against it; <b>heat</b> brings in more blood and swelling. Either one raises pressure in a wall that is about to give way — and rupture turns a local infection into <b>peritonitis</b>.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"49-gastroenteritis","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Gastroenteritis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Types and how it spreads (Table 49.1)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — diarrhea and/or vomiting from <b>inflammation of the mucous membranes of the stomach and intestinal tract</b>; the <b>small bowel</b> is most often affected; <b>viral is more common</b> than bacterial. Usually <b>self-limiting, about 3 days</b>; in the <b>immunosuppressed and older adults</b>, <b>dehydration and hypovolemia</b> may need medical care or hospitalization.</div>\n<div><b>Norovirus</b> — the <b>leading foodborne cause</b> of gastroenteritis; most common <b>November to April</b> (resists low temperatures, long viral shedding <b>before and after</b> illness). Spread <b>fecal-oral</b> person to person, by contaminated <b>food, water, surfaces and objects</b>; <b>vomiting can make it airborne</b>. Incubation <b>1–2 days (about 48 hours)</b>; affects all adult ages; <b>older adults</b> can become hypovolemic with electrolyte imbalances. Outbreaks: <b>prisons, cruise ships, nursing homes, college dorms</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Epidemic viral</b> — caused by many <b>parvovirus-type</b> organisms; <b>fecal-oral</b> in food and water; incubation <b>10–51 hours</b>; communicable during the acute illness.</div>\n<div><b>Bacterial</b> — <b><i>Campylobacter</i> enteritis</b>: fecal-oral or contact with <b>infected animals or infants</b>; incubation <b>1–10 days</b>; communicable <b>2–7 weeks</b>. <b><i>E. coli</i> diarrhea</b>: fecal contamination of food, water or <b>fomites</b>. <b>Shigellosis</b>: direct and indirect fecal-oral; incubation <b>1–7 days</b>; communicable until <b>4 weeks after</b> the illness; people may be <b>carriers for months</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing and treating it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — usually self-managed at home; hospitalized if severe or very dehydrated. Ask about <b>recent travel</b> — especially tropical <b>Asia, Africa, Mexico, Central or South America</b>; <b>restaurants in the past 24–36 hours</b>; produce (outbreaks from <b>contaminated spinach and lettuce</b>); <b>raw or undercooked</b> food such as <b>oysters, sushi and rare meat</b>.</div>\n<div><b>How it shows</b> — the patient looks ill; <b>nausea and vomiting come first, then abdominal cramping and diarrhea</b>. Older or immunocompromised patients may develop <b>weakness and cardiac dysrhythmias from hypokalemia</b>. Note <b>abdominal distention</b> and listen for <b>hyperactive bowel sounds</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Dehydration (Action Alert)</b> — signs: unintentional <b>weight loss</b>, <b>poor skin turgor</b>, fever (<b>not common in older adults</b>), <b>dry mucous membranes</b>, <b>orthostatic BP changes</b> (can cause <b>falls</b>), <b>hypotension</b>, <b>oliguria</b>. In older adults dehydration can be severe and fast, and <b>acute confusion</b> (from hypoxia) may be the <b>only early sign</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Fluids</b> — for any type, encourage <b>oral fluid replacement</b> — extra fluids to replace vomiting and diarrhea. <b>Oral rehydration therapy (ORT)</b> such as <b>sports drinks or Pedialyte</b> replaces fluids and electrolytes; <b>IV fluids</b> in the hospital depending on age and severity.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs</b> — <b>drugs that slow the gut may not be given</b> — they can keep the organism from being <b>eliminated</b>; if the provider decides one is needed, <b>loperamide</b> may be recommended. <b>Diphenoxylate with atropine</b> is used sparingly — it is <b>habit-forming</b> and should <b>not be used in older adults</b> (drowsiness → <b>falls</b>). <b>Antibiotics</b> for bacterial infection with <b>fever and severe diarrhea</b>: <b>ciprofloxacin or azithromycin</b> (also for <b>shigellosis</b>).</div>\n<div><b>Perianal skin</b> — stools rich in <b>electrolytes and enzymes</b> plus frequent wiping irritate the skin: <b>avoid toilet paper</b>; clean gently with <b>warm water</b> or absorbent material and dry thoroughly; a little cream, oil or gel on a damp warm washcloth lifts stuck stool; <b>protective barrier cream</b> between stools; <b>sitz baths 10 minutes, 2–3 times a day</b>. Leakage → absorbent pad held by snug underwear; incontinent at night → <b>pads instead of briefs</b> so air reaches the skin. Remind <b>assistive personnel</b> to keep the <b>perineal and buttock area clean and dry</b>, changing pads often.</div>\n<div><b>Stop the spread (Box 49.2)</b> — <b>wash hands for at least 30 seconds with soap</b>, especially after a bowel movement; keep <b>glasses, dishes, utensils and toothpaste</b> for personal use (disposables if severe); keep bathrooms clean; tell the provider if symptoms last <b>beyond 3 days</b>; <b>don't prepare or handle food</b> for others — <b>food handlers</b> consult the <b>public health department</b> before returning to work. Prevention: handwashing, <b>sanitizing surfaces</b>, hand sanitizer, proper food storage.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Why not just stop the diarrhea? In infectious gastroenteritis the diarrhea is partly how the body <b>gets rid of the organism</b>. A drug that slows the gut keeps the virus or bacteria inside longer — so the nurse <b>replaces what is lost</b> (fluid and potassium) instead of blocking the exit, and uses an antidiarrheal only when the provider decides it is needed.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Ulcerative colitis — the inflammation exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What happens</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Where and how</b> — widespread chronic inflammation of the <b>rectum and rectosigmoid colon</b> that can extend to the <b>entire colon</b>, spreading <b>continuously</b> from the rectum toward the cecum; the distribution can stay the same for years. <b>Remissions and exacerbations (flare-ups)</b>; <b>intestinal infection</b> can trigger flares. Most patients have <b>mild to moderate</b> disease. The lining becomes <b>hyperemic, edematous and red</b>; with more severe inflammation it <b>bleeds</b> and forms <b>erosions (ulcers)</b>; <b>abscesses</b> in the ulcers lead to <b>necrosis</b>; edema and thickening can <b>narrow the colon</b> and cause a partial obstruction.</div>\n<div><b>Signs</b> — stools with <b>blood and mucus</b>; <b>tenesmus</b> (unpleasant, urgent need to defecate); <b>lower abdominal colicky pain relieved by defecation</b>; <b>malaise, anorexia, anemia, dehydration, fever, weight loss</b>. Extraintestinal: <b>migratory polyarthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, erythema nodosum</b>. <b>Older adults</b> are at high risk of <b>dehydration and hypokalemia</b> from diarrhea.</div>\n<div><b>Causes and risk</b> — cause unknown: <b>genetic</b> (runs in families and twins), <b>immunologic</b> (autoimmune; <b>IgG epithelial antibodies</b> in some patients) and <b>lifestyle</b>. <b>Proinflammatory cytokines</b> (<b>interleukins, TNF-alpha</b>) are cytotoxic to the colon lining, and long disease brings cell changes that <b>raise colon cancer risk</b>. <b>Smoking</b> and a diet <b>high in animal fat and sugar, low in fruits and vegetables</b> raise risk. About <b>3 million</b> Americans have IBD, about half UC; most diagnosed at <b>20–35</b>. UC and Crohn's occur about equally; IBD is rising in non-White populations.</div>\n<div><b>Severity (Table 49.3)</b> — <b>Mild</b>: <b>&lt; 4 stools/day</b> with or without blood; asymptomatic; labs usually normal. <b>Moderate</b>: <b>&gt; 4/day</b>; minimal symptoms, mild pain, mild intermittent nausea, possible ↑ CRP or ESR. <b>Severe</b>: <b>&gt; 6 bloody stools/day</b>; <b>fever, tachycardia, anemia</b>, pain, ↑ CRP and/or ESR. <b>Fulminant</b>: <b>&gt; 10 bloody stools/day</b>; increasing symptoms; anemia may need <b>transfusion</b>; <b>colonic distention on x-ray</b>. <b>CRP</b> rises in the <b>first 6 hours</b> of inflammation and is more sensitive than ESR.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Complications (Table 49.4)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>In the bowel</b> — <b>hemorrhage or perforation</b> (lower GI bleeding from erosion of the wall); <b>abscesses</b> in the ulcerated lining; <b>toxic megacolon</b> — <b>massive dilation</b> of the colon and colonic ileus that can lead to <b>gangrene and peritonitis</b>; <b>intestinal malabsorption</b> (anemia and malnutrition — <b>most common in Crohn's</b>); <b>nonmechanical obstruction</b> from toxic megacolon or cancer; <b>fistulas</b> (mainly Crohn's — bowel to bladder → <b>pyuria and fecaluria</b>).</div>\n<div><b>Colorectal cancer</b> — UC for <b>more than 10 years</b> → <b>high colorectal cancer risk</b>; cancer causes about <b>one-third of UC-related deaths</b> → <b>frequent colonoscopies</b> when UC involving the whole colon has lasted over 10 years.</div>\n<div><b>Outside the bowel</b> — <b>arthritis</b>; <b>liver and biliary disease</b> (especially <b>cholelithiasis</b>); <b>oral and skin lesions</b>; eye disorders such as <b>iritis</b> (cause unknown); <b>osteoporosis</b>, especially in Crohn's.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Assessment</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — family history of IBD; past and current therapy; dates and types of surgery; nutrition — <b>intolerance of milk products</b> and fried, spicy or hot foods; bowel pattern (<b>color, number, consistency, character</b>); pain, <b>tenesmus</b>, anorexia, fatigue; links between diarrhea and <b>meals, emotional distress and activity</b>; <b>antibiotics in the past 2–3 months</b> (rule out <b><i>Clostridium difficile</b></i>); travel to or from tropical areas; <b>recent NSAIDs</b> (can <b>trigger a flare</b>); extraintestinal symptoms (arthritis, mouth sores, vision problems, skin disorders).</div>\n<div><b>Physical</b> — vital signs usually <b>normal in mild disease</b>; severe → <b>low-grade fever (99–100°F, 37.2–37.8°C)</b>. Findings are often <b>nonspecific</b> (normal in mild cases); viral and bacterial infections can mimic UC. Note <b>distention along the colon</b>; <b>fever with tachycardia</b> may mean <b>dehydration, peritonitis or bowel perforation</b>; check <b>inflamed joints</b> and <b>mouth lesions</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Psychosocial</b> — worry about <b>stool frequency and blood</b>; unpredictable diarrhea is disruptive and anxiety-producing; fear of <b>fecal incontinence</b> keeps patients home — feeling <b>\"tied to the toilet\"</b>; <b>anxiety and depression</b>; meals may bring pain and more stools; frequent visits and cancer surveillance cause anxiety. Explore: <b>life events and flares</b>, <b>stress factors</b>, <b>family and social support</b>, <b>genetic and cancer worries</b>, and <b>internet access</b> for reliable information and telehealth.</div>\n<div><b>Labs</b> — <b>↓ Hgb and Hct</b> (chronic blood loss → anemia, chronic disease); <b>↑ WBC, C-reactive protein or ESR</b> (inflammation); <b>↓ sodium, potassium and chloride</b> (diarrhea, malabsorption); <b>hypoalbuminemia</b> with extensive disease (protein lost in stool).</div>\n<div><b>Imaging and scopes</b> — <b>MR enterography (MRE)</b> is the <b>main test</b> for chronic IBD — it shows the lumen, wall, mesentery and nearby organs. Teach: <b>fast 4–6 hours</b>; the patient drinks a <b>large amount of contrast</b> (cramps, diarrhea) — <b>void before positioning</b> on the table; lies <b>prone</b> while the first of <b>two subcutaneous glucagon</b> doses slows the bowel. <b>Endoscopy or colonoscopy</b> (the prep is especially uncomfortable in IBD); <b>CT</b> for complications; <b>barium enema with air contrast</b> shows UC vs Crohn's and how deep and widespread the disease is.</div>\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — 1. <b>Diarrhea</b> from inflammation of the bowel lining. 2. <b>Acute or persistent pain</b> from inflammation, ulceration and skin irritation. 3. <b>Potential for lower GI bleeding</b> and resulting <b>anemia</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Treating ulcerative colitis — drugs, diet and bleeding watch","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Managing diarrhea</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Goal</b> — the major concern is <b>frequent, bloody diarrhea</b> and <b>fecal incontinence from tenesmus</b>; the expected outcome is <b>less diarrhea, formed stools and control of bowel movements</b>, which lets the lining heal. Measures reduce motility, decrease inflammation and promote healing — nonsurgical and/or surgical.</div>\n<div><b>Diet, smoking and rest</b> — teach a diet <b>low in animal fats and sugars</b> with <b>more fruits and vegetables</b>; patients with <b>food insecurity</b> → <b>food banks</b> and community resources. Ask about <b>tobacco</b> (a risk factor) and refer to <b>smoking cessation</b> — check for grants if it costs money. <b>Physical and emotional rest</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Track it</b> — teach the patient to <b>record stool color, volume, frequency and consistency</b> (paper or app); monitor <b>perianal skin</b> for irritation and ulceration; <b>stool cultures</b> if diarrhea continues. <b>Weight once or twice a week</b> at home; in the hospital, <b>daily weight before breakfast on the same scale</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Drug therapy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>5-aminosalicylates (5-ASA)</b> — commonly used for <b>mild to moderate</b> UC and to <b>maintain remission</b>; thought to be anti-inflammatory by <b>inhibiting prostaglandins</b> in the intestinal lining; usually effective in <b>2–4 weeks</b>. Drug classes for UC: <b>5-ASAs, glucocorticoids, immunosuppressants, immunomodulators, antibiotics</b> — the side effects and when to call are with each drug below (for example sulfasalazine: report <b>nausea, vomiting, anorexia, rash, headache</b>; steroids and biologics: report <b>any sign of infection</b>).</div>\n<div><b>Sulfasalazine</b> — a 5-ASA that <b>intestinal bacteria</b> split into <b>5-ASA</b> (the benefit) and <b>sulfapyridine</b> (the unwanted effects). Give a <b>folic acid supplement</b> — it decreases folate absorption. <b>Drug Alert</b>: report <b>nausea, vomiting, anorexia, rash and headache</b>; high doses → <b>hemolytic anemia, hepatitis, male infertility, agranulocytosis</b>. It is related to <b>sulfonamide antibiotics</b> → <b>check for sulfa allergy first</b>; a <b>thiazide diuretic</b> may be a contraindication.</div>\n<div><b>Mesalamine</b> — <b>better tolerated</b> than sulfasalazine — <b>no sulfapyridine</b>. <b>Delayed-release</b> (terminal ileum and colon) or <b>extended-release</b> (whole colon and rectum); also as an <b>enema or suppository</b> with <b>minimal systemic absorption</b> and fewer side effects.</div>\n<div><b>Glucocorticoids</b> — <b>prednisone</b> during <b>exacerbations</b> (dose increased in flares), then <b>tapered</b> after improvement because long-term use causes <b>hyperglycemia, osteoporosis, peptic ulcer disease, infection and adrenal insufficiency</b>. Rectal inflammation → steroid <b>retention enemas or suppositories</b>; <b>budesonide</b> acts mostly in the bowel → <b>fewer systemic effects</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Immunomodulators and biologics</b> — immunomodulators alone often don't work in UC but <b>combined with steroids</b> speed response and <b>lower the steroid dose</b>. <b>Biological response modifiers</b>: <b>infliximab</b> — an <b>IgG monoclonal antibody that reduces TNF activity</b> — for <b>refractory</b> disease, <b>toxic megacolon</b> or extraintestinal disease (not first line); <b>adalimumab</b> for refractory cases; both used more in Crohn's. They <b>suppress immunity</b> → teach to <b>report any beginning infection, even a cold</b>, and <b>avoid large crowds and sick people</b>. <b>Vedolizumab</b> is a gut-specific <b>leukocyte traffic inhibitor</b> that keeps white cells from migrating into inflamed bowel.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Sulfasalazine is really <b>two molecules joined together</b>. Gut bacteria cut the bond in the colon: <b>5-ASA</b> stays there to calm the lining, while <b>sulfapyridine</b> is absorbed and causes most side effects — including the <b>sulfa allergy</b> risk. Mesalamine is the 5-ASA half alone, so it is better tolerated. The same \"where does it act?\" logic explains why rectal enemas, suppositories and budesonide cause fewer whole-body effects.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Nutrition, rest and comfort</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Bowel rest</b> — hospitalized patients with severe symptoms are kept <b>NPO</b> for bowel rest; <b>TPN</b> for the severely ill and malnourished during flares; less severe → <b>elemental formulas</b>, absorbed in the small bowel with less stimulation.</div>\n<div><b>Food triggers</b> — diet is <b>not a major cause</b> of inflammation, but <b>caffeine and alcohol</b> may increase diarrhea and cramping; <b>raw vegetables and high-fiber foods</b> can cause symptoms; <b>lactose</b> may be poorly tolerated → reduce or eliminate. Common GI stimulants: <b>carbonated drinks, pepper, nuts and corn, dried fruits, smoking</b>. Tolerances differ for each patient.</div>\n<div><b>Rest and complementary therapy</b> — activity is restricted during flares — <b>rest reduces intestinal activity</b>, comforts and promotes healing; keep a <b>bedpan, commode or bathroom</b> close for urgency. Complementary options (need more study): <b>flaxseed, selenium, vitamin C</b>, biofeedback, hypnosis, yoga, acupuncture, ayurveda.</div>\n<div><b>Pain</b> — goal <b>2–3/10 or less</b>; multimodal drug and non-drug measures; surgery may relieve pain. <b>Increasing pain may mean peritonitis</b>. <b>Antidiarrheals</b> reduce discomfort but must be used <b>with caution and short term</b> — they can cause <b>toxic megacolon</b>. Perineal skin: <b>medicated wipes</b>; skin-care systems with a cleanser, healing cream and <b>petroleum-type barrier</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Lower GI bleeding (Critical Rescue)</b> — monitor stools for blood — <b>bright red (frank) or black and tarry (melena)</b>; monitor <b>Hgb, Hct, electrolytes and VS</b>; slow bleeding → <b>anemia</b>; watch for fever, tachycardia, volume depletion; <b>mental status changes</b> may be the <b>first sign</b> of dehydration or anemia in older adults. If bleeding begins, <b>notify the Rapid Response Team or provider immediately</b>; blood products for severe anemia — insert <b>two large-bore IV catheters</b>. A <b>GI bleeding scan</b> can localize bleeding <b>&gt; 0.5 mL/min</b> but takes hours and isn't for ICU patients.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Surgery for ulcerative colitis — the J-pouch and ileostomy","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">When and which surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>When</b> — when drugs fail, or for complications: <b>toxic megacolon, hemorrhage, bowel perforation, dysplasia on biopsy, colon cancer</b>. UC surgery is <b>more common and more successful</b> than surgery for Crohn's. <b>African Americans</b> have worse outcomes (higher <b>sepsis and death</b>) — linked to <b>delayed diagnosis, no insurance and poor access to an IBD specialist</b>.</div>\n<div><b>ERAS (Box 49.3)</b> — <b>Enhanced Recovery After Surgery</b> lowers sepsis and death with <b>nutrition, preventing paralytic ileus, nonopioid analgesia and goal-directed fluids</b>. <b>Before</b>: patient education, <b>frailty survey</b>, <b>reduced fasting time</b>, <b>carbohydrate loading</b>, glucose control. <b>During</b>: <b>minimally invasive surgery</b>, multimodal anesthesia, fluid administration. <b>After</b>: <b>early ambulation</b>, minimal IV, aggressive nausea and vomiting control, nutrition consult for <b>early feeding</b>, <b>multimodal pain management</b>, glucose control, <b>early removal of the urinary catheter</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Before surgery</b> — if an ileostomy is planned, give an <b>in-depth explanation</b> to the patient and family; the surgeon consults a <b>certified wound, ostomy and continence nurse (CWOCN)</b> on the <b>best stoma location</b>; a visit from an <b>ostomate</b> (a person with an ostomy) may help. An <b>ileostomy</b> brings a loop of <b>ileum</b> through the abdominal wall (stoma) to drain stool into a pouch with a <b>skin barrier (wafer)</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Approaches</b> — <b>minimally invasive</b> (laparoscopic, hand-assisted, robotic; <b>NOTES</b> through the anus or vagina) is common — several small incisions, often longer to perform; usually <b>no ICU stay</b>. <b>Obesity, previous surgery or dense adhesions</b> may rule it out → <b>open</b> surgery with general anesthesia and a short <b>ICU</b> stay.</div>\n<div><b>RPC-IPAA — the gold standard</b> — <b>restorative proctocolectomy with ileo pouch–anal anastomosis</b>, usually in <b>two stages</b> (Fig. 49.2). <b>Stage 1</b>: the <b>colon and most of the rectum</b> are removed; the <b>anus and anal sphincter stay</b>; an internal <b>pouch (J-, S- or pelvic pouch)</b> is made from the <b>last 18 inches</b> of small intestine and joined to the anus; a <b>temporary loop ileostomy</b> lets everything heal and the pouch grow. <b>Stage 2</b>, often <b>1–2 months</b> later: the ileostomy is <b>closed</b>. Continence is usually <b>excellent</b>, though some leak stool <b>during sleep</b> (antidiarrheals help); expect <b>frequent stools and urgency</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Total proctocolectomy with permanent ileostomy</b> — for patients who <b>can't have or don't want</b> the pouch: the <b>colon, rectum and anus are removed</b> and the anus is closed; the end of the ileum is brought out as a <b>permanent stoma</b> (Fig. 49.3).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The new ileostomy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Stoma check (Critical Rescue)</b> — usually in the <b>right lower quadrant below the belt line</b>; it should <b>not prolapse or retract</b>. Assess it <b>frequently</b>: <b>pinkish to cherry red</b> = good blood supply. <b>Pale, ash gray, bluish or dark → report to the surgeon immediately</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Output</b> — it starts draining within <b>24 hours</b> at <b>more than 1 L/day</b> — a <b>loose, dark green liquid</b> that may contain some blood → <b>add 500 mL or more of fluid a day</b> to prevent dehydration; after about <b>a week</b> output slows and thickens (antidiarrheals may be needed). With <b>ileostomy adaptation</b> the small bowel absorbs more <b>sodium and water</b> → stool becomes <b>pastelike, yellow-green or yellow-brown</b> with little or a sweet odor. A <b>foul odor</b> may mean <b>blockage or infection</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Skin is the priority</b> — the ileostomy drains <b>often</b>, so a pouch must <b>always</b> be worn. Small-intestine stool is full of <b>enzymes and bile salts</b> that <b>quickly excoriate skin</b>; a pouch with a <b>gelatin or pectin skin barrier</b> protects most patients.</div>\n<div><b>After surgery</b> — with ERAS and MIS most patients have <b>no NG tube</b>; after <b>open</b> surgery the patient is NPO with an <b>NG tube for 1–2 days</b>, then fluids and food are added slowly. Work with the <b>CWOCN</b> on teaching. Hospital stay <b>1–4 days</b>; MIS brings <b>less pain, fewer complications and faster return of bowel function</b>.</div>\n<div><b>After the J-pouch</b> — the pouch can become inflamed (<b>pouchitis</b>) → <b>metronidazole for 7–10 days</b>. After stage 2, <b>burning</b> with bowel movements is common because the ileum absorbs <b>gastric acid</b> poorly. Omit gas- and odor-causing foods (<b>cabbage, asparagus, brussels sprouts, beans</b>) and poorly digested ones (<b>nuts, corn</b>). Body image may change, though many feel better than before surgery.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>An ileostomy skips the colon — the organ that pulls <b>water and salt</b> back out of stool. That one fact explains the teaching: output is <b>liquid and high at first</b> (add fluid), the patient needs <b>extra salt and water</b>, especially with sweating or vomiting, the stool still carries <b>digestive enzymes and bile salts</b> that eat skin (barrier always), and a <b>laxative is never needed</b> — loose stool is normal, while <b>no output</b> may mean a food blockage.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Living with an ileostomy (Box 49.4)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Skin and pouch care</b> — <b>skin barrier</b> against output; <b>skin sealants and ostomy creams</b>, and a filler for uneven skin; watch the skin for <b>irritation or redness</b>. <b>Empty the pouch when one-third to one-half full</b>. <b>Change it during inactive times</b> — <b>before meals, before bed, on waking, or 2–4 hours after eating</b>. <b>Change the whole system every 3–7 days</b>. Most want a barrier that lasts <b>3–7 days</b>; <b>extended-wear</b> barriers suit high output; weigh pouch size (large = heavy when full) and <b>cost and insurance</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Food</b> — no special diet, but <b>chew food thoroughly</b> and be careful with <b>high-fiber, high-cellulose foods</b> that can cause diarrhea, constipation or <b>blockage</b>: <b>coconut, popcorn, tough-fiber meats, rice, cabbage</b>, and vegetables with skins (<b>tomatoes, corn, peas</b>); also avoid gas-forming foods (<b>nuts, raw cabbage, corn, celery, popcorn</b>). <b>Vitamin B12</b> may be needed if the <b>terminal ileum</b> is removed. Include <b>adequate salt and water</b>; be careful with <b>heavy sweating, strenuous activity, high heat, vomiting or diarrhea</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs</b> — <b>avoid enteric-coated and capsule</b> medicines; tell every prescriber and pharmacist about the ostomy. <b>No laxatives or enemas</b> — <b>loose stools are normal</b>; call the provider if <b>no stool has passed in 6–12 hours</b>.</div>\n<div><b>If it stops draining</b> — report any <b>drastic increase or decrease</b> in drainage. With <b>stomal swelling, abdominal cramping or distention</b>, or if <b>output stops</b>: <b>remove the pouch and faceplate</b>, <b>lie in a knee-chest position</b>, <b>massage the abdomen</b>, apply <b>moist towels</b> to the abdomen, <b>drink hot tea</b>. If none of these works or <b>pain is severe</b>, <b>call the provider right away</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Living with it</b> — concerns about <b>sexual and social</b> adjustment — help the patient discuss them with a partner (a <b>change in position</b> during intercourse may ease worry); body image and self-esteem; <b>clinical depression is common</b> in UC → <b>mental health referral</b>. Support: <b>United Ostomy Associations of America (UOAA)</b>, Ostomy Canada Society, <b>Crohn's and Colitis Foundation</b>, IBD support groups, ostomy clinics and ostomate visits after discharge.</div>\n<div><b>Teaching about UC</b> — teach the nature of UC — <b>flares and remissions</b> — and that relapses can be made less likely by <b>taking maintenance drugs (such as a 5-ASA) even when well</b>, not stopping steroids suddenly, <b>not smoking</b>, eating low animal fat and sugar with more fruit and vegetables, and limiting the foods that set off symptoms. Patients on <b>immunosuppressants (steroids, biologics)</b> must <b>report signs of infection</b> and <b>avoid crowds and anyone with an infection</b>. Report <b>fever &gt; 101°F (38.3°C), tachycardia, palpitations, more diarrhea, severe abdominal pain, or nausea and vomiting</b>. Home care: respiratory, incision and ostomy care and pain management continue; a <b>home care nurse</b> helps fit ostomy care into daily life.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — the patient has <b>no or fewer episodes of diarrhea</b>, <b>verbalizes less pain</b>, has <b>no lower GI bleeding</b>, and <b>self-manages</b> the ileostomy or ileoanal pouch.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"49-crohn-s-disease","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Crohn's disease","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What makes it different</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Where</b> — chronic inflammation of the <b>small intestine (most often), the colon or both</b>; it can affect the GI tract <b>from mouth to anus</b> but most often the <b>terminal ileum</b>. Slowly progressive and unpredictable, with diseased areas separated by normal bowel (<b>skip lesions</b>). Like UC it <b>remits and flares</b>, but <b>severe complications</b> and <b>repeated hospital stays</b> are more likely. Non-White patients tend to have <b>more extensive</b> inflammation.</div>\n<div><b>What it does</b> — inflammation <b>thickens the bowel wall</b>; <b>strictures and deep ulcers (cobblestone appearance)</b> form → risk of <b>fistulas</b> (abnormal tracts between organs). Result: <b>severe diarrhea and malabsorption</b> of vital nutrients; <b>anemia</b> (iron deficiency or malabsorption) is common. <b>Severe malabsorption</b> is more common than in UC → patients can become <b>very malnourished and debilitated</b>. Hemorrhage is more typical of UC but can occur. <b>Cancer</b> of the small bowel or colon is <b>rare</b>, after <b>15–20 years</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Fistulas and obstruction</b> — <b>fistulas are common in Crohn's but rare in UC</b>: between bowel segments, to the <b>skin (cutaneous)</b>, as <b>perirectal abscesses</b>, or into the <b>bladder or vagina</b> (Fig. 49.4). Obstruction comes first from <b>inflammation and edema</b>, later from <b>fibrosis and scar tissue</b> that narrow the bowel. <b>Most patients need surgery at some point</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Who and why</b> — almost <b>a million</b> Americans; usually diagnosed at <b>15–35</b>. Causes: <b>genetic, immune and lifestyle</b> factors. About <b>20%</b> have a <b>family history</b>; a <b>NOD2 gene</b> mutation affects monocytes that normally recognize and destroy bacteria; some <b>Asian Americans</b> carry a <b>TNFSF15</b> variant linked to more fistulas and strictures. <b>TNF-alpha and interleukins</b> drive inflammation (many drugs block them). Risks: <b>smoking</b>, <b>urban living</b> (pollution), <b>Ashkenazi Jewish</b> background (most common; rising in non-White groups), diet <b>high in animal fat and sugar</b>. Bacterial infection can trigger flares.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History and abdomen</b> — ask about <b>unintentional weight loss</b>, stool <b>frequency and consistency</b>, <b>blood</b>, <b>fever</b> and <b>abdominal pain</b>; assess nutrition and hydration. Inspect for <b>distention, masses or visible peristalsis</b>, and the perianal area for <b>ulcerations, fissures (cracks) or fistulas</b>. Bowel sounds may be <b>decreased or absent</b> with severe inflammation or obstruction, or <b>high-pitched and rushing</b> over narrowed loops; guarding, masses, rigidity or tenderness on palpation.</div>\n<div><b>Symptoms</b> — most have <b>diarrhea, abdominal pain and low-grade fever</b> (fever is common with <b>fistulas, abscesses</b> and severe inflammation). Ileum only → <b>5–6 soft, loose stools a day</b>; <b>steatorrhea</b> (fatty stools) is common; stools may contain bright red blood. Pain is usually <b>constant, in the RLQ</b>, sometimes <b>periumbilical before and after BMs</b>; both lower quadrants if the lower colon is involved. <b>Fatigue</b> is very common — linked to <b>disease activity and anxiety</b> → teach <b>energy conservation</b>. <b>Weight loss</b> from catabolism, anorexia, malabsorption or self-restricted eating.</div>\n<div><b>Watch for (Action Alert)</b> — be especially alert for <b>peritonitis</b>, <b>small bowel obstruction</b>, and <b>nutritional and fluid imbalances</b> — early detection of a change reduces these life-threatening complications.</div>\n<div><b>Labs and imaging</b> — <b>anemia</b> (slow bleeding, poor nutrition); <b>low folic acid and vitamin B12</b> (malabsorption); <b>↓ albumin</b> (amino acid malabsorption, protein-losing enteropathy); <b>↑ CRP and ESR</b>; <b>WBCs in the urine (pyuria)</b> from ureteral obstruction or an <b>enterovesical (bowel-to-bladder) fistula</b>; with severe diarrhea or a fistula, losses of <b>potassium and magnesium</b>. <b>X-rays</b> show narrowing, ulcers, strictures and fistulas; <b>MRE</b> shows bowel activity.</div>\n<div><b>Psychosocial</b> — needs a complete psychosocial assessment — the chronic course and complications affect patients and families; assess <b>coping and support systems</b>; <b>clinical depression and severe anxiety</b> are common.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Drugs</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Mild to moderate</b> — <b>5-ASA</b> drugs may help (research results are mixed for Crohn's). Most patients have <b>moderate to severe</b> disease and need stronger drugs: <b>azathioprine</b> and <b>6-mercaptopurine</b> suppress the immune system and can lead to <b>serious infections</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Biologics</b> — when other drugs fail: <b>infliximab, adalimumab and certolizumab pegol</b> <b>inhibit TNF-alpha</b> to calm inflammation, and are <b>not given</b> with a history of <b>cancer, heart disease or multiple sclerosis</b>. <b>Natalizumab</b> works differently: it <b>keeps white cells from leaving the blood for the tissues</b> (below). <b>Exam note:</b> the textbook lists natalizumab <b>in the same group</b> as these TNF-alpha inhibitors and gives the whole group the same caution (<b>not with cancer, heart disease or MS</b>) — so a question that follows the book may group them. Hispanic, African American and Asian patients are <b>less likely to receive biologics</b> (a health-equity gap).</div>\n<div><b>Biologic teaching (Drug Alert)</b> — <b>infliximab</b> is given in a <b>health care setting</b> (parenteral). Teach <b>self-injection</b> for subcutaneous forms and to report <b>injection-site redness or swelling</b>; <b>headache, abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting</b> are common side effects. <b>Avoid crowds and people with infection</b>, and <b>report any infection — even a cold or sore throat — immediately</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Natalizumab and PML</b> — given <b>IV every 4 weeks</b> under supervision for moderate–severe Crohn's when other drugs fail. It can cause <b>progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML)</b> — a <b>deadly brain infection</b>. Make sure the patient is <b>free of all infections</b> before each dose and teach them to <b>report cognitive, motor or sensory changes immediately</b>. <b>Vedolizumab</b> (IV at weeks <b>0, 2 and 6</b>, then <b>every 8 weeks</b>) has not raised PML risk, but the FDA urges PML teaching.</div>\n<div><b>Steroids and antibiotics</b> — <b>glucocorticoids</b> work, but they <b>mask infection</b> — and <b>sepsis</b> can come from abscesses or fistulas → use <b>short term with caution</b> and watch for infection; <b>don't stop them abruptly</b> (<b>adrenal insufficiency</b>). <b>Ciprofloxacin and metronidazole</b> help with <b>fistulas, anorectal abscesses</b> and infection.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>UC and Crohn's look alike at the bedside, but <b>depth</b> separates them. UC stays in the <b>lining</b> — so it <b>bleeds</b>, and it lies in one continuous stretch that surgery can remove completely. Crohn's goes <b>through every layer</b> in patches — so it <b>scars (strictures)</b>, <b>tunnels into neighbors (fistulas)</b>, and is too widespread for surgery to cure. Location matters too: Crohn's favors the <b>terminal ileum</b>, which absorbs <b>vitamin B12</b> — hence the low B12 and the monthly injections.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Nutrition, fistulas and surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Nutrition</b> — long-standing deficits impair <b>fistula and wound healing</b>, waste <b>lean muscle</b>, weaken <b>immunity</b>, and raise illness and death. Severe flares → hospital for <b>bowel rest and TPN</b>; supplements such as <b>Ensure or Sustacal</b> add calories; <b>avoid GI stimulants</b> such as <b>caffeine and alcohol</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Fistula types</b> — common in flares: <b>enterovesical</b> (bowel–bladder), <b>enteroenteric</b> (bowel–bowel), <b>enterocutaneous</b> (bowel–skin), <b>enterovaginal</b> (bowel–vagina). Multiple fistulas bring <b>systemic infection, skin problems</b> (abscesses, fissures) and <b>malnutrition</b>. An <b>abscess</b> needs <b>incision and drainage (I&amp;D)</b>; a fistula needs nutrition and electrolyte therapy, skin care and infection prevention.</div>\n<div><b>Fistula priorities (Action Alerts)</b> — <b>nutrition and fluid and electrolyte balance</b> come first: GI secretions are <b>high in volume and rich in electrolytes and enzymes</b> → risk of <b>malnutrition, dehydration and hypokalemia</b>. Watch <b>urine output and daily weight</b> — a drop in either suggests <b>dehydration</b> → give fluids promptly. The patient needs <b>at least 3000 calories a day</b>; <b>TEN or TPN</b> if oral intake falls short; with the dietitian: <b>high-calorie, high-protein, high-vitamin, low-fiber</b> meals, supplements, <b>calorie counts</b>. Then <b>protect the skin</b>: drainage must <b>not touch the skin</b> — intestinal enzymes are <b>caustic</b>; clean promptly to prevent breakdown or fungal infection.</div>\n<div><b>Fistula skin and drainage</b> — consult the <b>CWOCN</b>. Drainage <b>&lt; 100 mL in 24 hours</b> → <b>skin barrier or dressing</b>; heavier drainage → a <b>pouch</b> (protects skin and <b>measures output</b>); the wafer is cut <b>1/8 inch</b> around the fistula (Fig. 49.5). <b>Antifungal powder</b> helps prevent or treat <b><i>Candida</b></i>. If pouching isn't possible: <b>low continuous wall suction</b> with the catheter <b>in the wound bed, not the fistula tract</b> (<b>not for long-term</b> use), or <b>negative-pressure wound therapy</b> (wound VAC), which helps the wound heal by <b>secondary intention</b> — not for patients at <b>risk of bleeding</b> or just to contain drainage. Watch for <b>abscess and sepsis</b>: fever, abdominal pain, <b>mental status change</b>, <b>rising WBC</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Surgery</b> — for failed medical therapy or complications — <b>perforation, massive hemorrhage, obstruction or strictures, abscesses, cancer</b>; a fistula may need <b>resection</b> of the diseased area. Crohn's surgery is <b>less successful than UC surgery</b> (the disease is so extensive). <b>Small bowel or ileocecal resection</b> can be <b>laparoscopic</b> (less pain, faster recovery) or open; <b>stricturoplasty</b> widens a stricture. Care is like any abdominal surgery.</div>\n<div><b>Going home</b> — like UC, but <b>more hospitalizations and readmissions</b> (<b>1 in 4</b> IBD patients readmitted within <b>90 days</b>; earlier GI follow-up appointments may help). Arrange <b>bathroom access and privacy</b> for fistula care. Teach the disease course, complications and drugs; <b>vitamin supplements, including monthly B12 injections</b> (the ileum can't absorb them); with the dietitian, a <b>low-residue, high-calorie diet</b> avoiding <b>milk, gluten (wheat) and caffeine</b>. <b>Rest periods</b> in flares; <b>stress management</b>, counseling, activity; <b>frequent colorectal cancer screening</b>. Support <b>complementary therapies</b> the patient finds helpful (e.g., <b>ginger</b>, acupuncture, hypnotherapy, ayurveda), though evidence is lacking. Ideally the patient is <b>independent in fistula care</b> before discharge (or a caregiver is taught); supplies via the case manager; <b>UOAA</b> support.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"49-diverticular-disease","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Diverticular disease","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What happens</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Terms</b> — <b>diverticula</b> are small pouches of <b>mucosa pushed out through the muscular wall</b> of the gut — most often the <b>colon</b>, usually the <b>sigmoid</b> (Fig. 49.6). <b>Diverticulosis</b> is the presence of many diverticula; <b>diverticulitis</b> is their <b>inflammation or infection</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Why they form</b> — the colon muscle <b>hypertrophies, thickens and becomes rigid</b>; <b>high pressure inside the lumen</b> pushes the mucosa and submucosa out through <b>weak points</b> — often where <b>blood vessels interrupt the muscle</b>. Weakness comes with <b>aging</b> or a <b>low-fiber diet</b> (less bulky stool, constipation).</div>\n<div><b>Why they get infected</b> — pouches without inflammation cause <b>few problems</b>. If <b>undigested food or bacteria are trapped</b>, the <b>blood supply falls</b>, bacteria invade → <b>diverticulitis</b> → <b>perforation</b> with a <b>local abscess</b> → <b>peritonitis</b>; <b>lower GI bleeding</b> can also occur.</div>\n<div><b>Who</b> — millions, often <b>without symptoms or awareness</b>; found in <b>two-thirds of adults over 80</b>; <b>more men</b> than women.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Diverticulosis</b> — usually <b>no signs or symptoms</b>; often found on <b>routine colonoscopy</b>. Ask about <b>intermittent left lower quadrant pain</b> and <b>constipation</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diverticulitis</b> — abdominal pain, most often in the <b>LLQ</b> — intermittent at first, then <b>steady</b> (sometimes above the pubic bone or on one side; <b>generalized if peritonitis develops</b>); <b>nausea and vomiting</b>; <b>fever</b> from low-grade to <b>101°F (38.3°C)</b>, chills, <b>tachycardia</b>; constipation from inflammation; rectal bleeding. Distention, tenderness, local muscle spasm, <b>guarding and rebound</b> with peritoneal irritation. <b>Generalized peritonitis</b> → profound guarding, widespread rebound, and <b>sepsis, hypotension or hypovolemic shock</b>. A <b>tender mass</b> may be felt on rectal exam; <b>orthostatic BP</b> changes; massive bleeding → <b>shock</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Tests</b> — diverticulosis: <b>no labs</b> needed. Diverticulitis: <b>↑ WBC</b>; <b>↓ Hgb and Hct</b> with chronic or severe bleeding; stool <b>occult blood</b> sometimes positive. <b>Abdominal x-ray</b> for <b>free air and fluid (perforation)</b>; <b>CT</b> for an <b>abscess or bowel thickening</b>; <b>ultrasound</b>. <b>Colonoscopy 4–8 weeks after</b> the acute phase to rule out a <b>tumor</b>, especially with rectal bleeding.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Treatment</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Who is admitted</b> — mild → <b>ambulatory care</b> (watch for prolonged fever, more pain, blood in the stool). Admit for moderate to severe disease, especially <b>older</b> patients or <b>complications</b>: <b>temperature &gt; 101°F (38.3°C)</b>, <b>persistent severe pain for more than 3 days</b>, and/or <b>lower GI bleeding</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs</b> — <b>broad-spectrum antibiotics</b> — <b>metronidazole with TMP/SMZ or ciprofloxacin</b>; a <b>mild analgesic</b> for pain; severe → <b>IV fluids</b> for dehydration and <b>IV drugs</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Rest the bowel</b> — <b>rest</b> in the acute phase; <b>no lifting, straining, coughing or bending</b> — raised intra-abdominal pressure can <b>perforate</b> the diverticulum. <b>Low fiber or clear liquids</b> by symptoms; severe → <b>NPO</b>, an <b>NG tube</b> for severe nausea, vomiting or distention, and IV fluids. Intake rises slowly with the dietitian, and <b>fiber is reintroduced gradually</b> once inflammation resolves and bowel function is normal. <b>No laxatives or enemas</b> — they increase motility.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults</b> — watch for drug side effects — <b>confusion and orthostatic hypotension</b>; they may <b>not have typical pain or fever</b>, so a <b>sudden change in mental status</b> may be the sign of active disease; <b>frequent abdominal assessments</b> (distention, tenderness); check stools for <b>occult or frank blood</b>; <b>low-fiber diet while active, high-fiber after</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Surgery</b> — rupture with <b>peritonitis</b>, <b>pelvic abscess</b>, <b>bowel obstruction</b>, fistula, persistent fever or pain, or <b>uncontrolled bleeding</b>. <b>Emergency surgery</b> for peritonitis, obstruction or pelvic abscess. The most common procedure is <b>colon resection with or without a colostomy</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Fiber flips between \"avoid\" and \"add\" because the problem flips. In <b>diverticulosis</b> the danger is <b>pressure</b>: small, hard, low-fiber stools make the colon squeeze harder, and that pressure pushes out new pouches — bulky high-fiber stool keeps it low. In <b>diverticulitis</b> a pouch is already <b>inflamed and may perforate</b>, so anything that works the bowel harder (fiber, laxatives, enemas, straining) loads a weak, infected wall. <b>Rest the bowel first; prevent with fiber later.</b></div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Prevention and teaching</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Fiber</b> — <b>avoid all fiber while symptoms are present</b> (it irritates), then add it gradually up to a <b>high-fiber diet</b>: <b>25–35 g a day</b>, rich in <b>cellulose and hemicellulose</b> (<b>wheat bran, whole-grain breads and cereals</b>) plus fresh fruits and vegetables for bulk. Add it <b>slowly</b> to avoid gas and cramping; a <b>bulk-forming laxative</b> (<b>psyllium</b>) increases stool size and consistency; <b>drink plenty of fluids</b> to prevent bloating.</div>\n<div><b>Foods and habits</b> — <b>avoid alcohol</b> (irritates the bowel). Foods with <b>seeds or indigestible material</b> that may block a pouch — <b>nuts, corn, popcorn, cucumbers, tomatoes, figs</b> — may need to be eliminated. <b>No laxatives other than bulk-forming types, and no enemas</b>. With a high-fiber diet (25–35 g a day) and these limits, the disorder usually causes no problems.</div>\n<div><b>Report</b> — signs of acute diverticulitis: <b>fever, abdominal pain, and bloody, mahogany or tarry stools</b>.</div>\n<div><b>After surgery</b> — written and oral <b>incision care</b> instructions and what to report; <b>colostomy care</b> if one was made; encourage talk about <b>body image and sexual concerns</b>; a <b>home care nurse</b> checks healing and the ostomy; offer an <b>ostomate or ostomy nurse</b> visit and the <b>UOAA</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Celiac disease and parasitic infections","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Celiac disease</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — a <b>multisystem chronic autoimmune</b> bowel disease: <b>chronic inflammation of the small-intestine lining</b> that can cause <b>bowel wall atrophy, malabsorption and diarrhea</b>. Caused by a mix of <b>genetic, immune and environmental</b> factors; risk is higher with other autoimmune diseases such as <b>rheumatoid arthritis and type 1 diabetes</b>. Main complications: <b>cancer</b> (<b>non-Hodgkin lymphoma</b> or GI cancers) and <b>nutrition deficiencies</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Signs (Box 49.5)</b> — remissions and flares, usually tied to <b>how well the diet is followed</b>. <b>Classic</b>: <b>weight loss, anorexia, diarrhea and/or constipation, steatorrhea, abdominal pain and distention, vomiting</b>. Some have <b>no symptoms</b>; others have <b>atypical</b> ones in any body system: <b>osteoporosis</b>, joint pain, <b>lactose intolerance</b>, <b>iron deficiency anemia</b>, depression, migraines, epilepsy, autoimmune disorders, stomatitis, early menopause, protein-calorie malnutrition.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnosis and treatment</b> — diagnosed by a <b>screening blood test and endoscopy</b>. A <b>gluten-free diet (GFD)</b> is the <b>only treatment</b> for remission — the lining usually <b>heals in about 2 years</b>. <b>Gluten</b> is the main substance in <b>wheat</b> and wheat products (bread, noodles, cookies). Teach patients to check for <b>hidden gluten</b> in <b>foods, food additives, drugs and cosmetics</b>; <b>vitamin and mineral supplements</b> replace what is missed; include a <b>registered dietitian</b> in long-term care.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>\"Gluten-free\" here is a <b>treatment, not a preference</b>. In celiac disease gluten is the <b>trigger</b> for the immune attack on the small-intestine lining. Remove the trigger and the lining heals (about 2 years); keep eating it — even hidden in a pill coating or a lipstick — and the <b>inflammation, malabsorption and cancer risk</b> continue. That is why flares track diet adherence so closely.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Parasitic infections (Table 49.5)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>How they spread</b> — parasites usually enter by the <b>mouth (oral-fecal)</b> — contaminated <b>food or water</b>, <b>oral-anal sex</b>, or contact with an infected person's <b>feces</b>. The main test is <b>stool analysis</b>; the <b>WBC can be very high</b> with dysentery.</div>\n<div><b>Giardiasis (<i>Giardia lamblia</i>)</b> — cysts and trophozoites cause <b>superficial invasion and inflammation of the small-intestine lining</b> → <b>diarrhea, malabsorption, weight loss, nutrient deficiencies</b>. The acute phase is <b>self-limiting</b>; a chronic phase can last <b>years</b>. Treatment: <b>metronidazole</b> (drug of choice) or <b>tinidazole</b>; stools checked <b>2 weeks after</b> treatment.</div>\n<div><b>Amebiasis (<i>Entamoeba histolytica</i>)</b> — most common in <b>crowded areas with poor sanitation</b>; <b>invades and ulcerates the large intestine</b>; it can cause <b>no symptoms</b> at all, or mild to severe ones. Mild to moderate: several <b>strongly foul-smelling stools a day</b>, maybe mucus but <b>no blood</b>, cramps, gas, fatigue, weight loss; remissions and recurrences. <b>Severe amebic dysentery</b>: frequent liquid foul stools with <b>mucus and blood</b>, <b>fever to 104°F (40°C)</b>, <b>tenesmus</b>, abdominal tenderness, vomiting. Colon ulcers can cause pain, bleeding and <b>obstruction</b>; rectal ulcers put <b>blood on formed stool</b>; rarely appendicitis or <b>perforation</b>. <b>Extraintestinal</b>: <b>amebic liver abscess</b> (fever, pain, enlarged liver) — it can <b>rupture</b> and kill.</div>\n<div><b>Treating amebiasis</b> — all types get <b>amebicides</b>: <b>metronidazole followed by a luminal agent such as paromomycin</b>. Severe dysentery → <b>IV fluids</b> and possibly <b>diphenoxylate with atropine</b> to slow the bowel; hospitalize for <b>extraintestinal disease or severe dehydration</b> (especially older adults); <b>asymptomatic, mild or moderate</b> disease is treated with drugs as an <b>outpatient</b>. Cure is judged by <b>at least 3 stools at 2–3-day intervals</b>, starting <b>2–4 weeks after</b> treatment — stress <b>follow-up and finishing all drugs</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Cryptosporidium</b> — most common in the <b>immunosuppressed</b>; the source is often <b>contaminated swimming pools</b>; mainly <b>diarrhea</b>; <b>self-limiting</b> with normal immunity. Immunosuppressed patients may get <b>paromomycin</b> (an aminoglycoside antibiotic) — teach that it can cause <b>dizziness</b>.</div>\n<div><b>History</b> — <b>travel</b> to areas where parasites are common (GI symptoms may start <b>1–2 weeks after returning</b>); <b>immigrants</b> may arrive infected; if <b>several people in a group</b> are ill, think of a <b>shared water supply</b> (<b>Giardia or Cryptosporidium</b>); <b>pork</b> → consider <b>trichinosis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Stop the spread (Action Alert)</b> — <b>handwashing is the best way to prevent spread</b> of parasitic infections. The patient is <b>contagious until amebicides kill the parasites</b>. Teach: <b>avoid contact with stool</b>; <b>keep toilets clean</b>; <b>wash hands meticulously with antimicrobial soap after BMs</b>; <b>bathe or shower daily</b>; <b>avoid dog and beaver stool</b>. <b>No sex with rectal contact until treatment is finished</b>; <b>all household and sexual partners need stool exams</b>; test a suspected <b>water supply</b> (wells, unfiltered water) — household infections are often from shared water.</div>\n<div><b>Chagas disease</b> — caused by <b><i>Trypanosoma cruzi</b></i>, spread mostly in poor areas of <b>Latin America</b> by the <b>triatomine (\"kissing\") bug</b>. Acute infection → an asymptomatic period → <b>chronic</b> disease with <b>dysrhythmias or heart failure</b> and <b>dilation of the colon or esophagus</b> (impaired digestion and elimination). About <b>300,000</b> in the US (mostly the south); spread by <b>blood transfusion and organ transplant</b>; a CDC <b>neglected parasitic infection</b> expected to increase.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every organism in this box travels the same road: <b>feces to mouth</b>. That is why the teaching is all about <b>stool</b> (avoid contact, clean toilets, wash after BMs), <b>shared water</b> (test the well) and <b>close contacts</b> (examine the household and partners). Break the road anywhere and the cycle stops — which is why <b>handwashing is the best prevention</b>.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"49-adpie","ch":"49","chTitle":"Inflammatory intestinal conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Inflammation and infection of the bowel threaten the same things — <b>fluid balance, nutrition and skin</b> — and can turn into <b>peritonitis, bleeding or sepsis</b>. The nurse tracks stools, pain, vital signs and weight, protects skin from digestive enzymes, guards against spreading infection, and teaches patients to live with a chronic disease: drugs, diet, an ostomy and infection precautions (handwashing after every bowel movement, separate utensils and towels with infectious diarrhea, avoiding crowds and sick people on steroids or biologics).</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div><b>order of symptoms</b> (pain before or after vomiting); pain character, location and spread (periumbilical → McBurney; LLQ); fever; <b>travel</b>, restaurants, raw foods, <b>group illness or shared water</b>; <b>antibiotics in the past 2–3 months</b> (<i>C. difficile</i>), <b>NSAIDs</b>; family history of IBD; <b>smoking</b>; diet (fiber, fat, sugar, lactose, gluten); stool <b>number, blood, mucus, fat</b>; weight loss and fatigue; stress and flares; extraintestinal symptoms.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical</div>VS — <b>fever, tachycardia, hypotension, orthostatic changes, narrowing pulse pressure</b>; the abdomen — <b>rigidity (boardlike)</b>, <b>rebound</b>, guarding, distention, bowel sounds; McBurney point; LLQ tenderness; perianal <b>fissures and fistulas</b>; hydration (turgor, mucous membranes, <b>urine output, daily weight</b>); <b>mental status</b> (older adults); joints, mouth, eyes, skin; <b>stoma color</b> and output; fistula drainage and skin.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Labs and tests</div><b>WBC</b> (~20,000 peritonitis; 10,000–18,000 with shift to the left in appendicitis; &gt; 20,000 = perforation?); <b>blood cultures</b>; <b>CRP and ESR</b>; <b>Hgb/Hct</b>; <b>Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻, Mg²⁺</b>; <b>albumin</b>, folate, <b>B12</b>; BUN, creatinine, ABGs; x-ray <b>free air</b>; ultrasound, CT, <b>MRE</b>, colonoscopy; <b>stool analysis</b> (parasites, <i>C. diff</i>, cultures); celiac <b>blood test + endoscopy</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Psychosocial</div>fear and anxiety with acute illness; with chronic IBD — fear of incontinence (<b>\"tied to the toilet\"</b>), <b>anxiety and depression</b>, body image after an ostomy, sexual concerns, social isolation, coping and support, cancer worries, cost of supplies and food, access to care.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>peritonitis: <b>acute pain</b> and <b>potential fluid volume shift</b>. UC: <b>diarrhea</b>, <b>pain</b>, <b>potential lower GI bleeding and anemia</b>. Crohn's and fistulas: <b>malnutrition, dehydration, hypokalemia, skin breakdown, sepsis</b>. Gastroenteritis: <b>dehydration</b> (older adults) and <b>spread of infection</b>. Appendicitis and diverticulitis: <b>perforation → peritonitis</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>pain <b>2–3 or less on a 0–10 scale</b> (or acceptable); <b>fluid and electrolyte balance</b> (stable weight, adequate urine output); <b>fewer diarrheal episodes and formed stools</b>; <b>no lower GI bleeding</b>; the patient <b>self-manages the ileostomy or ileoanal pouch</b>; fistulas heal with intact skin and adequate nutrition; no spread of infection to others.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Acute abdomen</div>peritonitis: <b>frequent VS for septic shock</b>, <b>broad-spectrum antibiotics</b>, <b>IV fluids</b>, NG decompression, <b>strict asepsis</b> with drains and wounds, compare irrigation in vs out; after surgery <b>hourly LOC, VS, respiratory status and I&amp;O</b>, <b>semi-Fowler</b>. Appendicitis: <b>NPO</b>, IV fluids, semi-Fowler, opioids and antibiotics once confirmed — <b>no laxatives, enemas or heat</b>. Diverticulitis: antibiotics, <b>rest</b>, <b>no lifting, straining or bending</b>, low fiber or clear liquids (NPO if severe), <b>no laxatives or enemas</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Chronic IBD</div><b>stool diary</b> and weights; drugs — <b>5-ASA</b> (sulfasalazine: <b>sulfa allergy, folic acid</b>), <b>steroids</b> (taper; never stop suddenly), <b>biologics</b> (<b>avoid crowds, report infection</b>; <b>natalizumab → PML signs</b>); bowel rest, TPN or elemental formulas; limit caffeine, alcohol and lactose; monitor for <b>bleeding</b> (Rapid Response Team, two large-bore IVs); <b>antidiarrheals short term only</b>; fistula care — <b>≥ 3000 kcal</b>, I&amp;O and daily weights, <b>keep drainage off skin</b>, CWOCN; ileostomy — <b>stoma pink to cherry red</b>, <b>+ 500 mL fluid/day</b>, barrier, <b>no laxatives</b>, knee-chest if it stops.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Infection and celiac</div>gastroenteritis: <b>oral fluids or ORT</b>, IV if severe; <b>no antimotility drugs</b> unless prescribed (no diphenoxylate in older adults); perianal skin care, <b>sitz baths</b>; <b>handwashing ≥ 30 seconds</b>, separate utensils, no food handling. Parasites: <b>metronidazole</b> (± paromomycin), stool rechecks, <b>household and partner testing</b>, hygiene and water testing. Celiac: <b>strict gluten-free diet</b>, hidden gluten, supplements, dietitian.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>pain is controlled as the infection resolves; vital signs, weight, urine output and electrolytes are stable; fever and WBC fall; diarrhea decreases and stools form; <b>no bleeding</b>; the stoma stays pink and the patient can <b>demonstrate pouch care</b> and say when to call (dark or purple stoma, heavy bleeding, <b>no output with cramping and distention</b>, signs of dehydration); fistula output falls with intact skin and stable weight; the patient follows the diet (high fiber after diverticulitis, gluten-free in celiac), knows the drug precautions (folic acid with sulfasalazine; report any infection on steroids or biologics; never stop steroids suddenly; no antidiarrheals for infectious diarrhea) and keeps <b>colonoscopy</b> and follow-up appointments.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"50-overview","ch":"50","chTitle":"Liver conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">50 · Chapter 50</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cellular regulation · infection · inflammation · nutrition</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Liver conditions</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> The liver does <b>more than 400 jobs</b> — it makes albumin and clotting factors, clears ammonia, bilirubin and drugs, stores vitamins and makes bile — so when it fails, every system shows it. The <b>cellular regulation</b> exemplar is <b>cirrhosis</b>: scar tissue blocks blood flow through the liver, pressure backs up into the <b>portal vein</b>, and the patient develops <b>ascites, varices that can bleed, jaundice and encephalopathy</b>. The <b>infection</b> exemplar is <b>viral hepatitis</b> (A through E), the leading road to cirrhosis and liver cancer. <b>Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease</b> and <b>liver transplantation</b> round out the chapter. For the nurse: weigh daily, watch for <b>bleeding and confusion</b>, give lactulose to a goal, protect the patient from <b>alcohol, acetaminophen and other liver-toxic drugs</b>, and teach how each hepatitis spreads.</p><div id=\"ch50-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Portal blood from the gut flows through the liver at low pressure (about 3 mm Hg); hepatocytes make albumin, prothrombin and other clotting factors, turn ammonia into urea, conjugate bilirubin into bile, store vitamins and break down drugs and hormones.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Hepatocytes are destroyed (alcohol, hepatitis B or C, fat, drugs, biliary disease) and replaced by <b>fibrous scar and nodules</b>; blood and bile can't flow through, so <b>portal pressure rises to 10 mm Hg or more</b> and the liver stops doing its jobs. In hepatitis a virus inflames the liver cells.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Fatigue, weight change, anorexia</b> early; later <b>jaundice and icterus, ascites with bulging flanks, caput medusae, spider angiomas, palmar erythema, petechiae and bruises, pruritus, fetor hepaticus, asterixis</b>, confusion, hematemesis or melena; in hepatitis, RUQ tenderness, <b>dark urine and clay-colored stools</b>.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>AST, ALT, LDH</b> up (ALT most liver-specific; AST/ALT &gt; 1 in alcoholic disease); <b>albumin low, PT/INR long, platelets low, ammonia high</b>, bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase up; ultrasound first, <b>transient elastography</b> (&gt; 13 kPa = cirrhosis), biopsy, EGD for varices; <b>hepatitis serology</b> (HAV IgM, HBsAg, anti-HCV and HCV RNA).</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Sodium restriction, diuretics</b> (spironolactone with furosemide), <b>paracentesis</b>, TIPS; <b>propranolol</b> to prevent variceal bleeding, <b>octreotide + banding</b> for active bleeding; <b>lactulose</b> (and rifaximin) for ammonia; rest and nutrition in hepatitis; <b>antivirals</b> for chronic B and C; <b>liver transplant</b> for end-stage disease.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Variceal hemorrhage and hypovolemic shock</b>, <b>hepatic encephalopathy to coma</b>, <b>hepatorenal syndrome</b> (often the cause of death), <b>spontaneous bacterial peritonitis</b>, breathing trouble from massive ascites, liver cancer, fulminant hepatitis; after transplant, rejection and infection.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Vomiting blood or black stools</b>, a fall in BP with rising pulse, <b>new confusion or asterixis</b>, urine output <b>under 500 mL a day</b>, fever or abdominal pain with ascites, SpO₂ falling with ascites; after transplant, <b>fever, RUQ pain, rising jaundice or bloody oozing</b>.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Hepatocytes injured (alcohol, virus, fat)<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>inflammation → <b>fibrous scar and nodules</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>blood can't pass through → <b>portal hypertension</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>blood backs up into spleen, esophagus, abdomen → <b>splenomegaly, varices, ascites</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>liver stops making <b>albumin and clotting factors</b> and clearing <b>ammonia and bilirubin</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span><b>bleeding, encephalopathy, jaundice, kidney failure</b></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Compensated vs decompensated cirrhosis</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Compensated</b>: the liver is scarred but still does its essential work, so there are few or no symptoms — it is often found by accident (abnormal liver tests or a <b>low platelet count</b> on routine labs). <b>Decompensated</b>: liver failure is obvious — <b>ascites, jaundice, GI bleeding, spontaneous bruising, encephalopathy</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Hepatocellular vs obstructive jaundice</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Hepatocellular</b>: sick liver cells can't process and excrete bilirubin, so it builds up in the blood. <b>Intrahepatic obstructive</b>: swelling, fibrosis or scar in the small bile channels stops bile from leaving. Both cause <b>pruritus</b>; blocked bile flow also gives <b>clay-colored stools</b> (less urobilinogen reaches the gut) and <b>dark urine</b> (bilirubin spills into it).</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Hepatitis by route</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>A and E — fecal-oral</b> (contaminated food or water, shellfish, poor sanitation, oral-anal sex); acute and self-limiting, no chronic form. <b>B, C and D — blood and body fluids</b> (needles, sex, birth, transfusions before 1992, hemodialysis); <b>B and C can become chronic</b> and lead to <b>cirrhosis and liver cancer</b>. <b>D</b> only infects people who already have <b>B</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Lactulose</b> draws water into the colon and traps ammonia there so it leaves in stool — goal <b>2–3 soft stools a day</b>. <b>Propranolol</b> (nonselective beta blocker) slows the heart and lowers portal pressure so varices are less likely to bleed. <b>Octreotide</b> constricts splanchnic vessels to cut blood flow to bleeding varices. <b>Spironolactone + furosemide</b> pull off ascites while balancing potassium. <a href=\"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Daily weight</b> (same scale, every morning before breakfast — the most reliable measure of fluid retention), abdominal girth, I&amp;O, edema, electrolytes (Na⁺, K⁺), BUN and creatinine, <b>mental status, asterixis and fetor hepaticus</b>, stools (blood; 2–3 soft stools on lactulose), vomitus for blood, platelets and PT/INR, SpO₂ and lung sounds, skin and pruritus, signs of alcohol withdrawal.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Hematemesis or melena</b>, hypotension and tachycardia (variceal bleeding → shock); <b>new or worse confusion, asterixis</b> (encephalopathy); urine output <b>&lt; 500 mL/24 hr</b> with rising BUN and creatinine (<b>hepatorenal syndrome</b>); fever, abdominal pain or rigidity with ascites (<b>SBP</b>); falling SpO₂ or dyspnea; tremor, hallucinations, tachycardia and diaphoresis after stopping alcohol (<b>withdrawal</b>).</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why ascites forms</b> — two pressures push fluid into the abdomen at once. <b>Portal hypertension</b> raises the hydrostatic pressure in the vessels draining the gut, forcing plasma out; and the failing liver makes <b>less albumin</b> while protein leaks into the peritoneal fluid, so <b>colloid osmotic pressure falls</b> and nothing pulls fluid back. The result is <b>third spacing</b> — the patient can be <b>hypovolemic and edematous at the same time</b>. Massive ascites then squeezes renal blood flow, the kidney turns on <b>renin-angiotensin</b>, and the retained <b>sodium and water</b> make still more ascites.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why the patient bleeds three ways</b> — (1) <b>Varices</b>: portal blood detours through thin esophageal, gastric and rectal veins that stretch and can rupture. (2) <b>Clotting factors</b>: the liver makes <b>less prothrombin</b>, and with less bile it can't absorb <b>vitamin K</b>, so factors <b>II, VII, IX and X</b> fall — PT/INR lengthens. (3) <b>Platelets</b>: blood backs into the spleen, which enlarges and <b>destroys platelets</b> — <b>thrombocytopenia is often the first clinical sign of liver dysfunction</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why ammonia builds up</b> — gut bacteria break down <b>protein</b> (food, or <b>blood</b> from a GI bleed) into <b>ammonia</b>. Normally portal blood carries it to the liver, which converts it to urea for the kidneys to excrete. In cirrhosis blood is <b>shunted around the liver</b>, and the cells that remain can't keep up, so ammonia and <b>GABA</b> reach the brain → <b>hepatic encephalopathy</b>. Ammonia level doesn't match symptoms closely — some patients are very confused with modest levels — so the nurse <b>treats the patient, not just the number</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why bilirubin shows up in skin, urine and stool</b> — old red cells release bilirubin that the liver <b>conjugates</b> and sends out in <b>bile</b>; gut bacteria turn it into urobilinogen, which colors stool brown. When liver cells fail or bile channels swell shut, bilirubin backs up into the blood → <b>jaundice, icterus and pruritus</b>; the water-soluble form spills into <b>urine</b> (dark, tea-colored), and less reaches the gut, so stools turn <b>light or clay-colored</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Most of cirrhosis comes from two failures at once: blood <b>can't get through</b> the scarred liver (portal hypertension → varices, splenomegaly, ascites), and the liver <b>can't do its chemistry</b> (no albumin, no clotting factors, no ammonia clearance, no bilirubin excretion). Sort any sign into one of those two piles and the nursing action follows — pressure problems need volume and bleeding care; chemistry problems need lactulose, vitamin K, skin care and drug caution.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"50-cirrhosis-the-cellular-regulation-exempl","ch":"50","chTitle":"Liver conditions","title":"Cirrhosis — the cellular regulation exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The disease</b> — <b>extensive, irreversible scarring</b> of the liver, usually from long-standing inflammation and death of liver cells. Bands of <b>fibrous tissue</b> and <b>nodules</b> distort the liver, block bile ducts and squeeze blood and lymph vessels. Early on the liver is <b>large and firm</b>; as the disease advances it <b>shrinks and hardens</b> over weeks to years, and liver enzymes rise. Some patients have <b>no symptoms until a serious complication</b> appears.</div>\n<div><b>Three types (by cause)</b> — <b>Postnecrotic</b> — after viral hepatitis (especially <b>hepatitis C</b>) or certain drugs and toxins. <b>Alcoholic (Laennec's)</b> — from chronic alcohol use. <b>Biliary (cholestatic)</b> — from long-term bile duct obstruction or autoimmune disease (primary biliary cirrhosis, PBC).</div>\n<div><b>Compensated vs decompensated</b> — <b>compensated</b>: scarred but still doing its essential jobs, with few symptoms; <b>decompensated</b>: clear signs of liver failure. How sick the patient gets depends on how much liver is damaged.</div>\n<div><b>The main complications</b> — <b>portal hypertension</b>; <b>ascites and esophageal varices</b>; <b>biliary obstruction</b> (jaundice, vitamin K loss); <b>hepatic encephalopathy</b>; also <b>hepatorenal syndrome</b>, <b>spontaneous bacterial peritonitis</b> and breathing trouble from massive ascites.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Causes and risk</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Most common causes (US)</b> — <b>chronic alcohol use, chronic viral hepatitis and bile duct disease</b>; <b>NAFLD/NASH</b> increasingly. <b>Hepatitis C</b> is the <b>leading cause of cirrhosis and liver cancer in the US</b> — decades of inflammation scar the liver, and <b>alcohol speeds it up</b>. <b>Hepatitis B and D</b> are the most common causes <b>worldwide</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Alcohol</b> — alcohol is <b>directly toxic to hepatocytes</b>: the liver swells with fat and inflammatory cells (<b>alcoholic hepatitis</b>), then fibroblasts lay down collagen as malnutrition and drinking continue. <b>Fatty infiltration and inflammation are reversible if alcohol stops</b>; if it continues, cell death leads to widespread scar. Long-term <b>cocaine</b> and other illicit drugs damage the liver the same way. <b>Binge drinking</b> raises the risk of hepatitis and fatty liver.</div>\n<div><b>Women need less alcohol to get cirrhosis</b> — about <b>2–3 drinks a day for at least 10 years</b> can be enough for women; men reach the same damage at about <b>6 drinks a day</b> over the same time. Smaller amounts over years can still cause memory loss from alcohol toxicity to the cerebral cortex.</div>\n<div><b>Veterans and hepatitis C</b> — veterans — especially of the <b>Vietnam and post-Vietnam era</b> — are at high risk from blood exposure helping wounded comrades, shared razors and toothbrushes, unsanitary tattoos, and the <b>needleless \"jet gun\"</b> once used for mandatory vaccinations. <b>Ask every veteran whether they have been tested for hepatitis C</b>; if not, explain how or arrange testing.</div>\n<div><b>Prevention and equity</b> — <b>Healthy People 2030</b> (Box 50.2): <b>reduce cirrhosis deaths</b> and <b>increase the proportion of people who have cleared hepatitis C</b>. Teach people to avoid the risk factors for cirrhosis and hepatitis C. Compared with White patients, <b>Black patients with cirrhosis</b> have more admissions, longer stays and more 90-day readmissions despite the same quality of care — linked to transportation, access and more coronary disease and diabetes. Connect patients with <b>transportation and access resources</b> and teach self-management of other chronic diseases.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","ch":"50","chTitle":"Liver conditions","title":"Portal hypertension, ascites and varices","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Portal hypertension</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — a <b>persistent rise in portal vein pressure from about 3 mm Hg to at least 10 mm Hg</b> because scar resists blood flow through the liver. Blood looks for <b>collateral routes</b> around the blockage.</div>\n<div><b>Where the blood goes</b> — back into the <b>spleen</b> (<b>splenomegaly</b>), and into the veins of the <b>esophagus, stomach, intestines, abdominal wall and rectum</b>, which dilate. That gives <b>ascites, esophageal varices, caput medusae</b> (swollen veins radiating from the umbilicus) and <b>hemorrhoids</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Splenomegaly and platelets</b> — the enlarged spleen <b>destroys platelets</b> → <b>thrombocytopenia</b> and bleeding risk. A low platelet count is <b>often the first clinical sign of liver dysfunction</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Portal hypertensive gastropathy</b> — slow oozing from the stomach lining, with or without varices → <b>chronic blood loss, occult-positive stools and anemia</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Ascites</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — <b>free fluid in the peritoneal cavity</b> from <b>high hydrostatic pressure</b> (portal hypertension) plus <b>low albumin</b> (the liver can't make it and protein leaks into the fluid) → <b>low colloid osmotic pressure</b> → fluid shifts out of the vessels (<b>third spacing</b>). The patient can have <b>hypovolemia and edema at the same time</b>. Massive ascites narrows the renal arteries → <b>renin-angiotensin</b> → <b>sodium and water retention</b> → more ascites.</div>\n<div><b>What it looks like</b> — a <b>distended abdomen with bulging flanks</b>, a <b>protruding umbilicus</b>, <b>caput medusae</b>; <b>orthopnea and dyspnea</b> as the fluid limits lung expansion; trouble standing straight and with balance; <b>umbilical or inguinal hernias</b> from the pressure. <b>Small amounts are hard to detect</b>, especially in obese patients.</div>\n<div><b>Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP)</b> — bowel bacteria migrate through the bowel wall and lymphatics into the ascitic fluid — more likely with very advanced disease. Signs vary: <b>malaise, fever, chills, abdominal pain and tenderness</b> — or almost nothing, sometimes only <b>worse encephalopathy or jaundice</b>. Confirmed by <b>paracentesis</b> (cell count and culture); <b>more than 250 PMN leukocytes</b> suggests treatment is needed. Treated with <b>IV cephalosporins or fluoroquinolones</b>; after an episode, a <b>daily low-dose maintenance antibiotic</b> may follow.</div>\n<div><b>Breathing trouble from ascites</b> — the book calls it <b>hepatopulmonary syndrome</b>: massive ascites raises abdominal pressure, limiting <b>chest expansion and diaphragm movement</b> → <b>dyspnea</b>. <i>(Other sources use the term for low oxygen caused by widened vessels in the lungs; either way, watch SpO₂.)</i></div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Esophageal varices and biliary obstruction</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Varices</b> — blood backed up from the liver enters the <b>esophageal and gastric veins</b>, which become <b>thin-walled, swollen and twisted</b>; bleeding risk rises with size (seen on endoscopy). Most are in the <b>distal esophagus</b>, but they also form in the stomach and rectum.</div>\n<div><b>Bleeding varices — an emergency</b> — with <b>low prothrombin</b> as well, bleeding can be <b>massive</b> → <b>hypovolemic shock</b>. It shows as <b>hematemesis</b> or <b>melena</b>, and the patient may <b>lose consciousness before any blood is seen</b>. It can start with no trigger, but anything that <b>raises abdominal pressure</b> (heavy lifting, vigorous exercise), <b>chest trauma</b> or <b>dry, hard food</b> in the esophagus makes it more likely. Variceal bleeds are the <b>most common cause of upper GI bleeding</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Biliary obstruction and vitamin K</b> — cirrhosis cuts <b>bile production</b>, so <b>fat-soluble vitamins</b> (A, D, E, <b>K</b>) aren't absorbed. Without vitamin K, factors <b>II, VII, IX and X</b> fall → <b>bleeding and easy bruising</b>, confirmed by coagulation studies. Some people are prone to <b>biliary cirrhosis</b> from gallbladder disease or <b>primary biliary cirrhosis</b> (autoimmune).</div>\n<div><b>Jaundice in cirrhosis</b> — <b>hepatocellular</b> — liver cells can't excrete bilirubin; or <b>intrahepatic obstructive</b> — edema, fibrosis or scar in the bile channels. Both cause <b>pruritus</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A patient with varices can bleed to death before vomiting a drop — the blood pools in the stomach and the first sign is the circulation failing. That is why the nurse treats a cirrhotic patient's <b>sudden tachycardia, low BP or fainting as a variceal bleed until proven otherwise</b>, and why simple things like <b>straining, heavy lifting and coarse dry food</b> are part of the teaching.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"50-hepatic-encephalopathy-hepatorenal-syndr","ch":"50","chTitle":"Liver conditions","title":"Hepatic encephalopathy, hepatorenal syndrome and other complications","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hepatic encephalopathy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — also called <b>portal-systemic encephalopathy (PSE)</b> — a cognitive syndrome from liver failure. Early: <b>sleep and mood disturbance, mental status changes, speech problems</b> — and it <b>may reverse with early treatment</b>. Later: lowered level of consciousness, impaired thinking, neuromuscular signs. It develops <b>slowly in chronic</b> disease (often missed until late) and <b>fast in acute</b> liver failure.</div>\n<div><b>Why it happens</b> — portal blood is <b>shunted around the liver</b>, so substances from the gut aren't detoxified — <b>ammonia</b> (from protein breakdown) and <b>GABA</b> rise. <b>Ammonia doesn't track symptoms reliably</b>: some patients are badly impaired with modest levels, and some have high levels without encephalopathy.</div>\n<div><b>Stages (Box 50.1)</b> — <b>I</b> — subtle: personality and behavior changes (agitation, belligerence), mood swings (euphoria, depression), poor concentration, fatigue, drowsiness, <b>slurred or slow speech, sleep disturbance</b>. <b>II</b> — <b>confusion, disorientation</b>, <b>asterixis</b>. <b>III</b> — marked confusion, <b>stuporous but arousable</b>, abnormal EEG, muscle twitching, <b>hyperreflexia</b>, asterixis. <b>IV</b> — <b>unarousable</b>, no response to pain, <b>no asterixis</b>, <b>positive Babinski</b>, rigidity, <b>fetor hepaticus</b>, seizures. Patients can drift up and down between stages.</div>\n<div><b>What sets it off</b> — <b>high-protein diet, infection, hypovolemia, hypokalemia, constipation, GI bleeding</b> (a big protein load in the gut), and <b>drugs</b> — hypnotics, opioids, sedatives, analgesics, diuretics, illicit drugs. Prognosis depends on the cause, the trigger and how much liver function is left.</div>\n<div><b>Asterixis and fetor hepaticus</b> — <b>asterixis</b> is a coarse \"<b>hand-flapping</b>\" tremor — quick, irregular flexing and extending at the wrists and fingers. <b>Fetor hepaticus</b> is a <b>fruity or musty, sweet breath odor</b>. Both mean <b>worsening encephalopathy</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Kidneys and other complications</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Hepatorenal syndrome</b> — kidney failure in end-stage liver disease and a sign of <b>poor prognosis — often the cause of death</b>. Signs: a <b>sudden drop in urine output (&lt; 500 mL/24 hr)</b>, <b>rising BUN and creatinine</b> with <b>low urine sodium</b>, and <b>high urine osmolarity</b>. It often follows <b>GI bleeding or the onset of encephalopathy</b>, and can complicate acute hepatitis and fulminant liver failure.</div>\n<div><b>Infection</b> — besides SBP, infection is one of the most common <b>triggers of an acute variceal bleed</b>; up to <b>20%</b> of cirrhotic patients admitted with upper GI bleeding already have a bacterial infection.</div>\n<div><b>Liver cancer</b> — scar from chronic <b>HBV or HCV</b> often leads to cirrhosis, the main risk factor for <b>primary liver cancer</b>. Treatments include <b>TACE</b> (transarterial chemoembolization), cryotherapy, ablation, chemotherapy and <b>SIRT</b> (selective internal radiation). The liver is also a common site of <b>metastases</b> from other cancers, unrelated to hepatitis.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The kidneys in hepatorenal syndrome are not damaged themselves — they are starved. Fluid has shifted into the abdomen and the splanchnic vessels are dilated, so the kidney senses a low circulating volume, clamps down and holds every sodium it can. That is why the urine is <b>scant, concentrated and almost sodium-free</b>, and why a <b>GI bleed</b> (sudden volume loss) so often tips the patient into it.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","ch":"50","chTitle":"Liver conditions","title":"Assessing cirrhosis — history, signs and tests","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">History and psychosocial assessment</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Exposures</b> — age, gender and <b>job history</b> — especially exposure to <b>alcohol, prescribed and illicit drugs, herbal products and chemical toxins</b>, no matter how long ago; any <b>needlestick</b>; <b>sexual history and orientation</b> (<b>men who have sex with men</b> are at high risk for hepatitis A, B and C).</div>\n<div><b>Sensitive questions</b> — family history of <b>alcoholism or liver disease</b>; <b>how much alcohol</b> and over what period; illicit drugs by mouth, IV or nose; <b>tattoos</b> (when and where); <b>military or prison</b>; work as a <b>health care worker, firefighter or police officer</b>; length of sobriety if in recovery. <b>Explain why you are asking</b>, give <b>privacy</b>, and accept answers <b>without judgment</b> — patients may feel regret or embarrassment about behavior years ago.</div>\n<div><b>Past illness</b> — earlier <b>jaundice or hepatitis</b>, gallbladder or biliary disease (cholecystitis), viral infection, surgery, <b>blood transfusions</b>, autoimmune disease, obesity, abnormal lipids, heart failure, respiratory disease, liver injury.</div>\n<div><b>Mood and family</b> — personality, thinking and behavior change; <b>sleep disturbance, emotional lability, euphoria or depression</b>. Repeated hospitalizations strain the family emotionally, physically and financially, and substance use may continue as health worsens — involve <b>social work, substance-use counselors and mental health</b> professionals.</div>\n<div><b>Alcohol withdrawal</b> — if the patient is alcohol dependent, <b>prepare for withdrawal</b>. <b>Tremors (\"the jitters\") can start 6–8 hours</b> after the last drink. <b>Delirium tremens (DTs)</b>: acute confusion, anxiety, <b>delusions and hallucinations</b>, with <b>tachycardia, high BP and diaphoresis</b> — it can be a <b>medical emergency</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Physical signs</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Early (vague)</b> — <b>fatigue, significant weight change, anorexia, vomiting, abdominal pain and liver tenderness</b> (which patients often ignore). Compensated cirrhosis is often found <b>by accident</b> — abnormal liver tests or <b>thrombocytopenia</b> on routine, presurgical or insurance labs.</div>\n<div><b>Late-stage signs (Box 50.3)</b> — <b>jaundice and icterus</b>; <b>dry skin, pruritus</b>, rashes; <b>petechiae and ecchymoses</b>; <b>palmar erythema</b> (warm, bright red palms); <b>spider angiomas</b> (red center with radiating branches) on the nose, cheeks, upper chest and shoulders; <b>ascites</b>; <b>dependent edema</b> of the legs and sacrum; <b>deficiencies of vitamins A, D, E and K</b>. <b>GI bleeding, jaundice, ascites and spontaneous bruising</b> are what usually bring the patient in.</div>\n<div><b>Liver and spleen</b> — <b>hepatomegaly</b> is common early; the liver later becomes <b>small, hard and nodular</b>. <b>Splenomegaly</b> is common in <b>nonalcoholic</b> causes.</div>\n<div><b>Measuring abdominal girth</b> — patient <b>lying flat</b>; tape around the <b>largest diameter (usually over the umbilicus)</b>, read <b>at the end of exhalation</b>; <b>mark the skin and flanks</b> so the tape goes in the same place next time. But a <b>rapid weight gain is the most reliable sign of fluid retention</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Other findings</b> — check <b>vomitus and stool for blood</b> (frank or occult — from gastritis, ulcers or oozing varices); <b>fetor hepaticus</b>; <b>amenorrhea</b> in women; <b>testicular atrophy, gynecomastia and impotence</b> in men (hormones the liver normally inactivates). Check <b>neurologic status continually</b> — ask family about the patient's baseline — and look for <b>asterixis</b>; subtle personality change can progress to <b>coma</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Labs and imaging (Table 50.1)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Liver enzymes</b> — <b>AST, ALT and LDH rise</b> as inflamed cells leak them — but late in cirrhosis, burned-out cells may give <b>normal</b> values. <b>ALT is the most liver-specific</b> (AST is also in muscle, kidney, brain and heart). An <b>AST/ALT ratio above 1.0</b> usually points to <b>alcoholic</b> liver disease.</div>\n<div><b>Obstruction markers and bilirubin</b> — <b>alkaline phosphatase and GGT</b> rise with <b>biliary obstruction</b> (alkaline phosphatase also comes from bone and gut). <b>Total and indirect bilirubin</b> rise in cirrhosis; urine bilirubin and <b>urine urobilinogen</b> rise; <b>fecal urobilinogen falls</b> with biliary obstruction → <b>clay-colored stools</b>.</div>\n<div><b>What the failing liver can't make</b> — <b>albumin low</b> (less made → fluid leaks into tissues, ascites); <b>total protein low</b> in chronic disease (high in acute); <b>globulin high</b> (immune response). <b>PT/INR prolonged</b> (less prothrombin). <b>Platelets low</b> — the classic <b>thrombocytopenia of cirrhosis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Blood counts, ammonia, kidneys, sodium</b> — <b>anemia</b> (low RBC, Hgb, Hct) and a low WBC; <b>ammonia high</b> in advanced disease or encephalopathy; <b>creatinine high</b> with hepatorenal syndrome; <b>dilutional hyponatremia</b> with ascites. Signs of <b>hypovolemia</b>: <b>high BUN, low serum protein, high hematocrit</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Imaging</b> — plain x-ray (big liver or spleen, massive ascites); CT; <b>MRI</b> shows masses and helps tell benign from malignant. <b>Ultrasound is often the first test</b> — ascites, hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, stones or duct obstruction; with <b>Doppler</b> it finds <b>portal vein thrombosis</b> and the direction of flow. Arteriography or a <b>portal venogram</b> if ultrasound isn't conclusive.</div>\n<div><b>Elastography, biopsy, endoscopy</b> — <b>ultrasound transient elastography</b> measures liver <b>stiffness</b> without a needle: <b>normal about 5.0 kPa</b>, higher = fibrosis, <b>above 13 kPa = cirrhosis</b> (less reliable with heavy ascites or obesity). <b>Liver biopsy</b> shows the exact disease but risks <b>bleeding</b>, so it may be done <b>through the jugular vein</b> into the hepatic vein. <b>EGD</b> (under moderate sedation) finds <b>varices</b>, gastric irritation and ulcers; <b>ERCP</b> views the biliary tree and allows stone removal, sphincterotomy, biopsy and stents.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The liver panel splits into two kinds of tests. <b>Enzymes (ALT, AST)</b> measure cells <b>breaking open</b> — loud in acute hepatitis, quiet in burned-out cirrhosis. <b>Synthetic tests (albumin, PT/INR)</b> measure what the liver can still <b>make</b> — they worsen as cirrhosis advances. So a patient with near-normal enzymes but <b>low albumin and a long INR</b> is sicker, not better.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","ch":"50","chTitle":"Liver conditions","title":"Managing ascites — diet, diuretics, paracentesis and TIPS","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Diet and drugs</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — (1) <b>fluid overload</b> from third spacing (ascites); (2) <b>potential for hemorrhage</b> from portal hypertension and varices; (3) <b>acute confusion</b> from high ammonia and/or <b>alcohol withdrawal</b>; (4) <b>pruritus</b> from high bilirubin and jaundice. Ascites goal: <b>less ascites and edema while keeping enough circulating volume</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Sodium restriction</b> — first step for early ascites: often a <b>1–2 g (about 2000 mg) sodium</b> diet. With the <b>dietitian</b>, teach the patient and family to <b>read sodium labels</b> on all food and drinks and to leave out <b>table salt entirely</b>; flavor with <b>lemon, vinegar, parsley, oregano and pepper</b>. A taste for salt is learned — it fades with time.</div>\n<div><b>Nutrition</b> — late-stage patients are usually <b>malnourished</b>: give <b>IV thiamine, folate and multivitamins</b> — the liver <b>can't store vitamins</b>. In biliary cirrhosis there may be no bile to absorb <b>fat-soluble vitamins</b>. In one study, <b>51.9%</b> of transplant candidates were malnourished at the first visit and <b>61%</b> by transplant; most waited <b>over 180 days</b> and <b>over half were hospitalized</b> — screen and refer to the <b>dietitian</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diuretics</b> — to remove fluid and protect the heart and lungs. Monitor by <b>daily weight, I&amp;O, abdominal girth, peripheral edema and electrolytes</b>. <b>Loop diuretics</b> can cause <b>dehydration, hypokalemia and hyponatremia</b> — potassium supplements may be ordered. <b>Furosemide + spironolactone</b> together work differently and <b>balance sodium and potassium</b> (one wastes K⁺, the other spares it).</div>\n<div><b>SBP</b> — every patient with ascites can develop it: low-grade fever and poor appetite, or abdominal pain, fever and <b>mental status change</b>. Listen to bowel sounds and feel for <b>rigidity</b>. Treated with <b>IV cephalosporins or fluoroquinolones</b>, sometimes before culture results.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Paracentesis (Box 50.4) and breathing</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>When and how</b> — when ascites <b>interferes with breathing</b>. The provider places a <b>trocar catheter or drain</b> into the peritoneal cavity, <b>guided by ultrasound</b> — at the bedside, in interventional radiology or as an outpatient. A short-term drain may stay in while awaiting surgery, or a <b>tunneled drain</b> lets the patient or family drain fluid at home. A sample goes for <b>cell count and culture</b> if SBP is suspected.</div>\n<div><b>Before</b> — explain and answer questions; take <b>vital signs and weight</b>; have the patient <b>void first</b> (protects the bladder from the needle); <b>head of bed up</b>.</div>\n<div><b>During and after</b> — VS per protocol; <b>measure and record the drainage exactly</b>, describe the fluid, <b>label and send specimens</b> and document that they were sent; after the catheter is out, <b>dressing</b> and check for <b>leaking</b>; <b>bed rest</b> per protocol; <b>VS and weight again</b> — document both weights (it should <b>drop</b>). At home, never remove <b>more than 2000 mL at one time</b> (risk of <b>hypovolemic shock</b>).</div>\n<div><b>Breathing support (Action Alert)</b> — <b>pulse oximetry</b>; ask for <b>oxygen</b> if needed; <b>HOB at least 30 degrees</b> or as high as the patient likes, with <b>feet elevated</b> to reduce ankle edema; listen to the lungs <b>every 4–8 hours</b> for crackles. Remind assistive personnel to <b>weigh daily, every morning before breakfast, on the same scale</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">TIPS</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — the <b>transjugular intrahepatic portal-systemic shunt</b> — a <b>nonsurgical</b> procedure by interventional radiology for <b>ascites or bleeding that hasn't responded</b> to other treatment (cirrhotic patients are poor surgical risks). Through a sheath in the <b>jugular vein</b>, a needle is pushed through the liver into the <b>portal vein</b>; a balloon widens the tract and a <b>stent</b> holds it open, so portal blood <b>bypasses the scarred liver</b>. Detour veins may also be <b>embolized</b> so they won't rebleed.</div>\n<div><b>Before and after</b> — ultrasound of the <b>jugular vein</b> beforehand (non-emergency); IV sedation or general anesthesia; ultrasound afterward to confirm flow, and <b>follow-up ultrasounds for a year</b> for patency. Usually <b>home in 1–2 days</b>; serious complications are uncommon.</div>\n<div><b>Watch for</b> — <b>hepatic encephalopathy</b> — blood now skips the liver's filtering, so toxins circulate; treated with <b>lactulose</b> and diet, or by <b>narrowing or closing the shunt</b> (which may bring the bleeding back). <b>Pulmonary artery pressure</b> can rise from the sudden extra blood to the right heart — treated with diuretics, so patients are checked for <b>right-sided heart failure</b> first.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>TIPS and the patient's own varices are the same idea — a detour around the liver. The detour lowers pressure, so ascites and bleeding improve, but every drop of blood that skips the liver also skips its <b>ammonia filter</b>. That trade-off is why encephalopathy is the complication to watch after TIPS, and why closing the shunt to fix confusion can bring the bleeding back.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"50-bleeding-varices-prevention-and-emergenc","ch":"50","chTitle":"Liver conditions","title":"Bleeding varices — prevention and emergency care","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Preventing a bleed</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Screen everyone</b> — <b>all patients with cirrhosis are screened for varices by endoscopy</b> so preventive therapy starts before they bleed.</div>\n<div><b>Propranolol</b> — a <b>nonselective beta blocker</b> prescribed to <b>prevent</b> bleeding — slowing the heart and lowering the <b>hepatic venous pressure gradient</b> reduces the chance of rupture. Watch for <b>bradycardia and hypotension</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Antibiotics on admission</b> — infection is one of the most common <b>triggers of an acute variceal bleed</b>, and up to <b>20%</b> of cirrhotic patients admitted with upper GI bleeding are infected — so <b>every cirrhotic patient with GI bleeding gets antibiotics on admission</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Stopping an active bleed</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Vasoactive drugs + endoscopy</b> — combined quickly. <b>Octreotide</b> constricts vessels and suppresses gastrin, serotonin and gut peptides, <b>cutting GI blood flow and portal pressure</b>; <b>vasopressin</b> does the same but is <b>rarely used</b> (very potent, many adverse effects).</div>\n<div><b>Banding and sclerotherapy</b> — <b>endoscopic variceal ligation (EVL, banding)</b> places small <b>\"O\" bands</b> around the base of each varix to cut its blood supply — the patient can't feel them. <b>Endoscopic sclerotherapy (EST)</b> injects a sclerosing agent into the varices; it can cause <b>mucosal ulceration</b> and more bleeding. Both control bleeding well and improve survival.</div>\n<div><b>Rescue therapy</b> — if bleeding recurs: a second endoscopy, <b>balloon tamponade with esophageal stents</b>, or a shunt (<b>TIPS</b>). The <b>balloon tamponade</b> tube goes through the nose into the stomach like an NG tube and its balloon presses on the bleeding varices — very effective but risks <b>aspiration, asphyxia and esophageal perforation</b>, so the patient is usually <b>intubated and ventilated first</b>. It is used when a second endoscopy or TIPS isn't possible.</div>\n<div><b>Nursing care during a bleed</b> — an <b>NG tube</b> may be placed to detect <b>new bleeding</b>; give <b>packed RBCs, fresh frozen plasma, albumin, platelets</b> (and dextran) through <b>large-bore IVs</b>; <b>vital signs every hour</b>; follow <b>PT, PTT, platelets and INR</b>. Bleeding from gastric ulcers may be treated with an <b>H₂ blocker or PPI</b> (ch 47).</div>\n<div><b>NCLEX pattern</b> — for active variceal bleeding, expect <b>octreotide and endoscopic therapy (sclerotherapy or banding)</b>; a <b>beta blocker</b> belongs to <b>prevention</b>. Lactulose treats ammonia, not bleeding — though blood in the gut <b>raises ammonia</b>, so encephalopathy often follows a bleed.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A bleed in cirrhosis is three problems at once: the vein is under pressure, the blood can't clot (low factors, low platelets), and the blood in the gut becomes an <b>ammonia load</b>. Care answers each one — <b>octreotide and banding</b> for the pressure and the vein, <b>plasma and platelets</b> for the clotting, and watching mental status (and giving lactulose) for the ammonia that follows.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","ch":"50","chTitle":"Liver conditions","title":"Confusion, pruritus and going home with cirrhosis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Preventing and managing confusion</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Goal</b> — no acute or chronic confusion; if it starts, <b>intervene early</b> for safety and to prevent death from encephalopathy — by <b>slowing ammonia buildup</b>. Assess neurologic status throughout treatment; <b>safety</b> comes first in a confused patient.</div>\n<div><b>Diet</b> — cirrhosis raises nutritional needs: <b>high carbohydrate, moderate fat, high protein</b>. With high ammonia and signs of encephalopathy: <b>moderate protein and fat with simple carbohydrates</b> — <b>no strict protein restriction</b>, because protein is needed for healing. Include the family; keep directions <b>brief and simple</b> (the patient is weak and forgetful); respect cost, culture and allergies. <b>Small, frequent meals</b> are tolerated best at home.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs used sparingly</b> — a failing liver can't clear most drugs — <b>limit opioids, sedatives and barbiturates</b>, especially after encephalopathy.</div>\n<div><b>Lactulose (or lactitol)</b> — a thick, sweet liquid given <b>by mouth or NG tube</b> at <b>any stage</b> of encephalopathy. It <b>draws water into the colon</b> (osmotic laxative) and keeps ammonia from being absorbed, so ammonia leaves in the stool. <b>Goal: 2–3 soft stools a day and less confusion.</b> Side effects: <b>bloating and cramping</b>; too many stools → <b>hypokalemia and dehydration</b>. Ammonia levels are checked but <b>don't always match symptoms</b>. Ask assistive personnel to help with <b>hygiene and skin care</b> to prevent breakdown.</div>\n<div><b>Rifaximin or neomycin</b> — nonabsorbable antibiotics if lactulose doesn't work or isn't tolerated: they <b>kill gut bacteria</b> that break down protein, so <b>less ammonia is made</b>. The book says these drugs <b>shouldn't be given together</b> <i>(current practice often adds rifaximin to lactulose for repeated episodes)</i>. <b>Neomycin</b> is given by mouth or as a <b>retention enema</b>, but it is <b>nephrotoxic</b> — not for long-term use or for patients with kidney disease. Older adults can get <b>weak and dehydrated</b> from many stools.</div>\n<div><b>Watch closely</b> — level of consciousness and orientation, <b>asterixis and fetor hepaticus</b> (worsening encephalopathy). <b>Thiamine and benzodiazepines</b> if the patient is at risk for <b>alcohol withdrawal</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Pruritus and going home</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Pruritus</b> — from bilirubin in the skin; <b>worse when warm, in the early evening and at night</b>. Keep the patient <b>cool</b>, <b>moisturize</b>, avoid skin <b>irritants</b>; <b>cool compresses</b> or <b>corticosteroid creams</b> help briefly; if not enough, an <b>SSRI such as sertraline</b> may help.</div>\n<div><b>Setting up home</b> — the case manager coordinates care; PT and OT for independence; <b>hospice</b> for end-stage disease. Bedroom <b>near the bathroom</b> (diuretics and lactulose mean frequent trips); a <b>bedside commode</b> if needed; <b>incontinence briefs</b> with confusion; <b>semi- to high-Fowler</b> or a <b>recliner with footrest</b> for breathlessness. Teach use of a <b>tunneled drain</b> — <b>no more than 2000 mL at a time</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Teaching</b> — <b>no alcohol, smoking or illicit drugs</b>; <b>avoid all OTC drugs — especially acetaminophen and NSAIDs</b> — and liver-toxic herbs, vitamins and minerals; avoid <b>excess fat-soluble vitamins, iron and niacin</b>; ask the provider before any supplement. Teach the family to <b>recognize encephalopathy</b> and that <b>constipation, bleeding and infection</b> can bring it on. <b>Report any GI bleeding or black stools at once.</b> Keep follow-up visits; a daily low-dose antibiotic may follow SBP.</div>\n<div><b>Support</b> — help with <b>alcohol abstinence</b> (individual or group therapy; <b>Alcoholics Anonymous, Al-Anon</b>; SMART Recovery in Canada); help find a friend, neighbor or sponsor if family ties are strained; spiritual, financial and social support; the <b>American Liver Foundation</b>. If not a transplant candidate, discuss <b>end-of-life care and hospice</b>; expect grief.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — less or no ascites; fluid and electrolyte balance; no hemorrhage (or quick control); no encephalopathy (or quick treatment); abstinence from alcohol or drugs and adequate nutrition.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Lactulose is dosed by its effect, not just by the clock. Two or three soft stools a day show it is pulling ammonia out of the colon; <b>no stools</b> means ammonia is building again (constipation is a trigger), and <b>many watery stools</b> mean the patient is losing water and potassium — and <b>hypokalemia and dehydration are themselves triggers</b> for encephalopathy.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"50-nonalcoholic-fatty-liver-disease-nafld-a","ch":"50","chTitle":"Liver conditions","title":"Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD and NASH)","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">NAFLD and NASH</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — the liver <b>stores too much fat, unrelated to alcohol</b>. It is tied to <b>obesity, high cholesterol and triglycerides, type 2 diabetes and metabolic syndrome</b>, so <b>preventing obesity and a healthy lifestyle</b> lower the risk. An estimated <b>100 million Americans</b> have it, and it is rising fast. <i>(Since 2023 it is also called MASLD — metabolic dysfunction–associated steatotic liver disease — and NASH is called MASH.)</i></div>\n<div><b>Genetics</b> — carriers of the <b>PNPLA3</b> gene variant are at higher risk; people of <b>Latino</b> descent carry it more often and have the <b>highest risk</b>.</div>\n<div><b>NASH</b> — <b>nonalcoholic steatohepatitis</b> — the more severe form, with <b>inflammation, liver-cell injury and fibrosis</b>. About <b>20%</b> of people with NAFLD develop NASH; both can progress to <b>cirrhosis, liver failure and liver cancer</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Assessment</b> — risk factors: <b>age, obesity, metabolic syndrome, diabetes</b>; many are <b>symptom-free for years</b>. Early: <b>fatigue and malaise</b>; ask about <b>RUQ pain</b> (full pain assessment) and <b>pruritus</b>; <b>jaundice</b> later.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnosis</b> — <b>ALT and AST rise</b>, but enzymes alone <b>can't confirm</b> NAFLD. <b>Transient elastography</b> and <b>liver biopsy</b> gauge damage and fibrosis and confirm NAFLD or NASH.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — the book says <b>no approved drugs</b> — care rests on <b>lifestyle change for weight and fat loss</b>: nutrition, exercise and behavior change, with a <b>dietitian</b> and a <b>mental health</b> practitioner because the changes are hard. <i>(Since this edition, the FDA has approved <b>resmetirom</b> (2024) and <b>semaglutide</b> (2025) for NASH/MASH with liver fibrosis.)</i></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>In NAFLD the liver is the overflow tank for fat the body can't handle — insulin resistance releases more fatty acids from fat tissue and the liver keeps making more. That is why the treatment is the same as for the metabolic syndrome itself: <b>losing weight and moving more</b> empty the tank, and the fat (and early inflammation) can recede before scar forms.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"50-viral-hepatitis-a-to-e-the-infection-exe","ch":"50","chTitle":"Liver conditions","title":"Viral hepatitis A to E — the infection exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">How hepatitis damages the liver</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Definition and causes</b> — <b>widespread inflammation and infection of liver cells</b>. <b>Viral</b> hepatitis is the most common type (A, B, C, D, E; some cases are non-A–E). Others: <b>toxic and drug-induced</b> hepatitis from industrial toxins, alcohol and drugs, and hepatitis that rides along with other viruses (<b>Epstein-Barr, herpes simplex, varicella-zoster, cytomegalovirus</b>). All cases must be <b>reported to the local health department</b> (which notifies the CDC). Genetics play little role.</div>\n<div><b>What happens in the liver</b> — the liver <b>swells with inflammatory cells and fluid</b> → <b>RUQ pain</b>. Widespread inflammation, necrosis and regrowth distort the lobules and <b>raise portal pressure</b>; swollen bile channels cause <b>obstructive jaundice</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Fulminant and chronic</b> — if liver cells <b>fail to regenerate</b> as necrosis spreads → <b>fulminant hepatitis</b>, severe and often fatal. Hepatitis is <b>chronic after 6 months</b> of inflammation — usually <b>B or C</b> (or <b>D</b> on top of chronic B) — and can lead to <b>cirrhosis and liver cancer</b>. Many patients have more than one infection (HBV with HCV, HDV or <b>HIV</b>).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The five viruses</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Hepatitis A</b> — an RNA virus that <b>survives on hands</b> and resists detergents and acids but is <b>killed by chlorine bleach</b> and very high heat. Spread <b>fecal-oral</b> — person to person (including <b>oral-anal sex</b>) or by <b>contaminated food or water</b> (<b>shellfish</b> from polluted water, infected <b>food handlers</b>). Incubation <b>15–50 days</b> (peak 25–30). Usually <b>mild and flulike</b>, often unrecognized; <b>more severe after age 40</b> or with existing liver disease (such as hepatitis C), rarely needing a transplant. Common where <b>sanitation is poor</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Hepatitis B</b> — a <b>DNA</b> virus with a core antigen (<b>HBcAg</b>), surface antigen (<b>HBsAg</b>) and <b>HBeAg</b>. Spread by <b>blood and body fluids</b>: <b>unprotected sex</b>, <b>shared needles</b> and drug equipment, <b>shared razors or toothbrushes</b>, needlesticks (low incidence), unscreened transfusions <b>before 1992</b>, <b>hemodialysis</b>, contact with blood or open sores, and <b>birth</b>. Symptoms <b>25–180 days</b> after exposure; many have none. <b>Most adults clear it and become immune</b>; a few become <b>carriers</b> who can infect others and are at high risk for <b>cirrhosis and liver cancer</b>. Immunosuppressed people are more likely to get it.</div>\n<div><b>Hepatitis C</b> — an enveloped, single-stranded <b>RNA</b> virus with <b>at least 6 genotypes</b> (hard to make a vaccine — <b>there is none</b>). Spread <b>blood to blood</b>: <b>IV drug needle sharing (highest)</b>, blood or organs <b>before 1992</b>, <b>adults born 1945–1965</b>, needlesticks (<b>health care workers</b>), hemodialysis, <b>incarceration</b>, shared drug paraphernalia. Sexual spread is <b>low in a monogamous couple</b>, higher with multiple partners or unprotected sex between men. <b>Not spread by casual or household contact</b> — but don't share <b>razors, toothbrushes or pierced earrings</b>. Incubation <b>2 weeks–6 months</b>; usually <b>no symptoms</b> for years; <b>most people don't clear it → chronic</b> infection, cirrhosis and liver cancer.</div>\n<div><b>Hepatitis D (delta)</b> — a <b>defective RNA virus that needs hepatitis B</b> to replicate, so it occurs <b>only with HBV</b> — usually becoming <b>chronic</b>. Incubation <b>14–56 days</b>; spread like B, mainly <b>parenterally</b> (especially <b>IV drug use</b>) and by sex. <b>Preventing B prevents D.</b></div>\n<div><b>Hepatitis E</b> — a <b>waterborne</b>, <b>fecal-oral</b> virus behind epidemics in the <b>Indian subcontinent, Asia, Africa, the Middle East, Mexico and Central and South America</b>, often after <b>heavy rain and flooding</b>. Rare in the US — mostly in <b>travelers</b>. Incubation <b>15–64 days</b>; <b>self-limiting</b>, with <b>no chronic form</b> (per the book).</div>\n<div><b>How common</b> — hepatitis A and B are <b>declining because of vaccination</b>. About <b>2.7 million</b> people in the US have hepatitis C and up to <b>2.2 million</b> have hepatitis B; worldwide <b>over 58 million</b> have chronic hepatitis C, with <b>1.5 million new cases a year</b> — expected to rise with illicit drug use.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The route predicts the course. <b>A and E</b> enter through the gut, the immune system clears them, and they <b>never become chronic</b> — the risk is a short, miserable illness (worse in older adults). <b>B, C and D</b> travel in blood, can hide in the liver for decades, and cause their harm slowly through <b>chronic inflammation → cirrhosis → cancer</b>. So A and E teaching is about <b>handwashing and water</b>; B, C and D teaching is about <b>blood, needles, sex and screening</b>.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","ch":"50","chTitle":"Liver conditions","title":"Hepatitis — prevention, signs and blood tests","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Prevention</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Hepatitis A</b> — <b>handwashing</b>, especially after handling <b>shellfish</b>; avoid <b>contaminated food or water</b> (including tap water where hepatitis A is common); <b>immune globulin within 14 days</b> of exposure; <b>HAV vaccine</b> before travel to high-risk areas (e.g., <b>Mexico, the Caribbean</b>) and for people living or working in close quarters — <b>dorms, prisons, day-care centers, long-term care</b>. A <b>combined HAV/HBV vaccine</b> exists for adults.</div>\n<div><b>Who needs the hepatitis B vaccine</b> — people with <b>more than one sex partner</b>, an <b>STI</b> now or before, <b>men having unprotected sex with men</b>, <b>any chronic liver disease</b> (including hepatitis C or cirrhosis), <b>HIV</b>, <b>workplace exposure to blood</b> (health care workers, firefighters, police), people in <b>correctional facilities</b>, patients needing <b>immunosuppressants</b>, and <b>household and sexual contacts</b> of people with HBV. Childhood vaccines have cut A and B sharply. <i>(The CDC now recommends hepatitis B vaccine for all adults 19–59, and for those 60 and over with risk factors.)</i></div>\n<div><b>Health care workers (Box 50.6)</b> — <b>Standard Precautions</b>; <b>needleless systems</b> — needlesticks are the <b>major source of hepatitis B in health care workers</b>; the <b>hepatitis B vaccine series of 3 injections</b> (which also <b>prevents hepatitis D</b>); <b>immune globulin</b> right away after a hepatitis A exposure; <b>report every case</b> to the health department.</div>\n<div><b>Hepatitis C</b> — <b>no vaccine</b> (too many genotypes). Teach <b>baby boomers</b> (born 1945–1965) to get a <b>one-time HCV screening test</b>. <i>(The CDC now recommends one-time screening for <b>all adults 18 and older</b> and at every pregnancy.)</i></div>\n<div><b>Healthy People 2030 (Box 50.5)</b> — reduce rates of <b>hepatitis A, acute B and acute C</b>; increase the share of people who <b>know they have chronic B</b>; reduce <b>deaths from B</b>; increase the share who have <b>cleared C</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing hepatitis (Box 50.7)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — ask about <b>known exposure</b> to someone with hepatitis; if the patient has few symptoms but <b>high ALT or AST</b>, ask the full set of risk questions.</div>\n<div><b>Signs</b> — <b>abdominal pain</b>; <b>icterus and jaundice</b>; <b>joint and muscle aches</b>; diarrhea or constipation; <b>light clay-colored stools and dark yellow-brown urine</b>; fever; <b>fatigue, malaise, anorexia, nausea and vomiting</b>; <b>dry skin and pruritus</b>. Patients often come in <b>only after jaundice appears</b>, having blamed the rest on the flu. Most HCV infections cause no symptoms.</div>\n<div><b>Examining</b> — <b>lightly palpate the RUQ</b> for tenderness; the patient may hurt with <b>jarring movements</b>. Inspect skin, <b>sclerae</b> and mucous membranes for jaundice (from <b>swollen bile channels</b>); look for <b>scratch marks</b> from pruritus; inspect a <b>urine and stool</b> sample.</div>\n<div><b>Chronic hepatitis C beyond the liver</b> — <b>depression, polyarthritis, myalgia, kidney insufficiency, cognitive impairment</b>, and cardiovascular problems such as <b>vasculitis and heart disease</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Psychosocial</b> — fatigue and malaise feed <b>depression</b>; <b>guilt</b> is common when the source was drug use; <b>stigma</b> and embarrassment over precautions can lead to <b>isolation</b>; fear of infecting loved ones. <b>Family members may pull away</b> — let them voice fears and <b>teach how the virus actually spreads</b>. Weeks off work and medical costs cause <b>financial stress</b>, for years if chronic.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Blood tests</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Liver tests</b> — <b>ALT and AST can rise into the thousands</b> in acute or fulminant hepatitis; alkaline phosphatase normal or high; <b>total bilirubin high</b> (matches the jaundice).</div>\n<div><b>Hepatitis A</b> — <b>HAV IgM</b> antibody = <b>current infection</b> (stays 3–6 weeks); <b>HAV IgG</b> = <b>past infection</b> and <b>lifelong immunity</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Hepatitis B</b> — <b>HBsAg</b> and <b>IgM antibody to the core antigen</b> make the diagnosis, with <b>HBV DNA (PCR)</b> showing virus in the blood. The patient is <b>infectious as long as HBsAg is present</b>; <b>HBsAg for 6 months or more = carrier or chronic hepatitis</b>. <b>Antibody to HBsAg (anti-HBs) = recovery and immunity</b>. <i>(The book says vaccinated people have a positive HBsAg; it is actually <b>anti-HBs</b> that is positive after the vaccine — HBsAg stays negative.)</i></div>\n<div><b>Hepatitis C</b> — <b>anti-HCV antibodies</b> show up <b>within about 4 weeks</b>; the <b>HCV RNA</b> test confirms <b>active virus</b> and measures the <b>viral load</b>. The <b>OraQuick</b> rapid antibody test gives a point-of-care answer.</div>\n<div><b>Hepatitis D and E</b> — <b>D</b>: delta antigen in the liver or, more often, a <b>rising HDV antibody</b> titer within days. <b>E</b>: tested mainly in <b>travelers</b>; the virus can't be measured directly, so <b>HEV IgM</b> antibody confirms it.</div>\n<div><b>Liver biopsy</b> — confirms the diagnosis and <b>stages and grades</b> the damage, telling virus from drug, toxin, fat or iron. Usually <b>percutaneous</b> under local anesthesia as an outpatient; with abnormal clotting, a <b>CT-guided or transjugular</b> route lowers the risk of <b>pneumothorax or hemothorax</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Hepatitis B markers read like a timeline. <b>Surface antigen (HBsAg)</b> is a piece of the virus itself — while it is in the blood, the virus is there and the patient is infectious. <b>Antibody to the surface antigen (anti-HBs)</b> is the immune system's answer — it appears after recovery or after the vaccine (which is made of surface antigen alone). So <b>antigen = virus present; antibody to surface antigen = protected</b>.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve","ch":"50","chTitle":"Liver conditions","title":"Hepatitis — care, antiviral drugs and preventing spread","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Nursing care</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — (1) <b>weight loss</b> from liver inflammation; (2) <b>fatigue</b> from infection and reduced metabolic energy. Most patients stay <b>home</b>; <b>older adults and dehydrated</b> patients may be admitted briefly. The plan <b>rests the liver, helps it regenerate, strengthens immunity and prevents complications</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Nutrition</b> — anorexia, nausea and malaise make eating hard (especially in hepatitis A). Diet: <b>high carbohydrate and high calorie</b>, adding <b>moderate fat and protein once nausea and anorexia ease</b>. <b>Small, frequent meals</b>; <b>favorite foods</b> are tolerated best; <b>high-calorie snacks</b>; vitamin supplements.</div>\n<div><b>Rest and activity</b> — <b>rest</b> lowers the liver's metabolic demand and <b>increases its blood supply</b> for healing. <b>Complete bed rest isn't usually needed</b> — <b>alternate rest and activity</b>, adjusted to symptoms and liver tests, adding self-care and walking gradually.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs used sparingly</b> — to let the liver rest — an <b>antiemetic</b> may be given. Chronic <b>B and C</b> are life-threatening, so they get <b>antiviral and immune-modulating</b> drugs.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Antiviral drugs (Table 50.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Hepatitis B</b> — <b>interferon alfa</b> (interferon alfa-2b, <b>peginterferon alfa-2a</b>) — used less now; <b>nucleoside analogs</b>: <b>tenofovir, entecavir, lamivudine, adefovir</b>. All are cleared by the <b>kidneys → monitor kidney function</b>. <b>Tenofovir</b> can <b>thin bones</b> → teach <b>fall prevention</b>. <b>Lamivudine</b>: <b>don't stop without the provider</b> — stopping can cause a <b>hepatitis B flare</b>. <b>Entecavir</b>: <b>avoid alcohol</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Hepatitis C</b> — once <b>peginterferon + ribavirin</b>; now <b>direct-acting antivirals (DAAs)</b> chosen by <b>HCV genotype</b>, far more effective with fewer side effects. <b>Protease inhibitors</b> (glecaprevir, grazoprevir, simeprevir, paritaprevir): monitor <b>CBC and chemistry</b>; rash, itching, nausea, headache; kidney or liver impairment, electrolyte imbalance.</div>\n<div><b>NS5A combinations</b> — <b>glecaprevir/pibrentasvir</b>, <b>elbasvir/grazoprevir</b>, <b>ledipasvir/sofosbuvir</b> (book: genotype 1 only) and <b>sofosbuvir/velpatasvir</b> (almost all genotypes). <b>Before the first dose, ask about any history of hepatitis B</b> — these drugs can <b>reactivate hepatitis B and cause liver failure</b>. Teach that <b>liver enzymes, especially ALT, are monitored</b> (liver toxicity). <b>Never give ledipasvir/sofosbuvir with amiodarone</b> → <b>severe bradycardia</b>, chest pain.</div>\n<div><b>Infection risk</b> — these drugs affect the immune system → teach patients to <b>avoid crowds and people who may be infectious</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Home care and evaluation</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Preventing spread</b> — the main focus at home. For fecal-oral hepatitis (A, E): <b>careful handwashing</b> and <b>sanitary disposal of feces</b>. For bloodborne hepatitis: <b>no sharing of razors, toothbrushes or needles</b>; safer sex.</div>\n<div><b>Action Alert</b> — teach the patient and family <b>how to prevent transmission</b>; <b>avoid alcohol</b>; <b>check with the provider before any medication, vitamin, supplement or herbal product</b>. Increase activity <b>gradually</b>; <b>small, frequent high-carbohydrate meals</b>; plan <b>rest periods</b>. An <b>infection control practitioner</b> or infectious disease specialist can help.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — adequate nutrition; <b>rising energy</b> as the liver rests; infection and inflammation under control.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>DAAs cure most hepatitis C, but they remove the virus so fast that a quiet hepatitis B infection in the same liver can wake up — HCV had been keeping HBV in check. That is why the most important question before the first dose is \"<b>have you ever had hepatitis, especially B?</b>\", and why ALT is followed during treatment.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"50-liver-transplantation","ch":"50","chTitle":"Liver conditions","title":"Liver transplantation","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Who and how</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Who</b> — end-stage liver disease or <b>acute liver failure</b> that hasn't responded to other treatment. <b>Cirrhosis is the most common reason</b>; others: chronic <b>hepatitis B and C</b>, bile duct disease, autoimmune and <b>alcoholic</b> liver disease, <b>fatty liver</b>. Extensive physical and psychological evaluation comes first, and other options are explored.</div>\n<div><b>Not candidates</b> — <b>severe heart disease</b> with cardiovascular instability, <b>severe respiratory disease</b>, <b>metastatic tumors</b>, and <b>inability to follow the drug and self-care plan</b>. Criteria vary by center.</div>\n<div><b>Donor livers</b> — mostly from <b>trauma victims</b> with healthy livers, distributed through <b>UNOS</b> by region and <b>acuity</b> (sickest first, using the <b>MELD</b> score). The liver is kept in a preserving solution for <b>up to 8 hours</b>. In a <b>deceased donor transplant (DDLT)</b> the diseased liver comes out through an <b>upper abdominal incision</b> and the new one is connected to the vessels and bile ducts — long surgery with <b>large fluid and blood replacement</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Living donors</b> — <b>living donor transplant (LDLT)</b> — usually a close relative or spouse, voluntary, after careful testing; <b>one lobe</b> is removed and implanted. <b>Both livers regenerate</b> to meet the body's needs.</div>\n<div><b>Ethics</b> — living donors need <b>full informed consent</b> about the risks; <b>HCV-positive livers</b> are used more now that DAAs cure HCV; <b>more men than women</b> get transplants despite MELD; <b>fewer Black and Hispanic</b> patients receive them. These touch <b>nonmaleficence and beneficence</b> — nurses <b>advocate</b> for donors' understanding and for every patient in urgent need regardless of race, ethnicity or gender.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">After transplant (Table 50.3)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Most common complications</b> — <b>acute graft rejection, vascular or biliary obstruction, and infection</b>. Care starts in the <b>ICU</b>; report signs to the surgeon <b>at once</b>. Reassure the patient and family that most complications can be managed.</div>\n<div><b>Rejection</b> — usually <b>7–14 days</b> after surgery: <b>tachycardia, fever, RUQ or flank pain</b>, <b>less bile or a change in its color</b>, <b>increasing jaundice</b>; labs show <b>rising bilirubin, ALT, AST and alkaline phosphatase</b> and a <b>longer PT/INR</b>. Prevented with <b>prednisone, tacrolimus or cyclosporine</b> (cyclosporine made transplants succeed); treated with stronger antirejection drugs. If it fails, multiorgan failure, coagulopathy and encephalopathy follow — the only option is <b>emergency retransplantation</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Infection</b> — can occur <b>any time</b>; risk from <b>immunosuppression</b>, tubes and lines, immobility and long anesthesia. Early: <b>pneumonia, wound infection, UTI</b>; <b>after the first month</b>: opportunistic — <b>cytomegalovirus</b>, mycobacteria, parasites; latent <b>TB and herpes simplex</b> can reactivate. Prevent with <b>prophylactic antibiotics, vaccines, cultures of lines and drains, early line removal and handwashing</b>. Signs: <b>fever</b> or <b>foul-smelling drainage</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Biliary and vascular complications</b> — <b>bile leak, abscess or hepatic artery thrombosis</b>: <b>less bile drainage</b>, more <b>RUQ pain</b> with distention and guarding, nausea and vomiting, <b>rising jaundice, clay-colored stools</b>, rising bilirubin and transaminases. Keep the <b>T-tube dependent and secured</b>, empty it often and record the amount and quality of bile; report at once — surgery may be needed.</div>\n<div><b>Acute kidney injury</b> — from <b>hypotension, antibiotics, cyclosporine</b>, acute liver failure or <b>hypothermia</b> (shivering, hyperventilation, vasoconstriction, alkalemia). Early signs: <b>urine output changes, rising BUN and creatinine</b>, electrolyte imbalance. <b>Prevent hypotension</b> and monitor <b>levels of nephrotoxic drugs</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Action Alerts</b> — take the <b>temperature often</b> and report a rise, <b>abdominal pain, distention and rigidity</b> (<b>peritonitis</b>); watch <b>neurologic status</b> (encephalopathy from a <b>nonfunctioning liver</b>); <b>report bleeding signs at once</b> — oozing from catheters, petechiae, ecchymoses — they may mean the <b>new liver isn't working</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Long-term teaching</b> — immunosuppressant side effects: <b>hypertension, kidney toxicity, infection, GI upset</b>; lifelong screening for <b>cancer</b> (skin, breast and testicular self-exams; Pap tests), <b>metabolic syndrome and diabetes</b>; call the provider for <b>any sign of infection</b>; keep healthy lifestyle changes.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A new liver announces failure through its jobs. When the graft is rejected or its blood supply clots, it stops making <b>bile</b> (less drainage, rising jaundice), stops making <b>clotting factors</b> (oozing, rising INR) and stops clearing <b>toxins</b> (confusion). That is why the transplant nurse watches the bile bag, the IV sites and the patient's mental status as closely as the vital signs.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"50-adpie","ch":"50","chTitle":"Liver conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Every patient with liver disease is watched for the same turning points — <b>fluid shifting into the abdomen</b>, <b>bleeding the liver can no longer stop</b>, and <b>ammonia reaching the brain</b> — while the nurse protects the liver (no alcohol, no acetaminophen or other liver-toxic drugs, rest and good nutrition) and teaches how each kind of hepatitis spreads and how to prevent it.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div><b>alcohol</b> (amount, years, last drink), prescribed and <b>illicit drugs</b>, herbs, chemicals, <b>needlesticks, tattoos, transfusions before 1992</b>, sexual history, <b>military or prison</b>, job (health care, fire, police), travel and food or water exposure, <b>vaccines and hepatitis C screening</b>, past jaundice or biliary disease, obesity and diabetes; ask nonjudgmentally and in private.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical</div><b>daily weight and abdominal girth</b> (flat, umbilicus, end of exhalation, marked), bulging flanks, caput medusae, edema; <b>jaundice, icterus</b>, pruritus and scratch marks; <b>petechiae, bruises, spider angiomas, palmar erythema</b>; RUQ tenderness; <b>mental status, asterixis, fetor hepaticus</b>; vomitus and stool for blood; SpO₂ and lung sounds; <b>signs of alcohol withdrawal</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Labs and tests</div><b>ALT, AST, alkaline phosphatase, GGT, bilirubin</b>; <b>albumin, PT/INR, platelets</b> (what the liver can still make); <b>ammonia</b>; <b>BUN, creatinine, Na⁺, K⁺</b>; Hgb/Hct; hepatitis serology (<b>HAV IgM, HBsAg, anti-HBs, anti-HCV, HCV RNA</b>); ultrasound, <b>elastography</b>, biopsy, <b>EGD</b>; ascitic fluid cell count (&gt; 250 PMN = SBP).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>cirrhosis: <b>fluid overload</b> from third spacing (ascites); <b>potential for hemorrhage</b> (varices, low clotting factors and platelets); <b>acute confusion</b> (ammonia, alcohol withdrawal); <b>pruritus</b> (bilirubin). Hepatitis: <b>weight loss</b> and <b>fatigue</b>. Transplant: <b>rejection, infection, bile leak or clot, kidney injury</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>less ascites and edema with adequate circulating volume (<b>steady weight loss</b>, stable electrolytes); <b>no bleeding</b>, or prompt control; <b>no encephalopathy</b> — oriented, <b>2–3 soft stools a day</b> on lactulose; comfortable skin; in hepatitis, <b>stable weight and rising energy</b> and no spread to others; after transplant, <b>no rejection or infection</b>; abstinence from alcohol and drugs.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Fluid</div><b>1–2 g sodium diet</b> (read labels, no table salt); <b>spironolactone + furosemide</b> with <b>daily weight, I&amp;O, girth and electrolytes</b> (watch ↓ K⁺, ↓ Na⁺, dehydration); <b>paracentesis</b> care (void first, weigh before and after, measure fluid, send specimen); <b>HOB ≥ 30°</b>, feet up, <b>SpO₂</b> and oxygen; IV thiamine, folate and multivitamins; antibiotics for SBP; TIPS care.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Bleeding and confusion</div>prevent: <b>propranolol</b> (watch HR and BP), no straining, lifting or coarse dry food; active bleed: <b>antibiotics, octreotide, banding or sclerotherapy</b>, <b>large-bore IVs</b>, blood, FFP and platelets, <b>VS every hour</b>, PT/PTT/INR, NG tube; balloon tamponade with the <b>airway protected</b>. Confusion: <b>lactulose to 2–3 soft stools</b>, rifaximin, moderate protein, <b>limit sedatives and opioids</b>, check <b>asterixis</b>, safety, skin care, potassium; <b>thiamine and benzodiazepines</b> for withdrawal.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Hepatitis, NAFLD and transplant</div>hepatitis: <b>high-carb, high-calorie small meals</b>, <b>alternate rest and activity</b>, antiemetic, antivirals (<b>ask about hepatitis B before DAAs</b>; no ledipasvir/sofosbuvir with amiodarone; monitor kidneys and ALT), <b>handwashing and no shared razors</b>, vaccines for contacts. NAFLD: <b>weight loss, exercise</b>, dietitian. Transplant: <b>immunosuppressants on schedule</b>, frequent temperature, T-tube care, report <b>fever, RUQ pain, rising jaundice or oozing</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>weight and girth falling toward the goal with stable electrolytes and urine output; no hematemesis or melena; <b>oriented, no asterixis</b>, 2–3 soft stools a day; skin intact and less itchy; the patient and family can state <b>what to avoid</b> (alcohol, acetaminophen, NSAIDs, herbs, extra vitamins) and <b>what to report</b> (bleeding, black stools, confusion, fever); hepatitis patients eat enough, gain energy and know how to <b>protect others</b>; transplant patients take immunosuppressants and report infection or rejection signs.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"51-overview","ch":"51","chTitle":"Biliary system and pancreas conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">51 · Chapter 51</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Inflammation · pain · nutrition</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Biliary system and pancreas conditions</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> The gallbladder stores and concentrates <b>bile</b> to digest fat; the pancreas pours <b>digestive enzymes</b> (amylase, lipase, trypsin) into the duodenum and makes <b>insulin and glucagon</b>. Both exemplars are about <b>inflammation</b>: in <b>cholecystitis</b>, usually a <b>gallstone</b> blocks the cystic duct and the trapped bile inflames the gallbladder; in <b>acute pancreatitis</b>, enzymes switch on <b>inside the pancreas</b> and digest it — the patient can go into <b>shock, respiratory failure and DIC</b>. The chapter adds <b>chronic pancreatitis</b> (pain, fatty stools, diabetes and enzyme replacement) and <b>pancreatic cancer</b> with the <b>Whipple procedure</b>. For the nurse: <b>control pain, rest the gut (NPO), replace fluid and calcium, watch breathing and vital signs for shock</b>, and teach <b>low-fat eating and no alcohol</b>.</p><div id=\"ch51-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">The liver makes bile and the <b>gallbladder</b> stores and concentrates it, squeezing it through the cystic and common bile ducts when fat reaches the duodenum. The <b>pancreas</b> secretes <b>inactive</b> enzymes that switch on only in the intestine, plus bicarbonate; its islet cells make <b>insulin and glucagon</b>.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A <b>gallstone</b> (usually cholesterol) blocks the <b>cystic duct</b> → trapped bile irritates the wall → ischemia, infection, even gangrene. In pancreatitis, <b>gallstones or alcohol</b> trigger <b>early enzyme activation</b> inside the pancreas → <b>autodigestion</b> (fat necrosis, bleeding vessels, inflammation). Chronic damage → <b>fibrosis</b> and loss of enzymes and insulin. Cancer → a mass, usually in the <b>head</b>, that blocks the bile duct.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Gallbladder: <b>RUQ pain after a fatty meal</b> radiating to the <b>right shoulder</b>, nausea, belching, bloating, <b>Murphy sign</b>; obstruction → <b>jaundice, clay stools, dark urine</b>. Pancreatitis: <b>sudden, severe, boring epigastric or LUQ pain to the back</b>, <b>worse lying flat</b>, relieved by the <b>fetal position</b>; <b>Cullen and Turner</b> discoloration; fever, tachycardia, hypotension. Chronic: <b>steatorrhea</b>, weight loss, diabetes. Cancer: <b>painless jaundice</b>, weight loss, back pain.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Gallbladder: <b>WBC up</b>, bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase up with obstruction; <b>RUQ ultrasound</b> (best first test), HIDA scan, ERCP or MRCP. Pancreatitis: <b>amylase</b> (up in 12–24 hr, for 2–3 days) and <b>lipase</b> (stays up 5–7 days), <b>glucose, WBC, CRP up</b>; <b>calcium and magnesium down</b>; <b>ALT ×3 = biliary cause</b>; contrast CT. Cancer: CEA and <b>CA 19-9</b> to monitor, <b>endoscopic ultrasound</b> (gold standard).</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Gallbladder: low-fat diet, opioids or ketorolac, antiemetics, <b>laparoscopic cholecystectomy</b>, ursodiol, or a percutaneous drain. Acute pancreatitis: <b>NPO, IV fluids, PCA opioids, calcium and magnesium</b>, NG suction if vomiting, <b>jejunal feeding</b>, ERCP to remove the stone. Chronic: <b>pancreatic enzymes with every meal and snack</b>, insulin, high-calorie diet. Cancer: chemo (FOLFIRINOX), radiation, stents, <b>Whipple</b>.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Gallbladder: <b>perforation</b>, abscess, <b>peritonitis</b>, suppurative <b>cholangitis</b>, pancreatitis. Pancreatitis: <b>hypovolemic shock</b> from hemorrhage and third spacing, <b>ARDS</b> and pleural effusion (pulmonary failure causes over half of first-week deaths), <b>DIC</b>, AKI, paralytic ileus, infected necrosis, abscess, pseudocyst, diabetes. Whipple: <b>anastomotic leak and fistula</b>, hemorrhage, shock.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Biliary colic with pallor, diaphoresis, tachycardia and prostration</b> (shock); in pancreatitis <b>falling BP with rising HR, a change in level of consciousness</b>, SpO₂ dropping or crackles, <b>tetany or twitching</b> (low calcium), <b>boardlike rigid abdomen</b>; after a Whipple, <b>clear, bile-tinged or bloody drain output that suddenly increases</b>.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Gallstone or alcohol injures the pancreas<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>enzymes switch on <b>inside</b> the gland<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span><b>trypsin</b> activates elastase, lipase, kallikrein<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>fat necrosis (<b>calcium binds</b> → hypocalcemia), vessels dissolve (<b>bleeding</b>), vessels leak (<b>third spacing</b>)<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>inflammatory flood reaches lungs and kidneys<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span><b>shock, ARDS, AKI, DIC</b></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Cholecystitis vs acute pancreatitis pain</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Cholecystitis</b>: <b>RUQ</b> ache to steady pain, often <b>after a fatty or large meal</b>, radiating to the <b>right shoulder or scapula</b>; comes in \"attacks\"; <b>Murphy sign</b>. <b>Pancreatitis</b>: <b>sudden, intense, continuous \"boring\"</b> pain in the <b>mid-epigastrium or LUQ</b>, radiating to the <b>back, left flank or left shoulder</b>, <b>worse lying supine</b>, eased by <b>sitting up and leaning forward or the fetal position</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Amylase vs lipase</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Amylase</b> rises <b>within 12–24 hours</b> and stays up <b>2–3 days</b> — many other conditions raise it too. <b>Lipase</b> rises <b>later</b> and stays up <b>5–7 days</b>, and pancreatitis is its most common cause — so it is more useful when the patient arrives <b>days after the pain began</b>. Persistent elevation suggests <b>duct obstruction or a leak</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Acute vs chronic pancreatitis</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Acute</b>: a sudden inflammatory attack that can heal — or kill through shock, ARDS and DIC; enzymes <b>very high</b>; treated with NPO, fluids and pain control. <b>Chronic</b>: <b>progressive fibrosis</b> with flare-ups — continuous burning or gnawing pain, <b>steatorrhea, weight loss and diabetes</b> from lost exocrine and endocrine function; enzymes <b>normal or only moderately high</b>; treated with <b>enzyme replacement</b>, insulin and diet.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Pancrelipase</b> supplies the lipase, protease and amylase the pancreas no longer makes — so it must be taken <b>with every meal and snack</b>, swallowed <b>whole</b>. <b>Ursodiol</b> makes bile less saturated with cholesterol so small stones slowly dissolve. <b>Opioids by PCA</b> are the backbone of pancreatitis pain care; <b>all opioids can cause some sphincter of Oddi spasm</b>. <a href=\"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Pain (intensity, location, radiation, what relieves it), <b>vital signs</b> for tachycardia, hypotension and fever, <b>SpO₂ and lung sounds</b> every 4 hr in pancreatitis (left pleural effusion, ARDS), I&amp;O and <b>daily weight</b>, <b>calcium and magnesium</b> (twitching, numbness, irritability), <b>blood glucose</b>, bowel sounds and <b>flatus</b> (ileus), NG output, skin and sclera for jaundice, stool and urine color, level of consciousness (withdrawal, hypoxia, sepsis); after a Whipple, drain color and amount.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Hypotension with tachycardia</b> or a <b>change in LOC</b> (hemorrhage, third spacing, sepsis — call the Rapid Response Team); biliary colic with <b>pallor, diaphoresis, prostration</b> (shock — stay, keep the HOB flat); <b>rising RR, falling SpO₂, crackles</b> (ARDS, fluid overload); <b>tetany or muscle twitching</b> (hypocalcemia); <b>rigid, boardlike abdomen</b> (peritonitis); <b>bile-tinged, clear or bloody drainage that suddenly increases</b> after a Whipple (anastomotic leak).</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why trapped bile inflames the gallbladder</b> — the gallbladder concentrates bile until it is thick and viscous, mixing it only now and then with its mucus — a perfect place for stones when <b>cholesterol and bile salts are out of balance</b> or the gallbladder <b>empties slowly</b>. When a stone blocks the <b>cystic duct</b>, trapped bile is <b>reabsorbed and chemically irritates</b> the wall; with <b>edema, distention and poor circulation</b> the wall becomes <b>ischemic and infected</b> → sloughing, <b>necrosis and gangrene</b>, and finally <b>perforation</b> (a small one walls off as an abscess; a large one causes <b>peritonitis</b>).</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why obstructed bile gives jaundice, itching, clay stools and dark urine</b> — with the duct blocked, bile can't reach the duodenum: <b>bile salts collect in the skin</b> → <b>pruritus</b> or burning; <b>bilirubin can't reach the colon</b> to become urobilinogen (what makes stool brown) → <b>clay-colored stools</b>; extra water-soluble bilirubin is cleared by the kidneys → <b>dark, tea-colored urine</b>; and without bile, <b>fat and fat-soluble vitamins</b> aren't absorbed → <b>steatorrhea</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Autodigestion: the four processes</b> — enzymes activate <b>inside the pancreas</b> instead of the gut. <b>Trypsin</b> turns on the others. (1) <b>Lipolysis</b>: <b>lipase</b> causes <b>fat necrosis</b>; released fatty acids <b>bind ionized calcium</b> into a soaplike product faster than the parathyroids can compensate → <b>hypocalcemia</b>. (2) <b>Proteolysis</b>: protein splitting → thrombosis and gangrene of the pancreas. (3) <b>Necrosis of blood vessels</b>: <b>elastase</b> dissolves elastic fibers in vessels and ducts → bleeding into the retroperitoneum, from minor to massive. (4) <b>Inflammation</b>: white cells gather around the dead areas; <b>kallikrein</b> releases <b>bradykinin</b> and kallidin → <b>vasodilation and leaky vessels</b>. Many deaths come from <b>irreversible hypovolemic shock</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why chronic pancreatitis causes fatty stools, edema and diabetes</b> — fibrosis destroys <b>acinar cells</b> (enzymes) and later <b>islet cells</b> (insulin). Enzyme output must drop a lot before fat goes unabsorbed — then stools become <b>pale, bulky, frothy and foul</b> (bacteria act on unabsorbed fat and protein) and the patient loses weight and muscle. Unabsorbed protein lowers albumin → <b>\"starvation\" edema</b> of the feet, legs and hands. Loss of islets → <b>diabetes</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The pancreas makes enzymes strong enough to digest a meal, and keeps itself safe only by releasing them <b>inactive</b>. Once they switch on inside the gland, the damage spreads outward: digested fat steals <b>calcium</b>, dissolved vessels <b>bleed</b>, and kinins make every capillary <b>leak</b> — so a \"belly\" problem turns into <b>shock, low calcium and lung failure</b>. That is why pancreatitis care is mostly about <b>fluids, calcium, oxygen and resting the gland (NPO)</b>.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"51-cholecystitis-the-inflammation-exemplar","ch":"51","chTitle":"Biliary system and pancreas conditions","title":"Cholecystitis — the inflammation exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Acute and chronic cholecystitis</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — <b>inflammation of the gallbladder</b>, acute (most patients) or chronic, most common in <b>affluent countries</b> and <b>more common in women</b>; it often affects <b>nutrition</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Calculous (with stones)</b> — the most common type — <b>about 95%</b> of acute cases. <b>Gallstones (cholelithiasis)</b> block the <b>cystic duct</b> (most often), the gallbladder neck or the <b>common bile duct (choledocholithiasis)</b>. Trapped bile is reabsorbed and irritates the wall; with edema, distention and poor blood flow → <b>ischemia, infection, necrosis, gangrene</b> → <b>perforation</b> (abscess if small, <b>peritonitis</b> if large).</div>\n<div><b>The stones</b> — made of bile ingredients — <b>cholesterol</b>, bilirubin, bile salts, calcium, proteins; classed as <b>cholesterol, pigment or mixed</b> stones. <b>Cholesterol stones</b> (from an imbalance of cholesterol and bile salts) are the <b>most common in the US</b>. <b>Slow gallbladder emptying</b> (stasis) favors them. Bacteria can gather around stones → <b>suppurative cholangitis</b> (pus in the ducts), life-threatening if missed.</div>\n<div><b>Acalculous (no stones)</b> — from <b>biliary stasis</b> — anything that disturbs filling or emptying: low blood flow to the gallbladder, a <b>kinked or twisted</b> neck or cystic duct (letting pancreatic enzymes reflux in), <b>sphincter of Oddi dysfunction</b>. Most cases occur in the critically ill: <b>sepsis, severe trauma or burns, long-term TPN, MODS, major abdominal surgery, hypovolemia</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Chronic cholecystitis</b> — repeated cystic duct blockage → chronic inflammation; stones are often present. The gallbladder becomes <b>fibrotic and shrunken</b>, moves and absorbs poorly. Without stones it can cause pain <b>misdiagnosed as gastritis</b> — more likely with a <b>very low-fat (e.g., vegetarian) diet</b>. A shrunken gallbladder may stick to nearby organs. Complications: <b>pancreatitis and cholangitis</b> from bile backing up; <b>jaundice</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Jaundice</b> — possible in acute but <b>most common in chronic</b> cholecystitis. <b>Extrahepatic obstructive</b> jaundice from stones or swollen ducts; <b>intrahepatic</b> if the liver's bile channels are inflamed. Blocked bile → <b>bile salts in the skin (itching)</b>, <b>clay-colored stools</b>, <b>dark \"tea-colored\" urine</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Risk and prevention</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Risk factors</b> — family tendency (partly shared diet and <b>sedentary</b> habits; some gene variants put more cholesterol into bile), <b>type 2 diabetes, gastric bypass surgery, Crohn's disease, rapid weight loss, sickle cell disease</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Women</b> — <b>obesity</b> is a major risk, especially for women; <b>pregnancy, hormone replacement and birth control pills</b> slow gallbladder contraction; <b>multiple pregnancies</b> raise the risk. The old \"<b>four Fs</b>\" — <b>female, forty, fat, fertile</b> — still gets taught, but cholecystitis also strikes <b>younger, older and thin</b> women.</div>\n<div><b>Prevention</b> — habits that prevent obesity — <b>avoid excess high-fat, high-cholesterol food and exercise regularly</b>; if obese, <b>lose weight slowly</b> under a professional's care (rapid loss causes stones). Ask about <b>food insecurity, transportation and access</b>; offer resources, including <b>telehealth</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Fast weight loss and fasting cause gallstones for the same reason pregnancy does: the gallbladder sits <b>still</b>. When it isn't squeezed often by fat in the duodenum (or hormones slow its muscle), bile stays put, concentrates, and cholesterol crystallizes out. That is why the advice is to <b>lose weight slowly</b> and why critically ill patients on <b>TPN</b> — who eat nothing — get acalculous cholecystitis.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"51-recognizing-cholecystitis-signs-and-test","ch":"51","chTitle":"Biliary system and pancreas conditions","title":"Recognizing cholecystitis — signs and tests","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">History and signs</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — height, weight and vital signs (can be delegated to AP); <b>diet</b> — high fat, high calorie, <b>low fiber, refined white carbohydrates</b> raise risk (a <b>low-fat</b> diet can cause chronic cholecystitis in <b>young thin women</b>); whether certain foods cause pain; GI symptoms after <b>fatty food</b> — <b>flatulence, dyspepsia, eructation (belching)</b>, anorexia, nausea, vomiting; <b>activity level</b>; family history; <b>HRT or birth control pills</b>.</div>\n<div><b>The pain</b> — ask intensity, duration, triggers and relief. It ranges from <b>indigestion</b> or a mild persistent ache to <b>steady, constant RUQ pain</b>, which may <b>radiate to the right shoulder or scapula</b>; chronic pain may be vague. The usual pattern is <b>episodic</b> — \"<b>gallbladder attacks</b>,\" often after a <b>high-fat or large meal</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults and diabetes</b> — may have <b>no pain and no fever</b> — <b>localized tenderness</b> may be the only sign, and <b>acute confusion (delirium)</b> may be the <b>first</b> symptom in an older adult.</div>\n<div><b>Provider exam signs</b> — done only by the provider: <b>rebound tenderness (Blumberg sign)</b> — pain when deep pressure is suddenly released (peritoneal inflammation); <b>Murphy sign (inspiratory arrest)</b> — pain that stops a deep breath while the RUQ under the liver edge is pressed. A <b>sausage-shaped mass</b> (the swollen gallbladder) may be felt; percussion over the posterior ribs worsens the pain.</div>\n<div><b>Late and systemic signs</b> — chronic disease may go untreated until <b>jaundice, clay-colored stools and dark urine</b> appear, with icterus and <b>steatorrhea</b> (no bile → fat isn't absorbed). Inflammation brings <b>fever 99°F–102°F (37.2°C–38.9°C)</b>, <b>tachycardia</b> and <b>dehydration</b> from fever and vomiting; patients <b>avoid eating</b> because of pain.</div>\n<div><b>Key features (Box 51.1)</b> — episodic or vague upper abdominal pain to the right shoulder; <b>pain after a high-fat or high-volume meal</b>; anorexia, nausea and vomiting; dyspepsia, eructation, flatulence; fullness; <b>rebound tenderness (Blumberg)</b>; <b>inspiratory arrest (Murphy)</b>; fever; <b>jaundice, clay stools, dark urine</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Labs and imaging</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Labs</b> — first rule out <b>peptic ulcer, hepatitis and pancreatitis</b>. <b>WBC up</b> (inflammation); <b>alkaline phosphatase, AST and LDH</b> up with severe biliary obstruction; <b>direct and indirect bilirubin</b> up; <b>amylase and lipase</b> up if the <b>pancreas is involved</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Ultrasound first</b> — an <b>RUQ ultrasound</b> is the <b>best initial test</b> — safe, accurate, painless (it may provoke an \"ultrasonic Murphy sign\"); acute cholecystitis shows a <b>thick, swollen gallbladder wall</b> and fluid around it. <b>Plain x-rays</b> show only <b>calcified</b> stones.</div>\n<div><b>HIDA scan</b> — a <b>hepatobiliary (HIDA) scan</b>: a radioactive tracer is injected IV and about <b>20 minutes</b> later a gamma camera follows it out of the gallbladder to measure the <b>ejection rate</b>; <b>low bile flow = gallbladder disease with obstruction</b>. Teach <b>NPO before</b> and warn that the <b>camera is large and close</b> to the body.</div>\n<div><b>ERCP and MRCP</b> — <b>ERCP</b> when the cause is unclear or there are signs of obstruction (jaundice) — it can also remove stones. <b>MRCP</b> is less invasive and safer: oral or IV <b>gadolinium</b> contrast, then MRI. Ask about <b>hives or other allergies</b>; MRI is <b>contraindicated with a pacemaker</b> or other incompatible devices; gadolinium has <b>no iodine</b>, so allergy risk is lower.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Murphy sign works because the inflamed gallbladder sits right under the liver edge. When the patient breathes in, the diaphragm pushes the liver — and the gallbladder — down onto the examiner's fingers, and the sudden pain makes the patient <b>stop the breath mid-inhale</b>. Fat triggers attacks for a similar mechanical reason: fat in the duodenum releases <b>cholecystokinin</b>, the gallbladder contracts against a blocked duct, and the pressure is felt as colic.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"51-treating-cholecystitis-pain-drugs-and-dr","ch":"51","chTitle":"Biliary system and pancreas conditions","title":"Treating cholecystitis — pain, drugs and drains","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Nonsurgical care</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — (1) <b>acute or persistent pain</b> from inflammation and/or stones; (2) <b>weight loss</b> from eating less (pain, nausea, anorexia). Goal: pain <b>2–3 on a 0–10 scale</b>. If it can't be managed medically, <b>laparoscopic cholecystectomy</b> is the treatment of choice.</div>\n<div><b>Diet and fluids</b> — most patients need to <b>avoid fatty foods</b> to prevent more <b>biliary colic</b>; <b>withhold food and fluids</b> during nausea and vomiting; <b>IV fluids</b> prevent or treat dehydration.</div>\n<div><b>Pain drugs</b> — acute biliary pain needs <b>opioids</b> (<b>morphine or hydromorphone</b>); <b>all opioids can cause some sphincter of Oddi spasm</b>. <b>Ketorolac</b> (a potent NSAID) for mild to moderate pain — watch for <b>GI bleeding</b> (more pain, tachycardia, hypotension). <b>Antiemetics</b> for nausea; <b>IV antibiotics</b> depending on the cause or as a one-time surgical dose.</div>\n<div><b>Critical Rescue: biliary colic</b> — a stone moving through or lodged in the duct sets off <b>spasm</b>; the pain can be so severe that it brings <b>tachycardia, pallor or ash-gray skin, diaphoresis and prostration</b> (extreme exhaustion). <b>Assess for shock</b>, <b>call the provider or Rapid Response Team</b>, <b>stay with the patient</b> and <b>keep the HOB flat</b> if shock occurs.</div>\n<div><b>Dissolving stones</b> — for a few patients, <b>ursodiol or chenodiol</b> long term to <b>dissolve or stabilize</b> stones. <b>Gallbladder ultrasound every 6 months</b> in the first year to check the effect. Teach to report <b>diarrhea, vomiting or severe abdominal pain</b> (especially radiating to the <b>shoulders</b>) at once, and to <b>take it with food and milk</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Percutaneous biliary drain</b> — if surgery isn't possible: a <b>percutaneous transhepatic biliary catheter</b> placed under CT or ultrasound opens the blocked ducts (<b>cholecystostomy</b>). <b>Internal</b> drains send all bile into the duodenum; <b>internal/external</b> drains send part into a bag (may be <b>capped</b> long term — if <b>jaundice or leakage</b> develops, reconnect it to a bag and arrange a cholangiogram); <b>external-only</b> drains go to a bag kept <b>below the insertion site</b> (gravity). <b>Less bile drainage = the drain has stopped working.</b></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Pain this severe can drop the blood pressure on its own (a vagal reaction), and the same picture can also mean a complication — a perforating gallbladder or spreading infection. Either way the first moves are the same: keep the <b>head of the bed flat</b> so blood returns to the heart, <b>stay</b> to watch the trend, and get help fast so the provider can sort out which it is.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","ch":"51","chTitle":"Biliary system and pancreas conditions","title":"Cholecystectomy — laparoscopic and open","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Laparoscopic cholecystectomy (\"lap chole\")</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The gold standard</b> — minimally invasive and done far more often than open surgery, usually as <b>same-day surgery</b>: <b>few complications, very low death rate, rare bile duct injury, faster recovery, less pain</b>. No special preparation beyond routine general-anesthesia prep; review pain control, <b>deep breathing</b>, incision care and <b>leg exercises</b> (DVT).</div>\n<div><b>The procedure</b> — a small puncture at the <b>umbilicus</b> (plus a few incisions under an inch, or a single-incision approach); the abdomen is inflated with <b>3–4 L of carbon dioxide</b> (some centers use gasless lifting devices, easier on heart and lungs); the gallbladder is dissected free, the <b>cystic artery and duct closed</b>, stones crushed if large, and it comes out through the umbilicus.</div>\n<div><b>Action Alert</b> — check <b>SpO₂ by pulse oximetry often</b> until anesthesia wears off; remind the patient to do <b>deep breathing and incentive spirometry every hour</b>.</div>\n<div><b>After surgery</b> — food and water once <b>fully awake</b>; treat nausea and vomiting (<b>ondansetron</b> IV push or dissolving tablet; several doses may be needed); keep the IV until nausea stops; <b>HOB up</b> to prevent aspiration; help to the bathroom to void; <b>early walking</b> helps absorb the CO₂. Oral or IV push <b>opioid plus anti-inflammatory</b> — no continuous IV pain control; incisions are closed with <b>strips, small bandages or glue</b> that come off in <b>7–10 days</b>.</div>\n<div><b>CO₂ shoulder pain</b> — leftover <b>carbon dioxide</b> irritates the diaphragm and is felt in the <b>chest and shoulders</b>; <b>walking</b> helps absorb it, and many patients find <b>heat</b> helpful.</div>\n<div><b>Going home</b> — usually <b>the same day</b> (older or obese patients may stay overnight). <b>Ice and oral opioids</b> for incision pain for a few days; <b>shower the next day</b>; <b>rest 24 hours</b>, then resume activities — most are back to usual <b>within a week</b>. Warn about <b>intolerance to greasy food</b> (large fatty meals → pain and diarrhea, a mild <b>postcholecystectomy syndrome</b>) and <b>add high-fat foods back one at a time</b>.</div>\n<div><b>NOTES</b> — <b>natural orifice transluminal endoscopic surgery</b> — organs removed through the mouth, vagina or rectum; for the gallbladder, usually <b>through the vagina</b> (easily cleaned with antiseptic) via the cul-de-sac of Douglas. <b>No visible incision</b>, minimal pain, fewer complications.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Open cholecystectomy and after</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>When</b> — rare now — for <b>severe biliary obstruction</b> needing duct exploration, or <b>converted</b> from laparoscopy for poor visibility or suspected <b>gallbladder cancer</b>. A <b>right upper quadrant</b> incision; a <b>Jackson-Pratt drain</b> in the gallbladder bed — drainage is <b>serosanguineous and bile-stained in the first 24 hours</b>. A one-dose IV antibiotic before or during surgery.</div>\n<div><b>Care</b> — like any abdominal surgery: opioids, IV acetaminophen or anti-inflammatories; <b>cough and deep breathe with the incision splinted</b>; give <b>antiemetics early</b> (retching hurts the incision); NPO until fully awake; LOC, VS and pain; watch the incision for <b>redness or purulent drainage</b>; <b>walk early</b> (DVT, peristalsis). Diet <b>clear liquids → solids</b> as peristalsis returns; <b>home in 1–2 days</b>; a <b>low-fat diet</b> may ease early discomfort if bile flow is reduced.</div>\n<div><b>Nutrition</b> — goal: no weight loss (or regain). <b>High fiber, low fat</b>; avoid <b>gas-producing foods</b>; <b>small, frequent meals</b>; <b>favorite foods</b>; weigh regularly; follow <b>BUN, prealbumin, albumin, total protein and transferrin</b> if needed. Long term most need <b>no special diet</b> — eat nutritious meals and avoid <b>excess fat: fried food, butter, fast food</b>; weight reduction if obese.</div>\n<div><b>Postcholecystectomy syndrome (PCS, Box 51.2)</b> — <b>abdominal or epigastric pain with vomiting and/or diarrhea weeks to months after surgery</b> — teach the patient to report it. Biliary causes: <b>retained or new common bile duct stone</b>, bile duct leak, duct stricture, <b>sphincter of Oddi dysfunction</b>, pseudocyst, pancreatic or liver mass, primary sclerosing cholangitis. Nonbiliary: diverticular compression, coronary artery disease, intercostal neuritis, unexplained pain, psychiatric or neurologic disorders. Usually found and fixed with <b>ERCP</b>, plus pain control, antibiotics, fluids, nutrition (maybe short-term PN) and antiemetics.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — pain controlled by self-report and pain scale; nutrition adequate for metabolic needs.</div>\n<div><b>NCLEX pattern (lap chole teaching)</b> — tell the patient to expect <b>medication for nausea</b>, <b>closure strips or skin glue</b>, and <b>pulse oximetry</b> — not a 2-day stay, not a surgical drain and not an NG tube.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Without a gallbladder, bile still flows — it just drips continuously from the liver instead of arriving in a concentrated squirt when fat does. A modest fatty meal digests fine; a <b>large greasy meal</b> outruns the trickle, so undigested fat and extra bile acids reach the colon and cause <b>cramping and diarrhea</b>. That is why fats are added back <b>one at a time</b> and why most people need no special diet long term.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","ch":"51","chTitle":"Biliary system and pancreas conditions","title":"Acute pancreatitis — the inflammation exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What happens</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The disease</b> — a serious, sometimes <b>life-threatening inflammation</b> of the pancreas from <b>premature activation of its enzymes</b>, which destroy ducts and cells → <b>autodigestion</b> and fibrosis. It ranges from <b>mild edema and inflammation</b> to <b>necrotizing hemorrhagic pancreatitis (NHP)</b> — diffuse bleeding, fibrosis and tissue death. The pancreas is both <b>exocrine</b> (enzymes for starch, protein and fat, normally released <b>inactive</b>) and <b>endocrine</b> (insulin, glucagon).</div>\n<div><b>How it starts</b> — obstruction or injury causes <b>direct toxic injury</b> and release of <b>trypsin, lipase and elastase</b>; after duct obstruction, rising pressure can <b>rupture the ducts</b> and spill trypsin into the gland. Four processes follow: <b>lipolysis, proteolysis, necrosis of blood vessels and inflammation</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Fat necrosis and calcium</b> — the hallmark: <b>lipase</b> causes <b>enzymatic fat necrosis</b>; fatty acids <b>combine with ionized calcium</b> into a soaplike product, and the parathyroids can't keep up with the sudden drop → <b>hypocalcemia</b> (only ionized calcium is usable).</div>\n<div><b>Bleeding and shock</b> — <b>proteolysis</b> → thrombosis and gangrene of the pancreas. <b>Elastase</b> (activated by trypsin) dissolves the elastic fibers of vessels and ducts → bleeding from minor to <b>massive retroperitoneal hemorrhage</b>. <b>Kallikrein</b> releases <b>bradykinin</b> and kallidin → <b>vasodilation and leaky vessels</b>. <b>Many deaths come from irreversible hypovolemic shock.</b></div>\n<div><b>Abscess and pseudocyst</b> — white cells cluster around dead tissue; secondary bacterial invasion → <b>pancreatic abscess</b> (pus) that <b>must be drained promptly</b> to prevent sepsis. If the fluid is walled off by fibrous tissue → <b>pancreatic pseudocyst</b> (the book describes infected fluid walled off); pseudocysts can <b>rupture</b> or may need removal or drainage.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Complications (Box 51.3)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The list</b> — <b>pancreatic infection (septic shock), hemorrhage (NHP), acute kidney injury, paralytic ileus, hypovolemic shock, pleural effusion, ARDS, atelectasis, pneumonia, multiorgan failure, DIC and diabetes</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Lungs</b> — <b>left-sided pleural effusions</b> are common; <b>atelectasis and pneumonia</b> especially in older adults. <b>ARDS</b> (alveolar-capillary membrane disrupted) is a serious complication — <b>pulmonary failure causes more than half of deaths in the first week</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Circulation, kidneys and clotting</b> — <b>shock</b> from <b>vasodilation</b> (released vasoactive substances) plus <b>retroperitoneal loss of protein-rich fluid</b> → poor kidney perfusion → <b>AKI</b> (also from the systemic inflammatory response). Necrotic tissue and enzymes in the blood → <b>DIC</b> (clotting factors used up in microthrombi). <b>NHP</b> (up to <b>10%</b> of cases) causes <b>multiorgan failure</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Gut, bile and glucose</b> — <b>paralytic (adynamic) ileus</b> from peritoneal irritation by leaked enzymes; <b>jaundice</b> when a swollen pancreatic head, stones or a pseudocyst squeeze the common bile duct; <b>intermittent hyperglycemia</b> from glucagon release and less insulin; total destruction → <b>diabetes mellitus</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Causes and prevention</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Most common causes</b> — <b>gallstones (up to 40%)</b> and <b>alcohol (about 30%)</b>. Others (Box 51.4): <b>trauma</b> (blunt, stab, gunshot), <b>ERCP</b>, obstruction (tumors, cysts, abscesses, abnormal anatomy), <b>hyperlipidemia, hyperparathyroidism, hypercalcemia</b>, chronic kidney disease or transplant, inherited pancreatitis, penetrating ulcers, viruses (<b>coxsackievirus B, HIV</b>), <b>drugs</b> (opiates, anticonvulsants, <b>DPP-4 inhibitors and GLP-1 agonists</b>, sulfonamides, <b>thiazides, steroids</b>), <b>smoking</b>, cystic fibrosis, childbirth. <i>(The book calls GLP-1 agonists \"gliptins\"; gliptins are actually the DPP-4 inhibitors such as sitagliptin.)</i></div>\n<div><b>ERCP risk</b> — in one review of 824 patients having ERCP for bile duct obstruction, <b>almost 10% developed pancreatitis and 3% bled</b> — patients must be told before consenting.</div>\n<div><b>Who and outlook</b> — rising worldwide with alcohol and gallstones. Attacks cluster around <b>holidays and vacations</b> (heavy drinking, especially in <b>men</b>); in <b>women</b> it most often follows <b>gallstones or childbirth</b>. Early diagnosis and treatment reduce deaths; mortality is higher in hospitalized and older patients. <b>Prognosis is good with biliary pancreatitis and poor with alcoholic pancreatitis.</b></div>\n<div><b>Prevention</b> — <b>avoid excessive alcohol</b>; prevent obesity (a major cause of gallstones) with less high-fat, high-cholesterol food and regular exercise; if obese, <b>lose weight slowly</b> under professional care. Consider <b>food insecurity and access</b>; offer resources and telehealth. (NCLEX: the teaching point is \"<b>avoid excessive alcohol of any type</b>\" — not rapid weight loss, not avoiding ERCP or pregnancies.)</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Calcium falls in pancreatitis for a chemical reason, not a hormonal one: the fatty acids freed by lipase react with calcium the way soap scum forms in hard water. The calcium is still in the body, but it is <b>locked in dead fat</b> where it can't be used — so the nurse watches for <b>twitching, numbness and irritability</b> and expects IV calcium.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","ch":"51","chTitle":"Biliary system and pancreas conditions","title":"Recognizing acute pancreatitis — pain, signs and labs","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">History and signs</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — interview <b>after pain is controlled</b>. Ask whether pain follows <b>alcohol or a high-fat meal</b>; <b>how much alcohol over how long</b>; family or personal history of alcoholism, pancreatitis, trauma or <b>biliary disease</b>; recent <b>cholecystectomy or ERCP</b>; other diseases that cause pancreatitis; <b>recent viral infections</b>; every prescription, <b>OTC, nutritional and herbal</b> product taken recently.</div>\n<div><b>The pain</b> — diagnosis rests on symptoms plus labs and imaging. Typically <b>severe pain in the mid-epigastrium or LUQ</b>, <b>sudden onset</b>, radiating to the <b>back, left flank or left shoulder</b>; <b>intense, \"boring\" (going through the body) and continuous</b>; <b>worse lying supine</b>; eased by the <b>fetal position</b> (knees to chest, spine flexed) or <b>sitting up and leaning forward</b>. Nausea and vomiting may cause weight loss — weigh the patient.</div>\n<div><b>Abdominal exam</b> — look for <b>generalized jaundice</b>, <b>gray-blue discoloration around the umbilicus (Cullen sign)</b> and <b>of the flanks (Turner sign)</b> from enzymes leaking into the tissues. <b>Absent or decreased bowel sounds = paralytic ileus</b>. Tenderness, <b>rigidity and guarding</b> on light palpation = <b>peritonitis</b>. <b>Dullness on percussion = pancreatic ascites</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Vital signs and lungs</b> — check <b>often</b> (or assign and closely supervise): <b>fever, tachycardia, falling BP</b>. Listen for <b>adventitious or diminished breath sounds</b>; watch for <b>dyspnea or orthopnea</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Critical Rescue</b> — <b>hypotension and tachycardia</b> may mean <b>pancreatic hemorrhage, massive fluid shift or abdominal sepsis</b> → shock. A <b>change in behavior or LOC</b> may mean <b>alcohol withdrawal, hypoxia or impending septic shock</b>. Call the provider or <b>Rapid Response Team</b> for significant changes — <b>especially LOC</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Alcohol history</b> — once pain is controlled, <b>tactfully</b> explore drinking in <b>private</b>, building trust; the <b>CAGE questionnaire</b> or another screen helps. Ask when drinking increases — <b>holidays, vacations, weekends</b>, television — and about recent <b>losses or stress</b> (a death, a lost job).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Labs and imaging (Table 51.1)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Pancreatic enzymes</b> — <b>amylase, lipase, trypsin and elastase</b> rise from <b>pancreatic cell injury</b>. <b>Amylase</b> rises <b>within 12–24 hours</b> and stays up <b>2–3 days</b> (many other conditions raise it); persistent elevation suggests <b>duct obstruction or a leak</b>. <b>Lipase</b> rises <b>later</b> and stays up <b>5–7 days</b> — useful when the patient comes in days after the pain began; pancreatitis is its most common cause. Urine amylase and lipase rise too.</div>\n<div><b>Biliary pancreatitis</b> — <b>bilirubin</b> up; <b>ALT is a sensitive marker</b> — a <b>threefold or greater rise</b> confirms <b>acute biliary pancreatitis</b>; AST up with liver involvement.</div>\n<div><b>Inflammation and glucose</b> — <b>WBC (leukocytosis), ESR, C-reactive protein and glucose</b> rise and often <b>track severity</b>; glucose rises because injured islets release <b>less insulin</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Calcium, magnesium and the rest</b> — <b>calcium and magnesium fall</b> with fat necrosis (calcium may stay low <b>7–10 days</b>). <b>BUN, glucose and triglycerides</b> usually high; <b>hemoconcentration</b> from third-space loss; <b>thrombocytopenia</b>; <b>albumin low</b> because cytokines (e.g., TNF) let it leak out of vessels.</div>\n<div><b>Imaging</b> — <b>ultrasound</b> finds causes such as <b>gallstones</b> at the bedside but can't see the pancreas well through <b>bowel gas</b> — <b>contrast-enhanced CT</b> is more reliable and also rules out pseudocyst or duct stones. Abdominal x-ray may show stones; <b>chest x-ray</b> may show a <b>raised left diaphragm or pleural effusion</b>. Pancreatic stones are best found by <b>ERCP</b>.</div>\n<div><b>NCLEX pattern (labs)</b> — expect <b>elevated amylase, elevated lipase and increased leukocytes</b> — glucose goes <b>up</b>, not down, and trypsin and elastase go <b>up</b>, not down.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Cullen and Turner signs are bruises from the inside out. Enzyme-rich, bloody fluid from the retroperitoneum tracks along tissue planes to the skin around the <b>umbilicus</b> and the <b>flanks</b>, where it shows as gray-blue discoloration. Seeing them means the pancreatitis is <b>hemorrhagic and severe</b> — a reason to expect shock rather than wait for it.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","ch":"51","chTitle":"Biliary system and pancreas conditions","title":"Caring for acute pancreatitis — pain, NPO and nutrition","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Resting the pancreas and relieving pain</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Priorities</b> — problems: (1) <b>severe acute pain</b> from inflammation and enzyme leakage; (2) <b>weight loss and poor nutrition</b> (can't eat or absorb). Supportive care: <b>relieve symptoms, decrease inflammation, anticipate complications</b> — and always <b>ABCs</b> first, with oxygen and respiratory support as needed. Pain goal <b>2–3/10</b>. Severe cases go to <b>critical care</b> for hemodynamic monitoring.</div>\n<div><b>NPO and fluids</b> — <b>NPO</b> during the acute period to <b>rest the pancreas</b> and cut enzyme secretion; <b>IV isotonic fluids</b> for hydration; <b>IV calcium and magnesium</b> as needed; strict <b>I&amp;O</b> (some need an indwelling catheter). Mild pancreatitis: <b>IV fluids, pain control and drugs</b>.</div>\n<div><b>NG tube</b> — only for <b>severe</b> illness with <b>continuous vomiting or biliary obstruction</b> — decompression keeps gastric juice from entering the duodenum (which stimulates the pancreas).</div>\n<div><b>Action Alert: ileus</b> — <b>paralytic ileus is common</b>, so NG suction may be prolonged. Ask often whether the patient has <b>passed flatus or had a stool</b> — <b>bowel sounds aren't a reliable sign</b> that peristalsis is back; <b>flatus or a BM is the most reliable</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Pain drugs</b> — opioids by <b>PCA</b> — <b>morphine or hydromorphone</b>; also <b>IV or transdermal fentanyl</b> or <b>epidural</b> analgesia. Pain eases in <b>2–3 days</b> in mild cases but can last <b>up to 2 weeks</b> in severe ones. <b>Famotidine or pantoprazole</b> reduce gastric acid; <b>antibiotics mainly for necrotizing pancreatitis or abscess</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Comfort</b> — <b>side-lying with knees to chest (fetal position)</b> or <b>sitting with knees flexed</b>. Frequent <b>mouth and nares care</b> (delegate to AP) — NPO and drugs dry the mouth and can cause <b>parotitis</b>. Lower <b>anxiety</b> (it worsens pain): explain everything, reassure, offer <b>music, reading and visitors</b> for distraction.</div>\n<div><b>Action Alert: lungs and calcium</b> — assess <b>respiratory status every 4 hours</b> or more; <b>oxygen</b> for comfort; <b>pleural effusions</b> add pain; detect <b>fluid overload</b> by <b>weight gain, crackles and dyspnea</b>; watch for <b>respiratory failure</b>. Watch for <b>hypocalcemia</b>: <b>muscle twitching, numbness, irritability</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Procedures and surgery</b> — gallstone pancreatitis → urgent <b>ERCP with sphincterotomy</b>; if that fails, <b>laparoscopic cholecystectomy</b>. Surgery otherwise usually <b>isn't indicated</b> — except for <b>pseudocyst or abscess</b> (laparoscopic drainage, or <b>CT-guided percutaneous drainage</b> for high-risk patients).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Nutrition and going home</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Feeding</b> — <b>NPO early</b>, antiemetics as needed. Severe pancreatitis with no eating for <b>24–48 hours</b> → <b>jejunal tube feeding</b> (unless ileus) — early nutrition helps immunity and may prevent complications. <b>Enteral is preferred over TPN</b> (fewer <b>glucose spikes</b> and other TPN complications). <b>Weigh daily</b>; plan with the provider, dietitian and pharmacist.</div>\n<div><b>Eating again</b> — when tolerated: <b>small, frequent, moderate- to high-carbohydrate, high-protein, low-fat</b> meals, <b>bland</b> with little spice; <b>no caffeine</b> (tea, coffee, cola, chocolate) <b>or alcohol</b>. Report <b>nausea, vomiting or diarrhea</b> with the first meals at once. Supplements: liquid formulas, <b>fat-soluble vitamins</b> and minerals; sometimes glutamine, omega-3s, fiber, antioxidants.</div>\n<div><b>Home</b> — very weak patients may need to <b>stay on one floor</b>, limiting stairs and strenuous activity; the case manager plans where to recover; <b>home care</b> for wound care and rehab after a complicated stay. Teach early — as soon as acute pain subsides.</div>\n<div><b>Teaching</b> — goal: <b>no more attacks and no progression to chronic disease</b>. <b>Stop drinking</b> — alcohol brings the pain back and further autodigestion can lead to <b>chronic pancreatitis</b>. Report <b>acute abdominal pain</b> or signs of <b>biliary disease (jaundice, clay stools, dark urine)</b>. Follow-up care; <b>Alcoholics Anonymous</b>, <b>Al-Anon and Alateen</b> for families.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — pain controlled (self-report, pain scale); nutrition adequate for metabolic needs.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every intervention for acute pancreatitis is about <b>not waking the pancreas</b>. Food, acid in the duodenum and even the sight and smell of food tell it to secrete enzymes — the very enzymes digesting it. So the patient is <b>NPO</b>, acid is reduced, an NG tube drains the stomach if vomiting persists, and feeding goes <b>past the duodenum into the jejunum</b> where it barely stimulates the gland.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","ch":"51","chTitle":"Biliary system and pancreas conditions","title":"Chronic pancreatitis","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The disease</b> — a <b>progressive, destructive</b> disease with <b>remissions and flare-ups</b>; inflammation and <b>fibrosis</b> cause <b>pancreatic insufficiency</b>. There is <b>no known cure</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Types</b> — <b>chronic calcifying pancreatitis</b> — most common, mainly from <b>alcohol</b>: secretions <b>precipitate as protein plugs</b> in the ducts, the duct lining ulcerates, fibrosis and <b>calcification</b> follow, and the gland turns <b>hard and firm</b>. <b>Chronic obstructive</b> — inflammation, spasm and obstruction of the <b>sphincter of Oddi</b>, often from <b>gallstones</b>. <b>Autoimmune</b> — immunoglobulins invade the pancreas (and lungs, liver); linked to cancers elsewhere (possibly paraneoplastic). <b>Idiopathic and hereditary</b> — <b>SPINK1</b> (a trypsin inhibitor), <b>CFTR</b> (cystic fibrosis) and <b>BRCA2</b> mutations; hereditary pancreatitis carries a <b>50- to 60-fold</b> higher risk of <b>pancreatic cancer</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Loss of function</b> — <b>exocrine</b>: less enzyme and bicarbonate; once enzyme output falls far enough → <b>steatorrhea</b> — <b>pale, bulky, frothy, foul-smelling</b> stools with visible fat; <b>weight loss, muscle wasting</b>, debilitation; protein malabsorption → low albumin → <b>\"starvation\" edema</b> of the feet, legs and hands. <b>Endocrine</b>: <b>diabetes mellitus</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Lungs</b> — <b>pleuritic pain, pleural effusions, pulmonary infiltrates</b>; <b>pancreatic ascites</b> limits the diaphragm and lung expansion; <b>ARDS</b> in the very ill.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing it (Box 51.5)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Pain</b> — the major symptom: <b>continuous burning or gnawing dullness</b> with <b>acute flare-ups</b> — <b>intense and relentless</b>; flare-ups become <b>more frequent</b> as fibrosis advances. Abdominal <b>tenderness is less</b> than in acute pancreatitis.</div>\n<div><b>Other signs</b> — <b>ascites</b> (dullness, <b>fluid wave test</b>); possible <b>LUQ mass</b> (pseudocyst or abscess); <b>respiratory compromise</b> (adventitious or diminished sounds, dyspnea, orthopnea); <b>steatorrhea and clay-colored stools</b>; <b>weight loss</b>; <b>jaundice, dark urine</b>; <b>polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia</b> (diabetes). Ask for a <b>stool sample</b> or about stool appearance.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnosis</b> — symptoms plus <b>ERCP</b> (pancreatic and bile ducts), CT, contrast MRI, ultrasound and <b>endoscopic ultrasound</b>. <b>Amylase and lipase are normal or only moderately high</b> (little gland left to leak them). Bile duct obstruction → <b>bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase up</b>. <b>Intermittent hyperglycemia</b> — check fasting and nonfasting glucose.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Treatment</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Pain</b> — drugs are the main treatment, given by <b>assessed pain intensity</b> and evaluated; <b>opioids</b> are used most at first, but <b>dependency</b> can develop, so <b>nonopioids</b> are tried too.</div>\n<div><b>Pancreatic enzyme replacement (PERT)</b> — the <b>standard of care</b> to prevent malnutrition, malabsorption and weight loss: capsules or tablets of <b>amylase, lipase and protease</b> (pancrelipase). <b>Don't chew or crush</b> delayed-release capsules or enteric tablets; <b>take with every meal and snack</b>. Dose depends on the malabsorption; <b>record the number and consistency of stools</b> — <b>fewer, less fatty stools = it's working</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Acid control</b> — gastric acid <b>destroys lipase</b>, so <b>H₂ blockers or PPIs</b> (or oral <b>sodium bicarbonate</b>) may make PERT work better.</div>\n<div><b>Action Alert: glucose</b> — diabetes → <b>insulin or oral antidiabetic drugs</b>. Patients on <b>TPN</b> are especially prone to hyperglycemia and need <b>regular insulin added</b> to the solution; check <b>fingerstick glucose every 2–4 hours</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Nutrition</b> — patients eat less to avoid pain, so weight is hard to keep; <b>TPN or total enteral nutrition</b> with vitamins and minerals may be needed. With the dietitian: <b>up to 4000–6000 calories a day</b>, <b>high carbohydrate and protein</b>, <b>avoid high-fat food</b> (worse diarrhea), <b>no alcohol</b> (cessation programs).</div>\n<div><b>Surgery</b> — not primary — for <b>ongoing or incapacitating pain</b> or complications (<b>abscess, pseudocyst</b>): laparoscopic drainage; <b>cholecystectomy or choledochotomy</b> if biliary disease is the cause; <b>sphincterotomy</b> for a fibrotic sphincter (endoscopic for poor surgical candidates); <b>distal pancreatectomy</b>; endoscopic <b>necrosectomy</b> or NOTES to remove dead tissue. <b>Total pancreatectomy with auto islet transplantation</b>: the patient's own islet cells are isolated and infused into the <b>liver</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Living with it (Box 51.6)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Home setup</b> — case managers follow the patient; <b>toilet facilities easily accessible</b> (frequent fatty stools) or a <b>bedside commode</b>; help with <b>finances</b> (costs, inability to work); home nurses assess pain, enzyme therapy and coping.</div>\n<div><b>Skin</b> — frequent, abrasive fatty stools damage the skin: keep it <b>dry</b>, <b>clean thoroughly after each stool</b>, apply a <b>moisture barrier</b>; report any breakdown.</div>\n<div><b>Preventing flare-ups</b> — avoid triggers such as <b>caffeinated drinks</b>; <b>no alcohol</b> (self-help group); <b>no nicotine</b>; <b>bland, low-fat, high-protein, moderate-carbohydrate</b> meals without spices; <b>small, high-calorie meals and snacks</b>; <b>take the enzymes with meals</b>; <b>rest often</b> and stay on <b>one floor</b> until strength returns.</div>\n<div><b>Diabetes and support</b> — if diabetes develops, the <b>certified diabetes educator</b> teaches drugs, diet and glucose monitoring; refer to a <b>counselor, AA or Al-Anon</b> as appropriate.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Enzyme capsules only work if they are in the gut <b>at the same time as the food</b> — they digest whatever is there and then pass on. That is why they go with <b>every meal and every snack</b>, why they must not be crushed (stomach acid destroys unprotected lipase), and why the nurse judges the dose by the <b>stools</b>: fewer, smaller, less greasy stools mean the fat is finally being absorbed.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","ch":"51","chTitle":"Biliary system and pancreas conditions","title":"Pancreatic cancer — risk, signs and treatment","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The disease</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Why it is deadly</b> — a <b>leading cause of cancer death</b> in the US: the pancreas is <b>hidden</b> behind other organs, so most tumors are found <b>late</b>; treatment has limited results and <b>5-year survival is very low</b>.</div>\n<div><b>The tumor</b> — primary tumors are usually <b>adenocarcinomas</b> that grow fast and spread by <b>direct extension and lymph and blood</b> to the stomach, duodenum, gallbladder and intestine, then lungs, peritoneum, liver, spleen and nodes; it may also be <b>metastatic</b> from lung, breast, thyroid, kidney or skin. The <b>head</b> is the most common site — small tumors that <b>compress the common bile duct</b> → <b>jaundice</b> and a dilated gallbladder. <b>Body and tail</b> tumors are large and may be <b>palpable</b> in thin patients; spread through the splenic vein to the <b>liver</b> → hepatomegaly.</div>\n<div><b>Blood clots</b> — <b>venous thromboembolism</b> is common: necrotic tumor products are <b>thromboplastic</b> (make blood hypercoagulable), plus <b>immobility and extensive surgery</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Risk factors</b> — <b>smoking and tobacco, obesity, chronic pancreatitis, cirrhosis, age over 65, genetic syndromes</b>. More common in <b>men</b> and in <b>Black</b> people, who tend to be <b>diagnosed later</b> and have <b>less insurance coverage</b> — people without insurance may not seek care.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing it (Box 51.7)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Signs</b> — slow and vague, depending on where the tumor is. The <b>first sign may be jaundice</b> — which already suggests <b>advanced</b> disease. Also <b>icterus, clay-colored stools, dark urine</b>; <b>vague, dull abdominal pain radiating to the back</b>; <b>weight loss, anorexia, nausea, vomiting</b>; <b>glucose intolerance or new-onset diabetes</b>; splenomegaly, flatulence, <b>GI bleeding</b>, ascites; <b>leg or calf pain (thrombophlebitis)</b>; weakness and fatigue.</div>\n<div><b>Tests</b> — <b>no blood test is diagnostic</b>. Amylase, lipase, alkaline phosphatase and bilirubin rise (how much depends on the pancreatic and biliary damage). <b>CEA</b> is elevated in most patients; <b>CA 19-9</b> is used to <b>monitor</b> a proven cancer and watch for spread or recurrence. <b>Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) is the gold standard</b> for finding and diagnosing the tumor; also ERCP and CT. <b>Percutaneous transhepatic cholangiography (PTC)</b> with a <b>biliary drain (PTBD)</b> can decompress the blocked ducts. Paracentesis fluid may show cancer cells and high amylase.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Treatment (palliative for most)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Goal</b> — prevent spread and <b>decrease pain</b> — for most patients treatment is <b>palliative, not curative</b>; the cancer often recurs.</div>\n<div><b>Chemotherapy</b> — shrinks the tumor to relieve pain; before, after or instead of surgery; combinations beat single drugs. Standard: <b>6 months of FOLFIRINOX</b> (folinic acid, <b>5-FU, irinotecan, oxaliplatin</b>; the book spells it \"FOLFORINOX\"). If not tolerated: <b>gemcitabine with capecitabine</b> or <b>gemcitabine with nab-paclitaxel</b> (metastatic disease); <b>gemcitabine alone</b> for those who can't take combinations. Watch for <b>fatigue, rash, anorexia, diarrhea</b>. Targeted drugs (growth factor, angiogenesis and <b>kinase inhibitors</b>) are being studied.</div>\n<div><b>Pain control</b> — <b>high-dose opioids (usually morphine) given before the pain peaks</b>; with this prognosis, <b>dependency isn't a concern</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Radiation and stents</b> — <b>external beam radiation</b> for resectable or borderline tumors, and for <b>pain relief</b> in advanced cancer (shrinks the tumor, relieves obstruction, helps absorption) — it <b>doesn't improve survival</b> in advanced disease. <b>Biliary stents</b> (plastic tubes placed during <b>ERCP</b> or <b>PTC</b>) keep the ducts open in high-risk patients.</div>\n<div><b>Surgery</b> — <b>complete resection is the only effective treatment</b>, but only for <b>small tumors</b>. <b>Partial pancreatectomy</b> for resectable or borderline tumors: the <b>Whipple</b> or <b>pylorus-preserving pancreaticoduodenectomy</b> for the <b>head and neck</b>, <b>distal pancreatectomy</b> for the <b>body and tail</b>; <b>radical pancreatectomy</b> for locally advanced tumors. Laparoscopic (hand- or robot-assisted) surgery is growing; open surgery is still common. A laparoscopic bypass can divert bile into the <b>jejunum</b> to relieve obstruction.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Painless jaundice in an older adult is a classic warning for pancreatic head cancer. A tumor in the head sits right against the common bile duct, so even a small one squeezes it shut and bilirubin backs up — often before the tumor itself causes pain. By then, though, the cancer has usually spread, which is why the book calls jaundice a sign of <b>late, advanced</b> disease.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","ch":"51","chTitle":"Biliary system and pancreas conditions","title":"The Whipple procedure — surgery and recovery","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Before and during</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Getting ready</b> — malnourished, debilitated patients are poor surgical risks. A <b>jejunostomy</b> tube is often placed before a Whipple (or late in the disease) for <b>enteral feeding</b> — it <b>prevents reflux and helps absorption</b>; start feedings at <b>low concentration and volume</b> and increase as tolerated, by <b>pump</b>, watching <b>diarrhea</b> for tolerance. <b>TPN</b> may be added, often through a <b>PICC</b> — <b>meticulous line care and sterile dressing changes</b> prevent catheter sepsis; follow <b>prealbumin and albumin</b>. No bowel prep for laparoscopic surgery; <b>NPO 6–8 hours</b> before either approach.</div>\n<div><b>What is removed</b> — the <b>radical pancreaticoduodenectomy</b> removes the <b>head of the pancreas, the duodenum, part of the jejunum, part or all of the stomach, and the gallbladder</b> (sometimes the <b>spleen</b>), then reconnects the <b>pancreatic duct, common bile duct and stomach to the jejunum</b> — <b>pancreaticojejunostomy, choledochojejunostomy, gastrojejunostomy</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">After the Whipple</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Complications (Table 51.2)</b> — cardiovascular: <b>hemorrhage at the anastomoses</b> with hypovolemia, MI, heart failure, thrombophlebitis. Lungs: <b>atelectasis, pneumonia, PE, ARDS</b>, pulmonary edema. Endocrine/renal: <b>unstable diabetes</b>, chronic kidney disease. GI: <b>paralytic ileus</b>, gastric retention or ulcer, bowel obstruction from peritonitis, <b>acute pancreatitis</b>, hepatic failure, mesenteric thrombosis. Skin: <b>infection, dehiscence, fistulas</b> (pancreatic, gastric, biliary). Open surgery → <b>surgical ICU</b>.</div>\n<div><b>After laparoscopic Whipple</b> — shorter recovery, less pain, fewer severe complications. Still watch for: <b>diabetes</b> (check glucose often), <b>hemorrhage</b> (pulse, BP, skin color, <b>LOC</b>), <b>wound infection</b> (temperature, redness, induration), <b>bowel obstruction</b> (bowel sounds, stools), <b>intraabdominal abscess</b> (temperature, severe pain).</div>\n<div><b>Tubes and drains</b> — <b>NPO</b> with an <b>NG tube</b> to decompress the stomach (monitor drainage and patency) at <b>low continuous or high intermittent suction</b> — it keeps the remaining stomach or jejunum empty and <b>rests the remaining pancreas</b>. Biliary drains (open surgery) relieve stress on the anastomoses: keep them <b>dependent, free of tension and kinks</b>. Drainage should be <b>serosanguineous</b>; <b>clear, colorless, bile-tinged fluid or frank blood with rising output</b> may mean an <b>anastomotic leak</b> — most leaks happen <b>7–10 days</b> after surgery; hemorrhage can be early or late. <b>Semi-Fowler</b> position eases tension on the suture lines and helps the lungs.</div>\n<div><b>Critical Rescue: fistula</b> — a <b>fistula</b> — from partial or total breakdown of an anastomosis — is the <b>most common and most serious</b> complication. Leaking <b>bile, pancreatic enzymes or gastric juice</b> is <b>corrosive to the skin</b>, and internal leakage causes <b>chemical peritonitis</b> (<b>boardlike rigid abdomen</b>, needs multiple antibiotics). <b>Call the surgeon or Rapid Response Team at once</b> with your findings.</div>\n<div><b>Action Alert: fluids and shock</b> — the open Whipple is long: big <b>blood loss</b>, evaporation from exposed bowel, losses from the <b>NG and drains</b>, and <b>low albumin</b> (malnutrition) → <b>third spacing → shock</b>. Watch for <b>falling BP, rising HR</b>, low pulmonary artery pressures (ICU), <b>low urine output</b>, <b>pitting and sacral edema</b>, and <b>intake far exceeding output</b>. Keep <b>sequential compression devices</b> on (DVT).</div>\n<div><b>Fluids, electrolytes and glucose</b> — <b>IV isotonic fluids with colloids</b>; follow <b>Hgb and Hct</b> for transfusion; replace low <b>sodium, potassium, chloride and calcium</b>. Glucose can swing <b>high or low</b> from stress and handling of the pancreas. Most insulin-making cells are in the <b>body and tail</b>, so many keep enough gland to avoid diabetes (and many had diabetes already) — but after a <b>total (radical) pancreatectomy</b>, the patient <b>needs insulin</b>. <b>Check glucose often.</b></div>\n<div><b>NCLEX pattern (laparoscopic Whipple)</b> — assess for <b>bleeding, wound infection, intestinal obstruction, diabetes and abdominal abscess</b> — all five apply.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Going home and end of life</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Equity</b> — <b>Black patients</b> have <b>more complications and higher mortality</b> after pancreatic resection — partly because surgery is more often done in <b>low-volume hospitals</b> (less experienced teams) and partly from insurance and access gaps.</div>\n<div><b>Where next</b> — a <b>case manager</b> follows the patient in hospital and at home; the stage of the cancer and home resources decide <b>home, skilled nursing or hospice</b>; tailor plans to physical limits and coordinate with whoever gives care.</div>\n<div><b>Support</b> — the diagnosis frightens and overwhelms families: offer <b>compassionate emotional support</b>, help them look <b>realistically at the physical care</b> needed, warn that their own health is at risk and counseling may help; offer <b>spiritual support</b> or the <b>hospital chaplain</b> (the nurse may make the first contact if the family wishes).</div>\n<div><b>Preparing for death</b> — often diagnosed only <b>months before death</b>; care at home is mostly <b>palliative</b> (pain). Help the patient prepare — a <b>will</b>, seeing family and friends, <b>funeral or memorial wishes</b> — for a <b>dignified death</b>; arrange home care and AP visits, <b>palliative and hospice care</b> and cancer support groups.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The Whipple joins three things to the jejunum — the pancreatic duct, the bile duct and the stomach — and each join carries a fluid that can digest tissue. That is why a leak is so dangerous (pancreatic enzymes eat skin and cause chemical peritonitis) and why the nurse reads the <b>drain color</b>: serosanguineous is healing; <b>clear or bile-tinged</b> fluid means pancreatic juice or bile is escaping through a join.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"51-adpie","ch":"51","chTitle":"Biliary system and pancreas conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Every patient in this chapter is watched for the same turning points — <b>pain that changes or becomes overwhelming</b>, <b>fluid shifting out of the circulation into shock</b>, and <b>breathing that fails</b> — while the nurse rests the gut (NPO, NG when needed), replaces fluid, calcium and nutrition, and teaches the low-fat, no-alcohol habits (and enzymes with every meal) that prevent the next attack.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div>pain pattern (RUQ after fatty meals vs sudden boring epigastric pain to the back), <b>alcohol amount and years</b> (CAGE, after pain control, in private), <b>gallstones or biliary disease</b>, recent <b>ERCP</b> or surgery, <b>drugs</b> (thiazides, steroids, GLP-1 agonists, DPP-4 inhibitors) and supplements, high triglycerides or calcium, obesity and <b>rapid weight loss</b>, pregnancy, hormones, smoking, family history; weight loss and stool changes.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical</div>vital signs often (<b>fever, tachycardia, hypotension</b>), <b>SpO₂ and lung sounds</b> (left effusion, crackles); abdomen — <b>Murphy sign</b> (provider), guarding and <b>rigidity</b>, bowel sounds and <b>flatus</b>, <b>Cullen and Turner</b> signs, ascites; <b>jaundice, icterus</b>, clay stools, dark urine, <b>steatorrhea</b>; <b>twitching or numbness</b> (low calcium); LOC (withdrawal, hypoxia, sepsis); daily weight; drains and incisions after surgery.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Labs and tests</div>WBC; <b>amylase and lipase</b>; <b>ALT (≥ 3× = biliary)</b>, bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase; <b>glucose, calcium, magnesium</b>, BUN, triglycerides, albumin, CRP, Hgb/Hct, platelets; <b>RUQ ultrasound</b>, HIDA, ERCP or MRCP, <b>contrast CT</b>, chest x-ray; CEA, <b>CA 19-9</b> and <b>EUS</b> for cancer.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>cholecystitis: <b>acute or persistent pain</b> and <b>weight loss</b>. Acute pancreatitis: <b>severe acute pain</b> and <b>inadequate nutrition</b>, with risk of <b>shock, ARDS, hypocalcemia, DIC and AKI</b>. Chronic pancreatitis: <b>persistent pain, malabsorption, diabetes, skin breakdown</b>. Pancreatic cancer and Whipple: <b>pain, malnutrition, anastomotic leak and fistula, hemorrhage, glucose instability, grief</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>pain <b>2–3 on a 0–10 scale</b>; stable weight and adequate nutrition; <b>stable vital signs and urine output</b> without shock; clear lungs and normal SpO₂; normal calcium and glucose; <b>flatus or stool</b> returns; fewer, less fatty stools on enzymes; anastomoses heal without leak; the patient abstains from alcohol and states what to report.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Gallbladder</div>avoid fat; NPO for vomiting, IV fluids; <b>morphine or hydromorphone</b>, ketorolac (watch bleeding), antiemetics; <b>biliary colic with pallor, sweating, prostration → assess for shock, call RRT, stay, HOB flat</b>; ursodiol teaching (with food and milk; ultrasound every 6 months); biliary drain bag <b>below the site</b>; lap chole care — <b>SpO₂, incentive spirometry every hour, ondansetron, walk early, heat for CO₂ shoulder pain</b>, shower the next day, <b>add fats back one at a time</b>; report PCS signs.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Acute pancreatitis</div><b>ABCs and oxygen</b>; <b>NPO</b>, IV isotonic fluids, <b>IV calcium and magnesium</b>, strict I&amp;O; NG for continuous vomiting; <b>PCA opioids</b>; <b>fetal position</b>; mouth and nares care; <b>respiratory status every 4 hours</b> (crackles, weight gain = overload); watch <b>twitching, numbness</b>; <b>flatus or stool = peristalsis back</b>; <b>VS and LOC</b> for shock (call RRT); <b>jejunal feeding if no intake for 24–48 hours</b>; then small, low-fat, high-carb, high-protein, bland meals without caffeine or alcohol.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Chronic pancreatitis, cancer and Whipple</div>chronic: <b>pancrelipase with every meal and snack, not crushed</b>, count stools; H₂ blocker or PPI; <b>4000–6000 kcal</b>, high protein, low fat; <b>insulin</b> (fingerstick every 2–4 hours on TPN); skin barrier after each stool. Cancer: <b>scheduled high-dose opioids</b>, chemo side effects, jejunostomy feeds and sterile TPN line care. Whipple: <b>NG patency</b>, drains dependent, <b>report clear, bile-tinged or bloody drainage that increases</b>, <b>semi-Fowler</b>, watch for third spacing and shock, SCDs, electrolytes and <b>glucose</b>; hospice and family support.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>pain at goal; tolerating diet without nausea, vomiting or diarrhea; weight stable; vital signs, urine output, SpO₂, calcium and glucose in range; no rigidity, fever or leak; stools fewer and less fatty on enzymes; the patient can state the diet (<b>low fat, no caffeine or alcohol</b>), how to take enzymes, and what to report — <b>severe or new abdominal pain, jaundice, clay stools, dark urine</b>, pain and vomiting or diarrhea weeks after cholecystectomy, and bleeding or fever after surgery.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"52-overview","ch":"52","chTitle":"Malnutrition: undernutrition and obesity","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">52 · Chapter 52</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Nutrition · fluid &amp; electrolyte balance</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Malnutrition: undernutrition and obesity</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> <b>Malnutrition</b> means too little, too much or the wrong balance of energy and nutrients — so it covers both ends of the scale. The two exemplars are <b>undernutrition</b> (protein-energy undernutrition, starvation, the older adult who stops eating) and <b>obesity</b> (a BMI of 30 or more, with heart disease, diabetes and sleep apnea following). The nurse <b>screens every patient within 24 hours</b> of admission, <b>weighs accurately</b>, feeds by mouth whenever possible, and safely runs <b>tube feedings and TPN</b> — checking placement, flushing, keeping the head of the bed up, and watching for <b>refeeding syndrome</b>. For obesity, the nurse teaches realistic <b>diet, activity and behavior</b> change, knows the weight-loss <b>drugs</b>, and cares for patients after <b>bariatric surgery</b>, where the airway and <b>anastomotic leaks</b> are the big dangers.</p><div id=\"ch52-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Energy balance: weight is stable when intake equals energy used. Carbohydrate, protein and fat are digested and absorbed in the GI tract; when calories run short the body <b>burns its own protein</b> for energy. Healthy adults need about <b>1600–3200 calories a day</b> depending on age, sex, size and activity.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Undernutrition: too few calories and protein — poor access, NPO stays, illness, poor teeth, depression, eating disorders — so fat and muscle are broken down (<b>marasmus</b>) or protein runs short despite enough calories (<b>kwashiorkor</b>). Obesity: intake exceeds use; hormones that regulate appetite and fat (<b>adipokines</b>) are dysregulated; fat inflames vessels and strains every organ.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Undernutrition: <b>unintentional weight loss</b> (5% in a month, 10% in 6 months), muscle wasting, <b>cachexia</b>, poor wound healing, infections, dry flaky skin, brittle or easily pulled hair, edema (low protein), weakness, cold intolerance. Obesity: BMI ≥ 30, <b>waist &gt; 35 in (women) or &gt; 40 in (men)</b>, intertrigo in skinfolds, joint pain, sleep apnea.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Weight and height → BMI</b> (normal <b>18.5–24.9</b>); MNA screening; skinfolds, mid-arm and calf circumference; labs <b>low albumin, prealbumin, transferrin, cholesterol, Hgb and Hct</b> in undernutrition; waist circumference and waist-to-hip ratio for obesity; <b>phosphorus, potassium and magnesium</b> when refeeding.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Undernutrition: <b>high-calorie, high-protein</b> small meals, supplements (Ensure, Boost), multivitamins, zinc and iron, then <b>tube feeding (TEN)</b> or <b>parenteral nutrition (PPN, TPN)</b>. Obesity: a balanced <b>calorie deficit</b> (500 kcal/day ≈ 1 lb a week), <b>walking 20 minutes a day</b>, behavior change, <b>drugs</b> (orlistat, semaglutide, liraglutide, phentermine-topiramate, bupropion-naltrexone), <b>bariatric surgery</b>.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Undernutrition: infections, pressure injuries, falls, longer stays and readmission; on feeding: <b>aspiration pneumonia</b>, clogged or dislodged tubes, diarrhea, fluid overload, <b>refeeding syndrome</b>, catheter infection, fat overload. Obesity: CAD, stroke, type 2 diabetes, cancers, early death; after surgery: <b>airway problems, anastomotic leak</b>, PE, dumping syndrome.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A dislodged feeding tube (<b>remove it</b>); fever, crackles or diminished lower-lobe sounds on tube feeding (<b>aspiration</b>); <b>low phosphorus and potassium with edema, heart failure or seizures</b> when feeding restarts (<b>refeeding syndrome</b>); fever and high triglycerides on lipids (<b>fat overload</b>); after gastric bypass, <b>rising back, shoulder or abdominal pain, restlessness, unexplained tachycardia or low urine output</b> (<b>leak</b>).</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Not enough calories or protein<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>body burns <b>fat, then muscle and organ protein</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>albumin falls → <b>edema</b>; immune cells starve → <b>infection</b>; muscle wasted → <b>weakness, falls</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>wounds don't heal, lungs and heart weaken<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>feeding restarts too fast → insulin drives <b>phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells</b><span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span><b>refeeding syndrome</b>: heart failure, seizures, respiratory failure</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Marasmus vs kwashiorkor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Marasmus</b>: <b>calorie</b> malnutrition — <b>fat and muscle are wasted</b> but <b>serum proteins are often preserved</b>; the person looks emaciated. <b>Kwashiorkor</b>: <b>protein</b> lacking (in amount and quality) while <b>calories are adequate</b> — <b>body weight looks more normal</b> but <b>serum proteins are low</b>, so <b>edema</b> can hide the wasting. Both are forms of <b>protein-energy undernutrition (PEU)</b>; <b>starvation</b> (no nutrients at all) is its acute, severe form.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Food allergy vs food intolerance</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Allergy</b>: an <b>immune</b> reaction — the most common are <b>nuts, peanuts and shellfish</b> — that can cause <b>swelling of the face, lips, tongue and throat, shortness of breath and anaphylaxis</b>. <b>Intolerance</b>: a <b>GI</b> problem breaking food down — e.g., <b>lactose intolerance</b> (too little lactase) → <b>bloating, diarrhea, cramping and gas</b>, but no danger to life.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Enteral vs parenteral</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Enteral (TEN)</b>: formula into the <b>gut</b> by tube — keeps the gut working, safer and cheaper; risks are <b>aspiration, tube problems and diarrhea</b>. <b>Parenteral (PPN, TPN)</b>: nutrients straight into a <b>vein</b> when the gut can't be used — PPN through a large peripheral vein short term; <b>TPN</b> through a <b>central line</b>, hypertonic and high in glucose; risks are <b>infection, glucose and electrolyte swings, fluid overload</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Orlistat</b> blocks <b>lipase</b> so about a third of dietary fat passes unabsorbed — which is why a fatty meal causes <b>oily, loose stools</b> and why a <b>daily multivitamin</b> (fat-soluble vitamins) is needed. <b>Semaglutide and liraglutide</b> (GLP-1 agonists) slow stomach emptying and <b>reduce appetite</b>; they carry a risk of <b>pancreatitis</b>. <b>Iron</b> for anemia causes <b>constipation</b> — take it <b>just before or with meals</b>. <a href=\"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Weight</b> (same scale, same time — before breakfast — same clothing), BMI, intake and output and <b>percentage of meals eaten</b>, calorie counts, swallowing and choking, skin and skinfolds, <b>albumin, prealbumin, transferrin, Hgb, Hct</b>; on feeding: tube placement mark, residual volume every 6 hr, HOB ≥ 30°, bowel pattern, lung sounds, <b>glucose, BUN, electrolytes — especially phosphorus, potassium, magnesium</b>, IV site and line; after bariatric surgery: airway and <b>SpO₂</b>, pain pattern, heart rate, urine output, skinfolds.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A <b>dislodged or misplaced feeding tube</b> (stop and remove it); <b>fever, crackles, diminished breath sounds or dyspnea</b> on tube feeding (aspiration); <b>low phosphorus and potassium with heart failure, edema, seizures or weak breathing</b> (refeeding syndrome); <b>fever, rising triglycerides or bleeding on lipid emulsion</b> (fat overload — stop the lipid); a G-tube that <b>can't be rotated</b> (buried bumper); after bariatric surgery, <b>increasing back, shoulder or abdominal pain, restlessness, unexplained tachycardia or oliguria</b> (anastomotic leak — call the surgeon now).</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why starvation eats muscle</b> — the body protects its energy supply first. When calories run short it uses stored glycogen, then fat — and, because the brain needs glucose, it <b>breaks down body protein (muscle, then organ protein)</b> to make new glucose in the liver. That is why undernutrition shows up as <b>muscle wasting, weak breathing muscles (lower vital capacity), a smaller heart output, poor wound healing and infections</b>: the proteins that build those tissues and antibodies were spent as fuel.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why albumin falls and edema appears</b> — <b>albumin</b> is a protein the liver makes; it holds water in the blood vessels by <b>colloid osmotic pressure</b>. When protein intake is too low (kwashiorkor) albumin falls and fluid leaks into the tissues → <b>edema</b>, which can make a starving person look well fed. <b>Prealbumin</b> has a short half-life (about 2 days), so it shows recent change faster than albumin (about 3 weeks).</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Refeeding syndrome</b> — in starvation, cells are low on <b>phosphate, potassium and magnesium</b> even when blood levels look acceptable. When food (especially carbohydrate) returns, <b>insulin surges</b> and drives glucose — and those minerals — <b>into the cells</b> to rebuild energy stores (ATP needs phosphate). Blood levels <b>crash</b>: low phosphate weakens the heart and breathing muscles and breaks down red cells and muscle (<b>hemolysis, rhabdomyolysis</b>); low potassium and magnesium cause <b>dysrhythmias</b>; sodium and water retention causes <b>edema and heart failure</b>; <b>seizures</b> can follow.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why fat around the waist matters most</b> — fat is an active organ: it releases hormones (<b>adipokines</b>) that drive appetite and inflammation. <b>Abdominal (central) fat</b> drains straight to the liver and is the most inflammatory, which is why <b>waist circumference predicts coronary disease better than BMI</b> — over <b>35 inches (89 cm) in women or 40 inches (102 cm) in men</b> marks central obesity, and a waist-to-hip ratio of <b>0.95 or more in men (0.8 in women)</b> marks the \"apple\" (android) pattern.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Weight is the nurse's most powerful nutrition tool only if it is measured the same way every time. A liter of fluid weighs a kilogram, so a sudden change overnight is <b>fluid</b>, not fat or muscle — that is why the chapter insists on the <b>same scale, same time (before breakfast) and same clothing</b>, and why a <b>5% loss in a month</b> (or 10% in 6 months) is treated as a real warning rather than noise.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"52-healthy-eating-diets-and-food-sensitivit","ch":"52","chTitle":"Malnutrition: undernutrition and obesity","title":"Healthy eating, diets and food sensitivities","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Energy and guidelines</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Energy balance</b> — weight is gained when <b>intake exceeds energy used</b> and lost when use exceeds intake. The body meets its <b>calorie needs first, even at the expense of protein</b> — so when calories are short, body protein is burned for energy. Needs depend on preferences, location, culture, faith, money and food availability, plus age, size, sex, metabolism, activity, drugs, substances and fluids.</div>\n<div><b>How many calories</b> — the <b>estimated energy requirement (EER)</b> for healthy adults is about <b>1600–3200 calories a day</b>, set by age, sex, height, weight and activity. Needs <b>fall</b> for weight loss and <b>rise</b> for weight gain or <b>healing</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Guidelines</b> — <b>Dietary reference intakes (DRIs)</b> cover <b>40+ nutrients</b> by age, gender and life stage (US and Canada). The <b>Dietary Guidelines for Americans</b> (revised every <b>5 years</b>; 2020–2025, Box 52.1): a healthy pattern at every life stage; nutrient-dense choices that fit preferences, culture and budget; meet food-group needs within calorie limits; <b>limit added sugars, saturated fat, sodium and alcohol</b>. <b>MyPlate</b> (\"Start Simple with MyPlate\") shows the proportions of fruits, vegetables, grains, protein and dairy; Canada uses <b>Canada's Food Guide</b>.</div>\n<div><b>The ideal diet</b> — there is no single \"typical\" diet. Ideally: <b>complex carbohydrates, lean protein, and monounsaturated or polyunsaturated fats</b>, with needed vitamins and minerals. Undernutrition and obesity are both <b>malnutrition</b> — points on one continuum.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults</b> — to prevent <b>constipation</b>, plenty of <b>water and fiber</b>; other good drinks are unsweetened fruit or vegetable juice, low-fat or fat-free milk, or fortified soy drinks.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Vegetarian diets and food preferences</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Vegetarian types (Box 52.2)</b> — <b>lacto-vegetarian</b> — dairy, no meat, poultry, seafood or eggs; <b>ovo-vegetarian</b> — eggs, no meat, poultry, seafood or dairy; <b>lacto-ovo</b> — eggs and dairy, no meat, poultry or seafood; <b>pescatarian</b> — fish, no meat, poultry, dairy or eggs; <b>vegan</b> — plants only (some also avoid honey).</div>\n<div><b>Vegans and vitamin B12</b> — a vegan diet can cause <b>anemia from vitamin B12 deficiency</b> — teach a <b>daily B12 source</b>: fortified cereal, fortified soy drink or meat substitute.</div>\n<div><b>Culture</b> — build cultural food preferences into teaching — <b>never assume; always ask</b>. Undernutrition in the hospital can come from <b>meals unlike what the patient usually eats</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Allergy vs intolerance</b> — <b>food allergy</b> is immune-based and can be life-threatening — most commonly <b>nuts, peanuts and shellfish</b> (e.g., shellfish → cough, swelling of face, lips, tongue and throat, shortness of breath, <b>anaphylaxis</b>). <b>Food intolerance</b> is GI-based: <b>lactose intolerance</b> (too little <b>lactase</b> to turn lactose into absorbable glucose) → <b>bloating, diarrhea, cramping, flatulence</b> after milk products. <b>Ask exactly what reaction</b> the patient has; document and plan.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","ch":"52","chTitle":"Malnutrition: undernutrition and obesity","title":"Nutrition screening and body measurements","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Screening</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What a nutrition assessment includes</b> — nutrition history; food and fluid intake record; <b>access to food</b>; labs; <b>food-drug interactions</b>; health history and physical exam; anthropometric measurements; psychosocial assessment.</div>\n<div><b>Within 24 hours</b> — The Joint Commission requires a <b>nutrition screening within 24 hours of admission</b>, with a <b>full assessment</b> for anyone at risk. The screen: <b>inspection, measured height and weight, weight history, usual eating habits, ability to chew and swallow, and recent changes in appetite or intake</b>.</div>\n<div><b>The MNA</b> — the <b>Mini Nutritional Assessment</b> finds older adults who are malnourished or at risk. Its screening part scores <b>12–14 normal, 8–11 at risk, 0–7 malnourished</b>; the full MNA (out of 30) adds diet, drugs, skin, mid-arm and calf circumference — <b>24–30 normal, 17–23.5 at risk, under 17 malnourished</b>. A malnourished score means <b>contacting the provider for a nutrition consult</b>. A short form (MNA-SF) and an adult screening pathway also exist.</div>\n<div><b>Screening questions (Box 52.3)</b> — <b>nutrient loss</b> (malabsorption, wounds, prolonged diarrhea); <b>higher needs</b> (fever, burns, injury); <b>NPO 3 days or more</b>; modified or restricted diet; enteral or parenteral feeding; allergies, lactose intolerance, limited preferences; <b>recent unexplained weight loss</b>; all drugs and herbs. Also: mouth and esophagus sores, <b>chewing, swallowing and teeth</b>, GI obstruction, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea or constipation, ostomy; ascites, edema, heart failure, ADLs; intake vs output, <b>dialysis</b>; oxygen or ventilator, COPD or asthma; skin, nail and hair changes, rashes, dry or pale mucosa, low turgor, <b>pressure injuries</b>; <b>cachexia</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Measuring the body</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Height and weight</b> — can be <b>delegated to AP</b> — ask them to report the numbers right away. Patients <b>overestimate height and underestimate weight</b>, so <b>measure</b>. Use the <b>same scale and the same clothing (no shoes)</b> each time. A <b>knee-height caliper</b> (patella to heel) estimates height for those who can't stand, especially with knee or hip <b>contractures</b>. Use wheelchair or <b>bed scales</b> for nonambulatory patients — <b>document the sheets, pillows and blankets</b> and lift lines and equipment off the bed.</div>\n<div><b>Action Alert</b> — weigh at the <b>same time each day, preferably before breakfast</b>. Heart failure and kidney disease cause gain; dehydration and cancer cause loss. <b>Weight is the most reliable indicator of fluid gain or loss.</b></div>\n<div><b>Weight loss that matters</b> — an <b>unintentional loss of 5% in a month or 10% in 6 months</b> significantly affects nutrition and must be evaluated.</div>\n<div><b>BMI (Table 52.1)</b> — <b>weight (kg) ÷ height (m)²</b> — an indirect estimate of fat stores, so an <b>accurate height matters as much as weight</b>. <b>Below 18.5 underweight; 18.5–24.9 normal; 25–29.9 overweight; 30 and above obese.</b> Lowest malnutrition risk at <b>18.5–24.9</b>. Example: 123.4 kg ÷ (1.73 m)² ≈ <b>41</b>.</div>\n<div><b>BMI limits</b> — it <b>can't tell fat from lean mass</b> (athletes), <b>predicts fat poorly in older adults</b>, and ignores <b>sex and race</b> differences (women usually carry more fat than men; people of <b>Asian</b> descent usually carry more fat at the same BMI).</div>\n<div><b>Older adults' weight</b> — weight and BMI usually rise until about <b>age 60</b>; some older adults eat less, others keep eating as before and risk obesity (especially <b>older women</b>) — <b>don't assume</b>; assess each person.</div>\n<div><b>Body surface area</b> — <b>BSA</b> estimates total skin surface and is used for <b>drug dosing (especially anticancer drugs)</b> and <b>burn severity</b>. <b>Mosteller formula: √(height in cm × weight in kg ÷ 3600)</b>. Average <b>1.9 m² (men), 1.6 m² (women)</b>. Weight changes far more than height, so weigh accurately.</div>\n<div><b>Skinfolds and circumferences</b> — <b>triceps and subscapular skinfolds</b> (special caliper) estimate fat, recorded as percentiles. <b>Mid-arm and calf circumference</b> (needed for the MNA): flexible tape at the <b>midpoint</b>, wrapped <b>gently</b> without compressing, recorded in <b>centimeters</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A bed scale weighs everything on it. If one morning the patient is weighed with two extra blankets and a pump on the bed, an apparent \"gain\" of a kilogram may be nothing but linens — and a nurse may wrongly call it fluid overload. Recording <b>what is on the bed</b> and lifting off <b>lines and equipment</b> keeps the trend honest.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","ch":"52","chTitle":"Malnutrition: undernutrition and obesity","title":"Undernutrition — the nutrition exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The problem</b> — a <b>multinutrient</b> problem — too few calories and protein, so healthy nutrients fall short. It also starts <b>in the hospital or long-term care</b>: too little staff time to feed slow eaters (especially older adults), and <b>NPO periods</b> for tests, surgery, trauma and complications.</div>\n<div><b>Complications (Box 52.4)</b> — <b>reduced cardiac output</b>; <b>cold intolerance</b>; anorexia, diarrhea, malabsorption, vomiting, <b>impaired protein synthesis</b>, weight loss; <b>infections</b>; <b>dry, flaky skin, dermatitis, poor wound healing</b>; <b>cachexia</b>, less muscle mass and activity tolerance, impaired function; <b>weakness</b>; substance misuse; <b>reduced vital capacity</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Protein-energy undernutrition (PEU)</b> — formerly protein-calorie malnutrition. <b>Marasmus</b> — <b>calorie</b> malnutrition; <b>fat and protein wasted</b>, serum proteins <b>often preserved</b>. <b>Kwashiorkor</b> — <b>protein</b> lacking with <b>adequate calories</b>; weight <b>more normal</b>, <b>serum proteins low</b>. (A mixed <b>marasmic-kwashiorkor</b> form exists.) <b>Starvation</b> — a complete lack of nutrients (famine, exposure) — is the acute, severe form. Untreated PEU → disability, illness and death.</div>\n<div><b>Acute and chronic PEU</b> — <b>acute</b>: a previously well-nourished patient starves in the hospital while <b>catabolic</b> from infection, stress or injury. <b>Chronic</b>: long illness — <b>cancer, end-stage kidney or liver disease, chronic neurologic disease</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Eating disorders</b> — <b>anorexia nervosa</b> — self-induced starvation from <b>fear of fatness</b> while underweight, often with <b>body dysmorphic disorder</b> (obsessive pursuit of a \"perfect\" — here, thin — body). <b>Bulimia nervosa</b> — <b>binge eating then purging</b> (vomiting, laxatives, diuretics); untreated it can kill through starvation, infection or <b>suicide</b>. <b>Binge eating disorder</b> — binges with <b>loss of control</b> but <b>no purging</b>. Collaborate with the provider about a <b>psychiatric consult</b>.</div>\n<div><b>How common</b> — about <b>462 million</b> people worldwide are underweight. An estimated <b>50–71% of older adults are malnourished before admission</b>, and <b>a third of well-nourished older adults become malnourished in the hospital</b> — lengthening stays and raising readmissions. One hospital system's malnutrition readmission protocol (alerts, RDN referral, 30 days of home supplements, follow-up calls and visits) cut malnutrition readmissions from <b>42% to 13.3%</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Signs of deficiency (Table 52.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Hair and eyes</b> — <b>alopecia</b> — zinc; hair <b>easy to pull out, dull or losing pigment</b> — protein; <b>\"corkscrew\" hair</b> — vitamin C. <b>Dry conjunctiva, keratomalacia, Bitot spots</b> — vitamin A; corneal vascularization — riboflavin.</div>\n<div><b>Mouth and gut</b> — <b>swollen, bleeding gums</b> — vitamin C; <b>glossitis</b> — B vitamins (pyridoxine, niacin, folic acid, B12) and zinc; <b>cheilosis</b> and stomatitis — pyridoxine, riboflavin, iron; magenta tongue — vitamin A, riboflavin; fissured tongue — niacin; diarrhea — zinc, niacin; hepatomegaly — protein.</div>\n<div><b>Skin, muscle and bone</b> — <b>petechiae or bruises</b> — vitamin C; dry, scaling skin and follicular hyperkeratosis — vitamin A; nasolabial seborrhea and <b>bilateral dermatitis</b> — niacin; <b>subcutaneous fat loss</b> — calories; <b>muscle wasting</b> — calories and protein; <b>edema</b> — protein; <b>osteomalacia, bone pain</b> — vitamin D.</div>\n<div><b>Blood, nerves and heart</b> — <b>anemia</b> — B12, iron, folic acid, copper, vitamin E; <b>prolonged clotting</b> — <b>vitamin K</b>, manganese; <b>confabulation</b> — thiamine; disorientation — niacin, thiamine; <b>neuropathy and paresthesias</b> — thiamine, pyridoxine, B12; <b>heart failure, cardiomegaly, tachycardia</b> — <b>thiamine</b>; cardiomyopathy — selenium; <b>dysrhythmias</b> — magnesium.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Assessment</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Older adults (Box 52.5)</b> — physical: chronic illness, <b>constipation</b>, decreased appetite, <b>poor teeth or ill-fitting dentures</b>, drugs that change taste or appetite, <b>dry mouth</b>, \"<b>failure to thrive</b>\" (3 of 5: weakness, slow walking, low activity, unintentional weight loss, exhaustion), poor eyesight, pain, weight loss. Psychosocial: <b>can't prepare meals</b> (function, fatigue, memory), less enjoyment of meals, <b>depression</b>, <b>income</b>, <b>loneliness</b>, distance to and <b>transportation</b> to grocery stores. Refer to <b>mental health</b> for depression or loneliness and to the <b>case manager or social worker</b> for money and access.</div>\n<div><b>History</b> — with the dietitian: usual intake and timing, <b>preferences (cultural)</b>, eating patterns, appetite and weight changes, <b>money for food</b>; a <b>24-hour recall</b> and food frequency. Ask whether patients can <b>find, get and afford</b> nutritious food and have safe places to be active; watch for <b>food deserts</b> (urban areas where fresh food is scarce or costly); refer to community resources.</div>\n<div><b>Action Alert: swallowing</b> — assess for <b>difficulty or pain with chewing or swallowing</b> — unrecognized <b>dysphagia</b> in older adults causes undernutrition, dehydration and <b>aspiration pneumonia</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Physical and psychosocial</b> — inspect <b>hair, eyes, mouth, nails, muscles, nerves and skin</b> (red or open areas); measurements; <b>record all intake</b> — a <b>3-day calorie count</b> (delegated to AP, who report back and report <b>choking</b>); document mouth pain, chewing trouble, nausea, vomiting or heartburn. Psychosocial: money, job, education, living and cooking arrangements, mood — refer for community services if food money is short.</div>\n<div><b>Labs</b> — read them <b>with the whole patient</b> — one value alone misleads. Undernutrition may <b>lower cholesterol, hemoglobin, hematocrit, albumin, prealbumin and transferrin</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Priority problem</b> — <b>weight loss</b> from inability to <b>access, ingest or digest food or absorb nutrients</b>. Outcome: nutrients available to meet metabolic needs.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"52-feeding-the-undernourished-patient-meals","ch":"52","chTitle":"Malnutrition: undernutrition and obesity","title":"Feeding the undernourished patient — meals, supplements and drugs","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">By mouth first</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Why oral</b> — eating by mouth through the GI tract is <b>preferred</b> — it <b>supports the immune system</b> and is <b>safer, easier, cheaper and more enjoyable</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Meals</b> — as the provider and dietitian recommend: <b>high-calorie, nutrient-rich foods</b> (milkshakes, cheese, Boost or Ensure); <b>six small meals</b> are often tolerated better than three large ones; <b>pureed or dental soft</b> food for chewing problems or no teeth.</div>\n<div><b>Promoting intake (Box 52.6)</b> — environment: <b>remove bedpans, urinals and emesis basins</b>, cut <b>odors</b> and distractions, give <b>pain medicine or antiemetics at least 1 hour before meals</b>. Comfort: <b>toilet</b> and <b>mouth care before meals</b>; <b>glasses and hearing aids</b> in; ask AP to have the patient <b>sit in a chair</b>. Function: meals <b>appealing and at the right temperature</b>; <b>open cartons and cut food</b>; <b>document the percentage eaten</b>; encourage self-feeding or feed slowly (can delegate); <b>no nonurgent interruptions</b> at mealtime.</div>\n<div><b>Supplements</b> — fortified <b>medical nutrition supplements</b> (Ensure, Sustacal, Carnation Instant Breakfast — also lactose-free) when food isn't enough, especially for older adults; <b>disease-specific</b> products (e.g., <b>Glucerna for diabetes</b>; products for liver and kidney disease). Supplements come as liquids, powders, soups, coffee and puddings — e.g., <b>Duocal</b> (carbohydrate and fat), <b>Beneprotein</b> (protein).</div>\n<div><b>Drugs</b> — <b>multivitamins, zinc and iron</b> to treat or prevent anemia; follow <b>Hgb and Hct</b>. <b>Iron causes constipation</b>; <b>zinc causes nausea and vomiting</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Going home</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Settings and resources</b> — once stable: hospital, transitional care, long-term care or <b>home</b>. The <b>case manager</b> checks whether the patient can <b>afford supplements</b>; outpatient nutrition counseling follows <b>weight gain</b>. Patients on home enteral or parenteral feeding need a <b>home nutrition team</b> (provider, nurse, dietitian, <b>pharmacist</b>, case manager or social worker); commercial companies supply formulas, equipment and teaching.</div>\n<div><b>Teaching</b> — the dietitian teaches a <b>high-calorie, high-protein</b> diet and supplements. The nurse reinforces the diet, <b>reviews drugs</b>, teaches to take <b>iron immediately before or during meals</b>, warns that <b>iron causes constipation</b>, and teaches <b>fiber, fluids and exercise</b> to prevent it.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — the patient takes in enough nutrients to <b>maintain weight and total protein</b> and stays <b>hydrated</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Most hospital undernutrition isn't about the food — it is about everything around it. A tray left out of reach, a carton nobody opened, a bedpan on the table, nausea not treated before the meal, or rounds in the middle of lunch each cost a few hundred calories. Box 52.6 is a list of <b>fixable barriers</b>, and <b>documenting the percentage eaten</b> is how the team finds out a patient is quietly starving.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","ch":"52","chTitle":"Malnutrition: undernutrition and obesity","title":"Tube feeding (total enteral nutrition)","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Who, which tube and how</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Who gets TEN</b> — three groups: (1) people who <b>can eat but not enough</b> (older adults, cancer treatment); (2) <b>permanent neuromuscular impairment</b> — can't swallow (stroke, severe head injury, advanced MS) — long-term tube; (3) <b>temporarily unable to eat</b> because of their condition — stopped when oral intake resumes. Formula needs a <b>provider prescription</b>; the <b>dietitian</b> usually recommends the product and amount.</div>\n<div><b>Ethics</b> — some patients don't want artificial nutrition and hydration — <b>check for an advance directive</b>. If none but there is a <b>durable power of attorney</b>, that person decides when the patient can't. With <b>neither</b>, an <b>interprofessional ethics committee</b> helps reach consensus: review tube-feeding risks and benefits and the medical facts, look for evidence of the patient's wishes, hear all stakeholders, and <b>delay action until consensus</b>. (A comatose patient with a DPOA but no advance directive → <b>contact the DPOA</b>.)</div>\n<div><b>Short-term tubes</b> — <b>nasoenteric tubes</b> (e.g., a <b>Dobhoff</b>) for <b>less than 4 weeks</b>: the <b>NG</b> tube, the smaller <b>nasoduodenal (NDT)</b> and, less often, the <b>nasojejunal (NJT)</b>. For NG or ND feeding use a <b>soft, flexible, small-bore tube (smaller than 12 Fr)</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Long-term tubes</b> — <b>enterostomal</b> tubes placed surgically, endoscopically or laparoscopically: a <b>gastrostomy</b> (stoma into the stomach) with a <b>PEG</b> or dual-access <b>PEG/J</b>; a <b>jejunostomy</b> (e.g., <b>direct percutaneous endoscopic jejunostomy, DPEJ</b>) to <b>bypass the stomach</b> — gastric disease, upper GI obstruction, abnormal gastric or duodenal emptying.</div>\n<div><b>Feeding methods</b> — <b>bolus</b> — a set amount at intervals, typically <b>every 4 hours</b> (by hand or pump); <b>continuous</b> — small amounts nonstop by gravity or pump; <b>cyclic</b> — continuous but <b>stopped for 6 hours or more</b> each day (\"down time,\" usually in the <b>morning</b> for bathing and treatments). Follow the prescription for type, rate, method and <b>free water</b>. The patient may also eat by mouth if able to swallow.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Daily care (Box 52.7)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Placement</b> — <b>confirm the first placement by x-ray</b> — even if another method such as an electromagnetic placement device was used; if placement is <b>ever in doubt, x-ray again</b>. <b>Mark the tube at the exit point</b> after confirmation to recheck visually. Don't rely on <b>auscultation, pH testing, biochemical markers or capnometry</b>. <b>Suspect dislodgment → remove the tube at once.</b></div>\n<div><b>Securing and the site</b> — skin protectant, then tape or a commercial device; change tape regularly. <b>Gastrostomy or jejunostomy</b>: check for infection or excoriation; <b>rotate the tube 360° every day</b> and check for <b>about ¼ inch (0.6 cm) of in-and-out play</b>. Tubes with <b>ports</b> avoid opening the system for drugs.</div>\n<div><b>Action Alert: tube won't move</b> — if a gastrostomy or jejunostomy tube <b>can't be moved</b>, <b>notify the provider immediately</b> — the retention disk may be <b>embedded in the tissue</b>. Cover the site with a <b>dry, sterile dressing</b>, changed <b>at least daily</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Residuals and the pump</b> — <b>check and document residual volume every 6 hours</b> (or per policy) — and for a larger-bore NG tube, <b>before each intermittent feeding or drug</b>; if feeding is returned, ask the provider (usually <b>slow or stop</b> the feeding for a while) or follow ASPEN guidance. Check the <b>pump</b> and that the <b>rate (mL/hr)</b> is right.</div>\n<div><b>Preventing contamination</b> — change the <b>bag and tubing every 24–48 hours</b> (label date, time, initials); irrigation set <b>≤ 24 hours</b>; for continuous or cyclic feeding hang <b>only 4 hours of formula at a time</b> (closed systems preferred, <b>≤ 24 hours</b>). <b>Clean gloves</b> to open the system or add formula (<b>sterile gloves</b> for critically ill or immunocompromised patients); wipe the can lid; <b>label open cans, cover, refrigerate, discard after 24 hours</b>. <b>Never add food dye.</b></div>\n<div><b>Preventing aspiration</b> — <b>HOB at least 30°</b> during a bolus and for <b>at least 1 hour after</b>; <b>semi-Fowler continuously</b> for continuous or cyclic feeding.</div>\n<div><b>Monitoring</b> — <b>BUN, electrolytes, hematocrit, prealbumin and glucose</b>; <b>diarrhea</b>; <b>weight and I&amp;O</b> as ordered.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Keeping the tube open (Box 52.8)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Prevent clogs</b> — clogs are the <b>most common problem</b> and are easier to prevent than fix. Risks: several drugs <b>without flushing between</b>, no flush before and after drugs, <b>long or narrow tubes</b>. Ask the <b>pharmacist</b> about drug–formula compatibility; use <b>liquid drugs</b> when possible (unless they cause diarrhea); <b>never mix drugs with formula</b>; crush tablets finely and dissolve in <b>warm water</b> — but <b>never crush slow-release (SA/SR) drugs</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Flushing</b> — <b>30 mL of water</b> with <b>at least a 30-mL syringe</b> (smaller syringes create pressure that can <b>rupture</b> the tube): <b>every 4 hours</b>, <b>before and after drugs</b>, and <b>after any interruption</b> of feeding.</div>\n<div><b>Unclogging</b> — <b>warm water</b> is best: a <b>30- or 60-mL piston syringe</b> — pull back first, then flush with gentle pressure, rock the plunger, then <b>clamp for about 20 minutes</b> to soak. <b>No cola or cranberry juice</b> (acid makes formula proteins clump). Next, an <b>activated pancreatic enzyme</b> solution (ordered; experienced nurse), then a commercial declogging kit; if that fails, <b>replace the tube</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Complications</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Aspiration</b> — a misplaced or dislodged tube can cause <b>aspiration and death</b>; <b>aspiration pneumonia</b> is life-threatening, especially in older adults. Watch for <b>fever, dehydration</b> (dry mucosa, low urine output), <b>diminished breath sounds in the lower lobes</b> (listen <b>every 4–8 hours</b>), <b>shortness of breath, chest discomfort</b>; a chest x-ray confirms and antibiotics start.</div>\n<div><b>Distention, nausea and vomiting</b> — usually from <b>overfeeding</b> — check <b>residuals every 6 hours</b>; if high, <b>slow or stop</b> and restart at a lower rate after a rest.</div>\n<div><b>Fluid and electrolytes</b> — older adults and those with <b>heart or kidney</b> problems are most at risk. <b>Overload</b> → peripheral edema, <b>sudden weight gain, crackles, dyspnea, high BP, bounding pulse</b> (report). The <b>most common imbalances are hyperkalemia and hyponatremia</b>, linked to <b>hyperglycemia-driven hyperosmolarity</b> and osmotic diuresis; kidney patients with high potassium may get a <b>low-potassium formula</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diarrhea</b> — <b>hyperosmolar formula given fast</b> → diarrhea and dehydration (try a more <b>iso-osmolar</b> formula); <b>very foul diarrhea</b> → check for <b><i>C. difficile</b></i> or other infection (from handling of the system); <b>high-osmolarity liquid drugs</b> (elixirs and suspensions such as <b>acetaminophen, furosemide, phenytoin</b>) → ask about changing or diluting them.</div>\n<div><b>Critical Rescue: refeeding syndrome</b> — a life-threatening fluid and electrolyte shift when a <b>starved</b> patient is fed aggressively — prevent it by <b>feeding early, before severe malnutrition</b>. Recognize <b>hypophosphatemia and hypokalemia</b>, <b>heart failure, peripheral edema, rhabdomyolysis, seizures, hemolysis and respiratory insufficiency</b> — <b>contact the provider immediately</b>. (A TPN patient with <b>edema</b> and <b>falling potassium, phosphate and magnesium</b> = refeeding syndrome.)</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every rule for tube feeding guards against the same danger — formula in the lungs. The <b>x-ray</b> proves where the tip is, the <b>mark</b> shows if it has moved, <b>HOB up</b> lets gravity keep formula down, <b>residuals</b> catch a stomach that isn't emptying, and a tube that looks out of place is <b>removed rather than used</b>. Auscultation can't do this job: air pushed into a tube in the lung can sound just like air in the stomach.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"52-parenteral-nutrition-ppn-and-tpn","ch":"52","chTitle":"Malnutrition: undernutrition and obesity","title":"Parenteral nutrition — PPN and TPN","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Parenteral nutrition</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — nutrition into a <b>vein</b> when the GI tract can't be used effectively. Unlike regular IV fluid, it can supply <b>any or all nutrients</b> — carbohydrate, protein, fat, vitamins, minerals, electrolytes and trace elements.</div>\n<div><b>Compounded vs multichamber bags</b> — <b>compounded</b> by the pharmacist — <b>personalized</b>, but more chance of <b>human error</b> and a <b>7–9 day</b> shelf life. <b>Multichamber</b> premixed bags — dextrose, amino acids, electrolytes and lipids in separate chambers mixed just before use — <b>12+ month</b> shelf life but <b>less protein and fewer electrolytes</b>. ASPEN recommends <b>premixed or multichamber</b> solutions.</div>\n<div><b>PPN</b> — <b>peripheral parenteral nutrition</b> through a cannula in a <b>large distal arm vein</b>, <b>short term</b>, for patients who eat but not enough. It is <b>fat-based</b> and lacks full carbohydrate, so it isn't used long term. Needs good veins and tolerance of <b>large volumes</b>; lower osmolarity, so it is given in high volume and/or high fat. <b>Watch the site for irritation.</b></div>\n<div><b>Critical Rescue: fat overload</b> — with <b>lipid (fat) emulsions</b>, watch for <b>fever, rising triglycerides, clotting problems and multisystem organ failure</b> — <b>fat overload syndrome</b>, especially in the critically ill. <b>Stop the lipid infusion and report at once.</b></div>\n<div><b>TPN</b> — for <b>intensive support over a long time</b>: through a <b>central line</b> — a temporary line in the neck or chest, a <b>tunneled catheter or implanted port</b>, or a <b>PICC</b>. It is <b>hypertonic with high glucose</b>, so it runs on an <b>infusion pump</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">TPN safety (Box 52.9)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Checks</b> — follow the <b>Infusion Nurses Society</b> standards; <b>compare each bag with the prescription</b>; have a <b>second nurse check</b> the prescription and solution; give <b>insulin</b> as ordered; check the <b>pump rate</b>.</div>\n<div><b>If the bag isn't there or is behind</b> — if TPN is temporarily unavailable, ask the provider about <b>D10W or D20W</b> until it arrives (stopping high glucose suddenly can cause <b>hypoglycemia</b>). If it is <b>behind schedule, never \"catch up\"</b> by speeding it — that floods the patient with glucose and fluid.</div>\n<div><b>Monitoring</b> — <b>daily weight</b>; <b>electrolytes and glucose daily</b> (or per protocol); strict <b>I&amp;O</b>; watch for and report fluid and electrolyte problems; check the IV site for <b>infection or infiltration</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Line care</b> — <b>change the IV tubing every 24 hours</b>; change the dressing <b>every 48 hours (gauze) or 7 days (transparent)</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Complications</b> — from the <b>solution or the catheter</b> (infection, clots). Heart or kidney disease → <b>fluid overload, heart failure, pulmonary edema</b>. <b>Potassium and sodium</b> imbalances are common, especially with <b>insulin</b> in the bag; <b>hypercalcemia</b> is linked to TPN. A controlled rate and close watching reduce these risks — report major changes at once.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>TPN is mostly concentrated sugar, and the pancreas adjusts to it. Run it too fast to \"catch up\" and glucose and fluid surge; stop it abruptly and the insulin still circulating drives the glucose down — which is why a gap is bridged with <b>D10W or D20W</b> and the rate is never changed to make up for lost time.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","ch":"52","chTitle":"Malnutrition: undernutrition and obesity","title":"Obesity — the nutrition exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Definitions and risks</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — a complex disorder in which hormones called <b>adipokines</b> and other chemicals that control <b>appetite and fat metabolism</b> are dysregulated → more appetite, <b>autonomic overstimulation, vessel inflammation, ventricular hypertrophy</b>. Overweight and obese patients often still <b>lack healthy nutrients</b> while carrying excess fat.</div>\n<div><b>Definitions</b> — <b>overweight</b>: <b>BMI 25–29.9</b> (or 10% above ideal body weight); <b>obesity</b>: <b>BMI 30 or more</b> (or at least 20% above ideal). <b>Class I: 30 to &lt; 35; Class II: 35 to &lt; 40; Class III: 40 or more</b> (\"severe\" or \"extreme\").</div>\n<div><b>Where the fat is</b> — <b>fat distribution</b> predicts risk better than total weight: <b>waist circumference &gt; 35 in (89 cm) in women or &gt; 40 in (102 cm) in men</b> = <b>central obesity</b>, a major risk for <b>CAD, stroke, type 2 diabetes, some cancers (colon, breast), sleep apnea and early death</b>. <b>Waist-to-hip ratio ≥ 0.95 (men) or ≥ 0.8 (women)</b> = <b>android</b> obesity (fat at the waist).</div>\n<div><b>Complications (Box 52.10)</b> — cardiovascular: <b>CAD, hyperlipidemia, hypertension, PAD</b>; endocrine: <b>insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome, type 2 diabetes</b>; GI: <b>gallstones</b>; GU: erectile dysfunction, menstrual irregularity, <b>urinary incontinence</b>; skin: <b>slow wound healing, infections</b>; musculoskeletal: <b>back and joint pain, early osteoarthritis</b>; <b>stroke</b>; <b>depression</b>; respiratory: <b>obesity hypoventilation syndrome, obstructive sleep apnea</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Causes and prevention</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Diet and activity</b> — a common cause is a <b>high-fat, high-cholesterol diet</b> — <b>saturated fat</b> raises LDL; <b>trans fats, saturated fats and cholesterol</b> raise heart risk, while <b>mono- and polyunsaturated</b> fats are healthy. <b>Physical inactivity</b> is another cause — barriers are <b>time, comfort with a sedentary life and limited mobility</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs that cause weight gain</b> — long-term <b>corticosteroids, estrogens and some progestins, NSAIDs, antihypertensives, antidepressants and other psychoactive drugs, antiepileptics</b> and some <b>oral antidiabetic drugs</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Genetics</b> — <b>monogenic</b> (one gene), <b>syndromic</b> (e.g., <b>Prader-Willi</b>), <b>oligogenic</b>, or <b>polygenic</b> — the <b>most common</b>, many genes amplified by an environment that favors weight gain. Lifestyle still matters a great deal: encourage changes <b>within the patient's control</b> even if they blame their genes. (NCLEX: respond by acknowledging that <b>genes can contribute</b>, then explore <b>family history, activity and usual intake</b> — not by jumping to surgery.)</div>\n<div><b>How common</b> — worldwide overweight and obesity <b>doubled since 1980</b>; about <b>a third</b> of the world is overweight or obese — a <b>leading cause of preventable death</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Prevention</b> — <b>Healthy People 2030</b> (Box 52.11): <b>reduce the proportion of adults with obesity</b>; eat more <b>calcium, vegetables (dark green, red, orange), beans, fruit, potassium, vitamin D and whole grains</b>; eat less <b>added sugar, saturated fat and sodium</b>; reduce <b>food insecurity</b>. Teach that even a <b>5% weight loss</b> greatly lowers the risk of CAD and diabetes, and that activity can be as simple as <b>walking 20 minutes a day</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Assessment</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Approach</b> — patients may feel <b>embarrassed or fear judgment</b>. Use <b>RESPECT</b>: build <b>rapport</b> in a safe environment, ensure <b>safety and privacy</b>, encourage <b>realistic goals</b>, show <b>compassion</b>, and use <b>tact</b>.</div>\n<div><b>History</b> — the full screening and nutrition history, plus <b>appetite, attitude toward food, chronic diseases, all drugs</b> (including OTC and herbal), <b>activity and function</b>, <b>family history of obesity</b>, and <b>what weight-loss methods were tried</b> and how they worked.</div>\n<div><b>Physical</b> — <b>accurate height and weight</b>, other measurements. <b>Lift skinfolds</b> — pendulous breasts, the <b>abdominal apron (panniculus)</b> — to look for <b>Candida (intertrigo)</b> and other infections or lesions; infection of the panniculus is <b>panniculitis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Psychosocial</b> — how does the patient see their weight? (Some don't see it as a problem, which shapes the plan.) Ask: <b>what does food mean to you; do you want to lose weight; what stops you; what would motivate you; how would you benefit; who will support you?</b> Depressive symptoms <b>most days for more than 2 weeks</b> that affect ADLs → <b>mental health referral</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Priority problem and setting</b> — <b>weight gain</b> from excess calories, <b>stressing every vital organ</b>. Goal: return toward a <b>normal BMI</b> with nutrient-dense food and no overeating. If hospitalized, the patient needs a proper <b>bariatric room</b> (Table 52.3): single room, <b>at least 18 m²</b>, doorway <b>at least 60 inches</b> wide, <b>5 feet</b> of clearance around the bed, a <b>bariatric bed with a low-air-loss mattress</b>, a floor-mounted toilet and <b>grab bars rated for 1000 lb</b>, a <b>4 × 5-foot shower</b>, a <b>ceiling lift for up to 1000 lb</b>, right-sized gowns, <b>BP cuffs, SCDs</b>, gait belts and stretchers, and at least one airborne isolation room per unit.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A blood pressure cuff that is too small squeezes the arm before it squeezes the artery, so it reads <b>falsely high</b> — the same goes for sequential compression sleeves that don't fit, or a gown that won't close. Right-sized equipment isn't a courtesy in bariatric care; it is what makes <b>assessments accurate and care safe</b> for the patient and the staff.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","ch":"52","chTitle":"Malnutrition: undernutrition and obesity","title":"Treating obesity — diet, exercise, drugs and behavior","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Diet and exercise</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Diet programs</b> — <b>fasting</b> and <b>very-low-calorie diets (200–800 kcal/day)</b> need a <b>cardiac evaluation and team supervision</b> and aren't ideal — risky, and weight usually returns; fasting risks <b>ketosis</b>. <b>Nutritionally balanced diets (about 1200–1800 kcal/day)</b> use ordinary, affordable food with the usual mix of carbohydrate, protein and fat (± supplements). <b>Unbalanced low-energy diets</b> (e.g., <b>low-carbohydrate</b>) restrict one or more nutrients — popular, with mixed evidence.</div>\n<div><b>The diet plan</b> — built with the patient, caregiver, provider, nurse and <b>dietitian</b>; it must be <b>evidence-based, nutritionally balanced, low-risk and practical for the long term</b>, and fit the patient's habits and life.</div>\n<div><b>Calorie math</b> — resting metabolic rate (sex-specific formula × activity factor) = calories to <b>maintain</b> weight. <b>Subtract 500 kcal/day → about 1 lb (0.45 kg) a week; subtract 1000 kcal/day → about 2 lb (0.9 kg) a week.</b> A <b>5–10% weight loss</b> improves <b>glucose control, cholesterol and blood pressure</b> — and the benefit lasts if the loss is kept off.</div>\n<div><b>Exercise</b> — diet <b>plus</b> activity loses <b>more weight than diet alone</b>, and more of the loss is <b>fat</b> (lean mass is preserved); it shrinks waist circumference and waist-to-hip ratio. Start with a <b>minimum that can be kept up</b>: <b>walk 20 minutes a day</b> (all at once or split up) and build as endurance grows.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Drugs for weight loss (Table 52.4)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Orlistat</b> — <b>blocks lipase</b>, so fat is only partly digested and absorbed. Teach a <b>daily multivitamin</b> (fat-soluble vitamins are lost); <b>loose stools, cramps and nausea</b> happen unless fat is kept <b>under 30% of daily intake</b>; <b>liver injury</b> is rare — liver enzymes may be checked.</div>\n<div><b>Semaglutide and liraglutide</b> — injected GLP-1 agonists that <b>reduce appetite and increase fullness</b> (the book adds that semaglutide raises resting metabolism). Teach <b>injection technique</b>; monitor <b>ALT and AST</b>; risk of <b>pancreatitis</b>; semaglutide also rarely <b>thyroid tumors</b> (check thyroid tests). The book says <b>not to combine liraglutide with insulin</b> (<b>hypoglycemia</b>).</div>\n<div><b>Phentermine-topiramate</b> — a short-term appetite suppressant combined with an antiseizure drug. <b>Not for glaucoma or hyperthyroidism</b> (raises eye pressure and thyroid activity). <b>Causes birth defects</b> — check for <b>pregnancy or plans for pregnancy</b>; not while breastfeeding.</div>\n<div><b>Bupropion-naltrexone</b> — an antidepressant plus an opioid antagonist. <b>Contraindicated</b> with <b>uncontrolled hypertension, seizures, anorexia or bulimia</b>, or during <b>drug or alcohol withdrawal</b>; not with other <b>bupropion</b>. Monitor for <b>suicidal thoughts</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Setmelanotide</b> — injected; targets the <b>MC4R</b> hunger pathway (rare genetic obesity). Teach about <b>sexual side effects</b> (priapism, spontaneous erections, arousal changes), report <b>depression or suicidal thoughts</b>, get <b>full skin exams</b> before and during treatment (<b>darker skin and moles</b>).</div>\n<div><b>Other options</b> — <b>cryolipolysis (\"fat freezing\")</b> removes small stubborn fat deposits — <b>not a treatment for obesity</b>; redirect to the provider. An <b>oral superabsorbent hydrogel</b> (a device, not a drug) taken with water before meals swells to fill about <b>25% of the stomach</b> → fullness; about <b>300 fewer calories a day and ~5% weight loss</b> over 6 months; side effects <b>bloating, cramping, flatulence</b>. <b>Acupuncture, acupressure, Ayurveda and hypnosis</b> have mixed evidence — discuss with the provider.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Behavior</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Behavioral management</b> — <b>self-monitoring</b> (a <b>food diary</b> with activity, emotions and situations), <b>stimulus control</b> (manage cues that trigger overeating), <b>reinforcement</b> (self-reward for change), <b>cognitive restructuring</b> (replace negative beliefs with positive coping statements); counseling <b>before, during and after</b> treatment; <b>Overeaters Anonymous</b> (12 steps), especially for compulsive eating.</div>\n<div><b>Self-care</b> — plan activity with the provider; for nonsurgical patients, <b>cut overall fat</b> and <b>don't rely on appetite-suppressing drugs</b>; a <b>food journal</b> that records <b>mood and events</b> shows eating patterns.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A pound of body fat holds roughly 3500 calories, so eating <b>500 fewer calories a day</b> adds up to about a pound a week. That arithmetic is why the book sets a <b>slow, steady goal</b> and why a <b>5–10% loss</b> — not an \"ideal\" weight — is the target: it is achievable, and it already improves blood pressure, cholesterol and glucose.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","ch":"52","chTitle":"Malnutrition: undernutrition and obesity","title":"Bariatric surgery — care before and after","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Who and which surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Candidates</b> — adults who <b>haven't responded to traditional treatment</b> and have a <b>BMI of 40 or more</b>, or a <b>BMI of 35 or more with other health risks</b> (elsewhere the book also lists weight <b>more than 100% above ideal</b>). <i>(2022 ASMBS/IFSO guidelines now recommend surgery at <b>BMI 35 or more</b> regardless of other disease, and consider it from <b>BMI 30</b> with metabolic disease.)</i> <b>Liposuction</b> is cosmetic — fat returns if weight gain continues; it is <b>not</b> an obesity treatment.</div>\n<div><b>The operations</b> — <b>restrictive</b> (normal digestion, no deficiency risk): <b>adjustable gastric band</b> (small upper pouch, laparoscopic; inflatable or not) and <b>sleeve gastrectomy</b> (about <b>three-fourths of the stomach removed</b>). <b>Biliopancreatic diversion with duodenal switch</b> (less common): almost <b>80% of the stomach</b> removed and the pouch joined to the <b>lower small intestine</b> → most calories pass unabsorbed into the colon. <b>Roux-en-Y gastric bypass (RNYGB)</b> — the <b>most common in the US</b>, often robotic: the <b>stomach, duodenum and part of the jejunum are bypassed</b> → <b>fast weight loss</b> but <b>more invasive and more complications</b>. Also SADI-S and an implanted <b>vagal-blocking device</b> (early fullness).</div>\n<div><b>Commitment</b> — every patient must agree to <b>change lifestyle and follow strict protocols</b> for life. Many then lose obesity complications — <b>diabetes, hypertension, depression, sleep apnea</b>. Laparoscopic <b>LAGB</b> and <b>LSG</b> are the common minimally invasive choices, decided by weight, build, prior surgery and other disease.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Before surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Risks and testing</b> — higher risk of <b>pulmonary and thromboembolic complications</b> and death. Some surgeons require <b>weight loss before surgery</b>. A <b>thorough psychological assessment</b> looks for depression, <b>substance abuse</b> and other problems, plus cognition, coping, motivation, expectations and support. Patients who <b>aren't alert and oriented</b> or lack <b>strength and mobility</b> aren't candidates. The nurse <b>reinforces teaching</b> — often in <b>group classes</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">After surgery (Box 52.12)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Airway first</b> — the <b>priority is airway management</b> — a <b>short, thick neck</b> may need <b>aggressive respiratory support</b>, even a ventilator in the ICU. <b>Semi-Fowler</b> position (breathing, less <b>sleep apnea</b>, pneumonia and atelectasis); <b>SpO₂</b>; oxygen, <b>BiPAP or CPAP</b> as ordered.</div>\n<div><b>Safety and skin</b> — a <b>bariatric room</b>; <b>extra staff</b> for every move; <b>side rails must not touch the body</b> (pressure injuries); check <b>skinfolds</b> and where <b>tubes and catheters</b> press; keep skin <b>clean and dry</b>, <b>absorbent padding between folds</b>; <b>turn every 2 hours</b> with an <b>overhead trapeze</b>; <b>PT</b> for transfers and walkers.</div>\n<div><b>Clots, wound, bladder</b> — <b>sequential compression devices</b> and <b>prophylactic anticoagulants</b> (VTE, PE); an <b>abdominal binder</b> after open surgery (dehiscence); <b>remove the urinary catheter within 24 hours</b> (UTI); measure <b>abdominal girth daily</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Pain</b> — usually less with minimally invasive surgery; <b>PCA morphine up to the first 24 hours</b>, then <b>oral opioids — liquid when possible</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Action Alert: NG tube</b> — after open surgery an NG tube may drain the pouch and distal stomach. <b>Monitor patency</b> — but <b>never reposition it</b> (movement can <b>disrupt the suture line</b>). It is removed on <b>day 2 if the patient passes flatus</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Diet progression</b> — <b>clear liquids</b> slowly in <b>1-ounce cups</b> once water is tolerated → <b>full liquids</b> (usually the discharge diet) → <b>pureed</b> in about <b>a week</b>, about <b>5 tablespoons per meal</b> → <b>soft</b> foods after several weeks → <b>solid, nutrient-dense</b> foods around <b>week 8</b>. Teach: <b>eat and drink slowly</b>, <b>small meals</b>, <b>stop before feeling full</b>, choose <b>high-protein</b> foods, <b>avoid fatty and sugary</b> foods; <b>six small feedings</b> and plenty of fluids prevent dehydration.</div>\n<div><b>Dumping syndrome</b> — after gastric bypass, food enters the small intestine <b>too quickly</b> → within about <b>30 minutes</b> of eating: <b>tachycardia, nausea, diarrhea, abdominal cramping</b>, and vasomotor signs (dizziness, sweating, pallor, palpitations, fainting). Treated with <b>diet changes</b>, then <b>antidiarrheals and/or acarbose</b>, rarely surgery. (A client 2 months after RNYGB with nausea, diarrhea and cramping soon after eating → <b>dumping</b>: notify the provider, teach diet, watch the diarrhea resolve and diet adherence.)</div>\n<div><b>Critical Rescue: anastomotic leak</b> — the <b>most common serious complication and cause of death</b> after gastric bypass: <b>increasing back, shoulder or abdominal pain, restlessness, unexplained tachycardia and oliguria</b> → <b>contact the surgeon immediately</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Going home (Box 52.13)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Teaching topics</b> — <b>nutrition</b> (diet progression, <b>vitamin and mineral supplements</b>, hydration); <b>drugs</b> (analgesics, antiemetics); <b>wound care</b> (clean technique; <b>cover during shower</b>); <b>activity</b> (<b>no lifting</b>, progression, driving and work); <b>report</b>: <b>fever</b>, excessive nausea or vomiting, <b>epigastric, back or shoulder pain</b>, a red, hot or draining wound, <b>leg pain, redness or swelling</b>, <b>chest pain, trouble breathing</b>; follow-up visits, support groups, counseling; nutrition and exercise classes.</div>\n<div><b>Supplements for life</b> — bowel changes are common; <b>vitamin D, B-complex vitamins, iron and calcium</b> are prescribed — <b>taking them is part of the surgery's success</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Follow-up and resources</b> — followed by the <b>surgeon and dietitian for several years</b>; <b>shared medical appointments</b> help patients with type 2 diabetes; a <b>panniculectomy</b> (removing the abdominal apron) may follow once weight is stable, usually at <b>18–24 months</b>; <b>Overeaters Anonymous</b>, <b>ASMBS</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — the patient eats appropriate <b>nutrient-dense</b> foods without overeating; after surgery, <b>free of infection</b>. (NCLEX: discharge teaching includes <b>reporting back, shoulder or abdominal pain</b> and <b>about 5 tablespoons per reintroduced meal</b> — not fruit juice, solid food in a week, or low urine output being normal.)</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>After a gastric bypass the stomach pouch holds only an ounce or two and empties straight into the jejunum. A large or sugary meal arrives in the intestine all at once, pulls water in by osmosis and triggers a surge of gut hormones — that is <b>dumping syndrome</b>. The same small pouch is why fluids come in <b>1-ounce cups</b>, meals are <b>about 5 tablespoons</b>, and patients <b>stop before they feel full</b>: stretching the pouch strains the fresh <b>anastomosis</b>.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"52-adpie","ch":"52","chTitle":"Malnutrition: undernutrition and obesity","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Every patient in this chapter is watched for the same turning points — <b>weight moving the wrong way</b>, <b>formula or fluid going where it shouldn't</b> (the lungs, the tissues), and <b>electrolytes shifting when feeding restarts</b> — while the nurse screens early, weighs accurately, feeds by mouth whenever possible, runs tube feedings and TPN by the safety rules, and supports realistic, respectful weight change.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div><b>nutrition screen within 24 hours</b> (Box 52.3): weight history, <b>unintentional loss (5% in a month, 10% in 6 months)</b>, usual intake and <b>24-hour recall</b>, preferences and culture, allergies vs intolerances, <b>chewing and swallowing</b>, NPO days, drugs (including ones that cause weight gain), <b>money, food access, food deserts</b>, living and cooking arrangements, mood and <b>depression</b>, eating-disorder signs; for obesity, <b>attitude toward food, motivation, past attempts</b>, family history (with <b>RESPECT</b>).</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical</div><b>measured height and weight</b> (same scale, time, clothing; knee-height caliper; bed scale with linens noted) → <b>BMI</b>; <b>waist circumference and waist-to-hip ratio</b>; skinfolds, mid-arm and calf circumference; MNA; deficiency signs in <b>hair, eyes, mouth, skin, nerves and heart</b>; skinfolds for <b>intertrigo</b>; on feeding: tube mark and site, <b>residuals</b>, bowel pattern, <b>lung sounds</b>, edema and weight gain.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Labs</div><b>albumin, prealbumin, transferrin, Hgb, Hct, cholesterol</b> (low in undernutrition); <b>glucose, BUN and electrolytes</b> on TEN and TPN; <b>phosphorus, potassium and magnesium</b> when refeeding; <b>triglycerides</b> on lipids; ALT and AST on weight-loss drugs.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>undernutrition: <b>weight loss</b> from inability to access, ingest, digest or absorb food — with risks of <b>aspiration</b> (dysphagia, tube feeding), <b>refeeding syndrome</b>, infection and skin breakdown. Obesity: <b>weight gain</b> that stresses every vital organ; after bariatric surgery, <b>airway compromise, anastomotic leak, VTE, dumping syndrome, skin breakdown</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>undernutrition: takes in enough nutrients to <b>maintain weight and total protein</b>, stays hydrated, <b>no aspiration</b>, stable electrolytes on feeding. Obesity: moves toward a <b>normal BMI</b> (a <b>5–10% loss</b> is a realistic early goal) eating nutrient-dense food without overeating; after surgery, <b>clear airway, no leak, no infection</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Undernutrition and TEN</div><b>oral first</b>: high-calorie, high-protein <b>six small meals</b>, supplements, <b>Box 52.6</b> (bedpans out, <b>meds 1 hour before meals</b>, mouth care, chair, cut food, <b>record % eaten</b>); multivitamin, zinc, iron (<b>with meals; constipating</b>). TEN: <b>x-ray first placement, mark the tube, remove if dislodged</b>; <b>HOB ≥ 30°</b> (1 hr after bolus; always for continuous); <b>residuals every 6 hours</b>; <b>flush 30 mL every 4 hours and before and after drugs</b> with a ≥ 30-mL syringe; <b>4 hours of formula</b> in the bag; rotate a G-tube daily (<b>can't move → notify</b>); lungs every 4–8 hours.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Parenteral and refeeding</div>TPN by <b>pump</b> through a <b>central line</b>; <b>second-nurse check</b>; <b>never catch up</b>; <b>D10W/D20W</b> if the bag is late; <b>daily weight, glucose and electrolytes</b>; tubing every 24 hours; dressings 48 hours (gauze) or 7 days (transparent); stop <b>lipids</b> for signs of <b>fat overload</b>. Refeeding: start slowly in the starved, watch <b>phosphorus, potassium, magnesium</b>, edema and heart failure — <b>call the provider</b> for signs.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Obesity and bariatric surgery</div><b>RESPECT</b>; balanced <b>1200–1800 kcal</b> (−500 kcal/day ≈ 1 lb/week), <b>walk 20 minutes a day</b>, food and mood journal, behavior strategies; drug teaching (<b>orlistat</b>: multivitamin, fat &lt; 30%; <b>GLP-1</b>: injection, pancreatitis signs; <b>phentermine-topiramate</b>: pregnancy test). After surgery: <b>airway and SpO₂</b>, semi-Fowler, CPAP; <b>extra staff</b>, skinfolds, turn every 2 hours; <b>SCDs and anticoagulant</b>; catheter out in 24 hours; <b>never move the NG</b>; <b>1-oz clear liquids</b> → full liquids → pureed; watch <b>leak signs</b> and dumping; lifelong <b>vitamins D and B, iron, calcium</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>weight stable or rising toward goal in undernutrition (falling toward goal in obesity) measured the same way each time; meals and supplements eaten (percentage recorded); albumin and prealbumin improving; no aspiration, clogged tube or diarrhea; electrolytes and glucose stable on feeding with no refeeding signs; after bariatric surgery, clear lungs, no leak signs (pain pattern, heart rate, urine output), tolerating the diet stage, and able to state the <b>diet progression, supplements and what to report</b> (fever, back or shoulder pain, wound changes, leg swelling, chest pain, trouble breathing).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"53-overview","ch":"53","chTitle":"Assessment of the endocrine system","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">53 · Chapter 53</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Fluid &amp; electrolyte balance · glucose regulation</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Assessment of the endocrine system</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> The endocrine glands are <b>ductless</b>: they release hormones into the blood, and only <b>target tissues</b> with the matching receptor respond. Every endocrine problem is one of three things — <b>too much hormone, too little hormone, or receptors that respond poorly</b>. This chapter is the toolkit for chapters 55 (thyroid and parathyroid) and 56 (diabetes): which gland makes what, how feedback switches it on and off, what you look for on the body, and how the tests are collected so the result means something.</p><div id=\"ch53-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Glands secrete hormones into the blood; a hormone acts only where a receptor fits it (lock and key), and <b>negative feedback</b> turns secretion off once the need is met.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">A gland makes too much or too little hormone, or the target tissue stops responding to it (insulin resistance in type 2 diabetes).</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Changes in energy, weight, appetite, heat or cold tolerance, thirst and urination, skin pigment, hair, face and eyes, mood, menses and sexual function.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Hormone assays in blood, urine or saliva; stimulation and suppression tests; 24-hour urine; glucose and A1C; imaging, thyroid scan, fine needle aspiration.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Replace a missing hormone, block or remove an excess (drugs, surgery, radiation), and teach lifelong self-management.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Fluid and electrolyte crises (sodium, potassium, calcium), glucose crises, dehydration, dysrhythmias.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Pressure on a hyperthyroid gland (<b>thyroid storm</b>); an older adult who stops drinking (low ADH reserve); new confusion with abnormal glucose, sodium or potassium.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Body condition drifts from normal<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>gland secretes its hormone<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>target tissue corrects the change<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>hormone level rises<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>secretion slows or stops (negative feedback)</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Provocative vs suppression test</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Suspect <b>too little</b>: give a stimulator — <b>no rise = hypofunction</b>. Suspect <b>too much</b>: give a suppressor — <b>no fall = hyperfunction</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Single blood level vs 24-hour urine</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Many hormones are released in <b>pulses</b>, so one sample can mislead; a 24-hour urine reflects the whole day (adrenal hormones especially).</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Norepinephrine vs epinephrine</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Adrenal medulla: about 15% norepinephrine, mainly <b>alpha</b> (vasoconstriction); 85% epinephrine, mainly <b>beta</b> (heart rate, contractility, bronchodilation).</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Connections to other chapters</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Ch 55 uses the TRH → TSH → T3/T4 axis and PTH with calcium; ch 56 uses insulin and glucagon; the RAAS (ch 13) explains aldosterone.</div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Orthostatic vital signs, weight, intake and output with thirst, blood glucose, sodium, potassium and calcium, heart rate and rhythm.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Never press on or palpate a hyperthyroid gland. Report dysrhythmias, signs of dehydration, and glucose or electrolyte values far outside normal.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>What the glands keep steady</b> — working with every other body system, the endocrine glands regulate <b>temperature, metabolism, growth, nutrition, elimination, fluid and electrolyte balance and reproduction</b>. The control is tight: core temperature stays at or near <b>98.6°F (37°C)</b> whatever the weather, and <b>serum sodium stays between 136 and 145 mEq/L (mmol/L)</b> in a healthy adult with a daily intake anywhere from <b>2 g to 12 g of sodium</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Lock and key</b> — circulating hormones reach every tissue, but act only on <b>target tissues</b> whose receptors fit them. Each receptor type is specific for <b>one hormone</b>, like a lock that only one key opens. Once unlocked, the target cell changes its activity.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Simple negative feedback</b> — blood glucose rises above normal → the pancreas secretes <b>insulin</b> → cells take up glucose → glucose falls → insulin secretion slows. The hormone's action is the <b>opposite</b> of the change that started it — that is what \"negative\" means.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Complex negative feedback (an axis)</b> — low cortisol → the hypothalamus releases <b>CRH</b> → the anterior pituitary releases <b>ACTH</b> → the adrenal cortex releases <b>cortisol</b> → rising cortisol shuts off CRH and ACTH. The thyroid runs the same way: <b>TRH → TSH → T3 and T4</b>, and high thyroid hormone switches TRH and TSH off.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Hypothalamus and pituitary</b> — the hypothalamus makes releasing and inhibiting hormones and sends them straight to the anterior pituitary through a private <b>portal circulation</b>, so very little reaches the rest of the body. The posterior pituitary only <b>stores</b> what the hypothalamus makes — <b>ADH (vasopressin)</b> and <b>oxytocin</b> — and releases them when needed.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Three ways it goes wrong</b> — an <b>excess</b> of a hormone, a <b>deficiency</b> of a hormone, or <b>poor hormone-receptor interaction</b> that makes the target tissue less responsive. Each normal hormone range is well defined, so a value outside it can affect many body systems at once.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Because every hormone sits in a feedback loop, a single level means little on its own: it has to be read against what drives it. A \"normal\" cortisol can be abnormal at midnight, and some hormones have such wide normal ranges that only a <b>stimulation or suppression test</b> shows whether the gland responds. That is why so much of this chapter is about <b>timing and collection</b>, not just which test is ordered.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","ch":"53","chTitle":"Assessment of the endocrine system","title":"The adrenal glands — aldosterone, cortisol, catecholamines","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Adrenal cortex (about 90% of the gland)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Aldosterone — the sodium and potassium hormone</b> — a <b>mineralocorticoid</b> that keeps extracellular fluid volume and electrolytes in balance: the kidney <b>reabsorbs sodium and water</b> and <b>excretes potassium</b>. Secretion is driven by the <b>renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS)</b>, by <b>serum potassium</b>, and by ACTH.</div>\n<div><b>The RAAS, step by step</b> — a drop in extracellular fluid volume (<b>blood loss, sodium loss, standing up</b>) or <b>hypoxemia</b> makes kidney arteriole cells release <b>renin</b> → renin turns angiotensinogen into <b>angiotensin I</b> → an enzyme converts it to <b>angiotensin II</b>, the active form → angiotensin II stimulates <b>aldosterone</b> → the kidney reabsorbs sodium and water until plasma volume and osmolarity are back to normal.</div>\n<div><b>Potassium switches it on fast</b> — aldosterone is secreted when serum potassium rises above normal by as little as <b>0.1 mEq/L</b>, and it makes the kidney excrete potassium until the level is back to normal.</div>\n<div><b>Cortisol — essential for life</b> — the main <b>glucocorticoid</b>. It shapes the <b>stress response</b>, carbohydrate, protein and fat metabolism, emotional stability, immune function and sodium balance, and it must be present for the <b>catecholamines to work</b> and for normal heart muscle excitability.</div>\n<div><b>What glucocorticoids do (Box 53.1)</b> — prevent <b>hypoglycemia</b> (more liver glucose production — gluconeogenesis — and less glucose use by tissues); keep cardiac muscle responsive; increase <b>lipolysis</b> (glycerol and free fatty acids) and <b>protein breakdown</b>; degrade collagen and connective tissue; release more mature <b>neutrophils</b> from the marrow; <b>antiinflammatory</b> (fewer inflammatory cells reach injuries); maintain behavior and cognition.</div>\n<div><b>Cortisol's rhythm</b> — controlled by <b>ACTH</b> (and CRH above it), which respond to the free cortisol level, the <b>sleep-wake cycle</b> and <b>stress</b>. Cortisol <b>peaks in the morning</b> and is lowest about <b>12 hours later</b>; emotional, chemical or physical stress raises it.</div>\n<div><b>Sex hormones</b> — the cortex makes small amounts of androgens and estrogens — usually minor next to the gonads, but in <b>women the adrenal gland is the main source of androgens</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Aldosterone and cortisol explain most adrenal findings before you ever meet an adrenal disease. <b>Aldosterone = sodium in, water in, potassium out</b>, so too much causes fluid retention and low potassium, and too little causes volume loss and high potassium. <b>Cortisol = sugar up, inflammation down</b>, so excess looks like high glucose, infections and poor healing.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Adrenal medulla — the catecholamines</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>A sympathetic ganglion that secretes</b> — sympathetic stimulation releases <b>epinephrine (about 85%)</b> and <b>norepinephrine (about 15%)</b> into the blood. They are <b>not essential for life</b> (other tissues make them) but drive the <b>fight-or-flight</b> stress response; small amounts are secreted all the time.</div>\n<div><b>Receptors decide the effect (Table 53.2)</b> — adrenergic receptors come in two families, each with subtypes — <b>alpha (alpha1, alpha2)</b> and <b>beta (beta1, beta2, beta3)</b> — and the receptor on the target tissue decides the response. Norepinephrine acts mainly on <b>alpha</b>, epinephrine mainly on <b>beta</b> receptors. Eyes (alpha): pupils dilate. Heart (<b>beta1</b>): faster rate, stronger contraction. Vessels: alpha <b>constricts</b>, <b>beta2 dilates</b>. Bronchioles (<b>beta2</b>): relax and dilate. GI tract: sphincters tighten (alpha), motility falls (beta). Fat (beta): lipolysis. Liver (alpha): <b>gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis</b>. Pancreas: alpha lowers, beta raises insulin and glucagon release. Skin (alpha): sweating.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The receptor table is a map of what stress looks like on the body: fast heart, wide pupils, sweating, a gut that slows down, and <b>blood glucose that rises</b> because the liver releases sugar. It is the same reason a stressed or sick person with diabetes runs high, and why the beta-blocker you meet in chapter 55 calms thyroid-driven tachycardia.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","ch":"53","chTitle":"Assessment of the endocrine system","title":"Pituitary, thyroid, parathyroid and pancreas","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hypothalamus and pituitary (Tables 53.1 and 53.3)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Hypothalamus</b> — makes <b>CRH, GnRH, GHRH, TRH</b>, somatostatin (growth hormone–inhibiting), prolactin-inhibiting and melanocyte-inhibiting hormones. They either stimulate or inhibit release from the anterior pituitary.</div>\n<div><b>Anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis)</b> — some of its hormones are <b>tropic</b> — their targets are other endocrine glands — while others, such as <b>prolactin</b>, act directly on their final target tissue. <b>ACTH (corticotropin)</b> → adrenal cortex (corticosteroids and adrenocortical growth). <b>TSH (thyrotropin)</b> → thyroid (thyroid hormone). <b>FSH and LH</b> → ovary (estrogen, follicle maturation, ovulation, progesterone) and testis (sperm, testosterone). <b>Growth hormone</b> → bone and soft tissue (growth through lipolysis, protein building and insulin antagonism). <b>Prolactin</b> → breast development and milk production (no known function in men). <b>MSH</b> → pigmentation. Drugs, diet, lifestyle and disease can all change pituitary hormone release.</div>\n<div><b>Posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis)</b> — <b>vasopressin (ADH)</b> → kidney: <b>water reabsorption</b>. <b>Oxytocin</b> → uterus and breast: contractions and milk let-down. Both are made in the hypothalamus and only stored here. The gland sits in the <b>sella turcica</b> of the sphenoid bone.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Notice how many hormones on this list are <b>insulin antagonists</b>: growth hormone, cortisol, epinephrine and thyroid hormone all push glucose up. Insulin is the only hormone that lowers it — which is why so many different illnesses and drugs cause high blood sugar, and almost none cause low blood sugar.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Gonads — ovaries and testes</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What they are and make</b> — the <b>gonads</b> are the reproductive endocrine glands: the <b>ovaries</b> secrete <b>estrogen and progesterone</b>, the <b>testes</b> secrete <b>testosterone</b> (Table 53.1). They make far more estrogen and androgen than the adrenal cortex — except that in women the adrenal gland is the main androgen source.</div>\n<div><b>Switched on at puberty</b> — gonad function stays <b>dormant until puberty</b>. Then the <b>gonadotropins</b> of the anterior pituitary — <b>FSH and LH</b>, released in response to hypothalamic <b>GnRH</b> — make the glands and the <b>external genitalia mature</b>: the testes begin making <b>testosterone</b> and the ovaries <b>estrogen</b>, and these hormones produce the <b>secondary sex characteristics</b>. FSH matures ovarian follicles and drives sperm production; LH triggers ovulation and progesterone secretion and stimulates testosterone secretion (Table 53.3). Full structure and function: chapter 61.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The gonads sit at the end of the same three-step chain as the thyroid and the adrenal cortex: <b>GnRH → FSH and LH → estrogen, progesterone or testosterone</b>. So a sex-hormone problem can start in the gonad itself or higher up, in the pituitary or hypothalamus. And because these hormones build the secondary sex characteristics, their clues show up in the history (menarche, menses, menopause, libido) and on the exam (body and pubic hair, breasts, genital size).</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Thyroid gland</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Where and what</b> — in the anterior neck just <b>below the cricoid cartilage</b>: two lobes joined by an <b>isthmus</b> in front of the trachea. <b>Follicular cells</b> make <b>T4 (thyroxine)</b> and <b>T3 (triiodothyronine)</b>; <b>parafollicular cells</b> make <b>calcitonin</b>.</div>\n<div><b>T3 and T4 set the metabolic rate</b> — both raise metabolism, so every tissue uses more <b>oxygen</b> and makes more <b>heat</b>. Most circulating hormone is bound to plasma proteins; the <b>free</b> hormone enters cells, where <b>T4 is converted to T3, the most active form</b>. Conversion is impaired by <b>stress, starvation, dyes and some drugs</b>; <b>cold</b> increases it.</div>\n<div><b>Control — the thyroid axis</b> — the hypothalamus secretes <b>TRH</b> → the anterior pituitary secretes <b>TSH</b> → the thyroid makes and releases <b>T3 and T4</b>. <b>High</b> thyroid hormone levels inhibit TRH and TSH release; <b>low</b> levels increase it (negative feedback). <b>Cold and stress</b> also make the hypothalamus secrete TRH, which raises TSH.</div>\n<div><b>Making the hormone</b> — needs dietary <b>protein and iodine</b>. Iodide absorbed from the gut is trapped and concentrated by the thyroid, joined to the amino acid <b>tyrosine</b> to form T4 and T3, and stored on <b>thyroglobulin</b> in the follicles until TSH releases it. Inside cells the hormones bind DNA receptors and switch on metabolic genes that set the <b>basal metabolic rate</b>.</div>\n<div><b>What thyroid hormones do (Box 53.2)</b> — regulate the metabolic rate of all cells and <b>body heat</b>; act as <b>insulin antagonists</b>; support growth hormone, skeletal maturation and nervous system development; maintain <b>muscle tone</b>, calcium mobilization and RBC production; maintain <b>cardiac rate, force and output</b>, respiratory rate and oxygen use, and <b>GI secretion</b>; regulate protein, carbohydrate and fat metabolism and stimulate lipid turnover and cholesterol synthesis.</div>\n<div><b>Calcitonin</b> — <b>lowers serum calcium and phosphate</b> by reducing their release from bone — the opposite of PTH. <b>High</b> calcium increases calcitonin secretion; <b>low</b> calcium suppresses it.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Thyroid hormone is the body's <b>thermostat and accelerator</b> at once. Too much speeds every system — heat intolerance, fast heart, diarrhea, weight loss; too little slows every system — cold intolerance, slow heart, constipation, weight gain. Learn the function list in Box 53.2 and you can predict both diseases in chapter 55 without memorizing them separately.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Parathyroid, pancreas and pineal</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Parathyroid hormone (PTH) raises calcium</b> — four small glands on the back of the thyroid. PTH acts on three targets: <b>bone</b>, the main calcium store — releases calcium and phosphorus into the blood (resorption); <b>kidney</b> — reabsorbs calcium (and magnesium), excretes phosphorus, and <b>activates vitamin D</b>; <b>gut</b> — activated vitamin D increases calcium and phosphorus absorption. <b>Low calcium raises PTH; high calcium lowers it.</b></div>\n<div><b>Pancreas — two jobs</b> — <b>exocrine</b>: digestive enzymes through ducts into the duodenum. <b>Endocrine</b>: 1–2 million <b>islets of Langerhans</b> with <b>alpha cells (glucagon)</b>, <b>beta cells (insulin)</b> and <b>delta cells (somatostatin)</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Glucagon, insulin, somatostatin</b> — <b>glucagon raises glucose</b> — released when glucose falls (and amino acids rise) to prevent hypoglycemia. <b>Insulin lowers glucose</b> by moving it into cells and storing carbohydrate, protein and fat; it rises when glucose rises. <b>Somatostatin</b> (pancreas, gut, brain) inhibits glucagon and insulin, and gastrin, secretin and other GI peptides.</div>\n<div><b>Pineal and thymus</b> — the pineal gland, the size of a grain of rice, releases <b>melatonin in the dark</b> to set the circadian rhythm and sleep-wake cycle. The thymus works mainly in childhood and is also part of the lymphatic system.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Calcium is guarded by two opposing hormones: <b>PTH pulls calcium into the blood</b> (from bone, kidney and gut), <b>calcitonin pushes it back into bone</b>. An older woman with repeated fractures is therefore a calcium question first — and when the parathyroid glands are bruised or removed during thyroid surgery (chapter 55), calcium falls within hours.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g","ch":"53","chTitle":"Assessment of the endocrine system","title":"Aging and COVID-19 — what changes in the glands","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Age-related endocrine changes</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The general rule</b> — aging usually means <b>less glandular function and less hormone</b>. The notable exception: the <b>prostate enlarges</b> (hyperplasia). Ovarian function falls faster and more noticeably, and abruptly after a <b>hysterectomy</b> (surgical menopause). Chronic illness, diet and activity changes, poor sleep, slower metabolism and drugs make normal and abnormal hard to tell apart, so encourage regular screening.</div>\n<div><b>Less ADH</b> — urine stays <b>dilute</b> and may not concentrate when intake is low → <b>high risk for dehydration</b>. Assess for it often, and teach assistive personnel to <b>offer fluids at least every 2 hours</b> while awake unless restricted.</div>\n<div><b>Less estrogen</b> — <b>bone density falls</b>; skin becomes thinner, drier and easier to injure; perineal and vaginal tissues dry and <b>cystitis risk rises</b>. Teach <b>weight-bearing exercise</b>, adequate protein and <b>2 L of fluid</b> daily (unless contraindicated), <b>protect skin integrity</b>, perineal care at least twice a day, voiding right after intercourse, and vaginal lubricants.</div>\n<div><b>Less glucose tolerance</b> — weight above ideal with <b>elevated fasting or random glucose</b>, <b>slow wound healing</b>, <b>frequent yeast infections</b>, <b>polydipsia and polyuria</b>. Ask about family history of obesity and type 2 diabetes, teach exercise, a healthy weight and the <b>signs of diabetes to report</b> (<b>thirst, frequent urination, constant hunger, slow-healing wounds, repeated yeast infections, blurred vision, fatigue</b>), and suggest diabetes testing for persistent vaginal candidiasis, <b>a wound not healed within 2 weeks</b> (especially leg or foot), persistent hunger and thirst, or a lasting drop in energy.</div>\n<div><b>Slower general metabolism</b> — <b>less cold tolerance</b>, less appetite, lower heart rate and blood pressure. Teach dressing in layers. Look for signs of a real endocrine disorder: lethargy, new constipation, slowed thinking or speech, temperature consistently <b>below 97°F (36°C)</b>, heart rate consistently <b>below 60</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The older adult's normal changes look like mild hypothyroidism and mild diabetes, so the job is to spot the <b>difference from their own baseline</b> and the findings that go further — a temperature that stays below 97°F, a wound that will not heal in two weeks, thirst that will not stop. Low ADH reserve is the one that turns dangerous fastest: an older patient who simply drinks less becomes dehydrated quickly.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Endocrine effects of COVID-19</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Pancreas</b> — <b>new hyperglycemia</b> appeared often in people without diabetes, probably because SARS-CoV-2 can infect and replicate in pancreatic cells. <b>Ketoacidosis</b> was often diagnosed in older patients of non-White ethnic groups with type 2 diabetes. A registry is studying new-onset diabetes after COVID-19.</div>\n<div><b>Thyroid and ovaries</b> — <b>subacute thyroiditis</b> developed during infection in several studies, but thyroid function usually <b>returned to normal within months</b>. About a third of people in one survey reported <b>menstrual changes</b> — irregular, heavy, or postmenopausal bleeding.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","ch":"53","chTitle":"Assessment of the endocrine system","title":"Endocrine assessment — history and physical exam","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">History</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Age and sex</b> — <b>age</b> matters because diabetes, loss of ovarian function and decreased thyroid function are more common later in life; <b>sex assigned at birth</b> matters because some disorders favor one sex — <b>thyroid problems are more common in women</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Past history, menses and menopause</b> — ask about earlier <b>endocrine problems, symptoms and hospitalizations</b>. Ask women the <b>age at menarche</b> and whether <b>menopause</b> has occurred; Box 53.4 adds whether they still have <b>menstrual cycles</b>, the date of the <b>last period</b>, and any change in <b>flow, duration or frequency</b> since symptoms began. Ask everyone about a change in <b>libido</b> and concerns about how symptoms affect <b>sexuality or reproduction</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Drugs and hormone misuse</b> — ask about past and current drugs, <b>prescribed and over-the-counter</b>, because some alter endocrine function — for example <b>cortisone, levothyroxine, oral contraceptives, antihypertensives</b>. Hormones taken when not needed for replacement (<b>androgens, thyroid hormone</b>) can seriously disrupt many glands; teach <b>all</b> patients the danger of misusing hormones or steroids.</div>\n<div><b>Health equity</b> — <b>type 2 diabetes</b> is more common in some racial and ethnic minority groups and in people of <b>lower socioeconomic status</b>. Assess every patient, advocate for early intervention, and teach everyone with or at risk for T2DM so disparities shrink.</div>\n<div><b>Nutrition clues</b> — excessive <b>thirst</b> with AVP deficiency or resistance (formerly diabetes insipidus); <b>salt craving</b> with adrenal hypofunction; persistent <b>hunger and thirst</b> with diabetes; <b>rapid weight change without diet change</b> with thyroid or hypothalamic disease. <b>Iodine</b> is needed to make thyroid hormone and the body cannot make it, so it must come from the diet: ask which sources the patient eats and how much (Box 53.3) — <b>dairy</b> (cheese, milk, yogurt), <b>eggs</b>, <b>iodized table salt</b>, <b>saltwater fish</b> (salmon, sardines, tuna), <b>seaweed</b> (kelp, nori), <b>shellfish</b> (scallops, shrimp) and <b>iodine-containing multivitamins</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Family history and current symptoms</b> — ask about <b>obesity, growth problems, diabetes, infertility and thyroid disease</b> in the family (dominant, recessive or clustered inheritance). Box 53.4: when it started and how fast; past treatment and whether it worked; the effect on <b>activities of daily living</b>; energy; feeling <b>hot or cold</b>; sleep; hair and facial or body changes; <b>eye protrusion</b>; voice; diet and <b>weight in 6 months</b>; <b>urination amount and night waking</b>; bowel habits; menses; libido and sexual concerns.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Physical assessment</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Inspection — general and head</b> — height, weight, <b>fat distribution</b> and muscle mass for age; scalp and body hair. Face: <b>prominent forehead or jaw</b>, <b>round or puffy face</b>, dull or flat expression, <b>exophthalmos</b> (protruding eyes with retracted upper lids).</div>\n<div><b>Inspection — neck</b> — the thyroid is <b>normally not visible</b>; the isthmus may show as it moves up when the patient <b>swallows</b> (stand in front and ask them to swallow). A <b>visible thyroid needs further assessment</b>. <b>Jugular vein distention</b> can mean fluid overload.</div>\n<div><b>Inspection — skin</b> — hypo- or <b>hyperpigmentation</b>; <b>fungal infections, slow healing, bruising and petechiae</b> with adrenal hyperfunction; <b>skin infections, foot ulcers and slow healing</b> with diabetes; <b>darkened skin over joints and scars</b> with some adrenal disorders (high ACTH and MSH); <b>vitiligo</b> with primary adrenal hypofunction (autoimmune loss of melanocytes). Document location, color, size and distribution of any change since the last visit or from the patient's baseline.</div>\n<div><b>Inspection — body</b> — nails malformed, thick or brittle (<b>thyroid</b>); <b>edema</b> of extremities and sacrum (fluid balance); chest size and symmetry; <b>truncal obesity and a \"buffalo hump\"</b>, and <b>reddish-purple striae</b> on breasts or abdomen (<b>adrenocortical excess</b>). Look at the <b>breasts in both sexes</b> — size, symmetry, pigment and any discharge — <b>gynecomastia</b> (low testosterone). <b>Hirsutism</b> (excess hair on a woman's face, chest or midline abdomen), excessive scalp hair loss or a change in hair texture, told apart from normal aging. Genitals: the amount and distribution of <b>pubic hair</b> change in <b>hypogonadism</b>; ask whether the <b>scrotum and penis</b> (or <b>labia and clitoris</b>) have changed in size.</div>\n<div><b>Auscultation</b> — heart <b>rate and rhythm</b> as a baseline — some endocrine problems cause <b>dysrhythmias</b>, others dehydration. Record <b>orthostatic</b> BP and pulse lying, sitting and standing. Listen over an enlarged thyroid for a <b>bruit</b>: hypertrophy increases blood flow.</div>\n<div><b>Which glands can be felt</b> — the <b>thyroid</b> and the <b>testes</b> can be examined by palpation. Depending on the setting, the <b>primary health care provider</b> or the <b>nurse</b> performs these exams; testicular examination is covered in chapters 61 and 64.</div>\n<div><b>Palpating the thyroid</b> — from <b>behind</b>, patient sitting straight with the head bent slightly forward; thumbs on the back of the neck, fingers curved to the front on either side of the trachea; feel the gland <b>rise on swallowing</b>. For the right lobe, turn the head to the right, displace the trachea gently with the left fingers and palpate with the right hand; reverse for the left. Note size, shape, symmetry, nodules, tenderness.</div>\n<div><b>Action alert</b> — <b>do not palpate or press on the thyroid</b> of a patient who has or may have <b>hyperthyroidism</b> — it can release a burst of hormone and trigger <b>thyroid storm</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Psychosocial</b> — endocrine problems can change <b>behavior, personality, mood and self-image</b>: ask about handling stress, crying, patience and anger, and, with permission, ask family — patients often miss these changes. Changes in appearance, <b>infertility or impotence</b> affect sexuality and self-image; invite feelings. Many need <b>lifelong drugs and follow-up</b>: assess readiness to learn, self-management ability and <b>resources</b>, and refer.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The physical exam is a search for <b>the hormone's fingerprints</b>: cortisol leaves a round face, buffalo hump, striae and bruises; thyroid hormone changes the eyes, nails, heart rate and a visible neck; aldosterone and ADH show up as edema, JVD or orthostatic drops. And the one thing you must not do — pressing on a hyperthyroid gland — follows from the same physiology: the gland is full of stored hormone waiting to be released.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job","ch":"53","chTitle":"Assessment of the endocrine system","title":"Diagnostic tests and the nurse's job","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Laboratory tests</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Which fluid</b> — blood, urine and <b>saliva</b>. Salivary levels of <b>steroid hormones</b> (cortisol, testosterone, progesterone, estradiol) mirror blood levels; <b>protein hormones</b> (pituitary, thyroid) are <b>not</b> measured in saliva. Check the agency lab for tube type, timing and handling.</div>\n<div><b>Assays</b> — measure a specific hormone: <b>antibody-based immunoassays</b> and <b>chromatographic assays</b>, including <b>mass spectrometry</b> (identifies hormones by molecular mass and chemical makeup; very sensitive, many hormones at once).</div>\n<div><b>Provocative and suppression tests</b> — used when a level alone can't separate normal from abnormal. <b>Provocative (stimulation)</b>: for a suspected <b>underactive</b> gland, give a measured stimulating hormone — <b>failure to rise = hypofunction</b>. <b>Suppression</b>: for <b>high or high-normal</b> levels, give a substance that normally suppresses the gland — <b>failure to fall = hyperfunction</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Blood draws done right (Box 53.5)</b> — check tube type, timing and test drugs; some samples need special handling — <b>catecholamines go on ice to the lab immediately</b>. Explain restrictions. From an IV line, <b>clear it thoroughly</b> and <b>never sample from a double- or triple-lumen line</b> (contamination or dilution). Stress taking a <b>test drug on time</b> — set an alarm if it is due overnight.</div>\n<div><b>24-hour urine (Box 53.5)</b> — many hormones are released in pulses, so a <b>24-hour collection</b> reflects gland function (e.g., adrenal) better than one sample. Check the lab's handling rules first; any <b>preservative must be in the container when the collection begins</b>. <b>Start by emptying the bladder and discarding that urine</b> — timing starts then; note the time. Collect <b>all</b> urine for <b>exactly</b> 24 hours, then <b>empty the bladder at the end even without the urge</b> and add it. Keep any <b>preservative</b> in the container and avoid splashing (some are caustic); keep it cold if required in a cooler with ice, <b>not with food or drinks</b>. Shorter timed collections (<b>2, 4, 8 or 12 hours</b>) follow the same start and end rules. Check with the provider before any new drug — some interfere. That includes <b>over-the-counter</b> drugs.</div>\n<div><b>Glucose, A1C and genetic tests</b> — blood glucose and the <b>oral glucose tolerance test</b> diagnose diabetes; <b>A1C reflects average glucose over 100–120 days</b>. <b>Genetic testing</b> helps when hormone levels are too low to measure or family history is unknown.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Most endocrine test errors are made at the bedside, not in the lab: the first morning void kept instead of discarded, a dose of the test drug taken late, a sample drawn from the wrong lumen, a catecholamine tube left at room temperature. Each one changes the number the diagnosis rests on — so the teaching is the intervention.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Imaging and other tests</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Imaging</b> — skull x-rays show the <b>sella turcica</b> (erosion = a growth invading it). <b>MRI with contrast is the most sensitive for the pituitary</b> (CT also works). <b>CT</b> for the adrenals, ovaries and pancreas. <b>Ultrasound</b> for the thyroid, parathyroids, ovaries and testes.</div>\n<div><b>Thyroid scan</b> — a nuclear medicine test: the patient swallows a pill of <b>radioactive iodine</b>, which collects in the thyroid, and the gland is imaged <b>4 to 6 hours later</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Fine needle aspiration</b> — <b>ultrasound-guided FNA</b> is a quick, safe outpatient procedure that shows what a <b>thyroid nodule</b> is made of and whether surgery is needed.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"53-adpie","ch":"53","chTitle":"Assessment of the endocrine system","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">An assessment chapter has one aim for the nursing process: <b>find the gland and the direction of the problem</b> — excess, deficiency or poor response — then make sure the tests that confirm it are collected so the result can be trusted, while protecting the patient from the dangers that come with the work-up.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div>age and <b>sex assigned at birth</b>; onset (gradual or sudden) and past treatment; energy, sleep, <b>heat or cold intolerance</b>; weight change over 6 months; <b>thirst, urine volume, night waking</b>; bowel habits; <b>menarche, menses and menopause</b>, libido and sexual concerns; <b>prescribed and OTC drugs</b> (cortisone, levothyroxine, contraceptives, antihypertensives, misused hormones); <b>iodine intake</b>; family history of diabetes, thyroid disease, obesity, growth problems, infertility.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical</div>body build and <b>fat distribution</b>; face (puffy, round, prominent jaw, <b>exophthalmos</b>); <b>visible thyroid</b>, JVD; skin pigment, <b>vitiligo</b>, bruising, infections, <b>foot ulcers</b>, slow healing; nails; edema; <b>buffalo hump, striae</b>, gynecomastia, hirsutism; heart rate and rhythm, <b>orthostatic vital signs</b>, thyroid <b>bruit</b>; thyroid palpation — <b>never if hyperthyroidism is suspected</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Psychosocial and learning</div>coping, support, health beliefs; changes in <b>mood, patience, crying and anger</b> (ask family with permission); feelings about appearance, fertility and sexual function; <b>readiness to learn</b>, self-management ability and <b>money for lifelong treatment</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Endocrine onset is often <b>slow and insidious</b>, so the patient may not notice — weight creeping up over six months, a voice changing, a personality shift the family sees first. The history is built to catch slow change, which is why it asks about <b>months</b>, not days.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Fluid and electrolyte imbalance</div>potential for <b>dehydration</b> (especially older adults with low ADH reserve) or fluid overload, and for sodium, potassium and calcium imbalance, because aldosterone, ADH, PTH and calcitonin all regulate them.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Glucose dysregulation</div>potential for <b>hyperglycemia</b> with decreased glucose tolerance in aging, stress hormones, illness (including COVID-19) and steroid drugs; potential hypoglycemia when insulin acts unopposed.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Body image and knowledge</div><b>disturbed body image</b> from visible changes (face, eyes, hair, weight, striae); and a <b>need for teaching</b> about tests that only work when collected exactly right.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>the patient keeps <b>fluid balance</b> (stable weight, moist mucous membranes, no orthostatic drop); test specimens are collected <b>correctly the first time</b>; abnormal results are <b>reported promptly</b>; the patient <b>states feelings</b> about changes in appearance or function; and the patient explains how to avoid <b>misusing hormones</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Testing</div>confirm tube, timing and handling with the lab; <b>ice catecholamine samples</b> and send at once; draw from a <b>cleared single-lumen</b> line only; give or remind about <b>test drugs on time</b>; teach the <b>24-hour urine</b> (discard the first void, keep everything after, end with a final void, keep the preservative, keep cold if required). Report abnormal results and any <b>complications of testing</b> to the provider.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Safety and comfort</div><b>no palpation of a possibly hyperthyroid gland</b>; <b>orthostatic precautions</b> when blood pressure drops on standing; for older adults, <b>fluids at least every 2 hours</b> while awake unless restricted, layers for cold intolerance, skin protection and perineal care.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Teaching and support</div>teach the signs of diabetes to report (<b>thirst, frequent urination, constant hunger, slow-healing wounds, repeated yeast infections, blurred vision, fatigue</b>) and suggest testing when wounds will not heal or yeast infections persist; teach the <b>dangers of misusing hormones and steroids</b>; encourage regular screening; invite concerns about appearance and sexuality; refer for <b>cost of lifelong treatment</b>; communicate clearly what each test involves.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>weight, intake and output and orthostatic readings stay stable; the specimen arrives <b>valid</b> (right tube, timing, temperature, complete collection); abnormal values were <b>reported</b>; the patient can repeat the collection steps and name hormone-misuse dangers; the patient talks about body changes and knows where to get support.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"55-overview","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">55 · Chapter 55</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cellular regulation · nutrition · gas exchange</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Thyroid and parathyroid conditions</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> Thyroid hormone sets the <b>speed of every cell</b>, and parathyroid hormone guards <b>serum calcium</b>. So the thyroid diseases are two mirror images — <b>hypothyroidism</b> slows everything (the chapter's exemplar) and <b>hyperthyroidism</b> speeds everything — and the parathyroid diseases are calcium too low or too high. The dangerous moments are the extremes: <b>myxedema coma</b>, <b>thyroid storm</b>, the airway and calcium after <b>neck surgery</b>, and <b>tetany</b>. Learn the direction of the problem and the findings follow.</p><div id=\"ch55-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">TRH → TSH → T3/T4 sets the metabolic rate; T3/T4 switch TSH off. PTH keeps calcium in a narrow range by pulling it from bone, kidney and gut.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Too little thyroid hormone (Hashimoto, surgery, radioactive iodine), too much (Graves, toxic nodules, too much replacement), or too little or too much PTH.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Hypo: cold, tired, slow, constipated, weight gain, slow pulse. Hyper: hot, sweaty, wired, weight loss with hunger, fast pulse, eye changes. Calcium low: tingling, twitching, tetany; high: stones, bones, groans, confusion.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">T3, T4 and free T4 with <b>TSH</b> (high TSH + low T4 = primary hypothyroidism; low TSH + high T4 = Graves); TRAb; radioactive iodine uptake; ultrasound; calcium, phosphorus, PTH, magnesium, vitamin D.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Levothyroxine for life (hypo); methimazole, iodine, beta blockers, radioactive iodine or thyroidectomy (hyper); calcium and vitamin D (hypopara); parathyroidectomy or cinacalcet (hyperpara).</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Myxedema coma, thyroid storm, hemorrhage or stridor after thyroidectomy, hypocalcemic tetany, hypercalcemic confusion and coma.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Hypothyroid patient with falling consciousness, hypothermia or slow breathing; hyperthyroid patient whose temperature rises <b>even 1°F</b>; stridor, drooling or tingling lips after neck surgery.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">Low thyroid hormone<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>pituitary raises TSH<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>gland enlarges (goiter) but makes no more hormone<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>metabolism slows in every organ<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>slow heart, slow breathing, cold, constipation<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>levothyroxine — started low, raised slowly</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Hypo vs hyper — one line each</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b>Hypo</b>: cold intolerance, weight gain, constipation, bradycardia, lethargy, dry coarse skin. <b>Hyper</b>: heat intolerance, weight loss with increased appetite, more stools, tachycardia, anxiety, warm moist skin.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Myxedema coma vs thyroid storm</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Coma: <b>low</b> temperature, sodium, glucose, pulse, BP and breathing. Storm: <b>high fever</b> and <b>tachycardia</b>, then agitation, delirium, seizures.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Hypocalcemia vs hypercalcemia</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Low: <b>tingling around the mouth, fingers and toes</b>, twitching, Chvostek and Trousseau signs, tetany, seizures. High: kidney stones, fractures, GI upset, fatigue, confusion above 12 mg/dL.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Levothyroxine raises metabolism, so too much too fast stresses the heart → start low in older and cardiac patients. Methimazole blocks new hormone but not stored hormone → relief is delayed. <a href=\"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Pulse and rhythm, temperature, BP, respiratory rate and SpO₂, mental status, weight, bowel pattern and sleep, serum calcium after neck surgery.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Temperature up 1°F in hyperthyroidism; stridor, dyspnea, drooling or bleeding behind the neck after thyroidectomy; tingling lips or twitching; falling consciousness in hypothyroidism.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Why a goiter appears in both diseases</b> — when the thyroid makes too little hormone, the pituitary keeps raising <b>TSH</b>, and TSH makes thyroid cells <b>grow</b> — the gland enlarges without making more hormone. In <b>Graves disease</b>, antibodies stimulate the TSH receptor, so the gland grows <b>and</b> overproduces. A goiter says \"thyroid problem\", <b>not which direction</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Myxedema — what the swelling is</b> — with low metabolism, <b>glycosaminoglycans</b> (protein-sugar compounds) build up in tissues and hold mucus and water: <b>nonpitting edema</b>, especially pretibial and on top of the feet, a <b>thick enlarged tongue</b> (macroglossia), puffy face and periorbital edema.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The heart in each disease</b> — <b>hypo</b>: the heart muscle becomes <b>flabby</b> and the chambers enlarge → <b>cardiac output falls</b> → poor perfusion and gas exchange to brain and organs. <b>Hyper</b>: hormone drives rate and stroke volume up → <b>cardiac output, systolic BP and blood flow rise</b>, the diastolic pressure falls (<b>wide pulse pressure</b>), and <b>atrial fibrillation</b> appears.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Metabolism in hyperthyroidism</b> — protein breakdown exceeds buildup, <b>glucose tolerance falls</b> (hyperglycemia), fat stores fall — so the patient <b>loses weight despite a bigger appetite</b> and develops nutrition deficits.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Calcium and PTH</b> — PTH makes the kidney <b>reabsorb calcium and excrete phosphorus</b>, releases calcium from <b>bone</b> and, through vitamin D, from the <b>gut</b>. Calcium and phosphorus move in <b>opposite directions</b>: too little PTH = low calcium + high phosphorus; too much PTH = high calcium + low phosphorus.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Low calcium makes nerves and muscles <b>too excitable</b> — it normally steadies the sodium channels in nerve membranes — which is why hypocalcemia shows as tingling, twitching and tetany, and why it is an airway emergency when the larynx spasms. High calcium does the reverse: muscles weaken, the gut slows, the brain clouds.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Hypothyroidism — the cellular regulation exemplar","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is and who gets it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Definition</b> — reduced or absent thyroid hormone secretion → <b>whole-body slowing of metabolism</b>. Early on it is easily missed: onset is <b>gradual</b> and symptoms mimic many other diseases.</div>\n<div><b>Causes (Box 55.1)</b> — most US cases are <b>Hashimoto thyroiditis</b>, an autoimmune attack on thyroid tissue. Also common in North America: <b>thyroid surgery</b> and <b>radioactive iodine</b> treatment of hyperthyroidism. Worldwide: <b>iodine-poor soil and water</b> (endemic goiter) — the gland needs dietary <b>iodine and tyrosine</b> to make hormone. Drugs: <b>amiodarone, lithium</b>. Cancer (thyroid or metastatic), congenital problems. All of these are <b>primary</b> (the gland itself fails). <b>Secondary</b>: not enough TSH from congenital pituitary defects or pituitary or hypothalamic tumors, trauma, infection or infarction.</div>\n<div><b>Who</b> — <b>4–10% of adults</b>; women <b>5 to 8 times</b> more often than men. A congenital form is genetic, and thyroid dysfunction is linked to Down syndrome, Turner syndrome and atrial fibrillation. There is <b>no known prevention</b> — catch it early.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults</b> — secretion falls with age but so does clearance, so <b>don't rely on labs alone</b> — use <b>changes in symptoms</b> to judge replacement (symptoms are often milder at this age). Muscle loss is normal aging, so don't use strength as a sign. Age-related heart and nerve changes make them <b>more sensitive to replacement</b> — <b>start low, increase slowly</b> to avoid cardiac and neurologic toxicity, and assess for <b>chest pain, shortness of breath</b> and mental status changes (<b>confusion</b>, changes in sleep). Amiodarone can change thyroid levels.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What you find (Box 55.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Ask</b> — activity now <b>compared with a year ago</b> (changes are slow and easily missed, especially in older adults); more <b>sleep</b>; <b>fatigue</b>, weakness, muscle aches, <b>paresthesias</b>; <b>constipation</b>; <b>cold intolerance</b> (extra blankets or a sweater in warm weather); low libido, heavy prolonged periods or amenorrhea, trouble conceiving (anovulation), impotence and infertility in men; <b>lithium or amiodarone</b>; past hyperthyroid treatment and which one; <b>COVID-19</b> (can aggravate autoimmune thyroid disease).</div>\n<div><b>See</b> — <b>coarse dry scaly skin</b> (cool, pale or ash gray) with <b>poor wound healing</b>; dry, coarse, brittle hair that grows less, <b>loss of eyebrow hair</b>, thick brittle nails; <b>puffy face and periorbital edema</b>, <b>thick tongue</b>, <b>hoarseness</b>, <b>nonpitting edema of the hands and feet</b>; slow movement, <b>slow or slurred speech</b> (the thick tongue) and slow answers; <b>weight gain</b> without overeating (weigh and compare with a year ago); possibly a <b>goiter</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Measure</b> — pulse <b>below 60</b>, slow respirations (<b>hypoventilation</b>), temperature <b>below 97°F (36.1°C)</b>, <b>diastolic hypertension</b>, decreased tendon reflexes, <b>hearing loss</b>, decreased activity tolerance, dyspnea, pericardial or pleural effusion, abdominal distention or ascites.</div>\n<div><b>Mind and mood</b> — <b>apathy and depression</b> — sometimes the family brings the patient in; lethargy may keep them from noticing they have changed. <b>Confusion</b>, lethargy or somnolence, inattentiveness; impaired <b>attention and memory</b> and slowed thinking — families describe the patient as <b>withdrawn</b> → <b>social isolation</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Labs (Table 55.1)</b> — <b>T3 and T4 low</b>. <b>TSH high in primary</b> hypothyroidism (the gland fails, the pituitary shouts), <b>low or near normal in secondary</b> (the pituitary or hypothalamus fails). Older adults tend to run higher TSH.</div>\n<div><b>Normal ranges (Table 55.1)</b> — ranges vary by lab. <b>Serum T3</b>: <b>70–205 ng/dL</b> (1.2–3.4 nmol/L) at ages 20–50; <b>40–180 ng/dL</b> (0.6–2.8 nmol/L) over 50. <b>Total T4</b>: men <b>4–12 mcg/dL</b> (59–135 nmol/L); women <b>5–12 mcg/dL</b> (71–142 nmol/L); adults over 60 <b>5–11 mcg/dL</b> (64–142 nmol/L). <b>\"Direct\" free T4</b>: <b>0.8–2.8 ng/dL</b> (10–36 pmol/L). <b>TSH</b>: <b>0.3–5 μU/mL</b> (0.3–5 mU/L). T3, T4 and free T4 are <b>low in hypothyroidism</b> and <b>high in hyperthyroidism</b>. TSH is <b>high in primary hypothyroidism</b> but <b>low in secondary or tertiary</b> hypothyroidism; in hyperthyroidism it is <b>low in Graves disease</b> but <b>high in secondary or tertiary</b> hyperthyroidism.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every finding is the same idea — <b>not enough fuel burning</b>: less heat (cold intolerance, low temperature), less drive (slow pulse, slow breathing, slow gut, slow thinking), and tissues that collect water-holding compounds (puffy face, thick tongue). The danger is in the breathing: slow, shallow respiration plus any sedative can tip into respiratory failure.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Care and teaching</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Priority problems</b> — 1. <b>Decreased gas exchange</b> from low energy, muscle weakness and fatigue. 2. <b>Reduced perfusion</b> from low cardiac output. 3. Potential for <b>myxedema coma</b>. Respiratory failure is the most common cause of death in myxedema coma.</div>\n<div><b>Gas exchange</b> — record rate and depth, <b>SpO₂</b>, oxygen for hypoxemia, listen for decreased sounds or crackles; ventilatory support if severe. <b>Avoid sedation</b>; if needed, use <b>reduced doses</b> — hypothyroidism makes patients more sensitive — and watch gas exchange closely.</div>\n<div><b>Cardiac output</b> — watch for a rising BP and <b>dysrhythmias</b> — monitor <b>BP, heart rate and rhythm</b> and act on changes before complications set in; chronic disease may bring cardiovascular disease. Teach to report <b>chest pain or discomfort immediately</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Levothyroxine — how to give it</b> — lifelong synthetic thyroid hormone. Start at a set dose and raise gradually over weeks; the <b>lowest starting dose</b> for severe symptoms, older adults, coronary disease or unknown severity — too much or too fast causes <b>dysrhythmias, heart failure and MI</b>. Final dose is set by <b>TSH</b> and how the patient feels. <b>IV</b> in myxedema coma (the gut absorbs poorly).</div>\n<div><b>Levothyroxine — drug alert</b> — take it exactly as prescribed, never change the dose alone. <b>With water, on an empty stomach, 30–60 minutes before breakfast.</b> Keep <b>iron, calcium and antacids at least 4 hours apart</b>; fiber supplements can also block absorption. Watch for <b>chest pain and dyspnea</b> when starting, and teach the signs of <b>too much</b> — <b>fast or pounding heartbeat, chest pain, nervousness or tremor, trouble sleeping, feeling hot and sweaty, weight loss, more frequent stools</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Self-monitoring and teaching</b> — the two easiest checks are <b>sleep and bowels</b>: needing more sleep and <b>constipated</b> → dose may be too low; <b>trouble falling asleep and more bowel movements</b> → dose may be too high. Teach lifelong therapy, signs of hypo- and hyperthyroidism, <b>medical alert bracelet</b>, periodic blood tests, <b>no OTC drugs without asking</b>, a balanced diet with <b>fiber and fluids</b>, warmth until symptoms improve, rest. One person should be responsible for preparing doses so none are <b>missed or doubled</b>; one-floor living may help for a while. A patient on replacement who is <b>still tired and cold</b> (a sweater in warm weather) shows signs of too little hormone: take <b>vital signs and temperature</b>, <b>review the medication record</b> (dose, missed doses, how and when it is taken) and report — the provider may check the TSH and adjust the dose. Make sure whoever oversees daily care (family, facility staff) knows about the condition; arrange home health if needed.</div>\n<div><b>Evaluate</b> — pulse <b>above 60</b> and BP normal for the patient; <b>SpO₂ above 93%</b>; improving cognition.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Replacement has to be slow because the heart has adapted to a low metabolic demand. Thyroid hormone raises <b>oxygen demand</b> in every tissue, including the myocardium — give a full dose to a 70-year-old with coronary disease and you have created the conditions for angina, dysrhythmia and infarction. That is also why the empty-stomach rule matters: absorption varies with food and minerals, and a dose that is absorbed unevenly cannot be titrated.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"55-myxedema-coma","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Myxedema coma","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Myxedema coma</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — a rare but life-threatening complication of <b>untreated or poorly treated hypothyroidism</b>: dangerously reduced <b>cardiopulmonary and neurologic function</b> (few patients are actually comatose). Mortality is extremely high — early recognition is everything. Low cardiac output starves the brain and other organs of blood and oxygen; <b>respiratory failure</b> is the most common cause of death.</div>\n<div><b>Triggers</b> — any hypothyroid patient who is <b>newly diagnosed</b> or has other health problems is at risk. Triggers: <b>acute illness, surgery, chemotherapy, stopping thyroid replacement, sedatives or opioids</b>.</div>\n<div><b>What you see</b> — greatly <b>reduced consciousness and cognition</b>, <b>respiratory failure</b>, <b>hypotension</b>, <b>hyponatremia</b>, <b>hypothermia</b>, <b>hypoglycemia</b>, <b>bradycardia</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Action alert</b> — it can progress to <b>shock, organ damage and death</b>, so assess every hypothyroid patient at least <b>every 8 hours</b>, especially <b>mental status</b>, and report changes promptly. Treatment starts on the symptoms — <b>do not wait for lab confirmation</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Emergency care (Box 55.3)</b> — keep a <b>patent airway</b>; IV <b>normal or hypertonic saline</b>; <b>IV T3 and/or T4</b>; <b>IV glucose</b>; <b>corticosteroids</b>; temperature and <b>BP hourly</b>; <b>warm blankets</b>; watch mental status; <b>turn every 2 hours</b>; <b>aspiration precautions</b>.</div>\n<div><b>IV hormone and drug cautions</b> — thyroid hormone goes <b>IV</b> because the slowed gut absorbs pills poorly, but it carries the same risk as any start of replacement: too much too fast → <b>dysrhythmias, heart failure or MI</b> — watch rhythm, BP, chest pain and dyspnea, especially in <b>older or cardiac</b> patients. <b>Sedatives and opioids</b> both trigger and deepen the coma: avoid them; if one is essential, expect a <b>reduced dose</b> and monitor breathing and SpO₂.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Read the list of findings as \"everything that depends on metabolism has failed\": no heat (hypothermia), no drive to breathe (respiratory failure), no pump (bradycardia, hypotension), no fuel (hypoglycemia), and dilutional low sodium. Each line of the emergency box answers one of them — and warming is with <b>blankets</b>, not aggressive active rewarming, while the hormone is replaced <b>IV</b> because the slowed gut will not absorb a pill.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"55-hyperthyroidism-and-graves-disease","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Hyperthyroidism and Graves disease","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What it is</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Definition</b> — <b>excess thyroid hormone</b> — the same name is used when the cause is too much <b>synthetic hormone</b> in someone with a normal gland. It <b>speeds metabolism in every organ</b> and increases sympathetic activity; it can be temporary or permanent.</div>\n<div><b>Graves disease — the most common cause</b> — <b>autoimmune</b>: often after thyroid inflammation, antibodies called <b>thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSIs)</b> bind the <b>TSH receptor</b> and drive the gland — more cells, a <b>goiter</b>, and overproduction. Only Graves adds <b>exophthalmos</b> and <b>pretibial myxedema</b> (dry, waxy swelling on the front of the lower legs). Diagnosed most often in <b>women 30–50</b>; linked to other autoimmune diseases (diabetes, vitiligo, rheumatoid arthritis), often in both identical twins — ask about family. COVID-19 can aggravate it.</div>\n<div><b>Other causes</b> — <b>toxic multinodular goiter</b> — nodules or adenomas in a goiter present for years; milder, <b>no exophthalmos or pretibial myxedema</b>; mostly women until 50, then equal. <b>Exogenous</b> hyperthyroidism — too much replacement hormone.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">What you find (Box 55.4)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>History</b> — weight and usual weight; <b>unplanned weight loss</b> with <b>increased appetite</b> and <b>more bowel movements</b>. <b>Heat intolerance is often the first symptom</b> — sweating, light clothes in cold weather. Palpitations, chest pain, dyspnea. Blurred or <b>double vision</b>, eye fatigue. Fatigue with <b>insomnia</b>; restless or anxious (family notices). Amenorrhea or lighter periods and lower fertility; decreased libido in women and men. Changes can be so gradual that the patient hasn't noticed them. Past <b>thyroid surgery or neck radiation</b> (some stay hyperthyroid after surgery or resist radiation), thyroid hormone or antithyroid drugs.</div>\n<div><b>Eyes</b> — <b>exophthalmos</b> (Graves): edema of the eye muscles and fat behind the eye push the globe forward → focusing problems, <b>optic nerve pressure</b>, and if the lids don't close, <b>dry eyes and corneal ulcers</b>; look for tearing, redness, <b>photophobia</b>. In <b>all</b> hyperthyroidism: <b>eyelid lag</b> (upper lid doesn't follow the eye down) and <b>globe lag</b> (lid pulls back faster than the eye looking up) — ask the patient to look down, then up.</div>\n<div><b>Neck — look, don't touch</b> — note size and symmetry; a goiter may be <b>4 times normal size</b>. The generalist nurse <b>does not palpate</b> the thyroid — in hyperthyroidism it can release a flood of hormone and trigger <b>thyroid storm</b>. A <b>bruit</b> may be heard from increased blood flow.</div>\n<div><b>Heart, skin, muscles, mood</b> — <b>tachycardia</b>, palpitations, <b>higher systolic and lower diastolic BP (wide pulse pressure)</b>, <b>atrial fibrillation</b>, rapid shallow breathing. <b>Warm, smooth, moist skin</b>, diaphoresis, <b>fine silky hair</b>, thinning scalp hair. <b>Tremor</b>, <b>hyperactive reflexes</b>, muscle weakness. Wide <b>mood swings</b>, short attention, anxiety, <b>hyperactivity</b>, restlessness and irritability; \"full speed ahead or completely stopped\" (poor sleep → fatigue); crying or laughing without cause. Men: erectile dysfunction, gynecomastia.</div>\n<div><b>Tests</b> — <b>T3, T4 and free T4 high</b>; <b>TSH low in Graves</b> (high in rare secondary or tertiary disease) — ranges in <b>Table 55.1</b> (free T4 <b>0.8–2.8 ng/dL</b>, TSH <b>0.3–5 μU/mL</b>; T3 and total T4 by age and sex in the hypothyroidism notes); <b>TRAb</b> (antibody to the TSH receptor) confirms Graves. <b>Radioactive iodine uptake</b> (oral ¹²³I, short half-life — <b>no radiation precautions</b>, but <b>rule out pregnancy</b>): uptake is increased and shows active nodules. <b>Ultrasound</b> (30 minutes, painless) for size and nodules. <b>ECG</b>: SVT, atrial fibrillation, PVCs.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The excess hormone acts like a <b>permanent sympathetic surge</b> plus a furnace turned up: the heart races, the skin sweats, the gut hurries, the muscles shake, and the patient is hungry yet losing weight. The low TSH is the pituitary obeying negative feedback — it has stopped asking for hormone — which is how you know the gland itself (or an antibody) is driving the excess.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"55-treating-hyperthyroidism-drugs-radioacti","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Treating hyperthyroidism — drugs, radioactive iodine, comfort","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Nursing care while it is treated</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Monitor</b> — <b>apical pulse, BP and temperature at least every 4 hours</b>; teach to report palpitations, dyspnea, vertigo or chest pain at once. A rising temperature may be <b>thyroid storm</b> starting — <b>report an increase of even 1°F (1.8°C) immediately</b> <i>(1.8°C is the book's figure; a 1°F rise is really about 0.5°C)</i>; tell AP to report each temperature as soon as it is taken; if it is up, assess the heart right away and check the monitor for dysrhythmias.</div>\n<div><b>Reduce stimulation</b> — rest; a <b>quiet room</b> — door closed, fewer visitors, non-essential care postponed — to keep symptoms and cardiac risk down.</div>\n<div><b>Comfort</b> — <b>cool room</b>; AP keep <b>fresh ice water</b> at the bedside and <b>change damp linens</b> from sweating; cool showers or sponge baths several times a day; <b>artificial tears</b> for exophthalmos.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Drug therapy (Table 55.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Thionamides — first treatment</b> — <b>methimazole is preferred</b> (easier to use); <b>propylthiouracil (PTU)</b> is used much less because of <b>liver toxicity</b>. They block hormone production by <b>stopping iodide binding</b> in the gland. Response is <b>delayed</b> because stored hormone keeps being released. Remission is possible, especially with mild disease and a small goiter. The two are <b>not interchangeable</b> — PTU doses are much higher.</div>\n<div><b>Thionamide teaching</b> — <b>avoid crowds and sick people</b> — they lower the immune response; report <b>weight gain, slow heart rate, cold intolerance</b> (signs of hypothyroidism → dose may be too high); <b>methimazole causes birth defects</b> — report pregnancy (may be switched in the first trimester); with <b>PTU, report dark urine or yellow skin or eyes</b> (liver toxicity or failure).</div>\n<div><b>Iodine (SSKI)</b> — saturated solution of potassium iodide: give <b>orally, diluted in a glass of water or a beverage</b>; give <b>1 hour after a thionamide</b> or <b>1 week after radioactive iodine</b> to avoid worsening hyperthyroidism. Used <b>short term before surgery</b>: it reduces blood flow through the gland and hormone release; improvement within about <b>2 weeks</b>, though metabolism can take weeks longer to normalize. Can cause <b>hypothyroidism</b> — monitor and adjust the drug regimen.</div>\n<div><b>Beta blockers</b> — <b>atenolol or propranolol</b> relieve <b>sweating, anxiety, tachycardia and palpitations</b> — supportive only, they <b>do not lower hormone production</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Radioactive iodine (RAI) therapy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>How it works</b> — oral <b>¹³¹I</b>, dosed to gland size and sensitivity. The thyroid takes it up and the local radiation <b>destroys some hormone-producing cells</b>. Stored hormone means relief takes <b>6–8 weeks</b>, so antithyroid drugs may still be needed early on. <b>Not in pregnancy</b> — it crosses the placenta and damages the fetal thyroid. Outpatient; one dose is often enough, but some patients need a second.</div>\n<div><b>Precautions at home (Box 55.5)</b> — the low dose usually clears the body within <b>a month</b>, but the source is unsealed: some radioactivity is in <b>urine, saliva and stool</b> for the <b>first few days</b> (saliva through the first week), so precautions cover that time. <b>Sit to urinate</b>; <b>flush 2–3 times</b> with the lid closed; condom catheter (men) or <b>flushable tissue layers</b> (women) for incontinence instead of gel pads. Stay <b>at least 6 feet from pregnant women, infants and young children</b> for the prescribed time (usually <b>at least a week</b>), including not sharing a bed. <b>Don't share toothbrushes or toothpaste</b>, <b>don't prepare food or share drinks</b>. Return to work and travel may wait only <b>a few days</b>, depending on the dose.</div>\n<div><b>Afterward</b> — many patients become <b>hypothyroid</b> and need <b>lifelong replacement</b>; hypothyroidism can appear <b>years later</b>, so follow-up thyroid tests continue. Radiation thyroiditis (mild, gone within a week) can follow in 5–10 days.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Both the drugs and radioactive iodine work slowly for the same reason: the gland is <b>full of stored hormone</b> on thyroglobulin, and nothing that stops new production stops that store from being released. That is why beta blockers are given meanwhile (they treat the effects now), and why iodine is the one drug that acts fast before surgery — it shrinks blood flow and release.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"55-thyroidectomy-and-thyroid-storm","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Thyroidectomy and thyroid storm","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Thyroid surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>When and how</b> — for hyperthyroidism that doesn't respond to other treatment, <b>severe disease</b>, patients who <b>can't tolerate</b> other treatment, or a large goiter <b>compressing the trachea or esophagus</b>. <b>Total</b> (then lifelong replacement) or <b>subtotal</b> thyroidectomy, often minimally invasive; surgeons avoid the <b>parathyroid glands</b> and <b>recurrent laryngeal nerves</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Before surgery</b> — thionamides first so the patient is <b>euthyroid</b>; <b>iodine</b> to shrink the gland's size and <b>vascularity</b> (less bleeding, less storm risk). <b>Hypertension, dysrhythmias and tachycardia must be controlled.</b> Teach deep breathing, to <b>support the neck with both hands behind it</b> when coughing or moving, and to expect a drain and dressing.</div>\n<div><b>After surgery — the routine</b> — monitoring for complications is <b>the most important nursing action</b>. Vital signs <b>every 15 minutes until stable</b>, then every 30. Pain relief; pillows supporting head and neck; <b>semi-Fowler</b> while awake; <b>avoid neck extension</b>; deep breathe every <b>30–60 minutes</b>; suction as needed.</div>\n<div><b>Hemorrhage</b> — most likely in the <b>first 24 hours</b>. Inspect the dressing <b>and behind the neck</b> — blood pools there. A <b>moderate amount of serosanguineous drainage</b> in a drain is normal. Bleeding can show as <b>respiratory distress</b> from tracheal compression.</div>\n<div><b>Airway</b> — swelling, tetany or <b>laryngeal nerve damage</b> can cause obstruction; <b>laryngeal stridor</b> (harsh, high-pitched) means acute obstruction. Keep <b>emergency tracheostomy equipment</b> in the room and oxygen and suction working. <b>Critical rescue</b>: stridor, dyspnea, <b>falling SpO₂, inability to swallow, drooling</b> → <b>call the Rapid Response Team</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Hypocalcemia and tetany</b> — if the <b>parathyroids</b> are removed, bruised or lose blood supply, PTH falls → <b>hypocalcemia</b>. Ask about <b>tingling around the mouth or in the fingers and toes</b>; watch for <b>muscle twitching</b>. Keep <b>IV calcium gluconate</b> available.</div>\n<div><b>Laryngeal nerve damage</b> — <b>hoarseness and a weak voice</b>. Check the voice <b>every 2 hours</b> and document; reassure that hoarseness is <b>usually temporary</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Every complication after thyroidectomy is an <b>anatomy problem</b>: the gland sits on the trachea (bleeding and swelling compress it), the parathyroids are glued to its back (calcium falls), and the recurrent laryngeal nerves run beside it (the voice changes, or the cords close). Hoarseness alone is common and usually temporary; <b>stridor is not</b> — it is the airway closing.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Thyroid storm (thyroid crisis)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What and why</b> — a life-threatening surge in <b>uncontrolled hyperthyroidism</b>, usually Graves: excess hormone release drives the metabolic rate dramatically up; <b>fatal if untreated</b>, and deadly even with treatment. Triggers: <b>trauma, infection, DKA, pregnancy</b>, <b>vigorous palpation of the goiter</b>, iodine exposure, <b>RAI</b>. Rare after surgery now, thanks to drugs beforehand.</div>\n<div><b>What you see</b> — key signs: <b>fever and tachycardia</b>. Also abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea; anxiety and tremors → <b>restlessness, confusion, psychosis, seizures, coma</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Critical rescue</b> — in any hyperthyroid patient — <b>even after thyroidectomy</b> — check temperature often: a rise of <b>even 1°F (1.8°C)</b> <i>(the book's Celsius figure; really about 0.5°C)</i> may mean impending crisis → <b>report immediately</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Emergency care (Box 55.6)</b> — <b>airway and ventilation</b>; oral <b>methimazole or PTU</b> (if not possible, <b>cholestyramine</b>, a bile acid sequestrant, to lower hormone levels); <b>iodide</b>, <b>non-salicylate antipyretics</b> (acetaminophen) and <b>glucocorticoids</b>; oral <b>propranolol</b> for heart rate; <b>continuous cardiac monitoring</b>; VS every <b>15–30 minutes</b>; <b>cooling blanket or ice packs</b>; <b>normal saline</b> for dehydration.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The antipyretic must be <b>non-salicylate</b> because aspirin displaces thyroid hormone from its carrier proteins and raises the <b>free</b> hormone — the active form — making the storm worse. Propranolol does double duty: it blocks the racing heart and also slows the conversion of T4 to the more active T3.</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Graves eye care</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Eyes</b> — treating the hyperthyroidism usually <b>doesn't fully fix</b> the eyes; care is symptomatic: <b>elevate the head of the bed at night</b>, <b>artificial tears</b>, <b>dark glasses</b> for photophobia, <b>gently tape the lids closed at bedtime</b> if they don't close. Pressure behind the eye can cut blood supply → <b>ischemia and blindness</b>: short-term <b>high-dose prednisone, tapered</b>; selenium, <b>teprotumumab</b>; <b>orbital decompression</b> surgery if sight or the eye is threatened.</div>\n<div><b>Long-term teaching</b> — report any <b>return or increase</b> of hyperthyroid symptoms; know the signs of <b>hypothyroidism</b> and the possible need for <b>lifelong replacement</b>; keep follow-up — hypothyroidism can appear <b>years after RAI</b>, and most patients need <b>1–2 dose adjustments</b> after surgery.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"55-thyroiditis-and-thyroid-cancer","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Thyroiditis and thyroid cancer","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Thyroiditis — inflammation of the thyroid</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Infectious</b> — <b>bacterial</b>; acute: <b>sudden one-sided neck pain and tenderness</b>, malaise, fever → usually resolves with <b>IV antibiotics</b>. Chronic infection is less painful and may be on both sides.</div>\n<div><b>Subacute (granulomatous)</b> — <b>viral</b>, after a cold or upper respiratory infection: <b>neck pain</b> and a <b>hard, enlarged</b> gland (goiter). Thyroid function can stay normal. The usual course: <b>hyperthyroid → hypothyroid → back to normal</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Radiation and palpation</b> — <b>radiation thyroiditis</b> 5–10 days after radioiodine — mild, gone within a week. <b>Palpation or trauma-induced</b> thyroiditis after examination, biopsy, neck surgery or injury.</div>\n<div><b>Hashimoto (chronic autoimmune)</b> — antibodies and lymphocytes destroy thyroid tissue → <b>hypothyroidism</b>. <b>Painless enlargement</b> and <b>dysphagia</b>. Diagnosed by circulating antithyroid antibodies — <b>anti-TPO (TPO-Ab) is elevated</b>; hormone levels vary with the stage.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Thyroid cancer</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>First sign</b> — usually a <b>single, painless lump or nodule</b>; other signs depend on where it has spread.</div>\n<div><b>Four types</b> — <b>Papillary</b> — most common, women around 50, <b>slow growing</b> (often years pass before it reaches nearby <b>lymph nodes</b>), curable with partial or total thyroidectomy while confined. <b>Follicular</b> — ages 40–60, iodine-poor regions, spreads through blood to <b>bone and lung</b>; adhesion causes <b>dyspnea, dysphagia, hoarseness</b>. <b>Medullary</b> — sometimes inherited (<b>MEN2</b>, children and young adults), more often sporadic in adults 40–60. <b>Anaplastic</b> — rare, <b>rapid and aggressive</b>, invades nearby tissue, hard to treat: dyspnea, hoarseness, dysphagia. Papillary and follicular are called <b>differentiated</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Health equity</b> — <b>African Americans</b> have higher risk of <b>anaplastic</b> cancer. Advocate for access to care, <b>telehealth and transportation</b> so it is found early.</div>\n<div><b>Treatment</b> — <b>surgery</b> for most: one lobe if small and contained, otherwise <b>total thyroidectomy</b> with neck node dissection if nodes are involved (same post-op care as above). <b>RAI</b> afterward to destroy remaining tissue (<b>not</b> for anaplastic); kinase inhibitors if that fails. <b>External beam radiation and/or chemotherapy</b> mainly for anaplastic, which has usually spread by diagnosis; radiation can also be used before surgery (to shrink the tumor) or after it (for remaining cancer). Afterward the patient is <b>hypothyroid</b> — teach replacement. Rarely fatal; refer for palliative or hospice care when needed.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>"},{"id":"55-hypoparathyroidism-and-hyperparathyroidi","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Hypoparathyroidism and hyperparathyroidism","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Normal values (Table 55.3)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Parathyroid labs</b> — total <b>calcium 9.0–10.5 mg/dL</b> (2.25–2.62 mmol/L; slightly lower in older adults); <b>phosphorus 3–4.5 mg/dL</b> (0.97–1.45 mmol/L; critical below 1 mg/dL); <b>magnesium 1.3–2.1 mEq/L</b> (0.65–1.05 mmol/L; critical below 0.5 or above 3 mEq/L); <b>PTH 10–65 pg/mL</b> (10–65 ng/L); <b>vitamin D 25–80 ng/mL</b> (62.5–200 nmol/L). Hypoparathyroidism: calcium, magnesium, PTH and vitamin D <b>down</b>, phosphorus <b>up</b>. Hyperparathyroidism: calcium, magnesium and PTH <b>up</b>, phosphorus <b>down</b>, vitamin D <b>variable</b>. The table lists high calcium for <b>primary</b> hyperparathyroidism; in secondary disease (e.g., CKD) the trigger is low calcium (Box 55.7).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hypoparathyroidism — calcium too low</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What and why</b> — rare: too little PTH (or PTH that doesn't work) → <b>hypocalcemia</b>. Most often <b>after surgery</b> — thyroidectomy, parathyroidectomy, or head and neck cancer surgery; also autoimmune or genetic. Box 55.7 also lists <b>radiation ablation</b> of the thyroid and <b>congenital dysgenesis</b> (glands that never developed properly).</div>\n<div><b>Ask and look</b> — head or neck <b>surgery or radiation</b>; serious neck injury (car crash, strangulation). <b>Mild–moderate</b>: <b>tingling and numbness around the mouth and in the hands and feet</b>. <b>Severe</b>: <b>muscle cramps, carpopedal spasms, seizures</b> (without loss of consciousness or incontinence); irritability to psychosis. Muscle contractions flexing the fingers, hands and elbows signal <b>impending tetany</b>. <b>Trousseau</b> and <b>Chvostek</b> signs; bands or pits on the teeth.</div>\n<div><b>Diagnose</b> — serum <b>calcium, magnesium, PTH and albumin</b>; then a <b>24-hour urine calcium</b>; vitamin D (correct any deficiency).</div>\n<div><b>Treat</b> — acute severe hypocalcemia: <b>IV calcium gluconate</b>. Milder: oral <b>calcitriol</b> (active vitamin D) and <b>calcium carbonate</b>; vitamin D for mild cases; <b>daily calcium</b> long term. Keep calcium in the <b>low-normal range</b> — enough to prevent symptoms, low enough to keep <b>urine calcium</b> down and prevent <b>kidney stones</b>. Teach calcium-rich foods <b>in moderation</b> (milk, yogurt, cheese, ice cream), that therapy is <b>lifelong</b>, and a <b>medical alert bracelet</b>; sticking to the drug and diet plan usually prevents a <b>hypocalcemic crisis</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hyperparathyroidism — calcium too high</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What and why</b> — too much PTH → <b>hypercalcemia and hypophosphatemia</b>. In bone, PTH <b>slows osteoblasts and speeds osteoclasts</b> → calcium and phosphorus leave bone, <b>density falls</b>; chronic excess calcium <b>deposits in soft tissues</b>. <b>Primary</b>: one or more glands ignore calcium feedback. <b>Secondary</b>: <b>vitamin D deficiency</b> and <b>chronic kidney disease</b>. Others (Box 55.7): a <b>parathyroid tumor or cancer</b>, <b>congenital hyperplasia</b>, CKD with hypocalcemia, poor calcium absorption (celiac disease, bariatric surgery), drugs (diuretics, bisphosphonates), inherited <b>MEN1</b>, neck trauma or radiation, PTH-secreting cancers of the <b>lung, kidney or GI tract</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Ask and look</b> — fractures, weight loss, arthritis, stress, head or neck radiation. Chronic: <b>waxy pallor</b>, bone deformities. <b>Kidney stones</b> and calcium in the kidneys; <b>bone lesions, fractures, cysts, osteoporosis</b>. GI: <b>anorexia, nausea, vomiting, epigastric pain, constipation, weight loss</b>; high calcium raises <b>gastrin</b> → <b>peptic ulcer</b>. Fatigue and lethargy worsen as calcium rises; <b>above 12 mg/dL</b> (3.0 mmol/L): <b>psychosis and confusion → coma and death</b> if untreated. X-rays: stones, deposits, lesions; bone density loss.</div>\n<div><b>Without surgery</b> — for patients who aren't surgical candidates or want to delay surgery, monitor <b>calcium, kidney function and bone density</b> closely; nursing priorities are monitoring and <b>preventing injury</b>. Drug therapy: <b>cinacalcet</b> (a calcimimetic) binds calcium-sensing receptors on the parathyroids → <b>less PTH</b> → lower calcium, steadier minerals and slower bone damage; used for <b>more severe symptoms</b> or hypercalcemia from <b>parathyroid cancer</b>; monitor calcium routinely. <b>Bisphosphonates</b> are preferred for osteoporosis or fracture risk. Teach <b>regular exercise</b>, <b>good hydration</b> (prevents stones), <b>avoid lithium</b> if possible (it can raise calcium), <b>avoid prolonged bed rest</b>, a diet with <b>moderate</b> calcium and vitamin D. Bones are fragile: AP use a <b>lift sheet</b> to reposition — never pull.</div>\n<div><b>Parathyroidectomy</b> — the treatment of choice — usually <b>curative</b> (calcium returns to normal). Minimally invasive for a single adenoma, a transverse lower-neck incision when more than one gland must come out; all four glands are examined and only diseased glands are removed (all four if all are diseased). Pre- and post-op care match thyroidectomy; recurrent laryngeal nerve injury is <b>rare</b> — check for voice changes and hoarseness. The remaining glands have <b>atrophied</b> and take <b>days to weeks</b> to recover → <b>hypocalcemic crisis</b> risk: check every calcium result until stable, watch <b>tingling and twitching</b> of face and extremities, <b>Trousseau and Chvostek</b>. If it persists, hypoparathyroidism is <b>permanent</b> → lifelong calcium and vitamin D.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Hypercalcemia's old mnemonic — <b>stones, bones, abdominal groans and psychic moans</b> — is just PTH's targets turned against the patient: calcium dumped into urine forms stones, calcium pulled from bone leaves fractures, high calcium slows the gut and stimulates gastrin, and it dulls the brain. After surgery the problem flips overnight, because glands that were suppressed for years cannot restart at once.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"55-adpie","ch":"55","chTitle":"Thyroid and parathyroid conditions","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Every plan in this chapter starts by naming the <b>direction</b> — too slow or too fast, calcium too low or too high — and then guards the <b>organ that fails first</b> in that direction: breathing and cardiac output when metabolism is slow, the heart and temperature when it is fast, and the airway and calcium after neck surgery.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Thyroid history</div>activity and sleep <b>compared with a year ago</b>; heat or cold intolerance; weight with appetite; bowel pattern; palpitations, chest pain, dyspnea; vision and eye discomfort; mood, memory and attention (ask family); menses, libido, fertility; <b>lithium, amiodarone</b>, thyroid hormone or antithyroid drugs; past <b>thyroid surgery, RAI or neck radiation</b>; COVID-19; family autoimmune disease.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Thyroid exam</div>pulse (rate, rhythm, <b>apical</b> when hyperthyroid), BP and <b>pulse pressure</b>, temperature, respirations and <b>SpO₂</b>; skin, hair, eyebrows, nails; face, <b>tongue</b>, voice; <b>eyes</b> (exophthalmos, lid lag, globe lag, lid closure); <b>goiter size by inspection only — no palpation</b>; <b>bruit</b>; reflexes and <b>tremor</b>; nonpitting or <b>pretibial</b> edema; mental status.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Calcium checks</div>ask about <b>tingling around the mouth, fingers and toes</b>; look for <b>twitching</b> and flexion of fingers and hands; test <b>Chvostek and Trousseau</b>; check every <b>serum calcium</b> after neck surgery; ask about stones, fractures, GI upset and confusion in hypercalcemia.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The year-ago comparison is the key question for hypothyroidism because the change is so slow that neither the patient nor a single exam notices it — the difference between then and now is the finding.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Hypothyroidism</div>1. <b>Decreased gas exchange</b> from low energy, weakness and fatigue. 2. <b>Reduced perfusion</b> from decreased cardiac output. 3. <b>Potential for myxedema coma</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Hyperthyroidism and surgery</div>potential for <b>thyroid storm</b> and <b>dysrhythmias</b>; <b>weight loss and nutrition deficit</b> from hypermetabolism; <b>eye injury</b> from exophthalmos; after thyroidectomy — <b>airway obstruction</b>, <b>hemorrhage</b>, <b>hypocalcemia/tetany</b>, laryngeal nerve injury.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Parathyroid</div>hypo: potential for <b>tetany, laryngospasm and seizures</b>. Hyper: potential for <b>fragility fractures</b>, <b>kidney stones</b>, peptic ulcer and <b>confusion</b>; after parathyroidectomy, <b>hypocalcemic crisis</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>hypothyroid: <b>pulse above 60</b>, BP normal for the patient, <b>SpO₂ above 93%</b>, improving cognition, no myxedema coma. Hyperthyroid: heart rate controlled, <b>no temperature rise</b>, weight stable, eyes protected. After neck surgery: <b>patent airway</b>, no bleeding, <b>calcium in range</b>, voice returning. Parathyroid: calcium <b>low-normal</b> without symptoms or stones; no fractures.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Hypothyroidism</div>monitor breathing and SpO₂, give oxygen, <b>avoid or reduce sedatives</b>; monitor BP, rate and rhythm; give <b>levothyroxine on an empty stomach 30–60 minutes before breakfast</b>, apart from iron, calcium and antacids by 4 hours; <b>start low, go slow</b> in older and cardiac patients; warmth; fiber and fluids; assess at least <b>every 8 hours</b> for myxedema coma and give <b>Box 55.3</b> care (airway, IV saline, IV T3/T4, IV glucose, steroids, hourly temperature and BP, warm blankets, turning, aspiration precautions).</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Hyperthyroidism</div><b>apical pulse, BP, temperature at least every 4 hours</b>; report <b>any 1°F rise</b>; quiet room, rest, cool environment, ice water, dry linens; artificial tears and eye protection; <b>methimazole</b> (PTU less often), <b>SSKI</b> diluted, <b>beta blockers</b>; RAI precautions teaching; never palpate the gland; <b>Box 55.6</b> for storm (airway, antithyroid drug, iodide, acetaminophen, steroids, propranolol, cooling, saline, continuous monitoring).</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">After neck surgery</div>VS every 15 minutes until stable; <b>semi-Fowler</b>, neck supported, <b>no extension</b>; check the dressing <b>and behind the neck</b>; <b>trach equipment, oxygen and suction</b> at hand; voice every 2 hours; ask about <b>tingling</b> and check calcium; <b>IV calcium gluconate</b> available; stridor, drooling, inability to swallow or falling SpO₂ → <b>Rapid Response Team</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Parathyroid</div>hypo: <b>IV calcium gluconate</b> for acute tetany; oral calcium carbonate and <b>calcitriol</b>; seizure precautions; teach lifelong therapy and a medical alert bracelet. Hyper: <b>hydration</b>, mobility, <b>lift sheet</b>, cinacalcet or bisphosphonates, avoid lithium; after parathyroidectomy check <b>every calcium</b> and watch for tetany.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>the patient keeps a <b>pulse above 60</b> and <b>SpO₂ above 93%</b> with clearer thinking (hypo); heart rate and temperature stay controlled and weight stabilizes (hyper); the airway stays open with no bleeding and <b>calcium stays in range</b> after surgery; the patient can state <b>how to take levothyroxine</b>, the signs of <b>too much and too little</b> hormone, RAI precautions, and the <b>tingling</b> that means low calcium.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-overview","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Overview & how it fits together","kind":"overview","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:2px solid var(--color-text);padding-top:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-4);align-items:baseline\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">56 · Chapter 56</div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:12px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Glucose regulation · perfusion · tissue integrity · acid-base</div>\n</div>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(26px,3.4vw,40px);line-height:1.04;margin:var(--space-2) 0 0\">Diabetes mellitus</h2>\n</div><p style=\"margin:0;max-width:82ch;font-size:17px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><b style=\"color:var(--color-text)\">Intro.</b> Diabetes is a failure of <b>glucose regulation</b>: either the beta cells make <b>no insulin</b> (type 1) or the body <b>resists</b> it and the beta cells slowly wear out (type 2). Glucose is affected first, then fat and protein metabolism. Everything in this chapter follows from two timescales — <b>minutes to days</b> (hypoglycemia, DKA, HHS, which kill if treated late or wrong) and <b>years</b> (damage to large and small vessels: heart, brain, eyes, kidneys, nerves and feet). The nurse's job is to keep glucose in range, catch the emergencies early, and teach the person who manages this disease every day: the patient.</p><div id=\"ch56-map\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-accent-700);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:var(--space-3);align-items:baseline\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Connection Map</span>\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:20px;font-weight:600\">How this chapter fits together</span>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,190px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">1 · Normal physiology</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Insulin (\"hormone of plenty\") moves glucose into cells and stores fuel; glucagon (\"hormone of starvation\") releases liver glucose; the brain needs a continuous glucose supply.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">2 · What fails</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Type 1: autoimmune beta-cell destruction → absolute insulin lack. Type 2: insulin resistance → declining beta-cell secretion.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">3 · What the nurse sees</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>Polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia</b>, fatigue, weight loss (type 1), blurred vision, infections and slow healing; in emergencies, Kussmaul breathing and fruity breath (DKA), confusion (HHS), shaking and sweating (hypoglycemia).</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">4 · What proves it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\"><b>A1C ≥ 6.5%</b>, fasting glucose <b>≥ 126</b>, 2-hour OGTT <b>≥ 200</b>, or random <b>&gt; 200</b> with symptoms; ketones, pH and bicarbonate, osmolarity; urine albumin.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">5 · What targets it</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Balanced eating, activity, glucose monitoring, metformin and other antidiabetic drugs, insulin; IV fluids, IV insulin and potassium in DKA/HHS; the 15-15 rule for lows.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">6 · If it worsens</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">DKA, HHS, severe hypoglycemia; heart attack and stroke, retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy, foot ulcers and amputation.</span></div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px;min-width:0;border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-color:#c97f78;padding:var(--space-3);background:var(--color-bg)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78\">7 · Emergency trigger</span><span style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Glucose <b>&lt; 70</b> with symptoms, a patient who can't swallow, Kussmaul breathing with ketones, new confusion or seizures, potassium falling during insulin therapy.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-2);border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:12px\">\n<span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Cause and effect</span>\n<div style=\"font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-200);line-height:1.7\">No insulin action<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>glucose can't enter cells<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>blood glucose rises → osmotic diuresis<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>dehydration + electrolyte loss<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>fat burned → ketones → metabolic acidosis<span style=\"color:var(--color-accent-400);margin:0 6px\">→</span>fluids first, then IV insulin, with potassium watched</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Hypoglycemia vs hyperglycemia (Table 56.8)</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Low: <b>cool, clammy, sweaty</b> skin, no dehydration, anxious, shaky, hungry, tachycardia; glucose <b>&lt; 70</b>; ketones negative. High: <b>warm, dry</b> skin, <b>dehydration</b>, Kussmaul breathing with <b>fruity breath</b> in DKA; glucose <b>&gt; 180</b>; ketones positive in DKA.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">DKA vs HHS (Table 56.9)</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">DKA: <b>sudden</b>, glucose <b>&gt; 300</b>, <b>ketones positive</b>, pH <b>&lt; 7.35</b>, HCO₃⁻ <b>&lt; 15</b>. HHS: <b>gradual</b>, glucose <b>&gt; 600</b>, osmolarity <b>&gt; 320</b>, <b>no ketones</b>, pH <b>&gt; 7.4</b>, worse <b>neurologic</b> signs; mostly older adults with type 2.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Dawn vs Somogyi</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Both = high morning glucose. <b>Dawn</b>: adrenal hormones at night → give <b>more</b> overnight insulin (NPH at 10 p.m.). <b>Somogyi</b>: a night-time <b>low</b> triggers a rebound → bedtime snack and <b>less</b> insulin. Tell them apart by checking glucose <b>during the night</b>.</div></div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Why the drug makes sense</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Metformin lowers liver glucose output without forcing insulin out, so it rarely causes lows alone; <b>sulfonylureas</b> squeeze insulin out of beta cells, so they <b>do</b> cause lows. SGLT2 inhibitors dump glucose in urine → dehydration, UTIs, yeast. <a href=\"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html\">Drug cards →</a></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-2)\">\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-2-400);margin-bottom:4px\">Monitor</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Glucose before meals and at bedtime (and with symptoms), A1C, intake and output and weight, potassium during insulin therapy, mental status, feet and skin, BP and urine albumin.</div></div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid #c97f78;padding:var(--space-3)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#c97f78;margin-bottom:4px\">Act now</div><div style=\"font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-200)\">Glucose &lt; 70 → 15-15 rule now; can't swallow → glucagon or IV dextrose; Kussmaul breathing, ketones, vomiting or confusion; urine output &lt; 30 mL/hr before potassium; seizures or pupil changes in HHS.</div></div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:clamp(16px,3vw,26px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Down to the physiology</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,280px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Insulin — the hormone of plenty</b> — beta cells release insulin when glucose rises. It binds <b>membrane receptors</b> and opens cells to glucose (the \"key\"), especially skeletal and heart muscle. In the liver it <b>stores glycogen</b> (glycogenesis), <b>stops glycogen breakdown</b> (glycogenolysis), <b>stops gluconeogenesis</b> and <b>stops ketogenesis</b>; it builds protein and fat. Released in two patterns: a low steady <b>basal</b> amount, and a <b>prandial</b> burst within <b>10 minutes</b> of eating.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Glucagon and the balancing hormones</b> — <b>glucagon</b> (alpha cells), the \"hormone of starvation\", releases liver glucose when intake is low. <b>Epinephrine, norepinephrine, growth hormone and cortisol</b> also raise glucose. When glucose falls, insulin stops and glucagon rises; if liver glucose runs out, fat and muscle protein are broken down for fuel. <b>During a fast (no food for 8+ hours, or NPO)</b> insulin is suppressed, and the liver (and kidneys) hold glucose up by <b>glycogenolysis</b> (stored glycogen → glucose) and <b>gluconeogenesis</b> (amino acids and lactate → new glucose) — the two processes that directly prevent a low. Glycogenesis and lipogenesis store fuel instead, and ketogenesis makes acid fuel, not glucose.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Incretins</b> — gut hormones such as <b>GLP-1</b>, released when food reaches the stomach, <b>increase insulin</b>, <b>suppress glucagon</b> and <b>slow stomach emptying</b> — preventing spikes after meals. Several drugs copy or prolong them.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Without insulin (Box 56.2)</b> — less glycogen storage, <b>more glycogen breakdown, more gluconeogenesis, more lipolysis, more ketogenesis, protein breakdown</b> — the body acts as if it is starving while glucose piles up in the blood.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>The three Ps</b> — <b>polyuria</b>: glucose in the urine pulls water with it (<b>osmotic diuresis</b>), taking electrolytes too → dehydration → <b>polydipsia</b> (thirst). Cells get no glucose → <b>polyphagia</b> (hunger), yet the untreated patient stays in metabolic starvation.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Ketones, acid and Kussmaul breathing</b> — burning fat yields <b>ketone bodies</b> — acids. Dehydration adds hemoconcentration, poor perfusion and <b>lactic acid</b>. The result is <b>anion-gap metabolic acidosis</b>: the brain drives <b>deep, rapid Kussmaul breathing</b> to blow off CO₂, the breath smells of acetone (<b>fruity</b>), and ABGs show low pH, low HCO₃⁻ and a compensating low PaCO₂.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><b>Potassium can be low, normal or high</b> — urine losses drain total-body potassium (<b>low</b>), but acidosis shifts potassium <b>out of cells</b> in exchange for hydrogen ions, so the serum level may read <b>high or normal</b>. Insulin, correcting acidosis and fluids drive potassium back into cells — the serum level <b>falls fast</b> during treatment.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>The \"starvation in the middle of plenty\" picture explains every acute finding: glucose is everywhere except inside the cells. The body answers starvation by burning fat (ketones, acidosis), breaking down muscle (weight loss, high BUN) and releasing more glucose (making hyperglycemia worse). Insulin is the only thing that ends the cycle — and when it does, potassium follows glucose into the cells.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-types-risk-and-screening","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Types, risk and screening","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Types of diabetes (Box 56.1, Table 56.2)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Type 1 (T1DM)</b> — <b>autoimmune</b> destruction of beta cells → <b>absolute insulin deficiency</b>; viral infections (mumps, coxsackievirus, COVID-19) may trigger it. Usually before <b>age 30</b>, <b>abrupt</b> onset: thirst, hunger, polyuria, <b>weight loss</b>. Ketosis-prone; <b>everyone needs insulin</b>. Same obesity rate as everyone else. Ask about family type 1, type 2 and other autoimmune disease.</div>\n<div><b>Type 2 (T2DM)</b> — <b>insulin resistance</b> first (cells respond poorly), then <b>declining beta-cell secretion</b>; strong heredity plus environment (walkability, food security, air quality, access to care). Any adult age, often <b>no symptoms</b> — or thirst, fatigue, blurred vision, or a complication is the first sign. <b>60–80% obese</b>; ketosis-resistant; <b>20–30% need insulin</b>. About <b>90–95%</b> of adults with diabetes.</div>\n<div><b>Other types</b> — <b>LADA</b> (\"type 1.5\"): slow autoimmune diabetes after 30. <b>MODY</b>: inherited single-gene loss of insulin function in younger adults — resembles type 1 (insulin, DKA risk) but <b>not autoimmune</b>. <b>Gestational (GDM)</b>: glucose intolerance first found in pregnancy — screen every pregnant woman.</div>\n<div><b>Metabolic syndrome</b> — risk factors that cluster for type 2 diabetes and heart disease: <b>waist ≥ 40 in (men) / ≥ 35 in (women)</b>; <b>fasting glucose ≥ 100</b>; <b>BP ≥ 140/90</b> <i>(the book's cutoff; many guidelines use 130/85)</i>; <b>triglycerides ≥ 150</b>; <b>HDL &lt; 40 (men) / &lt; 50 (women)</b> — or on drugs for any of these. Each speeds atherosclerosis, stroke and early death. It is diagnosed when <b>at least 3</b> of these are present (Fig. 56.5); the figure also shows darkened skin folds (neck, armpits), fatty liver, fatigue, and polycystic ovary syndrome (women) or erectile dysfunction (men).</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Who gets it and who gets screened</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Numbers</b> — about <b>11.3%</b> of the US population has diabetes (37.3 million; <b>23% undiagnosed</b>); <b>96 million</b> have <b>prediabetes</b> (impaired fasting glucose, impaired glucose tolerance or <b>A1C 5.7–6.4</b>), with a <b>5–15 times</b> higher risk of type 2 within 3–5 years. Over <b>40% of hospitalized</b> patients have diabetes. Type 2 affects 12.3% of adults over 20 and <b>25.9% over 65</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Health equity</b> — higher rates and burden in <b>American Indian/Alaska Native (14.5%)</b>, <b>non-Hispanic Black (12.1%)</b>, <b>Hispanic (11.8%)</b> and Asian (9.5%) people than non-Hispanic White people (7.4%). Be alert at every assessment and <b>advocate for early screening</b>. The CDC <b>National Diabetes Prevention Program</b> offers affordable lifestyle coaching for people with prediabetes in under-resourced communities.</div>\n<div><b>Veterans</b> — about <b>25%</b> of veterans have diabetes; <b>Agent Orange (dioxin)</b> exposure in Korea and Vietnam may add risk — assess at every visit.</div>\n<div><b>Screening (Box 56.3)</b> — test adults of <b>any age</b> with <b>BMI &gt; 25 (&gt; 23 in Asian Americans)</b> plus a risk factor: a <b>first-degree relative</b>, inactivity, high-risk ethnic group, <b>hypertension</b>, <b>HDL &lt; 35 or triglycerides &gt; 250</b>, <b>polycystic ovary syndrome</b>, cardiovascular disease. <b>Prediabetes → test yearly.</b> <b>GDM history → test every 3 years for life.</b> Everyone else starts at <b>35</b>, repeated at least every <b>3 years</b> if normal.</div>\n<div><b>Prevention and health promotion</b> — nothing prevents type 1. <b>Diet, activity and weight loss</b> prevent or delay type 2: carbohydrates from <b>vegetables, fruit, whole grains, legumes and dairy</b>, at least <b>25 g of fiber</b> a day, <b>no sugar-sweetened drinks</b>, less saturated fat, and losing <b>5–10% of body weight</b> if overweight. Teach: glucose in target delays complications; <b>yearly eye exam</b> by an ophthalmologist; <b>yearly urine albumin</b>; healthy weight; <b>at least 150 minutes of activity a week</b>; <b>daily foot inspection</b> and prompt reporting of sores; stop smoking; limit alcohol.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Type 1 and type 2 end in the same complications but begin differently: type 1 is an empty factory (no insulin at all, so ketones form quickly and DKA is the danger), type 2 is a factory whose product is ignored (some insulin remains, enough to prevent ketones — so the danger is the slow dehydration of HHS and years of silent damage before diagnosis). That is why so much of type 2 is found by <b>screening</b>, not symptoms.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-diagnosing-and-monitoring-glucose-a1c-cg","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Diagnosing and monitoring glucose — A1C, CGM, meters","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Diagnosis (Table 56.3, Box 56.4)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The numbers</b> — <b>A1C</b>: normal 4–5.7% · prediabetes <b>5.7–6.4%</b> · diabetes <b>≥ 6.5%</b>. <b>Fasting glucose</b> (no calories for 8 hours): normal 74–100 · prediabetes <b>100–125</b> · diabetes <b>≥ 126</b>. <b>2-hour OGTT</b> (75 g glucose): normal &lt; 140 · prediabetes <b>140–199</b> · diabetes <b>≥ 200</b>. <b>Random glucose &gt; 200</b> with classic symptoms or a hyperglycemic crisis. Fig. 56.1 gives <b>74–106</b> as the normal fasting range — the reference range the book's questions quote; a low is still treated at <b>&lt; 70</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Confirming it</b> — without obvious hyperglycemia, <b>two abnormal results</b> are needed (same sample or two samples) to rule out lab error. The OGTT is sensitive but used mainly for <b>gestational</b> diabetes. Screening usually uses <b>A1C plus fasting glucose</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Ongoing monitoring</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>A1C</b> — glucose binds hemoglobin (<b>glycosylation</b>): the higher glucose runs, the more is bound. Reflects about <b>120 days</b> (the red cell lifespan) and is <b>not changed by what was eaten the day before</b>. Check at least <b>twice a year</b> when stable, <b>every 3 months</b> after a therapy change or when out of target. Target: <b>&lt; 7%</b> for most nonpregnant adults without significant lows; self-management glucose targets are <b>set individually</b> (diabetes duration, age and life expectancy, other illnesses, severity of heart disease, hypoglycemia unawareness). Average glucose by A1C (Table 56.4): 6 ≈ 126 mg/dL, <b>7 ≈ 154</b>, 8 ≈ 183, 9 ≈ 212, 10 ≈ 240, <b>11 ≈ 269</b>, <b>12 ≈ 298</b> — each 1% adds about 28–29 mg/dL.</div>\n<div><b>Continuous glucose monitoring (CGM)</b> — a sensor under the skin measures <b>interstitial</b> glucose every 1–5 minutes and shows it on a receiver or phone, with <b>trend arrows</b> and alarms; sensors last <b>6–14 days</b>. <b>Time in range (70–180 mg/dL)</b> goal: <b>70%</b> of the time. Recommended for all type 1 and type 2 on insulin. Interstitial glucose <b>lags up to 30 minutes</b> behind blood when glucose changes fast → <b>confirm extremes or low symptoms with a fingerstick</b> before treating. Most sensors need <b>at least 2 fingerstick calibrations a day</b> (accuracy depends on them); with older systems, <b>give insulin only after confirming</b> the CGM reading.</div>\n<div><b>Blood glucose monitoring (BGM)</b> — a meter and capillary blood. Targets: <b>before meals 80–130</b>, <b>peak after meals &lt; 180</b>. Test more often with symptoms, <b>hypoglycemic unawareness</b>, illness, around <b>exercise</b>, gastroparesis, drug changes, steroids, pregnancy. Insulin users on multiple doses or a pump: <b>3 or more times a day</b>; fewer injections, non-insulin drugs or no drugs: a <b>daily</b> check helps judge therapy.</div>\n<div><b>Getting a true reading</b> — <b>anemia falsely raises</b> and <b>polycythemia falsely lowers</b> meter readings; meters are unreliable at very low and very high values; <b>lab glucose is more accurate</b>. Common errors: too small a drop, badly stored or <b>expired strips</b>, wrong code, expired meter. Teach with demonstration and <b>teach-back</b>, check vision, retrain over time.</div>\n<div><b>Alternate sites and infection control</b> — arm, thigh or hand readings lag when glucose changes fast — <b>the fingertip is the only safe choice then</b>, and patients with <b>hypoglycemic unawareness should not use alternate sites</b>. Wash hands, <b>never reuse lancets</b>, <b>never share</b> a meter or lancet holder (infection spreads even with a new lancet), clean the meter; staff and family <b>wear gloves</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Using the results</b> — after-meal checks show the effect of a meal; checks around exercise show how to exercise safely. If readings are <b>out of range for 3 days</b> with no change in food, drugs or activity, make the agreed adjustment.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>A1C works because glucose sticks to hemoglobin and stays stuck for the life of the red cell — a 3-month memory that cannot be faked by a careful day before the appointment. A fingerstick is a snapshot and CGM is a movie; the movie arrives a few minutes late, which is why a scary CGM number gets a fingerstick before anyone treats it.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-chronic-complications-vessels-eyes-nerve","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Chronic complications — vessels, eyes, nerves, kidneys","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">How the damage happens</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The mechanism</b> — chronic hyperglycemia <b>thickens basement membranes</b>, glucose is directly <b>toxic</b> to cells, and small-vessel ischemia causes <b>tissue hypoxia</b>. Hyperglycemia is the main cause of <b>microvascular</b> damage and speeds <b>macrovascular</b> disease. Smoking, inactivity, obesity, hypertension and high lipids add to it. <b>Every 1-point drop in A1C</b> markedly reduces kidney and eye complications.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Large vessels (macrovascular)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Heart disease</b> — diabetes, prediabetes and metabolic syndrome raise cardiovascular risk; albuminuria and retinopathy predict coronary deaths. Targets (the book's cardiovascular section): <b>BP &lt; 140/90</b> (<b>120/80</b> in younger adults if reachable without excessive burden) — the kidney section gives <b>&lt; 140/80</b> (see Protecting the kidneys); <b>LDL &lt; 100</b>, or <b>&lt; 70 with cardiovascular disease</b>; lipid screen at diagnosis, then every 5 years under 40 or every 1–2 years. Omega-3s, fiber, plant sterols, weight loss, activity; stop smoking, manage stress, take prescribed lipid drugs and aspirin.</div>\n<div><b>Stroke and infection</b> — stroke risk is <b>2–4 times higher</b>, with more severe carotid atherosclerosis; hypertension, high lipids, nephropathy, peripheral vascular disease, alcohol and tobacco raise it further. Vascular changes plus high glucose <b>weaken white cells</b>, impair tissue oxygen and feed microbes → <b>more infections</b>, which turn serious fast and can lead to <b>sepsis</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Small vessels (microvascular)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Retinopathy</b> — blindness is <b>25 times more common</b>; after <b>20 years</b> nearly everyone has some retinopathy, with <b>few symptoms until vision is lost</b>. <b>Nonproliferative</b>: leaky, blocked vessels, edema, hard exudates, hemorrhages — slow, rarely blinding. <b>Proliferative</b>: hypoxic retina grows <b>new fragile vessels</b> (neovascularization) that <b>bleed</b> → vision loss. <b>Macular edema</b> is the main cause of vision loss. Also earlier <b>cataracts</b> and more <b>open-angle glaucoma</b>. High glucose blurs vision temporarily — <b>check refraction only once glucose is normal</b>. Prevent with glucose, BP and lipid control and <b>yearly eye exams</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults and vision</b> — age-related changes add to retinopathy: blurred, distorted or fluctuating vision and loss of color perception. Check whether they can <b>draw up insulin and read the meter</b>; offer <b>adaptive devices</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Peripheral neuropathy (DPN)</b> — progressive, <b>bilateral, permanent</b> nerve damage: sensory fibers → <b>pain first, then numbness</b>; motor fibers → weakness and <b>foot deformities</b>. Causes: hyperglycemia and high lipids, damaged vessels (nerve <b>hypoxia</b>), genes, smoking and alcohol; excess glucose becomes <b>sorbitol</b>, which collects in nerves. Table 56.1: <b>paresthesias</b> (burning, tingling from the toes up), <b>dysesthesias</b>, <b>loss of sensation</b>; high arch, <b>claw toes, hammertoes</b>, weight shifted to the metatarsal heads and toe tips.</div>\n<div><b>Autonomic neuropathy</b> — <b>heart and vessels (CAN)</b>: <b>orthostatic hypotension and syncope</b> (falls), exercise intolerance, <b>painless MI</b>. <b>Skin</b>: no sweating → dry, cracking skin. <b>GI</b>: reflux, <b>gastroparesis</b> (early fullness, bloating, nausea, vomiting; a cause of <b>hypoglycemia</b> because food arrives later than the insulin), bacterial overgrowth, <b>constipation — the most common GI problem</b> — alternating with diarrhea, nocturnal diarrhea, sweating while eating (Table 56.1). <b>Bladder</b>: incomplete emptying, <b>retention → UTIs</b>; frequency and urgency first, later no sense of fullness and incontinence. <b>Erectile dysfunction</b>. <b>Loss of hypoglycemia warning signs</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Nephropathy</b> — diabetes is the <b>leading cause of end-stage kidney disease</b> in the US. Hyperglycemia raises pressure in kidney vessels; the <b>glomerulus leaks albumin</b>; narrowed vessels starve kidney cells → CKD. <b>Hypertension speeds it</b>. Shown by rising <b>albumin excretion</b> and falling <b>GFR</b>; delayed by glucose control.</div>\n<div><b>Sexual and cognitive function</b> — nerve and vessel damage → <b>erectile dysfunction and retrograde ejaculation</b>; in women, less lubrication, painful intercourse, lower libido. Older adults with diabetes have a <b>higher risk of all dementias</b> (neuron damage, brain atrophy).</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Neuropathy is why the diabetic foot is so dangerous: sensory loss removes the warning (a stone in the shoe never hurts), motor loss deforms the foot and creates pressure points, autonomic loss dries and cracks the skin, and poor perfusion keeps the wound from healing. Each nerve type takes away one layer of protection — so foot care has to replace every layer by deliberate habit.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-antidiabetic-drugs-non-insulin","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Antidiabetic drugs (non-insulin)","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Principles (Table 56.5)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>When and how</b> — drugs are added for type 2 when <b>diet, activity and stress management</b> don't reach targets — always <b>with</b> them, never instead. Start at the <b>lowest effective dose</b> and increase; often more than one class. Choice depends on cost, side effects, ability to manage doses (once-daily or weekly helps adherence). Beta cells decline over time, so many eventually need <b>insulin</b> — indicated when 2–3 agents (including a GLP-1 agonist) fail, or <b>A1C &gt; 10% with symptoms</b>.</div>\n<div><b>For every antidiabetic drug</b> — teach the <b>signs of hypoglycemia</b> — hunger, headache, tremors, sweating, confusion — because many lower glucose even when it isn't high; and <b>check with the provider or pharmacist before any OTC drug, vitamin or supplement</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The classes</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Metformin (biguanide) — first line</b> — <b>lowers liver glucose production</b>, reduces intestinal glucose absorption and <b>improves insulin sensitivity</b>. <b>Take with food</b> (GI upset is common). <b>No alcohol</b> (lactic acidosis). Not for anyone with <b>kidney disease</b>. <b>Hold 24 hours before iodinated contrast or surgery with anesthesia</b>, and restart only when <b>kidney function is confirmed normal</b> — risk of lactic acidosis and acute kidney injury.</div>\n<div><b>GLP-1 agonists (incretin mimetics)</b> — <b>dulaglutide, exenatide, liraglutide, lixisenatide, semaglutide</b> act like the gut hormone: <b>less glucagon</b>, less liver glucose, <b>slower stomach emptying</b>. Mostly <b>subcutaneous</b> (semaglutide also oral) — teach injection. <b>Extended-release exenatide, dulaglutide and semaglutide are once WEEKLY</b> — stress the schedule to avoid overdose. Report persistent abdominal pain and nausea: <b>pancreatitis</b> risk; <b>contraindicated in gastroparesis</b>.</div>\n<div><b>SGLT2 inhibitors</b> — <b>canagliflozin, dapagliflozin, empagliflozin, ertugliflozin</b>: the kidney <b>can't reabsorb filtered glucose and sodium</b> → they leave in urine. Teach signs of <b>dehydration</b> (thirst, light-headedness, dry mouth, orthostatic hypotension), <b>hyponatremia</b> (weakness, cramps, fast heart, orthostasis), <b>UTI</b>, <b>genital yeast infection</b>, and report genital or perineal swelling, tenderness or redness — <b>Fournier gangrene</b>.</div>\n<div><b>DPP-4 inhibitors (gliptins)</b> — <b>alogliptin, linagliptin, saxagliptin, sitagliptin</b> (oral) stop the enzyme that breaks down GLP-1 and GIP, so natural incretins last longer; modest effect, <b>second line</b>. Watch for <b>rash and allergy</b>; <b>pancreatitis</b>; <b>heart failure</b> with saxagliptin and alogliptin (dyspnea, cough lying down, sudden weight gain).</div>\n<div><b>Pancreatitis warning</b> — with GLP-1 agonists and DPP-4 inhibitors, report at once: <b>jaundice</b>; sudden <b>intense abdominal pain radiating to the back, left flank or left shoulder</b>; <b>gray-blue discoloration</b> of the abdomen or around the navel.</div>\n<div><b>Insulin stimulators (secretagogues)</b> — <b>sulfonylureas</b> (<b>glipizide, glyburide, glimepiride</b>) and <b>meglitinides</b> (repaglinide, nateglinide) <b>release preformed insulin</b> from beta cells — only work if some beta-cell function remains. Sulfonylureas lower <b>fasting</b> glucose; meglitinides mainly boost <b>meal-related</b> insulin release. <b>Can cause hypoglycemia</b> (careful with insulin). Take <b>with or just before meals</b>. Sulfonylureas <b>interact with many drugs</b> — check before any OTC drug.</div>\n<div><b>TZDs (glitazones)</b> — <b>pioglitazone, rosiglitazone</b> improve <b>insulin receptor sensitivity</b> and lower liver glucose output. <b>Box warning</b>: not for <b>symptomatic heart failure</b> or certain cardiovascular disease; also more <b>heart-related deaths</b>, <b>fractures</b> and <b>macular edema</b>. With heart disease, <b>weigh daily</b> and report <b>&gt; 2 lb (1 kg) in a day or &gt; 4 lb (2 kg) in a week</b>; report vision changes at once; expect weight gain and edema.</div>\n<div><b>Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors</b> — <b>acarbose, miglitol</b> slow <b>carbohydrate digestion</b> in the intestine → no after-meal spike. Take <b>only with a meal</b>. Bloating, <b>flatulence</b>, diarrhea are common. No hypoglycemia <b>unless</b> combined with a sulfonylurea or insulin.</div>\n<div><b>Amylin analog — pramlintide</b> — copies amylin (made with insulin, <b>lacking in type 1</b>): <b>less glucagon, slower stomach emptying, satiety</b> → weight loss. Injected; for patients on insulin. Nausea and vomiting common. <b>Never mix in the same syringe as insulin</b> — incompatible pH.</div>\n<div><b>Combination products</b> — two mechanisms in one pill (e.g., dapagliflozin with metformin ER) — effective, fewer prescriptions, lower cost.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Sort the drugs by <b>where they act</b> and the warnings write themselves. Liver (metformin) → lactic acid if kidneys can't clear it. Beta cell (sulfonylureas) → lows, because insulin is forced out whether or not glucose is high. Gut and stomach (GLP-1, DPP-4, amylin, acarbose) → nausea, gas, pancreatitis. Kidney (SGLT2) → whatever leaves in the urine: water, sodium, and sugar that feeds bacteria and yeast. Receptor (TZD) → fluid retention and heart failure.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-insulin-types-timing-injection-and-stora","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Insulin — types, timing, injection and storage","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Types and regimens (Table 56.6)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Who and why injected</b> — all type 1 and <b>20–30% of type 2</b>. Insulin is a small <b>protein</b> destroyed in the GI tract, so it is <b>injected</b> (or inhaled). Older adults: poor vision, coordination or memory raise <b>dosing errors</b>. Concentrations U-100, U-200, U-300 and <b>U-500</b> (only for very large doses). <b>Insulin is a High-Alert drug.</b> <b>Analogs</b> (e.g., lispro) are human insulin with an altered structure that changes absorption and duration. The insulin type, injection technique and site all change its action, so the type is <b>changed only under the diabetes provider's supervision</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Timing by type</b> — <b>Rapid-acting</b> analogs — aspart (onset 10–20 min, peak 1–3 h, lasts 3–5 h), glulisine (10–15 min, peak 1–1.5 h, 3–5 h), <b>lispro</b> (15–30 min, peak 0.5–2.5 h, 3–6 h). <b>Short-acting regular</b>: onset <b>30–60 min</b>, peak <b>1–5 h</b>, lasts <b>6–10 h</b>. <b>Intermediate NPH</b>: onset 1–2 h, peak <b>6–14 h</b>, lasts 16–24 h. <b>Long-acting glargine</b> (onset 70 min, <b>no peak</b>, 18–24 h) and <b>detemir</b> (onset 1–2 h, no peak, 12–24 h). <b>Ultra-long degludec</b>: onset 30–90 min, <b>no peak</b>, 24+ h.</div>\n<div><b>Basal and bolus</b> — regimens copy the pancreas: <b>basal</b> insulin (NPH, glargine, detemir, degludec) about <b>50%</b> of the daily dose to cover between meals and overnight, plus <b>prandial</b> rapid-acting or regular before meals. Lower starting doses for older or very thin patients. The fasting (before-breakfast) glucose shows whether <b>basal</b> is right.</div>\n<div><b>Regimens</b> — <b>Once daily</b>: one intermediate, long or ultra-long injection (often with oral agents or a GLP-1 in type 2). <b>Two-dose mixed</b>: regular + NPH, <b>two-thirds before breakfast, one-third before the evening meal</b>; NPH's peak may require a <b>bedtime snack</b> to prevent night-time lows. <b>Multiple daily injections (intensified)</b>: basal plus a bolus before each meal plus <b>correction doses</b>, guided by up to <b>8 glucose checks a day</b> (2 hours after meals and within 10 minutes before the next).</div>\n<div><b>Skipped doses</b> — an occasional skip may follow an unusual meal or exercise day; if doses are skipped <b>often</b>, ask about barriers: <b>cost, fear of lows, fear of needles, needing help, embarrassment</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Giving it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Site and absorption</b> — <b>abdomen absorbs fastest</b> and is preferred — <b>except within 2 inches of the navel</b>; other usual areas are the upper arms, thighs and buttocks (Fig. 56.7). <b>Rotate within one area</b> (not area to area) so absorption is steady day to day; rotating lets each spot <b>heal completely</b> before it is used again. Longer-acting and larger doses absorb less predictably. <b>Heat, massage and exercising the injected limb speed absorption</b>; <b>scar tissue slows it</b> (and hurts less, which tempts reuse).</div>\n<div><b>Depth and angle</b> — <b>subcutaneous</b> — IM is faster and not used routinely. Lightly pinch and inject at <b>90°</b>; <b>45° for very thin or frail older adults</b>. <b>No aspiration.</b> Overweight patients can use <b>4–5 mm needles at 90° without pinching</b>. Insulin syringe needles come in <b>28, 29, 30 and 31 gauge</b> and <b>6, 8 and 12.7 mm</b> lengths.</div>\n<div><b>Lag time before meals</b> — <b>rapid-acting</b>: within <b>10 minutes before</b> eating when glucose is in range — or just before, or even right after the meal. <b>Regular</b>: <b>20–30 minutes before</b> eating. Glucose <b>high</b> → lengthen the lag; glucose <b>low</b> → inject right before eating (delay rapid-acting until after the meal).</div>\n<div><b>Mixing</b> — regular + NPH mixtures can give a more normal response in some patients, but mixing changes the <b>time of peak action</b>, and the response can differ from the same insulins given separately. <b>Never mix anything with glargine, detemir or premixed insulins (e.g., Humalog Mix 75/25)</b>; never mix pramlintide with insulin. <i>(Not in this chapter:)</i> when regular and NPH share a syringe, inject air into the NPH vial, then into the regular vial, and draw <b>regular (clear) first, then NPH (cloudy)</b>, so NPH never gets into the regular vial.</div>\n<div><b>Drawing up from a vial (Box 56.5)</b> — wash hands; check type and expiration; <b>gently roll</b> intermediate (NPH) insulin; clean the stopper; inject <b>air equal to the dose</b> into the vial; invert and draw the dose; remove bubbles and re-measure; use a site <b>not used in the past month</b>; clean, pinch, <b>90°</b>; push the plunger fully; release; pull straight out — <b>don't rub</b>; discard the syringe <b>uncapped in a puncture-proof container</b>. Syringes come in <b>1 mL (100 units), ½ mL (50 units) and 3/10 mL (30 units)</b>; the unit scale differs by size and maker, so always buy the <b>same type of syringe</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Pens (Box 56.5)</b> — check the label; NPH evenly mixed; wipe the tip, screw on the needle, remove both caps; <b>prime (\"air shot\") until a drop appears</b>; dial the dose; hold perpendicular, press slowly, <b>hold 6–10 seconds</b>; remove the needle into a hard container. Pens suit <b>poor vision</b> (each unit clicks). <b>ISMP alert</b>: a pen display read <b>upside down</b> (left-handed users) turns 52 into 25 and 12 into 21.</div>\n<div><b>Pumps and other devices</b> — <b>smart pens</b> (app calculates doses from insulin-to-carb ratio, correction factor and <b>insulin on board</b>). <b>Insulin pump (CSII)</b>: rapid-acting insulin every hour as basal plus meal boluses; flexible meals; <b>change the infusion set every 2–3 days</b> (skin infection); <b>DKA risk</b> from infection, blocked tubing or pump failure — <b>check ketones when glucose &gt; 300</b>; taking the pump off (shower, swim) can cause hyperglycemia, so have a backup insulin plan. <b>Automated insulin delivery</b> links a pump and CGM with an algorithm. <b>Inhaled</b> rapid insulin (onset ~12 min) only for nonsmokers without lung disease, after pulmonary testing; can cause bronchospasm.</div>\n<div><b>Dawn vs Somogyi (fasting hyperglycemia)</b> — <b>dawn phenomenon</b>: night-time adrenal hormones raise glucose around <b>5–6 a.m.</b> → more overnight insulin (e.g., evening NPH moved to <b>10 p.m.</b>). <b>Somogyi</b>: morning high <b>rebounding from a night-time low</b> → adequate <b>bedtime snack</b>, review insulin and exercise. Both are diagnosed by <b>checking glucose during the night</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Storage and inspection</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Storage</b> — <b>refrigerate insulin not in use</b>; the vial or pen <b>in use can stay at room temperature up to 28 days</b> (cold insulin stings). Never below <b>36°F (2.2°C)</b> or above <b>86°F (30°C)</b>, <b>never frozen</b>, no direct heat or light, no hard shaking. <b>Discard after 28 days</b>. Keep a <b>spare</b> of each type. Prefilled syringes: stable about <b>30 days refrigerated</b>, stored <b>needle up or flat</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Inspect before every dose</b> — look for <b>clumping, frosting, precipitate, or change in clarity or color</b> = lost potency → use another vial or pen. <b>NPH is uniformly cloudy after gentle rolling; every other insulin should be clear.</b> Needles and syringes are <b>single use</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Insulin types differ only in how fast they get from the injection site into the blood, so everything that changes blood flow at the site — heat, massage, exercise, scar tissue — changes the dose the patient actually receives. That is why rotation stays within one area, why the abdomen is preferred (steady blood flow), and why a hot shower after an injection can cause an unexpected low.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-eating-and-exercise-with-diabetes","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Eating and exercise with diabetes","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Nutrition therapy</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>The approach</b> — <b>medical nutrition therapy</b> for everyone, planned with the <b>registered dietitian nutritionist (RDN)</b>, built around <b>culture, money, food access and lifestyle</b>. <b>Consistent timing and amounts</b> help control glucose; insulin users eat in step with their insulin's action. No ideal split of carbohydrate, protein and fat — it is individualized. Overweight: <b>losing 5–10% of body weight</b> markedly improves A1C; weight-loss drugs for <b>BMI ≥ 27</b> (≥ 25 in Asian individuals). Screen for <b>food insecurity</b> and refer. The RDN sees each patient <b>yearly</b> (more often with low health literacy); share diet teaching with <b>whoever prepares the meals</b>, and teach how to adjust food: <b>illness</b> → sick-day rules (fluids every hour; if solids won't stay down, carbohydrate as liquids or soft foods, <b>at least 150 g a day</b>); <b>exercise</b> → unplanned activity needs <b>about 10–15 g of carbohydrate per hour</b>, planned activity a lower insulin dose; <b>social events</b> → alcohol only with food; <b>delayed meals</b> → a late meal while insulin or a sulfonylurea is working causes lows, so carry a carbohydrate snack and glucose.</div>\n<div><b>Goals of nutrition therapy (Box 56.6)</b> — glucose in — or as close as possible to — the <b>target range</b>; a <b>lipid profile</b> that lowers cardiovascular risk; <b>BP</b> normal or near normal; <b>prevent or slow complications</b> by changing food and lifestyle; meet nutrition needs while respecting <b>personal and cultural preferences, food access and willingness to change</b>; cover special life stages (<b>pregnancy, breastfeeding, older age</b>); and, for patients on <b>insulin or insulin stimulators</b>, self-management training to <b>exercise safely</b>, <b>prevent and treat hypoglycemia</b> and manage diabetes during <b>acute illness</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Carbohydrates, fats, alcohol</b> — carbs from <b>vegetables, fruits, whole grains, legumes, dairy</b>; at least <b>25 g of fiber</b> a day; <b>avoid sugar-sweetened drinks</b> and sucrose. Fat quality over quantity: <b>Mediterranean</b> pattern (avocados, nuts, seeds, olives), <b>omega-3s</b> from fish or plants; limit trans and saturated fat as for everyone. Alcohol: up to <b>2 drinks for men, 1 for women</b>, with and in addition to the usual meal plan (<b>1 drink = 12 oz beer, 5 oz wine or 1½ oz spirits</b>), <b>with or just after a meal</b> — it blocks liver glucose output and causes <b>delayed hypoglycemia</b> hours later (worse with insulin or sulfonylureas); have someone with them who can help.</div>\n<div><b>Carb counting</b> — counts <b>total grams of carbohydrate</b> (labels, weighing, estimating when dining out). Patients on intensive insulin or a pump use an <b>insulin-to-carbohydrate ratio</b> — a usual start is <b>1 unit of rapid-acting insulin per 15 g</b> — adjusted by the 2-hour after-meal or next pre-meal glucose. Patients on <b>fixed doses</b> must keep carb amounts and meal times <b>consistent</b>.</div>\n<div><b>MyPlate</b> — for low health literacy or numeracy: <b>half the plate non-starchy vegetables</b> (a little fruit optional), <b>a quarter lean protein, a quarter whole grains</b>, tailored to the person's foods.</div>\n<div><b>Weight with insulin</b> — intensive insulin can cause <b>hyperinsulinemia and weight gain</b>: follow the meal plan, exercise, and <b>don't over-treat lows</b>; sometimes calories are restricted rather than insulin increased.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults</b> — dental problems, money, appetite and shopping or cooking limits threaten nutrition; they sense lows, highs and dehydration less (<b>HHS risk</b>). Changing 60–70 years of habits needs realism: teach <b>same meal and snack times and same amounts every day</b>; home health visits help.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Physical activity</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Why and how much</b> — activity improves <b>insulin sensitivity</b>, glucose control, lipids (raises HDL), BP and heart function, and prevents or delays type 2. Aim for <b>at least 150 minutes a week of moderate aerobic activity over 5 days</b>, <b>no more than 2 days in a row without it</b>, and <b>resistance exercise twice a week</b>. <b>5–10 minute warm-up and cool-down</b>.</div>\n<div><b>What exercise does to glucose</b> — without enough insulin (uncontrolled type 1), glucagon and epinephrine pour out liver glucose while cells can't use it → exercise makes glucose <b>and ketones rise</b>. With insulin on board, muscles take up glucose and the liver holds back → <b>hypoglycemia during and up to 24 hours after</b> exercise. Hard anaerobic bursts (powerlifting, boxing) can raise glucose afterward.</div>\n<div><b>Safety rules (Box 56.7)</b> — check glucose <b>before, during and after</b>. <b>Urine ketones present → do NOT exercise.</b> Eat carbohydrate to get glucose <b>above 100</b> first; on insulin, exercise only with glucose <b>100–250</b> — <b>type 1: vigorous exercise only at 100–250 with no ketones</b>. <b>Planned</b> exercise → <b>lower the insulin dose</b>; <b>unplanned</b> → <b>extra carbohydrate</b> (about <b>10–15 g per hour</b> of moderate activity for a 70-kg adult). Snack first if more than an hour since eating or if intense. <b>Not within 1 hour of an injection or near peak action.</b> Carry <b>simple sugar</b> and <b>diabetes ID</b>; stay hydrated; avoid extreme heat or cold; <b>check feet</b> after.</div>\n<div><b>When complications are present</b> — <b>retinopathy</b>: avoid vigorous aerobic or resistance work, <b>heavy lifting, rapid head movement or jarring</b> (vitreous hemorrhage, retinal detachment). <b>Neuropathy</b>: proper footwear, daily foot checks; a foot injury or open sore → <b>non-weight-bearing</b> exercise (swimming, cycling, arm work). <b>Autonomic neuropathy</b>: poor temperature control, postural hypotension and impaired thirst → start short and gentle. Older adults: change activity <b>gradually</b> (a physical therapist evaluation may be needed); start with <b>short low-intensity sessions (under 5–10 min)</b> with warm-up, cool-down and active stretching — chair yoga, walking, water aerobics, bands — in safe places to prevent falls.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Ketones in the urine mean the cells are already starved of insulin — exercise would only add more stress hormones and push glucose and ketones higher, toward DKA. Without ketones, the same exercise works like extra insulin, which is why the danger flips to hypoglycemia for up to a day afterward. The ketone check is what tells you which way exercise will push.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Hypoglycemia — the 15-15 rule","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Recognizing it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What happens</b> — below <b>70 mg/dL</b> the body releases balancing hormones and fires the autonomic nervous system. Normally insulin falls at about <b>83</b> and glucagon and epinephrine switch on at about <b>67</b> — before symptoms. The <b>brain can't make glucose</b> and stores only minutes' worth, so low glucose quickly becomes a brain problem.</div>\n<div><b>Symptoms (Box 56.10)</b> — <b>neurogenic (the early warning)</b> — adrenergic: <b>shaky, pounding heart, nervous</b>; cholinergic: <b>sweaty, hungry, tingling</b>. <b>Neuroglycopenic (the brain running out)</b>: weakness, fatigue, <b>difficulty thinking, confusion, behavior change</b>, emotional instability, <b>seizures, loss of consciousness</b>, brain damage, death. Skin is <b>cool and clammy</b>; no dehydration; ketones negative.</div>\n<div><b>Severity and unawareness</b> — <b>mild</b>: alert and able to self-treat. <b>Severe</b>: needs <b>another person's</b> help. Long-standing diabetes can bring <b>hypoglycemic unawareness</b> — no warning symptoms — so these patients test more and <b>never use alternate sites</b>. <b>Beta blockers mask</b> the warning signs.</div>\n<div><b>Causes</b> — too much insulin for the food and activity; insulin at the <b>wrong time</b> or the <b>wrong type</b>; <b>missed or delayed meals</b>; <b>gastroparesis</b>; <b>alcohol</b> (liver stops releasing glucose); <b>kidney failure</b> (insulin cleared slowly); <b>exercise</b>. At risk: everyone on <b>insulin or sulfonylureas</b> (alone or with metformin), especially older adults and those with liver or kidney impairment.</div>\n<div><b>Older adults</b> — slower kidney clearance of sulfonylureas and insulin, <b>weaker epinephrine and glucagon responses</b>, frequent unawareness. Family checks glucose for unsteadiness, light-headedness, poor concentration, trembling or sweating; <b>bedtime snack</b> if appetite is poor.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Treating it</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>In the hospital</b> — check glucose <b>no more than 30 minutes before</b> a meal and give rapid-acting insulin <b>just before</b> the meal; coordinate trays and insulin. Monitor before antidiabetic drugs, before meals, at bedtime and with symptoms. Inpatient lows come from the <b>wrong insulin type</b>, insulin <b>not matched to the timing of food</b>, or a <b>changed eating plan without a dose change</b>; protocols give oral carbohydrate if the patient is alert and can swallow, otherwise <b>IV dextrose or glucagon</b>.</div>\n<div><b>The 15-15 rule</b> — glucose <b>&lt; 70</b> or symptoms → <b>15 g of fast carbohydrate</b> (<b>30 g if &lt; 50</b>), liquid if they can swallow — <b>avoid high-potassium choices like orange juice</b> in the hospital. <b>Recheck in 15 minutes</b>; still low → <b>repeat</b>. Once above the low range → a <b>complex carbohydrate with protein</b> (crackers with nut butter) to prevent a repeat. <b>Chocolate and fatty sweets absorb slowly</b> — use glucose.</div>\n<div><b>15 g examples (Box 56.11)</b> — glucose tablets or gel · <b>½ cup (120 mL) juice or regular soda</b> · <b>5 hard candies</b> · <b>4 sugar cubes or 4 teaspoons sugar</b> · <b>1 tablespoon honey or syrup</b>. Symptoms can <b>persist after glucose is back to normal</b> — go by the 15-minute recheck, then a small complex-carbohydrate and protein snack.</div>\n<div><b>Moderate and severe at home</b> — <b>moderate</b> (cold clammy pale skin, rapid pulse, rapid shallow breathing, mood change, drowsiness; usually &lt; 50): <b>30 g</b> fast carbohydrate, glucagon may be needed, recheck in 15 minutes, repeat if &lt; 60, then a snack. <b>Severe</b> (can't swallow, confused, combative, unconscious, seizing): family gives <b>glucagon IM, subcut or nasal</b>; <b>second dose in 10 minutes</b> if still unconscious; then <b>call 911</b> for IV dextrose; call the provider; a small meal once awake and not nauseated.</div>\n<div><b>Can't swallow — glucagon and dextrose</b> — <b>glucagon</b> (the main balancing hormone) is first-line for severe lows. It can cause <b>vomiting → turn the patient on their side</b>. <b>Concentrated IV dextrose</b> (e.g., D50) is <b>hyperosmolar</b> — give carefully to avoid <b>extravasation</b> and tissue damage. Both wear off: check glucose <b>every 15 minutes until resolved</b>, then monitor for hours; target <b>70–110</b>. <b>Critical rescue</b>: glucagon → repeat in <b>10 minutes</b> if still unconscious → notify the provider.</div>\n<div><b>Prevention and teaching</b> — personal hypoglycemia plan; <b>teach the family the 15-15 rule and how to give glucagon</b>; <b>medical alert bracelet</b>; <b>always carry glucagon, a carb source and a meter</b>; regular meal timing (<b>a meal delayed more than 30 minutes</b> raises risk on some regimens); test <b>before driving</b>; exercise when insulin isn't peaking; alcohol only with food; call the provider if lows occur <b>more than twice a week</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Fast sugar works because glucose needs no digestion — it goes straight from the gut into the blood. Fat in chocolate slows stomach emptying and delays it, and complex carbohydrates have to be broken down first; that is exactly why they are used <b>after</b> the low is fixed, to keep glucose up while the fast sugar runs out.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-dka-and-hhs-the-hyperglycemic-emergencie","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"DKA and HHS — the hyperglycemic emergencies","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — <b>uncontrolled hyperglycemia + metabolic acidosis + ketones</b>, from <b>insulin deficiency</b> plus burning fat and protein for fuel and a surge of stress hormones. <b>Most common trigger: infection or illness.</b> Onset is <b>sudden</b>.</div>\n<div><b>What you see</b> — <b>polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia</b>, <b>fruity breath</b>, <b>vomiting, abdominal pain</b>, weakness; dehydration — weight loss, dry skin, <b>sunken soft eyeballs</b>, poor turgor, oliguria with high specific gravity, weak rapid pulse, <b>flat neck veins</b>, postural hypotension. <b>Kussmaul respirations</b>; mental status from alert to coma. <b>Confusion or Kussmaul breathing → shock and coma can come quickly.</b></div>\n<div><b>Labs (Table 56.9)</b> — glucose <b>&gt; 300</b>; serum ketones <b>positive</b>; <b>pH &lt; 7.35</b>; <b>HCO₃⁻ &lt; 15</b>; sodium low, normal or high; <b>BUN &gt; 30 and creatinine &gt; 1.5</b> from dehydration; urine ketones positive; potassium may start <b>high</b> (shift) despite total-body loss.</div>\n<div><b>Monitoring</b> — first: <b>airway, LOC, hydration, electrolytes, glucose</b>. <b>BP, pulse and respirations every 15 minutes until stable</b>; <b>urine output, temperature and mental status hourly</b>; CVP every 30 minutes if present; then vital signs every 4 hours once stable. Glucose results decide when to switch from saline to dextrose.</div>\n<div><b>Fluids</b> — <b>first goal: restore blood volume</b> — about <b>15–20 mL/kg in the first hour</b>. Then <b>hypotonic</b> fluid at 4–14 mL/kg/hr. <b>When glucose reaches 250 → D5 in 0.45% saline</b>, to prevent <b>hypoglycemia</b> and <b>cerebral edema</b> from osmolarity falling too fast. Up to <b>6–10 L in 24 hours</b>; watch for <b>fluid overload</b> (heart failure, pulmonary edema) via BP, I&amp;O and daily weight.</div>\n<div><b>Insulin</b> — <b>regular insulin by continuous IV infusion</b> after an IV bolus (unless DKA is mild) — short half-life and slow subcutaneous onset make IV necessary. Aim to lower glucose about <b>50–75 mg/dL per hour</b>. Switch to <b>subcutaneous</b> when the patient takes oral fluids and <b>ketosis has stopped</b>. <b>Resolved</b> when glucose <b>&lt; 200</b>, bicarbonate <b>&gt; 18</b>, venous pH <b>&gt; 7.30</b>, anion gap <b>&lt; 12</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Potassium and bicarbonate</b> — insulin, fluids and correcting acidosis <b>drive potassium down</b>: start replacement once K⁺ <b>falls below 5.0</b>. <b>Action alert: urine output at least 30 mL/hr before giving IV potassium.</b> Watch for <b>hypokalemia</b> — fatigue, confusion, muscle weakness, <b>shallow breathing</b>, abdominal distention or ileus, hypotension, weak pulse, <b>ECG changes</b>. <b>Bicarbonate only for severe acidosis</b> (pH <b>≤ 7.0</b> or HCO₃⁻ <b>&lt; 5</b>), by <b>slow IV infusion over hours</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Hyperglycemic-hyperosmolar state (HHS)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>What it is</b> — extreme hyperglycemia and <b>dehydration</b> without significant ketosis: glucose <b>&gt; 600</b>, osmolarity <b>&gt; 320</b>; <b>ketones negative</b>; pH <b>&gt; 7.4</b>; HCO₃⁻ <b>&gt; 20</b>. Enough insulin remains to <b>prevent ketosis</b> but not hyperglycemia, so glucose climbs higher than in DKA and the osmotic diuresis is extreme. Onset is <b>gradual</b>; mostly <b>older adults with type 2</b>, many undiagnosed; mortality up to <b>16%</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Why older adults</b> — blunted <b>thirst</b>, poor <b>urine concentration</b> and <b>diuretics</b> → assess <b>all</b> older adults for dehydration, diabetic or not. Triggers: <b>infection</b>, poor fluid intake, <b>MI, sepsis, pancreatitis, stroke</b>, and drugs — <b>glucocorticoids, diuretics, phenytoin, beta blockers, calcium channel blockers</b>.</div>\n<div><b>The brain</b> — confusion to coma, <b>seizures</b>, reversible paralysis; <b>coma once osmolarity &gt; 350</b> (normal <b>270–300 mOsm/L</b>). Brain recovery lags hours behind normal glucose.</div>\n<div><b>Fluids first</b> — goal: rehydrate and normalize glucose over <b>36–72 hours</b>. <b>Normal saline in shock or severe hypotension; otherwise half-normal saline</b>, at <b>1 L/hr</b> until CVP rises or BP and urine output are adequate, then <b>100–200 mL/hr</b>; <b>half the deficit in the first 12 hours</b>, the rest over the next 36. Check for <b>cerebral edema hourly</b> (abrupt mental change, abnormal neuro signs, coma). No improvement = too little fluid or osmolarity not falling; <b>regression after improvement = osmolarity falling too fast</b>. <b>Slow, steady neurologic improvement</b> = fluids are right.</div>\n<div><b>Critical rescue and insulin</b> — changes in <b>LOC, pupils</b> (size, shape, reaction) or <b>seizures → notify the provider immediately</b>. <b>IV insulin only after adequate fluids</b>: bolus, then infusion until glucose reaches <b>250</b>, lowering <b>50–70 mg/dL per hour</b>. Potassium drops quickly → <b>electrolytes every 1–2 hours</b> and <b>continuous cardiac monitoring</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Sick days and prevention (Box 56.12)</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Sick-day rules</b> — <b>tell the provider you are ill</b>; check glucose <b>every 2–4 hours</b>; <b>test for ketones even if glucose is in range</b>, especially when vomiting; <b>keep taking insulin or antidiabetic drugs</b> unless told otherwise — <b>never omit insulin</b>; <b>8–12 oz (240–360 mL) of sugar-free, caffeine-free fluid every hour awake</b> (sugar-containing fluids if glucose is below target); keep regular meal times; if solids won't stay down, replace the meal's carbohydrate with easier foods or liquids (<b>at least 150 g of carbohydrate a day</b> prevents starvation ketosis); rest.</div>\n<div><b>When to call</b> — <b>persistent nausea and vomiting</b>; can't keep food or fluids down; <b>moderate or large ketones</b>; glucose <b>&gt; 250 not responding</b> or still high after <b>two supplemental insulin doses</b>; persistent hypoglycemia; temperature <b>101.5°F (38.6°C)</b> or rising, or fever <b>&gt; 24 hours</b>; illness <b>longer than 1–2 days</b>.</div>\n<div><b>DKA teaching</b> — check glucose <b>every 2 hours</b> while anorexia, nausea or vomiting continue or glucose stays <b>&gt; 250</b>; check <b>ketones when glucose &gt; 250</b>; drink at least <b>2 L a day</b>, more with infection; with nausea, sip liquids with <b>glucose and electrolytes</b> (regular soda, diluted juice, sports drinks); take extra rapid or regular insulin as the provider directs.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Both emergencies are dehydration emergencies first, which is why <b>fluids come before insulin</b>: insulin pulls glucose — and water with it — into the cells, and given to a patient with an empty circulation it can collapse the blood pressure. The two differ in one thing: in DKA there is no insulin at all, so fat is burned and ketoacids form; in HHS a little insulin stops the ketones but not the climbing glucose, so the danger shifts to the <b>brain</b> as osmolarity soars.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-foot-care-kidneys-and-preventing-injury","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Foot care, kidneys and preventing injury","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">The diabetic foot</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Why feet are lost</b> — most amputations follow a <b>foot ulcer</b>, and 5-year mortality after amputation is high. <b>Neuropathy is the main cause of ulcers; poor perfusion is the main cause of poor healing.</b> Motor neuropathy → <b>claw toes</b>, thinned fat pad under the <b>metatarsal heads</b>, pressure points → calluses → ulcers. <b>Charcot foot</b>: warm, swollen, painful; the arch collapses into a <b>\"rocker bottom\"</b>. Autonomic → dry, cracked skin. Sensory → numbness, injuries unnoticed. <b>PAD</b> slows healing.</div>\n<div><b>How injuries happen</b> — <b>walking barefoot</b>, <b>ill-fitting shoes</b>, <b>heat</b> (hot water bottles, heating pads, hot baths) and <b>chemical burns</b> from OTC corn removers. Pressure ulcers: <b>sole and ball</b> from walking, <b>top and sides</b> from shoes; typically at the <b>great toe, under the metatarsal heads, and on tops of claw toes</b>. Infection is common, hard to treat, and raises glucose further.</div>\n<div><b>Risk factors</b> — neuropathy, <b>male sex</b>, poor glucose control, cardiovascular, eye or kidney complications; poor gait, calluses, deformities, limited joint motion, severe nail problems, PAD, and <b>any prior ulcer or amputation</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Assessment (Box 56.8)</b> — history of ulcer or amputation; <b>dry, cracked skin</b>, ulcers, thick or ingrown nails, tinea pedis, fungal nails; circulation — <b>claudication</b>, <b>dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses</b>, prolonged capillary refill (the box prints \"greater than 25 seconds\", which looks like a misprint — normal refill is under about 3 seconds <i>(not in this chapter)</i>), <b>hair loss on the top of the foot</b>; deformity — calluses, corns, prominent metatarsal heads, <b>claw or hammertoes</b>, bunions, Charcot foot; strength — ankle and great-toe motion; sensation — numbness, burning, tingling and <b>Semmes-Weinstein monofilament at 10 points on each foot</b>. Professional foot exam <b>at least yearly</b>.</div>\n<div><b>Monofilament and risk categories (Table 56.7)</b> — a <b>10-g</b> nylon monofilament: <b>can't feel it anywhere = high ulcer risk</b>. Category 0 (sensation intact, no PAD or deformity): <b>yearly exam</b>, footwear teaching. Category 1 (no protective sensation ± deformity): <b>every 3–6 months</b>, consider a specialist. Categories 2–3 (no sensation <b>plus PAD</b>, or <b>prior ulcer/amputation</b>): <b>every 1–3 months</b>, specialist, <b>prescription footwear</b>, vascular consult.</div>\n<div><b>Footwear</b> — fitted by an experienced fitter (podiatrist): <b>½–⅝ inch longer than the longest toe</b>, <b>heels under 2 inches</b>; <b>change shoes at midday and again in the evening</b>. Soft, seamless, padded, moisture-absorbing socks — <b>white</b>, so blood or drainage shows. No tight stockings or constricting bands. Toe deformities → <b>custom shoes with high, wide toe boxes</b>; break new shoes in slowly with frequent checks.</div>\n<div><b>Teaching (Box 56.9)</b> — <b>inspect feet daily</b>, including <b>between the toes</b> (mirror on the floor for the soles); wash daily in <b>lukewarm</b> water and <b>dry thoroughly</b>; moisturize — <b>but not between the toes</b>; clean cotton socks daily; <b>alternate shoes</b> day to day, breathable leather or cloth; <b>check shoes for pebbles</b> and torn linings; buy shoes <b>later in the day</b>; <b>socks for warmth</b>; <b>cut nails straight across</b> and file; <b>check bath water with the wrist or a thermometer</b>; never hot-water bottles, heating pads or heaters; <b>don't self-treat corns, calluses, blisters or ingrown nails</b>; <b>never barefoot</b>; no open-toed sandals or thong straps; <b>don't cross legs</b>; see the provider <b>at once</b> for blisters, sores or infection (dry sterile dressing, <b>no tape on skin</b>). First check that the patient can see and reach every part of the foot; if not, <b>teach a family member</b> to inspect and care for it.</div>\n<div><b>Wound care</b> — standards: a <b>moist wound environment</b>, <b>débridement</b> of dead tissue, and <b>offloading</b> — <b>no shoe on the affected foot</b>; total-contact casts, half shoes, cast walkers, wheelchairs, crutches. Patients without sensation keep walking on painless ulcers, enlarging them. <b>Ulcers recur unless weight is permanently redistributed.</b></div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Protecting the kidneys</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Screening and targets</b> — <b>yearly urine albumin</b>: type 1 after <b>5 years</b>, type 2 <b>from diagnosis</b>. <b>Persistent albuminuria 30–299 mg/24 h</b> = earliest nephropathy (type 1) and a marker in type 2. Keep <b>BP below 140/80</b> (the kidney section's target; the cardiovascular section gives &lt; 140/90); control glucose and cholesterol; <b>stop smoking</b>. Once albuminuria appears, the focus is BP, glucose and <b>avoiding nephrotoxic drugs</b>.</div>\n<div><b>ACE inhibitors and ARBs</b> — for <b>persistent albuminuria</b> or advanced nephropathy they <b>slow progression and lower albuminuria</b> — but are <b>not</b> given just to \"protect\" the kidney when BP and albumin are normal. <b>Monitor potassium (hyperkalemia).</b></div>\n<div><b>Nephrotoxins</b> — the most common: <b>antifungals</b> and <b>aminoglycoside antibiotics</b>; also <b>NSAIDs</b> (ibuprofen, naproxen). Check with the provider or pharmacist before OTC drugs or herbs. <b>IV contrast</b> → monitor <b>IV hydration before and after</b> to prevent contrast nephropathy.</div>\n<div><b>Dialysis</b> — the same as for anyone, but <b>reduce insulin</b> when dialysis starts — <b>hypoglycemia</b> risk rises.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Kidney damage and insulin interact in a way that surprises students: the kidney also <b>clears insulin</b>, so as kidney function falls, each dose lasts longer and hypoglycemia becomes more common — the \"improving\" glucose in a patient with worsening kidneys is often a warning, not a success.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-diabetes-in-the-hospital-and-around-surg","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Diabetes in the hospital and around surgery","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Glucose targets in the hospital</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Why glucose rises</b> — illness, less activity, <b>held antidiabetic drugs</b>, <b>corticosteroids or antipsychotics</b>, changed nutrition. Inpatient hyperglycemia means <b>more infections, longer stays, more ICU care and higher mortality</b>; admission glucose <b>&gt; 198</b> predicts worse outcomes. <b>Hypoglycemia</b> also raises mortality.</div>\n<div><b>Targets</b> — <b>critically ill: 140–180</b> (continuous <b>IV insulin drip</b> works best in ICU). <b>Non-critical: before meals &lt; 140, random &lt; 180</b>, with scheduled <b>basal + mealtime + correction</b> subcutaneous insulin. <b>Review the regimen below 100; change it below 70.</b></div>\n<div><b>NPO patients</b> — give <b>basal</b> insulin (often at a <b>reduced</b> dose) — it controls baseline glucose. <b>Hold rapid- and short-acting insulin, amylin analogs and incretin mimetics</b> (they cause lows without food). <b>Don't give premixed insulins</b> (they contain rapid or short insulin).</div>\n<div><b>Unexpected rise = look for infection</b> — balancing hormones often raise glucose <b>before a fever appears</b>. When a patient's controlled glucose <b>rises unexpectedly</b>, check for <b>infection</b> — UTI, pneumonia, cellulitis, wound infection.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Why it is riskier</b> — anesthesia and surgery release <b>counterregulatory hormones</b> → hyperglycemia and <b>ketoacidosis</b> risk; <b>HHS</b> after surgery carries high mortality; diuresis → dehydration and <b>acute kidney injury</b>. Diabetes adds hypertension, ischemic heart disease, stroke, <b>MI</b> and cardiomyopathy risk; <b>urinary retention</b>; <b>gastroparesis and reflux (aspiration with anesthesia)</b>; <b>paralytic ileus</b> from autonomic neuropathy.</div>\n<div><b>Before surgery</b> — <b>sulfonylureas stopped 1 day before</b>; <b>metformin stopped at least 24 hours before</b> and restarted only when <b>kidney function is normal</b>; <b>other oral drugs stopped the day of surgery</b>; long-acting insulin may be switched to <b>intermediate-acting 1–2 days before</b>. Pre-op glucose <b>&lt; 200</b> (higher = more infection and poor healing).</div>\n<div><b>During surgery</b> — IV <b>insulin, glucose and potassium</b> with <b>hourly</b> capillary glucose; target <b>140–180</b>; about <b>5 g glucose per hour</b> to prevent hypoglycemia, ketosis and protein breakdown; stress may raise insulin needs.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">After surgery</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Insulin and analgesia</b> — continue glucose and insulin infusions until stable and eating; short-term insulin may be needed even for patients on oral agents. <b>Opioids slow the gut and change glucose</b>; in older adults they risk confusion, ileus, hypoventilation, hypotension and retention — <b>PCA</b> reduces respiratory problems and confusion.</div>\n<div><b>Monitoring</b> — autonomic neuropathy or vascular disease → watch for <b>hypotension and respiratory arrest</b>. <b>Beta blockers mask hypoglycemia</b> → monitor closely. <b>Hyperkalemia</b> in kidney failure; <b>hypokalemia</b> worsened by intraoperative insulin and glucose → <b>cardiac rhythm and K⁺</b>. <b>Continuous ECG</b> for older patients, long-standing type 1 and heart disease — ECG or K⁺ changes may be a <b>silent MI</b>. Fluid balance for <b>acute kidney injury</b> (hydrate well with nephrotoxic antibiotics); raised blood protein or nitrogen makes fluid management harder — check central venous or pulmonary artery pressure as needed.</div>\n<div><b>Post-op nutrition</b> — clear or full liquids: about <b>200 g of carbohydrate a day</b> in equal portions — <b>not sugar free</b>. <b>25–35 kcal/kg/day</b>. Advance to solids as fast as tolerated; once eating, at least <b>150–200 g of carbohydrate daily</b> to prevent lows. <b>TPN</b> can cause severe hyperglycemia → glucose at least <b>every 6 hours</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div><div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Pancreas transplant</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Who and what</b> — the most common surgical treatment: success means <b>no insulin, no glucose monitoring and few diet limits</b>, but long-term complications only partly reverse. <b>Lifelong antirejection drugs</b> (some raise glucose; infection, cancer and atherosclerosis risk) limit it to serious progressive complications or uncontrollable acute ones; often <b>with a kidney transplant</b> (pancreas alone, after kidney, or simultaneous) — the pancreas graft survives better when placed <b>at the same time as the kidney</b>. 1-year survival <b>&gt; 95%</b>; <b>HLA tissue matching</b> affects results.</div>\n<div><b>The operation</b> — a cadaver pancreas is placed in the <b>pelvis</b>; the native pancreas stays. Its <b>800–1000 mL a day</b> of fluid drains to the <b>bladder or bowel</b> — bladder drainage irritates and loses <b>bicarbonate (acidosis)</b>. Antiviral, antibacterial and antifungal prophylaxis first; adjust insulin if steroids are used.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Surgery is a controlled injury, and injury triggers the same stress hormones as illness — cortisol, epinephrine, glucagon — all of which raise glucose and blunt insulin. That is why insulin needs go <b>up</b> around surgery even while the patient eats <b>nothing</b>, and why the oral drugs that either force insulin out (sulfonylureas, low risk without food) or depend on healthy kidneys (metformin) are stopped first.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-teaching-self-management","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Teaching self-management","kind":"topic","tag":"","exam":"","html":"<div class=\"blueprint\" style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding:var(--space-4);min-width:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<i class=\"corner tl\"></i><i class=\"corner tr\"></i><i class=\"corner bl\"></i><i class=\"corner br\"></i>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400)\">Teaching self-management</div>\n<div style=\"font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px\">\n<div><b>Assess before teaching (Box 56.13)</b> — history, diet, activity, drugs and supplements; <b>literacy, numeracy</b>, motivation and health beliefs; current self-management and past training; <b>age, mobility, vision, hearing, dexterity, attention</b>; psychosocial support and <b>mental health</b>; substance use; work, money, culture, religion; <b>access to care</b>; language (use a <b>medical interpreter</b>). If not ready to learn, ask permission to teach a <b>family member</b>, and leave written materials and a number to call.</div>\n<div><b>Physical limits</b> — check that they can <b>read insulin labels and syringe markings</b> (vision worsens with fluctuating glucose); materials in the <b>preferred language</b>, pictographs if helpful. Hand injury, <b>tremor or arthritis</b> → dosing errors with syringes → consider <b>pens</b> or other preparations; meters with <b>prefilled strip drums</b>.</div>\n<div><b>How to teach</b> — written or picture handouts, step-by-step discussion, <b>hands-on</b> practice, and <b>teach-back</b>. In acute illness teach only <b>survival skills</b>; save detail for follow-up. Break skills into <b>small achievable tasks</b> (first just drawing up an accurate dose) to build <b>self-efficacy</b> — mastering injection reduces fear.</div>\n<div><b>Survival skills</b> — simple pathophysiology; <b>how to take insulin or the drug</b>; <b>recognize, treat and prevent hypo- and hyperglycemia</b>; basic diet (<b>meals at consistent times</b> and in step with the insulin, consistent carbohydrate amounts, and the fast carbohydrate that treats a low); <b>glucose and ketone monitoring</b>; <b>sick-day rules</b>; where to buy and <b>how to store</b> supplies; <b>when to call</b> the provider.</div>\n<div><b>In-depth education</b> — explain insulin action, how <b>diet, drugs and activity</b> move glucose, and why normal glucose prevents complications; <b>demonstrate injection</b>; state the insulin's <b>onset, peak and duration</b>, when, where and how it is stored; insulin-to-carb ratios and correction factors if supported; <b>dangers of skipping doses</b> and resources if unaffordable; drug interactions (especially older adults). Consistent carbs or carb counting; include whoever <b>buys and cooks</b> the food. SMBG and how to act on it. Sick-day rules <b>from diagnosis</b> — hyperglycemia often appears <b>before</b> infection symptoms.</div>\n<div><b>Living with it</b> — diagnosis can feel like a <b>loss of control and flexibility</b>; technology helps, but routines remain. Adherence follows strategies that <b>make sense and seem to work</b>; SMBG shows the patient their own cause and effect.</div>\n<div><b>The team (Interprofessional Collaboration)</b> — diabetes is diagnosed and managed mostly in the <b>clinic and community</b>, so teaching happens at every encounter. The team: primary health care provider, <b>endocrinologist</b>, diabetes-specialist advanced practice nurse, physician associate, <b>certified diabetes care and education specialist (CDCES)</b>, <b>ophthalmologist</b>, registered nurse, <b>pharmacist</b>, <b>registered dietitian nutritionist (RDN)</b>, <b>podiatrist</b>, physical therapist and <b>wound care specialist</b>; social workers and psychologists also teach.</div>\n<div><b>Home care (Box 56.14) and resources</b> — mental status; vital signs and weight (<b>fever = infection?</b>); vision; mouth, gums and teeth; <b>injection sites and rotation</b>; skin and old wounds; <b>foot care and feet</b> (pulses, sensation, skin, nails); bowels; the glucose diary; meter technique; insulin storage, preparation, injection and sharps disposal; drug knowledge. Older adults living alone need <b>daily phone contact</b>, help with shopping, meals, transportation and drawing up insulin; refer to home care or public health.</div>\n<div><b>Ready for home (Box 56.15)</b> — the patient (or significant other) can tell <b>why</b> the drug is prescribed, <b>name, dose and frequency</b>, how meals relate to its action, the diet plan; <b>prepare and inject insulin accurately</b>; test glucose and <b>urine ketones</b> (and when); <b>store insulin</b>; list <b>hypoglycemia symptoms</b> and the <b>carbs that treat them</b>; list <b>hyperglycemia symptoms</b>; what to eat and drink when <b>ill</b> (<b>8–12 oz of sugar-free, caffeine-free fluid every hour</b>; if solids won't stay down, the meal's carbohydrate as liquids or soft foods, <b>at least 150 g a day</b>; regular meal times; never skip insulin); <b>when to call</b> (frequent lows, signs of highs); and <b>foot care</b>.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Diabetes is managed by the patient roughly 99% of the time — every meal, every dose, every walk is a decision made at home. Teaching is therefore not a discharge task but the main treatment, and the most important thing to check is not what the patient heard but what they can <b>do</b>: draw up the dose, read the meter, treat a low.</div>\n</div>"},{"id":"56-adpie","ch":"56","chTitle":"Diabetes mellitus","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","kind":"adpie","tag":"Foundation","exam":"","html":"<div style=\"border-top:1px solid var(--color-divider);padding-top:var(--space-6);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-4)\">\n<div>\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300);margin-bottom:6px\">The nursing process for this chapter</div>\n<p style=\"margin:0;max-width:86ch;font-size:16px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">Diabetes care runs on two clocks at once. The fast clock is the emergencies — <b>hypoglycemia, DKA, HHS</b> — where minutes and the right first step matter. The slow clock is the years of vessel damage — <b>eyes, kidneys, nerves, feet, heart</b> — where daily habits matter. Every plan protects against both, and puts the patient in charge of the slow one.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">A — Assess</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">History</div>risk factors, <b>family history</b>, weight change, <b>3 Ps</b>, fatigue; infections — <b>vaginal yeast</b>, skin injuries that <b>heal slowly</b>; vision and touch changes; women: <b>large babies or gestational diabetes</b>; drugs (steroids, antipsychotics), alcohol, smoking; current regimen, meal pattern, activity, monitoring habits, past lows and highs; <b>cost</b> and other barriers.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Physical</div>vital signs with <b>orthostatics</b> (autonomic neuropathy); weight and BMI, <b>waist</b>; skin and <b>injection sites</b>; <b>full foot exam</b> (pulses, monofilament, skin, nails, deformity); vision; mouth and gums; hydration (turgor, mucous membranes); breathing pattern and <b>breath odor</b> (Kussmaul, fruity); mental status; bowel sounds.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Labs</div><b>glucose</b> (fingerstick, lab, CGM trend), <b>A1C</b>, <b>ketones</b> (urine or blood), electrolytes with <b>potassium</b>, <b>pH and bicarbonate</b>, anion gap, <b>osmolarity</b>, BUN and creatinine, <b>urine albumin</b>, lipids; ECG when potassium shifts.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border-left:2px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding-left:10px;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\"><span style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent-300)\">Why</span><br/>Ask about the <b>slow-healing wound</b> and the <b>yeast infection</b> on purpose: in type 2 they are often the first and only signs, years before thirst or weight loss — and they are things patients rarely connect to their blood sugar.</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">D — Diagnose &amp; prioritize</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Priority problems</div>1. <b>Potential for injury from hyperglycemia</b>. 2. <b>Potential for surgical complications</b>. 3. <b>Potential for injury from peripheral neuropathy</b>. 4. <b>Potential for kidney disease</b> from reduced kidney perfusion. 5. <b>Potential for acute complications</b> — hypoglycemia, DKA, HHS.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Prioritizing in the moment</div>a <b>conscious patient with glucose &lt; 70</b> comes before almost anything — treat first, document after. In DKA and HHS: <b>airway → fluids → insulin → potassium</b>. Unexpected glucose rise in a controlled patient → think <b>infection</b>.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">P — Plan</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Outcomes</div>glucose stays <b>in the target range</b> (A1C <b>&lt; 7%</b>, pre-meal 80–130, peak post-meal &lt; 180; in hospital &lt; 140 before meals and &lt; 180 random; ICU 140–180) <b>without significant lows</b>; recovery from surgery without complications; <b>intact skin on the feet</b>; optimal kidney function and urine output; no DKA, HHS or severe hypoglycemia; the patient meets the <b>Box 56.15</b> teaching criteria.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">I — Implement</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Glucose control</div>monitoring before meals and bedtime and with symptoms; give <b>rapid-acting insulin just before meals</b> after a check <b>within 30 minutes</b>; correct technique, sites and storage; <b>basal insulin when NPO</b>, hold mealtime insulin; reinforce the RDN's plan, carb counting or MyPlate; <b>activity rules</b> (ketones → no exercise; 100–250 to start); teach <b>sick-day rules</b> and <b>alcohol with food</b>.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Emergencies</div><b>hypoglycemia</b>: 15-15 rule (30 g if &lt; 50), <b>glucagon or IV dextrose</b> if unable to swallow, side-lying, recheck every 15 minutes, then a complex carb with protein. <b>DKA</b>: VS every 15 minutes, hourly UO and mental status, <b>15–20 mL/kg first hour</b>, <b>IV regular insulin</b>, <b>D5½NS at 250</b>, <b>K⁺ once &lt; 5.0 with UO ≥ 30 mL/hr</b>. <b>HHS</b>: <b>fluids first</b> (NS if shocked, otherwise ½NS), hourly neuro and cerebral edema checks, insulin after fluids, electrolytes every 1–2 hours, continuous ECG.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Surgery</div><b>sulfonylureas off 1 day before, metformin ≥ 24 hours before</b> (restart after kidney function confirmed), other orals off the day of surgery; pre-op glucose <b>&lt; 200</b>; intra-op IV insulin, glucose and potassium with hourly checks (140–180); post-op <b>continuous ECG</b> when at risk, potassium, I&amp;O for AKI, <b>non-sugar-free liquids (~200 g carb/day)</b>, PCA for pain.</div>\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Feet, kidneys and teaching</div>yearly <b>monofilament</b> exam and risk category; <b>daily foot inspection</b> and Box 56.9 habits; shoes fitted <b>½–⅝ inch longer than the longest toe</b> with <b>heels under 2 inches</b>, changed at midday and evening, and <b>white</b> socks; <b>offloading</b> and moist wound care for ulcers. Yearly urine albumin; <b>BP &lt; 140/80</b>; ACEI/ARB for albuminuria with <b>potassium checks</b>; avoid <b>NSAIDs, aminoglycosides, antifungals</b>; <b>hydrate around contrast</b>. Teach with <b>teach-back</b>, starting with survival skills.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--color-divider);border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent-600);padding:var(--space-4);display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:var(--space-3)\">\n<div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:22px;font-weight:600\">E — Evaluate</div>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,260px),1fr));gap:var(--space-4);font-size:15.5px;color:var(--color-neutral-300)\">\n<div style=\"min-width:0\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--font-heading);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-neutral-400);margin-bottom:6px\">Evaluate</div>glucose and A1C reach target <b>without frequent lows</b>; DKA resolution criteria met (glucose &lt; 200, HCO₃⁻ &gt; 18, pH &gt; 7.30, anion gap &lt; 12); HHS — <b>steady neurologic improvement</b>; surgery recovery without infection or AKI; <b>feet intact</b>; urine albumin stable; and the patient can <b>demonstrate</b> injection, glucose and ketone testing, treatment of a low, sick-day rules and foot care.</div>\n</div>\n\n</div>\n</div>"}];
